insert into question_context (question_id, context) values
  ('2009-1', 'ADHD was once framed primarily as a disruptive-behavior problem, but contemporary formulations emphasize its frequent overlap with internalizing and neurodevelopmental conditions. Anxiety can reduce apparent concentration through threat monitoring and worry; learning disorders undermine performance in a specific academic domain despite adequate opportunity to learn. This creates a useful memory connection: ADHD affects regulation across tasks, whereas a learning disorder produces a more circumscribed skill deficit, and anxiety often makes attention deteriorate in feared situations. More than one mechanism may operate in the same child.'),
  ('2009-2', 'The memorable paradox of adolescence is less wiring but better organization. Early development generates an excess of synaptic connections; later experience helps retain useful circuits and eliminate less useful ones. This resembles editing a crowded draft: removing material can improve coherence rather than diminish content. At the same time, increasing myelination improves the speed and coordination of surviving networks. The brain therefore becomes more efficient even as some measures of synaptic density, glucose use, and electrical amplitude decline.'),
  ('2009-3', 'The term borderline originated from an older idea that some patients lay at a border between neurotic and psychotic levels of functioning. Modern diagnostic use does not mean that a person is almost psychotic. A useful memory link is stress at the border: intense affective and interpersonal stress may transiently impair reality testing or produce dissociation. The diagnosis itself rests on a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image, affect regulation, and impulse control across contexts, rather than on isolated unusual perceptual experiences.'),
  ('2009-4', 'Metoclopramide is often remembered as a gastrointestinal drug, but its antiemetic action includes central dopamine D2 receptor antagonism. That mechanism links it to antipsychotic-like extrapyramidal adverse effects. A practical memory cue is that antiemetic does not mean non-neurologic: a newly added nausea medication can cause dramatic abnormal movements. Acute dystonia frequently begins soon after exposure and preferentially affects visible cranial, ocular, jaw, and neck muscles. Laryngeal involvement makes immediate airway assessment especially important.'),
  ('2009-5', 'Thomas and Chess described temperament through dimensions such as activity, rhythmicity, approach or withdrawal, adaptability, intensity, mood, distractibility, persistence, and sensory threshold. Their categories summarize recurring combinations of these dimensions. The concept of goodness of fit is the useful clinical extension: outcomes depend partly on how a child’s temperament interacts with environmental demands and caregiving responses. Remember difficult as intense, irregular, and slow to adapt—not unintelligent, deliberately oppositional, or destined to develop psychopathology.'),
  ('2009-6', 'Suicide assessment distinguishes relatively enduring vulnerability factors from dynamic or proximal warning signs. Age, sex, prior attempts, and longstanding diagnoses help describe baseline vulnerability; sleep loss, agitation, intoxication, acute humiliation, and access to lethal means can signal a changing state. A useful memory connection is static versus state: demographic facts establish context, whereas pervasive insomnia may reveal that the patient’s condition is actively worsening. No single factor predicts suicide with certainty, so structured clinical formulation remains essential.'),
  ('2009-7', 'Extrapyramidal syndromes can be remembered by their time course: acute dystonia usually appears early, akathisia commonly follows over days to weeks, parkinsonism evolves over weeks, and tardive syndromes generally emerge after longer exposure. Laryngeal dystonia is an uncommon but dangerous version of the same acute phenomenon that produces torticollis or an oculogyric crisis. Benztropine works by reducing excessive cholinergic influence within motor circuits after dopamine blockade, rather than by reversing antipsychotic binding directly.'),
  ('2009-8', 'The name Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory provides a memory aid: multiphasic refers to assessment across multiple domains rather than a single symptom or cognitive function. The instrument was developed using empirical item-keying—items were retained because groups answered them differently, not merely because each item seemed theoretically obvious. The MMPI-2 updated the original inventory’s normative base while retaining its broad clinical purpose. Its prominent validity scales reflect an enduring principle of psychological testing: interpretation depends on how the person approached the test.'),
  ('2009-9', 'Absence seizures arise from abnormal oscillation within thalamocortical circuits, producing abrupt, brief impairment of awareness with a generalized spike-and-wave pattern. This explains why antiseizure drugs are not interchangeable: agents helpful for focal seizures may worsen some generalized epilepsies. Ethosuximide is narrow and absence-focused, whereas valproate is broad-spectrum. A useful memory connection is narrow syndrome, narrow drug; mixed generalized syndrome, broad drug—while always incorporating the patient’s adverse-effect and reproductive-risk profile.'),
  ('2009-10', 'Fiduciary derives from a root associated with trust or faith. The concept is used when one person entrusts important interests to another who has specialized authority, such as a trustee or attorney. Medicine fits because patients disclose private information and rely on expertise they cannot readily evaluate. The memorable chain is vulnerability leads to trust, and trust generates duties. Those duties support, rather than replace, the patient’s autonomy: the physician supplies candid expertise while the capable patient retains decision-making authority.'),
  ('2009-11', 'Psychodynamic therapies are often described along an expressive-supportive continuum. Expressive approaches use clarification and interpretation to increase insight, whereas supportive approaches use interventions that reduce distress and reinforce adaptation. The distinction is not that supportive therapy lacks thoughtfulness or warmth. Its characteristic focus is function before interpretation: preserve coping, reality testing, and role functioning when a patient''s capacity is strained. Supportive treatment may be brief or long term and is often combined with practical care coordination.'),
  ('2009-12', 'Older psychodynamic writing often separated descriptive diagnosis from structural personality organization. Two patients could show similar surface traits yet differ in identity integration, defenses, reality testing, impulse control, and capacity for reciprocal relationships. The memory connection is style versus structure: flirtation or theatricality describes style, whereas the degree of modulation and reflection reveals structure. Current diagnostic systems emphasize observable trait patterns and impairment, so this high-level versus primitive distinction is best understood as an additional formulation lens rather than a separate categorical diagnosis.'),
  ('2009-13', 'The basal ganglia are not only motor structures; they participate in parallel loops connecting frontal cortex, striatum, pallidum, thalamus, and back to cortex. Small penetrating vessels supply many components of these circuits, making them vulnerable to hypertensive small-vessel disease and lacunar infarction. This creates a useful memory connection: damage to deep motor-executive loops can combine gait changes with slowed cognition and impaired initiation. Vascular dementia nevertheless remains a clinicopathologic umbrella rather than a single fixed lesion pattern.'),
  ('2009-14', 'The name stiff-person captures the visible phenotype, but the mechanistic memory aid is loss of inhibition. Glutamic acid decarboxylase helps synthesize GABA; autoimmunity associated with this enzyme is linked to reduced inhibitory control of motor pathways. Motor units then remain active when muscles should relax, producing a statue-like posture and superimposed spasms. Anti-GAD antibodies are supportive rather than independently diagnostic because they occur in other autoimmune neurologic and endocrine conditions, so the antibody result must fit the clinical and electrophysiologic pattern.'),
  ('2009-15', 'Epidemiologic assessment often uses a two-stage design. A brief, inexpensive questionnaire first casts a wide net; a more intensive diagnostic interview then clarifies cases in a subset. The GHQ belongs to the first stage and focuses on changes in usual psychological functioning rather than producing a full categorical diagnosis. Its name can be remembered as general health screening, whereas instruments labeled interview or examination usually imply an assessor who asks, probes, codes, or interprets responses.'),
  ('2009-16', 'NNT converts a probability difference into a patient-centered frequency. If treatment improves the desired outcome by an absolute 0.10, its reciprocal is 10, so ten patients must be treated for one additional benefit. The memorable pairing is benefit versus harm: NNT describes one extra good outcome or one prevented bad outcome, while number needed to harm describes one extra adverse outcome. Because absolute risk varies across settings, the same relative effect can yield different NNT values.'),
  ('2009-17', 'Nonsuicidal self-injury and suicidal behavior can overlap in the same patient but are not identical. Cutting may temporarily regulate unbearable affect, numbness, or dissociation, while suicidal intent and lethality vary over time. Alcohol is important in risk assessment because it narrows the gap between impulse and action. It may impair judgment, increase affective reactivity, reduce behavioral control, and make a patient''s self-report less dependable during an acute encounter. Risk assessment must integrate dynamic clinical information rather than rely on one historical feature.'),
  ('2009-18', 'Caffeine belongs to the methylxanthine family and is structurally suited to blocking adenosine receptors. Adenosine normally accumulates during wakefulness and contributes to sleep pressure, so antagonism produces the familiar sense of increased alertness. The memory connection is cultural invisibility: a substance can be pharmacologically active yet feel ordinary because it is sold as food and incorporated into daily rituals. That normalization helps explain why caffeine surpasses more conspicuously intoxicating substances in worldwide consumption.'),
  ('2009-19', 'Asperger disorder was a separate pervasive developmental disorder in DSM-IV. Its clinical memory pattern was autism-like social and behavioral features without significant early language or cognitive delay; pedantic speech was especially memorable because grammar and vocabulary could be strong while reciprocity and pragmatics were impaired. Later diagnostic systems consolidated Asperger disorder, autistic disorder, and related pervasive developmental disorders into autism spectrum disorder. The conceptual shift emphasizes a spectrum with specifiers rather than sharply separated categories, while preserving the importance of the person’s developmental history and functional needs.'),
  ('2009-20', 'Lithium is a simple ion with a complex psychiatric role. It became a foundational treatment for mania and maintenance of bipolar disorder, and its association with reduced suicidal behavior made it distinctive among mood stabilizers. A useful memory link is that lithium treats the long-term highs and lows of bipolar illness while also being the classic medication answer when the question emphasizes suicide prevention. Its narrow therapeutic index explains why laboratory monitoring and patient education are integral to treatment rather than optional add-ons.'),
  ('2009-21', 'Lewy body disease is a synuclein-related neurodegenerative disorder in which cognitive, psychiatric, sleep, autonomic, and motor symptoms can overlap. Its memorable board-style triad is fluctuating cognition, visual hallucinations, and parkinsonism. The practical connection is pharmacologic: impaired dopaminergic systems help explain why even modest dopamine D2 blockade may provoke disproportionate rigidity, sedation, or confusion. Hallucinations in an older person with dementia therefore require attention to the neurologic syndrome, not merely suppression of psychosis.'),
  ('2009-22', 'OCD is often chronic, but its severity can wax and wane. When those shifts occur in a distinctly episodic rhythm, mood-state linkage becomes an important conceptual clue. Bipolar disorder is defined by episodes, so it is a useful diagnostic frame when seemingly separate symptoms repeatedly rise and fall with changes in sleep, energy, activity, and affect. Comorbidity and symptom overlap do not eliminate the need to identify which syndrome is driving the current clinical state.'),
  ('2009-23', 'Case-control studies move conceptually from effect to possible cause. They are useful when an outcome is rare or has a long latency, because investigators need not follow a very large exposed and unexposed population until enough outcomes occur. Their central comparison is the odds of earlier exposure among cases versus controls. A mnemonic is cases first, causes second. Because exposure is often assessed retrospectively, careful selection of controls and reliable exposure measurement are crucial to reducing bias.'),
  ('2009-24', 'Clozapine is often remembered for two high-yield identities: treatment-resistant schizophrenia and reduction of recurrent suicidal behavior in schizophrenia-spectrum illness. Its broad receptor effects may contribute to benefit, but this question tests the clinical association rather than a single proven anti-suicide mechanism. The same features that make clozapine distinctive also make it operationally demanding. Prescribing requires careful titration, systematic adverse-effect surveillance, blood monitoring, and reliable follow-up rather than casual substitution for another antipsychotic.'),
  ('2009-25', 'Preschool cognition is rich in symbolic play: a block can become a car, a blanket can become a cape, and an invented companion can become part of a story. This imaginative capacity is developmental rather than inherently pathological. A helpful distinction is between playful, voluntary, context-sensitive pretend activity and involuntary perceptual experiences that are distressing, fixed, or associated with impaired functioning. The developmental meaning comes from the whole child’s functioning, not from the imaginary companion alone.'),
  ('2009-26', 'Pavlovian learning does not require a person to consciously decide to form an association. Repeated pairing of a cue with drug use can make the cue motivationally powerful. In substance-use disorders, this helps explain why a syringe, neighborhood, song, or social setting may trigger craving long after the pharmacologic drug effect is absent. The cue is not the drug itself; it is a learned signal that predicts prior drug-related experience.'),
  ('2009-27', 'Amphetamines increase synaptic catecholamine signaling, which helps explain their alerting and sympathomimetic effects as well as their capacity to produce agitation and paranoia. Stimulant psychosis can closely resemble schizophrenia phenomenologically, so timing, collateral history, physical findings, and course after abstinence matter. A compact memory link is that speed can accelerate salience: ordinary stimuli may feel intensely important, threatening, or personally directed. Acute assessment must also address medical instability and imminent risk to the patient or others.'),
  ('2009-28', 'Dopamine supports movement but also influences reward, salience, and motivation. Parkinson treatment can therefore improve motor function while provoking maladaptive reward-seeking in susceptible patients. The memorable association is a dopamine agonist, particularly pramipexole, followed by gambling, hypersexuality, compulsive shopping, or binge eating. The source’s original all-credit key was invalidated during adjudication, and the corrected answer is pramipexole. Recognition often depends more on direct questioning and collateral history than on the neurologic examination.'),
  ('2009-29', 'Alcohol enhances inhibitory GABA-related signaling and suppresses excitatory signaling. With chronic exposure, the brain adapts; abrupt cessation produces relative hyperexcitability. The clinical spectrum runs from tremor and anxiety to seizures and delirium tremens. A useful memory sequence is that early withdrawal can include tremor, seizures can occur during withdrawal, and delirium tremens adds severe confusion and autonomic instability. Hospitalization may unintentionally reveal dependence when alcohol intake abruptly stops.'),
  ('2009-30', 'The heart’s electrical cycle depends on tightly regulated electrolyte gradients. Repeated purging can disrupt potassium, chloride, volume status, and acid-base balance, which is why eating-disorder assessment includes medical as well as psychiatric monitoring. For recall, connect purging with potassium and the ECG: electrolyte disturbance may make repolarization unstable, reflected in T-wave or QT changes. This is one reason that symptom severity alone cannot determine medical risk.'),
  ('2009-31', 'The shift from dementia praecox to schizophrenia reflected a change in clinical emphasis. Dementia praecox suggested early onset and inexorable decline, whereas Bleuler''s term drew attention to disorganization or splitting among psychological functions and allowed for a more variable course. For memorization, Kraepelin named the course concept and Bleuler named schizophrenia. Modern classification has evolved beyond both historical formulations, but the distinction remains useful for examination questions and for correcting a common public misunderstanding.'),
  ('2009-32', 'Memory testing is organized by time interval and process. Immediate recall asks whether information can be held briefly; recent memory asks whether newly acquired information can be retrieved after a short delay; remote memory addresses more distant material. Breakfast yesterday is memorable because it is ordinary, individually verifiable through collateral when needed, and recent enough to test everyday encoding and retrieval. Attention is needed before a memory can be encoded.'),
  ('2009-33', 'Antidepressant prescribing rewards careful timing. A medication can have early effects on sleep, appetite, energy, or anxiety before dysphoria fully improves. This creates a common exam distinction: partial early improvement is evidence to continue a tolerable treatment, whereas nonadherence, severe adverse effects, worsening safety, or an incorrect diagnosis may require a different response. An adequate trial includes sufficient time and an appropriate dose, interpreted within the patient’s clinical context.'),
  ('2009-34', 'Language is learned in relationships, not merely overheard. Direct child-focused conversation gives a child both linguistic material and repeated opportunities to practice attention, response, and meaning. A useful memory phrase is serve and return: the child signals interest, an adult responds with language, and the exchange builds communication. This framework also explains why shared reading and conversation are useful even though children vary in temperament, hearing, neurodevelopment, and opportunity.'),
  ('2009-35', 'The alliance is often summarized as bond, goals, and tasks. It is a cross-theoretical concept: psychodynamic, cognitive-behavioral, supportive, and other therapies all rely on enough trust and collaboration for patients to participate meaningfully. The alliance is not a fixed trait of either therapist or patient. It develops over time, can rupture, and can be repaired. That makes it both a research finding and a practical focus of every psychotherapy session.'),
  ('2009-36', 'ACT was developed around a simple service-delivery insight: some patients need treatment brought to them rather than being expected to navigate fragmented systems alone. Its defining features are a multidisciplinary team, low caseloads, frequent outreach, and practical support across psychiatric and social domains. Think of ACT as an intensive wraparound model for people whose illness and circumstances repeatedly disrupt clinic attendance, medication continuity, housing, and crisis prevention.'),
  ('2009-37', 'Psychiatric terminology is most useful when anchored to observable behavior. Alogia concerns the quantity and productivity of speech, whereas neologisms and loose or unusual associations concern language form or thought organization. Source review confirmed alogia as the historical key even though the quotation is atypical and can suggest disorganization. That caveat should remain visible so the example is not treated as a perfect modern definition. A practical memory cue is “a-logia”: reduced speech rather than merely strange speech.'),
  ('2009-38', 'Sexual-response difficulties are best understood through several domains: desire, arousal or erection, orgasm or ejaculation, and pain. Premature ejaculation belongs to the orgasm and ejaculation domain and is memorable because it is commonly reported across age groups. Prevalence questions should not obscure the clinical principle that a sexual concern becomes a disorder when it is persistent and causes meaningful distress or difficulty, not merely because it differs from an imagined norm.'),
  ('2009-39', 'PSP is one of the atypical parkinsonian disorders. Its name refers to impaired voluntary vertical eye movements, but the broader syndrome is often easier to recognize clinically: balance and axial control deteriorate early, while executive dysfunction may alter initiative, affect, and behavior. In contrast, idiopathic Parkinson disease often begins with asymmetric bradykinesia, rigidity, or tremor and usually develops severe postural instability later. Dysphagia and recurrent falls early in illness are important clues that the syndrome may not be typical Parkinson disease.'),
  ('2009-40', 'Oxytocin is synthesized in the hypothalamus and released from the posterior pituitary. It is commonly remembered for uterine contraction and milk ejection, but it also has an important role in social affiliation and maternal behavior. In examination questions, oxytocin is paired with attachment, lactation, and caregiving behavior. Prolactin is more specifically associated with milk production. This distinction helps separate a hormone involved in feeding physiology from one classically linked to maternal-infant bonding in animals.'),
  ('2009-41', 'Charles Bonnet syndrome is a visual release phenomenon: when normal visual input is markedly reduced, visual association networks may generate internally produced imagery. It is not evidence that the person has a primary psychotic illness. The key triad is significant visual impairment, complex visual hallucinations, and retained insight. Naming the syndrome can reduce shame and prevent mislabeling. New symptoms, impaired insight, fluctuating attention, or cognitive decline should trigger reassessment for delirium, neurocognitive illness, medication effects, or neurologic disease.'),
  ('2009-42', 'Activated charcoal is a physical adsorbent rather than a universal antidote. Its benefit depends on whether the ingested substance binds to charcoal and whether administration can be performed without unacceptable aspiration risk. Lithium is memorable because its ionic chemistry prevents meaningful adsorption. Even when an agent is adsorbable, charcoal may be inappropriate in an unprotected airway, after caustic ingestion, or when the anticipated benefit is low. Toxicology management should be tailored to the substance, exposure pattern, and patient condition.'),
  ('2009-43', 'Memory has multiple components. Anterograde amnesia is impaired formation of durable new memories after an event, often due to medial temporal or diencephalic dysfunction. Herpes simplex encephalitis is notable for its temporal-limbic predilection, so cognitive and psychiatric symptoms may coexist with seizures. Preserved clock drawing does not exclude a circumscribed memory deficit; rather, it helps distinguish an amnestic presentation from diffuse delirium. The clinical question is whether the patient can encode and retain new information, not simply whether an event was witnessed.'),
  ('2009-44', 'Conversion disorder is now termed functional neurological symptom disorder. Older examinations sometimes used clues such as la belle indifférence or fantastic symptom quality, but these features are neither required nor sufficiently specific for diagnosis. The durable concept is positive functional findings that are internally inconsistent or incongruent with recognized neurologic disease in an appropriate assessment. Clinicians should not decide that a symptom is functional merely because it seems unusual, emotionally charged, or difficult to explain. This item preserves a historical phenomenologic teaching point.'),
  ('2009-45', 'OBRA nursing-home reforms sought to reduce inappropriate psychotropic prescribing and improve oversight of residents'' care. The historical examination memory point is target symptom, time limit, taper review: record why a drug was started, define improvement, and repeatedly test whether it remains necessary. Current federal requirements and facility policies have changed in wording and timing since 1987, so clinicians must apply the active regulatory standard rather than rely on this older question alone. Periodic reassessment remains sound geriatric prescribing because older adults are vulnerable to sedation, falls, orthostasis, and other adverse effects.'),
  ('2009-46', 'Medications for alcohol use disorder have different safety profiles. Naltrexone carries hepatic cautions and cannot be used casually in someone taking opioids. Acamprosate is chiefly associated with renal assessment, while disulfiram requires attention to hepatic safety and reliable alcohol avoidance. The source’s multi-answer response was adjudicated as ambiguous and corrected to naltrexone as the best-listed choice. Its fixed baseline-and-eight-week testing language should be understood as exam-era framing rather than a non-universal modern schedule for every patient.'),
  ('2009-47', 'Children’s understanding of death develops gradually. Younger children may not fully grasp permanence, universality, or biological finality, and they often express grief through play, behavior, questions, or imaginative statements rather than adult-like verbal sadness. Grief is not judged by whether a child stops mentioning the deceased. The clinically important questions are the child’s understanding, level of distress, function, safety, and whether symptoms indicate a separate trauma-related, anxiety, mood, or behavioral disorder.'),
  ('2009-48', 'Double bind is a family-systems communication concept. Its memorable structure is: do this, but do not make me feel what doing it means. The contradiction is often carried in tone, implication, or relationship demands rather than in two plainly incompatible instructions. Contemporary clinicians use the idea cautiously to explore communication and autonomy conflicts, not to assign families sole responsibility for complex psychiatric conditions. A useful formulation identifies the conflicting messages and opens space for direct discussion of the underlying fears, needs, and practical constraints.'),
  ('2009-49', 'Stimulant intoxication and withdrawal are useful mirror images. Intoxication commonly brings activation, decreased sleep, and reduced appetite; the subsequent crash brings fatigue, prolonged sleep, hunger, dysphoria, and vivid dreaming. Cocaine metabolites can remain detectable after the subjective high has ended, so urine testing may corroborate recent exposure without proving current impairment. The timing of symptoms, collateral history, examination, and repeated risk assessment remain more informative than a toxicology result alone.'),
  ('2009-50', 'Panic disorder is maintained partly by catastrophic interpretation of bodily sensations and avoidance of situations associated with attacks. SSRIs alter serotonergic signaling over time, whereas cognitive-behavioral therapy directly targets fear of internal sensations and avoidance. A board-style memory link is panic: SSRI or CBT first. Starting low is practical because patients with panic may be particularly sensitive to early activating effects. Treatment should include education about the expected time course, follow-up, and evaluation for comorbid depression, substance use, and agoraphobic avoidance.'),
  ('2009-51', 'The key distinction in migraine pharmacology is abortive versus preventive treatment. Triptans are designed to stop an established attack by acting on trigeminovascular serotonin receptors. Beta-blockers, certain antiseizure medicines, and other agents are taken regularly to reduce attack frequency over time. A memory link is that sumatriptan sums up the acute attack, whereas topiramate, valproate, and propranolol aim to prevent later attacks. Safe prescribing still requires assessment of vascular contraindications, headache pattern, and medication-overuse risk.'),
  ('2009-52', 'Epidemiologic labels differ primarily by their time window. Point prevalence is a snapshot, period prevalence covers a stated interval, and lifetime prevalence looks back over the respondent’s life. Incidence is different because it counts new onsets over time. “Lifetime equals ever” is a useful memory aid, but estimates can be influenced by recall bias, survival, diagnostic definitions, age distribution, and the population sampled. A prevalence measure reports how common a condition is, not how likely a particular person is to develop it in the future.'),
  ('2009-53', 'Lithium is an elemental ion cleared by the kidneys, so ordinary physiologic changes can meaningfully alter its exposure. It is a classic example of therapeutic drug monitoring, in which a measured blood concentration informs both efficacy and safety. The concept is broader than memorizing a target number: level interpretation depends on dose timing, clinical response, adverse effects, kidney function, hydration, sodium balance, and interacting medications. This narrow therapeutic index explains why lithium became a defining monitored medication in psychiatry.'),
  ('2009-54', 'Pupil size reflects a balance between parasympathetic constriction and sympathetic dilation. Horner syndrome is remembered as a small pupil with slight lid droop due to loss of sympathetic input. The sympathetic route extends from the hypothalamus through the brainstem and spinal cord, then through the cervical sympathetic chain to the eye. This long pathway explains why the same ocular pattern can arise from central, preganglionic, or postganglionic lesions and why associated signs are important for localization.'),
  ('2009-55', 'Delusions are fixed false beliefs that are not readily changed by ordinary counterevidence and are not culturally sanctioned. Communication is usually more effective when it addresses shared reality at the level of feelings and consequences, such as fear, insomnia, isolation, or risk. This stance is often summarized as neither confronting nor colluding. It preserves alliance while leaving room for careful assessment, treatment of the underlying disorder, and gradual examination of the patient’s experiences without strengthening the delusional framework.'),
  ('2009-56', 'The four-humor model organized physical health and emotional differences around blood, phlegm, yellow bile, and black bile. It persisted for centuries because it provided a coherent, observable-seeming system for interpreting illness and temperament. Its most useful modern role is historical and mnemonic. The term melancholia literally retains the black-bile association, demonstrating how older explanatory systems can remain embedded in clinical language long after their biological claims have been rejected.'),
  ('2009-57', 'Pyridoxine is memorable because chronic excess, as well as deficiency, can injure peripheral sensory nerves. Toxicity may present with burning, paresthesias, numbness, sensory loss, or impaired gait. Adjudication corrected an OCR-corrupted vitamin reference so the stem consistently identifies vitamin B6. Because the official key page is missing, burning sensations remains an explicitly inferred answer based on the corrected clinical concept rather than an official response. This distinction preserves both the medical teaching point and the source limitation.'),
  ('2009-58', 'Jung’s typology framed introversion and extraversion as attitudes describing where a person tends to direct attention and psychological energy. Later trait models adopted related terms, commonly spelling the latter extraversion. Everyday language can obscure the original concept: introversion is not necessarily timidity, and extraversion is not necessarily emotional health. The terms describe style and orientation, whereas psychiatric assessment evaluates symptoms, suffering, context, and functional impairment.'),
  ('2009-59', 'CBT for bulimia nervosa is memorable because it treats a self-reinforcing behavioral cycle rather than only exploring distant origins of symptoms. Restrictive eating can increase vulnerability to binge episodes, while compensatory behavior and self-criticism perpetuate the cycle. CBT makes these links explicit and introduces structured behavioral change alongside cognitive work. This present-focused approach distinguishes it from therapies designed around other primary mechanisms or disorders, while still allowing treatment to be adapted to the individual patient.'),
  ('2009-60', 'Forensic psychiatrists serve the legal process by offering independent professional opinions rather than outcome-driven advocacy. Contingent compensation can make an expert’s financial interest rise or fall with the success of one party’s claim. The central ethical distinction is between payment for work performed and payment for a consequence of the work. This principle applies in civil, criminal, disability, personal-injury, and custody matters. Transparent, outcome-independent fee arrangements help protect the credibility of the opinion and the integrity of the evaluation.'),
  ('2009-61', 'Countertransference originally referred narrowly to the therapist''s unconscious reactions rooted in personal conflicts. Contemporary usage is broader: emotional, cognitive, and bodily responses can provide information about a treatment relationship while also reflecting ordinary factors such as fatigue. The useful discipline is feel it, reflect on it, then decide what to do. This protects against impulsive interpretations and turns the therapist''s reaction into possible clinical data. It also preserves the patient''s care as the central task of the session.'),
  ('2009-62', 'Academic achievement, intellectual ability, and adaptive functioning are related but distinct domains. A child may have intact general reasoning yet show weakness in a particular learned academic skill, or may struggle because of attention, language, emotional distress, sensory impairment, or inadequate instruction. The WRAT is a practical screen for core school skills rather than a broad developmental or personality assessment. A useful memory cue is that it samples reading, writing or spelling, and arithmetic—the academic areas most directly relevant to classroom performance.'),
  ('2009-63', 'Lithium has a narrow therapeutic index, and neurotoxicity does not map perfectly onto one serum concentration; timing, chronic exposure, kidney function, dehydration, and interacting medicines all matter. A useful memory link is lithium to tremor and ataxia to cerebellum. The cerebellum coordinates movement rather than initiating strength, so its injury is remembered through incoordination, dysmetria, gait instability, and scanning or slurred speech. Persistent neurologic sequelae after lithium poisoning are often a cerebellar-predominant syndrome.'),
  ('2009-64', 'Benzodiazepines enhance inhibitory GABA-A signaling. With repeated exposure, the nervous system adapts, and abrupt discontinuation leaves relative neuronal hyperexcitability. This explains the combination of anxiety, tremor, insomnia, and seizures. Alcohol, barbiturates, and benzodiazepines share a potentially life-threatening withdrawal profile, unlike typical adult opioid withdrawal. Lorazepam lacks long-lived active metabolites, but regular use can still produce dependence and clinically significant withdrawal. In a substance-related seizure, the timing relative to the last dose is therefore essential.'),
  ('2009-65', 'ECT induces a therapeutic generalized seizure under controlled anesthesia and muscle relaxation. Its history gives memory effects special salience, but contemporary practice modifies treatment parameters to balance efficacy with cognitive burden. A useful exam contrast is that postictal confusion and memory effects are expected cognitive risks, whereas myalgia is often related to the procedure or anesthetic context and dental trauma is uncommon with proper precautions. Electrode placement is often taught partly through its cognitive tradeoff.'),
  ('2009-66', '“Shy-Drager syndrome” is an older term now generally included within multiple system atrophy, especially when autonomic failure is prominent. Degeneration of central autonomic pathways disrupts blood-pressure regulation, bladder control, sweating, and sometimes laryngeal function; parkinsonian or cerebellar signs may also develop. The memorable pattern is multiple systems failing together rather than one isolated autonomic symptom. Using the current name helps distinguish this neurodegenerative disorder from a focal posterior-circulation stroke or a transient drug-induced autonomic effect.'),
  ('2009-67', 'Meperidine is an opioid analgesic whose metabolite has clinically important effects distinct from the parent drug. Normeperidine is neurotoxic and can produce central nervous system excitation, making meperidine a classic examination exception among opioids. Typical opioid withdrawal instead causes craving, myalgias, gastrointestinal distress, and autonomic symptoms, not seizures in most adults. The memory cue is simple: meperidine produces a problematic metabolite. This distinction matters when interpreting a substance-related seizure in a patient with access to controlled medications.'),
  ('2009-68', 'GHB occurs naturally in small amounts in the body and is also misused for sedating and euphoric effects. Its association with pulsatile growth-hormone release gave it a reputation in some athletic settings despite substantial safety concerns. Nandrolone is the useful contrast: it is an anabolic-androgenic steroid that acts through androgen receptors rather than by increasing episodic growth-hormone secretion. Both substances may arise in a performance-enhancement history, but their mechanisms and acute clinical risks differ considerably.'),
  ('2009-69', 'Older DSM criteria tried to avoid automatically labeling acute grief as major depression. The DSM-IV bereavement exclusion used a two-month interval and severe-feature qualifiers as a practical boundary. DSM-5 removed that exclusion because bereavement can precipitate a clinically significant major depressive episode, while grief remains a normal human response. For examination questions, the diagnostic era implied by the item controls the answer. For patient care, evaluate symptoms, risk, impairment, history, and context rather than applying an obsolete interval mechanically.'),
  ('2009-70', 'The mental status examination is a bedside assessment of current cognitive and emotional functioning. Dementia is not defined by a low score or abnormal scan alone; it is an acquired cognitive decline that interferes with independence. Neuroimaging can identify structural causes, and neuropsychological testing can refine the cognitive profile, but the clinical examination integrates the observed syndrome in real time. A useful memory link is that dementia evaluation begins with the patient and informant, not with a scanner or a single test subscore.'),
  ('2009-71', 'Kleine-Levin syndrome is memorable as a recurrent sleeping-and-eating syndrome that typically begins in adolescence. Between episodes, patients often return close to their usual baseline. Its distinctive feature is not simply daytime sleepiness but a discrete episode of strikingly prolonged sleep accompanied by altered cognition and behavior. The normal organization of REM and NREM sleep in the vignette helps separate increased sleep quantity from a primary disruption of sleep architecture.'),
  ('2009-72', 'Fluoxetine is one of the most established SSRIs in pediatric depression teaching and has a long half-life. The board-relevant principle is not that every adolescent with depression needs medication; treatment choice depends on severity, impairment, safety, psychotherapy access, and patient or family preference. When pharmacotherapy is chosen, fluoxetine has especially strong support among the listed options. SSRIs require close monitoring in younger patients, particularly early in treatment and after dose changes, within a broader psychotherapy and safety plan.'),
  ('2009-73', 'Developmental milestones unfold across gross motor, fine motor, language, social, and cognitive domains. Early motor skills remain present long after their initial acquisition, so age-estimation questions often hinge on the most advanced cognitive skill rather than the earliest motor ability. A pincer grasp is an infant skill, while knowledge of calendar information reflects later cognitive and educational development. The practical test-taking strategy is to date the child by the newest milestone in the list, not by abilities the child has retained since infancy.'),
  ('2009-74', 'The AIMS was designed to make observation of dyskinetic movements systematic and reproducible. Tardive dyskinesia may involve the tongue, lips, jaw, face, trunk, or limbs after exposure to dopamine-blocking medications. The scale records more than movement severity: it also captures distribution, incapacitation, and patient awareness. This reflects the clinical importance of combining objective signs with the patient’s subjective experience. Repeated structured assessments help identify emerging movements and track change after medication adjustments or other interventions.'),
  ('2009-75', 'SSRIs increase serotonergic signaling not only in the brain but also in the enteric nervous system. This provides a simple memory connection: SSRI start-up can produce gut symptoms before benefit. In contrast, the desired antidepressant effect usually develops more gradually. Side-effect timing matters on exams and in practice: an adverse effect can be common overall yet not be the principal reason for stopping during the earliest phase of treatment.'),
  ('2009-76', 'Dementia is chronic decline; delirium is an acute disturbance in attention and awareness that can present as a sudden behavioral problem. Older adults may show infection or another medical illness through confusion, agitation, or altered perception rather than a clear complaint. The teaching shortcut is that new confusion is medical until assessed. Urine results require clinical interpretation because asymptomatic bacteriuria is common in older populations and does not by itself establish a symptomatic infection.'),
  ('2009-77', 'OCD is maintained partly through negative reinforcement: compulsions temporarily reduce anxiety and therefore become more likely to recur. ERP reverses this process by pairing feared triggers with nonperformance of the ritual. Exposure means approaching a feared cue in a planned therapeutic manner, and response prevention means withholding the compulsion. This is why generic discussion-oriented therapy is not equivalent to CBT for OCD. Remember ERP as the behavioral core of first-line psychotherapy for obsessive-compulsive disorder.'),
  ('2009-78', 'Capacity is a clinical, decision-specific assessment, whereas competence is traditionally a legal determination. Health-care proxy or durable-power-of-attorney documents allow a patient to appoint a surrogate in advance, often taking precedence over default next-of-kin hierarchies. A useful sequence is capacity absent, then check the advance directive, then contact the named surrogate. The surrogate generally uses substituted judgment when the patient’s wishes are known, or best-interest reasoning when they are not.'),
  ('2009-79', 'Cigarette smoke contains combustion products that induce certain hepatic enzymes; nicotine is not the relevant inducer. Therefore, patients who stop smoking while using nicotine replacement can still lose smoke-related enzyme induction. Haloperidol can cause extrapyramidal symptoms when dopamine blockade becomes excessive. The broader clinical lesson is to treat smoking status like a medication exposure: starting, stopping, or resuming cigarettes can alter concentrations of selected psychotropics. Carefully document tobacco use and recent changes when evaluating unexpected adverse effects.'),
  ('2009-80', 'IPT is a time-limited, structured psychotherapy based on the reciprocal effects of mood symptoms and interpersonal stress. Its distinctive early intervention is naming depression as an illness and granting a temporary sick role. This can reduce guilt and help patients accept assistance. IPT then addresses concrete relationship patterns and role changes, distinguishing it from therapy centered on cognitive distortions or unconscious conflict. The four classic focal areas are grief, role disputes, role transitions, and interpersonal deficits.'),
  ('2009-81', 'Antimuscarinic blockade reduces parasympathetic activity and produces dry skin and mucosa, mydriasis, tachycardia, urinary retention, reduced bowel motility, hyperthermia, and delirium. It contrasts with cholinergic toxicity, which produces secretions, diarrhea, and miosis. The toxidrome mnemonic is useful only when linked to examination findings; dry skin and constipation are particularly discriminating in this vignette. Psychiatric patients are vulnerable because several commonly used medications have anticholinergic properties.'),
  ('2009-82', 'Essential tremor is most closely associated with abnormal activity in cerebellar-thalamic-cortical pathways. The thalamus relays cerebellar output to motor cortex, making VIM a useful site for disrupting tremor transmission. A practical memory cue is “VIM for visible intention and action tremor.” In contrast, basal-ganglia targets such as the subthalamic nucleus and internal globus pallidus are classically associated with Parkinson disease. Target selection depends on the movement disorder being treated and the patient’s clinical goals.'),
  ('2009-83', 'Managed care introduces a review process focused on authorization and resource use. That process can inform planning, but it does not displace the treating clinician’s ethical obligation to prevent foreseeable harm. Administrative authorization may affect payment and appeals, whereas discharge requires a clinical determination that the patient can safely receive care at a lower level. Clear, contemporaneous documentation is especially important when the insurer’s judgment differs from the treating team’s assessment.'),
  ('2009-84', 'PTSD is not an inevitable result of trauma. A useful framework separates exposure dose from vulnerability and recovery factors. Severity and proximity describe exposure dose; childhood experiences and prior illness describe vulnerability; social support influences recovery and chronicity. This framework explains why people affected by the same event can have very different trajectories without implying that either response is a personal failure. It also supports a broad, individualized assessment after trauma.'),
  ('2009-85', 'Lewy body disorders can combine cognitive symptoms with parkinsonism, hallucinations, sleep disturbance, and autonomic symptoms. Their medication sensitivity reflects compromised dopaminergic signaling: further D2 blockade can sharply worsen movement and alertness. Visual hallucinations occurring early alongside parkinsonian signs are particularly suggestive. This does not mean every behavioral symptom should be medicated; evaluation for delirium and reversible triggers should come first. The examination clue is neuroleptic sensitivity in a patient with parkinsonism and hallucinations.'),
  ('2009-86', 'Suicide prevention includes treating underlying illness, strengthening connection, planning for crises, and making lethal methods less available during periods of heightened risk. Means safety is not limited to firearms, but firearms receive special attention because their lethality leaves little opportunity for rescue. The practical memory cue is that when suicide risk rises, access to the most lethal available methods should fall while additional care is arranged.'),
  ('2009-87', 'The boxed warning arose from concern that some younger patients experience increased suicidal thoughts or behaviors during antidepressant treatment. A useful examination distinction is signal versus outcome: the warning concerns ideation and behavior, not completed suicide. In practice, the warning reinforces active follow-up and shared safety planning. It should be considered alongside the risks of untreated depression, rather than used as a reason to ignore significant symptoms or withhold indicated treatment.'),
  ('2009-88', 'Grief often includes sadness, yearning, and waves of pain, while major depression more often features pervasive hopelessness, excessive guilt, worthlessness, or self-destructive thinking. The conditions can overlap, and preserved occupational functioning does not settle the diagnosis or establish safety. This item’s answer is inferred because the official key page is missing, but combined medication and psychotherapy is defensible under its framing. The decisive clinical responsibility is to assess risk explicitly and match the treatment setting to the patient’s current level of danger.'),
  ('2009-89', 'Object permanence is a foundational cognitive concept: absence from view is not absence from existence. Peek-a-boo makes this memorable because the caregiver disappears visually and then returns. The infant’s delighted anticipation suggests that the disappearance is understood as temporary. Development unfolds gradually, so real children may show partial or inconsistent searching before the concept is robust. For exam purposes, hidden-toy or peek-a-boo questions usually cue object permanence.'),
  ('2009-90', 'The nucleus accumbens is a central hub in the mesolimbic reward pathway. Dopamine signaling there helps assign motivational salience and reinforces behaviors associated with rewarding outcomes. Amphetamine enters presynaptic terminals and promotes monoamine release, whereas cocaine is classically taught as blocking monoamine reuptake. Both can increase dopamine in reward pathways, but the mechanism emphasized differs. This distinction is a frequent pharmacology examination point and helps separate reward effects from stimulant motor effects.'),
  ('2009-91', 'Group psychotherapy makes interpersonal expectations visible in real time. Consensual validation is testing one’s internal model against the experience of other people in the room. It differs from universalization: universalization reduces isolation by showing that others share a struggle, whereas consensual validation asks whether one’s interpretation of social reality fits others’ actual perceptions. Think of the phrase as consensus testing a conclusion. This process can support insight when feedback is offered respectfully and concretely.'),
  ('2009-92', 'Serotonin syndrome is an excess-serotonin state with altered mental status, autonomic activation, and neuromuscular hyperactivity. Hyperreflexia and clonus are particularly useful clues. Tramadol is easy to overlook because it is often regarded only as an analgesic, but it also has monoaminergic effects. The interaction becomes more plausible when symptoms follow a medication addition or dose increase. Prompt recognition matters because stopping serotonergic agents and providing supportive treatment can prevent progression to severe toxicity.'),
  ('2009-93', 'Length-dependent neuropathy begins where axons are longest: the feet, then the legs, and later the hands. This produces the familiar stocking-glove progression. Alcohol-related neuropathy is clinically important because chronic exposure may coexist with nutritional deficits, so its mechanism is often multifactorial. For examinations, contrast its gradual symmetric sensory pattern with the more motor-focused neuropathy associated with lead exposure. Autonomic symptoms and diminished deep tendon reflexes further support a generalized peripheral neuropathy.'),
  ('2009-94', 'Integrins are cell-surface adhesion molecules that help leukocytes attach to endothelium and move from blood into tissues. Natalizumab targets alpha-4 integrin, making adhesion blockade the key memory phrase. In multiple sclerosis, reducing inflammatory cell entry into the CNS can reduce disease activity. The tradeoff is reduced immune surveillance, which explains why clinicians remain alert for opportunistic CNS infection. This mechanism distinguishes natalizumab from general immunosuppressants and relapse treatments such as corticosteroids.'),
  ('2009-95', 'The classic teaching triad for spinal epidural abscess is back pain, fever, and neurologic deficits, although all three need not be present at once. Risk factors include injection drug use, bacteremia, diabetes, immunosuppression, and recent spinal procedures. Think anatomically: an expanding collection in the epidural space can compress neural structures; weakness, sensory change, saddle-region symptoms, or bladder dysfunction signal threatened neurologic function. The key memory phrase is back pain plus fever plus deficits, meaning epidural abscess must be excluded.'),
  ('2009-96', 'Carbon monoxide impairs oxygen delivery and cellular respiration, making high-demand brain regions vulnerable. The basal ganglia, particularly the globus pallidus, are classically associated with carbon monoxide injury. A memorable pattern is recover, then decline: delayed neurologic sequelae can emerge after a lucid interval. Parkinsonian motor findings in this setting should prompt consideration of toxic-hypoxic injury rather than idiopathic Parkinson disease alone. Cognitive and emotional changes can be part of the same delayed syndrome.'),
  ('2009-97', 'Diagnostic systems allow clinicians to record non-disorder conditions that are important to care. Religious or spiritual problems recognize that faith can be a source of resilience, conflict, identity, guilt, and support. The category distinguishes respectful clinical attention from unnecessary psychiatric diagnosis. A useful question is whether distress reflects a mental syndrome or a life-domain conflict requiring exploration and support. Both deserve care, but they are not the same thing.'),
  ('2009-98', 'Structural family therapy focuses on family organization, subsystems, roles, and boundaries. Boundaries need enough permeability for support and enough clarity for individual development. Enmeshment means boundaries are too diffuse, whereas disengagement means they are too rigid. The language “our disease” is a memorable cue that the parent-child boundary has become blurred, even though family closeness itself is not pathological. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2009-99', 'Acculturation is not a one-way replacement of one culture by another. People may maintain heritage practices, adopt new practices, move fluidly between contexts, or feel disconnected from both. Cultural assessment therefore begins with curiosity rather than demographic inference. The memory phrase is that identity is self-described and contextual: race, birthplace, and ethnicity may matter, but none alone defines a person’s cultural identity or preferences in care.'),
  ('2009-100', 'Therapeutic neutrality is not passive agreement. It means taking the patient’s distress seriously while remaining curious about the interpretation. In psychosis-informed interviewing, questions about onset, evidence, conviction, alternatives, and consequences provide diagnostic information and support rapport. This approach avoids two unhelpful extremes: colluding with a delusion or arguing immediately about whether it is true. Curiosity creates room for later reality testing, safety assessment, and treatment planning.'),
  ('2009-101', 'Speech intelligibility is a useful developmental marker because it reflects articulation, language production, and practical communication. Milestone questions commonly ask which findings should trigger evaluation, not which finding establishes a specific diagnosis. Autism spectrum disorder requires persistent social-communication differences together with restricted or repetitive patterns; delayed intelligible speech alone neither proves nor excludes it. By preschool age, a child should generally communicate effectively enough to make needs and ideas understandable to others.'),
  ('2009-102', 'Twelve-step facilitation is not simply attendance at a meeting; it is a clinical approach that encourages active involvement in a twelve-step recovery community. Its language of acceptance and help beyond oneself contrasts with therapies organized around skill rehearsal or resolving ambivalence. A useful exam association is acceptance plus surrender plus mutual-help steps equals twelve-step facilitation. The “higher power” concept is commonly framed broadly so patients may define it in a personally meaningful way.'),
  ('2009-103', 'The dural venous sinuses drain blood from the brain. Thrombosis obstructs venous outflow rather than arterial inflow and can raise intracranial pressure, cause venous infarction, and sometimes cause hemorrhage. This differs from many arterial strokes, which more often present with abrupt focal deficits. In an examination vignette, progressive headache, vomiting, papilledema, and a superior sagittal sinus contrast filling defect point to a cerebral venous sinus process.'),
  ('2009-104', 'Older psychometric descriptions grouped some subtests into statistical factors. The WAIS historically included a Freedom from Distractibility factor, linking tasks that require holding information in mind while resisting interference. Modern assessment commonly uses the terms working memory and attention, but examination questions may retain the older language. Broad cognitive abilities are assessed by Wechsler scales, whereas the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test is especially associated with cognitive flexibility and executive control.'),
  ('2009-105', 'Postpartum psychosis is uncommon but clinically distinctive because onset can be abrupt and impairment can be profound. Personal longitudinal history is especially informative because a prior episode signals vulnerability during the specific biological and psychosocial transition after delivery. Postpartum blues are common and transient and do not include psychosis. A useful memory principle is that prior illness is particularly predictive when recurrence occurs in the same highly specific context.'),
  ('2009-106', 'Route affects drug exposure: oral administration may have incomplete absorption and first-pass metabolism, whereas intramuscular administration bypasses first-pass hepatic metabolism. Physical dependence means withdrawal can occur after discontinuation, and tolerance means reduced effect with repeated exposure; neither is synonymous with addiction. Older examination vignettes commonly use meperidine, but repeated dosing is now generally limited because normeperidine accumulation can cause neurotoxicity, especially with renal impairment.'),
  ('2009-107', 'Vomiting removes gastric hydrochloric acid, linking it to low chloride and metabolic alkalosis; renal responses can worsen potassium loss. Salivary glands can contribute to elevated serum amylase in recurrent vomiting, whereas lipase elevation would more strongly suggest pancreatic involvement. A compact memory chain is vomiting to chloride loss to alkalosis and hypokalemia, with salivary amylase elevation. Laboratory findings support a formulation but do not replace careful assessment of eating behaviors and medical complications.'),
  ('2009-108', 'Alzheimer disease includes loss of cholinergic neurons and reduced acetylcholine signaling. Cholinesterase inhibitors slow acetylcholine breakdown, so their effect is symptomatic rather than disease reversing. Their role can be remembered as supporting the remaining cholinergic system. Dementia-related behavioral symptoms are multifactorial, and medication benefits are usually limited; medical, interpersonal, and environmental contributors should be assessed in parallel with medication decisions.'),
  ('2009-109', 'Lamotrigine is mainly eliminated by glucuronidation. Valproate inhibits this pathway, which lengthens lamotrigine exposure; many traditional enzyme-inducing antiseizure medicines do the reverse. The board mnemonic is that valproate raises lamotrigine, whereas inducers decrease it. This interaction matters because the rate of lamotrigine titration is tied to rash risk, making pharmacokinetics directly relevant to safe prescribing. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2009-110', 'Lithium is filtered by the kidney and partly reabsorbed in the proximal tubule alongside sodium and water. When the body conserves sodium because of volume depletion or low sodium intake, it can also conserve more lithium. This explains the classic safety teaching: low sodium intake and dehydration can raise lithium levels. The useful mnemonic is that the kidney handles lithium as sodium’s companion, so abrupt changes in sodium balance can change lithium exposure.'),
  ('2009-111', 'Classical psychoanalytic theory distinguishes latent dream thoughts from the manifest dream remembered on waking. Dream work transforms latent material through mechanisms such as condensation and displacement. Condensation is memorable because many elements are compressed into one image, much as vapor condenses into a smaller volume. The question asks for the named psychoanalytic mechanism, not a modern empirical explanation of dreaming or memory processing.'),
  ('2009-112', 'The right parietal lobe is especially important for broad spatial attention and awareness of the left side of the body and surrounding space. Damage can produce neglect, anosognosia, and disturbed body ownership. A memorable lateralization rule is right parietal lesion leading to neglect of the left. These syndromes demonstrate that awareness of one’s body and impairment depends on distributed brain networks, not merely intact primary motor strength or sensation.'),
  ('2009-113', 'Early developmental research changed the view of infants from unresponsive organisms to active social partners. Newborns can coordinate information across senses and show preferences that orient them toward human interaction. Attachment is not a single event but an evolving pattern of regulation, expectation, and caregiving response. A useful memory image is an infant already equipped to seek faces, voices, and rhythmic back-and-forth exchanges with caregivers.'),
  ('2009-114', 'Malpractice risk is not determined only by whether an adverse outcome occurs. Patients are more likely to remain engaged when clinicians communicate honestly, show respect, and maintain continuity. Documentation records clinical reasoning and informed-consent discussions, but it cannot substitute for the relationship itself. This question reflects a broader professionalism principle: trust is both an ethical obligation and a practical safety tool that can reduce misunderstandings and unresolved concerns.'),
  ('2009-115', 'Learning disorders and ADHD are distinct but frequently overlapping neurodevelopmental conditions. A reading disorder reflects a specific, persistent difficulty with reading skills; ADHD involves a broader pattern of inattention and or hyperactivity-impulsivity across settings. The memory connection is that an academic skill deficit plus a pervasive attention problem should prompt consideration of both diagnoses. Careful assessment avoids mistaking inability to decode text for inattention, or assuming that attention treatment alone addresses a specific learning disability.'),
  ('2009-116', 'Myasthenia gravis impairs neuromuscular transmission and produces fatigable weakness. A crisis is defined by respiratory failure or need for ventilatory support caused by that weakness. The emergency sequence follows general resuscitation principles: airway and breathing precede disease-specific therapy. Plasma exchange and IVIG can improve the underlying autoimmune process over time, but mechanical ventilation immediately bridges the patient through respiratory failure and impaired airway protection.'),
  ('2009-117', 'Syphilis has been called a great imitator because it can affect many organ systems and produce varied psychiatric and neurologic syndromes. Central nervous system involvement may include cognitive or personality change, sensory or motor findings, and abnormal CSF indices. The key contrast is degenerative dementia versus an inflammatory CSF process: the latter should prompt a search for alternative, sometimes treatable causes rather than attribution to age-related atrophy alone.'),
  ('2009-118', 'Temporal arteritis is now commonly called giant cell arteritis, reflecting its granulomatous vascular inflammation. A useful memory link is temporal pain plus jaw claudication plus eye danger. Although the disease may involve larger vessels, its most feared consequence is ischemic injury to structures supplied through the ophthalmic circulation. The optic nerve is especially vulnerable because its blood supply depends on small arterial branches with little tolerance for sudden flow reduction.'),
  ('2009-119', 'Multiple sclerosis affects the central nervous system and can create symptoms that overlap with depression, especially fatigue, slowed thinking, sleep disturbance, and reduced activity. This overlap makes careful mood assessment essential. Depression in chronic neurologic illness reflects interacting biologic, psychological, and social factors rather than a simple one-to-one relationship with lesion location or disability. Neurologic illness does not make depression less real, less impairing, or less treatable.'),
  ('2009-120', 'Alzheimer pharmacotherapy is often remembered as two broad symptomatic strategies: support cholinergic transmission or modulate glutamatergic excitotoxic signaling. Memantine belongs to the latter group. NMDA receptors are important for learning-related synaptic plasticity, but excessive persistent activation can permit harmful calcium influx. Memantine’s clinical concept is not turning glutamate off; it preferentially dampens pathologic sustained activation while preserving much ordinary signaling.'),
  ('2009-121', 'The medial longitudinal fasciculus is a compact brainstem pathway for coordinated horizontal eye movement. When looking left, the left abducens system activates the left lateral rectus and, through the medial longitudinal fasciculus, the right medial rectus. A right-sided lesion disconnects that second signal: the right eye fails to adduct while the left eye may develop abducting nystagmus. Remember that internuclear ophthalmoplegia is an internuclear disconnection, not an isolated weak extraocular muscle.'),
  ('2009-122', 'Heme synthesis requires several enzymes and insertion of iron into protoporphyrin. Lead interferes with this pathway, leaving more protoporphyrin unconverted in erythrocytes. This biochemical link explains lead-associated anemia and the historical use of erythrocyte protoporphyrin as a screening clue. Because the marker is not specific, it cannot diagnose lead exposure by itself. For recall, connect lead with heme-pathway disruption rather than only with nonspecific neurobehavioral symptoms.'),
  ('2009-123', 'Reinforcement is a learning concept: a consequence is reinforcing when it makes the preceding behavior more likely to recur. Drugs that produce reward quickly can form powerful associations between cues and drug seeking. Cocaine blocks monoamine reuptake and prominently affects dopamine signaling in reward circuitry. Questions using “reinforcing” test neurobehavioral learning mechanisms, which should be distinguished from tolerance, physical dependence, withdrawal, and toxicity.'),
  ('2009-124', 'Empathy is often contrasted with sympathy in clinical teaching. Sympathy is compassionate concern; empathy is perspective-taking and accurate attunement. Both may be humane, but only empathy necessarily asks what an experience is like for this particular person. In psychotherapy, empathy is foundational to alliance because it helps patients elaborate experiences that may initially be vague, shame-laden, or difficult to state directly.'),
  ('2009-125', 'SIADH reflects antidiuretic hormone activity that is excessive for the body’s osmotic needs. Water retention lowers serum sodium and often lowers blood urea nitrogen through dilution, while urine remains less dilute than expected. Medication effects, pulmonary disease, central nervous system disorders, and malignancy can produce this physiology. A useful physiologic anchor is that hyponatremia normally suppresses antidiuretic hormone and produces dilute urine; failure of urine dilution points toward persistent antidiuretic hormone effect.'),
  ('2009-126', 'Migraine with aura has an epidemiologic association with ischemic stroke, and estrogen exposure can add thrombotic risk. Posterior circulation symptoms may be confusing because migraine aura can include visual or sensory changes. For examination questions, distinguish a population-level risk association from a definitive causal diagnosis in one patient. A real-world evaluation still considers arterial dissection, cardioembolism, thrombophilia, substance exposure, and other vascular mechanisms.'),
  ('2009-127', 'Crossover and blinding describe different dimensions of a trial. Crossover concerns who receives each intervention and in what sequence; double-blinding concerns who knows the assigned treatment. A crossover trial may be blinded or open-label, randomized or nonrandomized. The historical question lacks a precise option, so E is retained only as the closest-listed response. The independent adjudication caveat is essential: learners should remember self-control, sequence, washout, and carryover rather than defining crossover as a variety of double-blinding.'),
  ('2009-128', 'Diagnostic overshadowing is the error of explaining new psychiatric or medical symptoms solely as part of a developmental condition. A practical anchor is to assess change from the person’s usual functioning. New aggression, withdrawal, sleep disruption, self-injury, or loss of skills may communicate distress when conventional symptom reporting is limited. Intellectual disability does not protect against psychiatric illness and should not preclude a careful differential diagnosis or treatment plan.'),
  ('2009-129', 'Night work can misalign internal circadian timing with required sleep and performance periods. Alcohol may seem to ease sleep onset, but it fragments sleep and can reinforce a cycle of fatigue, impaired performance, and escalating use. The examination principle is functional formulation: identify the maintaining behavior and the environmental condition that makes it likely, then alter both when feasible. This is particularly important when impaired alertness affects professional safety.'),
  ('2009-130', 'Biomedical ethics is often organized around autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, and justice. These principles can conflict rather than function as automatic rules. Capacity is a clinical, decision-specific assessment; competence is generally a legal determination. Coercive treatment may sometimes be lawful, but legality does not remove the obligation to explain, document, reassess, and minimize intrusion. The ethical issue in this question is the patient’s control over bodily and medical decisions.'),
  ('2009-131', 'The reproductive axis is sensitive to energy availability. During prolonged undernutrition, the body shifts away from energetically costly reproductive functions, producing central hypogonadal changes. Anorexia nervosa can affect cardiovascular, bone, endocrine, neurologic, and reproductive systems, not only eating behavior. Menstrual status remains clinically important for assessment of physiologic impact, although its absence is no longer required for diagnosis in modern classification systems.'),
  ('2009-132', 'Family psychoeducation developed partly from observations that stressful, hostile, or overinvolved interactions can correlate with relapse risk. It does not claim that families cause schizophrenia. A useful memory frame is information plus skills plus support: teach what the illness is, practice how to respond, and reduce avoidable stress around recovery. The model is practical and collaborative rather than primarily interpretive or focused on restructuring family hierarchy.'),
  ('2009-133', 'Prosody is the music placed on top of words. It tells a listener whether a phrase is a question, where emphasis belongs, and often how emotion is conveyed. Dysprosody literally denotes impaired prosody. A memorable contrast is that vocabulary and syntax carry the verbal message, while prosody carries its cadence and contour. This is why a patient can be linguistically intelligible yet have a strikingly abnormal speech pattern.'),
  ('2009-134', 'Cluster headache is named for attacks that occur in clusters over weeks to months, often with circadian or seasonal patterning. The autonomic features reflect activation of trigeminal-autonomic pathways. A useful examination image is a restless patient pacing during an intensely painful one-sided attack with tearing and a blocked or running nostril. This contrasts with the often quiet, light-avoiding patient with migraine.'),
  ('2009-135', 'Psychotic features in depression are mood-congruent when their content fits depressive themes, although mood-incongruent symptoms can occur. In later life, diagnostic boundaries can be difficult because cognitive symptoms, medical illness, sensory impairment, and delirium may coexist. A useful memory connection is that late onset warrants a wide lens: recognize depressive phenomenology while actively investigating secondary contributors and changes from the person’s baseline function.'),
  ('2009-136', 'Acute stress disorder was designed to identify an early, clinically significant trauma response, with dissociation particularly emphasized in DSM-IV. The duration distinction remains a useful historical board concept: acute stress disorder occurs in the first month after trauma, whereas PTSD requires persistence beyond one month. Dissociation can include depersonalization, derealization, emotional numbing, or a sense of detachment from oneself or surroundings.'),
  ('2009-137', 'Lithium is handled by the kidney in ways that parallel sodium. Reduced effective renal perfusion or sodium retention can increase lithium reabsorption and serum concentration. A high-yield interaction group includes NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors or ARBs, and thiazide diuretics. Risk is dynamic because hydration, acute illness, and changes in renal function can alter lithium handling quickly, even in someone previously stable on treatment.'),
  ('2009-138', 'Sexual aversion disorder was a DSM-IV diagnosis and is not retained as a separate diagnosis in current DSM terminology. Historical questions distinguish desire, arousal, orgasm, and avoidance by locating the main difficulty within sexual experience. The useful contrast is broad loss of interest versus active avoidance of a specific sexual situation or partner context. Careful assessment also considers pain, coercion, relationship safety, trauma, medications, and medical contributors.'),
  ('2009-139', 'Acculturation refers to changes that can occur when people live across cultural contexts. A simple two-question map helps: Is connection to the heritage culture maintained, and is participation in the host culture developed? High on both is integration; low on both is marginalization. The concept is useful only when applied humbly, because identity, community acceptance, migration history, and structural conditions all shape a person’s experience.'),
  ('2009-140', 'Remission specifiers describe the course of a disorder rather than declaring it erased. DSM-IV divided remission by duration, early versus sustained, and by whether symptoms persisted, partial versus full. This is a classification exercise: six months lies after the one-month minimum and before the twelve-month sustained threshold, while the absence of criteria makes the remission full. Longitudinal monitoring remains clinically important despite remission status.'),
  ('2009-141', 'Childhood-onset schizophrenia refers to onset before adolescence and is distinct from imaginary play, developmental communication differences, or isolated unusual experiences. Early disruption occurs while social, academic, and cognitive skills are developing, contributing to substantial functional impact. A useful memory hook is that earlier onset generally carries greater developmental burden and a more persistent course. Assessment should remain broad because true childhood-onset schizophrenia is uncommon.'),
  ('2009-142', 'Executive functions include planning, inhibition, set shifting, working memory, and monitoring behavior toward a goal. They depend on frontal-subcortical networks, yet everyday executive success is also supported by learned routines and accumulated knowledge. A useful contrast is that aging commonly affects the speed of completing a novel task more than the ability to use a familiar plan. This distinction helps separate uncomplicated aging from a neurocognitive disorder when functional decline is reported.'),
  ('2009-143', 'Festination evokes hastening. In Parkinson disease, impaired movement scaling and postural control contribute to a short-stepped gait that may progressively accelerate. It belongs with other hypokinetic findings such as bradykinesia, rigidity, reduced arm swing, freezing, and difficulty turning. Contrast this with cerebellar or alcohol-related gait, which is usually broad-based and unsteady rather than small-stepped and increasingly fast. The gait pattern is clinically important because it increases fall risk.'),
  ('2009-144', 'Acute hepatic porphyrias can produce neurovisceral attacks involving abdominal pain, autonomic activation, psychiatric symptoms, neuropathy, weakness, and seizures without a surgical abdomen. Hormones, fasting, illness, and certain medications may precipitate attacks. The source’s all-credit response was invalidated because the listed antiseizure drugs are not safely interchangeable; adjudication corrected the key to gabapentin as the defensible listed choice. The durable teaching point is to recognize the syndrome and select emergency medications using porphyria-specific safety information rather than a universal rule.'),
  ('2009-145', 'Object constancy is the capacity to retain a stable, emotionally meaningful representation of another person when that person is absent, disappointing, or frustrating. Difficulty with this capacity can make ordinary ambiguity feel like rejection or betrayal. This is a conceptual psychodynamic model rather than a diagnostic test. It is a useful memory link for the enduring mistrust, hypersensitivity, and expectation of exploitation that characterize paranoid personality disorder.'),
  ('2009-146', 'Dystonia is a movement disorder of sustained or intermittent muscle contractions that cause patterned, twisting movements or postures. A geste antagoniste, or sensory trick, is a light touch or maneuver that temporarily reduces dystonia and is a classic sign of cervical dystonia. The effect is not simply mechanical restraint, making it a useful bedside clue. Neck pain commonly accompanies the abnormal posture and can lead to incorrect attribution to muscle strain alone.'),
  ('2009-147', 'Tricyclic antidepressants block monoamine reuptake but also have anticholinergic, antihistaminic, alpha-adrenergic, and cardiac sodium-channel effects. Their cardiac effects explain both ECG screening and the seriousness of overdose. For examination recall, connect tricyclic antidepressants with cardiac conduction and establish the electrical baseline before exposure. Monitoring beyond baseline should respond to individual clinical risk rather than replace history, examination, and medication-interaction review.'),
  ('2009-148', 'Operant-conditioning terms can be separated into two questions: was something added or removed, and did behavior increase or decrease? Positive means added, and negative means removed. Reinforcement increases behavior, whereas punishment decreases it. Thus, an aversive stimulus added after a response is positive punishment. Escape from an aversive stimulus can instead negatively reinforce the behavior that produced escape, even if the behavior is clinically undesirable.'),
  ('2009-149', 'The classic structure is duty, breach, causation, and damages. It organizes legal analysis but should not be confused with ethical practice: clinicians should address errors and near misses even when no lawsuit is viable. Damages is the legal term that captures the injury or loss needed to complete the claim. Jurisdictional law determines the details, including standards for causation, expert testimony, and recoverable losses.'),
  ('2009-150', 'Perinatal psychopharmacology balances medication exposure against the risks of untreated illness. Older examination questions commonly contrast haloperidol with mood stabilizers whose fetal risks are more prominent, especially valproate. Acute control of dangerous manic agitation often calls for an antipsychotic, whereas maintenance treatment requires a broader individualized discussion. Gestational timing, symptom severity, treatment history, and obstetric factors all affect the current clinical decision.'),
  ('2009-151', 'Pharmacokinetics describes what the body does to a drug, and hepatic metabolism is a major route of clearance for many antipsychotics. With aging, liver blood flow and metabolic reserve may decline, while body composition and receptor sensitivity also change. Start low and go slow summarizes the usual clinical approach, but it should be paired with purposeful monitoring and reassessment rather than automatic underdosing.'),
  ('2009-152', 'Neural tube defects result from incomplete early closure of the neural tube. Valproate is a high-yield teratogen because this risk is particularly important during early development and because the drug is used for seizure and mood disorders. The memory association is direct: valproate is linked to neural tube defects, especially with first-trimester exposure. Early counseling matters because fetal exposure may occur before pregnancy recognition.'),
  ('2009-153', 'Repeat-expansion disorders are easiest to remember when linked to inheritance. Huntington disease is autosomal dominant, Fragile X is X-linked, and Friedreich ataxia is autosomal recessive. In Friedreich ataxia, expanded GAA repeats reduce frataxin, a protein important to mitochondrial iron handling. The resulting multisystem disorder explains why progressive ataxia can coexist with peripheral neurologic, cardiac, and endocrine findings. This inheritance pattern contrasts with autosomal dominant Huntington disease.'),
  ('2009-154', 'Social learning highlights that people learn not only from what happens to them, but also from what they see happen to others. Modeling can transmit skills, expectations, and emotional responses. In fear reduction, seeing another person approach a situation safely supplies information that the situation may be manageable. Remember that direct repeated exposure is habituation, whereas learning by watching is modeling.'),
  ('2009-155', 'Diphenhydramine is widely available and commonly used as a nonprescription sleep aid, making its toxicity clinically important. Its antihistamine sedation may initially resemble a simple hypnotic overdose, but muscarinic blockade produces distinctive peripheral signs. A useful memory phrase is dry mouth, blurred eyes, quiet bowel, and full bladder. The presence of delirium or cardiovascular instability should raise concern for severe poisoning and prompt emergency assessment.'),
  ('2009-156', 'Serotonin is often taught as one contributor to behavioral inhibition and impulse regulation. That teaching point is broader than mood: serotonergic systems influence multiple circuits involved in restraint, affective reactivity, and response to provocation. The association with aggression is probabilistic, not a biomarker-based diagnosis. A useful memory phrase is that serotonin supports the brake on impulsive behavior. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2009-157', 'Social referencing is related to joint attention but is not identical to it. Joint attention means sharing attention toward an object or event. Social referencing adds a question about meaning or safety and seeks an emotional cue from another person. Remember it as the infant checking the caregiver’s face before deciding what a new event means. It is an important late-first-year social-cognitive milestone, not a stand-alone developmental diagnostic test.'),
  ('2009-158', 'Temperament describes early, relatively stable differences in emotional reactivity and self-regulation. Behavioral inhibition is the pattern in which novelty feels threatening: the child cautiously observes, withdraws, or shows physiologic arousal around unfamiliar situations. It is memorable because it connects a normal trait dimension with later anxiety risk while preserving the distinction between a risk marker and a psychiatric diagnosis. Functional impairment, not shyness alone, determines whether clinical intervention is needed.'),
  ('2009-159', 'Absence seizures are generalized nonmotor seizures that often appear as a brief pause in awareness without a meaningful aftermath. Focal impaired-awareness seizures arise from a focal network and may leave transient altered cognition after the visible event ends. This distinction reflects recovery of brain networks supporting orientation and attention, not simply event duration. For examination questions and clinical histories, separate ictal behavior from the postictal state; the recovery pattern is often the cleaner discriminator.'),
  ('2009-160', 'Hand localization becomes easier when the thenar and hypothenar eminences are paired with their nerves: median corresponds to thenar, and ulnar corresponds to hypothenar. The ulnar sensory territory includes the fifth digit and the medial half of the fourth digit. Because the ulnar nerve travels near exposed bony and tunnel-like locations around the elbow and wrist, chronic external compression can create both sensory and intrinsic-hand motor findings.'),
  ('2009-161', 'Apperception refers to organizing perception through prior experiences and internal meanings. The TAT uses this idea by presenting socially evocative but ambiguous scenes and inviting a narrative response. It belongs to the family of projective techniques, which elicit open-ended material rather than fixed-choice answers. A useful contrast is simple: inkblots suggest Rorschach, pictures plus stories suggest TAT, and a lengthy true-or-false questionnaire suggests the MMPI.'),
  ('2009-162', 'Many antidepressants enhance serotonin signaling, which can dampen sexual desire or orgasm for some patients. Bupropion differs by emphasizing norepinephrine and dopamine reuptake inhibition and lacking substantial serotonergic reuptake inhibition. This distinction makes it memorable in psychopharmacology questions: when antidepressant treatment and sexual adverse effects are both relevant, bupropion is often the contrasting option. Its usefulness still depends on the full clinical context, contraindications, and treatment goals.'),
  ('2009-163', 'In psychodynamic interviewing, patients communicate through tone, pauses, behavior, and omissions as well as explicit statements. Confrontation makes such material available for reflection by pointing out a discrepancy or avoided area. It is best understood as a descriptive bridge: the clinician identifies what the patient says and what the clinician also observes. This differentiates confrontation from interpretation, which goes further by proposing why the pattern exists.'),
  ('2009-164', 'Avoidant and schizoid personality disorders can both look quiet, isolated, and socially restricted from the outside. The memorable internal distinction is avoidant: “I want connection but fear it”; schizoid: “I prefer distance.” Personality diagnosis depends on a pervasive, stable pattern and associated impairment, not on introversion or a temporary period of social withdrawal. Asking about longing, distress, and anticipated rejection reveals the key difference.'),
  ('2009-165', 'Blinding is a design safeguard against expectancy effects. “Double” conventionally refers to participants and investigators or outcome assessors, although the exact roles should be specified in a study. It is often paired with random assignment and placebo control, but these are separate concepts: randomization addresses who starts in each group, while blinding addresses what people know afterward. A useful memory cue is that blinding protects the measurement process from treatment expectations.'),
  ('2009-166', 'Classical conditioning provides a compact model for acquired fear: an aversive event can teach the nervous system that associated cues predict danger. After a crash, roads, cars, or driving may become fear signals even when no current collision is occurring. Fear can also be learned socially or through verbal information, so distinguishing the route of acquisition matters. The durable memory link is simple: personally experienced danger indicates direct conditioning.'),
  ('2009-167', 'Resistance is not simply refusal; it is a functional concept describing what keeps painful material outside awareness or discussion. It can appear as lateness, intellectualization, forgetting, silence, humor, or changing the subject. In contrast, derailment is a language and thought-organization problem often seen in psychosis or mania. Resistance tends to emerge around threatening material, whereas derailment reflects loosened associations rather than a defensive maneuver.'),
  ('2009-168', 'ADHD is developmental, so its outward appearance changes as children mature and environments become more structured. Overt hyperactivity is easiest to see in young children; later it may look like fidgeting, impatience, excessive talking, or inner restlessness. Meanwhile, planning, sustained attention, and organization are tested more heavily in adolescence. A useful memory image is that the motor component often quiets first while life-management demands become harder.'),
  ('2009-169', 'Delirium is an acute disturbance of attention and awareness with fluctuating cognition, usually caused by an underlying medical, toxic, or medication-related condition. Its cognitive signs are signals of the syndrome, not isolated medication targets. Sleep-wake reversal is common and can amplify inattention and nighttime distress. This supports the exam distinction that symptom-targeted medication may occasionally be needed, but recovery depends on correcting contributors and supporting the patient’s environment.'),
  ('2009-170', 'Disability burden combines how often a condition occurs with how much it limits functioning over time. This differs from simple prevalence: a very common mild condition and a rarer highly impairing condition can contribute differently. Depression is a classic example of a disorder with broad effects on role functioning and recurrent course. For exam memory, think common, disabling, and often beginning during working-age years.'),
  ('2009-171', 'Before effective combination antiretroviral treatment, opportunistic infections and severe HIV-related neurologic illness dominated discussion of HIV complications. Longer survival shifted attention toward chronic conditions, including cardiometabolic and vascular disease. Antiretroviral therapy alone does not determine stroke risk; traditional risk factors and HIV-related inflammation also matter. The memorable contrast is less opportunistic infection and greater emphasis on long-term chronic disease management.'),
  ('2009-172', 'Nicotine reinforces tobacco use by activating nicotinic acetylcholine receptors and downstream reward circuitry. Bupropion is remembered as an antidepressant that also supports smoking cessation, but its relevance here is not solely antidepressant action. It combines catecholamine reuptake inhibition with antagonism at neuronal nicotinic receptors. A helpful memory pairing is nicotine dependence with nicotinic receptor antagonism, while remembering that bupropion has more than one pharmacologic action.'),
  ('2009-173', 'Late sensorimotor development includes the emergence of mental representation: children no longer need to act directly on an object to think about it. A word can stand for a ball, a caregiver, or an action; a block can stand for a car in pretend play. This capacity links early cognition and language. Symbols free meaning from the present moment, allowing children to communicate about absent objects and imagined events.'),
  ('2009-174', 'Maple syrup urine disease results from impaired breakdown of the branched-chain amino acids leucine, isoleucine, and valine. Feeding introduces these amino acids, allowing accumulation to become clinically apparent soon after birth in severe forms. The evocative name comes from the odor associated with metabolites, but the high-yield concept is timing: severe disease can present as early neonatal encephalopathy. This contrasts with disorders whose major untreated developmental effects appear later.'),
  ('2009-175', 'Confidentiality protects more than diagnoses and session content. The existence of a treatment relationship can itself be sensitive information, particularly in psychiatry. Public encounters create a common boundary challenge because ordinary social politeness can unintentionally reveal that relationship. The ethical response is usually deliberately neutral and preserves the patient’s ability to decide whether to acknowledge the connection. This is why clinicians often discuss public-contact preferences at the beginning of treatment.'),
  ('2009-176', 'OCD pharmacotherapy differs from treatment of many depressive and anxiety presentations because meaningful response may require higher SSRI doses and a longer adequate trial. This does not mean every patient should receive the maximum dose; tolerability, contraindications, interactions, and patient preferences guide titration. The exam sequence is to optimize the current SSRI, judge response, and then consider switching or augmentation. Exposure and response prevention remains a central nonpharmacologic treatment.'),
  ('2009-177', 'Panic symptoms are manifestations of autonomic arousal, not a diagnosis by themselves. Acute medical disorders can produce fear, tachycardia, tremulousness, sweating, and dyspnea through the same physiologic systems involved in panic. A useful memory cue is that “air hunger plus alarm” may describe either panic or a cardiopulmonary emergency. Emergency psychiatric assessment therefore begins with medical safety and syndrome recognition before a primary psychiatric diagnosis is assigned.'),
  ('2009-178', 'Velocardiofacial syndrome is within the clinical spectrum of 22q11.2 deletion syndrome. The high-yield association is “22q11.2 deletion and schizophrenia risk,” particularly when psychiatric symptoms coexist with congenital cardiac, palatal, or developmental findings. This association illustrates how a copy-number genetic difference can affect neurodevelopment and increase later vulnerability to major psychiatric illness without determining an inevitable outcome for an individual patient.'),
  ('2009-179', 'Wraparound is a useful image: services are wrapped around the child and family rather than requiring the family to navigate disconnected systems alone. It emphasizes strengths, family voice, individualized goals, and collaboration across agencies. The least restrictive setting principle provides the memory link: effective coordinated support can make it more possible for a child to remain at home and in community settings.'),
  ('2009-180', 'Dependence becomes clinically significant when the need for support is pervasive, rigid, and undermines autonomous functioning. Planned separations in psychotherapy, such as vacations, can make attachment patterns visible. A helpful contrast is that dependent dynamics center on “I cannot manage without you,” whereas borderline abandonment reactions more often involve unstable affect, anger, and urgent attempts to prevent separation. The distinction depends on the broader longitudinal pattern rather than one reaction.'),
  ('2009-181', 'Methadone is vulnerable to clinically important drug interactions. A helpful board pattern is that inducers lower levels and inhibitors raise levels, although real-world metabolism is more complex than a single-enzyme mnemonic. Carbamazepine is a classic enzyme-inducing antiseizure medication. Because induction requires increased enzyme activity, its impact typically emerges over time rather than as an immediate one-dose effect. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2009-182', 'Children often communicate internal distress through behavior, affect, and physical complaints rather than adult-like descriptions of sadness. An irritable mood can substitute for depressed mood in youth when other features of a depressive syndrome are present. The memory link is that pediatric depression may look “mad” rather than tearful. Diagnosis still requires a broader pattern of symptoms and meaningful impairment, not irritability in isolation.'),
  ('2009-183', 'NMS is conceptualized as a severe reduction in central dopaminergic activity, explaining its relationship to antipsychotics and its parkinsonian motor findings. The contrast with serotonin syndrome is useful: NMS is classically associated with marked rigidity and dopamine blockade, whereas serotonin toxicity follows serotonergic exposure and often includes hyperreflexia or clonus. Both syndromes require urgent medical recognition, discontinuation of causative agents, and supportive management.'),
  ('2009-184', 'Cerebellar circuits fine-tune the timing and amplitude of movement. When that correction system is impaired, error grows as a limb approaches its intended endpoint, producing terminal oscillation and overshoot. The key memory pair is cerebellum equals intention plus incoordination. A tremor’s context—rest, posture, action, or target approach—is often more diagnostically useful than the word tremor alone. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2009-185', 'Early childhood self-control develops alongside language. As children gain words for wants and feelings, they acquire alternatives to physical action during conflict. A practical developmental cue is that fewer usable words can mean more action-based expression. This association does not mean all preschool aggression is benign. Frequency, severity, setting, developmental expectations, and risk to self or others determine whether further assessment and intervention are necessary.'),
  ('2009-186', 'The WCST requires the examinee to infer an unstated sorting rule from feedback and then adapt when that rule changes. Its structure makes it a classic measure of cognitive flexibility. Perseveration means persisting with a response pattern beyond its usefulness. The memory cue is simple: changing cards require a changing rule, and difficulty shifting produces perseverative errors. Performance can be influenced by many clinical and demographic factors, so interpretation is not purely localizing.'),
  ('2009-187', 'Transient global amnesia causes sudden anterograde amnesia with repetitive questioning and preserved consciousness. Patients may retain remote autobiographical knowledge and normal social behavior while being unable to hold new information. The dramatic presentation can alarm families, but the conceptual anchor is isolated temporary failure of memory encoding after dangerous neurologic and medical alternatives have been excluded. Atypical features, persistent deficits, or altered consciousness require reconsideration of the diagnosis.'),
  ('2009-188', 'Classical conditioning explains why places, people, money, or routines can evoke craving after drug clearance and prolonged abstinence. A cue acquires predictive value through repeated pairing with substance use. Extinction is not erasure: conditioned responses can reappear under stress or after renewed exposure. Nevertheless, repeated exposure to the cue without reinforcement usually reduces the intensity of the learned craving response over time. This principle supports anticipatory relapse-prevention planning.'),
  ('2009-189', 'Chronic alcohol use can impair intake, absorption, storage, and use of essential nutrients. Thiamine deficiency is especially important because the brain depends on thiamine-dependent energy metabolism. A useful memory sequence is alcohol misuse plus malnutrition leading to Wernicke-Korsakoff-spectrum illness. Preventing additional deficiency is therefore a core aspect of care. Nutrition should be addressed as a medical priority, not merely as lifestyle counseling.'),
  ('2009-190', 'Medication adherence is often shaped by common, immediate adverse effects rather than rare severe toxicities. For valproate, gastrointestinal symptoms are especially salient because they can emerge before patients experience meaningful benefit. This is a useful prescribing reminder: discuss expected adverse effects before initiation, provide a plan for reporting problems, and distinguish uncomplicated intolerance from symptoms that may require urgent medical evaluation.'),
  ('2009-191', 'Retentive encopresis refers to fecal soiling associated with constipation and stool retention. Painful defecation can start a withholding cycle: withholding produces harder stool, making later passage more painful and increasing the risk of impaction and overflow. The memorable distinction is overflow rather than defiance. Once the mechanism is understood, education, disimpaction when needed, and maintenance of soft regular stools follow logically.'),
  ('2009-192', 'PMDD is distinguished from nonspecific premenstrual symptoms by cyclical timing, symptom severity, remission after menses begins, and meaningful impairment. Serotonergic treatment can improve symptoms despite the disorder’s relationship to menstrual-cycle physiology, which is why SSRIs have a central role. The memory cue is that luteal-phase dysphoria responds to serotonin reuptake inhibition. Careful history helps distinguish PMDD from continuous symptoms with premenstrual worsening.'),
  ('2009-193', 'Tobacco epidemiology changes with public-health policy, social norms, product availability, and cohort effects. This question tests a broad historical age pattern rather than a current surveillance estimate. A durable conceptual link is that experimentation often begins earlier, but regular use and population prevalence may be most prominent in young adulthood, when independence, work, and peer environments change. Current smoking and vaping estimates require contemporary data.'),
  ('2009-194', 'DBT is built around a dialectic: two seemingly opposing truths can both matter. Patients need acceptance and validation, while they also need help changing behaviors that endanger them or impair their lives. This avoids a false choice between purely supportive therapy and confrontational demands for change. The core memory phrase is “acceptance plus change,” expressed through a supportive therapeutic relationship that still expects active participation.'),
  ('2009-195', 'Statistical power is the complement of the type II error rate: power equals 1 minus beta. It is easiest to remember as a study’s ability to detect a real signal when one is present. Power rises with a larger sample, a larger true effect, less measurement noise, and, at the cost of more false positives, a less stringent significance threshold. This is why underpowered studies may produce wide uncertainty or negative findings that do not reliably exclude clinically meaningful effects.'),
  ('2009-196', 'Periventricular white matter contains long fiber tracts and connections linking frontal cortex with basal ganglia and thalamus. Injury there can produce slowed cognition, impaired initiation, gait or motor abnormalities, and affective symptoms without a dramatic cortical syndrome. This is a useful contrast: cortical lesions often create deficits such as aphasia, neglect, or visual-field loss, whereas disruption of frontal-subcortical circuits more often presents with slowing, apathy, and impaired executive function.'),
  ('2009-197', 'Dopamine D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway produces extrapyramidal symptoms. Parkinsonism is a hypokinetic syndrome of slowness and rigidity, whereas akathisia is subjective and observable restlessness, acute dystonia is sustained abnormal muscle contraction, and tardive dyskinesia is typically a delayed involuntary hyperkinetic movement disorder. Amantadine supports dopamine-related motor function and also modulates glutamatergic signaling, fitting a parkinsonian rather than hyperkinetic adverse-effect pattern.'),
  ('2009-198', 'The older term hypochondriasis historically grouped excessive illness concern and symptom-focused distress. Contemporary nosology separates illness anxiety disorder, in which health anxiety predominates despite absent or mild symptoms, from somatic symptom disorder, in which burdensome symptoms are more central. For examinations, remember the contrast succinctly: illness anxiety centers on “What disease do I have?” whereas somatic symptom disorder centers more on distress and disruption from symptoms.'),
  ('2009-199', 'Serotonin receptors are a diverse family. Most are G-protein-coupled receptors, but 5-HT3 is an ion channel. The 5-HT2A receptor is expressed prominently in cortical networks involved in perception and cognition, making it a useful mechanistic anchor for classic psychedelic effects. The abbreviation can be read as “5-hydroxytryptamine type 2”; on examinations, “5-HT2” generally points to the clinically important 5-HT2A mechanism for LSD and mescaline.'),
  ('2009-200', 'The motor and sensory homunculi place face and upper-limb representations on the lateral cerebral surface, supplied mainly by the middle cerebral artery; leg representation lies more medially and is supplied largely by the anterior cerebral artery. This anatomic map makes the “face and arm more than leg” pattern memorable. The frontal eye fields drive gaze contralaterally, so a destructive hemispheric lesion leaves unopposed gaze toward the damaged hemisphere.'),
  ('2009-201', 'Motivational interviewing is a collaborative conversation style for resolving ambivalence about behavior change. Its central stance of partnership, acceptance, compassion, and evocation fits situations in which pressure or persuasion may increase resistance. In OCD, compulsions persist because they provide short-term relief, even while worsening long-term restriction. That tension provides a natural opening to explore the patient’s own reasons for pursuing treatment before asking for exposure-based behavioral change.'),
  ('2009-202', 'Formal thought disorder describes abnormalities in the organization and expression of thinking as inferred from speech. Clang associations are memorable because the word “clang” evokes the audible quality that drives the association. They differ from word salad, in which speech becomes markedly incoherent, and from flight of ideas, in which rapid transitions may still have understandable conceptual links. Precise descriptive terminology supports clearer communication across clinicians and over time.'),
  ('2009-203', 'Splitting is a defensive relational pattern in which self or others may be experienced in sharply polarized all-good or all-bad terms. It can emerge not only within one treatment relationship but across teams, consultations, and transfers of care. The phrase special patient is a useful countertransference warning: it may signal a pull to rescue, overidentify, or compete. Consistent boundaries and respectful clinician communication reduce opportunities for enactment.'),
  ('2009-204', 'Child-welfare systems distinguish between allegations of abuse and neglect, although real cases may involve both. Neglect is often chronic and intertwined with limited resources, substance use, mental illness, domestic violence, or caregiver impairment; these associated factors are not interchangeable with neglect itself. The board-relevant distinction is that termination concerns sustained inability or failure to meet a child’s essential needs under applicable law, not simply the presence of a parental psychiatric diagnosis.'),
  ('2009-205', 'Psychodynamic psychotherapy often seeks to increase reflective functioning: the capacity to observe one’s feelings, motives, and relational expectations rather than acting them out immediately. Dramatic affect can communicate genuine distress, interpersonal wishes, and defensive processes simultaneously. A therapist’s early response is usually most useful when it is curious, empathic, and grounded in what is observable. More interpretive interventions are timed according to the patient’s capacity to use them and the strength of the therapeutic alliance.'),
  ('2009-206', 'The femoral nerve arises from the lumbar plexus, chiefly L2-L4, and innervates the quadriceps, the major extensor group of the knee. The patellar reflex also depends on this pathway, which creates a useful clinical pairing: femoral nerve dysfunction may produce both weak knee extension and a reduced knee jerk. In contrast, the obturator nerve is remembered through adduction, and the common peroneal nerve through dorsiflexion and foot drop.'),
  ('2009-207', 'Physician substance use disorders are a professional-health issue rather than a moral failing. Risk estimates differ across surveys, eras, specialty groupings, and definitions of use versus disorder, so board-style comparisons should be read as population-level associations. Emergency medicine’s work structure often includes shift work, disrupted sleep, and intense acute-care exposure, which provides a conceptual link to the keyed answer. The practical lesson is early recognition, structured support, and patient-safety systems for clinicians in every field.'),
  ('2009-208', 'Play becomes increasingly social as children develop language, self-regulation, and the ability to understand another child’s intentions. A useful sequence is solitary play, parallel play, associative play, and cooperative play, although real children may move between these forms. Parallel play can appear detached because children may use similar toys close together without directly sharing an activity. It lets toddlers observe peers and tolerate a social setting before they can reliably negotiate turn-taking, rules, roles, or a common plan. The memory cue is that parallel activities run beside one another rather than together.'),
  ('2009-209', 'Vitamin B12 is needed for normal DNA synthesis and for pathways important to myelin integrity. Deficiency can therefore produce macrocytic anemia alongside neurologic and psychiatric manifestations, including cognitive or affective change. The board-style memory link is “macrocytosis plus neuropsychiatric symptoms: think B12.” Folate deficiency also produces macrocytosis, but B12 carries particular importance because neurologic injury may develop even when hematologic findings are subtle.'),
  ('2009-210', 'HSV encephalitis is memorable for its predilection for temporal and limbic brain regions. These networks support memory, emotion, behavior, and olfaction, so patients may develop personality change, amnesia, unusual sensory experiences, focal seizures, fever, and impaired consciousness. This neuroanatomic pattern helps distinguish encephalitis from a diffuse toxic-metabolic delirium, although either condition can cause agitation and confusion. Olfactory phenomena are particularly useful clues because they may arise from temporal-lobe seizure activity. Acute onset with fever and altered mental status makes an infectious or inflammatory central nervous system disorder especially concerning.'),
  ('2009-211', 'Alzheimer disease pathology includes extracellular beta-amyloid plaques and intracellular tau neurofibrillary tangles. APP is the precursor molecule from which beta-amyloid is generated, making the gene-to-pathology connection intuitive. The key distinction is between a protein involved in characteristic pathology and a mutation classically associated with inherited early-onset disease. APP mutations provide that direct connection, whereas tau is important pathologically but points more strongly toward certain frontotemporal degeneration syndromes when mutated.'),
  ('2009-212', 'ADHD medications are commonly divided into stimulants and nonstimulants. Stimulants often have a faster onset and stronger average effect on core symptoms, but misuse and diversion potential can change the risk-benefit assessment. Atomoxetine works through norepinephrine signaling and generally has a slower clinical onset. Co-occurring substance use may also complicate attention symptoms, adherence, sleep, mood, and safety. The useful test-taking distinction is not that stimulants are universally prohibited in substance use disorders, but that concern about misuse or diversion makes a nonstimulant option particularly attractive.'),
  ('2009-213', 'Therapeutic drug monitoring is most useful when blood concentration has a reasonably interpretable relationship to efficacy or toxicity and when pharmacokinetic variation is clinically meaningful. Among antidepressants, selected tricyclics have historically been used this way. Desipramine is a relatively noradrenergic TCA and a classic examination example. The broader principle is not to chase a number: obtain and interpret a level only when it can answer a concrete clinical question.'),
  ('2009-214', 'Children may express depression through persistent irritability, anger, withdrawal, or deteriorating school performance rather than explicitly describing sadness. Psychotic features in a depressive episode may have themes of guilt, worthlessness, punishment, or deserved badness. Even so, a formal major depressive episode requires an adequate symptom cluster, duration, impairment, and exclusion of other explanations. The independent adjudication therefore preserves B as the historical best-listed response while emphasizing that the short stem alone does not document every current diagnostic criterion.'),
  ('2009-215', 'Clozapine is uniquely effective for many patients with treatment-resistant schizophrenia, but its use requires deliberate safety systems. Its major hazards have different safeguards: blood dyscrasia is addressed with ANC surveillance, whereas concentration-related adverse effects such as seizures are reduced by cautious dosing. Dose, titration speed, interacting drugs, and individual vulnerability all affect seizure risk. A medication-specific monitoring plan should therefore distinguish the purpose of hematologic surveillance from the reason for gradual dose escalation.'),
  ('2009-216', 'Descriptions of thought process focus on the organization of ideas, not whether an idea is true. The memorable contrast is that circumstantial thought circles back, whereas tangential thought takes off. Circumstantiality can occur in several clinical settings and is not by itself diagnostic. On a mental status examination, it is best recorded as a formal thought-process observation alongside rate, coherence, associations, and the patient’s ability to engage with questions.'),
  ('2009-217', 'Fitness to drive is a functional question rather than a diagnosis-based automatic conclusion. Dementia can affect multiple abilities needed for driving, and insight into those deficits may be limited. In the United States, reporting rules are state-specific, so this question appropriately emphasizes checking local law rather than applying a single national rule. The situation illustrates the clinical task of balancing confidentiality, autonomy, and protection from foreseeable harm.'),
  ('2009-218', 'Alcohol is a central nervous system depressant. Clinical severity is shaped by tolerance, rate of intake, co-ingestants, medical illness, and BAC, so a numeric level should never replace bedside assessment. The practical emergency sequence prioritizes airway, breathing, and circulation. Withdrawal syndromes such as seizures and delirium tremens arise in a different physiologic state: declining alcohol exposure after dependence, rather than the peak of intoxication.'),
  ('2009-219', 'Positive and negative symptoms in schizophrenia describe direction of change, not whether a symptom is good or bad. Positive symptoms add unusual perceptions or beliefs, whereas negative symptoms diminish ordinary capacities. Affect is the observable emotional display, while mood is the patient’s sustained subjective emotional state. This distinction explains why a patient may report feeling upset while appearing relatively unexpressive, or may look flat without necessarily having a depressive episode.'),
  ('2009-220', 'Skeletal muscle contraction depends on precisely timed ion movement across the cell membrane. Voltage-gated sodium channels initiate and propagate the action potential, so altered channel gating can yield episodic weakness, stiffness, or both. A useful association is sodium channelopathy with hyperkalemic periodic paralysis and paramyotonia, versus chloride channelopathy with myotonia congenita. These are disorders of excitable tissue, so symptoms may be strikingly trigger-dependent rather than continuously present.'),
  ('2009-221', 'Sleep-related breathing disorders fragment sleep and can cause intermittent oxygenation changes even when a person believes they slept for an adequate duration. The resulting daytime presentation may resemble depression, inattention, or burnout. Passive and monotonous contexts often expose pathologic sleepiness because stimulation is insufficient to sustain alertness. Psychiatric assessment benefits from asking about sleep quality, snoring, witnessed breathing pauses, and unintentional dozing when fatigue or cognitive symptoms are prominent.'),
  ('2009-222', 'The suprascapular nerve is a compact anatomic memory pair: supraspinatus starts abduction and infraspinatus externally rotates. Both muscles sit on the posterior scapula and share this nerve supply. Shoulder abduction is phased: supraspinatus initiates it, while deltoid becomes the major abductor thereafter. This phased movement is why the wording “initial abduction” is especially localizing in an examination question. That distinction also separates it from axillary neuropathy.'),
  ('2009-223', 'Play is a child’s natural medium for communication and experimentation. In supportive play therapy, the clinician often uses descriptive, affect-focused language to help the child recognize and organize feelings. The therapist’s task is not to solve the story quickly or force an adult explanation onto it. This resembles reflective listening in verbal psychotherapy: accurately name the emotional experience while leaving room for the patient to define its meaning.'),
  ('2009-224', 'Forensic psychiatry separates several questions that are commonly conflated. Competence asks whether a defendant can proceed now, whereas criminal responsibility asks about mental state when the alleged offense occurred. The practical memory aid is that competence is present-tense capacity. Legal standards vary by jurisdiction, but the familiar formulation emphasizes rational and factual understanding of proceedings plus the ability to consult with counsel.'),
  ('2009-225', 'The prefix copro- refers to obscenity in this terminology, and praxis refers to action. Copropraxia therefore means obscene action, whereas coprolalia means obscene speech. These highly visible symptoms occur in only a subset of people with tic disorders and are not required for a tic-disorder diagnosis. Their clinical importance often lies in stigma, embarrassment, misunderstanding, and social impairment. Distinguishing an involuntary tic from deliberate behavior requires attention to its repetitive quality, intrusiveness, context independence, premonitory sensations, and the person’s effort to suppress or resist it.'),
  ('2009-226', 'Classical insanity tests differ mainly in the type of impairment they emphasize. M’Naughten centers on knowledge, irresistible impulse centers on behavioral control, and the Model Penal Code combines cognitive and volitional elements. A memory hook is that M’Naughten concerns the mind knowing. Modern jurisdictions use varying statutes and definitions, so a forensic clinician must apply the governing local standard rather than a generic psychiatric diagnosis.'),
  ('2009-227', 'Medical ethics is often summarized by four principles: autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, and justice. Beneficence asks clinicians to promote a person’s welfare, while nonmaleficence asks them to avoid inflicting harm. The principles can overlap in real decisions, but exam questions often distinguish them by whether the central problem is an actively imposed injury or a failure to provide benefit. Confidentiality protects personal information, and justice concerns fair treatment and allocation of burdens and benefits. “First, do no harm” is the conventional memory cue for nonmaleficence.'),
  ('2009-228', 'Buprenorphine’s sublingual route is a pharmacokinetic solution: absorption through oral mucosa bypasses much of gastrointestinal first-pass metabolism. Its partial agonism is clinically important but should not be mistaken for absence of overdose risk. The route is also a useful examination association: methadone is generally swallowed, whereas common buprenorphine formulations for opioid use disorder are sublingual or buccal. Appropriate induction timing reduces the risk of precipitated withdrawal.'),
  ('2009-229', 'Older terminology classified intellectual disability heavily by IQ and described expected mental age or school-grade attainment. Contemporary frameworks give greater weight to adaptive functioning because it better reflects support needs in daily life. For historical examination items, retain the traditional association while recognizing its limitations. Schooling, environment, strengths, and accommodations shape learning trajectories far more usefully than a rigid predicted grade level.'),
  ('2009-230', 'Piaget portrayed children as active learners who build and revise mental models through interaction with the world. His developmental stages are best remembered as changes in how thinking is organized, including increasing ability to use symbols, conserve quantity, and reason abstractly. The classic interview method asks a child to explain an answer, making the path of reasoning visible. This process orientation distinguishes Piaget from approaches focused chiefly on learned content or behavior frequency.'),
  ('2009-231', 'Psychodynamic psychotherapy treats the therapeutic relationship as a source of information about recurring interpersonal patterns. Transference refers to the patient’s expectations, feelings, and relational templates as they emerge with the clinician. Boundary tests may involve gifts, favors, business opportunities, special contact, or requests for exceptions. The frame of therapy, including roles, time, payment, and limits, protects both patient and treatment and makes these patterns safer to examine.'),
  ('2009-232', 'The angular gyrus is association cortex near the junction of parietal, temporal, and occipital processing systems. Its syndrome is memorable because body-part labeling, laterality, calculation, and writing all require integration of symbolic information. Gerstmann syndrome need not be complete in every patient, but the pattern is more informative than any isolated deficit. In most right-handed people and many left-handed people, language dominance is left-sided, so the relevant lesion is usually in the left angular gyrus.'),
  ('2009-233', 'Nicotine is the reinforcing drug in tobacco products, whereas cotinine is its major, longer-lived metabolite. Cotinine is commonly used as a biomarker of recent nicotine exposure. CYP enzymes are clinically relevant because metabolic differences can change drug concentrations, adverse effects, and patterns of use. For this item, the key association is CYP2A6 with nicotine metabolism, making tobacco the relevant addictive substance.'),
  ('2009-234', 'MS includes episodic inflammatory demyelination and longer-term neurodegenerative consequences. Examination questions commonly separate relapse treatment from prevention of future disease activity. Steroids are the short-course intervention for an acute flare. Interferons and glatiramer acetate belong to the maintenance framework, while symptomatic medications address consequences of lesions rather than the acute inflammatory mechanism. This time-frame distinction makes the treatment choices easier to separate.'),
  ('2009-235', 'Children’s reports of unusual perceptions must be interpreted developmentally. Fantasy play and imperfect distinctions between thoughts, dreams, and external events are common early in life. The important principle is not that hallucinations are unimportant, but that their diagnostic meaning changes with age and context. Persistent psychosis accompanied by decline, disorganization, negative symptoms, or loss of reality testing is much more concerning than an isolated anxiety-linked experience.'),
  ('2009-236', 'Tourette syndrome involves motor and vocal tics, typically beginning in childhood and waxing and waning over time. Treatment is not required simply because tics are present; impairment and patient preference matter. The classic medication contrast is between alpha-2 agonists, selected for tolerability, and dopamine antagonists, which may be more potent but carry a greater adverse-effect burden. Behavioral treatment is an important contemporary component of care.'),
  ('2009-237', 'Opioid withdrawal reflects rebound noradrenergic activation after opioid effects are removed. Alpha-2 agonists reduce norepinephrine release, explaining clonidine’s benefit for the adrenergic portion of withdrawal and its hypotension risk. A useful contrast is that benzodiazepines are withdrawal-specific treatments for alcohol and sedative-hypnotics, whereas opioid withdrawal uses different medication strategies. Withdrawal management and long-term treatment for opioid use disorder should be considered separately.'),
  ('2009-238', 'Cerebrospinal fluid circulates through the ventricular system and subarachnoid spaces before reabsorption. Subarachnoid blood can disrupt this circulation abruptly, making hydrocephalus an important early complication of hemorrhage. The abducens nerve is vulnerable to traction when intracranial pressure rises, so bilateral sixth-nerve palsy is a memorable clue to elevated pressure rather than a precise brainstem localization. Prompt recognition matters because the condition can deteriorate rapidly.'),
  ('2009-239', 'Postpartum psychosis is uncommon but is a psychiatric emergency because symptoms may emerge rapidly and include insomnia, confusion, mood disturbance, delusions, or hallucinations. Intrusive harm thoughts may also occur in anxiety or obsessive-compulsive presentations and are often ego-dystonic. The key differentiators are reality testing, intent, control, insight, and ability to maintain safety, rather than the mere presence of a disturbing thought.'),
  ('2009-240', 'Psychometrics separates consistency from accuracy. A scale that always reads five kilograms too high is reliable because it is consistent, but it is not valid for true weight. This distinction applies to psychiatric rating scales, diagnostic interviews, and research measures. Reliability is usually necessary for validity because an unstable measure cannot represent a construct consistently, but reliability alone is never enough to establish that the intended construct was measured.'),
  ('2009-241', 'Interpersonal theory provides a social and relational account of psychiatric symptoms. Its central memory point is that the self is not treated as an isolated entity: anxiety, security, and identity are shaped within relationships. This emphasis made the therapeutic relationship an important source of clinical information rather than merely a neutral setting for observation. The theory remains influential in formulations that focus on current relationships and social roles.'),
  ('2009-242', 'The terms primary and secondary gain come from psychodynamic accounts of conversion symptoms. Primary points inward: the symptom reduces anxiety tied to conflict. Secondary points outward: the symptom may bring attention, exemption, or other environmental consequences. Neither concept proves intentionality. Conversion symptoms are experienced as involuntary, whereas malingering involves conscious symptom production for an external incentive. The distinction is useful in examinations but should not replace careful medical assessment.'),
  ('2009-243', 'Catatonia is a syndrome of abnormal motor behavior, speech, and responsiveness, not a synonym for schizophrenia. Historical classifications treated it as a schizophrenia subtype, whereas contemporary practice recognizes it across mood disorders, psychotic disorders, medical illness, and substance-related states. Echolalia is automatic repetition of another person’s words; echopraxia is repetition of another person’s movements. Recognizing the syndrome is more clinically useful than relying on the historical subtype label.'),
  ('2009-244', 'Violence questions often distinguish a common population-level correlate from a deterministic claim about an individual. Substance use can affect inhibition, judgment, and conflict exposure, yet it cannot determine who will be violent. The most informative clinical risk factor is often a history of prior violence combined with dynamic factors and a specific threat context. Risk assessment should therefore be structured, individualized, and focused on actionable safety needs.'),
  ('2009-245', 'Violence-risk management intersects clinical care, confidentiality, civil commitment, criminal law, and duties to protect identifiable people. A serious threat may require public-safety action even when the vignette does not establish a qualifying psychiatric disorder. The historical key selects law-enforcement referral, but the independent adjudication limits that answer to the item’s framing. Actual custody, emergency-hold authority, warning recipients, permissible disclosures, and protective steps are jurisdiction- and fact-dependent. Careful assessment, consultation, communication, and documentation are the durable principles.'),
  ('2009-246', 'Neurotransmitter questions about aging are generally comparative. Dopamine and acetylcholine are memorable because their changes are linked to motor and cognitive syndromes, respectively. Serotonin’s relative stability in this traditional teaching contrast prevents an overly simple assumption that all neurotransmitters decline equally. Brain aging is heterogeneous across regions, receptors, transporters, and individuals, so this statement should be understood as a broad examination-level summary.'),
  ('2009-247', 'Suicide risk is dynamic rather than a single yes-or-no fact. Useful documentation captures a time-specific formulation: what raises risk now, what mitigates it, what uncertainty remains, what interventions were selected, and why the disposition is reasonable. A no-self-harm contract can create false reassurance because it neither assesses nor changes the drivers of risk. Documentation is not a substitute for clinical care and cannot eliminate legal exposure. The key learning point is to document the individualized formulation, decision-making process, and plan rather than merely a patient promise or denial.'),
  ('2009-248', 'Huntington disease produces irregular, flowing involuntary movements because basal-ganglia circuits that normally regulate movement selection are progressively impaired. A useful pharmacology association is that reducing dopamine signaling can reduce hyperkinetic movements, whereas increasing dopamine signaling may aggravate them. Haloperidol is a classic examination answer, although treatment decisions require balancing chorea reduction against adverse effects and the patient’s psychiatric presentation. The same medication may be more attractive when psychosis or dangerous agitation is also present.'),
  ('2009-249', 'Restless legs syndrome is identified by its pattern more than by the patient’s descriptive language. Symptoms emerge or worsen during inactivity, are usually strongest in the evening or at night, and improve with movement. This differentiates the syndrome from many causes of leg discomfort. Dopamine agonists historically supported a role for dopamine in symptom control, but long-term augmentation changed modern prescribing preferences. The examination memory pair is simple: rest plus evening worsening, relieved by movement.'),
  ('2009-250', 'The circle of Willis is best understood as a collateral arterial ring rather than a list of isolated vessels. The anterior communicating artery forms the short bridge across the front of the ring by joining the paired anterior cerebral arteries. Hemodynamic stress at arterial branch points contributes to saccular aneurysm formation. Visualizing this anterior cross-bridge distinguishes an anterior communicating lesion from aneurysms arising in posterior circulation near the basilar, posterior cerebral, or posterior inferior cerebellar arteries.')
insert into question_context (question_id, context) values
  ('2009-251', 'Sleep disorders often present through daytime symptoms that resemble depression, anxiety, attentional problems, or cognitive decline. Polysomnography records sleep stages, breathing, oxygenation, and physiologic events over a night. It is especially useful when obstructive sleep apnea is suspected from obesity, snoring, witnessed pauses, excessive sleepiness, or unrefreshing sleep. The practical memory cue is obesity plus hypersomnia and nonrestorative sleep: ask about sleep-disordered breathing before attributing fatigue entirely to a mood disorder.'),
  ('2009-252', 'Post-infarction depression matters in consultation-liaison psychiatry because mood, motivation, sleep, adherence, social connection, and rehabilitation can influence recovery. Depression supplies psychiatric prognostic information, while ejection fraction directly measures ventricular pump function. The historical examination answer is depression as the intended psychiatric factor, but the independent adjudication rejects an unrestricted claim that it is always the strongest mortality predictor. Clinical care should assess and treat depression alongside, rather than above, standard cardiovascular risk evaluation.'),
  ('2009-253', 'Guillain-Barré syndrome is an immune-mediated disorder of peripheral nerves that commonly produces an ascending weakness-and-areflexia syndrome. Demyelination disrupts saltatory conduction, explaining slow conduction velocity, prolonged latencies, and conduction block on nerve studies. The contrast with central motor disorders is useful: peripheral nerve dysfunction commonly reduces reflexes, whereas many central motor pathway lesions increase them. Weakness may progress rapidly and can affect respiratory, bulbar, and autonomic function. The pattern therefore requires urgent medical evaluation even when anxiety or distress is also present.'),
  ('2009-254', 'Projective identification is often taught as a relational extension of projection. It may help explain intense clinical interactions in which a clinician begins to feel a powerful state that appears organized by the patient’s interpersonal behavior. The key distinction is that projection says, “you have it,” whereas projective identification adds an interpersonal pull toward feeling or acting it. This concept is used as a psychodynamic formulation, not as proof that a patient intentionally causes another person’s emotional response. Supervision and reflective practice can help clinicians assess such experiences without losing therapeutic boundaries.'),
  ('2009-255', 'Stress research distinguishes exposure from vulnerability. Exposure asks whether a group encounters more adverse events. Vulnerability asks whether a similar event has a larger effect because security, coping resources, control, or social support are limited. This distinction matters in social psychiatry because socioeconomic conditions can shape the pathway from a stressor to symptoms and impairment. A useful memory phrase is “more load and fewer shock absorbers,” while recognizing that both exposure and vulnerability can contribute.'),
  ('2009-256', 'Psychiatric phenomenology often organizes beliefs along a continuum of conviction and insight. An overvalued idea lies between an ordinary strongly held opinion and a delusion: it may dominate behavior and cause major impairment, but it remains somewhat open to revision. Cultural context is essential because an unusual belief is not necessarily pathological when it is shared and accepted within a person’s culture or subculture. The enduring cue is “overvalued, not unshakable.”'),
  ('2009-257', 'MAOIs inhibit metabolism of monoamines and require unusually careful interaction screening. Their historical importance in psychopharmacology is matched by potential for severe reactions with certain serotonergic drugs and sympathomimetic exposures. Meperidine is the classic board-examination opioid to avoid with an MAOI. The memory pairing is direct: phenelzine plus meperidine can produce hyperthermic autonomic toxicity. This is an avoidable interaction, not a reason to withhold necessary emergency evaluation or stabilization.'),
  ('2009-258', 'Attunement is easiest to remember as getting in tune with another person’s internal state. In infancy, it often occurs through rhythm, facial expression, voice, pacing, and arousal level rather than through language. A caregiver’s response is usually similar but not identical to the infant’s behavior; that transformation turns a raw action or feeling into a shared interpersonal experience. Attunement differs from simple imitation because the caregiver reflects the infant’s underlying affective state in a responsive way. Such exchanges support early co-regulation, communication, and the child’s experience of being understood.'),
  ('2009-259', 'Kohut’s self psychology shifted attention from intrapsychic conflict alone toward the development and maintenance of a cohesive self. Selfobjects are people or experiences that support functions such as feeling valued, calm, admired, or connected. Empathy is therefore more than warmth; it is a clinical method for entering the patient’s subjective world and identifying the self function under strain. The board-style association is concise: Kohut is linked to empathy.'),
  ('2009-260', 'IPT is organized around the idea that mood symptoms and current relationships influence one another. Its problem areas provide a practical map for treatment rather than a broad exploration of personality: grief, role disputes, role transitions, and interpersonal deficits. Sessions identify current relational patterns, clarify communication, mobilize support, and encourage effective interpersonal action. The memory cue is “interpersonal problem solving in the present.” Although IPT can include emotional exploration, its defining focus is not cognitive restructuring, relaxation drills, or interpretation of unconscious childhood conflicts. Social isolation is addressed by strengthening existing and potential supportive connections.'),
  ('2009-261', 'Pharmacokinetic interactions are easier to understand by asking whether a new medication speeds up or slows down hepatic metabolism. Carbamazepine is a classic inducer, so it can lower concentrations of drugs metabolized through induced pathways. Enzyme inhibitors generally have the opposite effect and raise concentration-dependent adverse-effect risk. The useful memory phrase is that carbamazepine can make co-prescribed drugs disappear faster. Medication changes should therefore be reviewed whenever a previously stable psychiatric condition worsens.'),
  ('2009-262', 'Dementia is a syndrome of acquired cognitive decline that interferes with independence; it is not a synonym for normal aging. Alzheimer disease is memorable because it is common and characteristically insidious. Early episodic-memory impairment reflects difficulty learning and retaining new information, whereas normal aging may involve slower retrieval with better recall after time or cues. The central clinical pattern is progressive cognitive decline accompanied by loss of function in everyday activities.'),
  ('2009-263', 'Alcoholics Anonymous is a mutual-help fellowship rather than a professionally delivered medical service. Sponsorship is intentionally person-to-person and experiential: a member who has worked the program helps another member navigate it. This peer structure distinguishes a sponsor from someone who chairs a meeting, greets newcomers, distributes recognition chips, or records attendance. The compact memory phrase is “sponsor equals one-to-one program guide,” while remembering that clinical treatment may still be needed.'),
  ('2009-264', 'Lithium has a broad adverse-effect profile that is often organized by organ system: renal, thyroid, neurologic, gastrointestinal, and dermatologic effects. Psoriasis is a high-yield dermatologic association because it may worsen after lithium exposure and can complicate otherwise effective mood stabilization. Not every patient with psoriasis will experience a flare, and treatment decisions remain individualized. A compact memory cue is that lithium can affect skin as well as the thyroid and kidneys. New or worsening inflammatory skin symptoms after initiation merit clinical review rather than automatic discontinuation.'),
  ('2009-265', 'The tuberoinfundibular dopamine pathway is unusual because dopamine inhibits hormone secretion. When D2 receptors in this pathway are blocked, the inhibitory brake on prolactin release is removed. This creates a useful pharmacology association: more sustained D2 blockade generally produces more prolactin elevation. Clozapine is remembered as relatively prolactin-sparing, unlike risperidone and many first-generation antipsychotics. Clinical medication choice must still account for clozapine’s uniquely demanding adverse-effect monitoring.'),
  ('2009-266', 'CLAS means Culturally and Linguistically Appropriate Services. Its central ethical connection is that access to care includes understandable information, not merely physical arrival at a clinic. Qualified interpretation, translated materials, language-concordant services, and checking comprehension can reduce preventable inequity. The board-style anchor is that CLAS operationalizes language access. Effective communication requires more than handing a patient a translated form; the clinician must ensure the patient can ask questions and understand the plan.'),
  ('2009-267', 'Sleep disturbance in dementia commonly reflects circadian change, medical discomfort, medication effects, delirium, or an unfamiliar environment. Older adults are particularly vulnerable to sedation, falls, cognitive worsening, and anticholinergic toxicity. This item reflects an exam-era comparison in which zolpidem was favored among several problematic choices. The independent adjudication limits that conclusion: current safety-focused care prioritizes cause-finding and nonpharmacologic management, and use of a sedative-hypnotic is individualized rather than routinely first-line or universally appropriate.'),
  ('2009-268', 'Capgras syndrome belongs to the delusional misidentification syndromes. A useful model is a mismatch between visual recognition and the usual feeling of familiarity that accompanies it. The person may look correct yet feel profoundly wrong, and the patient supplies an impostor explanation. This differs from prosopagnosia, in which facial recognition itself is impaired rather than accompanied by a fixed delusional interpretation. The clinical task is to assess the associated psychiatric, cognitive, and neurologic syndrome.'),
  ('2009-269', 'In psychodynamic language, isolation separates an idea from the feeling ordinarily linked to it. The memory can remain detailed and accessible, but its emotional charge is held at a distance. This may reduce distress in the short term, especially around conflict or trauma, while making it harder to connect experiences, emotions, and current behavior. The useful distinction is that the thought is present, but the feeling is isolated from it.'),
  ('2009-270', 'Advance directives extend patient autonomy into periods when illness prevents communication. They may include a living will, designation of a health care proxy, or both. Their purpose is prospective expression of a capable person’s values, not prediction of every possible medical event. Revisiting a directive over time is sensible because health, prognosis, relationships, and treatment goals can change. The central requirement tested here is that the patient can meaningfully make the decision.'),
  ('2009-271', 'Behavioral exposure reduces avoidance and creates opportunities for new learning. Flooding evokes immersion in the feared cue: the patient encounters a strong version rather than taking small steps up a hierarchy. Exposure can produce habituation, but it can also teach that feared outcomes are less likely, less catastrophic, or more manageable than predicted. Appropriate treatment planning includes consent, safety, and a level of difficulty the patient can engage with.'),
  ('2009-272', 'MDMA has both stimulant-like and serotonergic effects. Its sought-after social and perceptual effects coexist with predictable autonomic and neuromuscular adverse effects, including sweating and jaw tension. The memorable pairing is ecstasy with a clenched jaw. Illicit tablets or powders can contain unexpected substances, so the reported drug name may not fully identify what was ingested. Recognition of the acute toxidrome remains clinically important.'),
  ('2009-273', 'Suicide-risk assessment depends on the ability to communicate and reflect on intent, planning, and supports. Intoxication can temporarily intensify dysphoria and impulsivity while making the history unreliable. The principle is not to dismiss intoxicated suicidal statements, but to maintain safety and defer final interpretation until adequate evaluation is possible. Clinical sobriety is a functional assessment, not merely a laboratory number or elapsed time.'),
  ('2009-274', 'Lithium is a small ion cleared almost entirely by the kidneys. When renal function is reduced, lithium can accumulate and neurologic toxicity can become profound. Whole-bowel irrigation may be useful in selected substantial ingestions, particularly with sustained-release preparations, but it does not replace extracorporeal clearance in severe poisoning. Levels must be interpreted with clinical status, timing of ingestion, renal function, and ongoing absorption in mind. The practical memory pattern is high concentration, serious symptoms, and poor clearance, which together strongly favor dialysis and close serial monitoring.'),
  ('2009-275', 'Young children form meaningful attachments to regular nonparental caregivers. Their understanding of a lasting separation is still developing, so they may ask repeatedly for the person or revisit the loss through conversation and play. Concrete reminders such as photographs make an absent relationship easier to represent mentally. This approach is not intended to prevent sadness; it helps the child integrate the separation within stable, responsive caregiving. Practical arrangements such as a new caregiver may still be needed, but emotional acknowledgment should accompany rather than be replaced by a quick substitution.'),
  ('2009-276', 'MCV is the average volume of circulating red blood cells. Macrocytosis means that the average cell is larger than expected. Alcohol is memorable because it is a common acquired cause, but the word nonspecific is central: elevated MCV is a clue to investigate, not biochemical confirmation of alcohol misuse. Consider a broad differential whenever macrocytosis is present, including nutritional deficiency, liver disease, thyroid disease, reticulocytosis, and medication effects.'),
  ('2009-277', 'Social referencing links emotional development with learning: the child does not merely see a facial expression but treats it as information about the environment. It becomes especially visible in unfamiliar or ambiguous situations, such as a novel toy, an uncertain drop-off, or a potential hazard. The child uses the caregiver’s response to regulate approach or avoidance behavior. This differs from object permanence, which concerns the continued existence of unseen objects, and from stranger anxiety, which is distress rather than use of social information. The memory cue is to check the parent’s face before deciding what the situation means.'),
  ('2009-278', 'ECT deliberately produces a controlled generalized seizure, so medications that affect seizure expression or recovery are clinically important. Lithium is effective for mood disorders but has a narrow therapeutic index and is affected by renal clearance and fluid balance. This creates a useful association: lithium plus ECT warrants caution about a long seizure and a prolonged confused recovery. In contrast, anticonvulsants such as valproate more often make an adequate ECT seizure harder to obtain rather than causing prolonged seizures.'),
  ('2009-279', 'Benzodiazepines differ in formulation-dependent absorption as well as in half-life. Diazepam has familiar rapid central nervous system effects when administered intravenously, but its intramuscular absorption is notably unreliable. Lorazepam is memorable as a versatile acute-care benzodiazepine because it can be administered orally, intramuscularly, or intravenously, with dependable oral and intramuscular use. Route questions therefore test pharmacokinetic reasoning rather than selection of the most familiar benzodiazepine.'),
  ('2009-280', 'Serotonin toxicity is remembered as a triad of mental-status change, autonomic hyperactivity, and neuromuscular hyperactivity. The neuromuscular findings help separate it from many causes of delirium: tremor, hyperreflexia, myoclonus, and clonus reflect excessive serotonergic signaling. Serotonin syndrome is often hyperkinetic and develops quickly after a medication change, whereas dopamine-blockade syndromes more often cause generalized rigidity and bradykinesia. The clinical context and examination are more informative than any isolated symptom.'),
  ('2009-281', 'Psychodynamic treatment focuses on how current experiences are shaped by relationships, conflicts, defenses, and meanings. Medication can become part of that field: it may represent care, control, failure, rescue, stigma, or autonomy for different patients. Integrating pharmacotherapy does not mean interpreting away adverse effects or symptoms. It means recognizing that practical treatment decisions and subjective meaning influence each other and can be discussed productively in treatment.'),
  ('2009-282', 'Vascular dementia often has a stepwise course because discrete vascular injuries may cause abrupt losses in cognitive function. This differs conceptually from the gradual neurodegenerative pattern classically associated with Alzheimer disease. If repeated ischemic events drive further decline, prevention focuses on vascular risk reduction and secondary stroke prevention. Aspirin is an antiplatelet agent rather than a direct cognitive enhancer. Treatment must be individualized to the person’s vascular diagnosis and bleeding risk.'),
  ('2009-283', 'Vitamin B12 supports DNA synthesis and neurologic function, linking macrocytic anemia with peripheral-nerve and posterior-column abnormalities. A useful cue is large red cells accompanied by impaired balance, vibration, or position sense. Folate deficiency shares the hematologic pattern but not the classic neurologic deficits. The source’s “vitamin By” wording was an OCR error corrected to vitamin B12. Since the official key page is missing, the answer is explicitly inferred from the corrected clinical pattern rather than presented as an official keyed response.'),
  ('2009-284', 'Informed consent protects autonomy by making treatment a collaborative choice rather than a unilateral medical act. Capacity is clinical and decision-specific, whereas legal competence may be determined by a court. Capacity can vary with the complexity and consequences of the decision. The practical triad is to provide information, ensure freedom from coercion, and confirm capacity to decide. Documentation supports, but does not substitute for, this communication process.'),
  ('2009-285', 'Tics commonly fluctuate with stress, fatigue, excitement, and time. This natural variability once contributed to overattributing tic changes to stimulant exposure. The board-relevant correction is that association is not proof of causation, and treatment should weigh the functional burden of ADHD and tics separately. Tics plus ADHD calls for monitoring and individualized prescribing rather than an automatic stimulant ban. Families should be told what changes to report and how response will be reviewed.'),
  ('2009-286', 'Postictal deficits are named for their timing: they follow rather than precede a seizure. A seizure involving motor or language cortex can leave that network temporarily dysfunctional, producing weakness or aphasia. The memory link is anatomical: right-sided weakness after a seizure points to the left hemisphere, and aphasia usually points to the language-dominant hemisphere. This makes a focused bedside examination useful even before formal EEG localization, while still requiring vigilance for stroke mimics.'),
  ('2009-287', 'Operant conditioning studies how consequences shape voluntary behavior. Ratio schedules are based on the number of responses, whereas interval schedules are based on time. Fixed means predictable and variable means unpredictable. Slot machines reward after a variable number of pulls, making them variable-ratio. Their unpredictability helps explain why responding can persist after repeated non-rewarded attempts. This describes a learning mechanism, not a moral failing by a person who develops gambling problems.'),
  ('2009-288', 'Double depression is an older diagnostic phrase describing one depressive syndrome layered on another. It captures a chronic, lower-grade depressive baseline with an episodic major depressive syndrome superimposed on it. Contemporary nomenclature emphasizes persistent depressive disorder and major depressive episodes, but the clinical reasoning remains useful. Ask which symptoms are longstanding, which are newly worse, and how sharply functioning and safety have changed. The historical term remains the keyed answer for this question.'),
  ('2009-289', 'Triptans abort migraine attacks through serotonin receptor actions affecting cranial blood vessels and trigeminal pathways. Their clinical advantage is migraine specificity, but vasoconstriction creates an important limitation. The memory connection is direct: a vasoconstrictor is a poor fit for narrowed or ischemia-prone coronary arteries. This contraindication concerns cardiovascular safety rather than migraine severity. Acute treatment selection should account for the patient’s vascular history and the current headache presentation.'),
  ('2009-290', 'Memory formation requires hippocampal encoding. Alcohol can disrupt that process while sufficient brain function remains for a person to speak, walk, and interact, creating a striking gap between observed behavior and later recall. Thus, awake does not necessarily mean forming memories. An alcohol blackout differs from passing out, which is impaired consciousness rather than isolated anterograde amnesia. Careful history is needed because patients may use the same word for either experience.'),
  ('2009-291', 'Adoption studies are powerful because they can compare biological relatedness with shared rearing. Their central ideal is separation of genetic and environmental influences. Selective placement violates that ideal: if adoptive homes resemble biological homes, an observed association may reflect inherited and environmental pathways. Remember the confound as similar families on both sides, making nature and nurture harder to disentangle. Prenatal exposures add another source of environmental influence that adoption cannot remove.'),
  ('2009-292', 'Missed-appointment policies involve professional boundaries, access to care, and practical scheduling limits. Ethical billing is not based on judging whether a patient’s explanation is sufficiently persuasive after the fact. Instead, it depends on transparency, consistency, and a policy communicated before services are missed. A written agreement helps both parties understand expectations when symptoms, stress, or conflict could otherwise complicate discussion. The key distinction is between an agreed administrative fee and a punitive response to absence.'),
  ('2009-293', 'The lateral medulla contains tightly packed pathways for vestibular function, facial sensation, cerebellar coordination, and swallowing or phonation. That anatomy explains why this syndrome is more than dizziness: vertigo pairs with ipsilateral facial sensory loss, ataxia, and hoarseness. A useful clinical pattern is vertigo plus focal brainstem signs, which should be treated as central until proven otherwise. The vascular risk profile in the stem reinforces a vascular mechanism rather than a peripheral vestibular disorder.'),
  ('2009-294', 'SSRIs share a primary mechanism but differ in receptor and pharmacokinetic profiles. Paroxetine is often remembered as the relatively less selective SSRI because it has appreciable muscarinic antagonism compared with many peers. Anticholinergic effects reflect reduced parasympathetic activity, including less salivation and gut motility, more urinary retention, and possible cognitive effects. This distinction matters most when aging, medical illness, or polypharmacy makes modest receptor effects clinically important.'),
  ('2009-295', 'The mode is the most frequent value, the median is the middle value, and the mean is the arithmetic average. A useful image is a balance beam: extreme observations tug the mean toward the tail, whereas the median depends mainly on rank order. Medians are therefore often useful for variables such as income, duration of illness, and length of stay, which may contain a few unusually large values. Skewness tells both the direction of the tail and the direction in which the mean moves.'),
  ('2009-296', 'Sexual orientation includes patterns of attraction, behavior, and identity, which may emerge or be named at different times. Historical survey findings should be understood as broad retrospective patterns rather than biological clocks. Adolescence often makes attraction more salient through pubertal development, peer relationships, and increased language for identity. Social safety and acceptance may also influence whether someone feels able to acknowledge or disclose an experience recognized earlier. Clinical care should not treat variation in timing as evidence of disorder.'),
  ('2009-297', 'Mesial temporal structures include the hippocampus, an area important for memory and vulnerable to epileptogenic scarring. In this setting, sclerosis denotes chronic gliosis and tissue loss. A concise imaging memory cue is “small and bright”: the affected hippocampus is small from atrophy and bright on T2 or FLAIR imaging because of gliosis. This contrasts with acute edema, in which swelling is generally more prominent than volume loss. The diagnosis is strengthened by a compatible chronic focal seizure syndrome.'),
  ('2009-298', 'Psychodynamic technique is sometimes caricatured as detached interpretation, but supportive interventions are integral to effective treatment. The therapist balances expressive work, such as clarifying defenses or transference, with interventions that support functioning and affect regulation. Questions asking for the optimal next response often test sequencing: first respond to the manifest emotional and interpersonal moment, then consider a more speculative interpretation if it becomes clinically supported. Sincere acknowledgment is not empty reassurance when it accurately reflects observed growth.'),
  ('2009-299', 'Drug testing identifies a substance or metabolite above a laboratory cutoff; it does not by itself establish impairment, intent, or a complete exposure history. Urine detection windows are often longer than blood windows because substances and metabolites are excreted over time. Amphetamine is a weak base, so urinary pH can affect its excretion and contribute to variability. A useful exam memory is that typical urine detection of amphetamine is measured in days rather than weeks.'),
  ('2009-300', 'Anticipation is often linked conceptually to expanding DNA repeats. A repeat sequence can become longer as it is passed through a family, sometimes altering the phenotype. The word itself is a cue: a later generation seems to arrive at illness sooner than the prior one. It is a pedigree pattern, not a psychological reaction and not a test performed before symptoms develop. Both younger onset and greater severity can express the pattern.'),
  ('2010-1', 'Confidentiality depends in part on the psychiatrist’s professional role. In ordinary treatment, information is protected within a therapeutic relationship, subject to recognized exceptions. In occupational, forensic, or administrative examinations, the psychiatrist’s primary duty is to the evaluation’s defined purpose rather than to treatment. The practical memory cue is to ask who retained the evaluator. When another party has requested the examination, the examinee requires clear notice that the interview is not confidential treatment.'),
  ('2010-2', 'Crisis assessment is organized around preserving options. A disconnected call without a location can eliminate the ability to send assistance if danger escalates. Command hallucinations alone do not establish violent behavior, but their content, the patient’s beliefs about them, intent, past behavior, access to means, and intoxication can markedly change risk. The address-first principle is a safety sequence, not a substitute for a focused risk assessment once reliable contact information has been obtained.'),
  ('2010-3', 'Memory testing is often organized by timing. Immediate registration occurs within seconds, recent recall is tested after a delay, and remote memory concerns more distant facts or autobiographical events. Digit span is a compact bedside measure because it minimizes the need for prior knowledge. It is closely linked to attention, which is why impaired digit repetition is common in delirium and other states of reduced concentration. “Repeat now” is a useful cue for immediate recall.'),
  ('2010-4', 'Parkinson disease reflects uneven dysfunction of nigrostriatal dopamine pathways early in its course, helping explain its often one-sided onset. Examination questions frequently test red flags for atypical parkinsonism by reversing the expected pattern: early falls or gaze palsy, early autonomic failure, early dementia, or poor levodopa response. The useful memory cue is that classic Parkinson disease starts on one side and usually responds to levodopa, whereas atypical syndromes often progress differently.'),
  ('2010-5', 'NMS is a pharmacologic mirror image of dopamine deficiency: it follows dopamine receptor blockade or sudden withdrawal of dopaminergic medication. This mechanism explains why bromocriptine, a dopamine agonist, is the classic board association. It also distinguishes NMS from serotonin syndrome, whose management begins with withdrawal of serotonergic agents and supportive treatment. The core clinical response remains emergency supportive care and removal of the precipitating dopamine-blocking exposure.'),
  ('2010-6', 'Rett disorder is a neurodevelopmental regression syndrome most often associated with pathogenic changes involving MECP2. Its memorable pattern is normal early development followed by loss of purposeful hand use, replacement by stereotyped midline movements, and decelerated head growth. The phenotype illustrates why developmental history must address both milestones achieved and skills later lost. Modern diagnostic systems place Rett disorder outside autism spectrum disorder, although social and communication difficulties can be prominent.'),
  ('2010-7', 'DSM categories are historical artifacts as well as clinical tools because they reflect prevailing concepts, evidence, and social values. The removal of homosexuality during the DSM-II period is a central example of diagnostic revision. It also illustrates the importance of distinguishing identity from distress that may arise around identity, especially when distress is shaped by prejudice or external pressure. The printed-year wording matters: the relevant milestone is associated with the 1974 DSM-II printing following the 1973 decision.'),
  ('2010-8', 'The neural tube closes early in embryonic development, so unrecognized early pregnancy is clinically important in teratogen counseling. Valproate’s association with neural tube defects is among the most durable psychopharmacology memory pairs. Contrast it with the historical lithium–Ebstein anomaly association and late-pregnancy benzodiazepine exposure–neonatal hypotonia. These pairings organize examination knowledge but never replace individualized risk-benefit decisions and coordinated obstetric or neurologic care.'),
  ('2010-9', 'MELAS is an acronym for mitochondrial encephalomyopathy, lactic acidosis, and stroke-like episodes. Mitochondria support high-energy tissues, so brain and muscle manifestations are prominent. The term stroke-like is important because lesions may migrate or cross expected vascular boundaries; the underlying disorder is cellular energy metabolism rather than a single arterial occlusion. This imaging and clinical pattern provides a useful contrast with typical ischemic stroke. Seizures and recurrent headaches further strengthen the syndrome recognition.'),
  ('2010-10', 'Stroke can alter mood through both lesion-related neurobiologic changes and the major life disruption of sudden disability. This dual pathway helps explain why poststroke depression is common and clinically consequential. The examination distinction is frequency rather than possibility: psychosis and mania are memorable but uncommon, whereas depression is the routine psychiatric presentation to anticipate. Screening remains important because language, cognitive, and motor deficits can obscure depressive symptoms or make them difficult to report.'),
  ('2010-11', 'Harm reduction shifts the first question from whether use has stopped to what preventable harm can be reduced today. Sterile equipment is a concrete example because infection risk is linked to sharing injection supplies. The broader philosophy is compatible with treatment and recovery: engagement, safety, and respect can create a bridge to medication treatment or abstinence-oriented care later. Harm reduction does not ignore substance-related risk; it addresses immediate risks while preserving access to care.'),
  ('2010-12', 'The therapeutic index is often expressed conceptually as a ratio of toxic dose to effective dose. A larger ratio suggests more room before toxicity, whereas a smaller ratio indicates a narrower safety window. In real patients, dose-response curves can overlap and individual vulnerability varies, so the index is a guide rather than a guarantee. The classic memory phrase is that therapeutic index means safety window, while tolerability refers to how acceptable treatment feels at ordinary therapeutic doses.'),
  ('2010-13', 'Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy is an idiopathic generalized epilepsy syndrome that often becomes apparent in adolescence. Myoclonic jerks are commonly most noticeable soon after waking, providing a memorable clinical clue. The pharmacologic lesson is syndrome matching: broad-spectrum agents are suited to generalized seizure types, whereas certain focal-seizure medications may aggravate myoclonus. Valproate has important adverse-effect and reproductive considerations, so the historical board answer should not be treated as an automatic choice for every patient.'),
  ('2010-14', 'Social attention develops through repeated shared-attention experiences: noticing another person, following their gaze or gesture, and sharing interest in an object or event. Autism spectrum disorder affects this reciprocal social-communication domain, although presentation varies widely. The useful distinction is that social anxiety centers on fear of social evaluation, whereas autism involves differences in reading and using social information itself. Both can coexist, so developmental history and direct observation are important.'),
  ('2010-15', 'Bell palsy is acute peripheral facial weakness rather than proof of a single pathogen in every patient. HSV may remain latent in sensory ganglia and later reactivate, providing the classic mechanistic association. The key examination distinction is peripheral versus central facial weakness: a peripheral lesion weakens the forehead as well as the lower face. Zoster is the tempting viral alternative because it can also involve the facial nerve, but associated ear pain, vesicles, or auditory symptoms support Ramsay Hunt syndrome.'),
  ('2010-16', 'Psychiatric liability may be alleged after a tragic outcome, especially suicide, because hindsight can make risk appear clearer than it was prospectively. Good practice emphasizes a contemporaneous account of symptoms, changing risks, supports, collateral information, and the reasoning behind treatment or disposition. This approach is not a guarantee that self-harm can be prevented. Rather, it supports reasonable care under uncertainty. The board-relevant association is patient self-harm rather than misconduct claims or medication complications.'),
  ('2010-17', 'Kraepelin made time course central to psychiatric diagnosis. His categories have been substantially revised, and contemporary diagnostic systems do not simply reproduce his model. Nevertheless, asking about episodicity, recovery, decline, and long-term functioning remains foundational. A useful memory link is that Kraepelin separated illnesses by their “movie,” not merely by their “snapshot.” This is especially valuable when mood symptoms, psychosis, and substance exposure overlap during an acute presentation.'),
  ('2010-18', 'Public narratives often overattribute violence to mental illness, but homicide more commonly arises from interpersonal circumstances and ordinary emotions. Psychiatry still has an important role in identifying acute, modifiable risk factors such as intoxication, escalating threats, severe agitation, access to weapons, and symptom-driven fear. The key contrast is between diagnosis-based assumptions and a specific risk formulation. The latter examines what the person has done, intends, fears, and can access in the current context.'),
  ('2010-19', 'A trinucleotide-repeat disorder involves a short three-base DNA sequence repeated many times; expansion can disrupt gene function. Fragile X is especially memorable because its molecular finding is a CGG repeat in FMR1, while its name recalls the historical cytogenetic appearance of the X chromosome under specialized conditions. Do not confuse trinucleotide repeat with trisomy: one concerns sequence repetition, whereas the other concerns chromosome number. This distinction directly resolves the wording in the question.'),
  ('2010-20', 'Cluster headache is a trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia, in which severe trigeminal pain occurs with cranial autonomic activation. The name reflects attacks occurring in bouts or clusters rather than one continuous headache. A useful memory image is a clock-like nocturnal attack around one eye accompanied by tearing and a running nose on the same side. This pattern differs sharply from the brief, triggered jolts of trigeminal neuralgia.'),
  ('2010-21', 'Operant conditioning concerns how consequences shape behavior. Reinforcement increases the future likelihood of a behavior, whereas extinction occurs when reinforcement is withheld. Intermittent schedules resemble everyday uncertain rewards: an occasional payoff can sustain repeated effort. The board distinction is that continuous reinforcement is useful for teaching a new response, while partial reinforcement helps explain why an established response endures despite repeated nonreward.'),
  ('2010-22', 'Alzheimer disease commonly affects medial temporal memory networks early, producing difficulty forming and retaining new episodic memories. Word-list tasks provide multiple opportunities to observe learning and then test delayed recall. This makes them better aligned with an early amnestic presentation than tests of motor speed or simple visual scanning. A useful memory hook is that Alzheimer disease often begins with difficulty retaining yesterday’s new information.'),
  ('2010-23', 'Preschoolers rapidly develop language, initiative, and awareness that different adults may respond differently. This creates everyday negotiations over food, play, and bedtime. Attachment security does not mean perfect compliance; it means the child can use caregivers as a dependable relational base while exploring autonomy. The useful memory connection is that normal development often looks like limit-testing paired with new verbal skills. Context, persistence, and impairment determine whether behavior deserves a pathological interpretation.'),
  ('2010-24', 'Narcissistic personality disorder is broader than ordinary vanity or confidence. Its central pattern involves regulating self-esteem through admiration, entitlement, and relationships that may become instrumental. A useful memory link is that narcissistic pathology centers on preserving or enhancing the self-image, so other people may be treated as resources for that purpose. Limited empathy, fantasies of exceptional success or power, and expectations of special treatment may accompany this pattern. Diagnosis requires a pervasive and inflexible pattern, not an isolated selfish act.'),
  ('2010-25', 'Chronic alcohol exposure alters inhibitory and excitatory signaling; abrupt cessation leaves a transient hyperexcitable state. Tremor, anxiety, insomnia, seizures, hallucinosis, and delirium can appear along a withdrawal spectrum, but they are not interchangeable syndromes. Timing is the board clue: withdrawal seizures occur early after cessation, whereas delirium tremens generally develops later. Remember “early seizure, later delirium” while assessing every patient for alternative causes.'),
  ('2010-26', 'ECT intentionally produces a controlled therapeutic seizure, so concurrent medications can affect seizure threshold, seizure duration, and recovery. Lithium has a narrow therapeutic index and can cause neurologic effects even apart from ECT. The memorable pairing is “lithium plus ECT: think confusion.” Other drugs may still matter clinically, especially anticonvulsants that can reduce seizure adequacy, but the cognitive-adverse-effect association tested here is lithium.'),
  ('2010-27', 'Rape is an acute trauma in which bodily safety and control have been violated. Trauma-informed care therefore emphasizes safety, trust, collaboration, empowerment, and patient choice. In emergency settings, this is practical rather than abstract: provide private space, clear explanations, permission before touch, and the ability to pause. The sequence matters because stabilization helps the patient use subsequent medical, forensic, legal, and supportive options at a manageable pace.'),
  ('2010-28', 'Psychotic symptoms can occur in major neurocognitive disorders, often as suspiciousness, theft beliefs, or infidelity delusions. Memory gaps and impaired interpretation can make these beliefs convincing to the patient. Treatment depends less on the mere presence of a delusion than on distress, danger, impairment, and reversible contributors. A useful rule is not to reflexively medicate a symptom that is mild and tolerated; first assess consequences and support the caregiving environment.'),
  ('2010-29', 'The terminology is often blurred in ordinary speech, making this a durable examination distinction. Capacity is functional, clinical, and specific to a particular decision. It can fluctuate with delirium, psychosis, intoxication, or treatment. Competence is a legal conclusion reached through judicial procedure and can apply to particular legal domains. A person may have capacity for a simple choice but not for a more complex one. Remember: capacity is assessed at the bedside; competence is decided in the courtroom.'),
  ('2010-30', 'OCD is maintained by negative reinforcement: a compulsion reduces anxiety in the short term, making it more likely to recur. This helps explain why reassurance and ritual performance can unintentionally strengthen symptoms. For medication questions, remember that “start low” does not mean “stay low”; effective dose ranges differ by disorder. Fluvoxamine is one SSRI classically associated with higher-dose OCD treatment. Medication reduces symptoms for many patients, while exposure and response prevention directly targets the ritual cycle.'),
  ('2010-31', 'Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy is a generalized epilepsy syndrome with characteristic developmental timing and an electroclinical pattern. Myoclonus is a sudden, brief muscle jerk; when bilateral, it can cause objects to fly from the hands without loss of consciousness. The memorable triad is adolescent onset, myoclonic jerks, and generalized tonic-clonic seizures. Because sleep loss often makes the pattern more apparent, a careful sleep history is diagnostically useful.'),
  ('2010-32', 'Declarative memory is often divided into semantic memory, which contains facts, words, and general knowledge, and episodic memory, which contains events situated in one’s life. Both depend substantially on medial temporal lobe systems for new learning. A memorable association is “hippocampus equals map and memory”: it helps link experiences to context, allowing later conscious recall. Procedural skills may remain relatively preserved despite severe declarative amnesia, because they rely more on basal ganglia and cerebellar circuits.'),
  ('2010-33', 'A dual relationship exists when a clinician has more than one role with a patient or related parties and the roles risk impairing objectivity, competence, or effectiveness. Family therapy naturally involves multiple participants, so confidentiality and alliance require special attention. The memorable question is: whom is the therapist serving in this moment? When the answer shifts between an individual and the family system, expectations and information-sharing rules must be explicit.'),
  ('2010-34', 'Older diagnostic systems placed both inhibited attachment behavior and indiscriminate sociability after pathogenic care under reactive attachment disorder. Contemporary classification separates these patterns: reactive attachment disorder involves limited comfort-seeking and responsiveness to caregivers, whereas disinhibited social engagement disorder involves overly familiar behavior with unfamiliar adults. The question reflects the broader exam-era usage. The history of repeated early placements supports an attachment-related formulation, but diagnosis still requires evidence of markedly inadequate caregiving and a developmentally inappropriate relational pattern.'),
  ('2010-35', 'Transient global amnesia is a temporary failure of new-memory encoding rather than global cognitive collapse. The hippocampal memory system normally lays down recent episodic memories, so affected people may retain identity and remote memories while being unable to retain information for more than minutes. The striking bedside clue is repetitive questioning because each answer is rapidly forgotten. The word transient matters: persistent cognitive impairment, impaired consciousness, or focal neurologic signs should prompt reconsideration of the diagnosis.'),
  ('2010-36', 'Schizoid personality disorder is characterized by detachment from social relationships and restricted emotional expression, not psychotic loss of reality testing. Its central difficulty is relational distance. Group therapy is memorable because the group is both a social microcosm and a practice environment: withdrawal, limited self-disclosure, and reactions to closeness become visible in real time. The aim is not to force sociability, but to expand the person’s capacity for chosen, satisfying interpersonal contact.'),
  ('2010-37', 'Validity concerns whether an instrument supports the interpretation made from its scores. A useful memory cue is that content validity is about the contents of the test: are its questions a representative sample of the knowledge, symptoms, or skills claimed? It is distinct from reliability, which is consistency or reproducibility. A perfectly consistent measure can still have poor content validity if it omits important parts of the target construct.'),
  ('2010-38', 'Thyroid hormone affects metabolic activity throughout muscle and nervous tissue. Low thyroid activity can produce fatigue, cognitive slowing, depressed mood, constipation, myalgias, and altered muscle relaxation. Percussion myoedema is a localized, transient mound or contraction after tapping a muscle; in this context, it is a useful clue to hypothyroid myopathy. Macroglossia is also associated with severe hypothyroid states because of tissue changes and mucopolysaccharide accumulation.'),
  ('2010-39', 'Hypochondriasis was the historical term for excessive preoccupation with having a serious illness. Contemporary systems separate this territory into illness anxiety disorder, in which fear of illness predominates with minimal somatic symptoms, and somatic symptom disorder, in which distressing symptoms and disproportionate thoughts or behaviors are central. The board-style distinction remains useful: the core issue is persistent illness-focused interpretation despite reassurance, not intentional symptom production.'),
  ('2010-40', 'Opioid withdrawal is intensely uncomfortable but is usually not medically fatal by itself; alcohol withdrawal can cause seizures and delirium and carries greater acute medical danger. Methadone stabilizes opioid receptor stimulation over a long interval, reducing withdrawal and craving. This item predates the current prominence of buprenorphine in many acute-care pathways, but its core contrast remains: an opioid agonist treats opioid withdrawal, whereas alcohol withdrawal requires a different strategy.'),
  ('2010-41', 'These terms describe behavior and motivation, not simply whether a symptom is unusual or unexplained. A useful memory pair is factitious equals patient role, while malingering equals material or situational reward. Because motives can be difficult to establish, clinicians should avoid overconfident labeling based on suspicion alone. A patient may also have genuine illness, so evidence of deception regarding one symptom does not automatically invalidate every reported symptom.'),
  ('2010-42', 'The classic opioid toxidrome is central nervous system depression, respiratory depression, and miosis. Respiratory failure, rather than coma alone, is the immediate life-threatening feature. Codeine is metabolized in part to morphine, so its toxicity belongs conceptually with other opioids. Crackles can occur when overdose is complicated by pulmonary edema, but they do not change the core toxidrome. Mixed ingestions are common in practice and can blur textbook presentations.'),
  ('2010-43', 'Opioid maintenance treatment primarily prevents withdrawal and reduces craving over a long interval. Analgesic effects generally last less long than withdrawal suppression, and tolerance reduces response to familiar doses. Thus, a maintenance dose should not be mistaken for adequate treatment of new severe pain. This principle counters a persistent clinical error: treating opioid tolerance or requests for pain relief as proof that pain is not genuine.'),
  ('2010-44', 'Professional boundaries protect the treatment frame, privacy, and the patient’s welfare. A boundary crossing is a departure from usual practice that may be benign or therapeutically useful after reflection. A boundary violation is harmful or exploitative and often involves financial, sexual, social, or power-related misuse. The distinction is not merely whether conduct seems unusual; it depends on context, value, intent, treatment effect, and whether the clinician’s needs are being served.'),
  ('2010-45', 'Psychological tests range from structured measures with fixed questions and scoring rules to projective techniques using ambiguous stimuli. The Rorschach is memorable as an extreme on the ambiguity end: an inkblot has no obvious narrative prompt, so the respondent supplies much of the organization. Unstructured does not mean unscored in every system; it refers to openness of the stimulus and response format compared with fixed-item inventories and standardized cognitive batteries.'),
  ('2010-46', '“Basilar migraine” is an older term based on a presumed basilar-artery mechanism. Current terminology generally favors migraine with brainstem aura, because a fixed arterial lesion is not required. The historical term remains common in older examination materials. Think “brainstem aura” when vertigo and dysarthria accompany bilateral sensory or visual symptoms before an occipital headache. Migraine aura is typically fully reversible, but its symptoms can resemble posterior-circulation ischemia; clinical context and emergency warning signs remain important.'),
  ('2010-47', 'The word semantic relates to meaning. Semantic memory therefore contains meaningful facts detached from the episode in which they were learned. Episodic memory is more like a mental replay of an event: it has a “when” and “where” in one’s own life. A simple cue is that a calendar fact belongs to semantic memory, while a memory of attending the parade belongs to episodic memory. Both are consciously accessible forms of declarative memory, unlike procedural memory for practiced skills.'),
  ('2010-48', 'The sick role is the social position of being recognized and cared for as ill. In factitious disorder, this role itself is the primary incentive, even when medical procedures and disruptions are burdensome. That distinguishes it from malingering, in which an external consequence, such as money, shelter, drugs, avoiding work, or avoiding legal responsibility, is sought. The distinction depends on motivation, which can be difficult to infer and should be assessed with humility.'),
  ('2010-49', 'The familiar maxim is a useful mnemonic, but modern clinical ethics frames it as one principle among several. Medicine cannot promise zero harm: procedures, medications, and withholding treatment can all carry risk. Nonmaleficence asks whether harms are unnecessary, disproportionate, or insufficiently justified. Pair it with beneficence: do not harm and actively seek the patient’s good. Autonomy and justice can also shape a sound decision, especially when a patient’s preferences or limited resources affect the available options.'),
  ('2010-50', 'The hippocampus is essential for encoding new episodic memories, which explains why early Alzheimer disease often presents with rapidly forgotten recent information. Neurodegeneration in medial temporal structures can produce visible volume loss on MRI. This creates a useful memory link: hippocampus relates to new-memory formation, and Alzheimer disease commonly produces early episodic-memory impairment. Imaging is one part of a broader diagnostic assessment and does not replace clinical history or cognitive testing.'),
  ('2010-51', 'Cognitive therapy distinguishes automatic thoughts from the patterns that distort them. Overgeneralization turns a single event into a broad rule: this happened once, so it always will. All-or-none thinking compresses a continuum into total success or total failure. These patterns are common under stress and do not by themselves indicate psychosis. Their clinical importance lies in amplifying hopelessness, shame, withdrawal, and depressed mood while narrowing perceived options.'),
  ('2010-52', 'Epidemiology separates a condition’s current burden from its rate of new onset. Prevalence is often visualized as a snapshot: everyone with the disease present in the frame counts. Incidence is more like a video over time because it counts new entrants. Point prevalence uses one exact time point, whereas period prevalence asks whether a person had the condition at any time during a specified interval. Duration influences prevalence because long-lasting conditions remain in the snapshot longer.'),
  ('2010-53', 'Prions are misfolded proteins that induce abnormal folding of the same normal host protein. This mechanism differs from bacterial, viral, and fungal infection because no conventional nucleic acid-based pathogen is required. Fatal familial insomnia is memorable because severe sleep disturbance reflects selective involvement of thalamic circuits important for sleep regulation. The term “familial” signals inherited susceptibility, whereas “fatal insomnia” highlights the dominant clinical presentation. Together, those clues point to a hereditary prion disorder rather than a typical infection or toxic exposure.'),
  ('2010-54', 'Suicide assessment is not a calculation that predicts an outcome with certainty. It is a structured clinical judgment made under uncertainty. Documentation is most useful when it captures what information was obtained, how it was weighed, and why one level of care or intervention was selected over other plausible options. This supports continuity when another clinician assumes care and makes the risk-management reasoning understandable without relying on unsupported predictions or conclusory language.'),
  ('2010-55', 'Thiamine, or vitamin B1, is required for key carbohydrate-metabolism pathways. Deficiency affects metabolically vulnerable brain regions and can produce the acute neurologic syndrome of Wernicke encephalopathy. Chronic alcohol use raises risk through poor intake, impaired absorption, reduced storage, and increased metabolic demands. A practical memory triad is “confusion, eyes, gait”: mental-status change, oculomotor findings, and ataxia. The clinical diagnosis should trigger treatment even when every element of the triad is not documented.'),
  ('2010-56', 'The name histrionic derives from a word associated with theatrical performance, which can help recall the disorder’s dramatic, attention-seeking style. That etymology should never be used as a dismissive description of a patient. The examination clue is not merely strong emotion; it is emotion that appears quickly shifting and shallow within a broader pervasive interpersonal pattern. Other features can include suggestibility, impressionistic speech, and a tendency to view relationships as more intimate than they are.'),
  ('2010-57', '“Spongiform” describes the microscopic vacuolated appearance of affected brain tissue in prion disorders. These illnesses are notable for rapid progression, severe neurologic dysfunction, and movement phenomena such as myoclonus. The clinical pattern is often more useful for exam recognition than memorizing every diagnostic test: rapidly progressive dementia plus startle-sensitive jerks should prompt consideration of a prion disease. Other medical causes remain important because rapidly progressive cognitive decline requires urgent evaluation rather than diagnostic anchoring.'),
  ('2010-58', 'Rapport is the patient’s sense that the clinician understands, respects, and can work effectively with them. It is not merely being pleasant. Specific microskills such as open questions, reflection, validation, clear explanations, and reliable boundaries help create it. Active listening and reflection are therefore tools used within the interview, while rapport is the broader therapeutic relationship they support. Good rapport also increases the likelihood that sensitive symptoms, safety concerns, and treatment preferences will be discussed accurately.'),
  ('2010-59', 'Motivational interviewing was designed for ambivalence, which is common in addictive behaviors and many health decisions. Its collaborative style emphasizes partnership, acceptance, compassion, and evocation. Developing discrepancy does not mean making a patient feel guilty; it helps the patient articulate how current behavior fits, or fails to fit, with what they value. Change is often more durable when the reasons are voiced by the patient rather than supplied by the clinician.'),
  ('2010-60', 'HCV is primarily hepatotropic, but cognitive and neuropsychiatric symptoms have been reported even when overt hepatic encephalopathy is not evident. The exam contrast is mechanistic: hepatic encephalopathy is a metabolic syndrome associated with liver dysfunction, whereas the keyed option invokes CNS effects associated with HCV itself. In clinical care, that distinction does not exclude other common and treatable causes of cognitive symptoms. The wording “infection of brain” is an older exam formulation, not a stand-alone modern diagnosis.'),
  ('2010-61', 'Exposure therapy relies on approaching trauma memories and safe reminders in a structured way so avoidance no longer maintains fear. Prognosis is influenced by more than trauma type: repeated trauma, active substance use, dissociation or amnesia, persistent danger, guilt, and unstable engagement can complicate delivery. “Random violent assault” is the exam’s best proxy for a single, circumscribed trauma without these listed barriers. Such factors guide formulation and treatment planning rather than determining whether a patient deserves care.'),
  ('2010-62', 'Clinical memory testing separates stages of memory by timing. Immediate repetition checks registration and immediate memory; recall after a short distraction interval checks recent or short-term retention; remote autobiographical facts probe long-term memory. This temporal framework prevents a common error: confusing successful immediate repetition with successful delayed recall. Because attention and language affect memory-task performance, clinicians should interpret results in the context of the full cognitive examination and the patient’s sensory and educational background.'),
  ('2010-63', 'Interferon is an immune-signaling protein, and older therapeutic interferon regimens were notable because they could affect mood and behavior. This produced a classic consultation-liaison teaching point: medical therapies can create or worsen psychiatric syndromes, so baseline assessment and longitudinal monitoring matter. The word relative is important; it signals risk requiring clinical judgment rather than an absolute prohibition in every circumstance. The item reflects historical hepatitis C management, not the usual treatment approach now.'),
  ('2010-64', 'Young children commonly explore their bodies and may touch their genitals because of curiosity, comfort, or sensory stimulation. Developmentally typical behavior is usually solitary, intermittent, easily redirected, and occurs in private or familiar settings. The clinical task is contextual rather than reflexively diagnostic: distinguish ordinary exploration from behavior that is intrusive, coercive, painful, unusually explicit, or linked to other safeguarding concerns. A single behavior alone cannot establish abuse, developmental delay, puberty, or sexually transmitted infection.'),
  ('2010-65', 'NMS is linked to an abrupt reduction in central dopaminergic activity, most often after dopamine-receptor blockade. Dopamine’s role in motor regulation helps explain the prominent rigidity, while hypothalamic and autonomic disruption contribute to hyperthermia and instability. A high-yield contrast is “NMS equals rigid” versus “serotonin syndrome equals hyperreflexic or clonic.” Real cases can overlap with other emergencies, so medication history, examination, laboratory testing, and urgent medical management are essential.'),
  ('2010-66', 'Post-attempt risk formulation gives special weight to lethality, planning, concealment, ongoing intent or ambivalence, available supports, and ability to engage in care. Denial of current suicidal ideation does not by itself erase risk, particularly immediately after a serious attempt. Levels of care should match the current risk formulation and the reliability of the outpatient safety environment. The immediate disposition should reflect current safety needs rather than insurance status or the patient’s medical readiness alone.'),
  ('2010-67', 'Lithium has a narrow therapeutic index and affects excitable tissues, including the heart. Its ECG effects are commonly taught alongside renal, thyroid, neurologic, and gastrointestinal adverse effects. The board-relevant distinction is frequency: benign T-wave changes are common, whereas clinically important conduction problems are less common but potentially serious, particularly in vulnerable patients. ECG findings should never be interpreted in isolation from symptoms, dose, serum concentration, comorbid cardiac disease, and interacting medications.'),
  ('2010-68', 'Legal informed-consent standards vary by jurisdiction, but examinations often contrast physician-centered disclosure with the reasonable-patient standard. “Material” means information likely to matter to a reasonable person considering the procedure. This approach emphasizes patient autonomy while recognizing that exhaustive disclosure of every remote possibility is neither feasible nor required. In practice, disclosure should also be responsive to the individual patient’s language, understanding, values, and questions, while meeting applicable local legal and institutional requirements.'),
  ('2010-69', 'A selfobject is not merely another person; it is a relationship or function experienced as supporting the self. Kohut described needs such as mirroring, idealization, and twinship or alter-ego connection. The memorable link is “self psychology equals empathic selfobjects”: early and therapeutic empathic responsiveness helps a person maintain self-esteem, stability, and continuity. This framework differs from theories centered primarily on intrapsychic conflict, drives, or broad interpersonal field forces.'),
  ('2010-70', 'The spinal cord is a compact pathway for motor, sensory, and autonomic function. Cervical compression can therefore produce symptoms below the neck, including gait impairment and bladder dysfunction, while radicular pain points toward cervical root involvement. A useful emergency mnemonic is pain plus progressive neurologic deficit plus sphincter change: image the spine now. Timely identification matters because ongoing compression can cause permanent deficits and may require urgent surgical or other specialty management.'),
  ('2010-71', 'The consultation-liaison principle behind this item is not that heart disease precludes antidepressants; medication selection should account for cardiovascular effects and interactions. Tricyclic antidepressants can affect conduction and orthostasis, making them less attractive in many cardiac settings. SSRIs became key options because they treat depression while generally carrying less of that burden. Clinical decisions still require review of the individual medical context, including antiplatelet or anticoagulant use, blood pressure, and other prescribed medications.'),
  ('2010-72', 'Blinding is a safeguard against expectancy effects: beliefs about which treatment a participant received can influence behavior, symptom reports, ratings, and clinical decisions. The “double” in double blind traditionally refers to both participant and investigator being unaware of assignment, though modern trials should specify exactly who was blinded. Keep the concepts separate: randomization allocates participants, blinding masks assignment, and statistics analyze results. Each addresses a different threat to valid inference in a study.'),
  ('2010-73', 'Klein described development in terms of changing ways of relating to internal and external objects. In the earlier paranoid-schizoid position, good and bad aspects of an object are more sharply split. In the depressive position, the person can recognize that the loved and hated object is the same person. This permits ambivalence, guilt, mourning, concern, and efforts at repair. Here, "depressive position" is a Kleinian developmental concept and does not simply mean a DSM depressive disorder.'),
  ('2010-74', 'Psychiatric phenomenology distinguishes abnormalities of belief from abnormalities of perception. Delusions concern what a person believes. Hallucinations are perceptions occurring without a corresponding external stimulus, while illusions are misinterpretations of real stimuli. Persecutory delusions are common and can occur in primary psychotic disorders, mood disorders with psychotic features, substance-related states, delirium, and medical illness. Describing the phenomenon accurately does not itself establish the underlying diagnosis.'),
  ('2010-75', 'Normal pressure hydrocephalus is often recalled as "wet, wobbly, and wacky," although that phrase oversimplifies real patients. Ventricular enlargement can affect frontal-subcortical circuits involved in gait and executive functioning. Patients may therefore show slowed thinking, apathy, difficulty initiating steps, and impaired balance. Not every patient has all three findings at presentation, and many disorders can mimic the syndrome. Imaging and specialist assessment are important parts of diagnostic evaluation.'),
  ('2010-76', 'ODD involves a persistent pattern of angry or irritable mood, argumentative or defiant behavior, or vindictiveness that causes impairment. Behavioral learning principles are central to treatment: consequences can maintain or reduce behavior over time. Parent management approaches target interaction patterns that may unintentionally sustain conflict while increasing positive attention for cooperative behavior. They do not imply that caregivers caused the child''s condition; instead, they offer practical, structured tools for managing it.'),
  ('2010-77', 'Sleep-disordered breathing can mimic or worsen poor concentration, irritability, fatigue, and depressed mood. Morning headache is a useful clue because repeated nocturnal breathing disturbance fragments sleep and may contribute to physiologic changes on waking. Sedative-hypnotic medications can make daytime somnolence more prominent and may worsen breathing during sleep in some patients. A sleep history is therefore important when psychiatric symptoms include persistent fatigue or nonrestorative sleep.'),
  ('2010-78', 'Aphasia is a language disorder, not simply confusion or low intelligence. The classic nonfluent-versus-fluent framework remains useful for bedside localization: Broca-type aphasia is effortful with relatively preserved comprehension, whereas Wernicke-type aphasia is fluent with impaired comprehension. Remember the short contrast: “Broca knows but cannot say; Wernicke says but may not know.” Real patients can have mixed deficits, and bedside findings depend on lesion extent, severity, hearing, attention, and the complexity of the language task.'),
  ('2010-79', 'PCP is a dissociative drug with NMDA-receptor antagonist effects. Its clinical effects can range from euphoria and detachment to severe agitation, psychosis, and delirium. Board-style questions often use nystagmus as the distinguishing physical sign, especially when it occurs with hypertension, bizarre behavior, and poor coordination. The syndrome is clinically important because severe behavioral disturbance, reduced pain awareness, and physiologic complications can lead to substantial injury.'),
  ('2010-80', 'Schneider’s first-rank symptom list was an influential attempt to identify experiences thought particularly characteristic of schizophrenia. Later evidence showed that such experiences can occur in other disorders, so contemporary diagnostic systems do not give them exclusive status. They remain useful for history-taking vocabulary and for understanding older psychiatric literature and board questions. The mnemonic pair is “voices arguing” and “voices commenting.” Their presence should prompt careful phenomenologic inquiry rather than premature diagnostic closure.'),
  ('2010-81', 'Venlafaxine is commonly taught as relatively more serotonergic at lower doses and increasingly noradrenergic at higher doses, although its pharmacologic effects overlap rather than switch at one threshold. Norepinephrine can increase sympathetic tone, providing the conceptual link to blood-pressure elevation. This differs from the special dietary and drug-interaction risk associated with irreversible monoamine oxidase inhibitors, which can also cause hypertensive crises in particular circumstances.'),
  ('2010-82', 'The term alexithymia is often glossed as having few words for emotion. The central problem is not simply intense affect, but limited emotional awareness and verbalization. Alexithymic traits can occur in varied clinical settings and may complicate assessment when distress is expressed mainly through physical symptoms, concrete descriptions, or interpersonal behavior rather than named feelings. It is important to distinguish this descriptive pattern from reduced pleasure, psychosis, or a primary inability to recognize sensory stimuli.'),
  ('2010-83', 'Older diagnostic manuals separated several pervasive developmental disorders. The distinction between autistic disorder and Asperger disorder was organized largely around developmental language history, not around whether social impairment or restricted interests were present. Modern diagnostic practice consolidated these categories into autism spectrum disorder because presentations overlap and support needs are more usefully described with dimensions and specifiers. This makes the keyed answer historically correct but not a current separate diagnostic distinction.'),
  ('2010-84', 'IPT treats depression in an interpersonal context without claiming that every symptom is caused by relationships. Its practical focus is the connection between mood and current social roles, losses, conflicts, and supports. The treatment''s broad phases provide a useful structure: identify and frame the problem, work actively on it in present relationships, then consolidate gains and prepare for ending treatment. This present-focused structure differentiates IPT from therapies centered primarily on cognition or intrapsychic conflict.'),
  ('2010-85', 'Akathisia derives from Greek words conveying an inability to sit still. It is an extrapyramidal adverse effect that often emerges after starting or increasing a dopamine-blocking medication. The syndrome has both an internal component of dysphoric restlessness and a visible motor component. This combination explains why it may be misread as psychiatric agitation unless clinicians ask specifically about the urge to move and establish the medication timeline.'),
  ('2010-86', 'Operant-conditioning schedules are easiest to remember by separating ratio from interval. Ratio means that the number of responses matters, whereas interval means that time matters. Fixed means predictable, and variable means unpredictable. Thus, reinforcement after every third completed behavior is fixed-ratio; reinforcement for the first response after a set time would be fixed-interval. Fixed-ratio schedules can produce high response rates followed by a brief pause after reinforcement.'),
  ('2010-87', 'Performance anxiety illustrates the difference between somatic arousal and the broader cognitive-behavioral syndrome of social anxiety. Beta blockade reduces manifestations of sympathetic activation, including tremor and tachycardia. It does not directly change feared beliefs about embarrassment or teach new social behavior. This distinction explains why beta blockers are framed as situational treatment for predictable performance demands rather than as comprehensive long-term treatment for generalized social anxiety.'),
  ('2010-88', 'Advance directives support patient autonomy if illness later impairs communication or decision-making capacity. A living will records preferences about future treatment, while a durable health-care power of attorney or proxy identifies a person to make decisions under applicable rules. Terminology and requirements vary by jurisdiction, but the recurring ethical principles are disclosure, voluntary choice, and respect for previously expressed values. Advance directives differ from informed consent for a treatment proposed in the present.'),
  ('2010-89', 'Kohlberg described moral development as movement from externally controlled reasoning toward conventional social rules and, later, principled reasoning. The earliest preconventional level is organized around avoiding punishment and obtaining rewards. A child''s explanation of why an act is wrong is more informative than merely knowing a rule. Real development is variable, so the stages are conceptual guides rather than strict age cutoffs or diagnostic labels.'),
  ('2010-90', 'Dystonia is sustained or intermittent muscle contraction that produces abnormal movements or postures. Blepharospasm is a focal example involving muscles around the eyes. Botulinum toxin works locally at the neuromuscular junction, decreasing involuntary contraction without requiring a systemic medication effect. The memory link is direct: a focal overactive muscle disorder is treated with focal chemodenervation. Treatment is usually repeated because the clinical effect gradually wanes, and careful injection placement helps limit adverse effects.'),
  ('2010-91', 'Learned helplessness is best remembered as a problem of perceived uncontrollability. When a person concludes that outcomes are independent of behavior, attempts to respond may decline. This fits operant learning because consequences influence whether behavior is repeated. Cognitive theories later added the importance of how uncontrollable events are explained, especially whether they are viewed as permanent, pervasive, and personal. For examination purposes, distinguish this response–outcome learning from observational learning and from stimulus–stimulus associations.'),
  ('2010-92', 'Psychotherapy depends partly on a predictable frame of time, attention, privacy, and boundaries. Departures from that frame may activate prior relational expectations, especially when a patient has experienced neglect, abandonment, or inconsistent care. Repair means naming what happened and remaining curious about its subjective meaning; it does not mean pretending that the event was insignificant. This question tests therapeutic use of the here-and-now and alliance repair while maintaining the schedule for other patients.'),
  ('2010-93', 'The central diagnostic distinction is between a trait and an episode. Many children have persistent emotional reactivity, impulsivity, or attentional symptoms, whereas bipolar disorder is conceptualized as an episodic disturbance in mood, energy, and behavior. A practical assessment reconstructs a before, during, and after timeline using collateral information. This approach helps avoid both missing episodic mania and overdiagnosing bipolar disorder from nonspecific disruptive symptoms. Environmental stress can precipitate symptoms or affect functioning, but it does not substitute for evidence of a discrete mood episode.'),
  ('2010-94', 'The common four-principle clinical ethics framework includes autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, and justice. Paternalism evokes acting protectively for another person’s welfare, which explains both its appeal and its danger. Care becomes unjustifiably coercive when clinicians disregard the values of a patient who has capacity to decide. A useful memory link is that beneficence means doing good, while autonomy asks who should define and choose that good. Capacity assessment is therefore central when clinicians contemplate overriding a patient’s expressed preference.'),
  ('2010-95', 'Adoption studies help distinguish biological relatedness from the environment in which a child is raised. When risk follows biological relatives more closely than adoptive relatives, inherited contribution becomes more plausible. Substance-use disorders remain multifactorial: genetic susceptibility, development, reinforcement, culture, access, and stress all matter. Older examination questions commonly place evidence of inherited susceptibility within the disease model, even though contemporary clinical care avoids treating any one explanatory model as complete.'),
  ('2010-96', 'Insomnia complaints are commonly divided into trouble initiating sleep, maintaining sleep, and waking too early. This distinction matters because sedative medications differ substantially in duration. Zaleplon is a short-duration option for a “sleep-start” problem, whereas longer-acting agents can persist into the next day. The broader principle is more important than memorizing drug names: identify the timing and cause of the sleep complaint, and prioritize behavioral treatment for chronic insomnia.'),
  ('2010-97', 'Klein’s positions describe recurring organizations of anxiety, defenses, and relationships rather than fixed age stages. The core contrast is splitting versus integration. In the paranoid-schizoid position, good and bad are kept apart to manage anxiety. In the depressive position, a person recognizes that one whole object contains both qualities and may feel concern about preserving or repairing the relationship. Here, “depressive” refers to guilt and concern, not necessarily to a depressive disorder.'),
  ('2010-98', 'Progressive supranuclear palsy is often recognized by its characteristic combination of axial-predominant parkinsonism, early falls, and vertical eye-movement abnormalities. “Supranuclear” indicates dysfunction in pathways above the ocular motor nuclei, so reflex eye movements may be relatively preserved earlier in illness. Slowness and rigidity alone are not specific, but slowed vertical saccades or impaired vertical gaze substantially narrow the differential. The tendency toward early backward falls also helps distinguish PSP from typical Parkinson disease, in which postural instability usually develops later.'),
  ('2010-99', 'REM latency is the interval from sleep onset to the first REM period. A useful memory cue is that depression can make REM arrive early. This classic sleep-research association may occur alongside fragmented sleep and reduced slow-wave sleep. It is not universal and can be affected by age, medication, medical illness, and co-occurring sleep disorders. Examination questions often contrast untreated depressive physiology with antidepressant effects that suppress REM sleep.'),
  ('2010-100', 'Acculturation is best remembered as adding and adapting, not necessarily erasing. People moving between cultures may participate in the host culture while preserving language, food, values, rituals, or relationships from their heritage. Assimilation is a related but stronger concept of absorption into a dominant culture. These terms are descriptive tools rather than fixed identities, and different members of a family may adapt differently across settings and generations.'),
  ('2010-101', 'Adolescent substance use occurs within a developmental and social environment. Caregivers, peers, school, transportation, money, and supervision can all influence access and reinforcement. Family engagement is therefore often central, while confidentiality must be handled transparently to preserve trust. Engagement first does not minimize harm; it creates enough alliance and structure for safety planning, assessment, and sustained change. Older examination questions commonly emphasize reducing drug-seeking behavior before expecting a durable abstinence commitment.'),
  ('2010-102', 'Cannabis can acutely cause anxiety, panic, tachycardia, perceptual changes, and, in susceptible people or at high exposure, psychotic symptoms. Emergency management follows the syndrome and severity rather than a specific cannabis antidote. Benzodiazepines act quickly for acute anxiety, whereas SSRIs and buspirone are maintenance treatments rather than immediate relief medications. The examination distinction is timing: acute panic calls for an acute intervention after medical causes are considered.'),
  ('2010-103', '“Complex partial seizure” is older terminology; current classification generally uses focal impaired-awareness seizure. “Partial” indicated focal onset, while “complex” referred to impaired consciousness or awareness. Automatisms are involuntary, seemingly purposeful behaviors that commonly occur during these seizures. A useful contrast is that absence seizures are typically brief and end cleanly, whereas focal impaired-awareness seizures often leave a postictal period of confusion and poor recall. The sequence of aura, behavioral arrest, automatisms, and recovery is more informative than any single symptom alone.'),
  ('2010-104', 'Caffeine is a widely used stimulant, so intoxication may be mistaken for stress or overwork. Its effects can resemble anxiety or hypomania, including restlessness, insomnia, increased speech, and autonomic arousal. The temporal relationship to a marked increase in caffeine use is the key diagnostic anchor. Sleep deprivation and alcohol used to initiate sleep can worsen impairment, but they do not exclude caffeine intoxication. A medication and substance history should include commonly overlooked over-the-counter products.'),
  ('2010-105', 'Mirtazapine can be remembered by linking its mechanisms to clinical effects. Alpha-2 blockade increases norepinephrine and serotonin release, whereas H1 blockade contributes to sedation and appetite increase. It is sometimes classified as a noradrenergic and specific serotonergic antidepressant because it increases monoamine release while blocking selected serotonin receptors rather than simply inhibiting reuptake. The high-yield contrast is alpha-2 antagonism, not alpha-2 agonism or norepinephrine reuptake blockade.'),
  ('2010-106', 'Delirium is an acute disturbance in attention and awareness with a fluctuating course caused by a medical condition, substance, or toxin. In children, behavioral change may be the most visible sign and can obscure the underlying medical syndrome. The examination association with central nervous system infection is a safety reminder: altered mental status with fever or neurologic findings requires urgent medical thinking. Agitation alone is insufficient for delirium; inattention and fluctuation are central clues.'),
  ('2010-107', 'Corticobasal degeneration links cortical dysfunction, especially apraxia and alien-limb phenomena, with basal-ganglia dysfunction, especially asymmetric rigidity and bradykinesia. The name is a useful memory aid. It is an atypical parkinsonian disorder, and response to levodopa is often limited. Diagnosis is clinical and may be supported by imaging, but pathology and clinical syndromes do not always map perfectly. The alien-limb experience reflects disrupted motor control and awareness networks.'),
  ('2010-108', 'Psychodynamic formulations distinguish ordinary resistance from the more paradoxical phenomenon of deterioration after an apparently useful intervention. Improvement itself can be emotionally threatening because it may challenge a familiar identity, important loyalties, or unconscious expectations of punishment. The word “negative” refers to the patient’s reaction to therapeutic progress, not to a negative therapist. This concept reminds clinicians to examine the meaning of a setback rather than judging an intervention only by its immediate surface effect. It does not excuse harmful, poorly timed, or inaccurate interpretations.'),
  ('2010-109', 'Obsessive-compulsive personality traits are often ego-syntonic: a person may value precision, conscientiousness, and control rather than experience them as unwanted symptoms. Medical illness can intensify these traits because it reduces autonomy and predictability. In consultation-liaison work, distinguish personality style from incapacity or intentional opposition. Predictable, respectful communication often reduces conflict and supports adherence. This differs from obsessive-compulsive disorder, which involves intrusive obsessions and compulsions that are commonly experienced as distressing.'),
  ('2010-110', 'The parotid glands are major salivary glands located near the angle of the jaw. Recurrent vomiting can be associated with noninflammatory salivary gland enlargement, making facial fullness a memorable physical clue in bulimia-spectrum purging. The finding is not specific to eating disorders, so it should never be treated as proof of a behavior or diagnosis. In the board-style context of an eating disorder, however, parotid swelling most strongly points to recurrent vomiting rather than metabolic complications of refeeding. A respectful, nonjudgmental history is essential.'),
  ('2010-111', 'Winnicott used “holding” to convey more than a parent’s arms. The idea is a psychologically protective surround involving ordinary reliability, adaptation to the infant’s needs, and gradual tolerance of frustration. It is often linked to the “good-enough” caregiver, meaning that perfection is neither possible nor required. A simple memory image is that secure development needs a dependable emotional container before a child can comfortably explore the world. The holding environment is therefore relational and developmental, rather than a specific therapy technique or biological process.'),
  ('2010-112', 'Myasthenia gravis causes fatigable weakness at the neuromuscular junction. Infection is a common precipitant of myasthenic crisis, in which bulbar or respiratory muscle weakness can progress rapidly. A useful emergency hierarchy is airway and breathing before disease-specific therapy. Intravenous immunoglobulin and plasmapheresis address the autoimmune process, but their benefit is not immediate enough to substitute for ventilatory support when respiratory failure is impending. Early recognition is crucial because weakness may worsen as muscles fatigue.'),
  ('2010-113', 'Mental-status testing separates attention from memory because memory encoding depends on attention. A person who cannot focus long enough to register information may appear forgetful even when storage is intact. Serial subtraction is a traditional task because it demands sustained mental effort and error monitoring, but it is influenced by education, language, and comfort with arithmetic. Its best use is as one observation within a broader examination, interpreted alongside arousal, orientation, immediate registration, and delayed recall rather than as a solitary cognitive measure.'),
  ('2010-114', 'Khat is a plant-based stimulant whose fresh leaves are chewed for their psychoactive effect. Cathinone is often remembered as an amphetamine-like compound, linking khat to increased arousal and reduced sleep. Substance histories that ask only about a fixed list of common local drugs can miss traditional, imported, herbal, or socially normalized psychoactive exposures. Open-ended questions about teas, leaves, supplements, customary practices, route of use, and desired effects improve diagnostic accuracy and support a respectful therapeutic alliance.'),
  ('2010-115', 'ADHD pharmacology is often organized by mechanism. Stimulants increase catecholamine signaling through transporter-related actions, atomoxetine selectively blocks norepinephrine reuptake, and guanfacine or clonidine act through alpha-2 adrenergic receptors. Although dopamine and norepinephrine signaling overlap in executive-function circuits, the board-level label for atomoxetine is selective norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor. This mechanism makes atomoxetine a useful contrast with stimulant medications, alpha-2 agonists, SSRIs, and antidepressant SNRIs.'),
  ('2010-116', 'Turner syndrome illustrates how a chromosomal condition can have a distinctive neurocognitive profile without global intellectual disability. A useful memory link is that X-chromosome abnormalities can affect growth and ovarian development, alongside relative verbal strength and vulnerability in spatial processing. Its psychiatric relevance is not a single diagnostic behavior; it is the need to recognize that medical, developmental, learning, and social features may arise together. Coordinated care can address cardiac, endocrine, educational, and psychosocial needs without reducing the patient to a syndrome.'),
  ('2010-117', 'Chronic vulvar pain syndromes have historically been vulnerable to psychologizing because symptoms affect sex and relationships and physical findings may be subtle. Provoked vestibular pain reproducible with light touch supports a pain-focused formulation. Tricyclic antidepressants are familiar in psychiatry as antidepressants, but their analgesic use reflects modulation of pain pathways rather than treatment of depression. Modern care commonly emphasizes multidisciplinary, individualized management and avoids assuming that one medication is universally effective.'),
  ('2010-118', 'Older sleep nomenclature divided slow-wave sleep into stages 3 and 4 according to the amount of delta activity; current American Academy of Sleep Medicine scoring combines them as N3. The mnemonic is that deep delta sleep is the major homeostatic recovery sleep. This question uses the older framework, so stage 4 is the best choice even though modern clinical reports generally refer to N3 rather than stage 4. The distinction is nomenclature, not a claim that present-day scoring recognizes a separate stage 4.'),
  ('2010-119', 'Specific phobias are organized around a discrete object or situation and maintained by avoidance, which brings short-term relief but prevents corrective learning. Choking fear is sometimes called phagophobia. It can resemble an eating disorder because both may produce restricted intake, but the feared consequence differs. Weight- and shape-based fears suggest an eating disorder, whereas fear of choking, vomiting, or contamination suggests an anxiety-based avoidance pattern. Medical causes of swallowing difficulty must still be assessed before attributing eating avoidance entirely to anxiety.'),
  ('2010-120', 'The initial interview is both an assessment and the beginning of a treatment frame. Collateral informants can provide useful context, but the patient remains the primary patient in individual treatment. A balanced response neither excludes a support person reflexively nor allows that person to define access or confidentiality. This communicates respect for autonomy alongside the clinician’s responsibility for privacy, safety, and an accurate evaluation. The exact structure of future sessions can then be negotiated after the clinician understands the patient’s needs and treatment goals.'),
  ('2010-121', 'Cocaine’s acute cardiovascular effects are largely explained by increased synaptic catecholamines. Tricyclic antidepressants have multiple pharmacologic actions, but desipramine is comparatively noradrenergic. Board questions often test interaction logic: when two agents increase sympathetic signaling, think hypertension, tachycardia, and arrhythmia risk. This is pharmacodynamic synergy rather than a need to memorize a unique metabolic interaction. Co-occurring substance use and prescribed medication therefore require careful review of timing, dose, symptoms, and cardiovascular risk.'),
  ('2010-122', 'The stress-vulnerability model is a useful way to remember bipolar relapse: biological predisposition and environmental disruption interact. Social rhythm disruption, interpersonal stress, and changes in sleep or routine can affect mood stability. In exam language, adverse life events have a particularly established association with depressive episodes. The association is probabilistic rather than deterministic, so a stressor neither proves nor rules out a bipolar episode. Careful longitudinal assessment remains necessary to distinguish an episode from an understandable but subsyndromal emotional reaction.'),
  ('2010-123', 'The anterior cingulate is part of medial frontal circuitry linked to motivation, salience, effort allocation, and initiation of behavior. A useful memory connection is that the cingulate connects concern to conduct: damage can leave a person awake but unable to generate purposeful action. Akinetic mutism is therefore a disorder of initiation rather than a primary loss of consciousness, strength, or language knowledge. Its recognition prevents clinicians from mistaking a neurologic disorder of volition for intentional refusal to participate or an uncomplicated psychiatric syndrome.'),
  ('2010-124', 'Many oral tablets contain inactive excipients that are safe when swallowed but not when injected. Talc is insoluble; when introduced intravenously, particles can lodge in pulmonary vessels and provoke a foreign-body granulomatous response. This is a memorable route-of-administration clue: lung granulomas with talc point toward injected crushed pills rather than the expected effects of the medication molecule itself. The pathology indicates exposure to particulate excipients and should lead to a careful, nonjudgmental history of prescribed and nonprescribed pill use.'),
  ('2010-125', 'Trauma-related diagnosis relies on both symptom pattern and duration. Acute stress disorder captures an early clinically significant post-traumatic syndrome occurring from 3 days to 1 month after trauma, while PTSD requires symptoms lasting longer than 1 month. Dissociation, such as unreality or detachment, was especially emphasized in older teaching of acute stress disorder. The practical memory cue is that acute symptoms occur within the first month, whereas persistence beyond that period supports PTSD if the applicable symptom criteria are met.'),
  ('2010-126', 'Varicella zoster virus causes primary varicella infection and then remains latent in sensory ganglia. Reactivation produces herpes zoster along the distribution of the affected sensory nerve. The word zoster evokes a girdle or belt, a useful memory image for the band-like dermatomal rash. The rash’s unilateral distribution reflects reactivation in one ganglion rather than a diffuse primary infection. Severe pain can precede visible vesicles, so early zoster may initially be mistaken for musculoskeletal, cardiopulmonary, or psychiatric symptoms.'),
  ('2010-127', 'ECT relies on producing an adequate, controlled seizure under anesthesia. Drugs can influence seizure threshold in opposite directions: bupropion tends to lower it, whereas anticonvulsants and benzodiazepines can raise it or reduce seizure duration. This pharmacologic principle explains why ECT preparation requires careful medication reconciliation rather than assuming all antidepressants follow the same rule. Decisions also depend on the reason for each medication, prior ECT seizure quality, medical comorbidity, and coordination among the treating, anesthesia, and ECT teams.'),
  ('2010-128', 'Functional neurological symptom disorder involves neurologic-type symptoms that are incompatible with recognized neurologic disease but are not intentionally produced. Symptoms may affect movement, sensation, speech, or vision. Prognosis is generally more favorable when symptoms begin abruptly, follow an identifiable stressor, and have been present briefly. The diagnostic distinction is between involuntary functional symptoms and intentional symptom production for external gain. Appropriate examination and follow-up remain important because functional and neurologic conditions can coexist.'),
  ('2010-129', 'Development is not perfectly linear. Children may briefly return to earlier behaviors, including accidents, clinginess, baby talk, or sleep difficulties, when routines or attachment relationships are disrupted. A new sibling is a classic trigger because it changes attention and family roles. Temporary regression can communicate distress and a wish for reassurance. Clinicians distinguish this circumscribed response from a medical problem, persistent impairment, or a broader developmental regression.'),
  ('2010-130', 'Split treatment developed as psychotherapy and medication management increasingly became separate clinical roles. Its challenge is not the division itself but avoiding fragmentation. Patients may experience the prescriber and therapist differently, and unshared information can create inconsistent plans, alliance strains, or missed safety concerns. The term therefore implies a treatment team rather than two isolated clinicians. One clinician manages medication while staying in regular communication with the psychotherapist, subject to appropriate consent and confidentiality limits. Role clarity helps patients understand where to bring medication questions, psychotherapy material, and urgent concerns.'),
  ('2010-131', 'This record is an editorial reconstruction, not a transcription of a complete original question. The printed item had only choices A–D and no defensible answer; E, St. John’s wort, was added for accurate teaching after the original response was invalidated. St. John’s wort is clinically important because enzyme induction can lower concentrations of drugs with vulnerable metabolic pathways, including cyclosporine. Medication reconciliation in transplant care must therefore include herbal and complementary products, which patients may not spontaneously identify as medications.'),
  ('2010-132', 'Cyclothymia consists of fluctuating hypomanic-like and depressive-like symptoms that remain below the threshold for hypomanic, manic, and major depressive episodes. The distinction from bipolar II disorder is not simply the presence of elevated mood; bipolar II requires both a qualifying hypomanic episode and a major depressive episode. Rapid cycling describes the frequency of full mood episodes, not ordinary affective lability or subthreshold shifts. Careful chronology and duration assessment are central to diagnosis.'),
  ('2010-133', 'An epidural abscess is an infectious collection in the space outside the dura that can compress neural structures. The classic teaching triad of back pain, fever, and neurologic deficits is useful but often incomplete, so the absence of fever should not reassure clinicians. Intravenous drug use is an important risk factor because bloodstream infection can seed the spine. A memorable sequence is severe localized pain followed by evolving signs of cord compression, including weakness, a sensory level, and bladder dysfunction. Early recognition matters because neurologic injury may become irreversible.'),
  ('2010-134', 'The WCST requires a person to infer a sorting rule from feedback and abandon that rule when it changes. It therefore models executive control: maintaining goals, learning from feedback, and shifting cognitive set. Vascular injury involving deep white matter and subcortical pathways can disrupt frontal networks, leading to slowed thinking, poor planning, and perseveration. Alzheimer disease can also impair executive function as it progresses, so this is a pattern clue rather than a standalone diagnostic test.'),
  ('2010-135', 'Developmental discussions of gender often combine separate concepts. Core gender identity concerns an internal sense of self. Gender expression concerns outward presentation, such as play, clothing, or mannerisms. Gender constancy concerns the cognitive understanding that identity is not changed by superficial features. Remembering “identity around three; constancy later” helps distinguish these constructs. Variation in gender expression is common and, by itself, does not indicate distress or disorder.'),
  ('2010-136', 'Tics are sudden, recurrent movements or vocalizations that are often preceded by an urge and may be temporarily suppressible. Pharmacologic tic treatment has historically focused on reducing dopaminergic signaling, which explains both antipsychotic use and the role of VMAT2 inhibitors. Tetrabenazine depletes presynaptic monoamines rather than directly blocking dopamine receptors. This mechanism helps explain tic suppression as well as risks of depressive and extrapyramidal adverse effects. The question’s wording reflects an older treatment context and does not imply that tetrabenazine is appropriate for every patient with Tourette syndrome.'),
  ('2010-137', 'Acute dystonia is an extrapyramidal adverse effect of dopamine receptor blockade. It often occurs early after exposure and can involve the eyes, jaw, tongue, neck, trunk, or larynx. Oculogyric crisis, with sustained involuntary upward eye deviation, is a classic clue. Anticholinergic treatment restores dopaminergic-cholinergic balance in basal ganglia circuits and often produces rapid relief. Because laryngeal dystonia can threaten the airway, severe presentations require urgent management.'),
  ('2010-138', 'Domestic violence is not simply conflict between equal partners; it may involve intimidation, surveillance, isolation, threats, and coercive control. This imbalance explains why a standard couples-therapy format can be unsafe. Effective early intervention centers the survivor’s safety, autonomy, confidentiality, and access to resources. Therapy may later have a role in selected circumstances, but clinicians should not presume that a shared session is neutral or protective when violence and unequal power are present.'),
  ('2010-139', 'Wilson disease is an inherited disorder of copper transport. Copper accumulates first in the liver and can later affect the basal ganglia, cornea, and other tissues. Basal ganglia involvement explains the coexistence of psychiatric symptoms with tremor, rigidity, slowed movement, dysarthria, and dystonia. The memorable clinical pattern is “young person plus liver disease plus movement disorder.” Urinary copper excretion is useful because excess body copper can be detected during diagnostic evaluation.'),
  ('2010-140', 'Clozapine has sedating, anticholinergic, autonomic, hematologic, and seizure-related risks. Acute inflammation and infection can reduce metabolic clearance and increase exposure even when the prescribed dose is unchanged. Delirium is characterized by impaired attention and altered awareness in the setting of medical illness; it should not automatically be mistaken for relapse of chronic psychosis. A stable clozapine dose therefore does not guarantee stable drug exposure during acute illness.'),
  ('2010-141', 'Behavioral and psychological symptoms of dementia reflect interactions among brain disease, physical discomfort, environmental demands, communication difficulties, and caregiver stress. Caregiver burnout is not a moral failing; it reflects the sustained physical and emotional burden of complex supervision and care. Stable routines, simplified communication, and support services often reduce strain more effectively than punitive approaches. Caregiver wellbeing is clinically relevant because it directly affects safety, sustainability, and the person’s ability to remain at home.'),
  ('2010-142', 'The posterior fossa contains the cerebellum and brainstem and offers limited space for an expanding mass. A tumor there can impair coordination and eye movements while obstructing cerebrospinal fluid flow, producing hydrocephalus and increased intracranial pressure. Morning vomiting is memorable because pressure-related symptoms may be more apparent after recumbency overnight. Medulloblastoma is classically a pediatric cerebellar malignancy, making it the best fit for this combined pressure-and-ataxia presentation.'),
  ('2010-143', 'Attribution theory concerns ordinary “why” questions. People often decide whether a cause lies within the person or outside them, whether it is stable or temporary, and whether it is broad or specific. These explanations influence emotion: an internal, stable explanation for failure may promote shame or hopelessness, whereas a temporary, specific explanation may preserve motivation. The concept links social cognition with cognitive psychotherapy without requiring a psychiatric diagnosis.'),
  ('2010-144', 'Stimulants acutely increase catecholaminergic signaling and can produce intense reward, alertness, and activation. Withdrawal is often remembered as a crash, but recovery has more than one phase: acute fatigue and dysphoria are followed by a longer period in which pleasure, motivation, and craving gradually normalize. This trajectory explains why someone may appear medically stable yet remain vulnerable to relapse. “Crash first, reward recovery later” is a useful memory aid.'),
  ('2010-145', 'Object relations is a psychodynamic term for internalized patterns of experiencing oneself and others in relationships. More mature object relations involve recognizing that people can have mixed qualities and that relationships can survive frustration, conflict, and ambivalence. This capacity supports exploration of loss and attachment without rapidly collapsing into idealization, devaluation, or withdrawal. In brief psychodynamic therapy, the therapeutic relationship becomes a focused setting in which these patterns can be observed and understood.'),
  ('2010-146', 'Empathy in psychiatric interviewing is not simply agreement or reassurance. It is an effort to understand and convey understanding of the patient’s perspective. Open-ended affective questions are especially useful when a patient arrives because of pressure from another person, since the referral itself may cause anger, embarrassment, or fear. A useful memory cue is that when someone reports what another person wants, first ask what that experience is like for the patient.'),
  ('2010-147', 'Neuromodulation alters neural-circuit activity rather than removing brain tissue. The vagus nerve provides a peripheral route to brainstem and widespread central networks, making VNS a memorable bridge between an implanted peripheral device and a neurologic effect. For examination purposes, connect VNS with refractory epilepsy and separately remember its use in treatment-resistant depression. Do not confuse it with deep-brain targets used chiefly for movement disorders.'),
  ('2010-148', 'Parkinson disease psychosis often includes visual hallucinations, especially in later disease or with dopaminergic treatment. The central treatment tension is that dopamine blockade may reduce psychosis but worsen motor symptoms. Quetiapine is the relatively motor-sparing answer among the options in this item. Contemporary treatment is individualized and includes review of reversible causes, the neurologic medication regimen, and available Parkinson disease psychosis therapies.'),
  ('2010-149', 'Age at onset changes the differential diagnosis of psychosis. Earlier-onset schizophrenia is often associated with a male preponderance and poorer premorbid function, whereas later-onset schizophrenia shifts toward women and often includes more prominent persecutory symptoms. A useful contrast is that later onset means one should look broadly for secondary causes, but the classic demographic association remains women rather than men.'),
  ('2010-150', 'Seizures are classified partly by where they begin. Focal onset provides a localization clue, and a new focal seizure later in life is a classic “lesion until proven otherwise” pattern. Glioblastoma is a high-grade primary brain tumor that can irritate adjacent cortex and provoke seizures. The key diagnostic framing is that age plus focal onset matters more than the later generalization of the seizure.'),
  ('2010-151', 'Drawing a clock requires several connected abilities: understanding the command, organizing space, recalling clock structure, planning, and executing a drawing. This multidomain demand explains why it is useful as a dementia screen. An analog clock is a compact test of the brain’s planning map. It remains a screen rather than a stand-alone diagnosis, and performance can be affected by education, vision, language, and motor impairment.'),
  ('2010-152', 'Lithium is the prototypical mood stabilizer and has its strongest classic association with episodic, textbook mania and a family history of bipolar disorder. Rapid cycling describes frequent distinct mood episodes and signals a more complicated longitudinal course. The useful memory pairing is that classic episodic mania tends to be lithium-responsive, whereas rapid cycling is a warning that response may be poorer. This association does not determine treatment for every patient.'),
  ('2010-153', 'Salicylates affect both respiration and cellular metabolism, explaining their memorable mixed acid-base pattern. Tinnitus is a classic early clinical clue and is often the fastest way to recognize the syndrome in a vignette. A compact memory phrase is “salicylates sing and acidify”: ringing ears plus metabolic acidosis. In clinical care, suspected overdose needs urgent medical evaluation because severity can change quickly.'),
  ('2010-154', 'Ethnicity is a cultural and social identity, not a fixed biological essence. It often combines ancestry with shared historical memory, customs, and a sense of belonging. Categories such as race, language, nationality, and religion may overlap with ethnicity, but each describes a different dimension of social life. A useful memory link is that ethnicity addresses which shared heritage and identity a group recognizes.'),
  ('2010-155', 'Sagittal sinus thrombosis is a form of cerebral venous thrombosis that can impair venous drainage and raise intracranial pressure, producing headache, vomiting, and papilledema. Routine noncontrast CT may be unrevealing, making venous imaging important when clinical suspicion persists. The cerebrospinal-fluid red cells and oral-contraceptive exposure further favor a vascular explanation over uncomplicated migraine. Because the official key page was missing, E is explicitly an inferred, independently adjudicated best-listed answer rather than a recovered official answer.'),
  ('2010-156', 'Development is not a perfectly straight line. The psychodynamic use of “regression” describes a return to an earlier mode of behavior or coping, and ordinary child development offers many mild examples. It is best understood as a temporary step backward within an overall forward trajectory. This differs from true developmental regression, in which a child loses established capacities and needs careful assessment. Context matters: a recent stressor can support a benign explanation, but persistent or progressive changes in skills, communication, movement, or social engagement should not be dismissed as normal regression.'),
  ('2010-157', 'CRAFFT is a brief adolescent-focused substance-use screen. Its name is an acronym drawn from its question domains, and the car question is commonly used first because it identifies a concrete, potentially life-threatening consequence. The screen focuses on adolescent realities such as transportation, peers, family concerns, and loss of control rather than relying only on adult alcohol-dependence questions. Positive responses call for further assessment, not automatic labeling.'),
  ('2010-158', 'A chronic subdural hematoma is classically a venous collection between the dura and arachnoid. With age-related brain volume loss, bridging veins are stretched over a greater distance and become more vulnerable to tearing during acceleration or minor trauma. A useful image is a smaller brain pulling the veins taut inside the same skull. The typically slow venous accumulation explains why symptoms may emerge over days to weeks rather than immediately. Anticoagulant or antiplatelet exposure may further increase bleeding risk, but cortical atrophy is the age-related change asked about here.'),
  ('2010-159', 'Adoption studies are often taught as a natural experiment: genes come from biological relatives while the postnatal home comes from adoptive relatives. Real-world adoption is not random, however. Selective placement can reconnect the two influences by matching families on traits that matter to the outcome. The memory cue is “matched placement mixes the signals”: when biological and adoptive parents resemble one another, nature and nurture are harder to disentangle.'),
  ('2010-160', 'The term frotteurism derives from a word meaning rubbing. Crowded public transportation is the classic memory setting because it provides proximity and anonymity, while the core issue remains nonconsent. Diagnostic framing distinguishes an atypical sexual interest from a disorder by considering distress, impairment, persistence, and whether behavior involves a nonconsenting person. The setting is a clue, but consent and risk are the clinically important concerns.'),
  ('2010-161', 'Psychotherapy relies on a clear treatment frame: roles, boundaries, confidentiality, fees when applicable, and how the work will proceed. In training settings, supervision is part of that frame, not an optional later disclosure. The ethical principle is prospective informed consent: patients should understand material features of treatment before relying on assumptions about privacy. A useful memory cue is that routine planned supervision should be disclosed routinely and early.'),
  ('2010-162', 'Depression-related cognitive symptoms can resemble dementia, historically called pseudodementia, although that term can oversimplify the overlap. Affective illness commonly impairs attention, speed, motivation, and learning efficiency. Dementia more often produces consistent deficits in specific cognitive domains, including language. Naming tasks are useful because they probe lexical retrieval rather than merely effortful attention. The memory contrast is that depression often slows and distracts, while dementia can add durable domain-specific deficits.'),
  ('2010-163', 'Oligodendrogliomas are glial tumors commonly associated with cerebral hemispheric lesions and seizures, but their course can change abruptly if intratumoral hemorrhage occurs. The central test-taking distinction is between a slowly enlarging lesion and an acute complication of that lesion. Hemorrhage explains the rapid headache, vomiting, neurologic deficits, and drowsiness better than the usual natural history of a neoplasm. The acute presentation therefore does not exclude a tumor; it may reveal one.'),
  ('2010-164', 'Psychometrics separates accuracy of the construct from consistency of the score. Validity concerns whether an instrument supports the intended interpretation of its results, while reliability concerns whether its scores are reproducible. The terms are commonly tested together because consistency can feel persuasive but does not guarantee correctness. A useful memory phrase is that reliable measures repeat, while valid measures mean what they claim to mean. Good assessment requires both, together with appropriate norms, standardized procedures, and careful attention to the setting and population in which a test is used.'),
  ('2010-165', '“Atypical” historically distinguished this pattern from the more classically described melancholic syndrome. The name is misleading because these features are common and clinically important. A useful contrast is that atypical depression often includes increased appetite, weight gain, and mood reactivity, whereas melancholia more often includes loss of pleasure, reduced appetite, and nonreactive mood. Rejection sensitivity is a particularly helpful clue when deciding between these two depressive patterns.'),
  ('2010-166', 'Benzodiazepines enhance inhibitory GABA-A signaling, and flumazenil competitively blocks the benzodiazepine recognition site. This receptor-level match makes it the classic antidote answer. The clinical caveat is equally important: abruptly removing benzodiazepine effect can unmask withdrawal or provoke seizures in susceptible patients. Thus an examination question may test the antidote, while real-world overdose management emphasizes resuscitation, identification of co-ingestants, and careful patient selection. The presence of an empty medication bottle alone does not establish that ingestion was isolated or explain all causes of coma and hypotension.'),
  ('2010-167', 'ODD is a disruptive behavior disorder centered on recurrent conflict with authority and difficulty regulating anger. It is not synonymous with a child being “difficult”; diagnosis requires a persistent pattern associated with distress or impairment. A useful contrast is that ODD involves arguing and defiance, whereas conduct disorder involves harming others, property destruction, theft, or serious rule violations. This distinction prevents overcalling a more severe disorder when the vignette describes defiance alone.'),
  ('2010-168', 'Projective techniques were developed around the idea that ambiguous stimuli may reveal characteristic ways of perceiving and organizing experience. Their proposed role differs from neuropsychological testing, which directly assesses defined cognitive abilities. For this historical examination item, link projective testing with qualitative features of thought and perception, especially when direct interview findings are subtle. Modern interpretation should be cautious because psychometric support varies across instruments and uses. A test response is not itself a diagnosis; it gains meaning only when considered with interview data, collateral information, functional change, and other appropriate clinical evidence.'),
  ('2010-169', 'Serotonin syndrome is an excess-serotonin state classically characterized by altered mental status, autonomic activation, and neuromuscular excitation. MAOIs reduce monoamine breakdown, whereas 5-HTP supplies substrate for serotonin synthesis. Their combination therefore creates a clear mechanism for serotonergic excess. Clonus, hyperreflexia, and myoclonus help distinguish serotonin toxicity from many other causes of delirium, agitation, or fever, although the complete clinical context always determines management.'),
  ('2010-170', 'ALS affects the motor system at more than one level. Loss of anterior horn cells produces weakness, atrophy, fasciculations, and denervation potentials; corticospinal tract involvement produces spasticity, brisk reflexes, and pathologic plantar responses. Sensory examination is often relatively preserved, which helps distinguish ALS from many peripheral neuropathies. The term “amyotrophic” refers to muscle wasting, while “lateral sclerosis” refers to degeneration of lateral corticospinal pathways. This dual-localization memory cue is especially useful in examination questions.'),
  ('2010-171', 'High smoking prevalence in schizophrenia is a classic psychiatric association with an important medication-management implication. The useful distinction is between nicotine and tobacco smoke: nicotine replacement does not reproduce the hepatic enzyme-inducing effects of combustible smoke. Thus, a change in smoking status can matter for medication levels even when a person continues to receive nicotine through patches, gum, or vaping. This board question uses older terminology, but the clinical issue is nicotine dependence and tobacco exposure.'),
  ('2010-172', 'Confidentiality is an ethical and professional duty, whereas privilege is an evidentiary legal protection against compelled disclosure in legal proceedings. The concepts overlap but are not identical. A concise memory cue is that the clinician owes confidentiality, while the patient holds privilege. Exceptions can exist, so this board principle should not substitute for local law, institutional policy, or legal advice in an actual records-disclosure situation.'),
  ('2010-173', 'Violence-risk assessment is probabilistic rather than a prediction of certainty. The principle that past behavior helps predict future behavior anchors assessment in observable actions instead of stereotypes about psychiatric diagnoses. It does not mean that future violence is inevitable, and it does not eliminate the need to assess dynamic, changeable factors. Historical violence establishes baseline risk, while current threats, intoxication, access to means, and decompensation help determine urgency at the present encounter.'),
  ('2010-174', 'Split treatment occurs when psychotherapy and pharmacotherapy are delivered by different clinicians. It can improve access and allow each clinician to work within expertise, but it creates predictable coordination risks: fragmented risk assessment, contradictory messages, and uncertainty about who responds to deterioration. Professional responsibility follows each clinician’s role and cannot be eliminated by informal role labels. Clear consent, communication pathways, documentation, and independent assessment make this arrangement safer for patients and clinicians.'),
  ('2010-175', 'Bupropion is often summarized as an NDRI, meaning norepinephrine-dopamine reuptake inhibitor. For examination purposes, connect its activating and smoking-cessation roles with enhanced dopamine and norepinephrine signaling. Dopamine is especially relevant to the possibility of worsening positive psychotic symptoms in vulnerable patients. This contrasts with many antidepressants that are primarily taught through serotonergic mechanisms, although medication effects and risks remain individualized.'),
  ('2010-176', 'Schizophrenia is not solely a disorder of hallucinations and delusions; it also commonly affects cognition. Attention, verbal and visual learning, working memory, processing speed, and abstraction influence daily functioning and often persist beyond an acute psychotic episode. Childhood onset places these deficits in the setting of active brain, social, and educational development, so their consequences can be especially visible at school and in peer relationships. A useful memory link is “same cognitive architecture, earlier developmental interruption.”'),
  ('2010-177', 'Psychopathology often classifies beliefs along a continuum of conviction and insight. Ordinary concerns are flexible and proportional; overvalued ideas are more dominant and emotionally invested; delusions are fixed false beliefs not amenable to reason or evidence. Obsessions differ because they are experienced as intrusive thoughts, images, or impulses and are commonly resisted despite the distress they cause. Remember: overvalued ideas are over-invested, whereas delusions are unshakeable. This framework helps avoid diagnosing psychosis from unusual content alone.'),
  ('2010-178', 'The period after psychiatric hospitalization is vulnerable because symptoms, routines, adherence, and supports may all be unstable. Therapeutic boundaries still matter, but a missed appointment is clinical information rather than merely an administrative event. A useful sequence is to reach out first, assess the context, and then escalate if risk or failed contact warrants it. This approach supports both the therapeutic alliance and prudent attention to safety.'),
  ('2010-179', 'Social learning theory broadens learning beyond direct reward: children may acquire behavior by watching others and observing consequences. “Modeling” is the key term. A compact contrast is that social learning watches and imitates, whereas cognitive-developmental theory emphasizes how a child understands and organizes gender concepts. Board questions using this framework test theoretical vocabulary and should not be interpreted as claiming that one model completely explains gender development.'),
  ('2010-180', 'PCP can cause dissociation, agitation, analgesia, psychosis-like behavior, abnormal movements, and dangerous physiologic complications. Older toxicology teaching sometimes emphasized urinary acidification to alter excretion, but modern management centers on stabilization and preventing complications. The useful clinical association is PCP with severe agitation or hyperthermia raising concern for muscle breakdown and renal injury. Hydration is therefore supportive care, not a substitute for emergency assessment and treatment of acute instability.'),
  ('2010-181', 'The brachial plexus organizes cervical roots into trunks before they divide into individual peripheral nerves. This anatomy explains why a plexus injury can impair movements served by several different named nerves. A useful mnemonic is upper trunk, upper arm: C5-C6; lower trunk, hand: C8-T1. Accurate localization relies on the overall pattern of weakness, sensory change, reflex findings, and associated injuries rather than one weak muscle alone.'),
  ('2010-182', 'Measurement theory separates consistency from truth. Reliability asks whether repeated measurements agree under similar conditions; validity asks whether the measure captures what it claims to measure. Interrater reliability is particularly relevant in psychiatry because many assessments depend on clinical observation and interviewing. Other forms include test-retest reliability, or stability over time, and internal consistency, or agreement among items intended to assess one construct. The memorable analogy is a clock set five minutes fast: consistent, but not accurate.'),
  ('2010-183', 'Cultural formulation asks clinicians to understand a patient’s experiences within the patient’s cultural, religious, and community context. An experience that resembles a psychiatric symptom in isolation may be normative, valued, or expected in a particular setting. The key questions are not merely whether an experience is unusual to the clinician, but whether it is shared or culturally intelligible, distressing, impairing, involuntary, or linked to risk. Cultural humility supports alliance and improves diagnostic accuracy.'),
  ('2010-184', 'The frontal lobes support executive functions including planning, inhibition, flexible behavior, judgment, and social conduct. “Frontal” does not mean that every irritable or irresponsible behavior has a neurologic cause. The important signal is a substantial and progressive departure from the person’s baseline character. A memorable pairing is orbitofrontal dysfunction with disinhibition, whereas dorsolateral frontal dysfunction more often emphasizes impaired planning, organization, and working memory.'),
  ('2010-185', 'The key distinction is motive, not whether symptoms appear convincing. In malingering, the goal is external, such as avoiding incarceration, obtaining money, drugs, housing, or another practical benefit. In factitious disorder, the primary goal is assuming the patient role. A forensic setting can raise suspicion but cannot establish the diagnosis by itself; the patient’s explicitly stated secondary gain makes this vignette direct. Clinical assessment must still take all self-harm behavior seriously.'),
  ('2010-186', 'Psychiatric diagnostic systems change over time, so historical board questions may test rules that are no longer used clinically. The old “special voices” rule was memorable because it treated certain auditory hallucinations as particularly characteristic of schizophrenia. DSM-5 removed both bizarre-delusion and special-hallucination single-symptom exceptions. The enduring clinical lesson is to assess hallucinations carefully while applying the diagnostic manual and criteria current for practice.'),
  ('2010-187', 'Older diagnostic systems used “mental retardation”; current clinical language is “intellectual disability.” The terminology changed, but the underlying construct remains impairment in intellectual and adaptive functioning beginning during development. Autism concerns social-communication differences and restricted or repetitive behaviors, whereas intellectual disability concerns global cognitive and adaptive functioning. They commonly overlap, but neither diagnosis automatically establishes the other. This distinction helps prevent both under-recognition of support needs and inaccurate assumptions about an autistic person’s abilities.'),
  ('2010-188', 'Dementia with Lewy bodies is a neurocognitive disorder associated with fluctuating cognition, recurrent visual hallucinations, and spontaneous parkinsonism. REM sleep behavior disorder and marked sensitivity to antipsychotic medication are additional associated features. The hallucinations are often detailed and visual, such as people or animals. Because symptoms can vary substantially from hour to hour or day to day, a single interview may underestimate impairment. The syndrome should be distinguished from delirium, medication effects, and other causes of new hallucinations in later life.'),
  ('2010-189', 'Abstraction is the ability to identify a rule, category, or relationship that is not limited to an item’s concrete features. Mental status examinations test it with similarities, proverbs, and odd-one-out questions. The key is whether the patient can state the concept joining the related items. For this question, pennies and a piggy bank share a money-related category, making the cow the outlier. Abstraction is distinct from thought-process assessment, which is based on the form and organization of spontaneous speech.'),
  ('2010-190', 'Methadone is a full opioid agonist with a long, variable elimination half-life and substantial potential for clinically meaningful drug interactions. Some antiretroviral regimens can reduce methadone effect and precipitate withdrawal, while others may increase opioid effects. The practical association is not that every HIV medication is incompatible with methadone, but that regimen changes require reassessment. Symptoms after a change are often more informative than a theoretical interaction list alone, and prescribers should coordinate rather than adjust doses reflexively.'),
  ('2010-191', 'Menninger’s triangle of insight is a psychodynamic framework with three points: early significant relationships, current interpersonal life, and the therapeutic relationship. It reminds clinicians that transference may express patterns also visible outside the therapy office. Interpretation is the intervention that makes the connection among these points explicit and potentially thinkable. Working through differs because it is a longitudinal process in which an interpretation is revisited, tested against experience, and integrated rather than simply delivered once.'),
  ('2010-192', 'Untreated severe mania during pregnancy can endanger the patient and fetus through impaired judgment, poor intake, trauma, inability to obtain prenatal care, and unsafe behavior. Perinatal prescribing weighs medication risks against the risks of uncontrolled illness. First-generation antipsychotics, particularly haloperidol, have longstanding clinical experience for acute behavioral control. “Acute” is the key qualifier in this question: emergency stabilization and maintenance mood-stabilization decisions require related but distinct risk-benefit assessments.'),
  ('2010-193', 'Diagnosis and disability are related but not interchangeable. A diagnosis classifies a pattern of symptoms, while disability assessment asks how symptoms affect functioning in a defined context. This distinction prevents the common error of assuming that a serious diagnosis automatically establishes vocational incapacity. Functional evidence may include consistency, pace, reliability, social interaction, adaptation, and self-care. A useful formulation is that diagnosis names the condition, whereas functional impairment describes its disability-relevant consequence.'),
  ('2010-194', 'Social referencing is an early form of social learning and emotion regulation. As infants become mobile and encounter unfamiliar situations, caregiver expressions help them decide whether to approach, avoid, or remain cautious. It is related to attachment because trusted caregivers provide information and reassurance, but it is conceptually distinct from attachment classification itself. A simple memory image is that the child reads the parent’s face before reading the room. This ability supports later cooperation, learning, and social understanding.'),
  ('2010-195', 'Cerebral hypoperfusion can cause brief tonic or myoclonic movements, allowing syncope to resemble a seizure. A useful sequence is pale, pass out, brief jerks, then recover. Vasovagal episodes often include lightheadedness, nausea, warmth, sweating, or visual dimming before loss of consciousness. Witness descriptions are particularly valuable because the patient may not remember the event. Although the described prodrome favors vasovagal syncope, a safe evaluation also considers cardiac red flags and other causes of transient loss of consciousness.'),
  ('2010-196', 'Avoidant personality disorder resembles social anxiety phenomenologically but refers to a pervasive personality pattern across relationships and social situations. A useful examination contrast is that avoidant patients are often lonely and fearful of rejection, whereas schizoid patients are more typically detached and uninterested in close relationships. This shorthand is not a substitute for full assessment, but it captures the key distinction in this vignette. The diagnosis requires an enduring pattern, not simply situational reticence or an isolated episode of anxiety.'),
  ('2010-197', 'Supportive-expressive and psychodynamic interventions are often taught by their depth. Clarification organizes and makes experience understandable; confrontation points out something the patient may be avoiding or holding inconsistently; interpretation proposes a meaning, often involving a recurring conflict or unconscious process. The terms describe clinical functions rather than rigid scripts. Confrontation in this context does not mean arguing with a patient. It means bringing an observation into the shared therapeutic space tactfully enough that it can be considered.'),
  ('2010-198', 'Hypericum perforatum is the botanical name for St. John’s wort. “Natural” does not mean pharmacologically inactive: herbal products can affect neurotransmission and alter medication metabolism. For this question, pair St. John’s wort with two high-yield concerns: serotonergic combinations and broad interaction potential. A medication history should explicitly include vitamins, supplements, teas, and products bought without a prescription, because patients may otherwise omit them from the history.'),
  ('2010-199', 'PML describes a progressive disorder affecting multiple areas of cerebral white matter. JC virus commonly remains latent after initial infection, but impaired cellular immunity can allow viral reactivation and oligodendrocyte injury, producing demyelination. Because lesions occur in different white-matter regions, symptoms can combine cognitive, visual, motor, language, and coordination deficits. A useful memory link is “PML equals progressive, multifocal, leuko- white matter disease” in an immunocompromised patient.'),
  ('2010-200', 'Erikson framed development as a sequence of psychosocial tensions that remain relevant across the life span. In middle adulthood, the contrast is generativity versus stagnation: outward investment in others and the future versus a sense of narrowed, unproductive self-absorption. Generativity can be expressed through family, work, teaching, institutions, art, and community care. The word itself offers the mnemonic: to be generative is to generate, cultivate, or pass something forward.')
insert into question_context (question_id, context) values
  ('2010-201', 'This item is anchored to DSM-IV’s former abuse-versus-dependence distinction. DSM-5 consolidated these categories into substance use disorder because the older split did not reliably represent a simple severity continuum. The historical memory link is “cannot cut down” equals dependence. The enduring clinical concept is impaired control: a person wants or tries to reduce use but repeatedly cannot, often despite mounting consequences. Current assessment should use contemporary criteria while interpreting older documentation in its historical context.'),
  ('2010-202', 'Bone responds slowly to nutrition, sex hormones, and mechanical loading. Anorexia nervosa can impair both maintenance of bone mass and acquisition of expected peak bone mass, especially when illness occurs during adolescence or young adulthood. This makes osteoporosis a memorable complication that may outlast visible restoration of weight. Other starvation-related abnormalities can improve more quickly with refeeding. The broader lesson is that physiologic recovery occurs at different speeds across organ systems and requires ongoing medical follow-up.'),
  ('2010-203', 'The mental-status examination traditionally separates thought process from thought content. Process describes the pathway of ideas—linear, circumstantial, tangential, loose, blocked, or sound-driven—whereas content includes delusions, obsessions, fears, guilt, and suicidal ideas. “Clang” evokes the sound of words striking together, a useful reminder that phonetic similarity rather than semantic logic links the associations. This distinction improves descriptive precision and prevents a thought-content label from being used for a speech-organization abnormality.'),
  ('2010-204', 'Long-term psychodynamic psychotherapy explores recurring relational patterns, affective conflicts, defenses, and self-experience over an extended treatment course. Its proposed benefits in complex disorders are often framed broadly, including symptoms, relationships, and personality functioning, rather than rapid symptom reduction alone. This item is historically tied to a specified meta-analysis, so its answer should not be generalized into a timeless claim that psychodynamic therapy is superior to every other psychotherapy for all patients or all outcomes.'),
  ('2010-205', 'Traumatic brain injury can disrupt frontal-subcortical systems involved in inhibition, emotion regulation, and behavioral control. Resulting aggression may be sudden and disproportionate even when the patient recognizes it afterward. Propranolol is memorable because it targets adrenergic arousal rather than psychosis. The therapeutic aim is reduced behavioral dyscontrol while preserving cognition and rehabilitation participation as much as possible. Before attributing aggression solely to brain injury, clinicians should assess for reversible medical, environmental, and medication-related contributors.'),
  ('2010-206', 'Atypical depression is a useful clinical specifier and does not mean that the illness is rare. Its classic cluster includes mood reactivity plus reversed vegetative symptoms, such as increased sleep and appetite, together with leaden paralysis and rejection sensitivity. A contrast with melancholic depression is helpful: melancholic episodes more often include nonreactive mood and early-morning worsening. MAOIs are historically associated with atypical depression but require careful dietary and drug-interaction management.'),
  ('2010-207', 'DSM-IV-TR classified sexual dysfunctions by the phase of sexual response affected, including desire and arousal. It also required clinicians to decide whether a sexual complaint was better explained by another psychiatric disorder, a substance, or a medical condition. This distinction prevents a symptom-focused diagnosis from obscuring a broader syndrome. Depression can reduce both pleasure and interest in normally rewarding activities, including sexual activity. Here, the concurrent depressive symptoms supply the explanatory context for the sexual complaint.'),
  ('2010-208', 'Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy is an immune-mediated peripheral neuropathy and a common subtype of Guillain-Barré syndrome. It often follows an infection that triggers immune activity directed at peripheral nerve components. Myelin permits rapid saltatory conduction, so demyelination slows conduction velocity on nerve studies. The high-yield clinical pattern is postinfectious onset, progressive symmetric weakness, paresthesias, and reduced or absent reflexes. Sensory symptoms and areflexia help distinguish this peripheral neuropathy from many neuromuscular-junction disorders.'),
  ('2010-209', 'The boxed warning reflects a safety concern identified when antipsychotics were used for behavioral disturbances associated with dementia. These medications can sometimes reduce severe psychosis or agitation, but they can also contribute to sedation, falls, metabolic effects, extrapyramidal symptoms, cardiovascular events, and mortality. The warning is not a claim that medication is never used; it is a reminder that use is not routine and should follow careful assessment of causes, severity, alternatives, goals, and ongoing response. In exam language, boxed warning should evoke increased mortality in dementia-related psychosis.'),
  ('2010-210', 'Neurasthenia literally evokes “nervous weakness” and has been used in several settings to describe fatigue, cognitive strain, irritability, and bodily symptoms. DSM-IV-TR’s culture-bound-syndrome discussion emphasized that distress may be expressed through culturally meaningful idioms rather than standard psychiatric terminology. The diagnosis in this question depends on the historical DSM-IV-TR mapping, not on an assumption that every person using the term has one identical disorder. The memory link is exhaustion plus multiple somatic complaints without a defining neurologic syndrome.'),
  ('2010-211', 'The National Practitioner Data Bank is a professional-information repository used by authorized entities for credentialing and oversight. Its reports do not mean that every named clinician has been found negligent. Instead, the system records specified adverse actions and payments that may matter to later credentialing decisions. A useful examination distinction is between whether a payment must be reported and whether a court or other body has determined fault. Those are separate questions, which explains why a settlement without an admission can be reportable.'),
  ('2010-212', 'In clinical ethics, justice addresses how health-care resources, protections, and opportunities should be distributed fairly. Mental-health parity is a memorable example because psychiatric illnesses have historically received different benefit limits and access rules than other medical conditions. For examination purposes, translate “parity” into equal or equitable treatment. That direct semantic connection points to justice. Autonomy focuses on individual decision-making, whereas nonmaleficence focuses on avoiding harm; neither principally addresses unequal insurance benefits across populations.'),
  ('2010-213', 'Dopamine systems are often taught as pathways rather than isolated nuclei. The mesolimbic pathway runs from the ventral tegmental area to limbic structures, especially the nucleus accumbens; the mesocortical pathway also begins in the ventral tegmental area and projects to cortex. A separate major dopaminergic pathway, nigrostriatal, arises from substantia nigra and supports movement. The mnemonic is direct: the ventral tegmental area is the takeoff area for dopamine headed toward accumbens reward circuitry.'),
  ('2010-214', 'Freud organized development into oral, anal, phallic, latency, and genital stages. The phallic stage is especially memorable because it is associated with the Oedipus complex, a foundational and controversial concept in classical psychoanalysis. “Phallic” is the cue for early-childhood genital-focused conflict, whereas “genital” refers to the later postpubertal stage. Keeping those terms separate prevents a common examination error. The question asks for Freud’s theoretical classification, not an empirically complete modern model of child development.'),
  ('2010-215', 'Cataplexy is strongly associated with narcolepsy and may appear dramatic despite preserved consciousness. It is linked to inappropriate intrusion of REM-sleep muscle atonia into wakefulness and is often triggered by strong emotion. Antidepressants can suppress REM-related phenomena, making clomipramine a classic teaching example for reducing cataplexy. A useful memory division is wake-promoting medications for excessive daytime sleepiness and REM-modulating medications for cataplexy. Modern treatment choices have broadened, but this question tests the historical association.'),
  ('2010-216', 'The lateral medulla contains vestibular nuclei, cerebellar connections, sensory pathways, and autonomic structures. Infarction there can therefore produce vertigo, nausea, sensory changes, ataxia, and hiccups in a mixed brainstem syndrome. The lesion-side clues in this question are facial sensory loss, limb ataxia, and falling toward the left. A useful distinction is isolated inner-ear vertigo versus vertigo accompanied by localizing neurologic deficits. The latter requires evaluation for a central process, particularly a posterior-circulation stroke.'),
  ('2010-217', 'Meperidine is a synthetic opioid and a longstanding drug-interaction teaching point. MAOIs can create severe reactions with several serotonergic or sympathomimetic medications, so careful reconciliation is essential. The historical board association is particularly strong for meperidine because of reports of hyperthermia, excitation, autonomic instability, seizures, and coma after coadministration. The key distinction is not that every opioid has the same interaction profile, but that the MAOI-meperidine pairing is a specifically dangerous combination that should be avoided.'),
  ('2010-218', 'Down syndrome usually results from an extra copy of chromosome 21. The amyloid precursor protein gene is also on chromosome 21, creating a memorable biologic link between trisomy 21 and Alzheimer-type amyloid pathology. This relationship makes Down syndrome a useful neurodevelopmental model for Alzheimer disease. The memory hook is chromosome 21: it connects Down syndrome with increased amyloid precursor protein gene dosage. Clinical assessment remains important because cognitive or functional decline can have many causes besides Alzheimer disease.'),
  ('2010-219', 'Ego psychology developed within psychoanalysis by giving greater attention to ego capacities and defenses, not only to instinctual drives. Defenses can protect a person from intolerable affect but can also create interpersonal or functional problems when rigid. For examination purposes, “defense mechanisms” is the clearest retrieval cue for ego psychology. Think of the ego as a conflict manager that uses coping and defensive operations. Other psychodynamic schools may discuss defenses, but they are not defined by that emphasis.'),
  ('2010-220', 'HSV encephalitis has a characteristic predilection for medial temporal and inferior frontal brain regions. Temporal-lobe involvement helps explain memory disturbance, emotional or behavioral change, seizures, and unusual smell or taste experiences. Olfactory hallucinations are often termed uncinate phenomena in neurologic teaching. The useful memory association is temporal lobe: memory, emotion, and smell, followed by HSV encephalitis as an important cause. This constellation should be approached as an acute medical problem rather than assumed to be primary psychiatric illness.'),
  ('2010-221', 'CATIE was designed as a practical effectiveness study, emphasizing how treatments perform in ordinary clinical use rather than only short-term symptom change under highly selected conditions. Its enduring teaching point is that efficacy, tolerability, and continuation are intertwined. A medication can reduce symptoms yet be discontinued because of adverse effects, poor acceptability, or insufficient benefit. The study helped move antipsychotic prescribing away from a simple first-generation versus second-generation hierarchy toward more individualized tradeoffs.'),
  ('2010-222', 'The term fugue evokes flight, which helps distinguish the historical syndrome from dissociative amnesia without travel. DSM-IV descriptions combined unexpected travel or wandering with autobiographical amnesia and identity confusion, often after overwhelming stress. The stem mentions identity loss and inability to recall the past but omits that central travel feature. The independent adjudication preserves A as the intended DSM-IV-era response while noting that current classification places fugue within dissociative amnesia as a specifier rather than recognizing a separate disorder.'),
  ('2010-223', 'Postvention refers to supportive responses after suicide for survivors, communities, and clinicians. Bereaved relatives may experience grief together with anger, confusion, guilt, shame, and an urgent need to understand what happened. Ethical communication begins with compassion and listening. Factual, legal, and confidentiality questions can be addressed thoughtfully as they arise rather than becoming the initial focus. The practical sequence is feelings first, with explanations later and within appropriate professional boundaries. This approach acknowledges bereavement without making unsupported assurances or disclosures.'),
  ('2010-224', 'Confidentiality protects not only clinical details but also the fact that a person receives psychiatric care. Public encounters create a subtle risk because even a friendly greeting or introduction may reveal or invite questions about the relationship. A standard boundary practice is to address this possibility proactively in treatment and let patients choose whether to initiate contact. This gives patients control over disclosure while allowing the psychiatrist to respond courteously if approached. The memory rule is that the psychiatrist does not publicly identify the patient relationship first.'),
  ('2010-225', 'Nicotine replacement provides nicotine without exposure to cigarette combustion products. Patch formulations provide relatively steady nicotine levels, whereas gum and lozenges can address acute cravings. Route selection matters clinically. Gum may be unsuitable with orthodontia or some dental problems, patches can cause local skin reactions, and nasal products can be poorly tolerated with chronic nasal irritation. In this item, the combination of braces, psoriasis, and allergic rhinitis makes the lozenge the best listed option.'),
  ('2010-226', 'Statins lower cholesterol effectively but may cause muscle symptoms ranging from mild myalgias to severe rhabdomyolysis. Increased statin exposure and interacting medications raise this risk. Gemfibrozil is a classic interaction because its coadministration with a statin increases concern for muscle toxicity. Creatine kinase is a marker of muscle injury, although its level and the patient’s symptoms must be interpreted together. Medication-linked symptoms and an elevated creatine kinase level are the key clues in this question.'),
  ('2010-227', 'Mahler’s separation-individuation model describes the development of a separate sense of self while attachment to a caregiver remains important. Rapprochement literally means coming close again. A useful image is a newly mobile toddler who ventures outward, becomes aware of separateness, and then returns for reassurance. This phase involves both pleasure in independence and anxiety about distance from the caregiver. It is distinct from social referencing, which involves looking to a caregiver for emotional guidance in an uncertain situation.'),
  ('2010-228', 'Prader-Willi syndrome is a genetic imprinting disorder involving loss of expression of paternally derived genes in a region of chromosome 15. The syndrome is memorable because appetite and feeding change across development: weak suck and hypotonia in infancy can later give way to impaired satiety and persistent food seeking. Developmental, endocrine, sleep-related, and behavioral features may coexist. The concise memory sequence is “floppy infant, hungry child,” paired with the need for structured environmental support.'),
  ('2010-229', 'Homocysteine is an amino-acid intermediate in methionine metabolism. Folate and vitamin B12 participate in remethylation pathways, while vitamin B6 is relevant to transsulfuration. These metabolic links make homocysteine memorable in questions connecting nutritional biology and vascular disease. The association is particularly notable when stroke occurs at a young age, but stroke causation is usually multifactorial. A result must therefore be interpreted alongside conventional risk factors, vascular anatomy, cardiac sources, inflammatory conditions, and thrombosis risk.'),
  ('2010-230', 'Automatic thoughts are brief appraisals that arise quickly in response to internal or external events. They may be accurate, distorted, incomplete, or overly certain, and they often influence emotion and behavior before the person has reflected on them. Cognitive therapy uses these accessible thoughts as an entry point to examine deeper assumptions and recurring beliefs. A common formulation is event, automatic thought, emotion, behavior, and consequence. The method is collaborative and emphasizes testing interpretations rather than simply replacing them with positive statements.'),
  ('2010-231', 'The postpartum period includes profound sleep disruption, hormonal change, psychosocial stress, and medical vulnerability. In susceptible individuals, these factors can precipitate a severe affective episode with psychotic symptoms. Postpartum psychosis is best understood as a clinical syndrome rather than automatically as a chronic primary psychotic disorder. Its temporal relationship to delivery and frequent association with manic or mixed affective symptoms are central examination clues. Prompt recognition matters because symptoms can fluctuate rapidly and may compromise the safety of both parent and infant.'),
  ('2010-232', 'Therapeutic drug monitoring is most helpful when a measured concentration provides information that symptom review alone cannot provide. Tricyclic antidepressants are memorable because metabolic variation, drug interactions, and active metabolites can cause substantial differences in exposure at the same prescribed dose. Imipramine is metabolized to desipramine, which has antidepressant activity of its own. A plasma level therefore supports clinical reasoning but is not a stand-alone measure of efficacy. The broader treatment assessment still includes diagnosis, adherence, symptoms, adverse effects, and safety.'),
  ('2010-233', 'During normal REM sleep, vivid dreaming occurs with near-complete skeletal-muscle atonia. REM sleep behavior disorder reflects failure of that protective atonia, causing dream enactment. Alpha-synuclein is the protein classically associated with Lewy-body pathology and related synucleinopathies. A compact association is “acting out dreams during REM points to synuclein.” Amyloid and tau are important neurodegenerative proteins, but they do not represent the classic pathologic association in this question.'),
  ('2010-234', 'DSM-I appeared in 1952, after World War II generated a major need for workable psychiatric classification in military and veterans’ settings. Earlier experience from World War I contributed to evolving ideas about combat-related psychiatric illness, but World War II was the more immediate institutional influence. In this record, OCR or source transcription duplicated World War I in options B and C. Adjudication corrected option C to World War II and preserved C as the answer, resolving the source defect.'),
  ('2010-235', 'Informed consent is more than a signed form. Capacity concerns whether a person can make the specific decision, whereas voluntariness concerns whether the decision is free of coercive influence. Disclosure and understanding address whether the person has meaningful information about the proposed intervention, alternatives, and foreseeable consequences. Emergency psychiatry often requires clinicians to assess both consent and legal authority for treatment or hospitalization. These concepts remain distinct: a patient can have capacity yet be coerced, or lack capacity without being coerced.'),
  ('2010-236', 'OCD is commonly maintained by a loop in which an obsession produces distress, a compulsion produces immediate relief, and that relief strengthens the compulsion. Exposure and response prevention deliberately reverses this pattern. The patient encounters an obsessional trigger but does not perform the usual neutralizing act, avoidance, reassurance seeking, or mental ritual. Anxiety often rises initially and then changes over time. In the terminology tested here, the decline in anxiety during repeated exposure is habituation, while the relief after a compulsion illustrates negative reinforcement.'),
  ('2010-237', 'Lithium has a narrow therapeutic index. Dehydration, acute illness, renal impairment, and medication interactions can raise lithium exposure and cause toxicity. SSRIs are commonly used antidepressants, but combined regimens still require careful medication reconciliation and monitoring. For this PRITE item, lithium plus an SSRI is associated with seizure risk. Serotonin toxicity should also be recognized clinically, but it is a separate syndrome characterized by mental-status, autonomic, and neuromuscular findings.'),
  ('2010-238', 'ADHD presentations vary in how visible they are to adults. Hyperactive and impulsive behaviors often receive immediate attention, whereas inattentive symptoms can appear as quiet disengagement, slow work, forgetfulness, or disorganization. Learning disorders are an important associated finding because skill deficits can coexist with and intensify impairment from inattention. Anxiety and somatic complaints may further obscure the presentation. A useful memory link is quiet ADHD with school difficulty, anxiety, and somatic complaints.'),
  ('2010-239', 'Older popular models often portrayed grief as a predictable sequence of stages. Clinical work recognizes wide variation in its timing, expression, cultural meaning, and coping style. A defense can be adaptive or maladaptive depending on reality testing and functional consequences. The central question is not whether a patient seems sufficiently sad, but whether the patient understands reality, participates in care, and has significant distress or impairment. A positive response to severe illness can be genuine adaptation rather than pathological denial.'),
  ('2010-240', 'The MMSE is a brief screening instrument rather than a stand-alone diagnostic test. Scores must be interpreted against the patient’s baseline, education, language, culture, sensory abilities, and current medical state. Contemporary distinctions between mild and major neurocognitive disorders depend especially on functional independence. A person may perform abnormally yet remain independent, supporting mild cognitive impairment, whereas decline that disrupts independent daily activities supports dementia. Consequently, the combined A/B key expresses uncertainty that the score alone cannot resolve.'),
  ('2010-241', 'Myotonia is delayed muscle relaxation after contraction. Channelopathies alter muscle-membrane electrical behavior, so symptoms may be triggered by exercise, rest, or cold. Paradoxical myotonia means that repeated contraction worsens stiffness rather than improving it. Paramyotonia congenita is therefore strongly associated with skeletal-muscle sodium-channel dysfunction. Chloride-channel myotonia more often demonstrates a warm-up phenomenon, with improvement after repeated contractions. The distinction between worsening and improvement with continued activity is highly useful in this question.'),
  ('2010-242', 'Bulimia nervosa involves recurrent binge eating followed by compensatory behaviors such as vomiting, laxative misuse, fasting, or excessive exercise. Treatment is multidisciplinary because the illness affects psychiatric symptoms, nutrition, and medical safety. Fluoxetine is a frequently tested medication association because it can reduce bingeing and purging. The contrasting board point is bupropion: despite its antidepressant role in other settings, it is generally avoided in bulimia nervosa because of seizure risk. Medical evaluation is particularly important when purging is frequent or symptoms suggest electrolyte or cardiac complications.'),
  ('2010-243', 'Formication derives from a Latin term related to ants and describes a crawling-insects sensation on or beneath the skin. It can occur in several medical and substance-related conditions, but the examination cluster of formication, heightened activity, paranoia, and repetitive movements strongly points to stimulants. Cocaine and amphetamines increase catecholaminergic activity and can produce sympathetic activation, insomnia, suspiciousness, and psychotic symptoms at high exposure. The diagnosis should still be considered alongside delirium, medication effects, dermatologic causes, and primary psychiatric illness when clinically relevant.'),
  ('2010-244', 'Operant conditioning describes how the consequences of a behavior influence the chance that the behavior will recur. Reinforcement increases a behavior; punishment decreases it. Positive reinforcement adds a rewarding consequence, whereas negative reinforcement removes an aversive one. In panic-related avoidance, leaving the setting produces rapid relief from fear and bodily discomfort. That relief makes future escape more likely even though it may progressively narrow the person’s life. Exposure-based treatment targets this contingency by reducing escape and avoidance in a gradual, collaborative manner.'),
  ('2010-245', 'Friendship patterns develop within changing social roles and opportunities. Earlier peer relationships are often organized by school cohorts and close age similarity because children and adolescents share structured settings. In midlife, employment, caregiving, neighborhoods, hobbies, community participation, and accumulated life experience create broader opportunities for connection across ages. Friendships can remain highly important even when schedules, geographic distance, and family responsibilities limit contact. Developmental generalizations describe tendencies rather than requirements; an individual person’s social network may differ substantially from the typical pattern.'),
  ('2010-246', 'Disorders of consciousness are organized around arousal and awareness. Eye opening and sleep-wake cycles show arousal but do not by themselves show awareness. Unresponsive wakefulness involves wakefulness without reproducible evidence of awareness. A minimally conscious state instead includes intermittent, meaningful signs of awareness, such as command following or intelligible verbalization. Because responses may fluctuate greatly, serial standardized examinations are especially important. Preserved consciousness with severe paralysis suggests locked-in syndrome, a different condition that can be mistaken for a disorder of consciousness.'),
  ('2010-247', 'Defense mechanisms can be distinguished by whether they are conscious and whether reality testing is maintained. Suppression is voluntary putting aside of a thought or feeling for later, unlike repression, which is unconscious. In real life, suppression can support effective functioning during emergencies or demanding tasks. It becomes clinically concerning only when avoidance remains inflexible and interferes with later processing, relationships, or functioning. The parent in this vignette remains organized, reality-based, and focused on necessary action.'),
  ('2010-248', 'Addictive behavior is shaped by both pharmacology and conditioning. Repeated smoking after a meal makes the meal a cue that predicts nicotine and evokes craving. Such cues can remain powerful after nicotine has cleared, which is why quitting often requires both withdrawal management and routine change. Environmental triggers are recurring situations, places, people, or activities linked to prior substance use. The post-meal craving in this item is therefore a learned association rather than a withdrawal symptom by itself.'),
  ('2010-249', 'OCD and tic disorders are often taught as overlapping neurodevelopmental-spectrum conditions. Tics are recurrent motor movements or vocalizations that often have a premonitory urge. Compulsions are repetitive behaviors linked to obsessions, distress reduction, or internal rules. Their overlap directs the evaluator to ask about both rather than assuming every repetitive behavior has the same function. The classic memory link is that pediatric OCD and Tourette-spectrum tic disorders show familial association.'),
  ('2010-250', 'Legal standards vary by jurisdiction, but competence to stand trial is fundamentally present-oriented. It protects fairness by requiring a defendant’s meaningful participation in proceedings. This differs from an insanity or criminal-responsibility evaluation, which looks backward to mental state during the alleged act. A useful examination distinction is “competence now; responsibility then.” Psychiatric diagnosis alone neither establishes nor excludes competence because the legal question concerns functional capacity in the current proceeding.'),
  ('2010-251', 'Lithium is handled by the kidney in ways that parallel sodium handling. Volume depletion or sodium depletion increases proximal tubular lithium reabsorption and can convert a stable maintenance dose into toxicity. Neurologic findings may progress from coarse tremor and ataxia to hyperreflexia, altered consciousness, and seizures. The dialysis connection is easy to remember: lithium has little protein binding and is readily removed from the bloodstream by hemodialysis.'),
  ('2010-252', 'Rapid cycling describes a recurrent episodic course, not mood changes that occur several times in one day. The useful threshold is four or more distinct mood episodes in 12 months, separated by remission or an opposite-polarity switch. This distinction prevents confusing rapid cycling with chronic affective instability, mixed symptoms, or inadequate recovery from one prolonged episode. Female sex is an associated risk factor, whereas diagnosis rests on the documented longitudinal course.'),
  ('2010-253', 'Psychoanalytic psychotherapy holds that symptoms and relationships may express conflicts, wishes, fears, and defenses that are not fully conscious. The therapeutic relationship provides a setting in which these patterns can become visible through transference and resistance. Interpretation and exploration aim to expand awareness and support working through of these conflicts. Insight, childhood history, alliance, and relationship improvement are clinically valuable across several therapy models, so they do not uniquely identify psychoanalytic work. The board-relevant distinction is the central emphasis on unconscious conflict and its manifestations.'),
  ('2010-254', 'Intellectual disability assessment requires more than an IQ score because adaptive functioning shows how cognitive limitations affect ordinary independence. Adaptive behavior refers to practical performance in real settings, not only what a child can demonstrate on a formal test. A simple memory cue is that the Vineland asks how a person navigates life: communicating, caring for self, socializing, and functioning in the community. These are the domains most relevant to self-care needs.'),
  ('2010-255', 'Panic disorder becomes disabling when fear of bodily sensations leads to anticipatory anxiety and avoidance. CBT directly targets that fear-and-avoidance cycle, while medication can reduce panic symptoms and anticipatory anxiety. The historical examination principle is not that treatment options are interchangeable in every individual. It is that comparative evidence did not require one universal initial treatment or combination strategy for all patients. Clinical decisions should be linked to the person’s symptoms, values, and access to care.'),
  ('2010-256', 'Venlafaxine provides a useful dose-dependent pharmacology model: serotonin first, then norepinephrine, then dopamine at the upper end of recommended dosing. This sequence explains why the drug is categorized clinically as an SNRI rather than as a broadly dopaminergic antidepressant. Transporter effects are relative rather than abrupt on-off switches, so the mnemonic is most useful for distinguishing answer choices and anticipating that dose increases may alter both benefits and adverse effects.'),
  ('2010-257', 'Nystagmus consists of a slow vestibular drift and a corrective fast phase; its direction is named for the fast phase. In peripheral vestibular imbalance, visual fixation helps compensate for the asymmetry, so the oscillation diminishes when the patient fixes on a target. Alexander’s law is the compact memory aid that peripheral nystagmus becomes more pronounced with gaze toward its fast phase. These findings help localize the process but must be interpreted with the full neurologic examination.'),
  ('2010-258', 'Preschool cognition is rich in symbols: one object can stand for another, and imagined stories organize play. Animistic thinking means attributing life, feelings, or intention to objects and fits this developmental style. The key distinction is between a child who knowingly inhabits a pretend narrative and a child with a fixed, distressing experience outside play. The former is usually ordinary preschool development and should be understood in the context of the child’s overall functioning.'),
  ('2010-259', 'The spinal cord’s long tracts create a memorable localization pattern. The anterior spinal artery supplies pathways carrying motor output and pain-temperature information, whereas posterior spinal circulation supplies the dorsal columns. Thus, anterior cord injury impairs movement and anterolateral sensation while position sense and vibration are relatively protected. This pattern is more informative than the precipitating story when selecting the affected vessel on an examination question.'),
  ('2010-260', 'GHB is a CNS depressant that can cause abrupt sedation and impaired coordination. Its association with nightlife can obscure another setting in which it has been misused: bodybuilding culture and products promoted for alleged hormone-related effects. The board-relevant pattern is a gym-related exposure story combined with sedation, bradycardia, and a negative routine toxicology screen. Such a screen does not reliably exclude many clinically important intoxications.'),
  ('2010-261', 'Confidentiality includes more than the content of psychiatric sessions. The fact that a person sought treatment, is scheduled with a clinician, or is known to a psychiatrist can itself be sensitive information. A neither-confirm-nor-deny response avoids accidental disclosure without accusing the questioner or inventing an explanation. It also preserves a consistent professional boundary when the request is made casually rather than through an authorized clinical process.'),
  ('2010-262', 'A classic pharmacokinetic memory aid is “LOT”: lorazepam, oxazepam, and temazepam undergo conjugation rather than oxidative metabolism. This makes them relatively less prone to prolonged accumulation in older adults or people with liver dysfunction. The clinical point is broader than metabolism alone: older adults are especially susceptible to sedation, impaired coordination, falls, and delirium from any benzodiazepine. Relative safety should not be mistaken for absence of risk.'),
  ('2010-263', 'IPT frames many mood episodes through disruptions in a person’s social world. A role transition is more than a stressful event: it changes identity, expectations, routines, and relationships and therefore requires adaptation. The four IPT problem areas provide an exam-friendly map: grief, disputes, transitions, and interpersonal deficits. This contrasts with CBT’s focus on automatic thoughts and cognitive distortions and psychodynamic therapy’s focus on unconscious conflict.'),
  ('2010-264', 'Dissociation involves disruption in the usual integration of consciousness, memory, identity, perception, emotion, or behavior. In dissociative identity disorder, identity disruption is paired with recurrent amnesia that exceeds normal forgetting. The memory gaps may involve daily events, important personal information, or trauma-related experiences. Symptoms can be intermittent, concealed, or recognized first by others, which makes careful history taking important. A diagnosis should not be made from a dramatic presentation alone; clinicians must assess distress, impairment, cultural context, medical explanations, substance effects, and other psychiatric conditions.'),
  ('2010-265', 'Tuberculous meningitis often involves the base of the brain, where inflammatory exudate can affect multiple cranial nerves. This explains why headache and confusion may occur with diplopia, hoarseness, or other cranial neuropathies. For memory, pair chronic or subacute meningitis with mononuclear-predominant CSF, high protein, and low glucose. This pattern differs from the more typical glucose profile of viral meningitis and supports a focused infectious evaluation.'),
  ('2010-266', 'Children initially use speech as a social tool and then increasingly use it to guide their own behavior. In early childhood, this private speech is audible and commonly accompanies planning, play, and emotion regulation. It becomes less overt with development. The key distinction is that the child is authoring a pretend story with toys rather than reporting an unwanted perception that occurs independently of play. Context and overall development determine whether further assessment is needed.'),
  ('2010-267', 'Adenosine accumulates with prolonged wakefulness and generally promotes sleepiness while dampening neural activity. Caffeine’s familiar alerting effect arises because it blocks adenosine receptors rather than because it directly activates dopamine receptors. Adenosine A2A receptors interact functionally with dopaminergic signaling in relevant brain circuits, which helps explain the question’s reference to increased dopaminergic activity. Caffeine can therefore feel stimulating even though its primary receptor target differs from cocaine or amphetamines. Individual response depends on dose, timing, tolerance, medical conditions, and concurrent medications or substances.'),
  ('2010-268', 'The familiar ethics principles can be remembered by their central questions. Autonomy asks what an informed patient chooses; beneficence asks what may help; nonmaleficence asks what may harm; and justice asks whether care is fair. Information is ethically important because it turns consent from a signature into a patient-centered decision process. Confidentiality is also vital but addresses protection of private information rather than the patient’s ability to choose among treatment options.'),
  ('2010-269', 'Benzodiazepines enhance GABA-A receptor signaling and can be useful short term for selected symptoms, but continued exposure can produce tolerance and physiologic dependence. Tolerance means the same dose has less desired effect; it does not by itself establish a substance use disorder. In older adults, sedative effects and impaired coordination are particularly consequential. A useful memory link is that when a hypnotic increasingly fails while daytime sedation grows, the medication may be maintaining the sleep complaint rather than solving it.'),
  ('2010-270', 'Agoraphobia is fear of being trapped, unable to leave, or unable to receive help if severe symptoms arise. The feared setting is not necessarily objectively dangerous; its meaning is that escape or rescue feels unavailable. Avoidance often expands through negative reinforcement: leaving or avoiding a setting immediately reduces fear, making future avoidance more likely. Earlier diagnostic systems separately described agoraphobia without a history of panic disorder, as reflected in this question''s wording.'),
  ('2010-271', 'For children, trauma is often embedded in relationships rather than a single public catastrophe. Interpersonal violence by or around caregivers can be especially disruptive because it threatens both safety and attachment. PTSD can follow many types of exposure, but prevalence questions commonly distinguish common private exposures from memorable but less frequent events such as terrorism or disasters. A practical memory cue is that pediatric trauma screening begins with environments in which the child depends on adults most.'),
  ('2010-272', 'SSRI discontinuation symptoms can occur when serotonergic exposure falls quickly after dose reduction or abrupt cessation. Common symptoms include dizziness, sensory disturbances, sleep changes, flu-like symptoms, irritability, and anxiety. Fluoxetine differs from many other SSRIs because both it and its active metabolite persist for a relatively long time, creating a gradual decline in exposure. This often reduces the likelihood of a discontinuation syndrome and can support a bridging strategy in selected cases. Clinical assessment must distinguish discontinuation symptoms from relapse, adverse effects, and unrelated illness.'),
  ('2010-273', 'Optic neuritis localizes to the optic nerve and commonly presents with subacute unilateral vision loss, pain that may worsen with eye movement, reduced color saturation, and a relative afferent pupillary defect. Optic-disc swelling may be visible when the anterior portion of the nerve is involved. Vision often improves over subsequent weeks, although recovery can be incomplete. In a younger adult, this syndrome is a classic demyelinating event and prompts consideration of multiple sclerosis. Other causes of optic neuropathy and visual loss remain important in the acute differential.'),
  ('2010-274', 'Benzodiazepine terminology can be confusing. Physiologic dependence refers to adaptation, such that stopping the drug can cause withdrawal; it can occur during appropriate prescribed use. Substance use disorder requires a maladaptive behavioral syndrome, not merely tolerance or withdrawal. Alcohol and benzodiazepines are both central nervous system depressants and are clinically linked in risk assessment. The memorable distinction is that withdrawal signals adaptation, while a broader substance-use history signals elevated misuse risk.'),
  ('2010-275', 'Trichotillomania is a body-focused repetitive behavior characterized by recurrent pulling that leads to hair loss and attempts to reduce or stop the behavior. Pulling may be focused and urge-driven or more automatic during sedentary activities. Its placement near obsessive-compulsive and related disorders reflects repetitive behavior, but it is not defined by classic obsessions. A helpful memory connection is that distress from hair pulling often travels with affective symptoms, making mood screening clinically essential.'),
  ('2010-276', 'Conversion disorder is now generally termed functional neurologic symptom disorder. The diagnosis is supported by positive clinical inconsistency or incongruence with recognized neurologic disease, not merely normal tests or an assumed psychological cause. A function-based approach shifts attention from proving whether symptoms are real to helping the patient regain capabilities. This framing is especially useful with adolescents, for whom school reintegration and family responses can strongly influence recovery.'),
  ('2010-277', 'Lithium is eliminated almost entirely by the kidneys and has a narrow therapeutic index. Anything that decreases effective circulating volume, changes sodium handling, or reduces renal perfusion can raise its concentration. Thiazides are a classic board association because their effect on sodium reabsorption predictably promotes lithium reabsorption. A useful memory phrase is that lithium follows sodium: when the kidney avidly retains sodium, it may retain lithium too.'),
  ('2010-278', 'Classical aphasia models emphasize language regions linked by white-matter tracts. The arcuate fasciculus is remembered as the arc connecting comprehension and production systems; severing the connection preserves each system more than their coordinated transfer. Modern language networks are more distributed than classic diagrams suggest, but this syndrome-to-tract association remains useful for examination and bedside localization. Repetition is the signature test because it requires rapid transmission between comprehension and speech output systems.'),
  ('2010-279', 'Personality disorders describe enduring patterns of inner experience and behavior that are inflexible, pervasive, and associated with impairment or distress. Paranoid personality disorder is organized around distrust: ambiguous actions are readily interpreted as demeaning, threatening, or exploitative. The distinction from delusional disorder is one of severity and fixity; personality-based suspiciousness does not necessarily reach delusional conviction. A memory cue is that the central problem is not simply anger, but the assumption that others harbor harmful motives.'),
  ('2010-280', 'Toluene is a volatile organic solvent found in numerous industrial and household products. Inhalation produces rapid intoxication because the chemical is highly lipophilic, but repeated exposure can injure white matter and the cerebellum. Inhalants are often missed in routine histories because they may be perceived as household products rather than drugs. The memorable imaging connection is that chronic solvent exposure can leave a diffuse white-matter and cerebellar signature.'),
  ('2010-281', 'Subarachnoid hemorrhage has an important delayed phase: patients may initially stabilize yet develop ischemic deficits as cerebral arteries constrict. Vasospasm is most associated with aneurysmal bleeding and generally emerges days after hemorrhage rather than immediately. It is a useful timeline diagnosis: sudden severe headache suggests the initial bleed, while delayed focal deficits suggest arterial narrowing and ischemia. Fluctuation reflects changing cerebral perfusion in vulnerable vascular territories.'),
  ('2010-282', 'Self-help groups are organized around mutual aid rather than a professional therapist delivering formal treatment. Their therapeutic power often comes from shared identification, modeling, repeated contact, and a community that supports behavior change between clinical encounters. They can address many problems, including substance use, grief, chronic illness, and caregiving. A simple memory link is that self-help is practical and peer-driven: members help one another do something different, not merely understand themselves differently.'),
  ('2010-283', 'Dissociation involves disruption in the usual integration of consciousness, memory, identity, perception, or behavior. Trance states illustrate the attentional side of dissociation: awareness becomes narrowly organized around one focus while ordinary surroundings recede. Similar experiences can be culturally sanctioned, spiritually meaningful, distressing, or clinically impairing depending on context. The diagnostic task is not to pathologize every absorbed state, but to determine whether it is involuntary, disruptive, unsafe, or part of another medical or psychiatric condition.'),
  ('2010-284', 'Sleep architecture changes across the lifespan. Deep slow-wave sleep is especially prominent in childhood and declines with aging; REM sleep also does not increase with normal aging. Older adults often have more awakenings and more time awake after initially falling asleep, termed wake after sleep onset. This is distinct from insomnia caused by a specific disorder, although medical and psychiatric conditions can worsen fragmentation. A useful memory phrase is that sleep in later life is often lighter and more interrupted, rather than simply shorter.'),
  ('2010-285', 'Professional ethics around fees emphasize transparent financial arrangements and avoidance of exploitation. A missed appointment represents reserved clinical time that cannot always be offered to another patient, so a cancellation policy can be legitimate. The critical ethical issue is not the existence of a fee but whether it is disclosed and administered fairly. This is a useful boundary lesson: clarity at the beginning of treatment protects both patient autonomy and the therapeutic relationship.'),
  ('2010-286', 'Central pontine myelinolysis is the traditional term for osmotic demyelination centered in the pons; lesions can also occur outside the pons. The pons contains compact long motor tracts, making dysarthria, dysphagia, and quadriparesis characteristic findings. The focal injury is related to osmotic stress during a major change in serum tonicity, not simply to the low sodium concentration itself. Symptoms may occur after an interval of apparent improvement following treatment of hyponatremia.'),
  ('2010-287', 'Cultural formulation provides a structured way to understand how identity, social context, illness meaning, coping, and the clinician-patient relationship shape psychiatric care. An explanatory model is not simply a belief to correct; it is a map of how the patient understands suffering and what treatment will seem credible. Open-ended questions about meaning can uncover values and supports that standard symptom checklists miss. The memory cue is: before explaining the illness to the patient, learn how the patient explains it.'),
  ('2010-288', 'Consultation-liaison psychiatry contains two linked roles. Consultation is patient-specific: evaluate, formulate, and recommend treatment for a referred patient. Liaison is the bridge-building role: teach other clinicians, improve communication, and integrate psychological and social understanding into medical care. It can also help teams recognize how their own responses affect difficult clinical encounters. A useful memory cue is that a liaison is a connector who links psychiatric knowledge to the everyday work of medical and surgical teams.'),
  ('2010-289', 'Carbamazepine is an aromatic anticonvulsant used in neurology and psychiatry. Its adverse-effect profile includes both routine monitoring issues and uncommon high-consequence reactions. For examination purposes, connect an early maculopapular rash with carbamazepine, then separately remember that mucosal involvement, systemic illness, blistering, or skin pain raises concern for a severe drug reaction rather than a simple exanthem. The timing, distribution, associated symptoms, and medication history all matter when evaluating a rash.'),
  ('2010-290', 'ADHD includes inattention and hyperactivity-impulsivity symptom domains. Development often changes the outward form of symptoms: a child may be unable to remain seated, whereas an adult may appear still but repeatedly fail to plan, prioritize, and finish tasks. A useful memory phrase is that movement may become internal while executive-attention problems remain evident in daily outcomes. Adult ADHD assessment should therefore explore organization, sustained effort, forgetfulness, and time management, not only visible motor activity.'),
  ('2010-291', 'Bell''s palsy is a peripheral facial-nerve syndrome. The facial nerve supplies the muscles of facial expression and stapedius and contributes to lacrimation and taste; this explains why facial weakness may occur with hyperacusis and tearing changes. Patients may describe a subjective facial numbness despite normal sensory testing. The classic localization shortcut is forehead involvement: a peripheral facial palsy affects the entire side of the face, whereas many central facial pareses preferentially affect the lower face.'),
  ('2010-292', 'Topographical literally suggests mapping places or regions. This makes it a useful mnemonic for Freud''s map of where mental contents lie in relation to awareness: conscious, preconscious, and unconscious. By contrast, the structural model names functional agencies—id, ego, and superego. The models are related historical psychoanalytic concepts but answer different questions. Keeping map versus agencies separate prevents a common examination-level mix-up and clarifies why the topographical model concerns accessibility to awareness.'),
  ('2010-293', 'The MCMI was developed as a multiaxial personality inventory, reflecting an era when personality disorders and clinical syndromes were formally separated in diagnostic organization. Its name is a useful memory cue: multiaxial inventory points toward personality-pattern assessment. In contrast, the Wisconsin Card Sort Test is best remembered as a measure of cognitive flexibility and frontal-executive functioning. Psychological testing may add structured information, but it cannot substitute for history, functional assessment, and diagnostic judgment.'),
  ('2010-294', 'Acute treatment aims for remission of the current episode. Continuation treatment protects that remission from relapse, and maintenance treatment seeks to reduce future recurrence. A useful pharmacologic memory rule is same dose, same protection: the dose that treated the episode is usually retained during continuation or maintenance rather than replaced with a reduced maintenance dose. Individual duration and medication choices should still account for previous episodes, residual symptoms, tolerability, medical factors, and patient preferences.'),
  ('2010-295', 'Vomiting removes gastric acid and chloride, tending toward metabolic alkalosis; volume depletion and renal compensation can further promote potassium loss. This creates a memorable pattern for recurrent vomiting: low chloride, elevated bicarbonate, and low potassium. Laxative misuse may produce a different acid-base pattern because of gastrointestinal bicarbonate loss, but hypokalemia remains an important shared risk across purging behaviors. The laboratory picture must therefore be interpreted with the reported behavior and clinical volume status.'),
  ('2010-296', 'Neurofibromatosis type 1 is a neurocutaneous tumor-predisposition disorder with recognizable skin, peripheral-nerve, ocular, and developmental manifestations. Café-au-lait macules often appear early, neurofibromas emerge over time, and Lisch nodules are iris hamartomas. The accepted gene symbol is NF1, not the OCR-corrupted “NEF-1” found in the source. Because the official answer-key page was missing, C is disclosed as the best answer inferred from the defining clinical findings rather than an official recovered key.'),
  ('2010-297', 'Rotter''s theory connects what a person expects, how much an outcome matters, and what behavior is likely to occur. Locus of control asks where a person locates the causes of reinforcement: internally, in one''s actions, or externally, in luck, fate, and powerful others. The concept is probabilistic rather than absolute. An internal orientation does not mean believing that a person controls every result, only that personal behavior can influence important outcomes.'),
  ('2010-298', 'Cognitive symptoms of depression were often called pseudodementia in older teaching traditions, but that term can oversimplify the overlap between mood and neurocognitive illness. A safer memory connection is that depression commonly slows mental tempo: thinking, responding, initiating, and shifting attention may all feel effortful. When mood recovers, this tempo can improve substantially. Persistent cognitive abnormalities after remission should not be assumed to be depressive because late-life depression and neurocognitive disorders may coexist.'),
  ('2010-299', 'Mental-status examination labels are easiest to remember by their target. Insight asks whether the patient understands what is happening to them, whereas judgment asks what the patient would do. Hypothetical scenarios sample social reasoning in a low-stakes setting. They are screening tools rather than direct tests of decision-making capacity, which requires assessment of the patient’s actual choice, understanding, appreciation, reasoning, and ability to communicate a choice. Actual behavior may diverge from a patient’s verbal response to a hypothetical question.'),
  ('2010-300', 'Frontotemporal dementia is a group of neurodegenerative syndromes involving frontal and anterior temporal networks. These networks support initiative, social judgment, inhibition, language, and emotional behavior, so illness may first appear psychiatric or interpersonal rather than amnestic. Frontal release reflexes are primitive reflexes that can reappear with frontal-system dysfunction and serve as a memorable localization clue. The behavioral syndrome is progressive and causes meaningful functional change, distinguishing it from an isolated personality style.'),
  ('2011-1', 'Bedside memory testing is often organized by time interval. Immediate recall is essentially registration: can the person take in and reproduce information right now? Recent memory adds a delay, and remote memory concerns more distant autobiographical or factual information. Digit span is also influenced by attention, which is why poor performance can be a useful clue to delirium or other attentional disturbance. Hearing, language, education, and the testing environment can also affect performance and should be considered.'),
  ('2011-2', 'The association between schizophrenia and tobacco smoking has long been striking in psychiatric settings. Potential contributors include shared vulnerability, social factors, reinforcement, and attempts to modulate symptoms or medication effects, but no single explanation applies to every patient. For this examination item, the key association is that nicotine is the most prevalent listed substance among people with schizophrenia. High prevalence does not make tobacco use benign; it substantially contributes to preventable medical morbidity and mortality.'),
  ('2011-3', 'The visual pathway crosses in a way that makes field loss easier to localize than eye anatomy alone. Information from the left visual field of both eyes travels to the right side of the brain after the optic chiasm. Thus, right brain, left field is a practical memory rule for posterior visual-pathway lesions, including right occipital infarction. A homonymous defect differs from monocular vision loss because it reflects damage after visual information from the two eyes has converged.'),
  ('2011-4', 'Ethnicity is best understood as a lived social identity rather than a fixed biological category. It may include ancestry, customs, language, religion, history, and shared experience, but its meaning varies across people and settings. A concise memory contrast is that ethnicity emphasizes cultural-historical belonging, whereas race is a social classification often tied to perceived physical difference. Because identities can be multiple, changing, and context-dependent, clinical assessment should use respectful self-identification rather than assumptions.'),
  ('2011-5', 'Pregnancy can alter both the consequences and the pattern of interpersonal violence. The pregnant abdomen may be deliberately targeted, and injury can have obstetric consequences even when external findings seem minor. Patients may minimize or conceal abuse because of fear, surveillance, financial dependence, or concern about child custody. Clinicians should distinguish routine private screening from interrogation, use trauma-informed language, and connect a positive screen to practical safety resources. The exam association is pregnancy-related violence plus abdominal trauma risk.'),
  ('2011-6', 'ECT is commonly divided into an acute treatment phase and a post-remission phase. Acute ECT aims to achieve response or remission; continuation treatment aims to prevent early return of the same episode. The term maintenance ECT is sometimes used broadly, although it may specifically describe longer-term treatment after continuation care. The key testing distinction is response versus durable relapse prevention: a patient who improves after acute ECT still needs an active plan rather than simple observation.'),
  ('2011-7', 'Whipple disease is an uncommon multisystem infection that can involve the gastrointestinal tract, joints, lymph nodes, skin, heart, and central nervous system. Its malabsorptive features include weight loss, anemia, and fatty stools. A useful teaching pattern is arthralgia preceding or accompanying gastrointestinal symptoms, followed by neurologic or psychiatric manifestations in some patients. PAS-positive macrophages are a classic histologic clue, but diagnostic interpretation must be integrated with clinical findings and organism-specific testing when available.'),
  ('2011-8', 'Interview interventions differ in how much new meaning the clinician adds. Clarification organizes or restates a patient’s communication while preserving the patient’s perspective. Interpretation goes further by proposing an inferred conflict, wish, or defense. Confrontation highlights a discrepancy between statements, behavior, or feelings. Redirection intentionally shifts attention to another topic. The practical cue is that clarification makes what was said more precise rather than explaining why it was said.'),
  ('2011-9', 'Psychiatric classification has often prioritized reliability: different clinicians should be able to identify and communicate about similar clinical syndromes. Reliability differs from validity, which asks whether a category corresponds to a distinct underlying disease process. DSM-IV used review of available research, reanalysis of data, and field testing to refine operational criteria. The historical memory contrast is descriptive criteria developed through empirical revision, not diagnoses derived from presumed causes.'),
  ('2011-10', 'The former DSM-IV distinction between autistic disorder and Asperger disorder relied heavily on developmental language history. Both diagnoses included social impairment and restricted or repetitive patterns of behavior or interests. Recognition of extensive overlap and variable developmental profiles contributed to their later consolidation into autism spectrum disorder. The board-style association is explicitly historical: language delay separated the old categories, whereas current assessment describes a dimensional developmental profile and support needs.'),
  ('2011-11', 'Light therapy is often associated with seasonal depression because light exposure influences circadian timing and sleep-wake regulation. Bipolar illness adds a key caution: an intervention that improves depression can sometimes shift mood too far upward. The appropriate teaching point is cautious, monitored use rather than universal prohibition. A useful memory cue is that light can lift mood, but in bipolar illness the degree and timing of that lift require close observation.'),
  ('2011-12', 'Executive function refers to goal-directed cognitive control, including planning, inhibition, monitoring, initiation, and mental set shifting. In card sorting, the participant must infer the current sorting rule from feedback and then adapt when the rule changes. Perseveration, or continuing to apply an old rule despite corrective feedback, is a memorable example of impaired flexibility. This makes the WCST a classic teaching measure for executive-function assessment within a broader neuropsychological evaluation.'),
  ('2011-13', 'The postpartum period brings major hormonal, sleep, role, and psychosocial transitions, making transient mood lability common. Maternity blues is appropriate only when symptoms are mild, short-lived, and not associated with major functional decline or loss of reality testing. The key clinical contrast is early, transient tearfulness versus a sustained depressive episode or postpartum psychosis. Because symptoms can evolve, reassurance should be paired with screening, support assessment, and a clear plan for follow-up.'),
  ('2011-14', 'Syphilis can produce diverse neurologic and psychiatric presentations. Meningovascular involvement causes inflammatory vasculopathy that can result in ischemic stroke, while other forms of CNS disease can impair cognition, mood, or behavior. Positive serology alone does not establish every neurologic diagnosis, but the combination of neurologic findings, inflammatory CSF abnormalities, and positive reagin testing makes neurosyphilis the unifying diagnosis in this vignette. The treatment association is penicillin with adequate CNS penetration.'),
  ('2011-15', 'Factitious disorder and malingering are distinguished primarily by motivation, not by whether symptoms are fabricated. Factitious disorder centers on assuming the patient role without an obvious external reward. Malingering centers on external incentives. In practice, motivation can be difficult to establish directly, and clinicians must avoid overinterpreting incomplete records or atypical illness behavior. The board-style contrast is internal sick-role motivation versus tangible external incentive, with appropriate ongoing medical assessment in either situation.'),
  ('2011-16', 'Freud’s psychosexual stages are a historical framework that organizes development around changing bodily interests and relational conflicts. The phallic stage is linked to the Oedipal triangle: child, one parent, and the other parent understood as part of a parental couple. Later stages include latency and genital organization. Although this framework is not a current evidence-based developmental classification, its vocabulary persists in older psychoanalytic writing and board-style questions. The tested association is parental-couple awareness with the phallic stage.'),
  ('2011-17', 'DLB is a synucleinopathy that overlaps clinically and pathologically with Parkinson disease. A memorable pattern is fluctuating cognition, recurrent visual hallucinations, spontaneous parkinsonism, and REM sleep behavior disorder. Cognitive fluctuations can resemble delirium, but the longitudinal syndrome helps distinguish them. The overlap with Parkinson disease explains why dopaminergic treatment can improve motor features without making idiopathic Parkinson disease the best overall diagnosis. Hallucinations and medication sensitivity are particularly important treatment clues in this disorder.'),
  ('2011-18', 'Attachment theory emphasizes an infant’s use of a trusted caregiver as a secure base for exploration and comfort under stress. The central construct is sensitivity: noticing the child’s signals, interpreting them reasonably, and responding in a timely, appropriate way. This is more informative than whether a caregiver is biologically related, employed, or new to parenting. Secure attachment does not require perfect caregiving; it is supported by an overall pattern of responsive, reliable care and repair after ordinary mismatches.'),
  ('2011-19', 'Suicide risk is dynamic and reflects vulnerability, stressors, intent, opportunity, and the lethality of available means. Means safety recognizes that suicidal crises can be brief and ambivalent. Firearms are especially important to assess because attempts with highly lethal methods allow little opportunity for rescue or reconsideration. Access therefore matters clinically beyond psychiatric diagnosis alone. A risk assessment should be individualized and should avoid assuming that any one diagnosis or demographic characteristic determines imminent danger.'),
  ('2011-20', 'Sexual response is shaped by physiology, attention, learning, safety, relationship context, and communication. Directed masturbation is a behavioral technique that can help identify effective stimulation and increase self-knowledge. Couples therapy addresses the interpersonal setting in which partnered difficulty occurs, including communication, expectations, and performance anxiety. A useful memory contrast is skill acquisition individually and communication or application together. Assessment should remain nonjudgmental and should include medication effects, pain, trauma, and relationship safety.'),
  ('2011-21', 'Maltreatment categories organize assessment but can overlap. Neglect may be physical, medical, educational, emotional, or supervisory, and it is often chronic rather than a single visible event. This can make it less immediately apparent than an acute injury. A useful memory cue is that the most common maltreatment concern is often an omission: needed care, supervision, or protection is not provided. Evaluators must distinguish unmet needs caused by remediable structural barriers from circumstances that place the child at risk.'),
  ('2011-22', 'Cluster headache is named for attacks occurring in clusters over time. Tearing, nasal congestion, and other ipsilateral autonomic signs reflect trigeminal-autonomic activation. Board questions often distinguish acute abortive treatment from preventive therapy: oxygen is used during an attack, whereas lithium may be used preventively in selected cases. Attack duration and frequency also help distinguish cluster headache from other unilateral headache syndromes. The practical cue is simple: cluster attack, oxygen now.'),
  ('2011-23', 'Acetaminophen is usually cleared through glucuronidation and sulfation. In overdose, a greater proportion is converted to the reactive metabolite NAPQI; when glutathione is depleted, hepatocellular injury can follow. NAC replenishes protective sulfhydryl capacity and is most effective when started early. The Rumack–Matthew nomogram applies to a single acute ingestion with a known time, not repeated supratherapeutic exposure or an unknown timeline. The reported dose raises concern, but the timed serum concentration determines how to apply the nomogram and whether antidotal treatment is indicated.'),
  ('2011-24', 'Operant conditioning classifies consequences according to whether they add or remove something and whether they increase or decrease behavior. Negative reinforcement is frequently confused with punishment, but the concepts have opposite effects on behavior: reinforcement strengthens behavior, whereas punishment weakens it. In body-focused repetitive behaviors, a sensory effect or reduction in tension may immediately reinforce the act even when delayed shame or impairment follows. Functional analysis focuses on this sequence of antecedent, behavior, and consequence. A useful memory cue is that relief reinforces the behavior that produced it.'),
  ('2011-25', 'ECT induces a generalized seizure under anesthesia and neuromuscular blockade. It can produce rapid benefit in severe mood illness, but cognitive effects are an important treatment tradeoff. Retrograde amnesia involves loss of memories formed before treatment, whereas anterograde impairment involves difficulty forming new memories afterward. Treatment frequency, electrode placement, pulse width, and stimulus dose all affect cognitive burden. Bilateral approaches generally carry greater memory risk than unilateral approaches. ECT planning therefore requires repeated assessment of both therapeutic response and cognitive tolerability throughout the treatment course.'),
  ('2011-26', 'Thought-process terms describe the form and flow of communication, whereas thought content concerns beliefs, worries, and themes. Tangentiality and circumstantiality both include excess material, but their endpoints differ. Circumstantial speech eventually reaches the requested answer; tangential speech does not. Flight of ideas is characterized by rapid movement across topics, usually with discernible associations, while pressured speech refers to rate and interruptibility. Using these terms precisely makes the mental-status examination clearer and avoids prematurely assigning a diagnosis based only on a communication pattern.'),
  ('2011-27', 'The ulnar nerve travels behind the medial epicondyle through the cubital tunnel, making prolonged elbow flexion or external pressure clinically relevant. Its sensory territory includes the small finger and the ulnar side of the ring finger. The intrinsic hand muscles it innervates coordinate fine finger movements, so patients may notice clumsiness before obvious weakness. The “funny bone” sensation is a memorable link to ulnar nerve irritation at the elbow, although persistent neuropathy requires formal assessment.'),
  ('2011-28', 'Mahler’s separation–individuation framework is a psychoanalytic account of the gradual emergence of a separate sense of self in relation to a primary caregiver. It divides early development into named phases, including differentiation, practicing, and rapprochement. Rapprochement is memorable because it literally suggests coming close again after increased exploration. The theory describes an approach–withdrawal pattern: the toddler can move outward independently but seeks reassurance that the caregiver remains available. It remains relevant to the historical terminology tested by this question, although it is not a validated contemporary diagnostic staging system.'),
  ('2011-29', 'Thiamine is required for enzymes central to carbohydrate metabolism. Alcohol use disorder can combine poor nutritional intake, impaired absorption, and reduced storage, increasing vulnerability to deficiency. Wernicke encephalopathy is classically taught as confusion, oculomotor dysfunction, and ataxia, but all three findings need not be present. Clinicians therefore maintain a low threshold to give parenteral thiamine when it is suspected. Untreated disease can progress to chronic amnestic impairment, often associated with confabulation. The key emergency principle is prompt replacement while evaluating other potentially serious causes of altered mental status.'),
  ('2011-30', 'OCD and tic disorders show an important subgroup overlap, particularly in childhood-onset presentations. Tics are typically brief motor movements or vocalizations that may be preceded by a premonitory urge. Compulsions are usually more rule-bound acts performed to reduce obsessional distress or avert a feared event. Both can be repetitive, so careful phenomenology is essential. Their familial association supports shared vulnerability in some families but is not a universal rule for all patients with OCD. The board-style association tested here is tic disorders, not merely any psychiatric comorbidity.'),
  ('2011-31', 'Alzheimer pharmacotherapy is often remembered as two mechanism families. The cholinergic group includes acetylcholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil, galantamine, rivastigmine, and tacrine. Memantine represents glutamatergic modulation through NMDA receptor antagonism. NMDA receptors participate in excitatory neurotransmission and learning, but excessive tonic activation may contribute to excitotoxic injury. Mechanism questions therefore separate memantine from the cholinesterase inhibitors even when several of the medications may appear on the same medication list. A useful mnemonic is that memantine modifies NMDA signaling.'),
  ('2011-32', 'Children’s emotional vocabulary and capacity for abstract self-description are still developing. Mood symptoms may therefore be communicated through behavior, irritability, school changes, or bodily discomfort. These symptoms are real and deserve both medical and psychiatric consideration; they should never be dismissed as fabricated. Depression in children can also involve loss of interest, sleep or appetite change, guilt, concentration difficulty, and suicidal thinking. Developmentally sensitive interviewing uses concrete examples and information from caregivers and school. A helpful memory link is that younger children may show distress through body and behavior before they can fully name it.'),
  ('2011-33', 'Niacin, also called vitamin B3, is a precursor of NAD and NADP, cofactors used in cellular energy reactions. Deficiency is remembered by the three Ds: dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia; severe untreated disease may be fatal. The dermatitis is often photosensitive, providing a useful diagnostic clue. Pellagra can produce psychiatric symptoms, apathy, irritability, cognitive impairment, or psychosis, so it may resemble a primary mental disorder. Recognition depends on integrating the dermatologic and neuropsychiatric presentation with nutritional and medical history rather than treating either finding in isolation.'),
  ('2011-34', 'Mutual-help organizations rely heavily on peer identification, shared experience, and voluntary participation. In addiction care, referral is most effective when it is collaborative: clinicians clarify barriers, provide a credible bridge, and retain the patient’s agency. A current member can describe what happens at meetings and address practical fears without the psychiatrist becoming personally involved in attendance. AA is not the only pathway to recovery, and refusal to attend should not jeopardize access to psychiatric treatment. The central professional-boundary principle is to introduce and support, rather than compel.'),
  ('2011-35', 'Reading develops through interaction among language skills, instruction, practice, attention, and sensory access. A home reading history is informative because it describes both the child’s opportunity to practice and specific points at which reading becomes difficult. Caregiver observations may reveal problems with letter-sound decoding, fluency, comprehension, avoidance, or frustration. This history is not a moral measure of parenting and cannot diagnose a learning disorder by itself. It must be integrated with school performance, developmental history, hearing and vision assessment, and formal educational testing when indicated.'),
  ('2011-36', 'ACT was developed as an alternative to office-based care for people whose illness and social needs make traditional referral models insufficient. Its defining feature is that a multidisciplinary team brings coordinated care to the patient rather than waiting for the patient to navigate fragmented systems. ACT commonly integrates psychiatric treatment, rehabilitation, case management, medication support, housing coordination, and practical assistance. It is therefore more than individual psychotherapy or routine case management. The model is best matched to persistent high-acuity needs, recurrent hospitalization, poor treatment engagement, and impaired community functioning.'),
  ('2011-37', 'Tremor is an involuntary rhythmic oscillation of a body part. Essential tremor is often familial and is most apparent with posture or voluntary action, unlike the classic resting tremor of Parkinson disease. It may become more visible with stress, fatigue, or stimulants and can interfere with eating, writing, and social confidence. Parkinsonism is suggested by bradykinesia, rigidity, gait change, and resting tremor, although presentations vary. Medication review is important because several drugs can cause or worsen tremor. A useful cue is that essential tremor appears during execution of action.'),
  ('2011-38', 'Frontotemporal dementia is a clinical-pathologic umbrella for neurodegenerative syndromes that often affect behavior, executive function, or language early in the illness. Protein-based categories connect syndromes to mechanisms: tau, amyloid, synuclein, and huntingtin each point toward different disease families. Tau normally stabilizes neuronal microtubules. When tau misfolds and accumulates inside neurons, it becomes a pathologic hallmark in several neurodegenerative disorders. The chromosome 17-linked form tested here is historically associated with tau pathology and may include parkinsonian features in affected families.'),
  ('2011-39', 'Chronic pain is shaped by sensory input, attention, mood, expectations, behavior, and social context; this does not mean that pain is imaginary. CBT uses a biopsychosocial formulation to identify modifiable contributors to disability. Pain intensity and functional impairment are related but not identical: avoidance may enlarge disability, whereas paced engagement can restore agency. In a group setting, patients observe shared patterns, rehearse skills, and receive feedback from peers with similar limitations. The central therapeutic target is effective living in the presence of symptoms.'),
  ('2011-40', 'Alprazolam is a short-acting benzodiazepine with misuse potential, particularly when it is sought for rapid relief. Examination questions may signal possible substance misuse through a cluster of features: a requested controlled medication, dose escalation, vague chronology, and mismatch between reported severity and observed behavior. No single feature establishes a diagnosis. The enduring clinical lesson is to distinguish a symptom complaint from the longitudinal evidence needed to diagnose panic disorder or a substance-related disorder, while maintaining a nonjudgmental and safety-focused assessment.'),
  ('2011-41', 'Cognitive models distinguish events from interpretations. Two people may experience the same setback, but one may view it as a limited disappointment while another treats it as proof of global personal failure. The latter appraisal reflects a negative self-schema expressed through automatic thoughts. A useful memory structure is Beck’s cognitive triad: negative views of the self, the world, and the future. Feelings of devaluation fit most directly into the negative-self component and can sustain depressed mood and behavioral withdrawal.'),
  ('2011-42', 'The older term somatization has been replaced by more descriptive diagnostic language, but the care principle remains stable: continuity is therapeutic. Regular visits create a reliable setting to evaluate symptoms and reduce pressure for repeated emergency testing or scattered referrals. This approach avoids two unhelpful extremes: dismissing symptoms and pursuing indiscriminate medical escalation. It also permits clinicians to remain alert for new findings that warrant evaluation. A useful shorthand is to validate, monitor, coordinate, and maintain function.'),
  ('2011-43', 'The memory aid “LOT” refers to lorazepam, oxazepam, and temazepam, benzodiazepines associated with glucuronidation rather than oxidative metabolism. This matters because several other benzodiazepines form active metabolites through hepatic oxidation and may have prolonged effects in hepatic dysfunction. Glucuronidation is not completely independent of liver function, but it is generally more reliable than oxidative metabolism in this setting. The relevant contrast is pharmacokinetic: direct conjugation and inactive metabolites versus oxidation and potentially accumulating active metabolites.'),
  ('2011-44', 'Attachment figures normally provide a child with a secure base from which to explore. Separation anxiety disorder reflects an exaggerated alarm response when that base is unavailable or threatened. The key is not simply preferring to remain near a parent, since many anxious children seek reassurance. A useful diagnostic question is, “What catastrophe does the child expect if separation occurs?” Fear that the child or caregiver will be harmed points toward separation anxiety, especially when the fear leads to avoidance and functional impairment.'),
  ('2011-45', '“Transverse” refers to dysfunction across the width of a spinal-cord segment, producing deficits below a recognizable level. Localization follows the pattern of involved tracts: dorsal-column dysfunction impairs vibration and position sense, corticospinal dysfunction causes weakness and hyperreflexia, and autonomic pathway dysfunction affects bladder control. This differs from peripheral neuropathy, where symptoms are commonly length-dependent and reflexes tend to be reduced. A sensory level is an especially important clue because it strongly directs the examiner toward spinal-cord localization rather than a primary peripheral nerve disorder.'),
  ('2011-46', 'Infant development is not a one-way process in which caregivers act and infants merely receive. Newborns have immature but meaningful abilities to detect socially relevant stimuli, including faces, voices, touch, rhythm, and contingency. These abilities make early reciprocal regulation possible and support attachment formation. A useful memory phrase is that the infant is prepared for relationship: development refines these capacities, but the groundwork for engaging caregivers is present from birth. This perspective emphasizes active infant participation without overstating newborn abilities as fully mature social understanding.'),
  ('2011-47', 'A cohort is a group linked by a shared defining feature or starting point. In epidemiology, cohort studies may be prospective or retrospective, but their essential structure compares outcomes over time among people grouped by an exposure or characteristic. Developmental research commonly uses cohorts because questions such as which early traits predict later outcomes require temporal order. Repeated measurement can show change across development and identify predictors, while also creating practical concerns about attrition and confounding. Remember: one defined group, observed over time, with later outcomes assessed.'),
  ('2011-48', 'Capgras syndrome belongs to the delusional misidentification syndromes. One proposed model is that visual recognition remains intact while the normal emotional sense of familiarity is disrupted, leading to an implausible explanation for the mismatch. This model does not itself establish a diagnosis or cause in an individual patient. The memorable contrast is that Capgras means a familiar face with a falsely assigned identity, whereas erotomania means a false belief that another person is in love with the patient.'),
  ('2011-49', 'Serotonin syndrome is a toxidrome caused by excess serotonergic activity, often after a drug combination or dose change. A helpful examination contrast is that clonus and hyperreflexia favor serotonin toxicity, whereas severe lead-pipe rigidity and hyporeflexia favor neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Presentations range from mild tremor and diaphoresis to severe hyperthermia and autonomic instability. Fluoxetine has a long persistence in the body, which can remain clinically relevant when serotonergic medications are changed or combined.'),
  ('2011-50', 'Elimination half-life is the time required for a drug concentration to decrease by half. A longer half-life generally produces a smoother decline after a missed dose, while a short half-life can make concentration changes more abrupt. This pharmacokinetic property can influence discontinuation effects and dosing practicality, but it does not determine efficacy. In multiple-choice questions, compare only the medications offered. A common memory trap is recalling fluoxetine’s unusually long persistence even when fluoxetine is absent from the options.'),
  ('2011-51', 'Ethanol metabolism proceeds from ethanol to acetaldehyde and then to acetate. The second step depends on aldehyde dehydrogenase. The flushing reaction associated with reduced enzyme activity provides the mechanistic analogy for disulfiram. This is an aversive strategy: it changes the immediate consequence of drinking rather than directly treating craving or alcohol withdrawal. The key sequence is alcohol to acetaldehyde to acetate; blocking the final conversion causes acetaldehyde to rise and produces the disulfiram-alcohol reaction.'),
  ('2011-52', 'Parkinsonism is defined clinically by bradykinesia together with rest tremor, rigidity, or both. Parkinson disease often starts on one side because dopaminergic circuit dysfunction is initially asymmetric. Reduced arm swing is an early and easily observed clue that reflects reduced automatic movement rather than simple weakness. Atypical parkinsonian disorders may progress more quickly and can feature early falls, gaze abnormalities, marked autonomic failure, pyramidal signs, or poor levodopa response. Those atypical features guide further diagnostic evaluation but do not override the pattern presented here.'),
  ('2011-53', 'Moral development includes emotion, behavior, relationships, and reasoning. In early childhood, rules are commonly experienced as externally imposed by caregivers and authority figures. Over time, children increasingly understand social expectations and adopt them as personal standards. Internalization means behavior is guided by those standards even when immediate monitoring, reward, or punishment is absent. This concept links developmental psychology with clinical questions about conscience, rule-following, self-control, and the meaning of developmentally appropriate behavior. It does not mean that children stop needing support, structure, or adult guidance.'),
  ('2011-54', 'Automatic thoughts are rapid interpretations that can feel like facts. Catastrophizing is a cognitive distortion in which a negative event or uncertainty is projected into an extreme, irreversible outcome. CBT does not merely replace negative thoughts with positive ones. Instead, it uses collaborative empiricism to ask whether an interpretation is accurate, complete, and useful. Listing evidence for and against a thought makes this process explicit. The resulting alternative thought should be balanced and testable, not unrealistically reassuring or dismissive of the person’s distress.'),
  ('2011-55', 'Antipsychotic-induced movement syndromes can be organized partly by timing. Acute dystonia commonly occurs early after starting or increasing a dopamine-blocking medication, whereas tardive syndromes generally develop after more prolonged exposure. In the striatum, dopamine and acetylcholine have partly opposing effects on motor output. D2 blockade can leave cholinergic activity relatively unopposed, and an antimuscarinic drug restores functional balance. This mechanism does not mean anticholinergics are appropriate treatment for every extrapyramidal syndrome.'),
  ('2011-56', 'The two principal stimulant classes have overlapping clinical effects but differ pharmacologically. Methylphenidate mainly blocks catecholamine reuptake, whereas amphetamines also promote catecholamine release. ADHD prescribing is therefore an iterative, measurement-based process: establish an adequate trial, assess benefit and adverse effects across settings, then switch classes if needed. A reported stimulant “failure” should mean failure of a well-conducted trial rather than a brief, underdosed, inconsistently taken, or poorly monitored exposure.'),
  ('2011-57', 'Negative symptoms include diminished expression and avolition, but these constructs are not identical. A helpful contrast is “liking versus wanting”: someone may retain the capacity to enjoy a rewarding event while having reduced expectation of reward or drive to pursue it. Social activities demand initiation, prediction, and reciprocal interaction, making social interest a particularly informative domain for evaluating negative symptoms. A careful interview separates primary negative symptoms from depression, medication adverse effects, active psychosis, and social deprivation.'),
  ('2011-58', 'Acetylcholinesterase normally terminates acetylcholine signaling. Inhibiting it amplifies parasympathetic-type effects, including increased secretions, increased gut motility, and slower heart rate. The same mechanism used to support cholinergic transmission in cognitive disorders can produce troublesome peripheral effects. Linking the drug class to a cholinergic toxidrome makes its adverse-effect profile easier to recall. Clinical monitoring should include gastrointestinal tolerance and weight as well as pulse, dizziness, and syncope symptoms.'),
  ('2011-59', 'Opsoclonus consists of rapid, chaotic, conjugate eye movements in multiple directions without normal pauses between saccades. It may occur with myoclonus and cerebellar dysfunction, producing an opsoclonus-myoclonus-ataxia syndrome. Cancer associations vary by age: neuroblastoma is the memorable childhood association, whereas adult cases have different paraneoplastic associations. Anti-Ri antibodies can be associated with some breast-cancer-related cases, but neither a negative nor positive antibody result alone replaces a clinically directed neurologic and malignancy evaluation.'),
  ('2011-60', 'Attachment theory describes the caregiver as both a safe haven during distress and a secure base for exploration. Secure attachment does not mean that a child never becomes upset; it means the child can seek, receive, and use comfort effectively. As the preschool social world expands, this regulatory foundation may be reflected in cooperation, empathy, and helpful behavior toward others. Attachment is assessed from patterns of behavior and relationships over time, not from one isolated act of dependence or independence.'),
  ('2011-61', 'Prescription drug abuse is historical wording; contemporary practice generally uses nonmedical use, misuse, and substance use disorder. Survey categories often separate pain relievers from stimulants and sedatives or tranquilizers. Population prevalence statistics do not directly determine individual clinical risk, which depends on dose, route, co-ingestants, medical vulnerability, and patterns of use. Pain relievers, especially opioid analgesics, remain clinically important because misuse can carry substantial risk, but historical survey rankings should not substitute for a current patient assessment.'),
  ('2011-62', 'Forensic ethics differ from ordinary clinical advocacy because the evaluator’s duty is to an accurate, impartial assessment for the legal process. Custody disputes are vulnerable to selective histories, adversarial framing, and substantial emotional pressure. The more consequential a recommendation, the more transparent and balanced the information-gathering process should be. Collateral records can add important information, but they do not replace direct assessment of the central parties when that assessment is feasible and relevant to the referral question.'),
  ('2011-63', '“Atypical” is a historical label, not a statement that the presentation is rare. The easiest contrast is atypical versus melancholic features: atypical features include mood reactivity and reversed vegetative symptoms, whereas melancholic features emphasize nonreactive mood and classic vegetative change. Leaden paralysis belongs in the atypical-features cluster, but it describes a bodily sensation rather than the central mood descriptor. The specifier is clinically useful only after establishing the underlying depressive episode and considering alternatives.'),
  ('2011-64', 'Behavioral enuresis treatment uses repeated learning around bladder sensation, awakening, and toileting. Its strength is not necessarily rapid suppression of symptoms; it is the opportunity for a learned change that persists beyond the intervention. Desmopressin reduces nighttime urine production while it is taken, which explains both its useful short-term effect and the possibility of recurrence after medication stops. Treatment selection should consider the family’s practical capacity, the child’s motivation, and the desired time horizon for symptom control.'),
  ('2011-65', 'Subcortical cognitive syndromes commonly feature slowed thinking, impaired attention and executive function, apathy, and gait or motor changes more prominently than aphasia or focal cortical deficits. Leukoencephalopathy refers to disease affecting cerebral white matter. Radiation effects may be acute, early delayed, or late delayed, so linking a progressive cognitive syndrome to prior treatment requires attention to the interval since exposure. The syndrome remains a clinical diagnosis requiring evaluation for competing or coexisting neurologic causes.'),
  ('2011-66', 'Lamotrigine is cleared predominantly through glucuronidation. A useful memory pair is “valproate inhibits; carbamazepine induces.” Inhibition means reduced metabolic clearance and higher exposure, whereas induction means greater clearance and lower exposure. This interaction illustrates why drug half-life and dose requirements are properties of a regimen, not only of one medication considered in isolation. Dose changes, additions, and discontinuations can all alter lamotrigine exposure and the associated risk of adverse effects.'),
  ('2011-67', 'Neuroanatomic localization is easier when symptoms are grouped. The lateral medulla contains vestibular nuclei, spinocerebellar pathways, trigeminal sensory pathways, sympathetic fibers, and nucleus ambiguus-related structures. The syndrome therefore combines vertigo and ataxia with ipsilateral facial findings and other crossed neurologic deficits. Hiccups are a memorable, though less universal, clue to medullary involvement. The absence of prominent weakness does not exclude a dangerous posterior-circulation stroke, particularly when gait and cranial-nerve signs are present.'),
  ('2011-68', 'An advance directive expresses autonomy and is intended to guide future care if a person later cannot decide. Capacity is task-specific and can fluctuate; it is not synonymous with diagnosis, age, or hospitalization. The ethical logic is prospective self-determination: a patient’s contemporaneous capable preferences should guide planning before a crisis narrows options. Conversations are often most useful when they focus on values, acceptable outcomes, and the person who should speak for the patient, rather than only on forms or isolated treatment choices.'),
  ('2011-69', 'Forensic questions are defined partly by their legal time frame. Competence to proceed is prospective and practical: can the person participate in the case now? Criminal responsibility is retrospective: what was the person’s mental state when the alleged act occurred? Keeping these questions separate prevents a common psychiatric-legal error. Treatment that improves current symptoms or cognition can restore competence, even though it cannot change the historical facts relevant to the alleged offense or a separate responsibility evaluation.'),
  ('2011-70', 'Activated charcoal works by adsorbing substances onto its large surface area in the gut. Important exceptions include poorly adsorbed metals and inorganic ions such as lithium. Lithium’s small inorganic nature explains why it behaves differently from many psychotropic overdoses. This pharmacokinetic fact does not justify delaying broader toxicology evaluation, serial laboratory testing, or assessment for coingestants. The need for enhanced elimination depends on the clinical presentation, kidney function, concentration pattern, and formulation rather than on one laboratory value alone.'),
  ('2011-71', 'Venlafaxine is an SNRI with dose-dependent pharmacologic effects. Serotonin reuptake inhibition predominates at lower doses, whereas norepinephrine reuptake inhibition becomes more clinically apparent with dose escalation. Increased noradrenergic activity provides a useful explanation for its potential to increase blood pressure. A practical contrast is that venlafaxine itself may raise blood pressure over treatment, while an MAOI combined with tyramine may trigger an acute hypertensive crisis. The distinction helps separate a medication adverse effect from a dangerous food or drug interaction.'),
  ('2011-72', 'The stages-of-change model describes readiness for behavior change rather than motivation as a fixed trait. Precontemplation involves little recognition of a problem; contemplation involves weighing change; preparation adds commitment and planning; action involves implementation; and maintenance involves sustaining gains. People may move back and forth between stages, especially with substance use. The central distinction in this vignette is between deciding to change and actually changing behavior. A firm commitment without sustained implementation is preparation, not action.'),
  ('2011-73', 'Temperament describes relatively stable patterns of emotional and behavioral response. In older psychiatric-genetics teaching, harm avoidance was linked to serotonin because inhibition, worry, and threat sensitivity were hypothesized to involve serotonergic signaling. Candidate-gene studies often sought one-to-one relationships between a trait and a selected gene. Contemporary genetics instead supports a more complex model in which many variants of small effect interact with development and environment. Thus, this is best retained as a historical board-style association rather than a deterministic rule.'),
  ('2011-74', 'Davanloo’s model is commonly called intensive short-term dynamic psychotherapy. “Intensive” refers mainly to the therapist’s active focus on resistance and defensive operations, rather than simply to frequent sessions. The model proposes that painful feelings and conflicts may be avoided through recognizable defenses such as intellectualization, distancing, or excessive rationalization. By making the defense immediate and observable within the session, the therapist seeks to facilitate contact with the underlying affect. This emphasis distinguishes the approach from less confrontational or primarily supportive psychodynamic work.'),
  ('2011-75', 'TCAs have several receptor and ion-channel actions that become dangerous in overdose. Muscarinic antagonism causes dryness, mydriasis, reduced gastrointestinal motility, and tachycardia. Cardiac sodium-channel blockade can impair conduction, classically widening the QRS complex and increasing dysrhythmia risk. This dual pattern distinguishes TCA poisoning from uncomplicated sedative intoxication. A toxidrome approach is useful: dry skin and dilated pupils point away from opioid toxicity, while cardiac conduction abnormalities identify a potentially lethal complication requiring urgent medical care.'),
  ('2011-76', 'Discontinuation syndrome is strongly influenced by pharmacokinetics. A short half-life allows medication exposure to fall rapidly, which helps explain why paroxetine is particularly associated with missed-dose symptoms. Fluoxetine persists for much longer because both the parent drug and active metabolite have long half-lives, creating a gradual self-taper. The syndrome is distinct from relapse of depression or anxiety, although clinical assessment should consider both. Timing, characteristic physical symptoms, and recent dose interruption help support the distinction.'),
  ('2011-77', 'The hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis proceeds from hypothalamic TRH to pituitary TSH to thyroid hormone production. Older neuroendocrine psychiatry used challenge tests to study possible physiologic differences in mood disorders. In historical teaching about depression, pituitary TSH release after administered TRH may be blunted. This does not mean that every depressed patient has this finding or that the finding establishes a psychiatric diagnosis. Remember it as an older exam association, separate from standard modern evaluation of mood symptoms and thyroid disease.'),
  ('2011-78', 'Levodopa crosses the blood-brain barrier and is converted to dopamine in the central nervous system, directly addressing striatal dopamine deficiency. Carbidopa has little central penetration and blocks peripheral levodopa conversion, improving delivery and reducing peripheral adverse effects. A helpful board-style rule is “older patient, choose levodopa.” Dopamine agonists may be considered in other circumstances but can produce sleepiness, hallucinations, and behavioral adverse effects. Anticholinergic agents are especially problematic when cognitive vulnerability or other anticholinergic burden is present.'),
  ('2011-79', 'Timed voiding is a behavioral and environmental continence intervention in which caregivers offer toileting on a regular schedule rather than waiting for a patient request. It is particularly useful when dementia, poor working memory, limited mobility, or communication difficulty interferes with independent continence. The approach reduces accidents by anticipating need rather than teaching the patient to suppress urgency. This differs from self-directed bladder training, which requires the person to recognize urges and progressively lengthen voiding intervals. The essential clinical principle is to adapt the care plan to cognitive capacity.'),
  ('2011-80', 'Childhood-onset schizophrenia is rare and has strong neurodevelopmental relevance. Early changes can appear nonspecific, including academic decline, social withdrawal, odd behavior, language difficulties, or impaired functioning, before hallucinations and delusions become clear. This makes the gradual onset a key distinction from many later-onset presentations. Earlier onset is generally associated with greater developmental burden and poorer long-term outcome. Because psychotic-like experiences in children have many possible causes, careful developmental assessment and a broad medical and psychiatric differential remain essential.'),
  ('2011-81', 'Freud distinguished the accessible surface of a dream from its proposed concealed meaning. Manifest content is what the person remembers and reports: scenes, images, people, and events. Latent content is the inferred unconscious material proposed to underlie that report. Dream work refers to the mental transformations said to convert latent content into manifest content. A simple mnemonic is that “manifest” means apparent or openly presented, so it names the dream narrative available to consciousness after waking.'),
  ('2011-82', 'Psychiatric malpractice is evaluated through professional duty and the applicable standard of care, not through perfect prediction of outcomes. Suicide-related claims are memorable because they often involve devastating harm and retrospective examination of clinical decisions made under uncertainty. The useful teaching point is clinical rather than merely legal: a clear formulation, thoughtful safety planning, communication among caregivers, and documentation of decision-making support continuity of care. These practices do not eliminate risk, but they make the clinical reasoning and plan visible.'),
  ('2011-83', 'CBT helps patients evaluate thoughts as hypotheses rather than unquestioned facts. Appropriate humor can function as a cognitive zoom-out, making a thought’s rigidity, exaggeration, or all-or-nothing quality easier to notice without attacking the person who has it. Timing, rapport, and patient preference are essential because humor can otherwise feel invalidating. It is an optional collaborative technique, not a substitute for empathy, evidence review, behavioral experiments, or attention to the patient’s actual problem.'),
  ('2011-84', 'Tuckman’s sequence is commonly remembered as forming, storming, norming, performing, and adjourning. The sequence becomes easier when linked to atmosphere: forming involves polite uncertainty, storming involves conflict, norming involves cohesion, performing involves effective task work, and adjourning involves ending. The clue that members are less afraid to reveal differences points to the psychological safety that develops in norming. The group is not necessarily conflict-free, but it has enough trust to address differences without immediate fragmentation.'),
  ('2011-85', 'Psychopathology terms are often easier to remember by their word roots. “Brady-” means slow, as in bradycardia, and “-lalia” refers to speech; bradylalia therefore literally cues slow speech. The term describes an observed sign rather than a diagnosis. Similar speech slowing can occur in depression, parkinsonism, medication sedation, delirium, or other neurologic and medical conditions. Clinical interpretation depends on the rest of the mental-status examination, history, medications, and neurologic findings.'),
  ('2011-86', 'Pain terminology is most useful when it describes a likely mechanism rather than implying whether symptoms are real. Nociceptive pain reflects activation of peripheral pain receptors by actual or threatened tissue injury. Neuropathic pain instead reflects abnormal signaling or processing within damaged sensory pathways. The practical descriptor cluster is burning, tingling, shooting, and electric. These words should make clinicians consider a neuropathic contribution, although one patient may have both nociceptive and neuropathic pain. Localization, neurologic examination, and the temporal course remain important for determining the cause.'),
  ('2011-87', 'Opioid receptors are present in central pathways that influence hypothalamic hormone release. Persistent opioid agonist exposure can dampen signaling from the hypothalamus to the pituitary and gonads. This creates a useful clinical connection: a medication used for pain can produce symptoms overlapping with depression, sexual dysfunction, fatigue, and reduced vitality. Thinking in terms of the hypothalamus, pituitary, and target gland helps organize these adverse effects. Other medical illnesses, aging, and concurrent medications can also affect testosterone, so the association must be interpreted in clinical context.'),
  ('2011-88', 'An IQ composite summarizes performance across multiple tasks, but an average can conceal meaningful strengths and weaknesses. Verbal measures sample language-mediated reasoning and acquired knowledge, whereas nonverbal measures emphasize visual-spatial or fluid problem solving, depending on the instrument. The useful memory principle is to look beyond the average: a large internal discrepancy may be more clinically informative than a single total score. Interpretation requires psychometric context and integration with real-world adaptive and academic functioning.'),
  ('2011-89', 'Stimulants enhance catecholaminergic signaling, supporting attention and behavioral control but also potentially reducing hunger and promoting wakefulness. Their adverse-effect profile often tracks timing: appetite effects are most noticeable while medication is active, and sleep effects may relate to a late dose, excessive duration, or rebound symptoms. A practical memory pair is that focus may improve while food intake and sleep may suffer. Monitoring should be individualized, incorporating baseline growth, sleep patterns, comorbid anxiety, and the child’s response to treatment rather than relying solely on a checklist.'),
  ('2011-90', 'Visual fixation normally helps suppress a peripheral vestibular imbalance. When abnormal eye movements persist or worsen during fixation, central ocular-motor circuitry becomes more likely. The brainstem integrates vestibular input with eye-movement control, so lesions there can create complex gaze-evoked patterns. A useful memory rule is that vertical, direction-changing, or fixation-resistant nystagmus is central until proven otherwise. This pattern does not itself establish a precise diagnosis, but it helps distinguish a potentially serious central process from the more typical findings of peripheral vestibular disease.'),
  ('2011-91', 'Consultation-liaison psychiatry often works at the boundary between symptoms, relationships, and disposition. A single unusual belief is not by itself enough to establish psychosis or justify hospitalization. Collateral and a structured family meeting can reveal context unavailable in an individual interview, including differing accounts of caregiving, safety concerns, or the medical workup. The consultant must still assess decision-making capacity and acute psychiatric risk. The memorable principle is to clarify the system before labeling the patient, while ensuring that the patient’s autonomy and safety remain central.'),
  ('2011-92', 'Psychopathology terms describe observable forms of speech and thought. “Neo-” means new, and “-logism” relates to a word or expression, making neologism a useful literal memory aid. These labels should be applied descriptively, with attention to culture, language, developmental context, and the patient’s intended meaning. A novel phrase that listeners readily understand is not necessarily a neologism. Clarifying what the patient means can preserve rapport and prevent clinicians from mistakenly pathologizing ordinary creativity, regional speech, or unfamiliar cultural language.'),
  ('2011-93', 'Serotonin is often remembered as a brain chemical, yet it also acts as a major peripheral signaling molecule. In the gut it participates in motility and sensory signaling, and platelets carry serotonin in the circulation. This physiology provides a useful psychopharmacology connection: medications intended to alter central serotonergic signaling can also produce peripheral gastrointestinal effects. The central nervous system remains clinically important for mood, sleep, and behavior, but it is not the principal location of total body serotonin. This distinction is a common board-style contrast.'),
  ('2011-94', 'A trinucleotide repeat is a three-base DNA sequence repeated multiple times. In Fragile X, the relevant repeat is CGG in FMR1. Repeat-expansion disorders are memorable because repeat length can increase across generations, but clinical consequences differ according to the affected gene and molecular mechanism. The fragile name derives from a historical cytogenetic observation. The practical association for this question is Fragile X, FMR1, and CGG expansion rather than a chromosomal deletion or trisomy.'),
  ('2011-95', 'Risk assessment is a clinical process, not a prediction exercise. Suicide cannot be forecast with certainty from a checklist, so high-quality documentation shows how available information informed a proportionate plan. The most defensible note makes the decision pathway understandable to another clinician: what was known, what alternatives were evaluated, why the plan fit the current risk, and how risk will be monitored. Documentation should be factual, individualized, and clinically useful rather than defensive. It should also record interventions that address modifiable risk factors and how the patient will access help if risk escalates.'),
  ('2011-96', 'Reading is highly practiced and automatic for literate adults, so the Stroop Test deliberately creates response conflict. The word’s meaning pulls one way while the ink-color instruction pulls another. Successful performance requires prioritizing the task rule over the habitual reading response. This makes the task a compact demonstration of inhibition and selective attention. Remember the contrast as “read the word” versus “name the color,” with the latter requiring executive control when the two conflict.'),
  ('2011-97', 'Dystonia is sustained or intermittent muscle contraction that causes abnormal movements or postures. Writer’s cramp is a focal, task-specific dystonia: the abnormal pattern emerges during a learned fine-motor task rather than as generalized weakness. Botulinum toxin works locally at the neuromuscular junction, fitting a disorder driven by excessive activation of selected muscles. The memorable pairing is focal dystonia followed by focal injection. Treatment requires attention to the exact movement pattern and the patient’s functional goals.'),
  ('2011-98', 'Adjustment disorders organize a common clinical presentation: distress meaningfully related to an identifiable stressor that does not better meet criteria for another mental disorder. The diagnosis requires attention to timing, severity, functional effect, and exclusion of a more specific syndrome. A helpful memory contrast is major stressor plus a subthreshold syndrome. Bereavement, trauma-related disorders, and mood disorders each require their own differential assessment. The presence of a stressor does not by itself establish adjustment disorder, because a patient may instead meet criteria for major depression or another disorder following that stressor.'),
  ('2011-99', 'Psychiatric treatment involves private disclosures, emotional dependency, and substantial professional power. Ethical boundary rules are designed to prevent exploitation where formal termination does not necessarily remove those effects. This is why the rule is broader than a ban limited to active appointments. The prohibition protects both individual patients and trust in psychiatric care. A useful memory phrase is that the treatment relationship leaves an enduring asymmetry. Clinicians should seek supervision early when boundary concerns arise rather than relying on their own assessment of whether a relationship seems mutual or harmless.'),
  ('2011-100', 'Cohesiveness in group therapy is analogous to the therapeutic alliance in individual therapy, but it belongs to the whole group rather than only the leader and one patient. It permits disagreement without exile and creates enough psychological safety for feedback to be useful. Group conflict is not inherently harmful; unexamined conflict that splits members into an outsider and a coalition erodes cohesion. Process-focused interventions can convert conflict into interpersonal learning, but they require the leader to protect participation and address exclusion before the group becomes organized around blame.'),
  ('2011-101', 'Freud contrasted primary process with secondary process to describe two broad modes of mental functioning. Primary process follows the pleasure principle and seeks immediate psychic gratification; secondary process follows the reality principle and supports planning and delay. The conceptual pairing is more useful than memorizing a list: primary process asks what is wished for, while secondary process asks what is possible in reality. Primary-process features can appear in dreams, fantasy, and regression, but their presence alone does not establish psychopathology. The terms are theoretical descriptions rather than contemporary diagnostic categories.'),
  ('2011-102', 'Cerebrospinal fluid patterns help narrow a differential but do not substitute for clinical judgment. Viral meningitis often produces lymphocytic inflammation with preserved glucose, whereas typical pyogenic bacterial meningitis produces more neutrophils and lower glucose. Enteroviruses are classic causes of aseptic meningitis, and Coxsackie virus belongs to that group. The memory link is lymphocytes plus normal glucose: think viral. Clinical severity, immune status, timing, imaging, cultures, and molecular testing may substantially alter the differential and urgent management of an individual patient.'),
  ('2011-103', 'Telepsychiatry changes the setting of psychiatric care, not the need for rapport, confidentiality, assessment, and emergency planning. Adoption has varied by clinician comfort, institutional resources, patient access, and regulatory environment. The durable lesson is that barriers are sociotechnical: reliable care requires both a functioning tool and people willing and prepared to integrate it into practice. Telepsychiatry may improve access for some patients while being less suitable for others because of privacy, technology access, acuity, or the need for physical examination. Modality selection should remain clinically individualized.'),
  ('2011-104', 'RLS is both a sensory and motor phenomenon: discomfort creates an urge to move, and movement gives short-lived relief. Dopaminergic responsiveness led to the historical use of dopamine agonists such as pramipexole. Augmentation is a distinctive treatment complication in which symptoms begin earlier, become more intense, or spread during ongoing dopaminergic treatment. Therefore, a board-style historical first-line answer should be distinguished from current individualized prescribing, which often prioritizes avoiding augmentation and correcting iron deficiency when present.'),
  ('2011-105', 'Cognitive therapy treats emotional distress as strongly shaped by interpretations, especially rapid, habitual appraisals that may feel like facts. “Socratic” refers to learning through disciplined questions rather than lecture. A useful memory link is that the therapist is a curious investigator, and the automatic thought is a hypothesis to examine. This distinguishes cognitive restructuring from simple reassurance or argument. Questions about evidence, alternative explanations, consequences, and perspective are common routes from an automatic thought to a more balanced appraisal.'),
  ('2011-106', 'Methadone has variable metabolism and a long, sometimes unpredictable duration of effect. Interactions can therefore produce clinically important changes in opioid exposure that are not obvious from a single reported dose. Board questions often contrast enzyme inhibitors, which can increase exposure, with enzyme inducers, which can decrease it. The useful direction is inhibition raises level and induction lowers level. However, respiratory depression after methadone ingestion is an emergency regardless of the suspected interaction, and clinicians must evaluate for additional sedatives, dose uncertainty, delayed toxicity, and other medical causes.'),
  ('2011-107', 'Lithium is filtered by the kidney and then partly reabsorbed along pathways that also handle sodium. That physiologic overlap makes changes in volume status and renal sodium handling central to lithium safety. A useful memory pair is: low sodium or low volume means lithium rises. Thiazides promote sodium loss, so they are a classic medication trigger for lithium accumulation. Other common board-relevant contributors include dehydration, renal impairment, and many NSAIDs; agents that increase renal clearance can have the opposite effect.'),
  ('2011-108', 'Continuous performance tests were designed to make vigilance observable over time during a repetitive, low-stimulation task. The simplest memory rule is omit equals attention and commit equals inhibition. The rule is conceptual rather than absolute: interpretation incorporates the specific CPT paradigm, reaction-time variability, validity indicators, and clinical setting. The task measures a narrow laboratory behavior, while attention disorders require evidence of persistent functional symptoms in everyday life. A single abnormal CPT result does not establish an ADHD diagnosis.'),
  ('2011-109', 'Excessive sweating is a recognized adverse effect of serotonergic antidepressants and can be severe enough to undermine otherwise successful treatment. Alpha-adrenergic activity contributes to sweating physiology, which provides a mechanistic rationale for alpha-1 antagonists in selected cases. The memorable clinical principle is to separate an ineffective medicine from an effective but poorly tolerated medicine. The latter may justify managing the adverse effect rather than reflexively abandoning an otherwise beneficial agent, after evaluating other potential causes of drenching night sweats.'),
  ('2011-110', 'Syringomyelia is remembered through crossing fibers: pain and temperature pathways cross near the center of the cord before ascending, so a central cavity selectively interrupts them first. Touch is carried through different pathways and may remain intact early, creating dissociated sensory loss. As the cavity enlarges, it can involve nearby anterior horn cells and long tracts. Trauma, congenital craniovertebral abnormalities, tumors, and inflammation can all be associated with syrinx formation and should guide the neurologic differential.'),
  ('2011-111', 'Dissociation can be understood as a protective disruption in the normal integration of awareness, memory, identity, or perception during overwhelming stress. It may look deceptively calm rather than overtly distressed. Grounding techniques use concrete present-moment cues, including orientation, sensory input, and paced interaction, to help reconnect attention to the immediate environment. They differ from debating a belief, which can increase distress, and from pretending the altered perception is accurate, which may prolong disorientation. Medical causes of confusion should also be assessed.'),
  ('2011-112', 'Little Hans is remembered as a foundational psychoanalytic child case centered on a fear of horses. In the psychodynamic account, displacement relocates anxiety from an internal conflict onto a more concrete external object. That makes “phobia” the high-yield association. Modern cognitive-behavioral models emphasize learning, threat appraisal, and avoidance, so the case is best understood as historical theory rather than a complete contemporary account of childhood fears.'),
  ('2011-113', 'The key organizing idea in PMDD is phase-linked symptom change, not a single symptom. The late luteal phase is the interval after ovulation and before menstrual bleeding; symptoms then remit around menses. PMDD is therefore best remembered as a cyclic affective disorder with real functional consequences, rather than as a mild form of premenstrual moodiness. This timing distinction also prevents confusion with a continuous mood disorder that merely worsens before menstruation and requires a different diagnostic formulation.'),
  ('2011-114', 'SLE can affect mental health through several pathways, including nervous-system involvement, chronic inflammation, pain, fatigue, organ dysfunction, treatment effects, and the psychosocial burden of illness. Neuropsychiatric lupus is therefore not a single uniform syndrome, and causation cannot be inferred merely because a psychiatric symptom occurs in someone with SLE. Depression and cognitive dysfunction are useful common-pattern answers, whereas dramatic syndromes such as psychosis are less typical. Their relative prevalence is not universal across definitions or clinical populations.'),
  ('2011-115', 'Substance-use epidemiology changes with culture, access, laws, products, and how surveys define use, misuse, and disorder. Board questions can reflect a historical broad-population pattern rather than the most visible drug in a particular clinical setting. Alcohol is the keyed answer in this 2011 question because it was a common adolescent exposure despite age restrictions. The clinical lesson is universal screening, since a population-level ranking does not predict an individual patient’s substance exposure, impairment, or risk.'),
  ('2011-116', 'Melatonin is a hormone whose normal secretion rises in darkness and helps signal biological night. In circadian sleep problems, its value is not merely that it can make someone sleepy; timing relative to the desired sleep schedule is central. This makes it conceptually different from sedatives. The memory hook is that melatonin moves the clock, while behavioral routines and light exposure help stabilize the clock’s daily cues. A schedule history helps distinguish circadian delay from insomnia caused by another disorder.'),
  ('2011-117', 'The Five-Factor Model organizes many narrower personality descriptors into five broad domains. OCEAN is the common mnemonic, though listing neuroticism first, as in the answer choice, does not change the model. It is a dimensional framework: people vary in degree on each trait rather than fitting into a single type. This makes it useful for remembering that a trait can be adaptive in one setting and problematic only when extreme, inflexible, or impairing. It is not a diagnostic system for personality disorders.'),
  ('2011-118', 'Medulloblastoma is an embryonal primary CNS malignancy strongly associated with childhood and commonly located in the posterior fossa. Neuroblastoma is also a major pediatric solid tumor but usually originates in adrenal or paraspinal sympathetic tissue outside the CNS. The original answer was clinically corrected to D because neuroblastoma did not fit the stem’s specified location. This remains a best-listed answer: pilocytic astrocytoma, which is omitted, is a broader contender when questions ask about the most common childhood brain tumor without restricting the choices or tumor category.'),
  ('2011-119', 'Winnicott’s developmental language links early dependable care with the child’s emerging capacity to be separate, creative, and real in relationships. Holding environment refers broadly to reliable physical and emotional care, not simply literal holding. Good enough does not mean careless; it means caregivers do not need to be perfect and can gradually fail in tolerable ways as the child develops capacity. Transitional objects are a memorable bridge between dependence and increasing independence, rather than a sign that must be prevented.'),
  ('2011-120', 'Benzodiazepines enhance inhibitory GABA-A signaling; chronic exposure can lead to physiologic adaptation. Flumazenil rapidly removes that receptor effect, so the body may shift abruptly into withdrawal. The board-style memory link is that an antidote does not equal harmless: reversal can be dangerous when the original drug is suppressing withdrawal or seizure activity. Its short duration also means sedation can recur after initial reversal, adding another practical limitation that requires continued clinical observation.'),
  ('2011-121', 'Falls in later life usually result from several interacting vulnerabilities rather than one diagnosis or one medication. Central nervous system effects, blood-pressure changes, electrolyte disturbances, frailty, neuropathy, poor vision, and environmental hazards may converge. Antidepressants matter because they can affect alertness and autonomic function even when prescribed appropriately. A useful teaching frame is to ask not only whether a medication is indicated, but also what it adds to this patient’s total fall burden and whether safer alternatives are available.'),
  ('2011-122', 'In professional negligence concepts, abandonment concerns an unjustified discontinuation of a clinician-patient relationship when ongoing care is needed and no reasonable transition is provided. It is often tested through vacations, sudden termination, or failure to arrange coverage. The principle does not require a clinician to be personally available at all times; it requires reasonable planning, communication, and continuity proportional to the clinical circumstances. Clear coverage arrangements protect patients and clinicians while supporting reliable access to urgent evaluation when necessary.'),
  ('2011-123', 'Serotonin is often taught as a broad modulator of behavioral inhibition and affective regulation rather than a single “mood chemical.” Borderline personality disorder is best remembered as involving instability of affect, relationships, self-image, and impulse control. The board-style connection is serotonin dysregulation leading to reduced behavioral restraint and thus impulsive aggression or self-injury. This is a risk-formulation concept, not a laboratory diagnosis or a claim that one neurochemical abnormality explains the disorder. Clinical assessment remains individualized and comprehensive.'),
  ('2011-124', 'Psychological tests are commonly divided into objective inventories and projective techniques. Objective inventories ask directly about experiences or traits and score responses using a fixed system; projective approaches invite responses to relatively ambiguous material. The MCMI is remembered as a broad profile of personality patterns and clinical syndromes. This distinction concerns test format and scoring, not whether one type of test is inherently more useful in every clinical situation. Results must always be interpreted within the full clinical evaluation.'),
  ('2011-125', 'Paraneoplastic cerebellar degeneration can precede discovery of a malignancy and may produce severe ataxia before routine MRI shows a structural abnormality. Onconeural antibodies support an immune-mediated cancer association but do not replace a complete, individualized tumor evaluation. Heavy tobacco exposure makes thoracic imaging especially reasonable in this vignette. Nevertheless, anti-Yo more classically points toward breast and gynecologic malignancies, so the testing pathway is anatomy- and context-dependent rather than universally limited to the chest.'),
  ('2011-126', 'Psychiatric terminology changes, and some older examination labels have no stable modern equivalent. Positive symptoms add experiences or behaviors such as hallucinations, delusions, and disorganized thought; negative symptoms involve reductions in motivation, expression, speech, or social engagement. The historical concept of deteriorative disorder emphasized decline without florid positive psychosis. Because the term is obsolete and ambiguous, it should be interpreted only within this exam-era question and should not be used as a contemporary diagnostic category.'),
  ('2011-127', 'The word “moral” in moral treatment has a historical meaning closer to psychological, social, and relational care than to enforcing virtue. It is a useful precursor to the idea that treatment is not only a procedure delivered to a patient: daily environment, relationships, work, and routine also shape experience. Pinel is commonly remembered in psychiatry’s reform narrative for replacing harsh custodial practices with more humane institutional care. The term identifies a historical movement rather than a current standalone treatment model.'),
  ('2011-128', 'Methadone is a long-acting full opioid agonist used in a structured maintenance model. The central historical teaching point is stabilization: replacing an unpredictable pattern of short-acting opioid intoxication and withdrawal with consistent treatment. Pregnancy brings both maternal and fetal considerations, making continuity of care particularly important. Contemporary practice recognizes more than one medication pathway, but methadone remains the classic PRITE answer and an important treatment option. Care planning includes prenatal care, addiction treatment, and preparation for neonatal observation.'),
  ('2011-129', 'Transference does not simply mean that a patient is irrational. It describes the way old relational templates can organize feelings and perceptions in a new relationship. Psychodynamic therapies pay particular attention when these patterns become visible in the therapeutic relationship because they can be examined directly and safely. The cancellation matters not merely as a scheduling event, but as a trigger that makes the patient’s recurring expectation of unavailability emotionally immediate. A therapeutic interpretation should remain tentative and responsive to the patient’s actual experience.'),
  ('2011-130', 'The phrase “ICU psychosis” persists in clinical language but can mislead because the syndrome is not usually a primary psychotic disorder. Delirium is a global brain dysfunction marked most reliably by impaired attention and fluctuation. The ICU adds recognizable precipitants: severe illness, multiple medications, sleep disruption, unfamiliar surroundings, and reduced orientation cues. Remember: psychotic-like content in an acute medical setting does not itself establish schizophrenia.'),
  ('2011-131', 'Many antidepressant adverse effects can be remembered by receptor and body-system patterns. Serotonin signaling is active in the gut, so serotonergic reuptake inhibition often produces gastrointestinal complaints. Mirtazapine is often recalled by its clinically visible cluster of sleepiness and increased appetite or weight rather than by SSRI-like gastrointestinal activation. The question tests a comparison: select the effect more characteristic of SSRIs, not merely an effect that mirtazapine can ever cause. Individual adverse-effect profiles and treatment response still vary.'),
  ('2011-132', 'CT uses x-rays and is particularly efficient for emergency imaging. Acute blood is conspicuous on noncontrast CT, making the modality central to rapid trauma assessment. MRI uses magnetic properties of tissues and excels at soft-tissue detail, including demyelination and many posterior-fossa or temporal abnormalities. A practical memory pair is CT for acute blood and speed, and MRI for finer soft-tissue characterization. The correct modality depends on the clinical question and the need for immediate results.'),
  ('2011-133', 'Clozapine is pharmacologically distinctive and clinically reserved because it can be uniquely effective in difficult-to-treat schizophrenia but requires intensive safety monitoring. Board questions often pair it with treatment resistance, suicidality, or co-occurring substance use. A memory connection is “clozapine is the high-monitoring, high-yield exception”: its burden is considerable, but its potential benefits extend beyond simple symptom reduction in selected patients.'),
  ('2011-134', 'Seizure semiology helps localize disease. A focal onset means the event begins in one cerebral region even if it later spreads bilaterally. In children and younger adults, genetic or longstanding epilepsy syndromes are more common considerations; later-onset focal seizures more strongly suggest acquired pathology such as vascular disease, trauma, infection, or tumor. Glioblastoma is a classic board example of a lesion that can first declare itself through seizures. The evaluation should be urgent because causes and consequences may be time-sensitive.'),
  ('2011-135', 'Self-help groups are built around mutual aid: people with shared experiences provide support and practical knowledge to one another. Their therapeutic force often comes from repeated participation, belonging, role modeling, and accountability outside the clinician’s office. This differs from individual insight-oriented psychotherapy, though participants may gain insight. A useful memory cue is self-help as help translating intention into everyday recovery behavior with peers. Groups vary in format, membership, and philosophy, so fit and accessibility matter when recommending them.'),
  ('2011-136', 'Psychoneuroimmunology captures the idea that psychological processes, the nervous system, hormones, and immunity communicate with one another. Stress does not bypass biology; it is translated into physiologic signals through systems such as autonomic arousal and the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis. Remember the answer as a network rather than a single organ: brain, hormones, and immune function influence one another. The relationship is bidirectional, so physical illness can also affect mood, cognition, and stress responses.'),
  ('2011-137', 'Drug-policy discussions commonly distinguish supply reduction, demand reduction, and harm reduction. Supply focuses on availability; demand focuses on prevention and treatment; harm reduction focuses on mitigating consequences of ongoing use. Needle exchange is a particularly clear example because it changes the safety of injection equipment rather than the supply of drugs or the person''s immediate motivation to stop. The mnemonic is simple: clean needles reduce harm, so they are harm reduction.'),
  ('2011-138', 'Toluene is a volatile organic solvent found in products such as glues, paints, and thinners, and it may be misused by inhalation. Inhalants can reach the brain rapidly and can have immediate intoxicating effects as well as chronic neurologic consequences. A high-yield memory connection is solvent inhalation plus white-matter disease: chronic toluene exposure is a classic cause of toxic leukoencephalopathy and cerebellar-type symptoms. Clinical evaluation must also identify the specific product, exposure pattern, and immediate medical complications.'),
  ('2011-139', 'Suicide risk is dynamic, not a demographic label. Schizophrenia has periods of especially high vulnerability, including early illness, relapse, post-discharge adjustment, and times when a person confronts functional changes. The age association is memorable because it points to the early course of illness, when insight, demoralization, mood symptoms, and instability may coexist. Good assessment still focuses on current, modifiable risk and protective factors, including safety, social support, treatment engagement, and access to lethal means.'),
  ('2011-140', 'Research ethics is often organized around respect for persons, beneficence, and justice. Tuskegee is remembered because exploitation was not only a failure of consent but also a failure to protect participants from foreseeable harm when treatment was available. The risk-benefit question is practical: even a potentially valuable question cannot ethically be pursued if people bear unjustified or avoidable harm. Independent review exists in part to challenge investigators’ assumptions before participants are exposed and to ensure safeguards remain adequate throughout a study.'),
  ('2011-141', 'Tolerance and withdrawal language originated in substance-use disorders, where repeated exposure changes physiology and behavior. Similar terms are sometimes applied to compulsive behaviors when repeated engagement produces escalating involvement and distress on stopping. This creates a useful memory link: tolerance means that more engagement is needed, whereas withdrawal means feeling worse when engagement is removed. In behavioral syndromes, these constructs require care because they can overlap with habit, frustration, and attempts to regulate difficult emotions.'),
  ('2011-142', 'The medial rectus moves an eye toward the nose, whereas the lateral rectus moves it toward the temple. Red-glass testing dissociates the two eyes’ images, allowing the examiner to identify the eye that is failing to move appropriately. The core localization rule is that a weak muscle cannot pull effectively into its normal field of action, so image separation is greatest there. Horizontal deviations implicate the medial or lateral rectus more directly than the oblique muscles, whose important actions include vertical and torsional movement.'),
  ('2011-143', 'The word hypnosis often evokes stage performance, but therapeutic hypnosis is better understood as an organized use of attention, expectation, imagination, and suggestion. Its clinical use has shifted away from the image of an all-powerful hypnotist toward collaboration and patient participation. A helpful memory cue is that effective hypnosis amplifies capacities the patient already has, such as attention control, imagery, and emotional regulation, rather than supplying an external will. The patient remains an active participant in setting goals and using the technique.'),
  ('2011-144', 'Neuromodulation treats a circuit by altering its activity rather than removing a lesion. The vagus nerve provides an accessible peripheral route that can influence widespread brain networks through brainstem connections. This makes VNS memorable as a nerve-to-network treatment. In epilepsy, the usual clinical goal is fewer or less severe seizures rather than guaranteed seizure freedom. That goal distinguishes implanted stimulation from potentially curative resection of a well-localized epileptogenic focus.'),
  ('2011-145', 'Behavioral and psychological symptoms of dementia are often relational and environmental as well as neurologic. A behavior described as combative may communicate pain, fear, confusion, overstimulation, or another unmet need. Caregiver burnout is therefore not peripheral: it can shape the home environment, limit safe supervision, and determine whether a care plan is sustainable. Thinking of the caregiver as a second patient in the clinical system helps identify modifiable strain and supports a safer plan for both people.'),
  ('2011-146', 'Lithium tremor resembles an enhanced physiologic tremor: fine, rhythmic, and most apparent with posture or action. Beta-blockers reduce peripheral adrenergic tremor, which is why propranolol is a useful association. The contrast with anticholinergics is high yield: anticholinergics target parkinsonian extrapyramidal symptoms, whereas propranolol is the classic treatment for lithium-associated action tremor. A new coarse tremor, ataxia, confusion, or gastrointestinal symptoms should instead prompt urgent consideration of lithium toxicity.'),
  ('2011-147', 'Panic disorder is sustained partly by a feedback loop: a bodily sensation is noticed, interpreted as dangerous, and amplified by fear-driven autonomic arousal. CBT interrupts this loop at the interpretation and avoidance stages. A memorable shorthand is fear of fear: patients may come to fear the sensations of anxiety themselves. Restructuring does not deny the sensations or medical evaluation when indicated; it changes their catastrophic meaning and permits corrective learning through exposure and reduced avoidance.'),
  ('2011-148', 'Schizophrenia most often begins earlier in adulthood, so a later presentation raises diagnostic caution. Late onset is a descriptive course pattern, not a shortcut that eliminates other explanations for psychosis. Female predominance is a classic exam association. Pair this concept with the clinical rule that later-life first psychosis requires careful assessment for delirium, neurocognitive disorders, medical illness, medication effects, substance exposure, and sensory impairment before a primary schizophrenia-spectrum diagnosis is assigned.'),
  ('2011-149', 'Sleep architecture changes across the lifespan. Deep slow-wave sleep is most prominent earlier in life and tends to decline with aging, while sleep becomes more vulnerable to awakenings. A helpful memory image is that older sleep is “lighter and more interrupted,” not deeper or longer. This normal tendency can be amplified by illness, medications, environmental noise, and circadian changes, which is why a careful assessment remains necessary.'),
  ('2011-150', 'The carotid sinus contains pressure-sensitive receptors that normally help regulate blood pressure. Excess stimulation can trigger a reflex that slows the heart or lowers vascular tone enough to cause transient loss of consciousness. This is a physiology-based clue: a tight collar, turning the head, or pressure near the carotid region points toward a baroreceptor reflex rather than a primary epileptic discharge. The brief motor activity in syncope results from hypoperfusion and should be interpreted with the entire event sequence.')
insert into question_context (question_id, context) values
  ('2011-151', 'Thomas and Chess used temperament to describe patterns of individual variation in how children react to the world. Their categories are styles, not diagnoses. The difficult pattern combines high emotional intensity with irregularity, negative mood, withdrawal from novelty, and slow adaptation. Their goodness-of-fit concept emphasizes that outcomes depend partly on how well the environment accommodates the child’s style. This framework avoids equating temperament with blame, poor intelligence, or inevitable psychopathology.'),
  ('2011-152', 'Salicylates are a classic toxicology pattern because they affect both respiratory drive and cellular metabolism. The memorable cluster is ringing ears, rapid breathing, and a mixed acid-base disorder. Thinking in sequence helps: respiratory alkalosis may appear early, while metabolic acidosis becomes more prominent with worsening toxicity. This is why a single acid-base label may fail to capture the whole physiologic picture. The combination in this question is far more characteristic of salicylates than of the other listed medications.'),
  ('2011-153', 'Trazodone is often remembered as a sedating antidepressant used in low doses for sleep, but board questions also pair it with priapism. The association is linked to alpha-adrenergic antagonism and failure of normal penile smooth-muscle regulation. A useful memory hook is trazodone: sleepiness, orthostasis, and the rare emergency of priapism. The rarity of this adverse effect does not reduce its urgency, because delayed treatment of ischemic priapism can cause permanent erectile dysfunction.'),
  ('2011-154', 'Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy is a high-yield example of how careful history reveals a syndrome hidden behind a first generalized tonic-clonic seizure. Myoclonus is a sudden, shock-like muscle contraction, not simply tremulousness or clumsiness. The memory pattern is adolescent onset, morning jerks, dropped objects, and generalized polyspike-and-wave activity. Sleep loss commonly makes the pattern more noticeable. Identifying the syndrome matters because seizure classification guides neurologic management and counseling about triggers.'),
  ('2011-155', 'Liaison psychiatry often treats the system around a critically ill patient as well as individual symptoms. Splitting occurs when anxiety and grief organize people into opposing alliances, with each side locating hope or blame in the other. Separate exploratory meetings are not avoidance; they lower reactivity, identify meanings behind positions, and prevent the consultant from being recruited into the split. Current practice distinguishes an unresponsive wakefulness syndrome from death by neurologic criteria, which must be determined using formal medical standards.'),
  ('2011-156', 'Object relations theory uses object to mean a psychologically important person or representation of a person, not an inanimate thing. Klein focused on how infants internalize early caregiving experiences and organize them into emotionally charged internal relationships. Her ideas provide a useful contrast with Freud: Klein moved the theoretical center of gravity toward very early, pre-Oedipal relationships, primitive anxieties, and primitive defenses. These are conceptual psychoanalytic constructs rather than direct diagnostic criteria for a clinical disorder.'),
  ('2011-157', 'The locus coeruleus is a pontine nucleus with widespread norepinephrine projections that influence vigilance, autonomic tone, and stress responses. Opioids acutely suppress firing in these neurons through inhibitory receptor signaling and reduced cAMP activity. With repeated exposure, intracellular pathways compensate for that inhibition. Sudden opioid discontinuation then leaves noradrenergic output relatively overactive, contributing to withdrawal symptoms. A helpful memory link is that opioid intoxication suppresses the brain’s alarm system, whereas withdrawal unmasks an overactive alarm system.'),
  ('2011-158', 'Diagnostic categories change as syndromes become more clearly described and studied. Repetitive skin picking historically fit near impulse-control conditions because behavior may be preceded by urge or tension and followed by relief. Later classification placed excoriation disorder among obsessive-compulsive and related disorders, reflecting overlap in repetitive behavior and treatment approaches. The chronology is the key memory aid: DSM-IV-TR used a residual impulse-control category, whereas DSM-5 created a named skin-picking diagnosis.'),
  ('2011-159', 'Classical conditioning links a previously neutral cue to a biologically meaningful event. Extinction weakens the learned response when the cue is no longer followed by that event; it is not best understood as erasing all prior learning. This distinction helps explain why conditioned fear can return in some contexts. For exams, remember the contrast: acquisition pairs cues; extinction breaks the cue–outcome pairing.'),
  ('2011-160', 'The MMPI includes validity scales because self-report results depend partly on how a person approaches the questions. “Lie scale” is historical shorthand; clinically, it is better understood as an indicator of response style rather than a moral judgment. L is remembered as looking unrealistically good, whereas F is remembered as endorsing infrequent or unusual responses. This distinction helps separate defensive minimization from atypical, random, or potentially exaggerated reporting.'),
  ('2011-161', 'Concern about antidepressant-associated suicidality in young people prompted closer review of treatment-trial safety data and warnings emphasizing monitoring. An important methodological limitation is that adverse events can be missed when clinicians wait for patients to volunteer them. The exam principle is proportionality: an uncommon safety signal can be clinically serious, yet it must be considered alongside the larger group experiencing improvement. Direct questioning and follow-up are therefore central to safe treatment.'),
  ('2011-162', 'No-harm contracts became common partly because they appeared to formalize a therapeutic commitment to safety. Their appeal can obscure a basic limitation: a promise does not measure intent, future behavior, or capacity to remain safe. Contemporary suicide prevention emphasizes ongoing assessment, consideration of means safety, and collaborative actionable planning. The memory distinction is simple: a safety plan specifies steps to use during crisis; a no-harm contract is only a promise.'),
  ('2011-163', 'ALS is a motor neuron disease involving both upper and lower motor neuron systems. Early disease may appear localized because degeneration begins in one motor region before spreading. A progressively failing and wasting hand or foot is a useful image for limb-onset ALS. The later coexistence of atrophy and fasciculations with brisk reflexes is especially memorable because it reflects injury at two levels of the motor system.'),
  ('2011-164', 'Bizarre delusions once had a formal role in schizophrenia criteria, although the reliability and cultural sensitivity of that judgment were debated. Later criteria moved away from giving one type of delusion special diagnostic weight. The memory hook is historical: DSM-IV allowed one characteristic symptom when a delusion was bizarre, while DSM-5 requires the usual symptom combination. Criterion questions should always be anchored to the manual and era implied by the stem.'),
  ('2011-165', 'Weight gain from some mood stabilizers and second-generation antipsychotics is clinically important because it can affect cardiometabolic health, self-image, and willingness to continue effective treatment. Metformin was developed as a glucose-lowering medication, but its metabolic effects made it a useful adjunct in selected psychiatric populations. A practical memory link is metformin for medication-related metabolic burden, not metformin as an antipsychotic or mood stabilizer.'),
  ('2011-166', 'IPT treats depression within its social and relational setting. Its early interpersonal inventory makes relationships visible: who is close, where support exists, what has changed, and where conflict or loss is occurring. The four classic focus areas are grief, role disputes, role transitions, and interpersonal deficits. A useful memory cue is that IPT begins with a map of the patient’s present social world rather than a map of childhood experiences.'),
  ('2011-167', 'Spongiform encephalopathies are prion diseases that cause characteristic microscopic vacuolar change in brain tissue. Clinically, their memorable pattern is rapidly progressive dementia accompanied by myoclonus, often exaggerated by sensory stimuli. This differs from most degenerative dementias, which generally develop over months to years. The high-yield recognition phrase is “fast dementia plus startle jerks,” while remembering that other urgent and potentially treatable causes must also be evaluated.'),
  ('2011-168', 'Legal psychiatry separates questions that are often conflated. Competence to stand trial is present-focused and concerns fair participation in the legal process. Criminal responsibility is retrospective and concerns the mental state during the alleged act. The practical memory pair is: competence asks, “Now, can the person work with counsel?”; insanity asks, “Then, what was the person’s mental state?” A serious mental disorder alone does not answer either legal question.'),
  ('2011-169', 'The opioid toxidrome is commonly summarized by depressed mental status, respiratory depression, and miosis. Individual findings vary, and mixed overdoses can blur the picture, but this cluster remains useful for rapid recognition. Codeine is metabolized in part to morphine, so excessive exposure can cause opioid toxicity. The memory cue is simple: pinpoint pupils and slow breathing should prompt consideration of opioid poisoning.'),
  ('2011-170', 'Neuropsychological interpretation often compares current abilities with an estimated prior level because a low average score may be normal for one person but represent a major decline for another. Crystallized knowledge, such as vocabulary accumulated over a lifetime, is generally more resilient than fluid abilities such as rapid novel problem-solving. Think of vocabulary as a snapshot of accumulated verbal knowledge, while timed executive tasks are more sensitive to current impairment.'),
  ('2011-171', 'Sexual medication effects are not uniformly undesirable. Delayed ejaculation from serotonergic treatment may be an adverse effect for a person treated for depression, yet it can be therapeutically useful for a person distressed by premature ejaculation. This is a useful pharmacology inversion: the same SSRI effect can have different clinical value depending on the treatment goal. Fluoxetine is a classic SSRI example for this association.'),
  ('2011-172', 'IPT is structured and time-limited. Its opening phase identifies a focal interpersonal problem, its middle phase works actively on that problem in current relationships, and its final phase consolidates gains and prepares for ending. This produces a useful memory sequence: interpersonal inventory first, present relationship work in the middle, and review plus separation at the end. The treatment remains connected to depression and its interpersonal context throughout.'),
  ('2011-173', 'The axillary nerve arises from the posterior cord of the brachial plexus, largely from C5–C6 roots. It passes through the quadrangular space with the posterior circumflex humeral vessels before innervating the deltoid and teres minor. Its cutaneous branch supplies skin over the lateral shoulder. A useful localization pattern is shoulder dislocation plus deltoid weakness plus lateral-shoulder numbness, which indicates axillary nerve injury.'),
  ('2011-174', 'Psychodynamic theory uses “ego” broadly for organizing, reality-testing, defensive, and self-reflective functions. The observing ego is sometimes remembered as the internal noticer: one part of the mind experiences a feeling while another can observe and describe it. This is conceptually related to reflective capacity and is clinically important for insight, though it is not identical to any single modern cognitive test.'),
  ('2011-175', 'Psychosis associated with epilepsy may be ictal, postictal, or interictal. Postictal psychosis typically follows a seizure or seizure cluster and has a limited course, often with a lucid interval; interictal psychosis occurs between seizures without that immediate temporal pattern. This item has a source limitation: its official answer-key page was absent, so C was independently inferred and retained through adjudication. The label should therefore be understood as an examination workaround, not a correction of the clinical terminology.'),
  ('2011-176', 'Psychoeducation treats family members as important partners in care while recognizing that living with severe mental illness can be stressful. It commonly includes information about symptoms, treatment, relapse prevention, and problem solving. The memorable distinction is that psychoeducation answers “What is happening, and what can we do next?” rather than focusing chiefly on interpretation of family dynamics. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2011-177', 'Feedback converts an internal bodily process into information a person can observe. Once the signal is visible or audible, small changes can be noticed and reinforced. The learning principle is operant conditioning: a response followed by a reinforcing consequence becomes more likely. The memory link is simple: biofeedback rewards the body response that the person is trying to increase or decrease.'),
  ('2011-178', 'The word “apperception” refers to interpreting new material through one’s existing inner experience. Projective methods use ambiguous stimuli so that narratives may reveal recurrent concerns, wishes, fears, and relational expectations. A useful exam contrast is ambiguous picture stories for the TAT versus standardized questions or performance tasks for structured interviews, inventories, and neuropsychological testing. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2011-179', 'Alphabetic writing systems require a bridge between spoken sounds and visual symbols. Phonological awareness includes skills such as identifying rhyme, segmenting words into sounds, and blending sounds into words. English spelling has irregularities, but efficient reading still depends strongly on representing speech sounds. Dyslexia is primarily a language-processing problem, not a problem of eyesight. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2011-180', 'Motivational interviewing is a collaborative conversation style developed for ambivalence about change. “Discrepancy” does not mean shaming a patient; it means helping the patient hear the contrast between what they do and what matters to them. Its memorable stance is guidance rather than persuasion: evoke the patient’s reasons instead of supplying all of them yourself. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2011-181', 'Vitamin B12 is required for normal neurologic function and DNA synthesis. Deficiency is often remembered for macrocytic anemia, but neurologic and cognitive manifestations can be clinically important even when blood-count changes are not the presenting clue. In cognitive evaluation, a patient’s baseline abilities, educational history, and functional trajectory matter alongside a screening score. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2011-182', 'Concussion is a functional brain injury caused by biomechanical forces and may occur without loss of consciousness. Symptoms can be cognitive, emotional, physical, or sleep-related, and they may not all be immediate. Older examination questions sometimes use fixed return-to-play intervals; contemporary practice instead emphasizes clinical recovery and gradual progression because recovery is variable. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2011-183', 'ANOVA partitions variability into variation between groups and variation within groups. If group membership explains more variation than would be expected from within-group variation alone, the overall test is significant. Despite its name, ANOVA is commonly used to compare means. Remember: a t-test is for two means, whereas ANOVA is for three or more means. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2011-184', 'Rapport comes from the idea of a harmonious relationship or connection. In clinical work, it is less about being agreeable than about creating enough trust and mutual understanding to do difficult work together. The relationship can support diagnosis, treatment adherence, and honest discussion of risk. A useful distinction is that reflection is something the clinician says, whereas rapport is the relationship that develops.'),
  ('2011-185', 'Suicide risk is transdiagnostic: it can occur in mood, psychotic, substance-related, trauma-related, personality, and anxiety disorders, as well as without a single explanatory diagnosis. Board questions often test the broad epidemiologic association with mood disorders. The useful conceptual connection is prevalence versus prediction: a common diagnostic association identifies a population burden, while bedside risk assessment requires dynamic, individual-level information. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2011-186', 'Diagnostic systems include non-disorder conditions that may appropriately be a focus of clinical attention. Religious and spiritual problems can involve changes in belief, loss of faith, conflict with a religious community, or distressing existential questions. The key memory point is that context matters: a belief conflict is not automatically evidence of obsession, delusion, or mood disorder. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2011-187', 'The median nerve supplies palmar sensation to the lateral three-and-a-half digits, while the ulnar nerve supplies the medial one-and-a-half digits. The ring finger is therefore a useful boundary digit: peripheral nerve lesions can split it down the middle, whereas radicular sensory loss generally does not respect that precise digital border. Carpal tunnel syndrome reflects compression of the median nerve beneath the transverse carpal ligament.'),
  ('2011-188', 'Autistic disorder is the older diagnostic label now incorporated within autism spectrum disorder. Likewise, “mental retardation” is obsolete terminology; current practice uses intellectual disability. Intellectual disability is defined by limitations in intellectual functioning and adaptive functioning with developmental onset. Autism describes social-communication and restricted or repetitive behavior domains, whereas intellectual disability concerns global cognitive and adaptive abilities. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2011-189', 'Enteroviruses are a large group of viruses that commonly circulate among children and may produce mild respiratory, gastrointestinal, rash, or febrile illnesses. Their name reflects replication in the intestinal tract, although disease is not limited to the gut. CNS invasion is uncommon relative to ordinary viral illness, but aseptic meningitis is a classic presentation when it occurs. A useful memory link is that a common childhood virus after a mild prodrome is more likely than an unusual fungal infection to explain aseptic meningitis.'),
  ('2011-190', 'In psychodynamic language, an “object” is usually a person or psychologically important other, not an inanimate thing. Object relations theory emphasizes that early relationships become internal templates for experiencing oneself and others. These templates can be stable, conflicted, idealized, devalued, or poorly integrated. A memory cue: object relations asks, “Who am I with you in my internal world?” This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2011-191', 'Mental health courts emerged from the broader problem-solving-court movement. Their organizing idea is practical: untreated serious mental illness, fragmented services, and repeated low-level legal involvement can reinforce one another. A court can use its convening power to coordinate care, but it does not replace clinical treatment or ordinary legal protections. Remember the contrast: competency asks whether a defendant can participate in legal proceedings, whereas a mental health court asks whether treatment-oriented diversion can be structured around the case.'),
  ('2011-192', 'MAOIs inhibit monoamine breakdown, making certain serotonergic and sympathomimetic interactions clinically dangerous. The classic examination warning is the meperidine–MAOI reaction, but opioids should not be divided simplistically into safe and unsafe classes. Fentanyl has serotonergic potential despite being comparatively less problematic than several listed alternatives. The answer is also inferred because the official key page was missing, so its appropriate use is as an exam-era, best-listed distinction with a prominent contemporary safety caveat.'),
  ('2011-193', 'IQ is a standardized comparison of performance with that of same-age peers, not a direct measure of a fixed biological quantity. The board-relevant idea is developmental stability: cognitive assessment becomes more predictive once early developmental variability has diminished. “Stable” is best remembered as stable relative standing over time, not as proof that learning, intervention, health, and educational environment cannot affect cognitive development or test performance.'),
  ('2011-194', 'ACT was designed around a service-delivery insight: some patients most in need of care cannot consistently access office-based systems. Its assertive quality refers to proactive outreach, not coercive psychotherapy. The model integrates disciplines that are often fragmented, including psychiatry, nursing, case management, rehabilitation, and substance-use services, around a shared caseload. Think of the team going to the patient, especially when repeated crises and disengagement make ordinary clinic referral insufficient.'),
  ('2011-195', 'The Nuremberg Code arose from postwar scrutiny of abusive human experimentation. Its importance is conceptual as much as historical: research participants are persons with agency, not merely sources of data. Later frameworks refined and broadened oversight, but the code remains strongly associated with voluntary consent and limits on research risk. A simple sequence is Nuremberg first, Helsinki later for medical-research guidance, and Belmont later for its three-principle United States framework.'),
  ('2011-196', 'Visual hallucination phenomenology is diagnostically useful. Formed, colorful images of people or animals with retained insight often point toward a visual-system or neurologic cause, whereas primary psychotic disorders more commonly feature auditory hallucinations and delusional interpretation. Posterior cerebral circulation supplies much of the occipital cortex. The memory link is “posterior brain, visual scene”: focal posterior dysfunction can create elaborate visual percepts without a global dementia syndrome.'),
  ('2011-197', 'ADHD is a clinical diagnosis based on a developmental pattern, functional impact, and reports across settings. The cross-setting requirement helps distinguish a pervasive regulation problem from behavior specific to one environment or relationship. Teachers are particularly informative because classrooms offer a consistent comparison with age expectations and require sustained self-regulation. Remember that rating scales organize observations; they do not replace a clinical assessment or the requirement for symptoms in more than one setting.'),
  ('2011-198', 'Operant conditioning describes how consequences alter the future likelihood of behavior. Ratio schedules are keyed to the number of responses, whereas interval schedules are keyed to time. Fixed means predictable, while variable means unpredictable. The phrase “every third time” is a direct cue for fixed ratio. A helpful memory pair is fixed-ratio for a fixed number of responses and variable-ratio for an uncertain number of responses.'),
  ('2011-199', 'In psychodynamic language, defenses reduce emotional conflict or distress, often outside awareness. Isolation of affect preserves the idea or memory while disconnecting its emotional charge. It is commonly confused with intellectualization, which shifts into abstract analysis, and with suppression, which is a conscious decision to set aside a thought or feeling. The visual memory cue is a vivid story delivered as though it were emotionally neutral.'),
  ('2011-200', 'Behavioral parent management applies learning principles to daily family routines. The goal is not to blame parents for ADHD; it is to give caregivers tools that make expectations and consequences more predictable. ADHD symptoms are especially sensitive to environmental structure because tasks requiring sustained organization, delayed reward, and inhibition recur at home and school. Think of parent training as changing the feedback system around behavior, not as trying to talk a child out of having ADHD.'),
  ('2011-201', 'Myopathies are often recognized by where weakness occurs. Proximal muscles move the body against gravity, including standing from a chair, climbing stairs, and lifting the arms, so proximal weakness points toward muscle disease. Neuropathic disorders more often produce distal weakness, sensory changes, or reflex abnormalities. The classic contrast for this question is polymyositis with proximal weakness versus inclusion body myositis with prominent finger-flexor and quadriceps involvement.'),
  ('2011-202', 'Young children are uniquely dependent on caregivers and physically vulnerable to inflicted injury. They also cannot reliably describe events, leave unsafe settings, or activate outside support. These features explain why the earliest years carry the greatest risk of death from abuse. For recall, connect “maximum dependence and minimum physical reserve” with the birth-to-three group. This epidemiologic fact supports vigilance; it does not determine whether an individual injury is abusive.'),
  ('2011-203', 'Preschool development includes symbolic play: children use objects, roles, and imagined characters to represent ideas and relationships. Imaginary companions can provide a safe way to rehearse social roles, feelings, and control. The crucial clinical distinction is whether the child remains developmentally engaged, flexible, and functional. A memory cue is that pretend play expands in preschool, whereas psychosis is more likely to disrupt reality testing and functioning.'),
  ('2011-204', 'The therapeutic alliance includes agreement on goals, agreement on tasks, and a working bond. In psychodynamic treatment, what occurs between patient and therapist can illuminate recurrent relational patterns, but interpretation should be timed and collaborative. A concern voiced directly in session is valuable material: it may be a practical question, an expectation shaped by prior relationships, or a rupture needing repair. Here-and-now process is often the best first move when alliance strain emerges.'),
  ('2011-205', 'SAMe is a naturally occurring methyl donor involved in many cellular reactions. Its name is easy to remember because it connects a biochemical role, methylation, to a supplement sometimes used in mood treatment. Board questions commonly distinguish it from products that may affect sleep, cognition, or other symptoms but do not have the same established association with antidepressant efficacy. Natural describes origin, not predictable efficacy, safety, purity, or lack of interactions.'),
  ('2011-206', 'Factitious disorder is organized around the psychological need to occupy the patient role, whereas malingering is organized around an external incentive such as avoiding work, obtaining money, or evading legal consequences. Personality-disorder associations are clinical patterns, not diagnostic shortcuts. Borderline personality disorder is memorable here because severe instability in relationships, identity, and self-directed behavior can complicate recurrent healthcare presentations. The key task remains distinguishing intentional symptom falsification from genuine disease without abandoning respectful care.'),
  ('2011-207', 'Pseudotumor cerebri, now generally called idiopathic intracranial hypertension, resembles an intracranial mass syndrome despite the absence of a mass. Its classic pattern includes symptoms of raised pressure, papilledema, normal brain imaging, normal cerebrospinal fluid composition, and a clearly elevated opening pressure. Here, 200 mm H2O is not clearly elevated by current adult criteria. The independent adjudication retains B as the historical best-listed answer but emphasizes that venous sinus thrombosis and other secondary causes still require appropriate exclusion.'),
  ('2011-208', 'Competence law separates two time points that are often confused on examinations. Competence asks about functioning now, during legal proceedings; criminal responsibility or insanity asks about mental state during the alleged act. This distinction explains why competence can change with delirium, psychosis, or treatment, whereas the alleged offense is fixed in the past. A useful memory cue is that competence concerns whether the defendant can “go forward” with the case by understanding proceedings and assisting counsel.'),
  ('2011-209', 'Glutamate is the brain’s major excitatory transmitter and is fundamental to learning and synaptic plasticity. Addiction research uses this fact to explain why places, people, or objects can become powerful triggers after repeated pairing with drug use. Dopamine is often remembered as the reward transmitter, but the persistence and learned reactivation of stimulant seeking rely heavily on glutamatergic connections between cortical control systems and reward-related striatal circuitry. Think of dopamine as assigning salience and glutamate as helping encode and reactivate the learned pathway.'),
  ('2011-210', 'Bulimia nervosa is organized around a self-perpetuating binge–compensate cycle. Attempts to rigidly control weight or shape can precede binge eating, and guilt or fear of weight gain can drive purging afterward. The diagnostic concept includes recurrent loss-of-control eating, inappropriate compensatory behavior, and undue influence of weight or shape on self-evaluation. The contrast with anorexia is especially testable: significantly low weight is required for anorexia nervosa, but not for bulimia nervosa.'),
  ('2011-211', 'A microdeletion can affect multiple organ systems and neurodevelopmental pathways, which is why 22q11 deletion syndrome has several names based on different clinical features. Its psychiatric teaching value lies in linking a recognizable genetic developmental syndrome with later vulnerability to psychosis. This is not a one-gene explanation for schizophrenia; rather, it illustrates how genetic variation can meaningfully shift risk while outcome remains dependent on additional developmental and environmental factors.'),
  ('2011-212', 'Emergency medication aims for calm, safe participation rather than unconsciousness. The corrected source adjudication retains lorazepam, but the underlying item remains clinically underspecified because “intoxicated” does not identify the substance. Benzodiazepines often help stimulant-related agitation and withdrawal syndromes, yet can compound respiratory depression from alcohol, opioids, or sedatives. Antipsychotics, supportive medical care, and temporary restraints may have roles depending on etiology and risk. Capacity standards, emergency authority, and restraint rules are also jurisdiction-dependent, making repeated bedside reassessment essential.'),
  ('2011-213', 'The term “kindling” comes from the idea that repeated small inputs can progressively lower the threshold for a larger response. In affective illness, it is used as a model of sensitization: repeated episodes may make subsequent episodes easier to trigger. The memorable progression is from stress-linked recurrence to autonomous recurrence. It is a theory of illness course, not a diagnostic criterion and not proof that a particular patient’s episode has no psychosocial meaning.'),
  ('2011-214', 'The facial nerve controls muscles of facial expression, so peripheral injury produces weakness of both the forehead and lower face on the affected side. Bilateral peripheral facial palsy is uncommon and therefore invites a systemic differential. Lyme disease is memorable because the infection can involve peripheral nerves and meninges, not only skin and joints. On examinations, pairing bilateral facial palsy with Lyme disease is useful, while remembering that geography, exposure, and accompanying symptoms shape real-world likelihood.'),
  ('2011-215', 'Adolescence is a period of increasing peer influence, experimentation, and risk taking; these features can be developmentally common without being benign. Psychiatric assessment separates use from disorder by asking what the use does to functioning, safety, control, and relationships. This distinction prevents both overpathologizing a brief behavior report and missing evolving problems. A simple memory anchor is that diagnosis depends on impairment, risk, and loss of control—not on moral disapproval of a substance.'),
  ('2011-216', 'Irreversible MAOIs reduce the metabolism of monoamines, so tyramine-rich foods or sympathomimetic medicines can cause marked catecholaminergic excess and severe hypertension. Older examination teaching often paired this syndrome with immediate-release nifedipine. Contemporary safety concerns make that association unsuitable as an unsupervised treatment rule because precipitous hypotension can compromise organ perfusion. The durable lesson is prompt recognition of an interaction syndrome and urgent monitored care; the specific answer here should remain explicitly labeled historical and exam-era.'),
  ('2011-217', 'Executive functions are the control processes that organize behavior toward a goal: sustaining a rule, shifting set, planning, monitoring errors, and inhibiting an impulsive response. Go/no-go tasks make inhibition visible at the bedside because the patient must resist doing the action they have just been asked to perform. A helpful contrast is that memory testing asks whether the rule is retained; go/no-go testing asks whether the rule can override an immediate habitual response.'),
  ('2011-218', 'The term selective refers to selectivity of setting, not a willful decision to refuse speech. Many affected children can speak normally in safe, familiar environments but become silent where social evaluation feels threatening. This situational pattern makes school the most memorable setting: classrooms require attendance, verbal responses, peer interaction, and performance in front of others. The diagnosis belongs among anxiety-related conditions, while still requiring attention to developmental and communication differentials.'),
  ('2011-219', 'Grief commonly includes yearning, sadness, disrupted sleep, and a continuing subjective bond with the deceased. The clinical task is to distinguish expected variation from symptoms that are severe, persistent, or associated with marked impairment and suicide risk. A useful memory connection is that grief is not made pathologic by its presence, but depression-level symptoms early in the course signal vulnerability. Context, trajectory, and function matter more than any single emotion.'),
  ('2011-220', 'Central pontine myelinolysis is now often grouped under osmotic demyelination syndrome because injury can occur outside the pons as well. The classic association is a patient who initially improves as sodium normalizes and then develops new brainstem-related neurologic deficits days later. The key physiology is adaptation: a chronically hypotonic brain has shed osmolytes, so abruptly restoring tonicity creates an osmotic shock. Correct slowly after chronic adaptation is the memorable preventive principle.'),
  ('2011-221', 'Child maltreatment is best understood through an ecological model: risks can arise from child vulnerability, caregiver functioning, family stress, and community conditions. Financial stress is a family-level stressor that may interact with isolation, mental illness, substance use, and violence. Remember that a risk factor is probabilistic, not diagnostic. The clinical aim is to identify danger and strengthen supports without equating poverty with maltreatment.'),
  ('2011-222', 'ECT passes electrical current through the scalp to induce a generalized therapeutic seizure. Magnetic stimulation can be focused more readily because magnetic fields pass through the skull differently from direct electrical current. Magnetic seizure therapy applies that physical principle to convulsive treatment: target relevant cortical circuitry while limiting unnecessary stimulation. The exam-level contrast is straightforward: its hoped-for gain is cognitive tolerability, not avoidance of anesthesia or merely producing a bigger seizure.'),
  ('2011-223', 'Interferons are immune-signaling proteins used therapeutically to modify antiviral and immune responses. Their systemic effects can resemble an inflammatory illness, including fatigue, malaise, and sleep disruption, and can be accompanied by mood and cognitive symptoms. This link illustrates the interaction between immune activation and behavior. For historical PRITE questions, interferon-alpha is the signature hepatitis C treatment associated with depression; hepatitis C treatment has changed substantially, but the adverse-effect association remains educationally important.'),
  ('2011-224', 'The mental-status examination separates related but distinct constructs. Insight is awareness and understanding of illness, whereas judgment is the application of reasoning to choices and consequences. Hypothetical questions offer a quick screen for judgment, while recent behavior, including medication management, financial decisions, and responses to illness, may provide stronger evidence. Consider the patient’s actual access to transportation, money, pharmacies, and support before interpreting an answer. A useful memory link is that insight asks, “Do I recognize the problem?” whereas judgment asks, “What will I do about it?”'),
  ('2011-225', 'Learning disabilities are specific impairments in acquiring academic skills, not a measure of overall intelligence, motivation, or parenting. Reading depends on efficiently linking speech sounds to written symbols, recognizing words, and developing fluency and comprehension. The contemporary diagnostic framework groups reading, written-expression, and mathematics impairments under specific learning disorder, but the older term “reading disorder” remains a high-yield board concept. Diagnosis requires persistent difficulty and clinically meaningful academic impact despite appropriate opportunity to learn. Remember that reading is the academic skill most commonly identified as impaired in school-age children.'),
  ('2011-226', 'Crossed brainstem syndromes combine a cranial-nerve deficit on the lesion side with motor or sensory deficits on the opposite side. In Weber syndrome, third-nerve fascicles and descending motor fibers run close together in the ventral midbrain. Posterior cerebral artery perforators supply much of this midbrain territory. By contrast, a cortical hemispheric lesion does not usually create an ipsilateral ocular-motor palsy. The memory pattern is simple: an eye-movement deficit on one side and body weakness on the other indicate a midbrain localization rather than a primary cortical syndrome.'),
  ('2011-227', 'Malingering differs from factitious disorder mainly in the incentive: malingering involves an external goal, such as avoiding work or obtaining money, whereas factitious disorder involves assuming the sick role without an obvious external reward. Litigation makes external incentives relevant, but it does not prove deception. No single symptom style, test result, or demeanor establishes intentional feigning. A sound forensic assessment compares history, examination, records, collateral information, and observed behavior over time. Neutral documentation protects both the fairness of the evaluation and the clinician’s ability to explain the basis for conclusions.'),
  ('2011-228', 'Quality improvement asks how a health-care system can reliably produce better care. Its logic is cyclical: measure, identify a gap, change a process, and measure again. Patient satisfaction or experience data are often included because quality encompasses more than clinical outcomes alone. For exams, distinguish continuous quality improvement''s ongoing systems focus from a root-cause review after one sentinel event or a utilization review of whether services were necessary.'),
  ('2011-229', 'Depression and obesity can form a bidirectional clinical cycle. Low mood may disrupt sleep, motivation, activity, appetite, and daily routines, while weight-related stigma, medical burden, and reduced participation can worsen mood. Longitudinal association does not establish that depression is the sole cause of later obesity for an individual patient. Board questions commonly test the association rather than a single biologic mechanism. Assessment should consider medications, family environment, socioeconomic barriers, endocrine illness, eating patterns, and access to activity. The memorable connection is that depression affects routines that are central to long-term weight trajectory.'),
  ('2011-230', 'The cortical motor map places the lower extremity on the medial surface of each hemisphere, supplied largely by the anterior cerebral artery. The face and upper limb are represented more laterally, where middle cerebral artery supply predominates. This creates a high-yield vascular-localization pair: anterior cerebral artery equals contralateral leg greater than arm weakness. “Anterior” here refers to the arterial territory, not simply a nonspecific frontal-lobe syndrome.'),
  ('2011-231', 'The prefrontal cortex supports executive functions, including organizing behavior, delaying responses, considering consequences, regulating attention, and modulating emotion. Its development is prolonged and depends on both local cortical changes and increasingly efficient connections with other brain regions. White matter growth and region-specific gray matter changes continue during adolescence, so no single simple measure captures development. Developmental neuroscience describes group-level tendencies, not an individual adolescent’s competence or responsibility. A useful memory image is that reward and emotion systems can be highly active while the neural systems that consistently regulate them are still maturing.'),
  ('2011-232', 'The mean uses every value, so extreme observations can shift it substantially. The median depends on rank order and is comparatively robust to outliers. In a right-skewed distribution, a few high observations create a tail to the right; commonly, the mean exceeds the median. In an even-sized sample, the median is the average of the two central ordered values. Dispersion measures, such as standard deviation or interquartile range, answer a different question from central tendency. A quick cue is “most values low, a few much higher”: choose the median to represent a typical observation.'),
  ('2011-233', 'Delirium is an acute disturbance in attention and awareness with additional cognitive change and a fluctuating course. Encephalitis can initially resemble a primary psychiatric syndrome because behavior, perception, sleep, and mood may change early. Anticholinergic medications such as diphenhydramine can worsen confusion by reducing central cholinergic signaling. A careful bedside assessment of attention and alertness is therefore essential when new paranoia or hallucinations arise. The practical memory link is that abrupt psychiatric change with impaired attention is medical until proven otherwise, especially after an infectious prodrome.'),
  ('2011-234', 'Huntington disease is memorable as a triad of movement, mind, and mood: chorea, cognitive or executive decline, and psychiatric or behavioral symptoms. Its autosomal dominant inheritance can produce a vertical pattern across generations, although family history may be incomplete because of estrangement, early death, or lack of diagnosis. Symptoms often emerge in adulthood and progress over time. Basal-ganglia dysfunction contributes to hyperkinetic movements. In psychiatric settings, personality change, irritability, depression, or cognitive decline may precede or accompany clearly recognizable motor findings, so neurologic clues and pedigree information are important.'),
  ('2011-235', 'Paraphilic disorders are not defined merely by unusual interests. In presentations involving nonconsenting persons, acting on urges with another person is clinically and ethically significant; formal diagnosis also depends on the full pattern and duration of symptoms. Frotteurism derives from rubbing and centers on contact with an unsuspecting, nonconsenting person. The stem identifies a behavior pattern rather than establishing a complete clinical diagnosis of the accused clinician. In a clinical setting, allegations of boundary violations must be addressed as misconduct and a patient-safety issue, not treated only as an academic diagnostic question.'),
  ('2011-236', 'Sedative-hypnotic withdrawal demonstrates physiologic dependence: the brain adapts to persistent drug-induced inhibition and becomes overactive when that inhibition is abruptly removed. Alcohol and benzodiazepines differ pharmacologically but share clinically important withdrawal features because both enhance inhibitory central nervous system signaling. A useful examination contrast is that opioid withdrawal is often dramatic and miserable, whereas alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal is the classic withdrawal state associated with seizures, delirium, and autonomic instability. This distinction guides emergency triage.'),
  ('2011-237', 'The stages-of-change model describes readiness as dynamic. Precontemplation denotes little recognition or intention to change; contemplation combines awareness with ambivalence; preparation adds intent and planning; action is active modification; and maintenance sustains gains. This record contains a corrected-key issue: the earlier precontemplation answer was replaced with B after adjudication because “It may be a problem” and “I’ll think about it” show consideration of change. External pressure, consequences, or appointment attendance alone do not determine the stage.'),
  ('2011-238', 'Kant''s ethics is often remembered through the categorical imperative: moral action should follow principles that can be applied universally while respecting every person as an end. In clinical work, this creates a counterweight to purely outcome-based reasoning. Psychiatry frequently involves consent, coercion, privacy, and capacity, making Kant''s emphasis on dignity and autonomy especially memorable. Although Kant did not create a psychiatric treatment system, his moral philosophy supplies an enduring basis for thinking about ethical duties toward patients.'),
  ('2011-239', 'Substituted judgment differs from a pure best-interests standard. It asks what treatment decision this particular person would make if able, using available evidence about values, preferences, beliefs, and prior choices. Rogers is especially associated with Massachusetts rights to refuse antipsychotic medication. The board-relevant distinction is that clinician recommendation, family consent, and legal authorization are not interchangeable sources of authority. Capacity is decision-specific and may change over time. Because procedures and emergency exceptions vary by jurisdiction and setting, clinicians should involve institutional legal resources when medication over objection is being considered.'),
  ('2011-240', 'Many psychopharmacology interactions are best remembered by mechanism rather than isolated drug pairs. Hepatic enzyme induction can lower concentrations of medications cleared through affected pathways. Several antiseizure drugs are well known for this effect, and oxcarbazepine is clinically relevant. The memorable link is that an antiseizure or mood-stabilizing drug that induces metabolism may make another medication disappear faster, including oral contraceptive hormones. Contraception should be reviewed whenever such a medication is initiated or discontinued.'),
  ('2011-241', 'HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder is linked to central nervous system effects of HIV and immune activation rather than a single focal tumor-like lesion. Historically, severe cases were called HIV-associated dementia; contemporary terminology recognizes a spectrum of cognitive impairment. The pathology clue is the combination of macrophage and microglial activation with multinucleated giant cells in diffuse white-matter disease. This contrasts with focal masses or characteristic viral demyelinating lesions from other opportunistic conditions. Cognitive slowing, executive dysfunction, apathy, and gait changes suggest a subcortical pattern, but a clinical evaluation must still exclude delirium, medication effects, and treatable central nervous system disease.'),
  ('2011-242', 'The term déjà vu is French for “already seen,” which makes its meaning easy to retain. Paramnesias are errors in memory or recognition rather than simple forgetfulness. A useful paired concept is jamais vu, in which a familiar stimulus feels strangely unfamiliar. Both show that memory involves more than recalling facts: it also generates a subjective feeling of familiarity. In paramnesia, that feeling is mismatched to reality, even when the person recognizes the mismatch.'),
  ('2011-243', 'Sensorium refers to the overall state of consciousness and awareness of self and environment. Delirium is clinically defined by a disturbance in attention and awareness that develops over a short period and tends to fluctuate. This differs from many primary psychiatric disorders, in which consciousness is generally clear despite severe mood, thought, or perceptual symptoms. The practical memory cue is simple: clear consciousness may fit a primary psychiatric syndrome, whereas clouding requires an urgent medical search for a cause.'),
  ('2011-244', 'DBT combines acceptance-oriented and change-oriented strategies. Its skills modules commonly cover mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, and interpersonal effectiveness. A central challenge is transferring these skills from a calm office to a high-arousal real-life situation; telephone coaching was designed to bridge that gap. In comprehensive DBT, coaching is planned and bounded by treatment agreements, not an informal promise of unrestricted availability. Diary cards help monitor targets, and therapist consultation teams support treatment delivery, but neither is the distinctive patient-directed feature tested here. The memorable phrase is “skills in the moment.”'),
  ('2011-245', 'AA uses spiritual language, including the idea of a power greater than oneself, but individual members interpret that language in diverse ways. Mutual-help groups can also provide practical social mechanisms: sober peers, routine, identity change, role models, accountability, and a place to seek help during vulnerability. This makes it misleading to reduce AA’s potential effects to one spiritual event. Participation is voluntary, and fit may differ across cultures, communities, and personal beliefs. The board-relevant conclusion is not that spirituality has no value, but that evidence does not establish it as the sole direct explanation for abstinence.'),
  ('2011-246', 'Urine toxicology commonly begins with immunoassays because they are rapid and practical, but these tests sacrifice chemical specificity. Medications or metabolites may bind the assay antibody sufficiently to produce a positive result. Bupropion is a useful example because it can trigger an amphetamine signal on some screening platforms. A screening result is therefore preliminary evidence, not a final forensic conclusion. Definitive testing uses a more specific analytic method when accurate identification of the substance matters clinically.'),
  ('2011-247', 'Suicide risk includes dynamic and more enduring elements. Intoxication, agitation, and a current plan may fluctuate rapidly, whereas a prior attempt records behavior during an earlier crisis. Hopelessness reflects a dangerous future-oriented cognitive state when it persists. A useful memory pairing is “past action plus no future”: prior suicidal behavior and a belief that improvement is impossible. Neither factor determines an individual outcome, but together they warrant sustained, careful reassessment within a broader clinical formulation.'),
  ('2011-248', 'Electrodiagnostic localization asks which anatomic structures share the observed abnormality. Tibialis anterior and tibialis posterior belong to different named peripheral nerves but converge at the L5 root. This pattern favors radiculopathy over a lesion of either peripheral nerve. Radiculopathy also commonly preserves sensory nerve action potentials because the sensory ganglion is outside the spinal canal. The practical rule is that abnormalities across different peripheral nerves sharing one root favor a root lesion.'),
  ('2011-249', 'Phobias are organized around a particular object or situation, whereas panic disorder is organized around fear of recurrent, often unexpected panic and its consequences. Driving fears may overlap with several phobia categories depending on the feared feature, such as heights, loss of control, or a particular route. The essential memory connection is expected panic in the presence of a cue: the cue is the diagnostic anchor. Avoidance may reduce distress briefly while preserving the fear over time.'),
  ('2011-250', 'Mirtazapine is often remembered as a noradrenergic and specific serotonergic antidepressant. Presynaptic alpha-2 receptors act as inhibitory brakes on neurotransmitter release. Blocking these receptors increases norepinephrine signaling and supports serotonin release, while postsynaptic serotonin-receptor blockade shapes some clinical effects. Histamine H1 antagonism explains the prominent sedation and weight gain that many patients experience. A useful memory hook is that mirtazapine removes the alpha-2 brake while also blocking H1.'),
  ('2011-251', 'Panic symptoms can result from acute autonomic activation rather than a primary anxiety disorder. Pheochromocytoma is memorable because catecholamine surges can create a body-first panic phenotype with racing heart, sweating, tremulousness, and intense alarm. The classic medical triad is episodic headache, sweating, and palpitations, often with hypertension. The diagnostic lesson is humility: psychiatric symptoms can be genuine while still arising from an endocrine disorder that requires medical evaluation.'),
  ('2011-252', 'Professional health programs were developed around early, systematic responses to impairment that protect both recovery and public safety. Their structure often combines clinical treatment with formal accountability measures, making them different from ordinary confidential treatment. This dual role explains why participants may have monitoring contracts, toxicology testing, workplace requirements, or reporting obligations. A useful memory phrase is “support plus surveillance”: the program helps an impaired physician recover while monitoring fitness for practice.'),
  ('2011-253', 'Advance care planning extends autonomy across time: a capable person communicates choices for a time when they may be unable to communicate or decide. This differs from guardianship, which is typically imposed through a legal process after incapacity has been established. In psychiatry, advance directives are particularly memorable because symptoms can fluctuate. Planning during wellness can guide future crisis care while still allowing clinicians to respond to immediate legal and safety obligations. The document is a planning tool, not a guarantee that every requested intervention will be available.'),
  ('2011-254', 'Public-health descriptions distinguish suicide deaths from attempts and ideation. Older men have often had high suicide mortality, influenced by factors such as depression, medical illness, isolation, alcohol use, and access to highly lethal means. This question deliberately specifies a historical period, so its demographic conclusion should not be generalized as a permanent ranking. The durable clinical lesson is that late-life depression and suicidal thinking in men require direct, careful assessment and attention to means safety.'),
  ('2011-255', 'Giant cell arteritis is a large-vessel vasculitis that commonly affects cranial branches of the carotid circulation. Its association with polymyalgia rheumatica creates a memorable cluster: headache and scalp tenderness paired with shoulder or hip girdle stiffness. Temporal artery biopsy samples affected tissue and can show inflammatory pathology. The critical clinical memory is that a psychiatric history should not distract from a syndrome carrying risk of irreversible visual injury. Treatment is urgent when clinical suspicion is high.'),
  ('2011-256', 'Pretend play is a visible expression of symbolic thought. The child is no longer limited to an object''s immediate, literal use and can mentally substitute one thing for another. This capacity supports expansion from simple object substitution to roles, stories, and shared imaginary scenarios. A useful memory image is a block held to the ear as a phone: the physical object remains a block, but its represented meaning changes. Language and social play enrich this ability without defining the core symbolic operation.'),
  ('2011-257', 'Antidepressant switches are guided by how long the prior drug and its active metabolites remain pharmacologically meaningful. Fluoxetine is unusual among SSRIs because both fluoxetine and norfluoxetine are long lived. Phenelzine irreversibly inhibits monoamine oxidase, so close sequencing can magnify serotonin-related toxicity. A simple memory hook is “fluoxetine lingers”: when an MAOI follows, think five weeks rather than the shorter interval sometimes used after other SSRIs.'),
  ('2011-258', 'Schizophrenia care moved beyond an exclusively individual model as clinical experience showed that relapse is affected by medication continuity, stress, communication patterns, and available support. Family psychoeducation translates this knowledge into practical skills and shared planning. It is distinct from blaming family dynamics for schizophrenia. The memorable concept is “teach the team around the patient”: informed relatives can recognize early warning signs, support treatment continuity, and help reduce avoidable crises.'),
  ('2011-259', 'Children often reach mental health services because behavior is observable and disruptive to home or school routines, whereas anxiety, depression, and other internal distress can remain hidden. Referral frequency should not be confused with prevalence, severity, or the child''s own chief concern. The useful distinction is “the referral is the signal, not the diagnosis.” Behavioral complaints invite assessment across neurodevelopmental, emotional, family, school, sleep, and trauma-related domains before treatment is selected.'),
  ('2011-260', 'Syphilis has long been called a great imitator because its manifestations vary across stages and organ systems. Its historical importance to psychiatry comes from general paresis, a late neuropsychiatric manifestation that populated many institutions before antibiotics. Fever therapy using malaria was an early attempt to alter the course of this otherwise devastating illness. The modern memory link is that psychiatric symptoms can be the surface manifestation of an infectious neurologic disorder, especially when cognitive or focal neurologic abnormalities are also present.'),
  ('2011-261', 'Mutual-help groups are not a single, uniform intervention because participants engage in very different ways. Sponsorship is memorable because it turns attendance into an ongoing supportive relationship with contact, guidance, and accountability. This helps explain why it can be more informative than merely counting meetings. The board-relevant concept is active involvement rather than a specific meeting location, reading habit, or program milestone. Recovery planning should remain individualized and should not depend on a single support model.'),
  ('2011-262', 'ECT intentionally induces a controlled generalized seizure, so medications that alter seizure quality or recovery require special attention. Lithium has a narrow therapeutic index and may become more hazardous with dehydration, renal impairment, physiologic stress, or ECT-related changes. The practical memory pair is “lithium plus ECT: hold or closely reassess.” This does not mean lithium can never be used during an ECT course; it means the combination requires deliberate specialist management and monitoring.'),
  ('2011-263', 'Hypocretin and orexin are two names for the same hypothalamic neuropeptide system. Its central role can be remembered as stabilizing behavioral states: wakefulness remains wakefulness, and REM-related muscle atonia remains within sleep. When the system is lost, state boundaries blur, producing daytime sleepiness and REM-like intrusions such as cataplexy. Recognizing orexin as an alternate name is useful in sleep-medicine discussions and examinations.'),
  ('2011-264', 'Prolactin rises physiologically during pregnancy and lactation. Dopamine normally inhibits prolactin release, explaining why dopamine-blocking medications can raise it. Clinical reasoning usually proceeds from common, reversible explanations toward specialized evaluation, while the degree of elevation and associated symptoms guide urgency. The useful sequence is “physiology first, then drugs and gland”: exclude pregnancy before interpreting a markedly abnormal prolactin result as medication-related or pituitary in origin.'),
  ('2011-265', 'Postpartum psychosis typically begins abruptly in the early period after childbirth and can include insomnia, mood symptoms, confusion, delusions, and hallucinations. It differs from more common and less severe postpartum mood symptoms because psychosis and impaired reality testing sharply elevate safety concerns. Its association with bipolar-spectrum illness is important for longitudinal care, but the emergency principle is straightforward: a delusion involving an infant requires immediate safety assessment, supervision, and urgent psychiatric evaluation. Both maternal and infant safety must be addressed.'),
  ('2011-266', 'The telescoping concept describes a compressed population-level trajectory from early alcohol use to dependence and related harms in women compared with men. It does not determine the course of any one patient. Differences in total body water and first-pass metabolism help explain why an equivalent amount of alcohol can yield greater physiologic exposure in many women. The memory image is a telescope collapsing distance: less time between first drink and serious consequences, with substantial individual variation.'),
  ('2011-267', 'A useful receptor-to-adverse-effect association is that H1 blockade makes patients hungry and horizontal. Central histamine signaling promotes wakefulness, so blocking H1 receptors tends to cause somnolence. Histamine pathways also participate in appetite regulation, helping explain why H1-antagonist drugs can increase food intake and weight. This pattern occurs to varying degrees with several psychotropics, but mirtazapine is particularly associated with it. Its alpha-2 and serotonin-receptor actions explain other features of its clinical profile and should not be confused with the H1 effects.'),
  ('2011-268', 'Psychotherapies differ partly in the degree of ego strength and reflective capacity they presume. Supportive approaches prioritize preserving or restoring functioning when a patient is under strain or has fragile coping. They use alliance, reassurance, structure, and practical assistance to reduce distress and support adaptation. Expressive approaches ask the patient to tolerate more sustained reflection on conflict, defenses, and affect. The useful contrast is “support before insight”: interpretation may be deferred when it would exceed the patient’s current capacity, but supportive treatment still has clear therapeutic boundaries and goals.'),
  ('2011-269', 'The word “binge” describes an episode of loss-of-control overeating; it does not itself determine the diagnosis. Bulimia nervosa pairs binge eating with repeated efforts to prevent weight gain. Binge-eating disorder retains the binge episodes but lacks recurring compensation. Thus, patients with bulimia may maintain a normal-range weight despite serious illness, whereas patients with binge-eating disorder often do not. Weight is informative but neither required nor sufficient for either diagnosis. A durable memory contrast is that bulimia attempts to undo binges, while binge-eating disorder does not.'),
  ('2011-270', 'A motor unit consists of one motor neuron and all the muscle fibers it innervates. In a primary myopathy, fewer healthy fibers contribute to each motor unit’s electrical signal, making the recorded potential smaller and briefer. In chronic denervation, surviving axons can reinnervate orphaned fibers, enlarging motor units and creating long-duration, high-amplitude potentials. Recruitment patterns also help: weak myopathic units are recruited early, whereas neurogenic disorders may show reduced recruitment. Think “muscle fibers lost equals small units; axons compensate equals large units.”'),
  ('2011-271', 'Theory of mind is the capacity to infer that other people have beliefs, intentions, knowledge, and feelings that may differ from one''s own. False-belief tasks make this invisible skill observable: the child must inhibit privileged knowledge of where the marble really is and use the character''s limited information instead. A compact memory cue is “Where will she look?” The answer must come from the doll''s perspective, not from the child''s knowledge of reality. This is distinct from merely understanding language or engaging in pretend play.'),
  ('2011-272', 'Acute trauma can narrow attention and sustain a fight-flight-freeze response even after external danger has ended. Psychological first aid emphasizes safety, calm, practical needs, connection, and choice rather than forced emotional processing. The initial task is not to erase distress or obtain a detailed trauma narrative. It is to help the person regain enough orientation, control, and immediate security to use available support and make informed choices. Ongoing safety assessment remains necessary, especially after interpersonal violence. “Safe first, then support” is a useful organizing principle.'),
  ('2011-273', 'TMS works because a rapidly changing magnetic field can induce electrical currents in superficial cortical tissue without an incision. Depression protocols focus on prefrontal networks because these networks participate in cognitive control and affect regulation and connect with deeper limbic circuitry. The motor cortex is important in TMS practice mainly as a convenient physiologic reference for measuring motor threshold. That distinction prevents a common examination error: motor cortex for calibration, prefrontal cortex for depression treatment. Different protocols may vary in laterality and stimulation pattern.'),
  ('2011-274', 'Therapeutic drug monitoring is most useful when a medication has a reasonably interpretable concentration-response relationship and substantial person-to-person pharmacokinetic variability. Nortriptyline is a classic psychiatry example because metabolism, adherence, age, and drug interactions can produce markedly different blood concentrations at the same dose. A carefully timed level estimates drug exposure, not symptom severity or treatment success by itself. Results should be considered with adverse effects, clinical response, cardiac risk, and medication changes. Think of the level as answering “how much drug exposure is present?” rather than “is the patient well?”'),
  ('2011-275', 'Many common laboratory abnormalities associated with alcohol use reflect downstream organ effects rather than alcohol exposure itself. Macrocytosis and transaminase elevations are therefore nonspecific and may persist or arise for unrelated reasons. CDT reflects an alcohol-associated change in transferrin carbohydrate composition, which gives it greater specificity for sustained heavy drinking in this historical comparison. It is not a perfect detector and should not be used in isolation to label a patient’s behavior. Board questions commonly contrast a more alcohol-linked biomarker with familiar but nonspecific complete blood count and liver-test abnormalities.'),
  ('2011-276', 'Group formats vary by leadership, structure, and therapeutic mechanism. Process-oriented psychotherapy groups often treat interactions among members as a live source of insight and change. Self-help groups instead draw much of their value from mutual identification, shared experience, role modeling, and ongoing support. The quick contrast is this: a process group examines what happens between members, whereas a self-help group helps members manage the problem they share. Both can be valuable, but their primary aims and leadership structures differ.'),
  ('2011-277', 'Reactive aggression is an impulsive response to perceived provocation, threat, or frustration, unlike planned instrumental aggression. Medication may reduce its frequency or intensity in selected youth, but it does not identify or resolve every driver of behavior. Risperidone became a commonly tested association because evidence supports benefit across several pediatric conditions involving severe irritability or aggression. Evidence-supported does not mean risk-free, nor does it mean medication should precede careful psychosocial assessment and behavioral intervention. Clear target symptoms, caregiver input, and regular adverse-effect monitoring are essential whenever an antipsychotic is used.'),
  ('2011-278', 'Buspirone is memorable because it separates anxiolysis from the classic benzodiazepine-and-GABA association. Serotonin 5-HT1A receptors occur at presynaptic and postsynaptic sites involved in anxiety circuitry, and partial agonism produces a gradual clinical effect. A practical contrast is “benzodiazepines: GABA and rapid relief; buspirone: 5-HT1A and gradual relief.” This distinction explains why buspirone is less useful as an as-needed rescue medication and why it does not share the usual benzodiazepine dependence profile. It remains only one component of treatment for an anxiety disorder.'),
  ('2011-279', 'Group psychotherapy uses the group itself as a therapeutic social environment. Cohesion, confidentiality, and reciprocal accountability are part of the treatment mechanism, not merely administrative expectations. Conditions or behaviors that substantially impair trust are especially important during group formation, when norms are still being established. Patients with many psychiatric diagnoses can benefit when symptoms are stable and they can participate safely. The central assessment is whether the person can respect limits, engage honestly, and tolerate feedback without endangering others or the treatment. Groups need enough safety to make honesty possible.'),
  ('2011-280', 'Tardive dyskinesia is a delayed hyperkinetic syndrome associated with chronic dopamine receptor blockade, often affecting the mouth, tongue, and face. Patients may be unaware of the movements, so routine observation and structured screening matter. It contrasts with drug-induced parkinsonism, which is hypokinetic and may respond to anticholinergic medication. The word “tardive” means delayed: chronic exposure plus stereotyped orofacial movements should trigger the association. Symptoms can persist despite medication changes, so recognizing them early and balancing movement risk against relapse risk are both clinically important.'),
  ('2011-281', 'Route of administration can markedly change a drug combination’s effect. Buprenorphine/naloxone uses this principle: the therapeutic sublingual route favors buprenorphine’s effect, whereas injection makes the antagonist consequential. The combination is sometimes called abuse-deterrent, but it is not a complete safeguard against misuse and does not replace comprehensive opioid use disorder care. The key pharmacology is route-specific bioavailability, not a weak affinity of naloxone for opioid receptors. Remember that naloxone is poorly absorbed under the tongue but becomes clinically active when injected.'),
  ('2011-282', 'Periodic paralysis is a channelopathy in which altered ion-channel function makes skeletal muscle electrically inexcitable during attacks. In hypokalemic forms, a fall in extracellular potassium is associated with transient weakness rather than muscle destruction, so recovery can be dramatic and complete between episodes. Attacks often follow strenuous activity followed by rest, and the inheritance pattern can be highly informative. This presentation differs from myotonia, in which stiffness and delayed relaxation predominate. Think “exercise, then rest, then weakness with low potassium,” while remembering that cardiac monitoring is important during an acute hypokalemic episode.'),
  ('2011-283', 'The terms relapse and recurrence help organize antidepressant duration. Relapse is return of symptoms before full recovery from the current episode, whereas recurrence is a new episode after recovery. Continuation treatment protects the recovery period, while maintenance treatment is especially relevant to recurrent illness. Evidence-based maintenance usually preserves the dose that achieved remission because a lower dose may not provide the same protection. Duration and eventual discontinuation remain individualized according to episode history, adverse effects, patient preference, and current risk. A useful memory principle is that the dose that produced remission is usually the dose that maintains it.'),
  ('2011-284', 'Professional boundaries protect a relationship with an inherent power imbalance. Yet rigid uniformity can itself be insensitive to culture, setting, or humane clinical need. The key distinction is not simply whether a customary boundary was crossed, but whether the act serves treatment and can be managed transparently without exploitation. Intent alone is insufficient; the likely effect on the patient, treatment frame, and future expectations also matters. Consultation and documentation can make reasoning explicit when a crossing is clinically justifiable. The useful conceptual pair is “crossing requires reflection; violation causes harm.”'),
  ('2011-285', 'Potassium is predominantly intracellular, so a low serum potassium value can represent a significant total-body deficit. Gastric acid loss and volume depletion from vomiting activate renal mechanisms that further promote potassium excretion. This produces the classic association of vomiting with metabolic alkalosis and low chloride, while the most clinically memorable electrolyte danger is hypokalemia. Laxatives and diuretics can also contribute to potassium loss, although their acid-base effects may differ from those of vomiting.'),
  ('2011-286', 'The facial nerve has motor, parasympathetic, and special sensory functions, explaining why a facial palsy can affect expression, eyelid closure, tearing, and sound tolerance. Hyperacusis is especially useful for localization because denervation of the stapedius muscle reduces sound dampening. Bell palsy is the idiopathic diagnosis used when this acute peripheral facial-nerve syndrome occurs without a more specific cause. Objective facial sensory loss should prompt consideration of an alternative or additional process.'),
  ('2011-287', 'Family therapies often focus on interactional patterns rather than locating pathology in one identified patient. Reframing changes the frame around the same observable event: the behavior remains, but its significance is described in a less adversarial and more constructive way. This can interrupt cycles of accusation, defensiveness, and escalation. It differs from techniques that map family structure, directly alter physical positioning, or address the management of triangles within a family system.'),
  ('2011-288', 'Animism is a useful developmental memory word because animate means living. Early childhood cognition can blur the boundary between an object''s physical presence and a living agent''s intentions. As children develop more mature causal reasoning and perspective-taking, they increasingly distinguish pretend qualities from literal properties of inanimate objects. Animism should be understood within the child''s developmental level and play context rather than automatically treated as a symptom of severe mental illness.'),
  ('2011-289', 'Lacunar infarcts result from occlusion of small penetrating arteries, often in the setting of chronic vascular risk factors. This mechanism differs from emboli arising from atrial fibrillation, cardiac thrombus, or severe proximal carotid disease. The useful framework is mechanism first: determine whether a stroke is likely cardioembolic, large-artery atherosclerotic, or small-vessel before matching an antithrombotic approach. The question supplies findings intended to support a small-vessel, noncardioembolic mechanism.'),
  ('2011-290', 'Aging changes pharmacokinetics as well as physiology. Renal blood flow, nephron reserve, and filtration capacity tend to decrease over time, while muscle mass may also decline. This combination makes serum creatinine an imperfect shorthand for renal function in later life. For psychiatry, the concept is memorable because several commonly used medications require renal dosing awareness or closer monitoring. Think less clearance even when the creatinine value appears deceptively ordinary. Medication decisions should therefore consider estimated clearance, comorbidity, and concurrent drugs.'),
  ('2011-291', 'A doctor-patient relationship commonly arises when a physician affirmatively undertakes to diagnose or treat a person and that person relies on the undertaking. The boundary is practical rather than ceremonial: an office location, payment, or paperwork may support the relationship but is not always necessary. Informal curbside encounters can therefore carry ethical and legal significance. General educational comments and referral information are less likely to constitute a specific professional undertaking than individualized treatment advice.'),
  ('2011-292', 'Tuberous sclerosis complex is a genetic neurocutaneous disorder that can involve the brain, skin, kidneys, heart, lungs, and eyes. A useful association is skin plus brain: recognizable skin lesions may coexist with seizures and neurodevelopmental or behavioral symptoms. TSC1 and TSC2 encode proteins that regulate a shared cellular growth-signaling pathway, so pathogenic variants in either gene can cause the disorder. The keyed gene in this question is TSC1.'),
  ('2011-293', 'Addison disease refers to primary failure of the adrenal cortex, causing inadequate cortisol and often aldosterone production. The pigmentation clue follows from feedback physiology: when cortisol is low, pituitary ACTH production rises, and related melanocortin activity increases pigmentation. It is a classic example of an endocrine disorder whose emotional and cognitive symptoms may precede clear medical recognition. The combination of psychiatric, constitutional, and pigmentation findings is more informative than any symptom alone.'),
  ('2011-294', 'Acute stress disorder describes clinically significant reactions occurring soon after trauma, particularly when dissociation is prominent. The duration threshold is the board-style distinction: DSM-IV acute stress disorder occurs from two days to four weeks after trauma, whereas PTSD requires persistence beyond one month. Detachment, derealization, and depersonalization can be trauma-related phenomena rather than evidence of a primary psychotic disorder. The vignette intentionally places symptoms at three weeks to test this temporal distinction.'),
  ('2011-295', 'A karyotype is an organized display of chromosomes. The method has enduring teaching value because it operates at a different scale from molecular sequencing: it is designed to identify whole-chromosome abnormalities and large rearrangements, not individual base changes. Think of it as a photograph of the chromosome set arranged for comparison rather than a readout of a gene’s DNA sequence. Its utility and limitations follow directly from this chromosome-level resolution.'),
  ('2011-296', 'COMT is an enzyme involved in metabolism of catechol-containing compounds, including levodopa. In Parkinson treatment, blocking this metabolic route helps levodopa remain available longer. A useful memory pair is COMT and continuation: these drugs do not replace lost neurons or reverse disease progression; they extend the clinical effect of a levodopa dose. They are especially relevant when benefit becomes shorter between scheduled doses.'),
  ('2011-297', 'Methadone is a long-acting full opioid agonist used for opioid use disorder in structured treatment settings. Board questions often test the historical lesson that maintenance outcomes improve when dosing is adequate to prevent withdrawal and craving. The useful contrast is between a dose that merely attenuates symptoms briefly and one that provides stable opioid-receptor coverage across the dosing interval. Dose adequacy must always be balanced with sedation, drug interactions, and respiratory safety.'),
  ('2011-298', 'Psychiatric ethics emphasizes transparency, fairness, and preservation of the treatment relationship. A cancellation policy balances the clinician’s reserved time and practice viability against the patient’s need for clear, noncoercive financial expectations. The advance-notice requirement illustrates a broader ethical principle: terms that materially affect treatment access should be discussed before they are enforced. Clear communication also reduces misunderstanding and allows patients to make informed choices about care.'),
  ('2011-299', 'Wilson disease is an inherited disorder in which copper accumulates in tissues, particularly the liver and brain. Its board-style signature crosses specialties: hepatic dysfunction plus movement abnormalities plus psychiatric or cognitive change. Increased urinary copper is memorable because it reflects abnormal copper handling and is part of diagnostic evaluation. No single finding should be interpreted in isolation; the overall clinical picture and additional medical testing guide diagnosis.'),
  ('2011-300', 'Conjoint therapy assumes that participants can speak freely and negotiate conflict without coercion or retaliation. Intimate-partner violence undermines that assumption because power and safety are unequal. The core rule is not to convert abuse into a shared relationship problem during an initial couples session. First address danger, privacy, coercion, access to support, and the patient’s ability to disclose without retaliation. Safety conditions must be established before considering any joint intervention.'),
  ('2012-1', 'Transference is central to psychodynamic thinking because treatment creates a sustained relationship in which familiar expectations can recur. It is not limited to romantic feelings or to negative reactions. A useful memory cue is “transfer” of an old relational template onto a current therapeutic figure. The therapist’s own reactions also matter, but they are called countertransference. In practice, neither phenomenon automatically proves a historical event; they are hypotheses about how the patient is experiencing the relationship in the present.'),
  ('2012-2', 'Gage’s case is memorable because a focal, survivable injury linked a brain region to complex human behavior. Modern accounts require caution because the historical record is incomplete, and claims that he became wholly unrecognizable are oversimplified. The broader concept remains useful: prefrontal networks integrate emotion, reward, social rules, and anticipated consequences. When frontal control is disrupted, behavior may become impulsive, poorly organized, socially inappropriate, or poorly responsive to long-term outcomes. A practical memory cue is “frontal equals forward planning,” while remembering that personality and behavior arise from distributed networks rather than one isolated brain site.'),
  ('2012-3', 'Cluster headache is a trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia, meaning trigeminal pain occurs with cranial parasympathetic activation. The term “cluster” refers to attacks recurring in bouts over weeks or months, often with circadian regularity. A useful contrast is behavior during pain: people with migraine often seek stillness and darkness, whereas people with cluster headache are often restless or agitated. The autonomic signs occur on the same side as the pain. The question’s early-morning attacks, rhinorrhea, tearing, and facial swelling are therefore more informative than the headache location alone.'),
  ('2012-4', 'Autism spectrum disorder is defined by social-communication differences and restricted or repetitive patterns of behavior. These core features usually require developmental and environmental supports rather than a medication-only approach. Board questions often contrast “core autism symptoms” with associated targets such as irritability and aggression. Remember the distinction as skills and access versus symptom burden: communication teaching builds skills, whereas medication may lessen a severe co-occurring behavior that interferes with safety, learning, or care.'),
  ('2012-5', 'Psychodynamic interventions can be pictured on a continuum from helping a patient function now to helping a patient understand underlying patterns. Supportive therapy emphasizes alliance, coping, reality testing, and adaptation. Expressive therapy emphasizes observation, confrontation, and interpretation to increase insight. The same therapist can shift along this continuum as the patient’s stability and treatment needs change. Advice supplies structure from outside, making it highly supportive. In contrast, interpretation asks the patient to consider meanings or conflicts that may not yet be tolerable or useful during acute distress.'),
  ('2012-6', 'CT detects fresh blood because it is denser than surrounding brain and cerebrospinal fluid. “Subarachnoid” means blood has entered the cerebrospinal-fluid-containing space between arachnoid and pia, so it outlines spaces rather than forming only a focal intraparenchymal collection. This distribution distinguishes it from a parenchymal hematoma. On examinations, the useful imaging phrase is “blood in the cisterns and sulci.” Clinically, subarachnoid hemorrhage may present with abrupt severe headache, vomiting, neck discomfort, reduced consciousness, or neurologic deficits, but the scan pattern is the central clue in this question.'),
  ('2012-7', 'ECT induces a controlled therapeutic seizure under anesthesia with muscle relaxation and physiologic monitoring. Modern ECT is a carefully managed medical procedure, despite stigma and inaccurate older portrayals. Its key examination association is not only treatment resistance, but the need for a rapid effective treatment: severe suicidal depression, psychotic depression, catatonia, or profound functional compromise. Treatment planning also includes immediate safety measures and longitudinal depression care. A helpful memory cue is that when depression is dangerous and time matters, ECT is often the highest-yield option.'),
  ('2012-8', 'Physician health programs were designed to connect clinicians with assessment, treatment, monitoring, and advocacy for safe practice while maintaining meaningful accountability. Medical boards have a different core function: regulation of licensure and protection of the public. Their roles frequently intersect, but program structure, confidentiality, reporting obligations, and disciplinary pathways vary by jurisdiction. This distinction explains the dual key: PHPs often manage the monitoring details, whereas boards supply regulatory authority and may require or supervise the arrangement.'),
  ('2012-9', 'A central communication skill in psychosis is separating the patient’s subjective experience from the factual claim. The distress is real even when the explanation may be delusional. This stance is empathic neutrality: acknowledge fear, pain, and isolation while avoiding collusion or blunt contradiction. It improves engagement and leaves room for careful diagnostic and safety assessment. It also avoids falsely assuming that every report by a person with psychosis is delusional. The practical memory cue is simple: affirm the feeling, not the belief, then assess the relevant risks.'),
  ('2012-10', 'Regression is a family of models rather than one single test. It asks how an outcome changes in relation to predictors, often while holding other included variables constant. Linear regression addresses continuous outcomes, logistic regression addresses binary outcomes, and count models address event frequencies. Survival regression is useful when the timing of an event and censoring matter. Odds ratios and relative risks may be reported from an analysis, but they are not themselves the flexible multivariable modeling approach implied by the question. The key cue is the simultaneous examination of two independent variables.'),
  ('2012-11', 'GABA and glycine are the nervous system’s major inhibitory brakes. Glycine is particularly important in spinal inhibitory interneurons, whereas GABA acts broadly throughout the central nervous system. When descending upper motor neuron control is disrupted, spinal reflex circuits can become hyperexcitable, producing the velocity-dependent increase in tone called spasticity. A useful contrast is that rigidity is classically associated with basal ganglia disorders and is less dependent on movement velocity. The examination association is therefore reduced inhibitory influence, especially from GABA and glycine, rather than a primary loss of dopamine.'),
  ('2012-12', 'Acute dystonia is an early extrapyramidal adverse effect of dopamine receptor blockade, particularly with high-potency first-generation antipsychotics. It can involve the eyes, jaw, tongue, neck, or, rarely, larynx. The examination shorthand is relative cholinergic excess in the striatum after dopamine blockade, which is why anticholinergic medication rapidly relieves symptoms. Oculogyric crisis is sustained involuntary upward eye deviation and may be accompanied by marked distress. The practical cue is “eyes stuck up after an antipsychotic,” which should prompt immediate treatment rather than prolonged diagnostic debate.'),
  ('2012-13', 'Panic disorder is often sustained by a feedback loop: bodily sensations are interpreted catastrophically, anxiety rises, symptoms intensify, and avoidance reinforces fear. Adverse early experiences may increase vulnerability through effects on stress regulation and learned danger signals. For examination purposes, distinguish panic disorder from PTSD. Panic disorder centers on unexpected panic attacks and fear of recurrence, whereas PTSD requires a trauma-related syndrome with intrusion, avoidance, negative cognitions or mood, and arousal changes. A history of childhood abuse is relevant to formulation, but it neither proves panic disorder nor explains away all symptoms.'),
  ('2012-14', 'Developmental theories describe adolescence as a period of identity consolidation. Young people increasingly connect past experience, current strengths and limits, social roles, and future aspirations. This makes disappointments feel especially consequential, but it also supports more mature self-reflection and increasingly realistic goal-setting. A useful sequence is autonomy in early life, competence in school-age years, and identity in adolescence. Reasonable goals require future thinking and a sufficiently integrated self-concept. The question asks when this capacity first becomes typical, not when it becomes complete or permanently stable.'),
  ('2012-15', 'Methadone maintenance treatment has long been regulated through opioid treatment programs because methadone is effective but requires systems addressing overdose risk and diversion. Older board questions often test the regulatory framework in place at the time rather than pharmacology or modern eligibility practice. The important distinction is between historical maintenance-treatment admission rules and the broader clinical principle that opioid use disorder warrants timely assessment and evidence-based care. Rules, waivers, and program requirements are particularly time-sensitive. Therefore, a historical keyed answer can be educationally correct while being inappropriate to use as a present-day admission rule.'),
  ('2012-16', 'Inclusion body myositis is an acquired inflammatory myopathy with a distinctive distribution that separates it from many other muscle disorders. “Inclusion body” refers to characteristic pathologic findings, but the examination pattern is often the more useful board clue. Remember the pair “knees and fingers”: quadriceps weakness causes trouble rising, while finger-flexor weakness affects grip and hand function. Its gradual progression and limited response to immunotherapy conceptually distinguish it from more treatment-responsive inflammatory myopathies. Normal sensation and relatively preserved reflexes also help localize the problem to muscle rather than a primary sensory neuropathy.'),
  ('2012-17', 'Prazosin blocks alpha-1 adrenergic receptors. PTSD nightmares are commonly taught through a noradrenergic hyperarousal model in which excessive nighttime adrenergic signaling may intensify fearful dream content and awakenings. Prazosin became a classic examination association for trauma-related nightmares, although individual response and trial results have varied. It is therefore best understood as a targeted option rather than a universal solution. Slow titration and attention to orthostatic symptoms are clinically important. A memory cue is that alpha-1 blockade can turn down the nighttime adrenergic alarm associated with nightmares.'),
  ('2012-18', 'IPT is a time-limited, diagnosis-focused psychotherapy that links symptom change with current relationships and social roles. Its classic focal areas include grief, role disputes, role transitions, and interpersonal deficits. The sick-role concept resolves a common depressive dilemma: symptoms deserve legitimacy and support, yet the person remains an active participant in care. In board questions, permission to be sick plus an expectation of recovery points to IPT rather than a therapy defined primarily by cognitive restructuring, skills training, or interpretation.'),
  ('2012-19', 'OCD is defined by obsessions, compulsions, or both. An obsession is intrusive and unwanted; a compulsion is a repetitive behavior or mental act intended to reduce distress or prevent a feared event, even when the connection is excessive or unrealistic. Contamination is a memorable prototype because washing can be highly visible. Still, OCD should never be reduced to cleanliness: checking, taboo thoughts, symmetry, and “just-right” experiences may be equally impairing for an individual patient.'),
  ('2012-20', 'Orthostatic hypotension reflects an inadequate physiologic response to standing, producing a fall in blood pressure and symptoms such as dizziness, weakness, or syncope. Parkinson disease can involve autonomic nervous system dysfunction in addition to motor symptoms. Fludrocortisone’s memorable mechanism is “fluid for pressure”: its mineralocorticoid effect retains sodium and water. This differs from dopaminergic therapies, which aim to improve bradykinesia and rigidity rather than autonomic blood-pressure control.'),
  ('2012-21', 'Defense mechanisms describe habitual ways the mind manages conflict, anxiety, and affect. They are not diagnoses and may be adaptive or maladaptive depending on rigidity, context, and consequences. Suppression is often considered relatively mature because it permits temporary prioritization without requiring denial that the feeling exists. A useful memory hook is that suppression has a pause button, whereas repression has no accessible button. The distinction hinges on awareness, not on the intensity of the underlying emotion.'),
  ('2012-22', 'The best-interests-of-the-child standard is intentionally broad because no single family arrangement fits every child. It replaced simpler presumptions favoring a particular parent based on sex or a single relationship concept. For board purposes, distinguish a legal standard from a psychological theory: attachment, continuity, and psychological-parent ideas may be clinically relevant, but they do not displace the court’s overarching best-interests analysis. Jurisdiction-specific statutes and procedures control an actual case.'),
  ('2012-23', 'Most hypothalamic hormones stimulate anterior-pituitary secretion, but prolactin is classically remembered as the exception: its dominant hypothalamic influence is inhibitory. Think, “dopamine holds prolactin down.” Interruption of hypothalamic dopamine signaling, use of a dopamine-blocking drug, or loss of pituitary-stalk delivery can therefore increase prolactin. This brake analogy links antipsychotic pharmacology to endocrine adverse effects that may emerge during treatment. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2012-24', 'Loss of appetite and thirst often accompanies advanced illness and the natural dying process. Families may understandably equate nutrition and fluids with care, so this discussion requires compassionate explanation rather than a purely technical recommendation. Palliative care distinguishes interventions intended to prolong physiology from those intended to relieve symptoms. Mouth care is memorable because it directly addresses a common source of discomfort while avoiding invasive interventions that may add burden in a profoundly frail person with limited capacity to benefit.'),
  ('2012-25', 'TCAs inhibit cardiac sodium channels in overdose, impairing conduction and creating risk for life-threatening dysrhythmias. Serum drug concentrations may help in selected contexts, but clinical status and ECG findings are central to acute risk assessment; a single reported level should not create false reassurance. This question tests consultation-liaison sequencing: an overdose is both a psychiatric and medical emergency, and safe psychiatric disposition depends on adequate medical clearance.'),
  ('2012-26', 'Neurosyphilis is not one uniform syndrome; it can involve meninges, blood vessels, brain parenchyma, spinal pathways, or cranial nerves. A useful organization is timing and target: meningovascular involvement produces acute vascular events, whereas parenchymal and spinal involvement tends to produce chronic neuropsychiatric or sensory syndromes. This variability is why syphilis has long been considered a diagnostic imitator. Board questions often use the word acute to separate stroke from dementia and tabes dorsalis.'),
  ('2012-27', 'Toddler tantrums occur as autonomy expands faster than communication, frustration tolerance, and inhibitory control. Limit setting is therefore a common trigger. The clinical distinction is not whether a tantrum occurs, but whether its pattern is unusually severe, dangerous, pervasive, persistent, or accompanied by developmental and functional concerns. This question rewards a developmental stance: first listen, normalize cautiously, and then screen further if the history reveals signals that the behavior falls outside expected variation.'),
  ('2012-28', 'Thiamine is vitamin B1 and is required for carbohydrate metabolism. The brain has high energy needs, so deficiency can rapidly impair vulnerable regions involved in eye movements, coordination, and cognition. A practical memory phrase is “confusion, eyes, and gait” for Wernicke encephalopathy. The syndrome is especially associated with alcohol dependence but can also occur with other causes of severe malnutrition. Early recognition matters because delayed treatment can leave persistent amnestic deficits.'),
  ('2012-29', 'Behaviorism emphasizes that behavior is influenced by learning history and by consequences in the environment. Operant conditioning is especially relevant when a behavior is followed by something that increases or decreases its future frequency. A practical memory cue is “behavior followed by consequence.” In clinical work, behavioral methods are not merely reward charts; high-quality plans identify the function of behavior, teach an alternative skill, and make caregiver responses predictable and feasible.'),
  ('2012-30', 'Forensic psychiatry distinguishes the treating role from the evaluating role. Treating clinicians have a therapeutic relationship and firsthand knowledge of care; forensic experts are commonly engaged to address a defined legal question with an independent, explicitly disclosed role. The distinction matters because rules for confidentiality, privilege, disclosure, compensation, and opinion testimony can vary. The board-level clue is straightforward: testimony about treatment facts is fact-witness testimony, while specialized opinions intended to assist the court are expert-witness testimony.'),
  ('2012-31', 'Early language first centers on the immediate environment: objects, requests, and ongoing actions. As memory, symbolic representation, and grammar expand, children can talk about a past event rather than only responding to what is present. This capacity is a foundation for autobiographical memory and later narrative skill. A useful memory connection is that age 3 opens the story window: the child can begin telling a simple story about something that happened before, although the narrative remains short and developmentally imperfect.'),
  ('2012-32', 'Pyridoxine is involved in many enzymatic processes relevant to nervous-system function. Its board importance comes from the paradox that a nutrient necessary in normal amounts can become neurotoxic in excess. This makes medication and supplement reconciliation clinically meaningful, especially when patients use several nonprescription products. A useful memory hook is that B6 has two neuropathy directions: deficiency and very high intake can both point toward peripheral nerve dysfunction.'),
  ('2012-33', 'The stages-of-change model frames behavior change as a process rather than a single decision. Its practical value is matching intervention style to readiness: raising awareness for precontemplation, resolving ambivalence for contemplation, planning for preparation, and supporting new behavior during action and maintenance. A reliable memory cue is the time horizon: someday or next year suggests contemplation, whereas a specific near-term plan suggests preparation. Movement between stages is common, and relapse can be part of the process.'),
  ('2012-34', 'Neurasthenia is a historical and cross-cultural diagnostic concept centered on persistent exhaustion and bodily or cognitive symptoms. Its continued relevance is conceptual: diagnostic labels are shaped by culture and classification systems, while distress and impairment remain real. In US practice, similar presentations may be coded through depressive, anxiety, or somatic-symptom-related categories after appropriate medical assessment. A useful memory link is that fatigue and tension often travel together, making anxiety a frequent diagnostic neighbor of neurasthenia.'),
  ('2012-35', 'DBT combines acceptance-oriented and change-oriented interventions. Its four core skills modules are mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, and interpersonal effectiveness. Mindfulness does not mean eliminating thoughts or feelings. Instead, it teaches present-focused awareness and a less reactive relationship to internal experience. This makes it easier for a patient to identify what is happening before selecting another skill. A useful memory cue is that mindfulness is the platform supporting the other DBT modules.'),
  ('2012-36', 'The thalamus relays somatic sensory information to the cerebral cortex. A lesion can initially interrupt that relay, causing numbness or sensory loss, then later lead to abnormal central processing with allodynia, dysesthesia, and hyperpathia. The posterior cerebral artery supplies the occipital cortex and deep structures through perforating branches. Therefore, PCA syndromes are not limited to visual deficits; deep PCA lesions can produce a characteristic thalamic sensory syndrome.'),
  ('2012-37', 'Substance-induced psychotic disorders involve delusions or hallucinations developing during or soon after intoxication or withdrawal and not being better explained by a primary psychotic disorder. Alcohol-related psychosis differs from alcohol withdrawal delirium. In alcohol withdrawal delirium, attention and awareness are impaired and fluctuate, commonly with autonomic activation. A useful distinction is psychosis with a relatively clear sensorium versus delirium with impaired, fluctuating consciousness.'),
  ('2012-38', 'The National Practitioner Data Bank is a confidential federal repository used by authorized entities for reporting and querying specified professional actions. It was designed to reduce the chance that serious professional concerns remain undisclosed when clinicians move between institutions. It is not a public licensing board, professional society, or health program. For examination purposes, pair an adverse hospital clinical-privilege action lasting more than 30 days with a required NPDB report.'),
  ('2012-39', 'The corrected record restores the shared vignette that OCR had omitted for questions 39–42. Its details matter: the child is calm and organized in clinic, denies current suicidal and homicidal ideation, has stable biological functioning, and maintains grade-level work and friendships. Those features support the keyed least-restrictive disposition, but frustration-linked suicide threats are not dismissed as harmless. A current safety assessment, caregiver evaluation, safety planning, means restriction, and follow-up remain necessary before sending her home.'),
  ('2012-40', 'The corrected source restores the shared vignette omitted during OCR for questions 39–42. Child psychiatric assessment is multi-informant because behavior can differ substantially across home, school, and peer settings. Here, similar episodes are reported at school, yet grade-level work and friendships are preserved, making detailed teacher observations especially useful. School collateral can clarify severity, triggers, reinforcement patterns, and functional impairment. It is the best-listed initial step, while specialized psychological or medical testing depends on findings from the broader evaluation.'),
  ('2012-41', 'Behavioral plans depend on consistency. Parental disagreement can become part of a child’s reinforcement environment and may also signal wider family stress. A systemic formulation does not begin by deciding which parent is correct. It asks what function each response serves and what keeps the disagreement going. This approach preserves alliance with both caregivers and makes later behavioral changes more likely to be sustained.'),
  ('2012-42', 'Negative reinforcement increases a behavior because the behavior removes or prevents something aversive. It is distinct from punishment, which decreases behavior. In an escape-maintained tantrum, the child learns that tantrumming removes a demand. When caregivers consistently return to the demand after calm behavior, the tantrum no longer produces escape. The memory cue is: negative means something is removed, and reinforcement means the behavior becomes more likely.'),
  ('2012-43', 'Bereavement is both an emotional experience and a social transition. The death of a spouse can alter routines, identity, finances, caregiving roles, and access to companionship at once. Social support is protective partly because it addresses these multiple domains: emotional connection, practical help, and a sense of belonging. Remember that the goal is not to force “closure,” but to help a bereaved person remain connected and gradually adapt to life after the loss.'),
  ('2012-44', 'Catecholamines include dopamine and norepinephrine. Presynaptic transport systems normally regulate their availability in the synapse. Amphetamines act through these systems to increase outward monoamine movement, thereby increasing synaptic signaling. Cocaine also increases synaptic monoamines, but it does so primarily by preventing reuptake. A compact memory contrast is that amphetamine pushes transmitter out while cocaine prevents transmitter from being taken back in.'),
  ('2012-45', 'Declarative memory includes semantic knowledge and episodic recollection, whereas nondeclarative memory includes skills, habits, and conditioning. Episodic memory contains personally lived events linked to a time and context, so it naturally includes much autobiographical material. Dissociative amnesia classically disrupts access to that material while other memory functions may remain relatively preserved. Calling the loss “biographical” is descriptively reasonable, but episodic is the formal memory-system classification tested by the question.'),
  ('2012-46', 'The key was corrected because the source’s official ABCDE response had been invalidated for all credit, not because all five metals were equally correct. Manganese is the specific listed metal linked to globus pallidus and striatal toxicity and a parkinsonian syndrome. Other heavy metals have distinct exposure sources, systemic effects, and neurologic patterns. The basal ganglia are metabolically active and vulnerable to several toxic or metabolic insults, so the anatomic pattern guides investigation but does not replace an exposure history and clinical evaluation.'),
  ('2012-47', 'The MMPI family comprises standardized self-report instruments assessing personality characteristics, psychopathology, and response style. MMPI-2 is designed for adults, whereas MMPI-A is the adolescent-specific form. Validity scales support interpretation but do not replace clinical judgment. The independent adjudication retains E as the historical intended answer because it is the best-listed standardized personality inventory, while explicitly correcting the stem’s implication that the adult MMPI-2 itself is the appropriate validated instrument for adolescents across all settings.'),
  ('2012-48', 'Sexual difficulties often reflect interacting biological, psychological, and relational factors. Menopause may affect desire, arousal, comfort, and body image, but those changes vary widely and do not eliminate sexuality or intimacy. Sex education is therapeutic when misinformation, shame, or unrealistic expectations intensify a couple’s distress. A practical memory cue is that when misunderstanding is central to the presenting problem, begin with education.'),
  ('2012-49', 'TCAs are commonly grouped into tertiary and secondary amines. Tertiary amines tend to have greater anticholinergic, antihistaminic, and sedating effects. Secondary amines tend to be relatively more noradrenergic and less anticholinergic. A useful memory pair is desipramine and nortriptyline as secondary amines. This pharmacologic distinction helps compare TCAs but does not remove their important cardiac and overdose risks. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2012-50', 'Reading requires intact visual input and transfer of visual information to dominant-language cortex. A dominant occipital lesion causes contralateral homonymous visual-field loss. Associated disruption of posterior interhemispheric visual connections can prevent information from the intact visual cortex from reaching language areas. The memorable dissociation is “can write it but cannot read it,” which identifies alexia without agraphia in a dominant posterior cerebral artery syndrome.'),
  ('2012-51', 'Adjustment disorders are organized around the relationship between a stressor and an emotional or behavioral response. The diagnosis does not minimize suffering: transplantation, major illness, loss, and role disruption can produce substantial distress even when a full anxiety or mood disorder is absent. A useful approach is to identify the precipitating event, characterize the predominant symptoms, and rule out a better-fitting disorder. Reconsider the diagnosis if symptoms persist independently of the stressor or evolve into a more specific syndrome.'),
  ('2012-52', 'Comparative psychotherapy research often finds that common therapeutic factors and competent delivery matter alongside technique-specific ingredients. CBT became especially prominent because it is structured, teachable, and extensively tested, but “most studied” does not mean “always most effective.” For exam purposes, distinguish evidence that several therapies work from a sweeping claim of exclusive superiority. Depression has multiple validated psychotherapy pathways, so treatment matching, engagement, and access remain clinically important.'),
  ('2012-53', 'Social psychology often contrasts independent self-construals with interdependent self-construals. An independent self is commonly organized around personal attributes, choices, and individual goals; an interdependent self is organized around relationships, roles, and obligations. These are dimensions rather than mutually exclusive categories, and individuals may draw on both. If the self is relationally defined, self-esteem is often tied to being a valued contributor who maintains harmonious group relationships.'),
  ('2012-54', 'A polyneuropathy affects multiple peripheral nerves, so findings are often diffuse and symmetric rather than restricted to one root or nerve. Myelin enables rapid saltatory conduction; demyelination therefore produces slowed conduction velocity. Guillain-Barré syndrome is the classic exam association for acute progressive weakness plus areflexia. A useful pattern-recognition triad is symptoms developing over days, diffuse weakness, and diminished reflexes, followed by electrodiagnostic evidence of impaired peripheral conduction.'),
  ('2012-55', 'An elimination half-life is the approximate time required for a drug concentration to decrease by half. It provides a practical bridge between pharmacology and prescribing: a shorter half-life generally means faster concentration changes after a missed dose, whereas a longer half-life means more prolonged exposure. Fluoxetine is commonly remembered as the long-half-life SSRI; fluvoxamine is the short-half-life contrast in this option set. Half-life should be distinguished from the delayed onset of antidepressant benefit.'),
  ('2012-56', 'OCD is maintained partly by negative reinforcement: a compulsion briefly reduces distress, making the ritual more likely the next time. ERP interrupts that loop by separating the trigger from the ritualized response. “Response” includes visible acts such as washing and checking as well as mental reviewing, praying, and reassurance seeking. The treatment is typically graded and collaborative, not coercive. A compact memory cue is: expose the obsessional trigger and prevent the ritual.'),
  ('2012-57', 'Alcohol enhances inhibitory signaling and suppresses excitatory signaling; chronic exposure leads the brain to adapt in the opposite direction. When alcohol is removed, unopposed hyperexcitability produces autonomic and neuropsychiatric withdrawal symptoms. The timeline is best understood as a progression over hours to days, not an instantaneous event. For testing, link about two days with the general peak of withdrawal severity, while remembering that seizures and delirium are separate high-risk complications.'),
  ('2012-58', '“Atypical” or second-generation antipsychotic is a pharmacologic grouping, not a guarantee of greater efficacy for every outcome. Clozapine is the notable exception in many exam comparisons because of its role in treatment-resistant schizophrenia. Its unique clinical value is balanced by a uniquely intensive monitoring burden. A useful memory connection is that clozapine is often reserved not because it is weak, but because its potential benefit and potential risks both require a structured treatment system.'),
  ('2012-59', 'Capacity is functional, not a global label. Its central question is whether this person can make this decision at this time, with reasonable supports in place. Appreciation is often the most subtle domain because it asks for personal application rather than rote recall. A memory cue is: understand the facts, appreciate their personal relevance, reason through options, and state a choice. Legal competence may rely on a court process, but bedside capacity is a clinical assessment.'),
  ('2012-60', 'Psychodynamic and integrative therapies emphasize that patients bring relational expectations into treatment. The therapist’s consistent response can allow those expectations to be recognized, tested, and revised. “Corrective” does not mean the therapist becomes a parent or recreates childhood; it means the patient experiences a different, safer pattern within professional boundaries. A helpful memory link is that correction comes from a relationship that is both emotionally accepting and realistically limit setting.'),
  ('2012-61', 'Peripheral nerve localization is often easier when linked to digit patterns. The median nerve supplies much of the thumb through middle finger, while the ulnar nerve supplies the small finger and ulnar side of the ring finger. The ulnar nerve is vulnerable at the elbow, where flexion can narrow the tunnel and increase mechanical stress. Remember “funny bone, fourth and fifth fingers”: the familiar elbow location is an anatomic clue to ulnar neuropathy.'),
  ('2012-62', 'MAOIs reduce monoamine breakdown, allowing sympathomimetic decongestants and some other exposures to provoke abrupt catecholaminergic excess. The combination of severe headache, palpitations, sweating, and marked hypertension should trigger immediate concern for a medication interaction. This item’s answer was inferred because the official key page was missing, and its nifedipine framing reflects historical examination practice. Modern management emphasizes controlled, monitored blood-pressure treatment because rapid, unpredictable reduction with immediate-release nifedipine can be harmful.'),
  ('2012-63', 'Behavioral approaches to depression emphasize that reduced activity and loss of reinforcement can maintain low mood. Activity monitoring turns a global impression, such as “my whole day was useless,” into observable data. Mastery captures accomplishment, while pleasure captures enjoyment; both may be low in depression, but they need not move together. A simple memory distinction is that monitoring records current activity, whereas scheduling plans future activity.'),
  ('2012-64', 'Sleep-related movement disorders are easiest to distinguish by asking whether the phenomenon occurs while awake or asleep and whether it is an urge, pain, or observed movement. Restless legs syndrome is an urge with discomfort during wakefulness at rest; periodic limb movements are observed stereotyped movements during sleep. The great toe is a useful visual cue because its extension is part of the classic periodic limb movement pattern.'),
  ('2012-65', 'Suicide-focused CBT applies cognitive and behavioral principles directly to suicidal crises rather than treating suicidality only as a downstream symptom of depression. It emphasizes understanding the individual pathway to the attempt and building usable alternatives before the next crisis. A recent attempt is a major risk marker, so follow-up should be active, structured, and specifically responsive to suicidal thinking and behavior. This therapy complements rather than replaces comprehensive safety care.'),
  ('2012-66', 'Bereavement often comes in waves, with reminders provoking acute pangs of sadness. Traumatic grief adds features that interfere with adapting to the loss, including avoidance of reminders and difficulty engaging with the reality of the death. Avoidance is a memorable cross-diagnostic concept: it can temporarily reduce distress but often maintains trauma-related symptoms over time. In children, grief may appear through play, behavior, separation fears, or school functioning as well as verbal reports.'),
  ('2012-67', 'The Institute of Medicine described six aims for high-quality health care: safety, effectiveness, patient-centeredness, timeliness, efficiency, and equity. Questions about quality aims are best answered by identifying the principal purpose of the intervention rather than its administrative form. Screening for a predictable adverse effect before or during exposure to a medication is a classic safety activity. In psychiatry, adverse-effect surveillance is part of treatment because it helps prevent avoidable medical complications and supports safer long-term prescribing.'),
  ('2012-68', 'Spinal-cord tracts provide a useful localization map. Dorsal columns carry vibration and conscious proprioception and lie posteriorly, while the spinothalamic pathways for pain and temperature lie anteriorly and laterally. Preservation of dorsal-column modalities alongside loss of pain and temperature is dissociated sensory loss. The concise anterior-cord pattern is motor weakness plus pain-temperature loss, often with autonomic dysfunction. In the acute setting, reduced reflexes can occur early because of spinal shock and do not exclude a spinal-cord lesion.'),
  ('2012-69', 'Sexual adverse effects, including reduced libido, arousal difficulty, and delayed orgasm, are strongly associated with increased serotonergic activity. Bupropion is memorable as a commonly used antidepressant without prominent direct serotonergic action. Medication selection is never based on one adverse effect alone: prior response, overdose toxicity, weight effects, comorbidities, and patient preferences all matter. Board questions commonly signal bupropion with SSRI sexual dysfunction, especially when avoiding weight gain is also desirable.'),
  ('2012-70', 'Psychotherapy research distinguishes modality-specific ingredients from common factors shared by many approaches. The therapeutic alliance is commonly described through bond, goals, and tasks. A good alliance does not require agreement on everything; it requires sufficient trust and collaboration to work through disagreement. This makes the concept clinically practical: techniques are more likely to be used effectively when the patient understands the plan, has meaningful goals, and can discuss problems in the therapeutic relationship without disengaging.'),
  ('2012-71', 'The historical term somatoform disorders grouped conditions in which distressing bodily symptoms or illness fears were central. Current diagnostic language has changed, but the management principle remains memorable: continuity is more effective than episodic reassurance. Planned visits shift care from urgent symptom response to a stable therapeutic frame. They also allow clinicians to recognize genuine new medical illness while reducing iatrogenic harm from fragmented and repetitive investigation. This approach validates distress without reinforcing an emergency-based reassurance cycle.'),
  ('2012-72', 'ME/CFS is a clinical syndrome identified by a pattern of disabling fatigue and associated symptoms rather than a single diagnostic laboratory test. Postexertional malaise is the key memory anchor: ordinary physical or cognitive activity can provoke a disproportionate symptom flare with delayed recovery. Terminology has changed over time, but the board-era label chronic fatigue syndrome refers to the same core syndrome. Careful attention to the activity-and-recovery timeline is often more informative than a generic review of fatigue alone.'),
  ('2012-73', 'Normally, REM sleep includes muscle atonia that prevents most dream content from being acted out. In RBD, that protective paralysis is reduced or absent. The condition is memorable as dreams with movement, unlike an ordinary nightmare in which the person remains physically still. Clonazepam became the classic board answer because it frequently suppresses disruptive motor behaviors. Medication response does not eliminate the need for environmental safety measures, which reduce immediate risk to the patient and bed partner.'),
  ('2012-74', 'Autoinduction means that a medication accelerates processes that clear itself. Carbamazepine is a classic example; pharmacology teaching often associates this with its epoxide metabolite pathway. The memorable pattern is that a fixed dose works initially and then appears to wear off over weeks because the body is clearing the drug faster. This differs from random absorption variability and does not by itself prove nonadherence. Therapeutic drug monitoring can help interpret a change in clinical response when used with the full clinical picture.'),
  ('2012-75', 'DSM-III is a historical turning point from more theory-laden diagnostic descriptions toward explicit criteria sets. The former multiaxial format separated clinical disorders, personality disorders or developmental conditions, medical conditions, psychosocial stressors, and global functioning. Although current DSM practice no longer uses axes, the historical association remains high yield: DSM-III introduced the system, later editions revised or retained it, and DSM-5 removed it. This chronology distinguishes introduction from later refinement or continued use.'),
  ('2012-76', 'GHB is a central nervous system depressant used recreationally that can cause a deceptively rapid change from euphoria or disinhibition to coma-like sedation. Its short detection window and limited inclusion on routine toxicology testing make clinical pattern recognition important. A useful board-style cue is socially relaxed, then suddenly deeply sedated. Mixed ingestion remains possible in real practice, so supportive emergency management must be guided by physiology and clinical deterioration rather than a toxicology result alone.'),
  ('2012-77', 'Pediatric antidepressant questions often test comparative evidence rather than claiming one medication is universally best. Fluoxetine’s relatively robust trial evidence made it the traditional reference medication for child and adolescent major depression. The broader lesson is to pair medication decisions with monitoring for clinical worsening and activation, especially when treatment starts or doses change. Evidence, approvals, and local guidance can evolve, but this historical keyed comparison specifically centers on fluoxetine rather than the other listed antidepressants.'),
  ('2012-78', 'Piaget framed children as active learners who build increasingly complex ways of understanding their environment. Assimilation means fitting new experience into an existing mental framework; accommodation means modifying the framework when it no longer fits. These concepts help remember why Piaget is a cognitive-developmental theorist. The central question is not merely “What did the child learn?” but “What kind of reasoning is the child now able to use?”'),
  ('2012-79', 'Operant conditioning concerns how consequences alter the future likelihood of behavior. Differential reinforcement is a family of techniques that selectively strengthens desirable behavior. “Incompatible” means the two responses cannot reasonably happen together, such as sitting in a chair and running away. This principle is often more clinically useful than trying to suppress behavior in isolation because it gives the person an achievable alternative response that can meet a similar need.'),
  ('2012-80', 'Dextromethorphan was developed as an antitussive, but at supratherapeutic doses it can produce dissociation and perceptual changes. The memorable association is “DXM in cough medicine.” Over-the-counter availability does not imply low risk, particularly because formulations vary and may combine several active drugs. In toxidrome reasoning, the product label can be as important as the named drug: a single ingestion may expose a patient to multiple pharmacologic mechanisms.'),
  ('2012-81', 'Acute mania treatment is organized around severity and urgency. Lithium and valproate are core antimanic treatments, while antipsychotics can provide faster control of behavioral dysregulation and psychotic symptoms. Combination therapy therefore fits severe presentations better than waiting for monotherapy to work. Lamotrigine is a useful contrast because it has roles in bipolar maintenance and bipolar depression but is not an acute antimanic treatment. This distinction makes the keyed combination readily recognizable in a management question.'),
  ('2012-82', 'Hypochondriasis historically referred to excessive concern about serious illness, not deliberate fabrication. Modern terminology reframed the condition to emphasize anxiety and maladaptive health-related behavior. A useful contrast is that illness anxiety centers on fear of disease, while somatic symptom disorder centers on distress associated with prominent symptoms. Both can involve repeated care seeking, but this vignette emphasizes an unshakable fear or belief of serious disease despite reassuring evaluation. Historical terminology is appropriate when interpreting an older diagnostic question.'),
  ('2012-83', 'Glossopharyngeal neuralgia resembles other cranial neuralgias: brief attacks of severe shock-like pain follow a nerve distribution and are provoked by ordinary sensory actions. Cranial nerve IX supplies sensation from the posterior third of the tongue and pharyngeal region, explaining why throat stimulation can trigger pain that radiates toward the ear. The compact memory phrase is throat to ear with swallowing equals glossopharyngeal neuralgia. This distribution distinguishes it from trigeminal neuralgia, which is principally facial.'),
  ('2012-84', 'Countertransference refers to the clinician’s emotional responses to a patient, including reactions shaped by the clinician’s own history and by the patient’s interpersonal style. Such reactions are common and can be informative, but acting on them can harm patients. Supervision turns an unexamined reaction into material for clinical reflection. A practical memory cue is “feel it, consult, do not enact it.” Professional boundaries protect the patient and preserve the treatment’s purpose.'),
  ('2012-85', 'The nucleus accumbens lies in the ventral striatum and is commonly taught as part of the brain’s reward and motivation circuitry. A simple circuit memory is ventral tegmental area dopamine projecting to nucleus accumbens. Reward is broader than pleasure alone: it includes learning which cues predict outcomes and mobilizing behavior toward them. This is why addiction questions may connect the nucleus accumbens with reinforcement, craving, and salience rather than only with subjective euphoria.'),
  ('2012-86', 'An introject is an internalized representation of another person’s attitudes, rules, or emotional tone. Classical psychodynamic accounts view latency as a period in which earlier conflicts may be relatively quiescent, with adolescence reactivating themes of authority and separation. A harsh parental introject is therefore a historical theory term for a severe internal critic derived from parental authority. It is not a modern descriptive diagnosis, but it explains the terminology and developmental mechanism tested in this question.'),
  ('2012-87', 'Forensic evaluations separate legal questions that may sound similar clinically. Competency to stand trial asks whether a defendant currently understands the proceedings and can assist counsel rationally. Criminal responsibility, commonly called insanity, is retrospective: it asks whether a qualifying mental condition affected legally defined capacities during the alleged act. The governing legal test varies by jurisdiction, but the timing remains central. Symptoms are evidence rather than conclusions; an evaluator must connect them to the relevant legal standard, the specific conduct, and corroborating information. A useful memory cue is that competency is now, whereas insanity is then.'),
  ('2012-88', 'The key distinction is between perception and interpretation. With an illusion, the sensory input itself is misidentified, such as mistaking a coat rack for a person in dim light. With an idea of reference, a broadcast, gesture, headline, or conversation is perceived accurately but given a private self-referential meaning not shared by others. The evaluator should ask about conviction and consequences, because fleeting self-conscious interpretations differ from fixed psychotic beliefs. This phenomenon is not the same as thought insertion, which concerns the perceived origin of one’s own thoughts.'),
  ('2012-89', 'Apolipoprotein E participates in lipid transport and has common isoforms conventionally called ε2, ε3, and ε4. Board-style memory framing is straightforward: ε4 increases late-onset Alzheimer risk, ε3 is the common reference allele, and ε2 is relatively protective. These are associations, not deterministic outcomes. The biology is complex and includes lipid handling and processes relevant to amyloid accumulation and clearance. Risk also reflects age, family history, vascular health, and other genetic and environmental factors. Genetic association should never be confused with a diagnosis or with a prognosis for one person.'),
  ('2012-90', 'CT is especially useful in emergency neuroimaging because it is fast, broadly available, and detects acute blood and mass effect well. MRI provides greater soft-tissue detail but often takes longer and can be harder to perform in a patient who is unstable, agitated, or unable to cooperate. A practical memory connection is that acute trauma requires a quick search for blood, pressure, and mass effect with CT. MRI is then selected when finer tissue characterization will change management. Imaging choice depends on the clinical question and urgency rather than on a universal hierarchy between modalities.'),
  ('2012-91', 'The amygdala is often taught as a “fear center,” but that phrase is an oversimplification. Different amygdala nuclei have different roles; the central nucleus is especially important for sending fear-related output to autonomic and brainstem systems. The hippocampus supplies contextual information, and cortical systems can regulate or reinterpret threat. A useful memory chain is threat cue → amygdala output → bodily and behavioral defense response.'),
  ('2012-92', 'Transferrin is a circulating protein whose carbohydrate side chains can be altered by prolonged heavy alcohol exposure. That mechanism explains the name carbohydrate-deficient transferrin. Board questions often contrast CDT with indirect markers: MCV reflects red-cell size and AST reflects tissue injury, so both have many nonalcohol explanations. CDT is better understood as evidence of sustained heavy exposure than as a real-time measure of intoxication or a definitive diagnosis of alcohol use disorder. Laboratory results require interpretation in the patient’s medical context, including potential liver-related and other confounders.'),
  ('2012-93', 'Relapse in schizophrenia is influenced by treatment interruption, stress, substance use, limited insight, and difficulty recognizing early symptom change. Family interventions moved away from blaming families for causing schizophrenia and toward practical support for illness management. Psychoeducation typically combines clear information about the disorder with communication strategies, problem solving, and a plan for early warning signs. It may include both the patient and relatives or be tailored to family needs. A useful memory phrase is “education plus problem solving”: families learn what symptoms may mean, how to communicate, and how to act early when relapse warning signs appear.'),
  ('2012-94', 'Myotonia means delayed muscle relaxation after voluntary contraction; a sustained handshake or inability to promptly open the hand after gripping is a classic demonstration. Myotonic dystrophy is an inherited muscular dystrophy with systemic manifestations, so its clues extend beyond muscle symptoms. Cataracts provide a memorable non-neurologic association, and facial weakness may produce a characteristic appearance. Contrast this with myasthenia gravis: myasthenia causes fatigable weakness that fluctuates with use, whereas myotonia is delayed relaxation after contraction. The combination of weakness, myotonia, and cataracts is more informative than any finding alone.'),
  ('2012-95', 'Social-skills training is a behavioral and rehabilitative approach. It treats social competence as a set of teachable behaviors rather than assuming it will emerge from insight alone. Sessions commonly explain a skill, demonstrate it, rehearse it, provide feedback, and assign real-world practice. Relevant targets include starting conversations, making requests, declining unsafe offers, resolving conflicts, and interacting with employers or landlords. This practical orientation fits outpatient rehabilitation, where improved participation and community functioning are central goals. It does not mean that all group treatment for schizophrenia has the same format or purpose.'),
  ('2012-96', 'The Valsalva maneuver increases intrathoracic pressure and may increase vagal effects that slow atrioventricular nodal conduction. That is why it is classically associated with some supraventricular reentrant tachycardias. “Paroxysmal atrial tachycardia” is older terminology; contemporary practice often uses supraventricular tachycardia until the precise rhythm is established on ECG. A Valsalva response supports but does not independently prove the rhythm diagnosis. The crucial psychiatric differential point is that panic-like symptoms with extreme tachycardia, hypotension, syncope, or other abnormal vital signs require medical assessment first.'),
  ('2012-97', 'Pharmacokinetics asks what the body does to a drug: absorption, distribution, metabolism, and elimination. Cytochrome P450 enzymes contribute to the metabolism of many psychiatric medications, and inherited variation can make a person a poor, normal, rapid, or ultrarapid metabolizer for a given pathway. Desipramine is substantially metabolized through CYP2D6. A useful board pattern is low dose plus unexpectedly high drug concentration plus toxicity: think reduced clearance before assuming nonadherence. A full assessment must still consider interactions, organ dysfunction, and an incorrect or excessive dose.'),
  ('2012-98', 'Language development depends on hearing, motor planning, social communication, cognition, and exposure to language. Because hearing loss can alter several observable behaviors, it is a foundational assessment step in young children with delayed speech. Before interpreting absent speech as solely a social-communication symptom, confirm that speech is accessible to the child. This is not an either-or decision: audiologic and developmental assessments can proceed together, especially when delay is substantial. The clinician should also identify communication strengths, regression, medical history, and functional needs so that supportive services are not delayed while diagnostic clarification continues.'),
  ('2012-99', 'Serum sodium concentration reflects water balance as well as total body sodium. When water intake exceeds excretory capacity, serum sodium can fall, allowing water to shift into cells; brain swelling accounts for neurologic symptoms in more severe or rapid cases. The memory link is “too much free water, too little sodium concentration.” Psychiatry commonly encounters this issue through primary polydipsia and through medications or illnesses that alter water regulation.'),
  ('2012-100', 'Papilledema is optic-disc swelling caused by raised intracranial pressure and should be distinguished from other causes of an abnormal optic disc. Idiopathic intracranial hypertension is a syndrome of elevated intracranial pressure without a mass lesion and with otherwise appropriate cerebrospinal fluid findings. The diagnostic sequence emphasizes safety: obtain suitable imaging to exclude a lesion that could make lumbar puncture unsafe, then measure opening pressure. Evaluation also considers secondary causes of raised pressure, including venous outflow obstruction when clinically relevant. This explains why lumbar puncture remains diagnostically central after appropriate imaging.')
insert into question_context (question_id, context) values
  ('2012-101', 'Alcohol use disorder medications are often remembered by mechanism: disulfiram creates an aversive reaction after alcohol exposure, whereas naltrexone blocks opioid receptors involved in alcohol reward. Board questions also test their distinct safety issues. Their shared hepatic consideration matters because alcohol-related liver disease may already be present in people seeking treatment. Disulfiram has additional potential neurologic and psychiatric adverse effects, while naltrexone requires screening for opioid exposure because it can precipitate withdrawal. A concise memory link is: different mechanisms, overlapping liver-injury concern.'),
  ('2012-102', 'Child maltreatment assessment uses an ecological view: caregiver factors, child vulnerabilities, family stress, and social supports can interact. Population-level associations are not moral judgments and should not be used to stereotype families. The examination distinction is between a risk factor and evidence of abuse. A risk factor can raise the need to ask, assess, and support; it cannot establish what happened in an individual case. Findings, history, collateral information, and appropriate investigation determine whether maltreatment occurred. Clinicians should remain alert to immediate safety needs while using a trauma-informed, nonaccusatory approach with every family.'),
  ('2012-103', 'Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a rare but life-threatening reaction associated with dopamine blockade or abrupt dopaminergic withdrawal. The classic cluster is hyperthermia, severe rigidity, altered mental status, and autonomic instability, although findings can evolve over time. Rigidity can damage skeletal muscle, releasing creatine kinase and myoglobin; this explains why CK is clinically useful. Myoglobin-related kidney injury and electrolyte disturbances make renal and metabolic monitoring important as well. Remember the pathophysiologic chain: rigidity causes muscle injury, muscle injury elevates CK, and severe injury can threaten kidney function.'),
  ('2012-104', 'Child development includes an early period of curiosity about bodies and sex differences. Children at this age think concretely, so questions about genitals often reflect observation rather than adult meanings such as erotic intent or identity. A useful memory connection is curiosity plus redirectability: ordinary exploration tends to respond to calm limits, whereas concerning behavior is more likely to be coercive, persistent, secretive, distressing, or far beyond the child''s developmental understanding.'),
  ('2012-105', 'Cultural concepts of distress are locally meaningful ways people organize and communicate suffering. They may describe a recognizable episode without mapping neatly onto one diagnostic category. Ataque de nervios is often linked to acute interpersonal stress and intense affective expression. For exams, remember that its importance is not exotic labeling; it is the reminder to assess broad mood, anxiety, trauma, dissociative, medical, and safety dimensions while respecting the patient''s own language for the experience.'),
  ('2012-106', 'Processing speed is the rate at which a person can take in, manipulate, and respond to information. It is not the same as intelligence or wisdom. Many people retain extensive knowledge and verbal skill while needing more time for complex or novel tasks. This distinction is clinically useful because timed testing can make normal aging look more impaired than untimed performance. A memorable phrase is “slower, not necessarily less capable.”'),
  ('2012-107', 'Stimulants increase catecholaminergic signaling and can produce both cardiovascular symptoms and a paranoid, hypervigilant mental state. The pairing of agitation, hypertension, tachycardia, and chest discomfort is a useful pattern-recognition cue. Urine toxicology is a differential-building tool rather than a complete answer: timing of exposure, false negatives, prescribed medications, and substances not included on a standard panel all affect interpretation. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2012-108', 'Ethical principles often overlap in real cases. Fidelity focuses on the reliability of a clinician''s commitments and the trust patients place in the therapeutic relationship. Vacation itself is not unethical; unmanaged absence is the problem. A useful distinction is that nonmaleficence asks whether harm is avoided, while fidelity asks whether the clinician has honored the relationship and the reasonable expectations created by accepting responsibility for care.'),
  ('2012-109', 'Lewy body disease is associated with abnormal intracellular aggregates containing alpha-synuclein and can affect both cognitive and motor networks. The memorable clinical triad is visual hallucinations, fluctuations, and parkinsonism. REM sleep behavior disorder and marked sensitivity to dopamine-blocking medications are additional commonly tested associations. Early cognition may show attentional, executive, or visuospatial impairment more prominently than the amnestic pattern classically associated with Alzheimer disease.'),
  ('2012-110', 'ECT intentionally produces a controlled generalized cerebral seizure under anesthesia. Drugs that change seizure threshold, seizure duration, or postictal recovery can influence safety and efficacy. Lithium has a narrow therapeutic index and depends on renal clearance, making physiologic changes around treatment especially relevant. The exam memory link is simple: ECT plus lithium raises concern for delirium and prolonged postictal effects.'),
  ('2012-111', 'The term depersonalization literally points to altered experience of one''s person or self. It is often described with metaphors of being detached, robotic, numb, or an observer of oneself. In phenomenology, perception includes how the self and world are experienced, not only sensory input. Pair the terms: depersonalization means the self feels unreal, whereas derealization means the world feels unreal.'),
  ('2012-112', 'Twin studies are useful because they compare people with differing degrees of genetic relatedness. If schizophrenia were determined by genes alone, monozygotic twins would always be concordant. Their discordance instead supports a multifactorial model. “Two-hit” is a memorable shorthand, not a literal claim that exactly two events are required; it captures the idea that vulnerability and later influences combine across development to shape risk.'),
  ('2012-113', 'Memory formation depends heavily on medial temporal structures, including the hippocampal system. Transient global amnesia is a clinical syndrome of temporary failure to lay down new episodic memories, with a striking repetitive-questioning presentation. A memorable contrast is anterograde failure during the event, an amnestic gap afterward, and no persistent focal deficit. Its diagnosis is clinical and should not replace evaluation for dangerous acute neurologic alternatives.'),
  ('2012-114', 'The word frame evokes the border around a picture: it does not create the patient''s inner experience, but gives the work a stable container. In psychodynamic treatment, regularity makes patterns in transference, attachment, and responses to limits easier to recognize. Frame elements can vary by setting, but their clarity and consistency are central. The frame is distinct from the therapy''s interpretive content.'),
  ('2012-115', 'REM sleep is associated with rapid eye movements, low-voltage mixed-frequency brain activity, and relative skeletal-muscle atonia in mature sleep physiology. Developmentally, active sleep appears early and occupies a high proportion of sleep in newborns. The simple memory sequence is fetal active sleep first, then abundant infant REM, then a lower adult proportion. This is why in utero is the earliest answer when development stages are compared.'),
  ('2012-116', 'Study design follows the direction of inquiry. Cohort studies move forward from exposure to outcome and are especially useful when an exposure is rare; case-control studies work backward from outcome to exposure and are efficient when the outcome is rare. The memorable pairing is rare disease to case-control and rare exposure to cohort. Because cases are selected based on outcome, incidence and absolute risk cannot usually be calculated directly from a standard case-control study.'),
  ('2012-117', 'Set shifting is the capacity to alter a mental rule when circumstances change. The WCST makes this visible by requiring sorting according to an unstated principle that changes over time; the examinee must infer the rule from feedback and abandon it when it no longer works. The memorable association is WCST: categories, flexibility, and perseveration, linking it to executive-function and frontal-system questions.'),
  ('2012-118', 'Self psychology emphasizes the development of a cohesive self through relationships that provide empathic mirroring, idealization, and a sense of likeness or belonging. Selfobject refers to another person experienced as serving needed psychological functions, not merely as a separate object of instinctual conflict. The memory link is empathic failures leading to fragile cohesion and heightened sensitivity to narcissistic injury. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2012-119', 'Sleep architecture in depression is often summarized as REM disinhibition: REM appears earlier, is concentrated earlier in the night, and may show greater eye-movement density. This contrasts with narcolepsy, in which sleep-onset REM periods are central to diagnosis only in the appropriate clinical setting. The finding is best remembered as a laboratory correlate of depression, not as a stand-alone diagnostic test or a replacement for clinical assessment.'),
  ('2012-120', 'Exposure-based CBT relies on new learning that feared cues can be tolerated and do not reliably predict the expected catastrophe. NMDA-receptor signaling is involved in synaptic plasticity and extinction learning, which led to interest in D-cycloserine as an adjunct. The exam association is D-cycloserine, NMDA-related learning, and exposure-based CBT augmentation. Its effect is best understood as conditional on the learning experience it accompanies.'),
  ('2012-121', 'Lithium is handled by the kidneys and affects several organ systems, especially thyroid and parathyroid regulation. Its interference with thyroid hormone synthesis can lead to elevated TSH and clinical hypothyroidism in susceptible patients. For recall, pair lithium with its monitoring domains: kidneys, thyroid, calcium, and serum drug concentration. Baseline data make subsequent adverse-effect monitoring much more interpretable. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2012-122', '“Cerebritis” is an older term for inflammatory brain dysfunction. Neuropsychiatric systemic lupus can include mood symptoms, cognitive impairment, psychosis, seizures, and other neurologic syndromes, but none is specific in isolation. Discoid lupus, by contrast, is ordinarily skin-limited. The independent adjudication retains E as the best-listed answer because the new inflammatory joints, myalgias, fatigue, and neuropsychiatric changes imply systemic activity, while preserving the caveat that the stem must be understood as evolution to systemic lupus rather than uncomplicated discoid disease.'),
  ('2012-123', 'Bereavement involves adapting to an altered relationship and an altered life, not simply getting over a person. Meaning-making is a broad process of integrating the loss into one''s beliefs, identity, relationships, and future plans. It can include continuing bonds, rituals, or value-based action. The useful memory contrast is that meaning supports integration, whereas isolation, unresolved conflict, and cumulative stress may make adaptation harder.'),
  ('2012-124', 'Professional boundaries are not merely rules about setting or timing; they protect the asymmetry inherent in clinical care. Psychotherapy can involve intimate disclosure, strong attachment, and transference that may persist after visits stop. Psychiatry therefore uses a particularly protective approach to sexual boundaries. A useful memory cue is that ending treatment ends appointments, not necessarily the influence of the treatment relationship. This differs from some nonsexual dual relationships, which may require contextual judgment; sexual involvement is treated as categorically unacceptable.'),
  ('2012-125', 'Peripheral myelin acts like insulation that speeds nerve conduction and can often be rebuilt. The axon is the nerve fiber''s transmission core, so axonal injury requires regrowth and reconnection with the target muscle. This distinction makes the prognosis intuitive: slowed conduction from demyelination may reverse, whereas loss of the conducting fiber carries greater risk of persistent weakness. Guillain-Barré syndrome is clinically heterogeneous, so electrodiagnostic patterns inform prognosis without replacing serial clinical assessment.'),
  ('2012-126', 'The hippocampus is often remembered as the brain''s new-memory system: damage to it particularly impairs the ability to lay down new episodic memories. Endocannabinoid signaling normally modulates synaptic activity, and external cannabinoids can alter that modulation. CB1 receptors are widely distributed, which explains cannabis'' broad effects, but their prominence in hippocampal circuits creates a memorable link to recent-memory impairment. The effect concerns encoding and retrieval during intoxication, not necessarily a global loss of all memory.'),
  ('2012-127', 'An evoked potential is a time-locked brain or nerve response to a repeated stimulus. Because the signal is averaged across many stimuli, it can reveal pathway conduction that may not be obvious from subjective report alone. Visual evoked potentials are therefore conceptually suited to a visual complaint. Functional neurologic symptom disorder involves altered functioning rather than fabricated symptoms; objective testing may assist diagnosis when interpreted together with the complete neurologic examination and positive clinical findings.'),
  ('2012-128', 'Informed consent rests on disclosure, comprehension, voluntariness, capacity, and authorization. Therapeutic privilege sits uneasily within that framework because it permits limited nondisclosure for anticipated harm. Modern ethics emphasizes patient autonomy, so this doctrine is construed narrowly and should not be confused with paternalism. A useful contrast is that waiver comes from the patient, whereas therapeutic privilege is invoked by the clinician. In either case, the central issue is what information is shared during consent.'),
  ('2012-129', 'The term cosmetic can obscure an important distinction: procedures may restore appearance or function after injury as well as alter a feature for aesthetic reasons. Psychological outcome depends less on the procedure label than on the match among the physical finding, the patient''s expectations, motivation, and mental state. Body dysmorphic disorder is the memorable contrast: perceived defects are often slight or not observable, preoccupation is excessive, and procedural satisfaction may be poor. This vignette instead describes a proportionate restorative request.'),
  ('2012-130', 'A ceiling effect occurs when a test is too easy for part of the population, causing scores to cluster near the maximum and reducing the ability to detect differences. Cognitive screening is not one-size-fits-all because performance reflects education, language, culture, sensory limitations, and premorbid ability as well as illness. A useful memory link is high baseline, hidden decline: people with substantial cognitive reserve may compensate well and still show meaningful change in complex daily tasks.'),
  ('2012-131', 'PSP is an atypical parkinsonian neurodegenerative syndrome. Supranuclear means the problem affects higher control of eye movements rather than the ocular muscles or cranial nerve nuclei themselves; reflex eye movements may be relatively preserved early on. The bedside memory triad is falls, gaze palsy, and axial stiffness. Looking down is clinically important because impairment contributes to falls when navigating curbs or stairs. This pattern distinguishes PSP from more typical Parkinson disease.'),
  ('2012-132', 'TCAs are a pharmacologically diverse group despite sharing a three-ring chemical structure. Their relative effects on serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake, along with receptor blockade, help explain why they are not interchangeable across disorders. Clomipramine is memorable as the TCA most strongly associated with serotonergic treatment of obsessions and compulsions. The broader conceptual link is that OCD pharmacotherapy has historically emphasized serotonergic agents alongside exposure and response prevention.'),
  ('2012-133', 'Psychodynamic treatments vary along a continuum from supportive to expressive. Supportive work seeks to maintain or restore functioning, whereas expressive work more actively interprets unconscious conflict and defenses. Defenses are automatic psychological operations that protect a person from anxiety or painful affect; they can be maladaptive, but they also serve a stabilizing function. The memory contrast is simple: supportive therapy shores up current coping, while interpretive therapy examines what that coping may conceal.'),
  ('2012-134', 'PCP can produce dissociation, analgesia, hallucinations, aggression, hypertension, hyperthermia, and seizures. Emergency management prioritizes reduced stimulation, rapid control of dangerous agitation, and detection of medical complications. Historical examinations sometimes favored antipsychotics for prominent psychotic agitation, but contemporary toxicology generally favors benzodiazepines. This record also preserves a source-text issue: “Smg” appears to be an OCR or typographic rendering of “5 mg,” yet the keyed answer text is reproduced rather than silently rewritten.'),
  ('2012-135', 'Egocentrism in early childhood means difficulty fully appreciating perspectives and causal processes outside one''s own experience. It can lead to magical thinking, in which thoughts or statements are believed to influence external events. This is not equivalent to a delusion when it occurs in a developmentally expected form and context. The memorable clinical response is to correct causation without shaming the child: feelings are allowed, but feelings do not cause accidents. Developmental stage is essential when interpreting unusual beliefs.'),
  ('2012-136', 'Family systems theory focuses on reciprocal patterns: one person''s symptoms and behavior affect, and are affected by, the broader relational system. This perspective makes family a functional clinical concept as well as a demographic one. A family can be structurally diverse and still be cohesive, adaptable, and supportive. Conversely, a conventional household structure does not guarantee healthy communication or boundaries. The useful memory cue is that structure describes who is present, whereas function describes how the system works.'),
  ('2012-137', 'Child psychiatric assessment is inherently multi-informant. Parents observe home routines and emotional recovery; teachers observe peers, sustained academic demands, and classroom structure. A rating scale is therefore not a laboratory measurement with one universally correct observer. Differences can reflect setting-specific symptoms, thresholds for concern, or different opportunities to observe behavior. This is especially important for attention problems, for which diagnostic reasoning considers cross-setting impairment but recognizes that supports and demands vary between home and school.'),
  ('2012-138', 'Trauma-related diagnoses are organized partly by the relationship between symptoms and time. Acute stress disorder captures clinically significant trauma responses in the immediate aftermath, while PTSD applies when the syndrome persists beyond one month. Dissociation, such as feeling detached or that surroundings are unreal, can occur during or after trauma and is not itself evidence of psychosis. A useful memory cue is that acute stress disorder occupies the first-month window; its symptom pattern may resemble PTSD, but the duration differs.'),
  ('2012-139', 'Horner syndrome is a localization syndrome rather than a diagnosis of one disease. Sympathetic innervation travels from the hypothalamus through the spinal cord and neck to the eye, so lesions at several levels can create the same bedside pattern. The ptosis is usually mild because it involves the sympathetically innervated Müller muscle, unlike the more marked ptosis that may occur with third-nerve dysfunction. A useful memory cue is that sympathetic loss makes the pupil small and the eyelid slightly low.'),
  ('2012-140', 'A partial agonist has lower intrinsic activity than a full agonist but still activates its receptor. Therefore, its net effect depends on the signaling environment: it can compete with abundant endogenous dopamine while retaining limited agonist activity when dopamine signaling is lower. This is the conceptual reason aripiprazole is remembered differently from conventional dopamine-blocking antipsychotics. The board-relevant association is not that it lacks dopamine effects, but that it modulates D2 signaling through partial agonism.'),
  ('2012-141', 'Medication-induced psychiatric syndromes are often recognized by chronology: a plausible agent begins or changes, then a characteristic symptom cluster appears. Mefloquine is memorable because its adverse-effect profile includes prominent neuropsychiatric symptoms despite its use for an infectious disease. This question also illustrates why a prior psychiatric diagnosis should not automatically be assumed to explain every recurrence of psychosis. New symptoms in a medically treated patient require review of drugs, illness, and toxic-metabolic contributors.'),
  ('2012-142', 'Atrial fibrillation promotes blood stasis in the atria, especially the left atrial appendage, allowing thrombus formation and systemic embolization. This mechanism differs from platelet-rich arterial thrombosis caused by atherosclerotic plaque, helping explain why anticoagulants and antiplatelet agents are not interchangeable. Warfarin was long the standard oral anticoagulant for atrial fibrillation; current practice often selects a direct oral anticoagulant when appropriate. The question tests the mechanism-based distinction, not an acute-stroke treatment decision.'),
  ('2012-143', 'CBT and IPT differ in their organizing models: CBT centers on cognitions and behaviors, whereas IPT centers on relationships and social roles. Their common practical orientation makes them easy to contrast with therapies that place greater emphasis on unconscious conflict, early development, and the therapeutic relationship as the principal arena of interpretation. A useful exam shortcut is to ask whether the therapy primarily reformulates the past or targets what is currently sustaining symptoms; CBT and IPT favor the latter.'),
  ('2012-144', 'Narcolepsy is often remembered as REM sleep crossing boundaries: dream imagery may appear at sleep onset, and REM atonia may persist briefly into wakefulness as sleep paralysis. Cataplexy is a related phenomenon in which sudden emotion triggers transient loss of muscle tone while consciousness is maintained. This REM-intrusion framework distinguishes narcolepsy from ordinary sleep deprivation, sleep apnea, and non-REM parasomnias such as sleepwalking.'),
  ('2012-145', 'Psychiatric confidentiality is fundamental, but its application depends on the role. In treatment, privacy supports candor and care. In forensic, occupational, administrative, or fitness-for-duty evaluations, the psychiatrist may be retained to provide an opinion to a third party. The ethical safeguard is transparent notice at the outset, coupled with restraint in reporting: communicate information relevant to the stated purpose rather than treating the evaluation as either completely confidential or completely unrestricted.'),
  ('2012-146', 'Human growth is heterochronic: different tissues and body regions change at different rates and times. The familiar adolescent pattern of rapidly growing feet and hands before the rest of the body reflects this uneven timing. Sex differences in average pubertal timing add another visible feature: girls often have their growth acceleration earlier, while boys commonly reach peak growth later. This can produce a temporary reversal in height among same-age peers without implying abnormal development.'),
  ('2012-147', 'Reinforcement describes how consequences increase the chance that a behavior will recur. Positive reinforcement adds a rewarding outcome; negative reinforcement removes or reduces an aversive outcome. Negative reinforcement is not punishment and does not mean the behavior is morally negative. In addiction, repeated cycles of intoxication and withdrawal can make relief from distress a central driver of use, providing a memorable bridge from impulsive reward-seeking to compulsive, hard-to-stop behavior.'),
  ('2012-148', 'The lucid interval is a memorable but not universal teaching pattern for epidural hematoma. It reflects a period in which the patient is awake while bleeding continues to expand in the potential space between dura and skull. Because the dura is tightly attached at sutures, the blood collection tends to form a lens-like shape rather than spreading broadly across the cerebral hemisphere. The anatomy explains both the CT appearance and the potential for abrupt deterioration.'),
  ('2012-149', 'Couples work commonly uses a systems perspective: a difficulty can be maintained by reciprocal responses between people, even when neither person intended the result. This does not deny individual responsibility or psychiatric illness; rather, it keeps the treatment lens on patterns of communication, attachment, conflict, and repair. A useful memory cue is that the identified patient in couples therapy is often the relationship itself. That framing is especially helpful at intake, when both partners may hope the therapist will validate their side.'),
  ('2012-150', 'Alpha rhythm is commonly called the posterior dominant rhythm because it is strongest over occipital and posterior parietal electrodes in relaxed wakefulness. Its reactivity is as important as its frequency: opening the eyes or engaging attention reduces the rhythm. This makes alpha a useful physiologic marker of a responsive awake brain state. The term alpha should not be confused with an abnormal focal discharge; in the appropriate setting, it is a normal EEG background feature.'),
  ('2012-151', 'Complex partial seizure is the older term for a focal seizure with impaired awareness. The modern name emphasizes that the seizure begins in a localized network and affects awareness, often with automatisms such as lip smacking, chewing, picking, or repetitive movements. Temporal lobe seizures are a classic association, but semiology and EEG evaluation guide localization. A normal structural MRI does not exclude an electrical seizure disorder, which is why history remains central.'),
  ('2012-152', 'Undoing is often remembered through the image of mentally or behaviorally erasing something intolerable. It is classically associated with obsessive-compulsive symptoms, although defense mechanisms are descriptive formulations rather than diagnoses. The same act can be understood in more than one framework: psychodynamically as an effort to neutralize a thought and behaviorally as a ritual reinforced by short-term anxiety relief. The vignette’s symbolic link between hostile wish and checking is the clue to undoing.'),
  ('2012-153', 'Rating scales differ not only by diagnosis but also by rater. Self-report tools prioritize the person’s direct account of internal symptoms, whereas clinician-rated scales standardize an examiner’s assessment after interview and observation. This distinction matters in research, measurement-based care, and board questions. A simple memory anchor is that the Beck Depression Inventory is an inventory the patient fills out, while the Hamilton scale is classically administered and scored by a clinician.'),
  ('2012-154', 'Validity questions are easier when framed as two different audiences. Internal validity asks whether the result is trustworthy for people actually in the study. External validity asks whether it travels to the target population. Selection, setting, clinician expertise, adherence, and co-occurring conditions can all limit transportability even when randomization has minimized confounding within the trial. A result can therefore be internally sound but have limited applicability in ordinary practice.'),
  ('2012-155', 'Wilson disease is an inherited disorder of copper transport that leads to copper accumulation, particularly in the liver, brain, and cornea. Its varied presentation makes it a classic medical mimic in psychiatry and neurology: hepatic disease, tremor or dystonia, dysarthria, cognitive changes, and psychiatric symptoms may occur in different combinations. Kayser-Fleischer rings provide a memorable visual clue, while low ceruloplasmin is a commonly tested laboratory association. The disease illustrates why multisystem findings should redirect diagnostic thinking.'),
  ('2012-156', 'BDD belongs conceptually near obsessive-compulsive and related disorders because intrusive appearance concerns drive repetitive neutralizing behaviors. The feared defect may be slight or unobservable to others, but the distress and impairment are genuine. Insight exists on a spectrum: some people recognize that their appraisal may be exaggerated, whereas others are fully convinced. This spectrum helps explain why BDD can resemble a somatic delusion while still responding to treatments used for obsessive-compulsive phenomena.'),
  ('2012-157', 'GABA is the brain’s major inhibitory neurotransmitter. The GABA-A receptor acts quickly because it is an ion channel, making it a useful memory link for sedative effects. Alcohol is pharmacologically broad rather than receptor-specific, which is why questions distinguish GABA-A enhancement from NMDA inhibition. Think of GABA-A as inhibitory braking and NMDA as excitatory learning circuitry; both are affected by alcohol, but GABA-A best matches the effects listed here.'),
  ('2012-158', 'The stages of change model frames behavior change as a process rather than a single decision. Its practical value is matching interventions to readiness: education and curiosity may help precontemplation, exploration of ambivalence may help contemplation, and concrete planning supports preparation. The stages are not a rigid linear sequence; people can move forward, relapse, and re-enter planning. A useful memory cue is that preparation is the getting-ready stage immediately before action.'),
  ('2012-159', 'Psychiatric symptoms often occur on dimensions rather than in isolated categories. Health concern can range from ordinary worry to persistent anxiety with reassurance seeking, to an unshakeable delusional conviction. Insight describes awareness that one’s interpretation may be inaccurate, not whether distress is genuine. This dimensional view is especially useful because medical reassurance alone can fail in both conditions, though for different psychological reasons.'),
  ('2012-160', 'Tic severity naturally waxes and wanes, making it easy to overattribute changes to a recently started medication. Tourette syndrome requires both motor and vocal tics over time, whereas transient tics are common in childhood. Alpha-2 adrenergic agonists reduce noradrenergic signaling and are often remembered as a bridge between ADHD treatment and tic management. Their usefulness is practical, not absolute: treatment is guided by which symptoms create the greatest impairment.'),
  ('2012-161', 'Working memory is often described as a mental workspace rather than a passive storage bin. It allows a person to retain a phone number long enough to dial it, compare two ideas in conversation, or calculate while remembering interim results. Attention functions like the gate and spotlight for that workspace: without it, information is poorly encoded or quickly displaced. This relationship explains why attentional deficits can look like memory failure on bedside testing.'),
  ('2012-162', 'Pavlovian conditioning explains how emotionally neutral cues can acquire motivational power through association. The terminology is easiest when organized by time: before learning, identify what naturally causes the response; after learning, identify which formerly neutral cue now triggers it. In this example, the loud noise needs no training to be frightening, whereas the rat gains its fear value through pairing. Many avoidance patterns in anxiety disorders can be understood through this learning framework.'),
  ('2012-163', 'Forensic evaluations separate clinical diagnosis from legal capacity. A person may have a severe mental illness yet be competent if they can understand the proceedings and assist counsel; conversely, a person without a chronic diagnosis may become incompetent during delirium or severe mania. The memorable distinction is temporal: competence is here and now, while criminal responsibility focuses on the mental state during the act.'),
  ('2012-164', 'Dopamine has important roles in movement and reward learning. Parkinson treatments aim to improve deficient dopaminergic motor signaling, but some agents can also alter reward-driven behavior. Dopamine agonists are therefore a classic board-style link between a movement-disorder medication and behavioral disinhibition. The clinical clue is a new, out-of-character repetitive behavior with real-world harm after treatment exposure. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2012-165', 'The term mixed agonist/antagonist emphasizes that opioid drugs need not have the same action at every opioid receptor. Buprenorphine’s partial mu agonism explains why it can suppress withdrawal and craving, while its receptor affinity explains why it can precipitate withdrawal after recent full-agonist exposure. A useful memory association is partial activation with strong binding: it produces opioid effect but can block access by other opioids.'),
  ('2012-166', 'Bereavement is shaped by the relationship, prior functioning, social support, circumstances of death, and cultural meaning. Violent deaths can add traumatic exposure and unanswered questions to ordinary separation distress. This does not mean every survivor develops a psychiatric disorder, but it explains why the course may be more prolonged or complex. Remember the distinction between validating enduring grief and assuming a fixed sequence of universal stages.'),
  ('2012-167', 'Alcohol-related medical complications are often multifactorial: nutrition, gastrointestinal loss, renal handling, liver disease, and acute withdrawal can all affect laboratory values. Magnesium and phosphate are easy to overlook because they may not appear on a basic electrolyte panel, yet both are important for neuromuscular and cellular function. A useful pairing is alcohol plus malnutrition leading to magnesium and phosphate depletion.'),
  ('2012-168', 'The word catatonia often evokes stupor, but the syndrome has both retarded and excited forms. The unifying feature is a marked disturbance in motor behavior and responsiveness, not simply low activity. This is clinically memorable because excited catatonia can be mistaken for severe anxiety, mania, or disruptive behavior. Looking for accompanying motor signs helps organize the differential diagnosis. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2012-169', 'Seizure semiology can localize brain dysfunction. A unilateral motor onset before a bilateral convulsion suggests that seizure activity began in a focal cortical region and then spread. In younger people, genetic epilepsies are relatively more prominent, whereas a first focal seizure in an older adult more strongly raises concern for acquired structural disease. MRI is therefore remembered as the structural counterpart to EEG’s electrical information.'),
  ('2012-170', 'Exposure therapy is based on new learning through repeated approach to feared cues while reducing avoidance and safety behaviors. The exposure can be imaginal, interoceptive, virtual, or in vivo, depending on the fear and practical constraints. Imaginal is a literal memory aid: the feared situation is evoked in imagination. The essential therapeutic feature is deliberate, sustained approach rather than passive discussion of the fear.'),
  ('2012-171', 'Intellectual disability is defined by both intellectual-functioning and adaptive-functioning deficits that begin during development. Older terminology emphasized IQ bands, but functional skills now carry equal diagnostic importance. School represents a major developmental stress test because it adds structured expectations for symbolic learning, pace, self-organization, and independent performance. This is why relatively mild limitations may be recognized later than profound developmental disabilities.'),
  ('2012-172', 'Alcoholics Anonymous originated from a meeting between two men with alcoholism who sought to support abstinence through mutual help. Bill Wilson, often called Bill W., met Dr. Robert Smith, known as Dr. Bob, in 1935. Their collaboration later developed into AA''s fellowship and Twelve Step approach. Several other individuals were important influences or facilitators, but they are distinct from the two founders. The question tests recognition of the historical cofounder who was traveling on business when the meeting occurred.'),
  ('2012-173', 'Family-systems formulations emphasize recurring interaction patterns rather than assigning all pathology to one person. A triangle may be temporarily stable because attention shifts away from a tense dyad, but it often maintains the unresolved problem. Grandparents and other relatives may be drawn into parent-child disagreements, particularly in multigenerational families. The key image is a conflict line between two people becoming a three-cornered pattern when another person is recruited to take a side, carry messages, or mediate tension.'),
  ('2012-174', 'Cushing syndrome is the clinical state caused by sustained excess cortisol, whether from exogenous glucocorticoids or endogenous hormone production. Cortisol affects metabolism, blood pressure, muscle, immune-cell distribution, and skin. Reduced collagen and tissue fragility make bruising easier and may also produce wide purple striae. Neuropsychiatric manifestations can include depression, anxiety, irritability, cognitive changes, mania, or psychosis. This overlap means that a careful physical review is important when mood symptoms are unusually persistent or accompanied by systemic findings.'),
  ('2012-175', 'Piaget used schemas to describe organized mental frameworks for understanding the world. Cognitive growth involves an ongoing balance between assimilation and accommodation rather than simple accumulation of facts. Assimilation is efficient because it relies on a familiar framework. Accommodation becomes necessary when the existing framework repeatedly fails to explain new information. A useful memory cue is “assimilate equals add in”: a new experience is added to an existing mental category. These concepts describe how children reason, not whether their first conclusion is correct.'),
  ('2012-176', 'Alprostadil is a synthetic form of prostaglandin E1. It directly promotes vascular smooth-muscle relaxation in penile tissue, unlike oral therapies that work through systemic pharmacology. Its two classic erectile-dysfunction routes are a urethral pellet or suppository and injection into the corpora cavernosa. This route pairing is the high-yield association tested by the question. The same local vascular effect that enables erection also explains why priapism, local discomfort, hypotension, and injection-related fibrosis are important safety considerations.'),
  ('2012-177', 'The trigeminal nerve conveys facial sensation through ophthalmic, maxillary, and mandibular divisions. Trigeminal neuralgia classically affects the maxillary or mandibular territory. Its pain is often called electric because attacks are abrupt, intense, and short-lived instead of a continuous ache. The classic board pattern is “touch trigger plus seconds of facial shock.” Although many cases are idiopathic or classic, clinicians should remain alert to atypical features that suggest another neurologic process and require further assessment.'),
  ('2012-178', 'Erikson''s psychosocial model organizes development around recurring tensions rather than fixed diagnoses. Industry is the sense that one can learn, make, and accomplish things valued by family, school, and peers. Inferiority can develop when children repeatedly experience themselves as less capable or less accepted. Thus, achievement, hobbies, rules, peer comparison, and feedback are particularly salient during the school-age period. A useful memory sequence is preschool initiative, school-age industry, and then adolescent identity.'),
  ('2012-179', 'The transtheoretical model describes change as a process that may include precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action, maintenance, and recurrence of the behavior. These stages guide intervention matching rather than define fixed personality types. Contemplation is often the “yes, but” stage: a person can state both compelling reasons to change and compelling reasons to remain the same. Motivational interviewing and motivational enhancement techniques are designed to work with this ambivalence rather than fight it through persuasion or confrontation.'),
  ('2012-180', 'Transference consists of feelings, expectations, and relationship patterns organized around the therapist. Idealization can include a wish to borrow the therapist''s valued qualities, lifestyle, or identity; it is not inherently erotic or dangerous. Psychodynamic technique seeks to make the meaning of behavior thinkable while preserving a clear professional frame. The frame protects both patient and clinician and makes it possible to explore the relationship instead of enacting it. Boundary assessment remains necessary even when a patient''s motivation appears nonthreatening.'),
  ('2012-181', 'Neuropsychological assessment links measured cognitive performance to brain functioning and everyday abilities. Classic batteries use multiple tasks because neurologic disorders rarely affect only one cognitive construct. The Halstead-Reitan Battery is a well-known historical example and remains a useful board association, although neuropsychologists now often select flexible batteries based on the referral question. Projective and personality instruments assess personality constructs, whereas neuropsychological batteries assess cognitive and behavioral consequences of brain dysfunction. The distinction between these test categories is central to this item.'),
  ('2012-182', 'Panic disorder involves recurrent panic attacks accompanied by persistent concern or maladaptive behavior related to future attacks. The disorder is closely tied to interoception, or attention to and interpretation of internal bodily sensations. Breathlessness is especially salient because it can be interpreted as an immediate threat to survival. This helps explain the connection with respiratory illness and why panic may be confused with cardiopulmonary disease. A useful memory link is that panic often centers on feared bodily sensations, especially difficulty breathing.'),
  ('2012-183', 'Anticholinergic toxicity results from blockade of muscarinic acetylcholine receptors. It classically causes dry skin, dilated pupils, tachycardia, urinary retention, decreased bowel activity, hyperthermia, and delirium. Physostigmine inhibits acetylcholinesterase and increases acetylcholine at synapses; unlike some related agents, it enters the central nervous system. That central activity explains its potential to reverse delirium. The board association is anticholinergic delirium with physostigmine, tempered in practice by the need for toxicology-informed precautions.'),
  ('2012-184', 'The necessaries principle is broader than psychiatry: a custodian who controls a person’s liberty must provide essential care that the person cannot reasonably secure independently. In correctional psychiatry, it supports the principle that incarceration does not erase health needs or the institutional obligation to respond to them. The memory connection is simple: control of access creates responsibility for access. This rationale is distinct from punishment, deterrence, or assumptions about dangerousness.'),
  ('2012-185', 'Older psychiatric texts used HIV-associated dementia for a subcortical syndrome of cognitive slowing, apathy, and motor changes, often in advanced HIV disease. Effective antiretroviral therapy has changed the epidemiology and presentation of HIV-associated neurocognitive disorders. Consequently, older medication associations should be understood in their historical exam context rather than as complete current practice guidance. The stimulant association reflects an attempt to improve activation and psychomotor slowing, not treatment of the underlying viral process or every cognitive complaint in a person with HIV.'),
  ('2012-186', 'The hippocampal formation lies in the medial temporal lobe and is central to encoding and consolidating new declarative memories. It does not store every memory in isolation; it helps organize new experiences so longer-term cortical systems can retain them. Bilateral damage is much more disruptive than unilateral injury. The classic pattern is preserved immediate attention with rapid forgetting of newly presented information, producing an anterograde amnesia syndrome. This pattern distinguishes medial temporal dysfunction from disorders of coordination, language, or learned motor acts.'),
  ('2012-187', 'Young children are especially vulnerable to head injury because their heads are proportionally large and they depend completely on caregivers. Abusive head trauma is the preferred descriptive clinical term for inflicted injury to the head and brain; it emphasizes evaluation of the complete medical and historical evidence rather than reliance on a single label. For this examination framework, severe inflicted head trauma is the memorable fatality association, while real cases require comprehensive medical investigation.'),
  ('2012-188', 'Early development includes learning both that people and objects persist and that the child is a person distinct from caregivers. These achievements are related but not interchangeable. Object permanence asks whether an object still exists when absent. Self-other differentiation asks whether the child recognizes separate intentions, wishes, and ownership. Possessive language makes the latter visible in everyday interactions. A useful memory cue is that “mine” concerns the child''s claim of selfhood, whereas object permanence concerns an object that is no longer visible.'),
  ('2012-189', 'Withdrawal timing reflects pharmacokinetics and neuroadaptation. Short-acting opioids produce a relatively early, intense syndrome; long-acting opioids delay and prolong it. A useful test-taking timeline is heroin: onset within roughly a half-day, peak around one to three days, and substantial improvement after several days. Do not confuse this with alcohol withdrawal, whose dangerous complications have a different timeline and physiology. Timing guides assessment but does not replace evaluation of the whole patient.'),
  ('2012-190', 'Hypnosis is best understood as an alteration of attention rather than sleep or loss of control. Commonly taught components include absorption, dissociation, and suggestibility. Absorption answers what attention has become deeply engaged with; dissociation concerns altered connections among experiences; and suggestibility concerns receptiveness to direction. In this vignette, attention is occupied enough that background stimuli lose salience, much as nearby conversation can fade while someone is immersed in a compelling book. The components can overlap, but the narrowed focus is the key clue here.'),
  ('2012-191', 'ADHD pharmacotherapy commonly begins with either methylphenidate-based or amphetamine-based stimulants. Individual response differs substantially between these two families, so insufficient response to one does not establish insufficient response to all stimulants. Nonstimulants have an important role when stimulant treatment is unsuitable or ineffective, but sequencing questions often test a trial of the alternate stimulant family after an adequate trial of the first. The useful memory cue is “same treatment category, different stimulant family,” while continuing to evaluate functioning and adverse effects.'),
  ('2012-192', 'During starvation, total-body mineral stores may be depleted even if an initial serum measurement is not strikingly low. Reintroduction of carbohydrate stimulates insulin release and cellular anabolism, increasing intracellular phosphate demand. Phosphate is essential to energy-dependent cellular processes, so a rapid decline can affect skeletal muscle, respiratory function, and cardiac function. The memory link is that feeding turns metabolism back on and can reveal phosphate depletion. Refeeding syndrome also involves fluid and other electrolyte shifts, but hypophosphatemia is its classic laboratory hallmark.'),
  ('2012-193', 'The Nuremberg Code is a foundational response to abuses in human experimentation. Its emphasis on voluntary consent remains a conceptual cornerstone even though contemporary research ethics also relies on institutional review, additional protections for vulnerable populations, and later frameworks such as the Belmont principles. The memory anchor is chronological and conceptual: Nuremberg established the indispensable consent principle, while later documents refined research governance and ethical analysis.'),
  ('2012-194', 'Suicide risk assessment is a clinical formulation rather than a checklist score. Population-level risk factors must be interpreted alongside the patient’s current state, recent changes, history, and available supports. Prior suicidal behavior is especially important because it shows that suicidal thoughts or distress have previously progressed to action. It does not make suicide inevitable, but it warrants careful assessment and prevention planning. Acute intent, planning, access to lethal means, intoxication, psychosis, agitation, and social disconnection can change near-term risk substantially and require direct evaluation.'),
  ('2012-195', 'Family assessment treats symptoms as occurring within relationships and routines, not only within one identified patient. A household can include biological relatives, adoptive relatives, partners, or nonrelatives; the important question is who repeatedly affects one another’s behavior and emotional climate. Asking who is involved when the problem occurs directs attention to functional relationships rather than a family tree alone. A genogram can broaden historical understanding, whereas direct exploration of current interactions clarifies present dynamics, roles, and patterns of reinforcement.'),
  ('2012-196', 'Delirium is an acute disturbance in attention and awareness caused by a medical, toxic, or medication-related condition. Older adults and people with dementia have reduced cognitive reserve and are particularly vulnerable. SSRIs are often useful, but medication adverse effects should be considered whenever new confusion follows a recent prescription. The practical memory connection is “acute change plus a new drug”: review medications and evaluate common reversible medical causes first. Sodium disturbance is a classic SSRI-related concern in this setting.'),
  ('2012-197', 'The adrenal cortex produces glucocorticoids and mineralocorticoids, which help regulate stress responses, blood pressure, salt balance, and energy metabolism. In primary adrenal failure, low cortisol removes feedback inhibition on ACTH; ACTH-related melanocyte stimulation helps explain hyperpigmentation. Mineralocorticoid deficiency contributes to salt craving and volume depletion. A compact memory phrase is “thin, tan, and salt-seeking” for primary adrenal insufficiency. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2012-198', 'Child assessment often uses multiple informants because behavior varies across home, school, and peer settings. Broad-band instruments cast a wide net across emotional and behavioral domains, whereas narrow-band scales concentrate on a particular syndrome, such as ADHD. The CBCL is memorable as a checklist of child behavior that can identify patterns needing fuller evaluation. Norms place reported behavior in developmental context, but they do not replace clinical judgment, a longitudinal history, or assessment of impairment across settings.'),
  ('2012-199', 'Dystonia is sustained or intermittent muscle contraction that produces abnormal movements or postures. Focal dystonias affect one body region, and some are task-specific: symptoms emerge during a highly practiced skilled action such as writing or playing an instrument. This can seem paradoxical because the limb may work normally at rest. Botulinum toxin acts locally at the neuromuscular junction, making it suitable for excessive contraction in selected muscles. The useful cue is “one task, one body region, targeted treatment.”'),
  ('2012-200', 'A common memory aid is “LOT”: lorazepam, oxazepam, and temazepam are primarily metabolized by conjugation rather than oxidative hepatic pathways. In board questions, lorazepam is often the available option. Drugs that rely on oxidative metabolism can have prolonged effects when hepatic function is impaired, especially when active metabolites are present. The pharmacology principle is more useful than memorizing a list: impaired liver oxidation favors agents cleared mainly through glucuronidation, while careful clinical monitoring remains essential.'),
  ('2012-201', 'Personality disorders are enduring, inflexible patterns that begin by early adulthood and cause impairment across contexts. Substance use can mimic or amplify impulsivity, hostility, unstable relationships, and legal problems, so diagnosis requires evidence that the pattern persists outside intoxication and withdrawal. The association tested here is epidemiologic and conceptual, not deterministic. A useful contrast is that antisocial personality disorder centers on disregard for others’ rights and rules, whereas borderline personality disorder centers more on instability in relationships, self-image, and affect.'),
  ('2012-202', 'IPT is often remembered by four problem areas: grief, role disputes, role transitions, and interpersonal deficits. Its core premise is that mood symptoms and interpersonal context influence one another in the present. It is not simply supportive therapy because it uses a clear formulation and structured focus. It is also not classic psychoanalysis because it does not primarily explore unconscious conflict or transference. The concise memory cue is “current relationships, current roles, current symptoms,” with active work on the identified interpersonal problem.'),
  ('2012-203', 'Human speech is a highly structured social signal containing rhythm, prosody, and patterns that infants can detect before they know words. Early preference for voices provides a memorable starting point for language development, followed by progressively more experience-shaped perception. The useful contrast is preference at birth and language mastery over years. Developmental variation is normal, but concerns about hearing or communication still require appropriate clinical assessment rather than inference from this finding alone.'),
  ('2012-204', 'Diagnostic systems distinguish primary mood disorders from syndromes caused by substances, medications, or medical conditions. The distinction matters because timing, causality, and persistence help determine what an episode signifies about the underlying course. Older PRITE questions may use the broad term substance-induced mood disorder; newer nomenclature separates medication-induced bipolar presentations from medication-induced depressive presentations. The enduring lesson is causal reasoning: determine whether the syndrome arose during exposure to, and is better explained by, a medication rather than assuming a primary mood disorder.'),
  ('2012-205', 'ECT is a somatic treatment that induces a controlled therapeutic seizure under anesthesia and muscle relaxation. It remains particularly important because some depressive states are too severe to wait for the usual time course of medication response. Catatonia and refusal of nutrition or fluids are memorable signs of urgency. Rather than viewing ECT as a last resort in every circumstance, remember that its relative priority rises with severity, medical compromise, psychosis, catatonia, and acute suicidality. Medical stabilization and a careful pre-ECT evaluation remain necessary.'),
  ('2012-206', 'The cross-setting requirement reflects that ADHD is a pervasive developmental condition, although its visibility varies with structure, demands, and supports. School may expose symptoms because it requires sustained attention and behavioral regulation, while home may reveal symptoms during routines and homework. Multi-informant assessment is therefore part of testing the diagnosis, not merely administrative work. Remember that ADHD should travel across settings: impairment must appear in at least two settings rather than only in one difficult relationship, classroom, or environment.'),
  ('2012-207', 'Diffusion-weighted MRI detects restricted water movement that develops early in acute ischemic stroke, making new infarcts conspicuous relative to older structural changes. Vascular-territory localization links anatomy to expected deficits. Posterior cerebral circulation is strongly associated with occipital cortex and vision, whereas middle cerebral circulation is associated with lateral hemispheric language, neglect, and face-arm motor findings. A compact memory cue is that the PCA points posteriorly toward the visual cortex. Imaging findings must be interpreted with the clinical examination and full stroke evaluation.'),
  ('2012-208', 'Insanity standards are legal constructs that use psychiatric evidence but are not interchangeable with diagnosis, competence, or risk assessment. A person may have a severe mental disorder yet not meet a jurisdiction’s insanity standard, and a clinician does not decide guilt. The memorable sequence is law first, then facts and clinical evidence, then an opinion tied to the legal elements. This sequence prevents a generic psychiatric assessment from answering the wrong legal question.'),
  ('2012-209', 'Clozapine has clinically important interactions with environmental exposures as well as prescribed medications. Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons from burning tobacco induce CYP1A2; nicotine is not the relevant inducer. A useful memory pair is that smoking lowers clozapine concentrations, whereas smoking cessation can raise them. Clozapine is especially important because a previously tolerated dose may become toxic after an abrupt change in smoking behavior. Ask specifically about combustible tobacco rather than simply asking whether the patient uses nicotine.'),
  ('2012-210', 'Older models of mourning often implied a relatively uniform progression toward detachment from the deceased. Broader cultural awareness has challenged that narrow expectation. Many people maintain meaningful bonds through memories, rituals, prayer, stories, or symbolic communication while continuing to function and adapt. Grief has many culturally meaningful expressions, so clinicians should distinguish an unfamiliar mourning style from a persistently impairing syndrome. Symptoms must be interpreted in the context of the person''s community, beliefs, relationships, and functioning.'),
  ('2012-211', 'Mentalizing means treating behavior as meaningful but not fully self-explanatory: actions arise from changing internal states that can be misunderstood. Attachment relationships are central because they are an early setting in which reflective capacities develop and a common source of later emotional activation. MBT is deliberately humble and inquisitive. Instead of claiming to know hidden motives, the clinician and patient examine possible meanings together and tolerate uncertainty about them.'),
  ('2012-212', 'Personality-disorder clusters are a study aid rather than a complete explanation of a person. Cluster A is often remembered as odd or eccentric, cluster B as dramatic or erratic, and cluster C as anxious or fearful. Social withdrawal provides a useful bridge to the schizophrenia spectrum, but it does not establish schizophrenia. Personality disorders involve enduring patterns, whereas schizophrenia requires characteristic psychotic symptoms and clinically significant functional change over time.'),
  ('2012-213', 'The name selective mutism can misleadingly suggest willful refusal. In most affected children, silence is better understood as a fear-driven avoidance response in particular social settings. The contrast between normal speech in a comfortable environment and silence at school is diagnostically memorable. Behavioral methods make the feared behavior gradual, predictable, and reinforced, allowing the child to practice communication without escalating shame or pressure. The intervention should involve the settings in which mutism occurs.'),
  ('2012-214', 'Trail making is memorable because the task depicts its cognitive demands: the examinee must visually locate the next target and trace an ordered path without losing the rule. Part A is commonly conceptualized as a simpler speeded sequencing task. Part B adds alternating sets, such as numbers and letters. Comparing the two portions can be informative, but neither score should be interpreted outside the larger neurologic and neuropsychological context.'),
  ('2012-215', 'Trauma responses range from mobilization, including fear and arousal, to shutdown-like experiences of detachment or unreality. Dissociation is not synonymous with psychosis and can occur transiently in otherwise reality-based people under extreme stress. For examination purposes, connect descriptions such as outside my body, dreamlike, numb, or time slowed during trauma with peritraumatic dissociation and increased later PTSD risk. It is a risk marker, not a guarantee of chronic PTSD.'),
  ('2012-216', 'Lithium is handled by the kidney in ways closely tied to sodium and water balance. Its serum level is therefore sensitive to dehydration, renal impairment, and medications that alter renal perfusion or tubular handling. This gives a useful board-style association: older age plus medical comorbidity increases the practical complexity of lithium. Valproate shifts the monitoring problem rather than eliminating it, because liver function, platelet effects, and other adverse effects remain clinically relevant.'),
  ('2012-217', 'Modern family interventions for schizophrenia moved away from blaming families for causing illness. Psychoeducation instead treats relatives as key partners who often provide daily support and can benefit from practical knowledge and skills. The early therapeutic task is relational: acknowledge burden, identify existing competencies, and establish shared goals. Remember engagement as the stage that earns the alliance before asking families to undertake structured educational or behavioral work.'),
  ('2012-218', 'Glucocorticoids can produce insomnia, irritability, euphoria, depression, delirium, and psychosis. A close onset after initiation or dose escalation, especially at higher doses, is a useful causal clue. The first principle in medication-induced syndromes is to remove or reduce the provoking agent when safe to do so. Psychiatric medications may be important adjuncts, but they do not replace coordination with the clinician managing the underlying medical illness and corticosteroid indication.'),
  ('2012-219', 'Peripheral nerves carry different sensory modalities. Vibration and joint-position sense are often called large-fiber modalities, and their marked distal loss helps localize a sensory neuropathy. Cisplatin is a memorable oncology association because treatment can damage peripheral sensory pathways as exposure accumulates. Pair common agents with signature toxicities: cisplatin with neuropathy, bleomycin with pulmonary toxicity, and doxorubicin with cardiotoxicity. This pattern helps distinguish treatment toxicity from other causes of distal sensory symptoms.'),
  ('2012-220', 'Diagnostic systems change as evidence and clinical practice evolve. DSM-IV-TR separated several pervasive developmental disorders, whereas DSM-5 combined them under autism spectrum disorder. Historical questions often test the former distinction, so the memory anchor is Asperger disorder with no clinically significant language delay. That statement concerns early language acquisition, not whether a person has pragmatic-language differences, social-communication challenges, or a need for support.'),
  ('2012-221', 'Separation anxiety is normal during some developmental periods, but it becomes a disorder when fear is excessive for the child''s developmental stage, persistent, and impairing. School refusal is a behavior rather than a diagnosis; it can arise from anxiety, depression, bullying, learning difficulties, or other causes. The memory connection is attachment proximity: the child seeks to remain near a caregiver and avoids settings, including school or bedtime, that require separation.'),
  ('2012-222', 'Lithium pharmacology follows a simple rule: the kidney controls the level. Because lithium handling parallels aspects of sodium and water handling, volume depletion and medications affecting renal tubular or hemodynamic function can have outsized effects. A patient may tolerate the same lithium dose for years and then become toxic after an apparently routine medical prescription. In contrast with many psychotropics, CYP interactions are not central to lithium toxicity.'),
  ('2012-223', 'Time is central in acute ischemic stroke because brain tissue at risk may be salvageable if blood flow is restored quickly. Last known well is more important than when a patient was discovered symptomatic. Noncontrast CT is used emergently to identify hemorrhage, while early ischemic changes can be subtle or absent. The memorable sequence is sudden focal deficit, determine onset, exclude hemorrhage, and rapidly assess reperfusion treatment eligibility.'),
  ('2012-224', 'Alcohol withdrawal reflects neuroadaptation to chronic alcohol exposure: inhibitory signaling is reduced, and excitatory signaling becomes relatively unopposed when alcohol is removed. Benzodiazepines temporarily enhance GABA-A receptor activity and are tapered as withdrawal resolves. A useful contrast is long acting equals a smoother landing, whereas short acting can create peaks and troughs. Shorter-acting agents can still be appropriate, especially when accumulation is a concern, but their pharmacokinetics make consistent monitoring and dosing particularly important.'),
  ('2012-225', 'Beck-style cognitive therapy describes depression as being sustained partly by automatic negative thoughts and deeper conditional assumptions or core beliefs. A stressor does not inevitably create depression; its personal interpretation helps shape emotion and behavior. The cognitive triad of negative views of self, world, and future is a useful memory framework. Here, the self-directed conclusion that he is a failure is the proximal CBT target, although the relationship event remains important context.'),
  ('2012-226', 'The caudate is part of the dorsal striatum and is functionally connected with frontal cortex and thalamus. OCD is often remembered as a problem of an overactive error signal or inadequate filtering of repetitive mental and behavioral loops. This is a circuit-level model, not a diagnostic imaging test for an individual patient. Its practical counterpart is response prevention: learning that anxiety can decline without performing the ritual. The distinction between an intrusive thought and a neutralizing ritual is central to recognizing the syndrome.'),
  ('2012-227', 'Children occupy several environments, including home, school, peer groups, and medical settings, and symptoms often look different in each. Developmental stage also limits the completeness of self-report. This is why child psychiatry relies on a multi-informant formulation rather than treating the child interview as the sole source of truth. Caregiver interviews supply both history and context for interpreting the child’s account, even when additional information from school or testing is needed.'),
  ('2012-228', 'Lithium has a narrow therapeutic index and is cleared almost entirely by the kidneys. Its handling is closely tied to sodium and water balance, so dehydration or reduced renal perfusion can raise levels even without an intentional overdose. A compact memory cue is lithium: gastrointestinal symptoms, tremor, and neurologic change. The same pharmacology that makes serum monitoring useful also makes dose changes, hydration, renal function, and interacting drugs clinically consequential.'),
  ('2012-229', 'Myasthenia gravis is a postsynaptic disorder of the neuromuscular junction. Each nerve impulse releases acetylcholine, but fewer functional receptors and damaged postsynaptic folds reduce the safety margin for transmission; repeated activity makes failure more apparent. Contrast it with Lambert-Eaton syndrome: myasthenia involves the postsynaptic receptor, whereas Lambert-Eaton involves the presynaptic calcium channel. That location-based distinction predicts both the antibody testing and the clinical pattern of weakness.'),
  ('2012-230', 'Hopelessness refers to pessimistic, rigid expectations about future outcomes and one’s ability to influence them. It is distinct from sadness: someone may feel intensely sad yet still expect change, or feel emotionally blunted while believing there is no future. In cognitive terms, hopelessness is a future-oriented negative appraisal. For exam recall, depression often emphasizes negative self-evaluation, while suicide risk adds the question of whether the person sees any possibility that circumstances can change.'),
  ('2012-231', 'Brief psychodynamic therapies focus limited time on a central conflict or recurring interpersonal pattern. A useful sequence is notice, name, and link: notice the repeated outcome, name the behavior or feeling, and link it to the patient’s stated aim. Discrepancy points to two things that do not fit together, such as wanting acceptance while repeatedly eliciting rejection. Recognition of that mismatch can make a new response possible and can help organize a focused treatment formulation.'),
  ('2012-232', 'Turning against the self is a classic psychodynamic formulation in which an unacceptable feeling toward another person is redirected inward. The child’s dependency makes anger toward caregivers especially conflicted because the same people who seem unavailable are also needed for comfort and protection. The central memory link is anger at a loved other, action toward self. This explains symbolic meaning within the theory; it does not imply that every episode of head banging has the same cause.'),
  ('2012-233', 'Antidepressant comparisons often turn less on whether a medication can work than on whether a patient can safely and comfortably remain on it long enough. TCAs affect multiple receptor systems, helping explain anticholinergic, antihistaminic, and alpha-adrenergic adverse effects. SSRIs act more selectively on serotonin transport, although selective does not mean side-effect free. The concise memory contrast is SSRIs are generally better tolerated, whereas TCAs carry a broader receptor-mediated adverse-effect burden.'),
  ('2012-234', 'In the mental-status examination, attention is the capacity to focus, while concentration is sustained or effortful focus over a task. Backward sequencing adds a working-memory demand, so it is more sensitive to impaired concentration than simple rote repetition. The task is simple enough to use without equipment but remains a screen rather than a diagnosis. Remember the mapping: date equals orientation, breakfast equals recent memory, similarities equal abstraction, and backward alphabet equals concentration.'),
  ('2012-235', 'Stimulants increase catecholaminergic signaling, which can improve attention but can also rarely be associated with psychotic symptoms in susceptible contexts. The key teaching distinction is severity rather than inconvenience. Appetite change and insomnia are common dose-related tolerability problems, whereas hallucinations alter the safety and diagnostic frame. A helpful memory cue is psychosis changes the plan: stop the suspected medication and reassess the full clinical picture.'),
  ('2012-236', 'Human infants are unusually dependent for a prolonged period. Attachment behaviors such as crying, following, clinging, and seeking contact are therefore understood as proximity-promoting behaviors. The memorable contrast is attachment for protection and exploration from safety. A reliable caregiver functions as a safe base: the infant moves outward to explore and returns when frightened, tired, or overwhelmed. This model explains why separation and reunion behavior is central to attachment observation.'),
  ('2012-237', 'Malpractice claims commonly concern how care was managed rather than dramatic unusual events alone. Incorrect treatment includes errors of commission and omission, such as choosing an unsuitable intervention, failing to monitor it, or not escalating care when warranted. Claim categories and their frequency depend on the population and reporting system studied. A useful practical memory rule is that the record should show the clinical question, information considered, rationale, and follow-up plan. Documentation supports care but cannot repair care that was not provided.'),
  ('2012-238', 'CSF immunoglobulin results are most informative when considered with the serum result. An isolated intrathecal signal points toward antibody production within the CNS, whereas parallel elevation in serum and CSF more readily suggests a systemic inflammatory state influencing both compartments. Sarcoidosis is a multisystem granulomatous disease that can involve the nervous system, though no single CSF marker establishes the diagnosis. A memory cue is that multiple sclerosis favors intrathecal bands, while sarcoidosis can show a systemic pattern in serum and CSF.'),
  ('2012-239', 'Akathisia derives from Greek roots conveying an inability to sit still. It is an extrapyramidal adverse effect associated with dopamine-blocking drugs and can be profoundly dysphoric. The high-yield distinction is akathisia equals inner restlessness, whereas parkinsonism involves slowed, rigid movement. That distinction helps recall treatment: propranolol is classically used for akathisia, while anticholinergics are classically more helpful for parkinsonism. Careful symptom description prevents mislabeling akathisia as worsening psychiatric agitation.'),
  ('2012-240', 'Thiamine, or vitamin B1, supports several carbohydrate-metabolism pathways. Alcohol dependence increases the risk of deficiency because intake, absorption, storage, and utilization may all be impaired. Wernicke encephalopathy may present with confusion, ataxia, and eye-movement abnormalities, although many patients lack the complete classic triad. The traditional emergency teaching point is to provide thiamine with glucose in a nutritionally vulnerable patient, while prioritizing immediate correction of dangerous hypoglycemia.'),
  ('2012-241', 'Passive-aggressive behavior refers to indirect expression of anger, hostility, or opposition. It may appear as procrastination, intentional inefficiency, forgetfulness, or failure to complete agreed tasks. Older classifications attempted to make this pattern a standalone personality-disorder category. Contemporary diagnostic systems no longer do so, partly because the pattern overlaps with mood, anxiety, trauma, interpersonal, and situational problems. The term remains useful as a descriptive behavioral shorthand when used cautiously.'),
  ('2012-242', 'Psychiatric assessment after a reproductive event should avoid both minimization and stigma. Emotional responses vary widely and are shaped by prior mental health, wantedness, social support, coercion, medical complications, and violence exposure. A useful conceptual anchor is that psychiatric history is often more predictive of later depression than a single life event considered in isolation. The evaluation should also identify immediate safety concerns and respect the patient’s autonomy and confidentiality.'),
  ('2012-243', 'Erikson described development as a series of lifelong psychosocial tasks. In the final stage, integrity versus despair concerns whether a person can accept a lived life as meaningful and internally coherent. Generativity looks outward toward caring for future generations, whereas integrity looks backward toward one’s own life course. This distinction helps separate the two adjacent adult developmental themes on examinations and in clinical discussions of life review.'),
  ('2012-244', 'Idioms of distress are culturally meaningful ways of communicating suffering. They may emphasize emotions, bodily symptoms, interpersonal strain, or spiritual concerns rather than standard diagnostic labels. “Nervios” is a broad expression whose meaning varies among individuals and communities; it may encompass anxiety, stress, somatic symptoms, or depressive distress. Culture shapes presentation and explanatory models, but careful individualized assessment determines the syndrome, severity, and immediate risk.'),
  ('2012-245', 'Bell’s palsy is a peripheral facial paralysis syndrome, generally considered idiopathic after other causes are excluded. CN VII supplies muscles of facial expression and contributes to stapedius function, taste from the anterior tongue, and lacrimal and salivary functions. The high-yield localization distinction is forehead involvement in peripheral facial palsy versus relative forehead sparing in typical central facial weakness. Hyperacusis further supports involvement of the facial nerve.'),
  ('2012-246', 'Mixed states challenge the simplified idea that mania and depression are separate opposites. A person may have agitation, insomnia, racing thoughts, and increased activity while also experiencing despair, guilt, or suicidal thinking. This combination is clinically important because depressive distress may coexist with increased energy and impulsivity. For recall, classic episodic euphoric mania is the prototypical lithium-responsive pattern, whereas dysphoric mixed activation is the caution flag.'),
  ('2012-247', 'GAD can be remembered as “worry plus wear-and-tear.” The worry is broad, persistent, and difficult to control rather than limited to one feared situation. Its associated symptoms reflect sustained arousal and depletion: feeling keyed up can coexist with fatigue. This apparent contradiction is useful for recall because prolonged hypervigilance is exhausting rather than energizing. Sleep disturbance, concentration difficulty, irritability, and muscle tension commonly reinforce the pattern.'),
  ('2012-248', 'The entorhinal cortex is part of the medial temporal lobe and connects widespread association cortex with the hippocampus. Because it helps route information into memory circuits, early involvement provides a bridge from pathology to symptoms: impaired encoding of recent experiences. A practical memory cue is that the entorhinal cortex is an entry point to hippocampal memory processing. This helps explain why recent episodic memory is often prominently affected early in Alzheimer disease.'),
  ('2012-249', 'Older psychiatric classifications divided schizophrenia into named subtypes. “Simple” did not mean mild or uncomplicated; it referred to a presentation dominated by progressive loss of motivation, social engagement, and affective expression. Contemporary systems emphasize longitudinal symptoms, impairment, and dimensional negative symptoms because older subtype boundaries were unstable and often overlapping. The historical term remains relevant for understanding older examination language and psychiatric literature.'),
  ('2012-250', 'Pregnancy changes the risk calculus in opioid use disorder because withdrawal, relapse, and inconsistent opioid exposure can destabilize maternal and fetal care. Methadone is a long-acting opioid agonist and was the classic maintenance answer in older board questions. Modern treatment may also include other evidence-based medications, but the enduring principle is maintenance-based stabilization rather than routine detoxification during pregnancy. Prenatal, addiction, and psychosocial care should be integrated.'),
  ('2012-251', 'Lithium is a monovalent, water-soluble cation handled by the kidneys in a manner related to sodium. It circulates with minimal protein binding and is eliminated unchanged, without active metabolites. General dialysis principles favor removal of small, water-soluble substances with low protein binding and limited distribution volume. Lithium matches those properties, although intracellular distribution can produce post-dialysis rebound. Thus, the keyed statement is true and the only viable listed answer, while the other pharmacokinetic features more directly explain efficient dialysis.'),
  ('2012-252', 'Disruptive behavior disorders are diagnosed from longitudinal patterns, but aggression has many possible pathways. Neurodevelopmental difficulties can impair impulse control, trauma can heighten threat reactivity, and mood or substance disorders can intensify irritability and disinhibition. A useful examination principle is to diagnose broadly before attributing narrowly: chronic behavior problems do not rule out a co-occurring condition that is clinically urgent, treatable, or central to the immediate risk assessment.'),
  ('2012-253', 'TCAs have multiple toxic effects, but blockade of fast cardiac sodium channels makes the EKG especially high yield. This mechanism slows ventricular depolarization and may widen the QRS complex. The board-style association is direct: TCA overdose requires cardiac conduction assessment. A drug level may support confirmation, whereas the EKG helps establish immediate physiologic danger and urgency of management. This distinction is especially important after intentional ingestion.'),
  ('2012-254', 'Delirium is an acute neuropsychiatric syndrome whose hallmark is fluctuation. A patient may appear relatively clear at one moment and profoundly confused later, particularly across the sleep-wake cycle. Unlike a chronic dementia syndrome, delirium is a medical emergency because it commonly signals an acute physiologic, medication-related, toxic, or environmental insult. Attention and level of awareness are especially important bedside features for distinguishing it from a primary amnestic disorder.'),
  ('2012-255', 'The New Freedom Commission era is commonly remembered through a systems-level message: mental health services should be accessible, coordinated, recovery-oriented, and connected to general medical care. Parity captures the memorable core idea that mental illness warrants comparable attention to physical illness. This framing shifts focus from isolated services toward whether a person can obtain meaningful, continuous care across settings and throughout recovery.'),
  ('2012-256', 'Sulfatides are lipid components enriched in myelin. In arylsulfatase A deficiency, lysosomal degradation of sulfatides is impaired, allowing progressive accumulation that damages white matter and peripheral nerves. A useful memory link is that sulfatase deficiency causes storage of sulfatides. The key contrast is Krabbe disease: Krabbe is caused by galactocerebrosidase deficiency, whereas metachromatic leukodystrophy is associated with arylsulfatase A deficiency. This distinction is frequently tested among lysosomal storage disorders.'),
  ('2012-257', 'Light is an important cue for circadian rhythms. Reduced morning light during darker seasons may contribute to delayed or disrupted daily rhythms in vulnerable people, helping explain the association between winter-pattern depression and light-based treatment. On examinations, connect recurrent seasonal depressive symptoms with morning bright-light therapy. The broader lesson is that sleep-wake timing, environmental light exposure, and mood symptoms can be clinically connected, even though treatment must still include a full psychiatric assessment.'),
  ('2012-258', 'Psychotic features in major depression can be mood-congruent and may focus on guilt, sin, poverty, bodily disease, catastrophe, or deserved punishment. Their presence indicates a severe depressive episode and can resemble a primary delusional disorder if the clinician focuses only on the unusual belief. A useful organizing question is whether the delusional content occurs within a sustained mood syndrome and reflects the patient’s dominant depressive themes. This helps distinguish psychotic depression from isolated health anxiety or obsessional doubt.'),
  ('2012-259', 'Binge-eating disorder and bulimia nervosa can both involve recurrent binge episodes. The central diagnostic divider is whether recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviors occur after the binges. Binge eating plus purging, fasting, or compensatory exercise suggests bulimia nervosa; binge eating without recurrent compensation suggests binge-eating disorder. Both conditions can involve secrecy, distress, medical complications, and substantial psychosocial burden. Body size alone does not establish or exclude either diagnosis, so the behavioral pattern is the most useful starting point.'),
  ('2012-260', 'PTSD symptoms are commonly organized into intrusion, avoidance, negative changes in cognition or mood, and arousal or reactivity. SSRIs can improve several symptom domains, although response is variable and trauma-focused psychotherapy remains central to care. The examination principle is to choose a treatment directed at the syndrome rather than an agent that only provides short-term sedation. This distinction helps separate SSRI use from chronic benzodiazepine use in patients with persistent trauma-related anxiety and insomnia.'),
  ('2012-261', 'Neuromuscular transmission normally has a safety margin: sufficient receptor activity remains available to trigger muscle action potentials repeatedly. In myasthenia gravis, reduced functional postsynaptic receptors lower that margin. Repeated stimulation progressively exposes transmission failure, producing the characteristic decremental response. Contrast this with presynaptic Lambert-Eaton syndrome, where repeated activity can transiently increase acetylcholine release and facilitate the response. This contrast helps distinguish two disorders that can both present with weakness.'),
  ('2012-262', 'Drug-induced parkinsonism results from dopamine blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway. It often presents with bradykinesia, rigidity, masked facies, and sometimes tremor after starting or increasing an antipsychotic. Adverse effects are best managed by first reconsidering dose and ongoing need before adding another medication. This approach reduces unnecessary polypharmacy and preserves the ability to identify the causal agent. It also minimizes the risk that treatment of an adverse effect will worsen the underlying psychotic illness.'),
  ('2012-263', 'The phrase three-Hz spike and wave is a high-yield association for childhood absence seizures. Think of a rhythmic generalized electrical pattern that matches brief, repetitive interruptions in awareness. Typical age at onset and rapid recovery help distinguish absence seizures from focal impaired-awareness seizures, which are often longer and may involve automatisms or confusion afterward. Hyperventilation is a classic activation maneuver that can provoke typical absence events during assessment. These features are more useful than interpreting staring behavior alone.'),
  ('2012-264', 'Bioequivalence is a pharmacokinetic concept concerning comparable systemic exposure to an active drug over time. It is commonly assessed through measures of the rate and extent of absorption. This provides a practical bridge between a generic formulation and the already established clinical evidence for its reference product. A useful memory cue is that a generic is not required to look the same as the brand or cost the same amount; it must demonstrate comparable exposure to the active medication.'),
  ('2012-265', 'MDMA is often grouped with stimulant-type recreational substances, but serotonergic effects are also clinically important. Crowded hot environments, prolonged exertion, and inadequate hydration can magnify toxicity. An elevated CPK is a useful clue to muscle injury or rhabdomyolysis in a patient with hyperthermia, severe agitation, or prolonged exertion. On examinations, combine party exposure, mydriasis, hypertension, dehydration, and hyperthermia-risk physiology to recognize MDMA intoxication. Immediate stabilization takes precedence over trying to identify the exact recreational drug.'),
  ('2012-266', 'Behavioral approaches draw on learning theory and the idea that consequences shape behavior. A consequence need not feel pleasant to reinforce behavior; escape from anxiety can reinforce avoidance because it brings immediate relief. This negative-reinforcement pattern helps explain why phobias, compulsions, and avoidance persist. Behavioral therapy therefore asks what maintains the behavior now, rather than primarily seeking a remote cause. Identifying triggers and consequences provides concrete targets for interventions such as exposure, response prevention, or contingency management.'),
  ('2012-267', 'Wilson disease causes abnormal copper accumulation, particularly in the liver and brain, which explains its combination of hepatic, neurologic, and psychiatric manifestations. Young age, unexplained liver injury, behavioral change, and similarly affected relatives form a strong examination pattern. Ceruloplasmin is often low and is the best-listed blood clue, but its sensitivity and specificity are insufficient for stand-alone diagnosis. The independent adjudication therefore retains B as the intended answer while requiring integration with other copper studies and the broader clinical evaluation.'),
  ('2012-268', 'Catatonia is a motor-behavioral syndrome that can occur with mood disorders, psychotic disorders, and medical or neurologic illness. Signs include stupor, mutism, negativism, posturing, echophenomena, withdrawal, and purposeless agitation. It can cause serious complications through poor intake, immobility, fever, or autonomic instability. A useful memory link is that lorazepam serves both as a bedside therapeutic probe and as a first-line treatment, whereas severe or refractory catatonia warrants ECT.'),
  ('2012-269', 'Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis obstructs venous drainage, raising intracranial pressure and sometimes causing venous infarction or hemorrhage. Unlike many arterial strokes, presentation may be dominated by headache, papilledema, seizure, or altered mental status rather than a single focal deficit. The key memory link is that a normal noncontrast CT does not rule out venous sinus thrombosis. Venous imaging is needed when the clinical pattern remains concerning, particularly with marked intracranial hypertension or laboratory clues to thrombosis.'),
  ('2012-270', 'A developmental theory is easier to apply confidently when different clinicians can observe, test, and reproduce its claims. Retrospective accounts of early childhood, especially when interpreted through adult symptoms, make that difficult. This is the main conceptual critique of rigid libidinal-stage models. The issue is not that childhood development is unimportant; it is that a universal sequence inferred from adult analysis is difficult to validate consistently. Remember retrospective reconstruction as the central methodological limitation tested here.'),
  ('2012-271', 'Learned helplessness links uncontrollable aversive experiences with behavioral passivity. The model became influential because it offered an observable outcome, reduced attempts to escape, that changed after antidepressant treatment. A memory image is an animal that has learned that its actions do not alter the outcome; antidepressant exposure in the classic paradigm restores attempts to act. The model is useful for understanding research terminology, but it is not a full explanation of depression in people.'),
  ('2012-272', 'Nonverbal learning disability describes an uneven profile rather than a simple absence of language. The nonverbal difficulties may involve visual-spatial processing, motor coordination, and reading social signals such as gesture, facial expression, and prosody. The historical association with Asperger disorder is useful for older examination material, while current clinical practice evaluates autism-spectrum symptoms directly and does not treat the two constructs as interchangeable. This distinction avoids assuming that any single cognitive pattern establishes an autism-spectrum diagnosis.'),
  ('2012-273', 'Acculturation is not a one-way process in which a person simply becomes part of a new culture. People may maintain language, traditions, relationships, and values from a heritage culture while participating fully in other cultural settings. Integration is the label for this bicultural orientation. A useful contrast is that integration means both cultures, assimilation means primarily the broader culture, separation means primarily the heritage culture, and marginalization means neither cultural connection is maintained.'),
  ('2012-274', 'The modern term functional neurologic symptom disorder emphasizes that the symptom is real and functionally impairing rather than imagined. Older teaching often used conversion disorder, reflecting a psychodynamic idea that conflict could be expressed through bodily symptoms. For examinations, the durable diagnostic divider is conscious intent: conversion symptoms are not intentionally produced, whereas factitious disorder and malingering require intentional production or falsification. Positive examination signs of inconsistency or incongruence support the diagnosis; a normal workup alone does not.'),
  ('2012-275', 'Alcohol questions often test the distinction between a stable pattern and alcohol use disorder. Diagnostic concern rises with impaired control, risky use, social impairment, tolerance, withdrawal, or consequences—not simply because a person drinks. Aging can change pharmacologic sensitivity and comorbidity, so a previously tolerated pattern still merits periodic review. The durable point is that counseling should be individualized to the pattern of use, context, medications, and vulnerability rather than based solely on one feared outcome.'),
  ('2012-276', 'TCAs are commonly divided into tertiary amines and secondary amines. Tertiary amines tend to be more sedating, anticholinergic, and orthostatic; secondary amines tend to be relatively better tolerated in these domains. Alpha-1 adrenergic blockade contributes to TCA-related orthostasis. A useful memory cue is that secondary amines usually have a simpler side-effect burden, while remembering that all TCAs can be hazardous in frail older adults.'),
  ('2012-277', 'The cerebellum refines movement rather than initiating it. Its circuits compare intended movement with actual performance, producing smooth, accurately scaled actions. Dysfunction causes movements to overshoot or undershoot a target, difficulty with rapid alternating movements, and tremor that becomes most apparent near a target. The high-yield cluster of dysmetria, dysdiadochokinesia, and intention tremor points to cerebellar dysfunction. Limb findings are usually ipsilateral to the involved cerebellar hemisphere.'),
  ('2012-278', 'Professional boundaries protect a relationship in which one person seeks care and the other holds specialized authority. Boundary crossings are contextual departures from ordinary practice, whereas violations misuse that authority. A helpful memory distinction is that a crossing may be considered in context, while a violation serves the clinician at the patient''s expense. This framework avoids both reflexive rigidity about every nonroutine interaction and rationalization of conduct that exploits a patient.'),
  ('2012-279', 'Level-of-care decisions in addiction treatment are not determined solely by diagnosis or by the number of substances used. They also consider the recovery environment: housing stability, exposure to substances, reliable supports, and ability to follow a treatment plan. Residential programs occupy a middle ground between medically intensive inpatient care and outpatient treatment. The memory connection is simple: when the environment repeatedly undermines recovery, treatment may need to provide an environment as part of the intervention.'),
  ('2012-280', 'Gelastic means laughter. Gelastic seizures are not ordinary laughter: they are stereotyped, involuntary events often lacking genuine mirth. Hypothalamic hamartomas are congenital, non-neoplastic malformations whose location explains the memorable two-part presentation—seizures from an epileptogenic network and early puberty from hypothalamic endocrine dysregulation. The combination is far more informative than either feature alone, so it should redirect assessment toward neurologic and endocrine causes.'),
  ('2012-281', 'ECT cognitive effects include short-term confusion, anterograde learning difficulty around treatments, and variable retrograde loss for autobiographical memories. Electrode placement is a classic examination lever: bilateral placement produces greater cognitive effects than unilateral placement. This does not mean one method is always preferable; ECT technique is individualized to symptom severity, prior response, and cognitive risk. The useful memory contrast is bilateral placement equals greater memory burden.'),
  ('2012-282', 'Sexual fantasy can be a private space for exploring themes that a person may not want to enact. Developmental assessment therefore distinguishes thoughts and fantasies from behavior, coercion, and impairment. Unusual content alone is not evidence of a disorder, trauma history, or future sexual behavior. Clinically significant distress, functional impairment, consent, and actual conduct determine whether more focused assessment is needed. This distinction supports nonjudgmental care and reduces unnecessary pathologizing of normal developmental exploration.'),
  ('2012-283', 'Twelve-step facilitation is a structured treatment approach designed to help patients engage with mutual-help programs such as AA. It is distinct from simply providing supportive therapy or requiring a particular spiritual belief. Its practical focus is participation: meetings, peer connection, and recovery-oriented actions. A useful exam cue is that the therapist facilitates the patient’s entry into a Twelve-Step community, especially after an early effort or obstacle.'),
  ('2012-284', 'Dyssomnia was an older umbrella term for disorders of sleep amount, quality, or timing; current classifications use more specific sleep-wake diagnoses. Board questions from this era may use dyssomnia NOS when the problem is clinically meaningful but does not fit a named primary sleep disorder. The memorable diagnostic move is temporal: if concentration difficulty begins when sleep is halved, first explain the complaint through sleep loss before assigning a lifelong neurodevelopmental diagnosis.'),
  ('2012-285', 'A lumbar puncture traverses the dura to access cerebrospinal fluid. If the small dural opening continues to leak, CSF pressure falls. Upright posture then accentuates downward traction on intracranial pain-sensitive structures, creating the classic postural headache. This mechanism gives a reliable memory cue: low CSF pressure feels worse when upright, in contrast to many conditions associated with elevated intracranial pressure.'),
  ('2012-286', 'Williams syndrome is memorable for an uneven cognitive profile: relative verbal and social strengths contrast with pronounced visuospatial construction difficulty. The social style may look like disinhibition or indiscriminate friendliness, but it exists alongside substantial anxiety. Its chromosomal association is commonly tested as 7q11.23. Rather than memorizing isolated traits, connect the phenotype to the pattern of socially engaging but visually disorganized and anxious.'),
  ('2012-287', 'Abstraction is the ability to recognize the category behind individual objects or the principle behind a particular example. It is often contrasted with concrete thinking, in which language and similarities are interpreted narrowly or literally. A useful memory cue is that "fruit" is the abstraction, while "apple" and "orange" are instances. This makes abstraction distinct from insight, which concerns illness awareness, and judgment, which concerns practical decision-making.'),
  ('2012-288', 'Inpatient violence prevention includes patient factors, staff interactions, and the physical and social milieu. Transitional periods are especially vulnerable because responsibility, information, and routines are transferred simultaneously. This is a systems concept: risk can rise even without a change in a patient’s diagnosis or intent. A useful memory phrase is that handoffs are hazards unless teams deliberately preserve continuity of observation and communication.'),
  ('2012-289', 'Clinical ethics is not limited to solving dramatic dilemmas. Much of it is preventive design: consent discussions, boundaries, documentation, supervision, and routine attention to confidentiality. These practices create reliable safeguards before a conflict occurs. Confidentiality has important limits, but patients can participate meaningfully in care only when those limits are explained clearly. The conceptual memory link is that a safeguard is built before the hazard, like a guardrail rather than an emergency response.'),
  ('2012-290', 'Essential tremor is typically an action or postural tremor and is often treated with propranolol or primidone. Propranolol blocks beta-1 and beta-2 receptors; beta-2 blockade is the important concern in asthma because it may promote bronchoconstriction. Primidone is an antiseizure medication with established tremor benefit. The exam association is direct: essential tremor plus asthma points away from propranolol and toward primidone.'),
  ('2012-291', 'Habeas corpus is a longstanding legal protection against unlawful detention. Its central function is procedural: it asks a court to examine the authority for custody rather than deciding every clinical question related to the person’s health. Psychiatry commonly tests it alongside commitment and capacity because all involve hospitalization but answer different questions. The memory anchor is that the custodian must justify why the person is being held.'),
  ('2012-292', 'IBS is a disorder of gut-brain interaction: symptoms reflect altered communication among the intestine, nervous system, sensation, and behavior rather than a structural lesion visible on routine testing. Hypnosis in this setting is usually structured and symptom-focused, not stage hypnosis or simple relaxation. Its intuitive role is to modify attention, autonomic arousal, and responses to gastrointestinal sensations. The memory connection is gut-directed hypnosis for a gut-brain disorder.'),
  ('2012-293', 'Longitudinal research must address outcomes that are missing because participants do not complete follow-up. LOCF became a familiar convention because it produces a complete data set using a simple rule. However, simplicity is not neutrality: the method embeds an assumption of no change after the last observation. Modern analyses commonly use methods with more explicit missing-data assumptions and assess robustness. For this historical examination question, LOCF is the classic dropout-handling method.'),
  ('2012-294', 'The term “fencing posture” describes asymmetric tonic positioning in which one arm extends and the other flexes, resembling a fencer’s stance. In epilepsy, it is a motor semiologic sign rather than a diagnosis by itself. Frontal networks include supplementary motor and other motor-control regions, so seizures there can produce organized-looking but involuntary movements. A useful memory link is that frontal lobe seizures often create an abrupt motor “storm”: fast, forceful, repetitive, and commonly nocturnal. Video documentation is valuable because the outward appearance can be misleading.'),
  ('2012-295', 'Migraine pharmacotherapy is often organized into medications taken during an attack and medications taken regularly to reduce attack frequency. Triptans belong to the acute-treatment group and are intended to stop or substantially reduce an established migraine attack. Topiramate belongs to the preventive group. This distinction is clinically important because repeated use of acute medications can contribute to medication-overuse headache, whereas preventive treatment is considered when attack burden is recurrent or disabling.'),
  ('2012-296', 'The MMSE samples orientation, registration, attention, recall, language, and simple visuospatial construction in a short format. Its brevity is both a strength and a limitation: it efficiently detects many established deficits but does not probe every cognitive domain deeply. Think of it as a broad net with relatively large openings. It can identify a need for fuller evaluation, but subtle decline may pass through, especially when premorbid cognitive ability is high or the earliest deficits are executive rather than global.'),
  ('2012-297', 'Dopamine is often remembered only as a reward transmitter, but it also regulates salience, learning from prediction errors, movement, and executive function. Developmental questions commonly pair dopamine with the prefrontal cortex because this system is refined over a long developmental interval. A memory connection is “lasting prefrontal tuning”: dopamine-dependent modulation of frontal control circuits remains especially dynamic into adolescence. This is a comparative developmental concept, not a claim that other transmitter systems cease changing during childhood.'),
  ('2012-298', 'NF2 is a tumor-predisposition syndrome centered on Schwann-cell tumors, especially vestibular schwannomas affecting cranial nerve VIII. The memorable association is “two vestibular tumors”: bilateral disease distinguishes the syndrome from the more common sporadic unilateral vestibular schwannoma. Merlin, the NF2 gene product, functions as a tumor suppressor. Hearing loss and ataxia follow from vestibulocochlear involvement. The clinical syndrome is caused by a pathogenic NF2 variant, not merely an asymptomatic carrier state.'),
  ('2012-299', 'DBT combines acceptance with change-oriented behavioral strategies. Its familiar skills modules make it adaptable across settings, but adaptation can involve changing examples, pacing, and treatment targets to match a population’s needs. Emotional constriction is memorable as the geriatric add-on because it describes narrowed access to emotion, whereas standard DBT more broadly teaches emotion regulation, distress tolerance, mindfulness, and interpersonal effectiveness. The construct should be assessed collaboratively rather than presumed from age alone.'),
  ('2012-300', 'Adoption studies help distinguish biological relatedness from the rearing environment. Their findings are best interpreted as evidence of familial vulnerability rather than inevitability for any individual child. A useful formulation is that risk can be inherited or shared within families, but outcome is not fixed. In clinical practice, family history informs a broader, dynamic suicide-risk assessment that includes current symptoms, stressors, substance use, past behavior, supports, and access to lethal means.'),
  ('2013-1', 'The practical distinction between malingering and factitious disorder concerns the intended payoff. Malingering seeks an external benefit, whereas factitious disorder seeks the patient role itself, an internal psychological benefit. This distinction does not make motive easy to determine in practice because motives can be mixed and evidence incomplete. A useful mnemonic is “malingering = material or situational motive.” The clinician should describe observed behavior and context precisely rather than assuming deception from a patient’s background or presentation alone.'),
  ('2013-2', 'Dystonia is a movement disorder involving sustained or intermittent muscle contractions that produce abnormal postures or repetitive movements. Cervical dystonia is particularly suited to local treatment because the muscles responsible for the posture can often be identified clinically. Botulinum toxin weakens selected overactive muscles at the neuromuscular junction rather than broadly suppressing nervous-system function. This localized mechanism explains why it is highly effective for spasmodic torticollis.'),
  ('2013-3', 'Psychodynamic language describes the mind as shaped by internal representations of important relationships. Introjection is one route by which outside figures become psychologically “inside.” This makes the term easy to remember: “intro-” means inward. It is not inherently pathological; internalized values, ideals, and comforting images can support development, while punitive internalized voices can contribute to distress. The term identifies a psychological process, not a diagnosis or a conclusion about a person’s motives.'),
  ('2013-4', 'Young children commonly explore bodies, gender differences, and privacy rules as part of development. The clinical question is not whether any sexual behavior is present, but whether its form and context are developmentally expected. Red flags include coercion, pain, marked distress, aggression, a large developmental gap, repetitive intrusive behavior, or explicit knowledge beyond expected exposure. A useful memory frame is “curiosity versus compulsion or coercion.” Assessment must be careful, calm, and grounded in the specific context rather than assumptions.'),
  ('2013-5', 'Clozapine occupies a distinctive place in schizophrenia treatment because it benefits treatment resistance and is associated with reduced recurrent suicidal behavior. Its demanding monitoring requirements are inseparable from clinical use. A memory anchor is “clozapine has two major board associations”: treatment-resistant schizophrenia and persistent suicidality in schizophrenia-spectrum illness. These benefits do not eliminate the need to address acute safety, co-occurring depression, substance use, treatment adherence, or psychosocial stressors in a comprehensive plan.'),
  ('2013-6', 'Thiamine is essential for cerebral energy metabolism. The brain depends heavily on glucose metabolism, so deficiency can produce abrupt neuropsychiatric and neurologic dysfunction. Wernicke encephalopathy is classically associated with alcohol use disorder, but poor intake, vomiting, malabsorption, and increased nutritional demand can also cause it. The memorable triad is “eyes, gait, and mind”: ocular abnormalities, ataxia, and confusion. Bariatric surgery is an important nonalcohol-related setting in which this syndrome must be recognized quickly.'),
  ('2013-7', 'Cultural formulation examines how a person explains distress, what help is acceptable, and which social supports matter. Karma is often simplified in exam questions as consequences of past actions, but lived Buddhist traditions are diverse and may also emphasize compassion, mindfulness, and relief of suffering. The useful learning point is recognizing the explanatory model while avoiding the error of treating any religion as a single fixed belief system. Individual assessment is more clinically useful than assuming beliefs from ethnicity or immigration status.'),
  ('2013-8', 'Sensate focus is a behavioral sex-therapy approach built around gradual, structured touching exercises. Its central idea is that self-monitoring and pressure can disrupt sexual response. By temporarily removing a performance endpoint, couples can attend to sensory experience and communication. The name itself is a memory aid: “sensate” means sensation-focused, not intercourse-focused. Later steps can be tailored to the presenting sexual concern, relationship context, and the couple’s preferences, always with voluntary participation.'),
  ('2013-9', 'Biofeedback turns an ordinarily unnoticed body signal into immediate feedback, such as a tone, meter, or visual display. That feedback permits operant learning because actions followed by improvement in the signal are reinforced. Skeletal muscle activity is especially suited to this approach because it is readily measured with surface electromyography and can be voluntarily modulated. Think “EMG equals muscle tension.” The intervention teaches a learned self-regulation skill and is often combined with other behavioral or rehabilitative treatments.'),
  ('2013-10', 'Vigilance means maintaining attention over time, particularly during repetitive or low-stimulation tasks. CPTs operationalize this by presenting a stream of stimuli and requiring a response rule to be sustained. The memory link is direct: “continuous” performance requires continuous monitoring. This differs from memory tests, global cognitive screens, and intelligence batteries, which may include attention-related components but have different primary purposes. Results must be interpreted in light of the patient’s symptoms, functioning, and test conditions.'),
  ('2013-11', 'Dextromethorphan is marketed as a cough suppressant, but supratherapeutic doses can cause dissociative and perceptual effects. Its availability in nonprescription products makes a specific exposure history important, particularly for adolescents. Many cough preparations contain additional ingredients, so toxicity may not be attributable to dextromethorphan alone. A useful contrast is that this intoxication can appear activated and perceptually altered, whereas the listed withdrawal syndromes have different typical symptom patterns.'),
  ('2013-12', 'Alcohol and benzodiazepines both enhance inhibitory signaling in the central nervous system through related actions at GABA-A receptor systems. Their combined effects can be more impairing than either drug alone. A useful exam association is “benzos plus alcohol”: think disinhibition, amnesia, falls, impaired driving, and respiratory risk. Behavioral effects can be misleading because a person may later have limited recall. Acute assessment must prioritize physiologic safety as well as psychiatric risk.'),
  ('2013-13', 'Psychosomatic cardiovascular research once focused heavily on broad personality types, particularly Type A behavior. That broad construct proved less consistently useful than examining its individual components. Hostility is the memorable specific trait because recurrent anger and cynical mistrust may be accompanied by repeated autonomic stress responses and adverse health behaviors. For this examination-style distinction, remember that Type A is the broad historical category, whereas hostility is the specific trait most closely associated with higher blood pressure.'),
  ('2013-14', 'The traditional ethical framework includes beneficence, nonmaleficence, autonomy, and justice. Nonmaleficence is commonly summarized as avoiding harm, although clinical ethics requires more than simply refusing difficult care. It includes recognizing outdated or insufficient skills, evaluating immediate safety, and facilitating a safer transfer when possible. In a resource-limited setting, competing obligations may be difficult, but patient vulnerability does not remove the need for competent care.'),
  ('2013-15', 'Executive functioning refers to goal-directed control processes, including inhibition, working memory, cognitive flexibility, and planning. Development is gradual rather than all-or-none. Preschool is especially important because daily routines begin to require rule-following, delay of action, transitions, and management of frustration. These new demands make differences in self-regulation easier for adults to observe. A child may have early precursors of executive control in toddlerhood, but preschool is the examination answer for marked individual differences.'),
  ('2013-16', 'Subdural hematomas commonly result from tearing of bridging veins, especially when cerebral atrophy stretches those veins in older adults and people with chronic alcohol use. Blood beneath the dura can spread widely over a hemisphere, producing a crescent shape on imaging. CT density changes over time: acute blood is usually hyperdense, whereas subacute collections may become isodense. The delayed course and characteristic shape distinguish subdural hematoma from the more lens-shaped epidural hematoma.'),
  ('2013-17', 'The MMPI separates substantive clinical content from indicators of response style. This makes the term validity potentially confusing: in an MMPI report, it often refers to whether a particular person''s protocol is interpretable rather than to the research concept of test validity. The practical question is how the examinee answered the items. Response consistency, omissions, favorable self-presentation, and unusual symptom endorsement can all affect confidence in the resulting clinical profile.'),
  ('2013-18', 'Psychophysiological insomnia is often understood through conditioning. Repeatedly lying awake while worrying can turn the bedroom from a cue for sleep into a cue for alertness, frustration, and monitoring. The disorder is memorable because trying harder to sleep can worsen it: effort and vigilance oppose the relaxed state that facilitates sleep. The clinical pattern differs from poor sleep habits alone because the worry about insomnia itself becomes a central maintaining factor.'),
  ('2013-19', 'Stimulus control is based on behavioral learning principles: a setting repeatedly paired with a state becomes a cue for that state. In chronic conditioned insomnia, the bedroom can become a cue for vigilance, frustration, and sleep-related monitoring. Treatment reverses this pairing by limiting bed exposure during alert wakefulness and linking it more consistently with sleepiness and sleep. The intervention addresses the mechanism maintaining psychophysiological insomnia rather than simply making bedtime more comfortable.'),
  ('2013-20', 'Physiologic tremor is present at a subtle level in healthy people but can become clinically visible when adrenergic tone rises. Medication questions often test whether a drug tends to activate or dampen this system. A useful pairing is that corticosteroids can make physiologic tremor more noticeable, while beta-adrenergic blockade is a classic tremor-reducing strategy. The pattern is generally fine and bilateral, but diagnosis still requires attention to onset, associated neurologic signs, substances, and medical illness.'),
  ('2013-21', 'Perception begins with sensory input and ends with the brain''s interpretation of that input. An illusion contains both elements: a real stimulus is present, but its meaning is mistaken. A concise memory rule is "illusion equals input present; hallucination equals input absent." This distinction is especially useful in delirium, sensory impairment, and noisy or low-stimulation environments, where ambiguous sounds and visual stimuli can be misread.'),
  ('2013-22', 'Earlier educational descriptions of intellectual disability often linked severity to expected academic attainment. Contemporary practice emphasizes adaptive functioning because daily functioning and support needs are more clinically meaningful than an IQ score or grade-equivalent result alone. For older examination questions, academic skills through approximately the sixth-grade level are a useful cue for the mild category. This historical shorthand should not replace a full modern diagnostic assessment of intellectual and adaptive functioning.'),
  ('2013-23', 'Drug passage into breast milk depends on maternal plasma concentration, protein binding, lipid solubility, ionization, timing of feeds, and active metabolites. A measured milk concentration is not the same as infant dose or clinical effect. Fluoxetine is memorable because its active metabolite, norfluoxetine, persists for a long time, making time-course questions important. The item tests the historical depth of pharmacokinetic description, not an unconditional recommendation for lactating patients.'),
  ('2013-24', 'Supportive and insight-oriented interventions exist on a continuum. Supportive work stabilizes, validates, and strengthens coping, whereas expressive work helps a patient examine meanings, conflicts, and recurring patterns. Clarification is a relatively gentle exploratory technique: it makes what is already present clearer before the therapist attempts a deeper interpretation. It differs from advice or praise because it focuses on the patient''s understanding rather than directing behavior or offering reassurance.'),
  ('2013-25', 'Radiculopathy is dysfunction of a spinal nerve root. Localization combines dermatomes, which describe sensory territories, with myotomes, which describe muscle groups, and reflex arcs. C6 is commonly remembered through thumb sensory symptoms and the biceps reflex. Real clinical patterns can overlap, so a complete examination is more reliable than any isolated complaint. The vignette combines sensory, motor, and reflex findings to make the C6 localization especially strong.'),
  ('2013-26', 'Capacity is a clinical determination, whereas legal competence is generally determined by a court. The sliding-scale concept recognizes that accepting a low-risk intervention and refusing a high-benefit, high-risk intervention may require different depths of evaluation. It is not a license for paternalism: the same functional abilities remain central, and disagreement with clinicians or reliance on religious values does not itself establish incapacity. The goal is a fair assessment of this patient''s ability to decide about this treatment.'),
  ('2013-27', 'Psychological measures differ in breadth and intended purpose. A useful memory approach is to match each instrument''s name to its function: a symptom checklist surveys symptoms broadly, a state-trait inventory focuses on anxiety, and an achievement test assesses school skills. Broad inventories are descriptive tools that organize clinical inquiry. They do not provide a stand-alone psychiatric diagnosis or replace a careful interview, collateral history, and direct safety assessment.'),
  ('2013-28', 'Temperament refers to early-emerging, relatively stable patterns of emotional and behavioral reactivity. Behavioral inhibition resembles a slow-to-warm response to novelty: the child is watchful, cautious, and reluctant rather than simply oppositional. The memory connection is direct: early avoidance of unfamiliar people and situations can become a pathway toward later anxiety, particularly when avoidance is repeatedly reinforced or coping opportunities are limited. It remains a vulnerability marker, not an inevitable psychiatric outcome.'),
  ('2013-29', 'Dissociation involves disruption in the usual integration of memory, identity, perception, and behavior. Trauma-related disorders and dissociative disorders overlap in experiences of altered memory, detachment, and heightened threat responses, but they remain distinct diagnoses with separate criteria. For examination recall, dissociative identity disorder belongs to the trauma-associated spectrum, and posttraumatic stress disorder is the most characteristic comorbid condition among the options provided.'),
  ('2013-30', 'The middle cerebral artery supplies much of the lateral cerebral hemisphere, including cortical regions governing face and arm function, language, and spatial attention. The anterior cerebral artery primarily supplies medial frontal and parietal surfaces, while the posterior cerebral artery supplies occipital and inferomedial temporal regions. These territorial patterns provide useful localization anchors but are not absolute because vascular anatomy, collateral circulation, and infarct extent vary. For this corrected category C record, direct inspection of the referenced CT supported retention of the MCA answer.'),
  ('2013-31', 'Vascular dementia is cognitive impairment caused by clinically significant cerebrovascular injury, including strategic infarcts, multiple infarcts, or diffuse small-vessel disease. A practical memory cue is “cognition with localization”: aphasia, hemiparesis, visual-field deficits, asymmetric reflexes, or gait-predominant subcortical signs can point toward vascular brain injury. Cognitive symptoms and neurologic signs need not begin simultaneously, because accumulating vascular lesions may lower cognitive reserve over time.'),
  ('2013-32', 'Trichotillomania is a body-focused repetitive behavior that may occur automatically, with little immediate awareness, or deliberately in response to an urge, tension, or uncomfortable sensation. Behavioral treatment targets this cycle by improving awareness and introducing an action incompatible with pulling. This category B item preserves fluoxetine as the best-listed historical medication answer, but that convention does not supersede contemporary evidence favoring habit-reversal-based therapy. Medication decisions should instead address individual symptoms, comorbidities, prior response, and tolerability.'),
  ('2013-33', 'The WCST is memorable because the sorting principle changes while the examinee learns only from right-or-wrong feedback. Its central contrast is memory storage versus rule shifting: remembering information is not the same as abandoning a once-successful strategy when circumstances change. This makes it a classic executive-function task and a useful teaching example of perseveration. The test evaluates a cognitive process rather than establishing a diagnosis by itself.'),
  ('2013-34', 'Biofeedback turns a normally unnoticed physiologic process into visible or audible feedback, allowing practice in altering it. In insomnia, the useful concept is arousal learning: worry about sleep can become a cue for alertness, tension, and monitoring. Relaxation and feedback-based methods aim to weaken that learned association. These are skills-based treatments, so benefit depends on repeated practice rather than a one-time procedure.'),
  ('2013-35', 'Cognitive-behavioral therapy distinguishes the form of a thought from whether the situation is genuinely difficult. Catastrophizing means expanding a manageable event until it becomes irreversible ruin. The term is memorable because the thought process creates a catastrophe in successive steps. Identifying the chain helps a patient test each link rather than arguing vaguely about whether anxiety is rational. It differs from rigid either-or appraisals that do not include a predicted cascade.'),
  ('2013-36', 'PMDD is a timing diagnosis as much as a symptom diagnosis. A useful memory anchor is “premenstrual rise, postmenstrual reset.” Symptoms cluster before menstrual flow and diminish after it starts, with the following week serving as the clearest low-symptom comparison period. Prospective charting matters because retrospective impressions may overestimate how exclusively symptoms are tied to the premenstrual phase. The pattern must cause clinically significant distress or impairment.'),
  ('2013-37', 'The name polymyalgia rheumatica highlights pain in muscle regions, but the clinical problem is inflammatory stiffness rather than true muscle weakness. A helpful contrast with polymyositis is pain and stiffness with normal CK versus objective weakness with muscle-enzyme elevation. ESR and C-reactive protein are often raised, although normal inflammatory markers do not entirely exclude the disorder. Its association with giant cell arteritis makes targeted symptom review especially important.'),
  ('2013-38', 'Erikson used generativity to describe a developmental orientation toward creating, nurturing, and guiding what comes next. A compact memory cue is “leave something living or useful behind.” Children are one route, but so are protégés, institutions, creative work, and community projects. The opposing risk, stagnation, is a sense of being psychologically stalled or concerned only with the self. The concept describes a possible source of meaning, not a requirement for healthy adulthood.'),
  ('2013-39', 'Prevalence asks how much exists, whereas incidence asks how much newly developed. Point prevalence is a snapshot: the numerator and denominator are defined at one time or a narrow assessment period. Screening tools are suitable for estimating the frequency of elevated symptoms in a population, but their results depend on the chosen cutoff and do not automatically equal diagnostic prevalence. The study design determines which measure can be derived.'),
  ('2013-40', 'Calcineurin inhibitors are essential post-transplant drugs with narrow therapeutic margins, so changes in hepatic metabolism can have important consequences. An antidepressant tolerated before renal failure or transplantation must be reconsidered within the new regimen. Prior efficacy remains valuable because it reduces uncertainty about antidepressant benefit, but interaction potential and organ function determine how treatment is implemented. This category C record corrects an earlier C/E multi-key to fluoxetine alone while preserving the requirement for coordinated transplant-pharmacology review and monitoring.'),
  ('2013-41', 'Childhood-onset schizophrenia refers to schizophrenia beginning before adolescence and is rare. Its teaching importance lies in a diagnostic trap: poor attention and falling grades can be early nonspecific concerns, but thought disorganization, psychotic experiences, social withdrawal, and loss of function change the diagnostic frame. The diagnosis requires careful exclusion of developmental, medical, neurologic, trauma-related, and substance-related explanations. Collateral history is especially important in children.'),
  ('2013-42', 'Ramelteon targets melatonin receptors involved in circadian sleep signaling rather than broadly enhancing sedative neurotransmission. A useful memory cue is “melatonin signal, not a sedative hammer.” This mechanism explains its appeal when confusion, falls, or residual sedation are concerns. It is most aligned with difficulty falling asleep and does not replace evaluation of medical, environmental, circadian, or behavioral causes of insomnia.'),
  ('2013-43', 'Interpersonal psychotherapy treats psychiatric symptoms in interpersonal context without claiming relationships are the sole cause of illness. Its phase structure offers a simple sequence: define the link, work the link, consolidate the work. The middle phase is where the patient applies the formulation to current interactions and practices communication changes, role adaptation, or grief processing. A focal problem area guides this work and prevents treatment from becoming generic supportive therapy.'),
  ('2013-44', 'Stimulant-use-disorder pharmacotherapy has historically been difficult because a broadly effective approved medication counterpart to opioid agonist treatment has not emerged. Bupropion is memorable here because its dopamine and norepinephrine activity makes it a recurring research candidate. “Promise” in this older exam stem means investigational or potentially helpful, not established efficacy or a replacement for behavioral treatment. Current care should emphasize evidence-based psychosocial interventions.'),
  ('2013-45', 'HSV-1 is commonly remembered through its predilection for temporal-limbic brain regions, which helps explain presentations with memory disturbance, aphasia, seizures, or striking behavioral change. This is an important psychiatry crossover diagnosis because severe acute psychiatric symptoms may arise from brain inflammation. The exam association is sporadic encephalitis to HSV-1, with urgency driven by the availability of antiviral treatment. Presentation varies, so absence of one classic feature does not exclude it.'),
  ('2013-46', 'Irreversible MAOIs reduce monoamine breakdown and can interact dangerously with serotonergic or sympathomimetic medications. Serotonin toxicity features mental-status change, autonomic hyperactivity, and neuromuscular excitation, although severe MAOI reactions may overlap clinically with other toxidromes. This category B item retains oxycodone only as the best-listed opioid answer. Meperidine and tramadol are more classic high-risk examples, and clinical prescribing should use medication-specific interaction review rather than extending the historical key into a universal opioid rule.'),
  ('2013-47', 'Psychosis is a syndrome shared by primary psychotic, mood, substance-related, neurologic, and medical disorders. Brief psychotic disorder has abrupt onset, lasts at least one day but less than one month, and is followed by full return to prior functioning. Schizophrenia requires a much more persistent illness course. Acute confusion or poor recent recall may fit the item’s intended contrast, but these findings also require careful delirium assessment because impaired attention and fluctuating awareness are not core diagnostic requirements of brief psychotic disorder.'),
  ('2013-48', 'Tremor phenomenology helps localize neurologic disorders. Essential tremor is an action tremor, meaning it appears during voluntary movement or sustained posture rather than primarily at complete rest. A family history is common and can be a useful clue, although its absence does not exclude the diagnosis. Parkinson disease more often produces a resting tremor with bradykinesia or rigidity, while cerebellar disease produces an intention tremor that worsens as the limb approaches its target.'),
  ('2013-49', 'NF2 is an inherited tumor-predisposition disorder caused by loss of a tumor-suppressor function. Its best memory anchor is “two ears”: bilateral vestibular schwannomas affect the eighth cranial nerves and are highly characteristic. Meningiomas, other schwannomas, ependymomas, and juvenile cataracts can occur as additional manifestations. This pattern differs from neurofibromatosis type 1, which is more associated with café-au-lait macules, cutaneous neurofibromas, and optic pathway gliomas.'),
  ('2013-50', 'Adolescent major depressive disorder may present with irritability, physical complaints, withdrawal from friends, loss of interest, appetite or sleep changes, and suicidal thoughts rather than only reported sadness. Regulatory approval is narrower than the broader range of treatments that may be clinically useful. For this item, escitalopram is the approved antidepressant option for adolescents. Suicidal comments require a safety evaluation regardless of whether medication, psychotherapy, family intervention, or a higher level of care is ultimately selected.')
insert into question_context (question_id, context) values
  ('2013-51', 'Contingency management is based on operant conditioning, in which consequences influence the future likelihood of behavior. In addiction treatment, rewards can include vouchers, prizes, clinic privileges, or other valued outcomes. The reinforcement must be specifically tied to an observable target behavior, such as attendance or a negative toxicology result. It is distinct from general encouragement because the consequence is planned, concrete, and contingent on the behavior. Take-home medication privileges can serve this function when permitted by program policy.'),
  ('2013-52', 'Informed consent includes capacity, adequate disclosure, understanding, and voluntariness. Psychiatric care can involve unavoidable pressure during acute illness, but transparent explanation of legal realities differs from manipulating a patient into agreement. A clinician may recommend admission strongly and may initiate an involuntary evaluation when statutory criteria are met. However, the patient should not be induced to accept a nominally voluntary admission through an improper threat about commitment or length of stay. “Voluntary” describes the quality of the patient''s decision, whereas involuntary treatment needs its own legal and clinical justification.'),
  ('2013-53', 'Attachment theory emphasizes using trusted caregivers as a secure base for exploration and a source of comfort under stress. An infant can form meaningful attachment relationships with parents, grandparents, siblings, or other regular caregivers. Multiple attachments do not imply identical relationships: a child may seek different caregivers for play, routines, soothing, or unfamiliar situations. The quality and consistency of care matter, not simply the caregiver''s gender or amount of time spent together. Think of attachment as a network of reliable relationships, not a one-person role another caregiver must replace.'),
  ('2013-54', 'Alzheimer disease commonly begins with difficulty learning or retrieving recent information and gradually affects independent functioning. Overlearned social behaviors may remain relatively preserved longer than complex new-learning tasks. This can produce a social façade in which casual acquaintances do not appreciate the extent of impairment. Collateral history is therefore essential: family members may notice medication errors, financial problems, repeated questions, or missed routines that are not apparent during a brief conversation. The progression over time helps distinguish a neurocognitive disorder from transient inattention or delirium.'),
  ('2013-55', 'The medial longitudinal fasciculus connects brainstem eye-movement nuclei, allowing both eyes to move together horizontally. A lesion produces internuclear ophthalmoplegia, with impaired adduction of one eye and nystagmus of the opposite abducting eye. Multiple sclerosis is especially memorable because demyelination can involve several central nervous system locations, accounting for ocular-motor and cerebellar findings in a younger patient. In older adults, a vascular brainstem lesion is a more common alternative cause of internuclear ophthalmoplegia.'),
  ('2013-56', 'Alexithymia can be remembered as having few words for inner emotional experience. It describes a gap between emotional arousal and the ability to identify or communicate what that arousal means. Patients may focus on somatic sensations, practical details, or external events instead of named feelings. This differs from flat affect, an observed reduction in emotional expression, and from anhedonia, reduced capacity for pleasure. A patient can have alexithymia while still experiencing intense emotion.'),
  ('2013-57', 'Crossover trials use within-person comparisons to control for stable individual differences. Each participant receives more than one intervention, usually in a randomized order, so variability between participants is reduced. Their key vulnerability follows from that strength: the earlier intervention may persist after the period ends and influence outcomes during the next period. A washout interval is intended to limit this contamination, but it cannot reliably correct effects that are durable or irreversible. The memory cue is simple: a crossover needs a clean crossing; residual treatment effects muddy it.'),
  ('2013-58', 'Pharmacokinetic interactions often arise when one medication changes hepatic enzyme activity and therefore changes another medication’s clearance. Oxcarbazepine is grouped with antiseizure medications that can lower hormonal contraceptive exposure through enzyme induction. A useful memory cue is “induction lowers levels.” This is clinically relevant regardless of the neurologic diagnosis being treated. The interaction concerns hormonal contraceptive reliability, so medication counseling should include reproductive planning and coordination among the relevant prescribers.'),
  ('2013-59', 'Neurotransmitter transporters normally remove released neurotransmitter from the synaptic space. Atomoxetine inhibits the norepinephrine transporter, allowing norepinephrine to remain available longer for signaling. This offers a simple contrast with selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, which target serotonin transport, and with stimulant medications, which have broader catecholaminergic effects. Atomoxetine is commonly remembered as the prototypic nonstimulant medication for attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder that is linked to norepinephrine reuptake inhibition.'),
  ('2013-60', 'The brain receives substantial blood flow and is a frequent site of hematogenous metastasis. Lung cancers are common sources of brain metastases and may be discovered during investigation of neurologic symptoms. Board questions often distinguish the most common primary malignant brain tumor, glioblastoma, from the most common brain tumor overall, metastatic disease. Multiple lesions favor metastases, but a solitary lesion does not exclude them. Age, smoking history, systemic symptoms, and lesion pattern all help guide evaluation.'),
  ('2013-61', 'Methadone maintenance reduces withdrawal, craving, and the unstable cycle of illicit opioid use. Pregnancy does not remove the importance of maternal recovery stability; it adds obstetric and newborn-care coordination. A useful memory principle is that relapse prevention is also fetal care, because predictable treatment and prenatal engagement are generally safer than forced withdrawal. Neonatal opioid withdrawal is an expected, treatable pediatric issue that should be planned for rather than used as a rationale for discontinuing effective maternal treatment.'),
  ('2013-62', 'Thyroid hormone has major effects on the developing brain during periods of neuronal organization and myelination. Congenital hypothyroidism may be subtle or clinically unapparent at birth, which is why newborn screening is important. Without timely recognition and treatment, deficiency during a critical developmental window can cause persistent cognitive impairment. A helpful memory cue is that thyroid hormone supports developmental brain timing: its absence early in life has consequences that later replacement may not fully reverse.'),
  ('2013-63', 'Correctional mental-health law is often taught through the principle that deliberate indifference to serious medical needs is constitutionally impermissible. Applying that principle depends on the patient’s actual clinical need and the correctional setting; it does not mean every beneficial program has identical legal status. Substance-use disorders frequently coexist with psychiatric illness and are central to withdrawal management, safety, and reentry planning. These clinical realities explain why many correctional programs include addiction services even when the service is not characterized as a separate universal entitlement.'),
  ('2013-64', 'Dopamine participates in movement, motivation, reward learning, and salience. Parkinson treatment therefore illustrates an important clinical principle: restoring dopamine signaling for motor symptoms can, in some patients, promote excessive pursuit of rewarding activities. This pattern is termed a dopamine agonist-associated impulse-control disorder. A useful memory connection is that dopamine supports motor “go” signals and can also amplify reward-driven behavioral “go” signals. The association is especially important when a previously reliable patient develops unexplained gambling or other compulsive behavior.'),
  ('2013-65', 'Self-disclosure is not inherently beneficial or harmful; it is a clinical intervention whose value depends on purpose and effect. Therapeutic traditions differ in how often they use it, but all require attention to the therapeutic frame and the patient’s needs. A useful memory cue is “patient benefit, not therapist relief.” The more a disclosure gratifies the clinician’s needs, changes roles, or makes the patient responsible for the clinician, the less likely it is to be appropriate. Disclosure should remain limited and relevant to treatment.'),
  ('2013-66', 'Neurofibrillary tangles are intracellular aggregates of abnormal tau and are classically associated with Alzheimer disease, but they are not exclusive to it. Tauopathy is a useful organizing term for illnesses in which tau aggregation is central, including PSP. Neurodegenerative disorders may also have mixed protein pathology, especially in older adults. This explains why an examination question can test an association with a lesion without implying that the lesion is the defining pathology of every listed disorder. Distinguishing primary from accompanying pathology prevents overgeneralization.'),
  ('2013-67', 'Schizophrenia is defined by a longitudinal syndrome of psychotic symptoms, negative symptoms, disorganization, and impaired functioning. Positive symptoms add experiences or behaviors, such as delusions and hallucinations. Negative symptoms reflect reductions in ordinary function, such as diminished social engagement, motivation, or expression. A useful diagnostic connection is that schizophrenia is not diagnosed from a single unusual behavior alone: duration, functional change, symptom pattern, and exclusion of important alternative causes all matter. Briefer psychotic syndromes have shorter time courses.'),
  ('2013-68', 'Serotonin syndrome reflects excessive serotonergic activity, often after a drug addition, interaction, overdose, or dose increase. Its onset is typically rapid compared with many other medication-induced syndromes. The classic pattern includes altered mental status, autonomic excitation, and neuromuscular hyperactivity. Clonus is especially useful diagnostically: spontaneous or inducible clonus in an agitated, febrile patient with serotonergic exposure strongly supports serotonin toxicity. Gastrointestinal hyperactivity, including diarrhea, is also a helpful distinguishing feature.'),
  ('2013-69', 'Melatonin is a signal of biological night rather than a conventional sedative. Its clinical effect depends greatly on timing: when used strategically, it can shift the circadian clock. Delayed sleep-wake phase disorder can be remembered as “the clock is late, not broken.” This distinction explains why a person may be unable to sleep at a socially desired bedtime yet sleep soundly at a later biologically preferred time. The history of normal sleep quality on a delayed schedule is diagnostically important.'),
  ('2013-70', 'Childhood anxiety disorders commonly overlap because avoidance, threat sensitivity, reassurance seeking, and somatic distress occur across diagnoses. The key differentiator is the focus of fear: separation anxiety centers on attachment-related separation, whereas generalized anxiety involves many everyday areas. A useful examination strategy is to identify the organizing worry, then determine whether anxiety remains limited to that theme or extends across life domains. This distinction guides both diagnosis and the questions asked of caregivers, teachers, and the child.'),
  ('2013-71', 'Clozapine is a classic example of an environmental factor changing drug metabolism. Polycyclic compounds in tobacco smoke induce CYP1A2, while nicotine replacement alone does not reproduce this effect. More smoking generally lowers clozapine exposure; smoking cessation can raise exposure and increase adverse-effect risk. Hospitalization may interrupt smoking, whereas discharge may allow a patient to resume it. Therefore, an apparent loss of response after discharge should prompt a careful history of smoking changes, including the amount and type of smoke exposure, alongside assessment of adherence and other interacting factors.'),
  ('2013-72', 'Polypharmacy makes pharmacokinetics especially important in geriatric psychiatry. Older adults may have multiple prescribers, medical comorbidities, and altered clearance, so antidepressant choice is shaped by the entire medication regimen. Bupropion primarily enhances norepinephrine and dopamine signaling rather than inhibiting serotonin reuptake. The key nuance is comparative: fewer interactions than many SSRIs does not mean bupropion has no interaction potential. A complete medication review remains necessary before prescribing or changing its dose.'),
  ('2013-73', 'Elder mistreatment includes physical, emotional, sexual, and financial abuse as well as neglect. Risk is relational: the older adult’s vulnerability matters, but caregiver characteristics and dependence patterns often determine whether harm occurs. Alcohol misuse can impair judgment, increase disinhibition, worsen conflict, and coexist with stress or violence. A practical clinical rule is to assess both sides of the caregiving relationship while maintaining a nonjudgmental, safety-focused approach. Private interviewing can reveal concerns that are not voiced when a caregiver is present.'),
  ('2013-74', 'The basal ganglia regulate selection and scaling of movement. Dystonia is thought to reflect abnormal network activity involving basal ganglia, cortex, cerebellum, and sensorimotor integration. DBS modulates circuit activity without creating a destructive lesion, allowing programming to be adjusted over time. A useful target map is GPi for dystonia, subthalamic nucleus for many Parkinson disease cases, and ventral intermediate thalamus for tremor. Associating each target with its typical movement phenotype helps distinguish otherwise similar answer choices.'),
  ('2013-75', 'ECT is performed under short-acting general anesthesia with neuromuscular blockade and assisted ventilation. Much of its medical risk therefore arises from anesthesia and the transient autonomic changes around the induced seizure rather than direct brain injury. Pulmonary reserve matters because impaired airflow and gas exchange can make ventilation and recovery more difficult. Severe respiratory disease is consequently a high-yield pre-ECT concern. Risk assessment should be individualized according to current stability, severity of pulmonary disease, and the urgency of the psychiatric indication.'),
  ('2013-76', 'Separation anxiety is developmentally normal in early childhood, but becomes a disorder when fear is excessive for developmental stage, persistent, and impairing. School refusal is a behavior rather than a diagnosis; it can result from several psychiatric, social, developmental, or medical problems. The useful diagnostic question is what the child is trying to avoid. When the feared consequence is separation from attachment figures or harm befalling them, separation anxiety is the organizing diagnosis. Somatic symptoms commonly accompany this anxiety.'),
  ('2013-77', 'Methadone is a full mu-opioid agonist, whereas buprenorphine is a high-affinity partial agonist. Partial agonism means receptor activation reaches a plateau, producing a ceiling effect for some opioid effects, particularly respiratory depression. Its high receptor affinity also explains why starting buprenorphine too soon after certain opioids can precipitate withdrawal. The memorable contrast is full agonist versus partial agonist, not safe versus unsafe. Both medications require individualized treatment planning and attention to overdose risk.'),
  ('2013-78', 'Symbolic play bridges direct action and mental representation. In Piagetian terms, it reflects transition from the sensorimotor period toward preoperational thought. The practical memory image is a toddler holding a block to the ear and speaking into it like a phone. The object has not changed physically, but the child uses it to represent something absent. This cognitive step supports language, imaginative play, and the ability to act on mental representations rather than only immediately available objects.'),
  ('2013-79', 'OSA results from recurrent partial or complete upper-airway obstruction during sleep. The cycle of obstruction, oxygen disturbance, arousal, and resumed breathing prevents sustained restorative sleep even when time in bed seems adequate. Patients may not notice their own arousals, so collateral reports of snoring, gasping, or witnessed apneas are useful. Morning headache and excessive daytime sleepiness are common clues, although neither is specific by itself. Nocturnal sweating may accompany repeated respiratory events and sleep disruption. A clinical sleep history helps distinguish OSA from other causes of fatigue or sleepiness.'),
  ('2013-80', 'Bulimia nervosa is maintained by a cycle of binge eating, compensatory behavior, and often rigid dietary restraint. CBT targets this cycle directly by regularizing eating patterns and addressing beliefs and triggers that perpetuate binge-purge behavior. Fluoxetine is the high-yield medication association. A paired memory rule is useful for examinations: fluoxetine can help bulimia nervosa, whereas bupropion is avoided because it can lower the seizure threshold. Medical assessment remains important because purging may cause clinically significant electrolyte and cardiac complications.'),
  ('2013-81', 'Psychotherapy boundaries protect the asymmetry of the clinical relationship and help make treatment predictable and safe. They are not simply a list of prohibited gestures. A crossing goes over an ordinary line but can still be examined and justified in the patient’s interest; a violation breaches the line in a way that exploits, harms, or obscures therapeutic purpose. The same behavior can have different meanings across patients and treatments, which is why reflection, discussion, and supervision are central.'),
  ('2013-82', 'Myelopathy means dysfunction of the spinal cord. Cervical cord disease produces upper motor neuron findings below the level of injury because descending motor pathways are disrupted. The combination of stiff, weak legs, exaggerated reflexes, clonus, and an upgoing plantar response is more localizing than weakness alone. A brain disorder may explain gait difficulty, but a painful neck plus pyramidal findings directs the examiner toward the cervical spine and a possible compressive process.'),
  ('2013-83', 'Alcohol use disorder treatment combines psychosocial care with medications that address different relapse pathways. Naltrexone blocks opioid-mediated reinforcement, making it memorable as a medication that reduces alcohol''s reward and can reduce craving and heavy drinking. It is not appropriate during current opioid use because it can precipitate opioid withdrawal and block opioid analgesia. Disulfiram instead relies on an aversive reaction after alcohol exposure, while benzodiazepines have a role in withdrawal management rather than routine anti-craving treatment. Medication selection remains individualized to contraindications, treatment goals, and patient preference.'),
  ('2013-84', 'Lithium is handled by the kidney and has a narrow margin between therapeutic and toxic exposure. Sodium balance matters because renal sodium conservation tends to increase lithium reabsorption. Thiazides are memorable because they promote sodium loss and can ultimately increase proximal sodium and lithium retention. The practical association is simple: lithium plus a thiazide requires especially careful medication reconciliation, dose planning, and monitoring for toxicity.'),
  ('2013-85', 'Erotomania refers to a delusional conviction of being loved, rather than ordinary infatuation or wishful thinking. It is commonly remembered as a relationship-themed example of delusional disorder, alongside persecutory, grandiose, jealous, somatic, and mixed themes. Diagnostic reasoning hinges on whether the delusion is relatively circumscribed or embedded in a more global disturbance of thought, mood, consciousness, or functioning. The broader syndrome, not the romantic content alone, determines the diagnosis.'),
  ('2013-86', 'Continuation treatment prevents relapse of the same episode, whereas maintenance treatment aims to reduce future episodes in recurrent illness. A useful board-style heuristic is that more prior episodes generally strengthen the rationale for longer preventive pharmacotherapy. Sexual adverse effects are common with serotonergic antidepressants and deserve direct inquiry, but their presence does not automatically outweigh a medication’s preventive benefit when they can be managed. Treatment decisions should still incorporate patient preferences and the burden of adverse effects.'),
  ('2013-87', 'GABA is the principal inhibitory neurotransmitter in the central nervous system. GABA-A receptors are ligand-gated chloride channels, so increasing their activity generally enhances fast inhibitory signaling. Topiramate is memorable as a multimodal antiseizure medication: sodium channels, excitatory glutamate receptors, and GABA-A signaling all appear in its standard mechanism summary. That broad profile helps explain why its effects and adverse effects can extend beyond seizure frequency alone.'),
  ('2013-88', 'Classic sleep-stage nomenclature divided non-REM slow-wave sleep into stages 3 and 4 according to the amount of high-amplitude delta activity. Contemporary scoring groups them as N3. The conceptual memory link is that deepest delta sleep has the strongest homeostatic recovery association. Board questions written with the older labels often use stage 4 as shorthand for the deepest slow-wave sleep, not as a separate modern stage with a distinct function.'),
  ('2013-89', 'Statistical power is the probability of detecting a true effect of the prespecified size under the planned design. Low power raises the risk of a type II error, meaning failure to detect a real effect. A prospective power analysis makes assumptions explicit before results are known: effect size, expected variance, significance threshold, and desired probability of detection. With the other inputs held constant, detecting a smaller effect generally requires a larger sample. Thus, power analysis is a study-planning tool rather than a method for modeling outcomes after data collection.'),
  ('2013-90', 'Starvation can impair concentration, cognitive flexibility, affect regulation, and the ability to use insight-oriented psychotherapy. This creates an important treatment-sequencing principle: early care emphasizes safety, nutrition, structure, and observable behavior. More elaborate psychological formulations are not dismissed; they are timed for when the patient can make use of them. A helpful memory phrase is “first feed and support, then formulate and prevent relapse.” This approach complements, rather than replaces, medical monitoring.'),
  ('2013-91', 'Stimulants acutely increase arousal and reward signaling; after sustained use ends, patients may experience the opposite state of exhaustion, dysphoria, and sleepiness. This is often remembered as the cocaine crash. In contrast, withdrawal from central nervous system depressants generally produces rebound activation, including tremor, anxiety, insomnia, and autonomic symptoms. Matching the withdrawal state to the drug’s acute direction is a useful first-pass examination strategy, while still assessing for dangerous co-occurring conditions.'),
  ('2013-92', 'After fibers from the two eyes meet at the optic chiasm, visual information is organized by visual hemifield rather than by eye. Temporal-lobe optic radiations loop forward before heading to the occipital cortex. Their inferior retinal fibers encode the contralateral superior visual field, so a temporal lesion yields a superior quadrantic loss on the opposite side. “Pie in the sky” is memorable because it maps temporal-lobe injury to an upper visual-field cut.'),
  ('2013-93', 'ADHD is developmentally expressed. The same underlying tendency toward excessive activity can look like running and climbing in a young child, fidgeting in an adolescent, or internal restlessness and overcommitment in an adult. This is why age-appropriate examples matter in assessment. The durable memory connection is that the motor volume often turns down over time, but executive-function and impulse-control difficulties may remain. Functional impairment, rather than a single childhood behavior, remains central to assessment.'),
  ('2013-94', 'Competence to stand trial is a functional, present-focused legal construct. Its familiar formulation separates factual knowledge from rational appreciation: a defendant may know the names of courtroom actors yet be unable to use that knowledge rationally because of severe psychosis, delirium, or cognitive impairment. Keep it separate from the retrospective question of criminal responsibility at the time of the alleged act. The diagnosis itself does not determine competence; the relevant issue is present legal functioning.'),
  ('2013-95', 'The financing distinction reflects different program roles. Medicare is a federal health-insurance program commonly associated with acute and postacute medical care. Medicaid is jointly administered by federal and state governments and has a major role in covering long-term services for eligible people with limited resources or qualifying needs. A simple memory contrast is “Medicare rehabilitates; Medicaid supports long-term nursing-home care.” Individual eligibility and covered services can vary, so clinical teams should not make case-specific coverage promises.'),
  ('2013-96', 'Dialysis dementia is strongly associated with an earlier era of renal replacement therapy, when aluminum exposure from dialysate or aluminum-containing treatments could accumulate. Improvements in water purification made the syndrome far less common. Its historical importance is educational: chronic medical treatments can create distinctive neuropsychiatric syndromes, and a temporal association with treatment may be diagnostically meaningful even when routine tests are unrevealing. It should not replace a modern medical evaluation for acute or progressive cognitive change.'),
  ('2013-97', 'Hypnosis involves focused attention, absorption, and responsiveness to suggestion. Dissociation concerns disruptions in the ordinary integration of experience, including memory, identity, perception, or awareness. Their shared emphasis on altered attention and compartmentalization provides the classic examination association. However, there is substantial individual variation, and a person''s response to hypnotic suggestion does not diagnose a dissociative disorder. It also does not establish the accuracy of recalled events. Clinical diagnosis requires a full history, assessment of impairment, and consideration of trauma-related, substance-related, neurologic, and psychotic conditions.'),
  ('2013-98', 'Monoamine oxidase normally helps metabolize dietary tyramine. When this enzyme is inhibited, tyramine can produce an exaggerated release of norepinephrine and a dangerous hypertensive reaction. This is why classic MAOI teaching emphasizes aged, cured, fermented, and certain bean products rather than all foods indiscriminately. The durable mechanism memory is that less tyramine breakdown means a greater pressor effect. The scope of dietary counseling should be tailored to the particular MAOI regimen.'),
  ('2013-99', 'NMS is a life-threatening reaction to dopamine-receptor blockade, especially after dose escalation or parenteral high-potency antipsychotic exposure. Its major features are hyperthermia, generalized rigidity, altered mental status, autonomic instability, and elevated CK from muscle breakdown. Rhabdomyolysis can release myoglobin and injure the kidneys, so hydration and urine-output monitoring are central supportive measures. Profound rigidity and reduced mobility also increase thrombotic risk. The testable principle is that NMS management begins with withdrawal of the offending drug plus meticulous medical support rather than a single curative medication.'),
  ('2013-100', 'Psychiatric confidentiality is essential to treatment but has defined limits when safety and statutory reporting obligations apply. Child-protection laws recognize that minors may be dependent on the people who harm them and may be unable to secure safety independently. The clinician’s task is to recognize concerning information, report through the designated channel, and continue providing respectful care. Reporting is not a finding that abuse has been proven. This distinction helps clinicians act promptly while avoiding an inappropriate forensic role or promising a child secrecy that cannot be maintained.'),
  ('2013-101', 'Freud''s primary-secondary distinction is a psychoanalytic model of mental functioning, not a modern neurocognitive classification. Primary process is easiest to remember through dreams: identities merge, opposites coexist, wishes appear in disguised form, and time behaves strangely. It is associated with unconscious, pleasure-oriented mental activity. Secondary process describes everyday planning, delayed gratification, and checking ideas against external reality. In examination questions, the decisive feature of primary process is not merely symbolism or nonverbal experience; it is the absence of ordinary logical connections and reality-oriented organization.'),
  ('2013-102', 'Sleep becomes less consolidated across adulthood: awakenings increase and deep slow-wave sleep diminishes. Older adults may therefore report lighter sleep, earlier waking, or more daytime napping despite spending substantial time in bed. These patterns are common but do not make clinically significant insomnia or sleep disorders inevitable. Pain, nocturia, medication effects, depression, sleep apnea, restless legs syndrome, and circadian disruption can worsen fragmentation. The age-related comparison in this question is with young adults, for whom nighttime sleep is generally more consolidated and contains a greater proportion of slow-wave sleep.'),
  ('2013-103', 'Combined treatment pairs symptom-focused medication with psychotherapy that builds skills and supports functioning. In adolescent depression, CBT commonly addresses behavioral activation, cognitive patterns, coping, and relapse prevention. Medication and psychotherapy are not competing approaches: greater severity or impairment may make their combination especially useful. Developmental fit, caregiver involvement, and active safety monitoring remain important regardless of the selected treatment. For this study question, the critical pairing is fluoxetine with CBT, not fluoxetine with interpersonal psychotherapy.'),
  ('2013-104', 'Medication selection for alcohol use disorder depends on treatment goals, prior response, adherence, co-occurring conditions, and organ function. Acamprosate is classically associated with renal elimination, making it a useful option when significant hepatic disease limits other choices. It is not an acute alcohol-withdrawal treatment and should be paired with psychosocial recovery support. The complementary safety reminder is to check kidney function before prescribing. This question contrasts renal considerations for acamprosate with hepatic concerns associated with disulfiram.'),
  ('2013-105', 'Dialectics is a way of working with tension between opposing positions rather than choosing one extreme. DBT applies it to recurring clinical polarities, including acceptance and change, autonomy and support, and validation and accountability. This stance is particularly useful when patients or clinicians are drawn toward all-or-nothing interpretations of relationships and behavior. The memorable formulation is “acceptance and change,” not acceptance instead of change. DBT then operationalizes that stance through skills training, behavioral analysis, and a hierarchy of treatment targets.'),
  ('2013-106', 'Delirium is an acute disorder of brain function whose hallmark is instability in attention, awareness, and arousal over short intervals. Dementias are usually chronic neurocognitive syndromes with a steadier level of consciousness, although behavior and symptoms may vary for other reasons. Bedside attention testing and time course are therefore especially useful diagnostic anchors. The word “fluctuating” is most meaningful when it describes arousal and attention, not simply changing mood or ordinary variation in memory complaints.'),
  ('2013-107', 'Brain maturation involves both formation of connections and selective refinement of existing networks. Synaptic pruning is a useful teaching model for how neural systems become more efficient and specialized with development and experience. The prefrontal cortex contributes to planning, inhibition, working memory, and flexible problem solving, all of which continue to mature over a long developmental period. This creates a board-style bridge between rapid learning and cortical refinement: development is not simply more brain tissue, but better organized circuitry.'),
  ('2013-108', 'Piagetian cognitive development includes movement from early egocentric reasoning toward coordination of multiple perspectives. Decentration helps children understand that other people have separate motives, knowledge, and causes for their actions. In emotionally charged family events, younger children may interpret adult conflict through their own behavior. Developing decentration permits a more accurate account of why events occurred. A useful memory cue is that to decenter is to step away from the center of one’s own viewpoint.'),
  ('2013-109', 'Dopamine is not a single uniform signal: receptor subtype and neural circuit influence its effects. D1 receptors are prominent in prefrontal cortical signaling and are commonly associated with working-memory function. D2 receptor blockade is the classic pharmacologic mechanism for reducing positive symptoms of schizophrenia. This distinction prevents the oversimplified idea that either increasing or decreasing dopamine is globally beneficial. Different receptor actions may affect different symptom dimensions, including psychosis, cognition, motivation, and medication adverse effects.'),
  ('2013-110', 'Defense mechanisms are unconscious ways of managing conflict, anxiety, or unacceptable affect. Projection is memorable because the direction of movement is outward: what is difficult to recognize internally is experienced as belonging to another person. It can shape interpersonal perception in ordinary life as well as in psychopathology. The key distinction from projective identification is that projection does not itself require interpersonal pressure or enactment that induces the projected state in someone else.'),
  ('2013-111', 'The ulnar nerve supplies sensation to the little finger and the ulnar side of the ring finger. It is vulnerable to compression near the elbow, particularly with sustained flexion, explaining symptoms while holding a phone or sleeping with the elbow bent. Electrodiagnostic testing measures conduction across nerve segments and can demonstrate abnormalities near the entrapment site. The anatomy provides a simple memory cue: ulnar neuropathy affects the ulnar-side digits, not the thumb and index finger.'),
  ('2013-112', 'Sex-prevalence questions describe population patterns, not rules for diagnosing any individual patient. The standard contrast is that unipolar depressive disorders show a female predominance, whereas bipolar disorder is generally taught as having an approximately equal male-to-female distribution. This epidemiologic association is useful alongside the core diagnostic distinction: bipolar disorder requires a history of mania or hypomania, regardless of the patient’s current mood state, sex, or other demographic characteristics.'),
  ('2013-113', 'Dantrolene is also associated with malignant hyperthermia, another syndrome in which abnormal skeletal-muscle contraction drives excessive heat production. This association makes its role in NMS easier to remember: severe rigidity is not merely a neurologic sign, but a source of metabolic heat and muscle injury. The relevant mechanism is peripheral skeletal-muscle relaxation, not a central dopamine-receptor action. Supportive management and withdrawal of the causative dopamine-blocking drug remain fundamental.'),
  ('2013-114', '“Anaclitic” refers to dependence or leaning upon another, making the term a direct memory cue for an infant’s reliance on a caregiving attachment. Historical observations of separated and institutionalized infants helped establish that relational deprivation can affect growth, behavior, and development as well as affective expression. The term remains important in psychiatric history and examination questions. Contemporary assessment, however, should evaluate attachment disruption within a broader developmental and medical context rather than applying this historical label as a modern diagnosis.'),
  ('2013-115', 'Motivational enhancement therapy is a brief, structured adaptation of motivational interviewing principles. Its central premise is that ambivalence about change is common and can be explored rather than confronted. Readiness rulers make ambivalence visible: asking why a number is not lower elicits reasons already favoring change, while asking why it is not higher explores obstacles. The therapist uses a collaborative, nonjudgmental stance that evokes motivation instead of imposing it. This distinguishes motivational enhancement from advice-giving or direct persuasion.'),
  ('2013-116', 'Cyclosporine suppresses T-cell activation by inhibiting calcineurin and reducing cytokine transcription. This mechanism made it central to transplant immunosuppression, but its narrow therapeutic range and interaction potential make adverse effects clinically important. Neurologic adverse effects range from common tremor and headache to confusion, seizures, and encephalopathy. A useful memory link is that cyclosporine is a transplant medication that can produce tremor with striking mental-status change. Tacrolimus, another calcineurin inhibitor, can produce a similar neurotoxic syndrome.'),
  ('2013-117', 'Behavioral treatments for disruptive behavior are grounded in learning principles: behavior that reliably produces escape, attention, or access to a desired item tends to recur. Parent management training translates these principles into daily routines, emphasizing positive reinforcement, clear expectations, and consistent follow-through. It is not simply better discipline; it is a structured intervention that helps caregivers identify antecedents and consequences. When the problem is organized around parent-child interactions, the treatment must actively include the parent and practice changes at home.'),
  ('2013-118', 'Lithium is a small monovalent cation that the kidney handles in ways closely linked to sodium and water balance. When effective circulating volume or sodium is low, the kidney reabsorbs more lithium along with sodium, potentially elevating the serum lithium level. This explains why gastrointestinal losses, dehydration, and several common medications matter so much. Baseline BUN and creatinine estimate renal status before exposure, while later measurements help detect declining clearance during treatment. The memory cue is: lithium follows the kidney.'),
  ('2013-119', 'Covert means internal or imagined. Covert sensitization applies conditioning principles without delivering an external noxious stimulus: the person imagines the target cue and then an unpleasant consequence. This contrasts with overt aversion, in which a real external stimulus is used. The term is mainly useful as an exam distinction because covert techniques operate in imagery. Mindfulness changes one’s relationship to thoughts and urges rather than attempting to make the thought itself aversive. Risk assessment and ethically appropriate treatment remain separate clinical responsibilities.'),
  ('2013-120', 'Predictive values reverse the usual direction of diagnostic-test questions. Sensitivity and specificity begin with the patient’s true disease state and ask what the test does. Predictive values begin with the test result and ask what it means for the patient. Thus, a negative test followed by the question “no disease?” is negative predictive value. Unlike sensitivity and specificity, negative predictive value changes substantially with prevalence and pretest probability. When disease is uncommon, a negative result is generally more reassuring.'),
  ('2013-121', 'NMDA receptors are ionotropic glutamate receptors important in synaptic plasticity and learning. Their activation is unusual because glutamate binding alone is insufficient: a co-agonist, classically glycine, must also occupy a separate modulatory site. This two-key arrangement is a useful memory device: glutamate is one key and glycine is the other. D-cycloserine is remembered for acting at the glycine-related keyhole rather than at the primary glutamate-binding site. This receptor mechanism does not establish it as routine clinical treatment for schizophrenia.'),
  ('2013-122', 'Erikson framed development as recurring psychosocial tasks, each organized around a central conflict. Identity is remembered by the question, “Who am I?” and includes vocational direction, values, affiliations, and social roles. Intimacy is more specifically the task of forming close reciprocal relationships without losing a stable sense of self. A young adult may confront both themes, but examination vignettes should be classified by the dominant conflict. Here, uncertainty about a parent-supported career path and personal direction makes self-definition the central issue, rather than relationship formation or later-life generativity.'),
  ('2013-123', 'Phenytoin is an older antiseizure medication with a memorable adverse-effect profile. Its metabolism becomes saturated within the therapeutic range, producing nonlinear kinetics: a modest dosing change can cause a large concentration change. Gingival hypertrophy and hirsutism often point to chronic exposure, whereas nystagmus and ataxia suggest excessive central nervous system effect. Other chronic effects can include peripheral neuropathy and bone disease. The recognition pattern remains especially useful when evaluating unexplained gait impairment in a patient taking antiseizure medication.'),
  ('2013-124', 'OCD pharmacotherapy is closely tied to serotonin reuptake inhibition. Clomipramine is a tricyclic agent notable for particularly potent serotonin reuptake blockade, which explains its enduring place in OCD teaching. Its tricyclic properties also account for more anticholinergic, cardiovascular, and overdose concerns than many SSRIs. SSRIs are commonly preferred initially because they are generally better tolerated. The key examination pairing is clomipramine with strong anti-obsessional efficacy, while real-world treatment weighs efficacy, patient preference, and safety.'),
  ('2013-125', 'Agoraphobia is maintained by avoidance: avoiding a feared setting provides immediate relief, which reinforces future avoidance. Exposure reverses this learning cycle by helping the person remain in feared situations long enough to discover that distress can be tolerated and predicted catastrophe need not occur. The central exam distinction is not merely CBT but CBT with exposure. Relaxation can be useful for some patients, yet it does not by itself confront the avoidance that organizes agoraphobia. Exposure work should be collaborative and tailored to the patient’s feared situations.'),
  ('2013-126', 'Autism spectrum disorder and intellectual disability are distinct diagnoses that often co-occur. Autism concerns social-communication differences and restricted or repetitive patterns of behavior; intellectual disability requires deficits in intellectual and adaptive functioning with developmental onset. The conceptual memory point is to separate how a child communicates and relates from how broadly the child learns and functions adaptively. Both domains need direct assessment, especially when language limitations make standard testing difficult. Co-occurring ADHD and anxiety also warrant active assessment because they may cause additional impairment.'),
  ('2013-127', 'Nontreponemal tests such as VDRL are useful screening and activity-monitoring tests but can be false-positive. Treponemal tests such as FTA-ABS provide evidence of exposure to Treponema pallidum in serum, though CSF treponemal testing is not perfectly specific for active neurosyphilis. A CSF VDRL is more specific but has limited sensitivity. The clinical distinction is that serum evidence of infection is not the same as evidence of CNS involvement. Neuropsychiatric symptoms supply the reason to examine CSF and interpret results in context.'),
  ('2013-128', 'PCP is a dissociative drug that antagonizes NMDA receptors. Its intoxication can resemble psychosis, mania, delirium, or severe agitation, while distinctive neurologic signs help reveal a toxicologic cause. Vertical nystagmus is a high-yield association, although it is not exclusive to PCP. Hypertension, tachycardia, and altered perception support the overall syndrome. Because trauma, hyperthermia, rhabdomyolysis, and co-ingestion may coexist, recognizing the substance does not replace emergency medical assessment and stabilization.'),
  ('2013-129', 'Parkinson disease is a synucleinopathy: abnormal alpha-synuclein accumulates within neurons as Lewy bodies and Lewy neurites. Degeneration of substantia nigra dopaminergic neurons disrupts basal-ganglia circuits, producing the cardinal motor syndrome. Lewy body disorders can also include cognitive decline, visual hallucinations, sleep disturbance, and autonomic symptoms. The memory connection is Lewy bodies leading to loss of nigral dopamine and a Parkinsonian movement disorder. Neuropathology explains the syndrome but is not usually required for antemortem diagnosis.'),
  ('2013-130', 'Memory is often divided into declarative and nondeclarative systems. Declarative memory can be consciously recalled and includes episodic memory for events and semantic memory for facts. Nondeclarative memory is expressed through performance: habits, conditioning, and priming may remain intact even when a person cannot report learning them. The classic contrast is knowing that versus knowing how. Recalling a daily event is knowing that it happened, making it declarative. Attention must be adequate when testing new memory because inattention can impair encoding.'),
  ('2013-131', 'School-age children increasingly understand that death is permanent, but their emotional expression remains developmentally variable. They may ask repeated questions, become temporarily clingy, play, or seem cheerful between periods of distress. Grief becomes more concerning when symptoms persist, disrupt developmental tasks, or narrow the child’s relationships and functioning. Normal grief can be intermittent and expressive; unresolved grief is more likely to be persistent and impairing. Assessment should also consider trauma exposure, depression, anxiety, family stress, and the child’s developmental understanding of the loss.'),
  ('2013-132', 'MAOIs inhibit degradation of serotonin, norepinephrine, and dopamine. Their interaction burden reflects increased monoamine availability rather than only their antidepressant effect. Medications with serotonergic properties may become dangerous when monoamine oxidase is inhibited. Opioid questions often emphasize meperidine, but other opioids, including fentanyl, can be implicated in serious MAOI-associated reactions. A medication list should include prescribed drugs, perioperative agents, supplements, and nonprescription products because interactions may arise across care settings.'),
  ('2013-133', 'Psychiatric symptoms and medical illness can coexist. Health-related fears may accompany genuine disease, and common symptoms such as reflux or indigestion require attention to duration, red flags, risk factors, and prior workup. The useful principle is “both/and”: validate the patient’s distress while maintaining a medical differential diagnosis. Once evaluation is adequate, treatment can shift from repeatedly seeking certainty to addressing the cognitive and behavioral cycle that sustains health anxiety.'),
  ('2013-134', 'Lithium has a distinctive place in psychiatric history because it helped move mania treatment toward targeted pharmacology. Its earlier historical association with gout reflects beliefs about uric acid and lithium salts, not current bipolar pathophysiology or a current gout treatment recommendation. John Cade''s observations made lithium central to the history of mania treatment. In current clinical care, lithium is selected on its merits for a particular patient and requires monitoring for serum concentration, renal function, thyroid effects, interactions, and toxicity. The memory chain is gout to Cade to mania to mood stabilization.'),
  ('2013-135', 'Classical conditioning is learning through association between stimuli. In substance use, places, people, objects, sensory cues, and internal states can become triggers because they repeatedly accompany intoxication, withdrawal relief, or drug rituals. A previously neutral setting may then evoke craving before any drug is obtained. This differs from operant conditioning, in which a behavior becomes more likely because its consequences are reinforcing. A place that makes craving appear is therefore Pavlovian; the rewarding effects that strengthen drug-taking behavior illustrate operant learning. Both processes can contribute to relapse risk.'),
  ('2013-136', 'Early trauma care recognizes that assault can abruptly remove safety, control, and trust. Helpful initial interventions therefore emphasize practical protection, informed choice, connection, and stabilization rather than compulsory emotional processing. Acute stress reactions may include fear, insomnia, hyperarousal, numbness, intrusive memories, or dissociation; these reactions alone do not establish a chronic disorder. A useful memory rule is to make the present safer before attempting to process the past.'),
  ('2013-137', 'Lithium is a foundational treatment for bipolar disorder because it can treat and prevent mood episodes and is associated with reduced suicide and suicide-related behavior. Its narrow therapeutic window belongs in the same memory package: it can be highly valuable but requires monitoring and patient education. Remember that lithium supports the long-term course, whereas acute suicide prevention still requires immediate assessment, safety planning, environmental intervention, and an appropriate level of care.'),
  ('2013-138', 'The neuromuscular junction converts a nerve signal into muscle contraction when acetylcholine binds postsynaptic receptors. Myasthenia gravis is memorable as a receptor-side autoimmune disorder: transmitter is released, but the muscle-side response is weakened. Lambert-Eaton syndrome is usually remembered as a release-side disorder involving presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels. This anatomic distinction predicts the different clinical patterns, associated conditions, and electrophysiologic findings of the two disorders.'),
  ('2013-139', 'Emergency psychiatry balances respect for autonomy against protection from imminent harm. Law and clinical practice therefore distinguish a psychiatric diagnosis from the narrower circumstances of an emergency. An emergency is a clinical and legal threshold requiring an urgent, documentable need for immediate intervention. The least-restrictive principle organizes care: use verbal de-escalation, environmental modification, and voluntary treatment whenever these approaches are safe and feasible, while preserving the ability to act quickly when danger is immediate.'),
  ('2013-140', 'Perinatal prescribing is rarely a simple choice between medication risk and no risk. The real comparison is often medication exposure versus recurrent, untreated illness. Lithium illustrates individualized risk-benefit reasoning because it is highly effective for some patients while requiring close physiologic monitoring as pregnancy changes volume status and renal handling. A useful memory anchor is that the severity and recurrence of prior relapse strongly predict the potential harm of discontinuing effective maintenance treatment.'),
  ('2013-141', 'Secondary psychosis describes psychotic symptoms caused or substantially contributed to by medical, neurologic, or substance-related conditions. Later onset, cognitive change, fluctuating course, abnormal neurologic findings, or atypical symptom patterns increase concern for these causes. The memory rule is simple: when a presentation is new and atypical, widen the diagnostic lens before narrowing it. Collateral information is useful for confirming changes from baseline, but it does not substitute for medical assessment.'),
  ('2013-142', 'Stage theories use different language but can be aligned by broad developmental period. Freud''s latency phase is associated with school-age years and a relative shift toward learning, peers, rules, and culturally valued skills. Erikson describes the major psychosocial task of this period as industry versus inferiority. Children gain a sense of competence through schoolwork, play, and productive activities, while repeated failure or humiliation can foster feelings of inadequacy. The correspondence is primarily developmental and age-linked; the two theories are not claiming the same psychological mechanism. Think: latency equals school years equals skills and competence.'),
  ('2013-143', 'Edrophonium was historically used as a brief pharmacologic probe of neuromuscular transmission. By inhibiting acetylcholinesterase, it temporarily increases acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction; improvement in characteristic weakness supported myasthenia gravis. The symptom pattern is highly memorable: weakness fluctuates, worsens with sustained use, and often begins with ptosis or diplopia. Modern diagnostic pathways favor serology and electrophysiology, but the pharmacologic mechanism remains foundational for interpreting this historical question.'),
  ('2013-144', 'Anabolic-androgenic steroids are synthetic testosterone-related agents sometimes used illicitly for muscle gain or performance enhancement. Their psychiatric effects vary, so the label “roid rage” is an oversimplification; however, clinically significant irritability and aggression can occur. A useful examination association is increased training, a muscular-performance context, and new behavioral dyscontrol. The differential should remain broad, and safety assessment should never rely on stereotypes about athletes, body size, or substance use.'),
  ('2013-145', 'CYP3A4 is a major hepatic and intestinal enzyme involved in the metabolism of numerous medications. Its activity can be altered substantially by coadministered inhibitors or inducers and varies across individuals for many reasons. This category B record preserves a historical population-level PRITE association while explicitly rejecting deterministic use of racial identity. Ancestry labels and socially defined racial categories do not substitute for an individual medication history, physiology, observed response, adverse-effect assessment, or direct testing when clinically appropriate.'),
  ('2013-146', 'Spongiform refers to the microscopic vacuolated appearance of affected brain tissue in prion disorders. The classic board pattern joins rapidly progressive cognitive decline, myoclonus that may be exaggerated by startle, and periodic sharp-wave complexes on EEG. This contrasts with typical neurodegenerative dementias, which generally evolve over years rather than weeks or months. Although uncommon, recognizing this syndrome prevents an inaccurate primary psychiatric formulation and prompts urgent neurologic evaluation.'),
  ('2013-147', 'Group psychotherapy treats the group as a social laboratory. Members can observe expectations, avoidance, conflict, attachment, and repair as they unfold in real time. Because several relationships are active simultaneously, the leader often makes process more explicit than in individual therapy. Transparency does not mean unrestricted personal disclosure; it means using the therapist’s observations and limited responses purposefully to make interpersonal patterns available for examination and change.'),
  ('2013-148', 'Clozapine is uniquely effective for some patients with treatment-resistant schizophrenia and has an important role in reducing suicidal behavior in schizophrenia-spectrum illness. Its distinctive benefits are inseparable from its safety infrastructure. The mnemonic is “clozapine counts neutrophils”: blood monitoring is designed to detect loss of the white-cell defense most critical for bacterial and fungal infection risk, rather than simply any CBC abnormality. Reliable laboratory follow-up is therefore part of safe prescribing.'),
  ('2013-149', 'Dopamine replacement therapies improve Parkinson motor symptoms but can affect alertness, behavior, and autonomic function. Dopamine agonists stimulate dopamine receptors directly and are memorable for a cluster of behavioral and sleep-related adverse effects, including sleep attacks and impulse-control disorders. In examination questions, falling asleep while driving after a new Parkinson treatment is a high-yield association with a dopamine agonist. Safety counseling is an important part of routine medication education and follow-up.'),
  ('2013-150', 'The efficacy–effectiveness distinction separates “can it work?” from “does it work in usual care?” Efficacy trials maximize internal validity by controlling influences on outcome, often at the cost of generalizability. Effectiveness research deliberately incorporates ordinary clinical variation so its results are more applicable to routine care. A pragmatic effectiveness trial may still be randomized; pragmatism describes how closely the study resembles actual practice, not whether it lacks rigor.'),
  ('2013-151', 'Confidentiality supports trust and candid communication in psychiatric care. It does not prohibit a clinician from receiving information from family; the important distinction is between hearing collateral and disclosing protected information. Authorization should clarify who may receive information and the scope of what may be discussed. Limited exceptions can arise in emergencies or when disclosure is legally required, but a routine question about medication does not itself create an exception.'),
  ('2013-152', 'Lewy body disease links cognitive symptoms with synuclein-related pathology that also affects motor systems. A memorable pattern is visual hallucinations, parkinsonism, and fluctuating cognition or attention. The syndrome can overlap clinically with Parkinson disease dementia; the relative timing of cognitive and motor symptoms helps distinguish those labels clinically. In this vignette, the decisive task is recognizing the characteristic Lewy body phenotype rather than assigning a separate cause to each symptom.'),
  ('2013-153', 'Delirium tremens is named for the prominent tremor and delirium seen in severe alcohol withdrawal. Chronic alcohol exposure leads the brain to adapt to alcohol’s inhibitory effects; abrupt cessation can leave a hyperexcitable state. The clinical memory anchor is autonomic overdrive plus impaired attention: sweating, tachycardia, tremor, agitation, hallucinations, and fluctuating confusion. Hallucinations alone do not establish primary psychosis when delirium is present.'),
  ('2013-154', 'Grief is not governed by a fixed timetable. Attachment to a person can continue after death, and memories may remain emotionally charged throughout life. The clinical distinction is not simply the number of years since the loss; it is whether grief has become persistently impairing, all-consuming, or part of another psychiatric syndrome. Functioning, relationships, sleep, appetite, and capacity for pleasure therefore matter more than the mere presence of sorrow.'),
  ('2013-155', 'Presynaptic autoreceptors function as feedback brakes: transmitter release activates receptors that reduce further release. Alpha-2 receptors have this inhibitory role for norepinephrine and can regulate serotonin release through heteroreceptor effects. Mirtazapine removes this brake. The mnemonic “NaSSA” captures its classification as a noradrenergic and specific serotonergic antidepressant, rather than a serotonin reuptake inhibitor. This mechanism explains the answer without conflating all of mirtazapine’s receptor actions.'),
  ('2013-156', 'Phobias are organized around a particular cue, such as an animal, height, injection, or situation. Avoidance gives immediate relief, which can reinforce fear over time: escape reduces distress, but the child loses opportunities for repeated safe experiences that could update the fear response. In children, behavior often conveys the syndrome more clearly than a sophisticated verbal description of anxiety. The diagnostic focus is the circumscribed trigger and resulting avoidance or distress.'),
  ('2013-157', 'The adolescent brain undergoes substantial developmental change, making timing relevant in substance-risk discussions. The board-relevant association should be distinguished from intoxication syndromes: cannabis is associated epidemiologically with later psychosis risk, whereas amphetamines and PCP are classic causes of acute psychotic presentations. An association describes a population-level relationship, not a simple one-cause explanation for an individual who develops schizophrenia or another psychotic disorder.'),
  ('2013-158', '“Supranuclear” means that the problem lies in brain pathways controlling voluntary gaze above the ocular motor nuclei, rather than in the eye muscles or ocular motor nuclei themselves. Vestibulo-ocular reflexes can therefore be relatively preserved early. Progressive supranuclear palsy is often remembered by vertical gaze limitation and early postural instability. These features distinguish it from typical Parkinson disease, where resting tremor and asymmetric onset are more characteristic.'),
  ('2013-159', 'Sleep is experienced, not merely recorded. Brief awakenings, light sleep, hyperarousal, and attention to sleep-related sensations can make someone feel awake for much longer than a polysomnogram suggests. The term “paradoxical” captures the tension between convincing subjective insomnia and relatively normal objective sleep. A marked mismatch is not the same as malingering or factitious disorder; the complaint may be sincere and highly distressing despite near-normal laboratory findings.'),
  ('2013-160', 'Therapeutic alliance is built through accurate listening, respect, collaboration, and emotional attunement. Reflection names the experience the clinician hears without claiming to know more than the patient has said. Open-ended questions let the patient decide where to begin. In contrast, early advice, excessive self-disclosure, and rapid problem-solving can make a patient feel unheard, particularly during an initial encounter involving grief, shame, or relational pain.'),
  ('2013-161', 'Alcohol acutely enhances inhibitory signaling and suppresses excitatory signaling, but the brain adapts during chronic exposure. When alcohol is removed, adaptation can leave an imbalance favoring excitation. Acamprosate is commonly taught as a medication that addresses this glutamate-related adaptation during abstinence, although its precise mechanism is not fully established. The broader memory map is acamprosate—glutamate, naltrexone—opioid receptors, and disulfiram—alcohol metabolism.'),
  ('2013-162', 'Dopamine is the major physiologic brake on prolactin secretion, a relationship that organizes several psychiatry pharmacology questions. Dopamine receptor blockade can raise prolactin, whereas increased dopamine signaling can lower it. Cocaine’s acute and withdrawal states have different neurochemical effects. The memory link for this item is that loss of dopaminergic restraint can permit prolactin release; it is not simply the presence of a psychiatric symptom that explains galactorrhea.'),
  ('2013-163', 'The modern diagnostic term is specific learning disorder, whereas older examinations often use “learning disability.” The key contrast is global versus specific impairment. Intellectual disability affects general intellectual and adaptive functioning; a learning disorder reflects an unexpectedly focal problem with academic skills. Testing helps identify this pattern, but educational opportunity, sensory function, language exposure, persistence of difficulty, and functional impact must also be considered during a real clinical evaluation.'),
  ('2013-164', 'Methadone has a long and variable half-life, so drug interactions can produce delayed, clinically significant accumulation. A useful contrast is inhibitor versus inducer: inhibitors may raise concentrations and toxicity, while inducers may lower concentrations and cause withdrawal. The vignette’s respiratory depression establishes the clinically important direction of the interaction—excess opioid effect—before the specific medication is recalled. This pattern warrants emergency management rather than watchful waiting.'),
  ('2013-165', 'Visual pathways reorganize information at the optic chiasm. Light from the temporal visual world falls on the nasal retina, and nasal retinal axons cross to the opposite side at the chiasm. A central chiasmal lesion therefore interrupts temporal-field information from both eyes. Lesions before the chiasm usually produce monocular deficits, while lesions after the chiasm produce same-side field loss in both eyes. A useful memory aid is that nasal retina crosses, so temporal visual fields are lost with chiasmal injury.'),
  ('2013-166', 'Most fear responses are remembered as fight or flight, but blood-injection-injury phobia is notable for a later fainting response. The initial alarm phase resembles other phobias, followed by increased vagal activity and reduced vascular tone that can lower blood pressure. This biphasic pattern explains why someone may fear not only needles or blood, but also loss of consciousness. It also provides a useful contrast with panic symptoms, which do not characteristically progress to vasovagal hypotension. Remember: blood phobia may go up first, then down physiologically.'),
  ('2013-167', 'Age can change the expression of psychiatric illness without changing the underlying diagnosis. Mixed mood states combine manic symptoms with depressive affect or cognition, which can make them difficult to recognize in older adults. Medical illness, cognitive changes, and polypharmacy can further complicate assessment. A practical memory cue is that late-life mood episodes may appear less classically euphoric and more blended. Careful longitudinal history and collateral information help distinguish bipolar mixed symptoms from anxiety, unipolar depression, delirium, and medication-related activation.'),
  ('2013-168', 'FTD comprises neurodegenerative syndromes affecting frontal and temporal networks that support social judgment, inhibition, motivation, language, and emotional behavior. Early illness can therefore appear psychiatric, particularly when personality change, compulsive behavior, loss of empathy, or disinhibition predominate. The memory contrast is useful: FTD often presents with frontal behavioral or temporal language symptoms first, whereas typical Alzheimer disease begins with prominent episodic-memory impairment. This is a pattern, not an absolute rule. New progressive psychiatric and cognitive symptoms in midlife require a medical and neurologic evaluation rather than an automatic primary psychiatric diagnosis.'),
  ('2013-169', 'Dose-response concepts answer different pharmacologic questions. Potency asks how much drug is needed to produce a defined effect. Efficacy asks how much effect is possible at the plateau of the dose-response relationship. Safety margin asks how separated desired and toxic effects are. Keeping these concepts distinct prevents a common error: assuming that a small numerical dose means a medication is more effective. A concise memory phrase is that potency is dose position, whereas efficacy is effect height.'),
  ('2013-170', 'Sensate focus is a behavioral sex-therapy method centered on sensory experience rather than achieving intercourse or orgasm on demand. Anxiety and self-monitoring can interrupt sexual response, creating a cycle in which pressure worsens the difficulty. By temporarily removing performance demands and progressing gradually, the exercises can support relaxed learning, communication, and attention to bodily experience. The approach is not a substitute for assessment of pain, medical illness, medication effects, trauma, relationship safety, or whether the concern causes clinically meaningful distress.'),
  ('2013-171', 'Language development includes more than acquiring words or producing longer sentences. Pragmatics concerns using language appropriately in social context, while metalinguistic awareness is the ability to reflect on language as an object. Jokes based on ambiguity require a child to hold more than one interpretation in mind and recognize that the literal meaning may not be intended. Wordplay is therefore a memorable marker of more mature language processing. Development varies, so one ability should be interpreted with the child’s overall communication profile.'),
  ('2013-172', 'Classical conditioning links a previously neutral cue with an aversive event. After pairing, the cue alone can trigger a conditioned response. In this example, traffic has acquired threat value through association with the accident. Extinction does not erase the event from memory; instead, it creates new learning that the cue can be experienced without the predicted catastrophe. Avoidance may preserve fear by preventing corrective learning. A useful summary is that association acquires fear, while exposure weakens the learned threat prediction.'),
  ('2013-173', 'Paraphilic terminology distinguishes the focus of arousal rather than making a global judgment about a person. A useful way to differentiate the options is to identify what is central: an object or body part in fetishism, exposure in exhibitionism, observation in voyeurism, rubbing in frotteurism, or suffering in masochism. Contemporary diagnostic practice also distinguishes a paraphilic interest from a paraphilic disorder. Distress, impairment, and consent determine whether an atypical interest is clinically diagnosable as a disorder.'),
  ('2013-174', 'Cocaine increases monoaminergic signaling and can produce sympathetic overactivation, including agitation, insomnia, tachycardia, hypertension, diaphoresis, paranoia, and sometimes psychosis. Benzodiazepines do not reverse cocaine itself; they reduce the dangerous and distressing adrenergic behavioral response. This differs from opioid overdose, in which naloxone is used to reverse respiratory depression. Medical assessment remains essential because stimulant intoxication can cause severe cardiovascular, neurologic, and hyperthermic complications. A concise contrast is stimulant agitation: sedate and monitor; opioid hypoventilation: give naloxone.'),
  ('2013-175', 'After primary varicella infection, varicella zoster virus can remain latent in sensory nerve ganglia. Reactivation travels along a sensory nerve to the skin, producing the band-like dermatomal eruption called shingles. This neuroanatomic distribution explains both the neuropathic pain and the characteristic rash pattern. Thoracic dermatomes are common sites. Zoster is therefore not merely a vesicular rash; the important pattern is vesicles accompanied by pain or dysesthesia in a unilateral dermatome. Disseminated or atypical disease warrants particular attention in immunocompromised patients.'),
  ('2013-176', 'Both major stimulant classes can improve core ADHD symptoms, but their pharmacology and individual response profiles differ. Treatment questions often follow a sequence: confirm that the first stimulant trial was adequate, then try the alternate stimulant class before moving to many nonstimulant options. This is a useful clinical reminder that stimulant nonresponse should not be concluded after only one class. Medication decisions still require assessment of adverse effects, comorbidities, cardiac history, misuse risk, family preferences, and the need for behavioral and educational interventions.'),
  ('2013-177', 'The four commonly taught biomedical ethics principles are autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, and justice. They are complementary lenses rather than rigid rules that always yield one answer. The Latin phrase is memorable because it separates two related ideas: promoting good is beneficence, while avoiding unjustified injury is nonmaleficence. In psychiatry, nonmaleficence includes attention to medication harms, coercion, stigma, confidentiality, and the consequences of failing to treat serious illness. Ethical practice requires balancing these considerations transparently and respectfully.'),
  ('2013-178', 'NMS is a potentially life-threatening reaction to dopamine blockade that commonly includes hyperthermia, altered mental status, severe motor abnormalities, and autonomic instability. Its presentation overlaps with several medical and toxicologic emergencies, making diagnostic anchoring unsafe. Core evaluation also assesses muscle injury, renal complications, electrolytes, and systemic illness. This category C record was reinspected and retained CSF evaluation as the best-listed answer because CNS infection must sometimes be excluded, while expressly rejecting lumbar puncture as an unconditional requirement for every suspected NMS presentation.'),
  ('2013-179', 'Culture can shape the content, language, and meaning of psychiatric symptoms while leaving dimensions of fear and avoidance recognizable. In a familiar Western description of social anxiety, the feared event is personal humiliation. In taijin kyofusho-like presentations, the feared event may be interpersonal offense or burden to others. A useful contrast is “I will look foolish” versus “I will make others uncomfortable.” Cultural formulation should guide questions about meaning and impairment without treating a cultural background as a diagnosis.'),
  ('2013-180', 'Psychodynamic therapies vary in how much they emphasize insight versus support. Supportive therapy is designed to strengthen defenses and adaptive functioning when a person is overwhelmed, acutely symptomatic, or has limited capacity for intensive interpretation. Its present focus is purposeful attention to problems that most immediately affect safety and functioning. A helpful contrast is that expressive therapy seeks insight into conflict, while supportive therapy helps the patient cope now. Both approaches can be valuable when matched appropriately to clinical needs and treatment goals.'),
  ('2013-181', 'Lamotrigine is eliminated mainly after hepatic glucuronidation. Valproate inhibits this pathway, whereas several antiseizure agents, notably carbamazepine, induce drug-metabolizing pathways and tend to have the opposite effect. The memorable pairing is that valproate makes lamotrigine last longer. Because lamotrigine-associated serious rash is linked to excessive initial exposure or rapid escalation, this pharmacokinetic interaction has direct practical consequences rather than being merely a laboratory fact.'),
  ('2013-182', 'The older term reading disorder corresponds broadly to current diagnostic language of specific learning disorder with impairment in reading. Reading depends on mapping speech sounds to written symbols, building fluent word recognition, and extracting meaning from text. Difficulties in phonological processing are common mechanisms in dyslexia, which helps explain why a child can have normal intelligence and adequate opportunity yet struggle markedly with decoding. Remember the broad prevalence rule: among specific academic-skill disorders, reading is the leading category.'),
  ('2013-183', 'Alcohol chronically enhances inhibitory signaling and produces compensatory excitatory adaptation. Abrupt cessation leaves relative central nervous system hyperexcitability, causing tremor, anxiety, insomnia, autonomic activation, seizures, and, in severe cases, delirium. Benzodiazepines act at the GABA-A receptor and are cross-tolerant with alcohol, allowing symptoms to be controlled while the nervous system readapts. The useful time-course memory is that acute alcohol withdrawal evolves over days, not merely hours, although individual risk varies substantially.'),
  ('2013-184', 'West Nile virus is a mosquito-borne flavivirus. Most infections do not cause severe neurologic illness, but neuroinvasive disease may manifest as meningitis, encephalitis, or acute flaccid paralysis. Its affinity for anterior horn cells helps explain a motor-predominant, often asymmetric weakness pattern with relatively preserved sensation. That syndrome is the memorable discriminator: West Nile encephalitis can look neurologically polio-like, rather than simply causing diffuse confusion and headache.'),
  ('2013-185', 'People often describe suffering through locally meaningful idioms rather than psychiatric diagnostic terms. Nervios is a broad Spanish-language idiom that can frame distress as problems of the nerves, particularly in the setting of hardship, loss, or social stress. It may overlap with anxiety, depression, somatic symptoms, or no single DSM syndrome. The durable memory point is to translate the patient’s own meaning before translating it into a diagnostic formulation.'),
  ('2013-186', 'REM latency is the time from falling asleep to the first episode of rapid eye-movement sleep. Depression has traditionally been described as a state of REM disinhibition: REM begins earlier and may be denser. This makes a compact memory pair: depression means early REM. Sleep architecture varies across individuals and changes with age, drugs, alcohol, and many illnesses, so this pattern is a group-level association rather than a diagnostic test for an individual patient.'),
  ('2013-187', 'Cerebral amyloid angiopathy is vascular amyloid deposition in small and medium cortical and leptomeningeal vessels. Fragile amyloid-laden vessels can bleed repeatedly near the cerebral cortex, producing lobar hemorrhages. Contrast this with hypertensive arteriolar disease, which favors deep perforator territories. The imaging-location mnemonic is useful: amyloid angiopathy is lobar, whereas chronic hypertension is usually deep. Both conditions can coexist in older adults, but distribution guides the differential.'),
  ('2013-188', 'Lithium is not metabolized; it is cleared almost entirely by the kidneys and is handled similarly to sodium. Consequently, changes in renal blood flow, sodium balance, or tubular reabsorption can alter its concentration. Methylxanthines, including theophylline in aminophylline, tend to increase lithium clearance. A practical memory contrast is that caffeine and theophylline lower lithium, while thiazides and many NSAIDs raise it. Clinical monitoring remains essential because effects vary by patient and context.'),
  ('2013-189', 'Adaptive functioning concerns how a person manages everyday conceptual, social, and practical demands. It is not synonymous with IQ and may be affected by opportunity, language, physical disability, and environmental supports. Modern diagnostic framing emphasizes functional impairment and needed support rather than treating a single test score as determinative. The Vineland is memorable because it asks about life skills—what the child actually does in daily settings—rather than only performance on a cognitive test.'),
  ('2013-190', 'Organ transplantation can transform prognosis while also creating chronic uncertainty and a demanding treatment burden. Psychiatric symptoms after transplant may arise from psychological stress, preexisting vulnerabilities, medication effects, and medical complications. Depression is especially important because it can be difficult to distinguish from fatigue, sleep disruption, or reduced appetite caused by illness, yet it has meaningful effects on functioning and adherence. The core consultation principle is integrated assessment: psychiatric syndromes and transplant-related medical factors must be evaluated together.'),
  ('2013-191', 'ECT is most predictably helpful for severe, episodic mood syndromes, especially when psychosis, catatonia, or marked biologic symptoms are present. Personality disorders describe enduring patterns that generally require longitudinal, skills-based, and relational treatment rather than an acute somatic intervention alone. This creates a helpful formulation: ECT targets the current depressive episode, whereas borderline personality pathology needs its own ongoing treatment plan. Comorbidity can therefore affect observed response without invalidating the depressive diagnosis.'),
  ('2013-192', 'Solution-focused brief therapy is organized around preferred futures and exceptions rather than exhaustive problem analysis. An exception is a time when the complaint is absent, reduced, or managed more effectively despite similar circumstances. By identifying what is different in those moments, therapist and clients generate concrete, achievable next steps. The memorable question is not why the couple fights, but what happens when they do not fight and how they can do more of that.'),
  ('2013-193', 'Theory of mind refers to appreciating that people have internal mental states—beliefs, wishes, knowledge, and emotions—that guide behavior and can differ from one’s own. Preschool development brings rapid growth in language, symbolic play, and perspective-taking. A classic developmental challenge involves understanding that another person can act on a belief the child knows is false. Later childhood adds more nuanced understanding of mixed emotions, intentions, and social context.'),
  ('2013-194', 'Synesthesia is a phenomenon in which one sensory input evokes an additional experience in another sensory system, such as colors with sounds or colors with letters. Some people report stable synesthetic experiences without illness; psychoactive substances can also alter sensory integration and produce transient cross-modal perceptions. The memory hook is simple: one sense triggers another, rather than creating a perception from nothing. This distinguishes synesthesia from a primary hallucination.'),
  ('2013-195', 'Suicide prevention distinguishes between suicidal thoughts, attempts, and deaths because method lethality strongly affects whether an attempt is fatal. In the United States, firearms account for the largest share of suicide deaths. This is why lethal-means counseling focuses on creating time and distance between an acute suicidal crisis and immediate access to highly lethal methods. The goal is short-term safety during a potentially fluctuating period of risk, alongside treatment of the underlying crisis.'),
  ('2013-196', 'Platinum chemotherapy agents are remembered for characteristic organ toxicities. Cisplatin is associated with peripheral neurotoxicity, nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity, and severe nausea and vomiting. Its peripheral neuropathy preferentially affects sensory pathways, making distal paresthesias, impaired vibration, and gait insecurity useful clues. The risk generally rises with cumulative exposure. Although other conditions can cause neuropathy, the temporal relationship to cisplatin is especially important in a treatment-history question. A memory cue is that cisplatin can produce “platinum pins and needles” in the hands and feet.'),
  ('2013-197', 'Actus reus and mens rea are paired criminal-law concepts. Actus reus refers to the prohibited conduct, including a legally significant omission in some circumstances. Mens rea refers to the culpable mental state, which varies by offense and jurisdiction; it does not always mean specific intent. Insanity doctrines address a separate question: whether a person should be held criminally responsible because severe mental illness affected legally defined capacities. Thus, a defendant may have committed an act, had or lacked the required mental state, and still raise an insanity defense.'),
  ('2013-198', 'Tantrums alone are common in childhood, so diagnosis depends on a persistent pattern and meaningful impairment rather than one behavior. ODD is often remembered as conflict with authority, but persistent anger and irritability are also central features. It differs from conduct disorder, which includes more severe aggression, destruction of property, deceitfulness, theft, or serious rule violations. The diagnosis also requires that behavior not be better explained solely by another condition or ordinary developmental reactions to stress and inconsistent limits.'),
  ('2013-199', 'Working memory is often described as a mental workspace. It links attention to higher-order functions such as planning, reasoning, and goal-directed behavior. Schizophrenia is not defined by a single isolated cognitive deficit, but broad cognitive impairment helps explain why symptomatic improvement may not produce equivalent recovery in school, work, or independent living. Deficits can also precede overt psychosis and are relevant to functional outcomes, although cognitive findings alone do not establish the diagnosis.'),
  ('2013-200', 'Fast sodium channels are essential for rapid electrical conduction through ventricular myocardium. A useful memory link is that TCAs can behave like sodium-channel-blocking antiarrhythmics in overdose, but with a dangerous toxicity profile. This explains why ECG evidence of conduction delay has major importance in acute evaluation of a medication class otherwise remembered for anticholinergic effects. The same pharmacologic action accounts for the potential for wide-complex dysrhythmias and seizures in significant poisoning.'),
  ('2013-201', 'Friendship concepts develop from concrete, shared activities toward reciprocal and emotionally meaningful relationships. This shift parallels increasing ability to take another person’s perspective and understand loyalty. Development is gradual, not a rigid age cutoff, but examination questions often contrast an earlier playmate model of friendship with a later mutual-support model. Close friendships can support resilience, while peer conflict or exclusion may have important emotional and behavioral consequences during this developmental period.'),
  ('2013-202', 'CA1 is a hippocampal subfield important for memory processing and is notably vulnerable to several forms of injury. The hippocampus is also central to mesial temporal seizure circuits. A useful connection is hippocampus as a structure relevant to both memory and temporal seizures. CA1 loss can therefore occur in both neurodegenerative and epileptic pathology, even though their broader mechanisms differ. The question asks for a histologic association rather than a clinical diagnostic criterion.'),
  ('2013-203', 'The term periodic emphasizes the recurring, stereotyped pattern seen during sleep rather than a voluntary movement or dramatic parasomnia. Periodic limb movements may be a polysomnographic finding; their clinical significance depends on associated sleep disruption and symptoms. They are distinct from restless legs syndrome, in which an awake person has an urge to move the legs that is usually worse at rest and in the evening. The two conditions can coexist, but one does not establish the other.'),
  ('2013-204', 'Capacity is a clinical determination, whereas guardianship and a guardian’s authority are legal constructs. Substitute decision-makers should use the patient’s known values when available and otherwise apply a best-interests standard. The more irreversible and burdensome an intervention, the more carefully disagreement between a surrogate and clinicians is scrutinized. Legal processes are jurisdiction-specific, so this item’s court-order formulation should be understood as its keyed historical answer, not as an identical rule in every current setting.'),
  ('2013-205', 'Transferrin is an iron-transport protein whose carbohydrate side chains can be altered by sustained heavy alcohol consumption. This makes CDT more directly related to alcohol exposure than routine liver enzymes. In this context, recent heavy use should not be confused with immediate detection after a single drink. Biomarker detection windows and test performance vary with drinking pattern, timing, liver function, and individual patient factors. Laboratory findings are most useful when interpreted alongside history, examination, and collateral information.'),
  ('2013-206', 'Cluster headache belongs to the trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias, a group defined by unilateral head pain plus cranial autonomic features. The name cluster refers to attacks occurring in bouts, often with circadian regularity. A memorable contrast is that people with migraine often seek stillness, whereas people with cluster headache may appear intensely restless during an attack. Headache assessment should still include examination for unusual onset, focal deficits, systemic symptoms, or other features that warrant evaluation for secondary causes.'),
  ('2013-207', 'Self-report questionnaires capture the patient’s own rating of symptoms, whereas clinician-rated scales and structured interviews depend on an examiner’s assessment. The Beck Anxiety Inventory and Taylor Manifest Anxiety Scale both belong to the self-report category, making the original single-answer source item ambiguous. The corrected key therefore includes A and C. The other listed instruments are conventionally associated with structured interviewing or clinician rating in their standard forms, though differently administered versions may exist. Administration method, symptom target, and version should always be confirmed before interpreting a score.'),
  ('2013-208', 'Biofeedback translates a physiologic signal into information a person can observe and learn to influence. In thermal biofeedback, peripheral skin temperature is the feedback signal. Raynaud symptoms make this memorable because cold exposure and sympathetic arousal can promote digital vasoconstriction, making hand-warming training intuitively relevant. Raynaud disease is idiopathic primary Raynaud phenomenon; secondary causes and ischemic complications require medical evaluation. Behavioral interventions complement rather than replace appropriate vascular and rheumatologic assessment when indicated.'),
  ('2013-209', 'Adolescent sexual development includes curiosity, emerging attraction, and increasing interest in sexual information. Clinical interpretation depends on consent, context, intensity, impairment, and safety, not simply on whether an adolescent has encountered or sought sexual material. A developmental framework helps distinguish ordinary exploration from behavior that is coercive, compulsive, traumatic, exploitative, or harmful. Confidentiality discussions should also explain their limits when there are concerns about abuse, exploitation, or risk to the adolescent or others.'),
  ('2013-210', 'Trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias combine unilateral trigeminal-distribution pain with ipsilateral cranial autonomic symptoms. Duration and frequency help distinguish them: paroxysmal hemicrania produces multiple brief attacks and has a characteristic indomethacin response, whereas cluster headache attacks generally last longer. This category C record corrects a prior erroneous multi-answer presentation by selecting indomethacin alone. The association is highly useful, but treatment still requires contraindication review and assessment for secondary headache when clinical features warrant it.'),
  ('2013-211', 'Dissociation is disruption in the usual integration of consciousness, memory, identity, emotion, perception, or behavior. Dissociative identity disorder is not simply mood lability or a changing self-image; its high-yield features are discontinuity in identity and recurrent memory gaps beyond ordinary forgetting. Differential diagnosis requires attention to neurologic, sleep-related, substance-related, and psychotic conditions. Diagnosis depends on a careful clinical assessment rather than on suggestive questioning, a single reported gap, or a presumed history of trauma.'),
  ('2013-212', 'Consultation-liaison psychiatry works at the intersection of psychiatric symptoms, behavior, and medical illness. Its role extends beyond diagnosing delirium or prescribing medication. C-L clinicians help teams understand why a patient may be unable or reluctant to participate in care, such as through depression, anxiety, cognitive impairment, substance use, mistrust, or family conflict. The liaison function also strengthens communication and bedside education among the patient, family, psychiatry service, and medical team.'),
  ('2013-213', 'A good death is best understood as a patient-defined experience rather than a single medical outcome. Relief of pain and other distressing symptoms is a highly salient end-of-life goal. Pain also carries symbolic meaning because patients may fear that it will erase autonomy, dignity, or meaningful connection with others. Symptom control is necessary but not sufficient: communication, family relationships, place of care, spiritual concerns, and unfinished business may also shape an individual patient’s preferences. The question asks for the most typical concern, not the only meaningful one.'),
  ('2013-214', 'Triphasic waves are a descriptive EEG morphology with three sequential phases, often occurring in generalized periodic runs. They were historically associated with hepatic encephalopathy but can also appear in other severe toxic-metabolic encephalopathies. Asterixis is a clinical sign of diffuse metabolic dysfunction and supports the vignette’s diagnosis. For exam recall, pair liver disease, delirium, and asterixis with diffuse slowing or triphasic waves rather than a focal EEG abnormality.'),
  ('2013-215', 'Erikson framed development as a sequence of psychosocial tensions. In middle childhood, industry means sustained effort toward socially valued skills, not merely being busy. Preschool children are primarily negotiating initiative versus guilt and may ask whether they are allowed to act. School-age children increasingly ask whether they can do things well. Adolescents then shift toward identity versus role confusion, with a focus on who they are and where they belong.'),
  ('2013-216', 'Nonassociative learning changes the response to a stimulus without requiring a learned connection between two separate events. Habituation decreases responding after repeated, nonthreatening stimulation. Sensitization increases responding, especially after a strong or noxious event, and can generalize to milder stimulation. These basic learning concepts help explain clinical phenomena such as heightened startle, hypervigilance, and increased reactivity to bodily sensations. The key contrast is simple: habituation turns the response down, whereas sensitization turns it up after aversive exposure.'),
  ('2013-217', 'Lamotrigine is useful in bipolar disorder, particularly for prevention of depressive episodes, but it is titrated slowly because uncommon serious rashes can be life-threatening. Exam questions signal urgency by pairing a rash with fever, sore throat, mucosal symptoms, facial involvement, blistering, skin pain, or systemic illness. The practical recall rule is that a lamotrigine rash plus systemic features requires stopping the drug first and obtaining urgent evaluation.'),
  ('2013-218', 'This question tests the ethical principle commonly called the Goldwater Rule. It arose from psychiatrists publicly commenting on a political candidate’s mental health. The principle does not prohibit all public discussion of mental illness or substance use. Rather, it distinguishes general education from a professional diagnostic opinion about a specific person whom the psychiatrist has not evaluated and is not authorized to discuss. Public status does not create an exception to that boundary.'),
  ('2013-219', 'RLS is defined phenomenologically by four features: an urge to move, worsening at rest, relief with movement, and evening or nighttime predominance. Dopaminergic responsiveness established the historical association between RLS and dopamine pathways. Augmentation is an important later complication of chronic dopaminergic treatment: symptoms may begin earlier in the day, intensify, or spread to other body areas. That risk explains why current long-term treatment recommendations differ from older board-style teaching.'),
  ('2013-220', 'Pregnancy can increase vulnerability to intimate partner violence and creates linked concerns for the safety of the pregnant person and possible obstetric consequences of trauma. Older exam teaching associates pregnancy-related assault with abdominal injury because of concern for the gravid uterus. Clinically, however, injury patterns are neither sensitive nor specific for violence. Confidential inquiry, trauma-informed care, medical assessment, and attention to the patient’s immediate safety are more reliable than assumptions based on body location alone.'),
  ('2013-221', 'Cognitive therapy emphasizes that distress is often intensified by rapid interpretations rather than by events alone. The word arbitrary is a useful mnemonic: the conclusion is selected without a sound evidentiary basis. Related distortions operate differently. Overgeneralization turns one event into a broad rule, personalization makes an event about oneself, and selective abstraction narrows attention to one detail while overlooking more relevant information. Naming the operation helps guide cognitive restructuring.'),
  ('2013-222', 'ECT produces a generalized therapeutic seizure and is followed by characteristic EEG changes, including postictal slowing. Frontal delta activity refers to slow-wave activity over frontal regions. This item asks for a treatment-associated physiologic correlate rather than a broad clinical predictor. The test-taking contrast is between the keyed EEG change and variables such as age, personality pathology, medication history, and structural abnormalities, which have less specific implications in this particular question.'),
  ('2013-223', 'ASD and anxiety can amplify each other. Social uncertainty, sensory sensitivity, difficulty predicting change, and negative prior experiences may increase anxiety, while anxiety can heighten rigidity and avoidance. A useful memory cue is “ASD plus change or avoidance: think anxiety.” This is a comorbidity question, not a claim that anxiety explains ASD or is inevitable in every autistic child. Careful assessment requires attention to the individual’s baseline behavior and communication style.'),
  ('2013-224', 'IIH was historically called pseudotumor cerebri because it can resemble a brain mass despite the absence of a mass lesion. Papilledema is optic-disc swelling caused by elevated intracranial pressure and is the central visual clue in this vignette. The memorable pattern is young adult, obesity, headache, papilledema, and normal structural imaging. The term idiopathic is appropriate only after reasonable exclusion of secondary causes of intracranial hypertension.'),
  ('2013-225', 'Panic is a rapid alarm response: the defining time course is a sharp surge that peaks within minutes rather than a gradual hour-long rise. Unexpected attacks distinguish panic disorder from panic attacks that are consistently cued by a specific phobic or social situation. A useful memory link is that panic disorder needs surprise: there must be at least some unprovoked attacks. Panic attacks themselves can occur across several psychiatric and medical conditions.'),
  ('2013-226', 'Advance care planning answers two related questions: what would the patient want, and who should speak for the patient? A living will addresses the first question by recording treatment preferences. A durable power of attorney for health care addresses the second by designating a surrogate decision-maker. DNR and DNI orders may result from these discussions, but they are narrower orders about particular interventions and are not substitutes for the overall advance-directive process.'),
  ('2013-227', 'The ejaculation-delaying effect of serotonergic antidepressants is often an unwanted effect during depression treatment but can be used therapeutically for premature ejaculation. This is an example of using a predictable medication effect for another indication. A useful memory association is that paroxetine is potent for sexual delay. That same effect can be unacceptable to other patients, so treatment must account for sexual goals, tolerability, and the patient’s broader clinical context.'),
  ('2013-228', 'Paraneoplastic neurologic syndromes result from an immune response associated with cancer that also targets nervous-system antigens. Anti-Yo is a classic marker in subacute cerebellar degeneration and directs the evaluation toward an underlying tumor rather than isolated brain imaging alone. The memorable chain is anti-Yo, cerebellar syndrome, and occult cancer search. The neurologic presentation can develop before the associated malignancy is clinically apparent, making timely systemic investigation especially important.'),
  ('2013-229', 'Couples therapy assumes that both partners can participate voluntarily and negotiate safely. Domestic violence is not simply severe relationship conflict: coercive control creates an unequal and potentially dangerous treatment setting. A useful memory principle is that the couple becomes the treatment unit only after each individual can safely speak. Early screening also prevents the clinician from mislabeling fear, silence, or compliance as poor communication or resistance.'),
  ('2013-230', 'Attachment behavior is especially visible when a child is stressed by separation from a familiar caregiver. Secure attachment does not mean that the child never protests or needs reassurance. It means the caregiver functions as a reliable safe base: the child seeks closeness when distressed and returns to exploration after comfort. Remember the sequence: separation may bring distress; reunion brings contact, soothing, and then play.'),
  ('2013-231', 'The relevant distinction is not medical versus psychiatric in an absolute sense; a patient may have both physical illness and excessive health anxiety. The cognitive error to avoid is diagnostic overshadowing, in which a psychiatric label is used to dismiss new symptoms. The care setting matters: in an inpatient medical consultation, clinicians should remain alert to medical causes even when anxiety is prominent or prior testing has been unrevealing.'),
  ('2013-232', 'Brain death is a legal and clinical determination of permanent loss of all brain function, including brainstem function. The apnea test focuses on the medullary respiratory response because carbon dioxide normally stimulates breathing. The key association is absent spontaneous respiratory effort despite an adequate hypercapnic stimulus. Given the gravity of the determination, prerequisites, safety monitoring, and local policy are integral to the assessment.'),
  ('2013-233', 'Derealization concerns the external world; depersonalization concerns feeling detached from oneself. Both can occur during intense anxiety, when perception and attention are altered by severe autonomic arousal. A useful memory pair is: derealization means surroundings feel unreal, whereas depersonalization means the self feels unreal. Panic attacks are episodes; panic disorder additionally requires recurrent unexpected attacks with persistent concern or maladaptive behavioral change.'),
  ('2013-234', 'Nitrous oxide is familiar as an anesthetic and analgesic, which can obscure its harm with repeated exposure. Its relationship to functional B12 disruption produces a memorable pattern of posterior-column and peripheral-nerve dysfunction: sensory loss, impaired balance, and clumsy hands. An apparently psychiatric referral may therefore conceal a toxic or nutritional neurologic disorder, especially when occupational access provides an important exposure clue.'),
  ('2013-235', 'Defense mechanisms are habitual ways of managing conflict, affect, and anxiety. Isolation of affect permits a painful thought to remain conscious while its emotional tone is held at a distance. Intellectualization shifts attention toward concepts and analysis. Both are commonly associated with obsessive-compulsive personality traits, in which control and correctness may manage inner tension. This is distinct from obsessive-compulsive disorder, which is defined by obsessions, compulsions, or both rather than by a pervasive personality style. Reaction formation involves adopting an attitude or behavior opposite to an unacceptable impulse.'),
  ('2013-236', 'Terminology for problematic sexual behavior has changed, and diagnostic classification remains nuanced. The concept tested is the potential value of a recovery community: regular meetings, peer support, accountability, and relapse-prevention structure. This differs from labeling sexual orientation, identity, or consensual variation as pathology. The clinical target is behavior experienced as out of control or causing impairment, harm, or nonconsensual risk—not consensual sexual diversity.'),
  ('2013-237', 'Piaget’s sensorimotor period describes how infants learn through perception and action. Object permanence changes the meaning of absence: an unseen person or object is no longer simply gone. Peek-a-boo turns disappearance and return into a social game that can engage this developing expectation. Centering, egocentrism, and conservation refer to later cognitive phenomena and are less applicable to this infant interaction. The broader developmental lesson is that early cognitive understanding grows through repeated interactions with caregivers and the physical environment before abstract reasoning is established.'),
  ('2013-238', 'Group therapy creates interpersonal learning because members respond to one another, not only to the therapist. Universalization counters the belief that one’s suffering is uniquely shameful or incomprehensible. Contagion is the cautionary counterpart: emotions and behaviors can spread through a group, particularly around self-harm. Remember the pairing as “I am not alone” versus “this may intensify others’ risk.” Both processes may be present in one exchange.'),
  ('2013-239', 'Clozapine’s special status derives from benefit in schizophrenia that has not responded to standard antipsychotic treatment. Its monitoring burden is inseparable from clinical use and contributes to underuse despite its role in refractory illness. A high-yield cue is “two adequate failures, then think clozapine,” while verifying that apparent failures were truly adequate trials and not caused by nonadherence, substance exposure, or an incorrect diagnosis.'),
  ('2013-240', 'Inhalants are defined more by route and volatility than by one pharmacologic mechanism. Their high-yield presentation is an acute central nervous system depressant-like syndrome with neurologic signs and risky behavior. Accessibility is clinically important because household, garage, and school products may be used without usual markers of illicit-drug access. The memory pattern is volatile exposure plus ataxic disinhibition, with potentially serious cardiopulmonary and neurologic complications.'),
  ('2013-241', 'This is an adverse-effect matching question. Thiazide diuretics can lower sodium, whereas older centrally acting agents such as reserpine and methyldopa are memorable for possible depressive effects. Spironolactone avoids thiazide-associated hyponatremia but shifts attention toward potassium and renal monitoring. The memory map is sodium problem: avoid a thiazide; depression history: avoid reserpine and methyldopa. Contemporary antihypertensive selection remains individualized. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2013-242', 'A band-like trunk sensation is a useful localization clue because it suggests a sensory level rather than a distal peripheral neuropathy. When paired with bilateral leg symptoms and bladder dysfunction, it points toward spinal cord disease. MRI is especially useful because it visualizes cord tissue, discs, epidural space, inflammation, and compression. The memorable triad is legs, level, bladder: image the spine urgently.'),
  ('2013-243', 'Schizotypal personality disorder sits on the schizophrenia spectrum rather than merely sharing superficial traits. The name is a useful cue: schizophrenia-like traits that are persistent but generally do not reach the threshold for sustained psychotic disorder. Schizoid means detached and paranoid means suspicious; both can overlap phenomenologically, but schizotypal personality disorder has the strongest classic familial connection to schizophrenia. This framing helps distinguish the keyed concept from plausible neighboring answers during examinations and clinical reasoning.'),
  ('2013-244', 'Validation communicates that an emotion makes sense within the person’s experience; it does not mean agreeing with every interpretation or permitting aggression. Hostility can reflect fear, shame, pain, perceived disrespect, psychosis, intoxication, or loss of control. The memory contrast is simple: validate the feeling, not the harmful action. This preserves dignity and makes later assessment, negotiation, and treatment more possible.'),
  ('2013-245', 'Validity is evidence supporting the interpretation and use of assessment scores, not a single fixed property of a questionnaire. Content validity begins with a practical question: do the items cover what the measure claims to assess? For a syndrome, the items should sample defining and clinically meaningful features rather than merely related phenomena. External comparisons and theoretical correlations can be useful later, but they cannot repair an item set that omits essential elements of the target domain. A useful memory cue is that content validity concerns the contents of the questionnaire itself.'),
  ('2013-246', 'HIV is important in psychiatry because it can affect the central nervous system, increase vulnerability to opportunistic illness, and coexist with psychosocial stressors or treatment effects. The memorable pattern is not that HIV always causes depression, but that depression plus decline warrants a broad differential. Fever and weight loss are systemic clues, while ataxia and memory loss add neurologic concern and urgency.'),
  ('2013-247', 'Body dysmorphic disorder is classified among obsessive-compulsive and related disorders. Patients may repeatedly examine mirrors, camouflage a perceived flaw, compare themselves with others, seek reassurance, or avoid mirrors and social settings altogether. Insight varies widely: a person may recognize that the concern is excessive or may hold the belief with delusional intensity. The degree of insight does not change the central syndrome or the usual SSRI-based treatment approach. A careful assessment also distinguishes appearance preoccupation from an eating disorder, psychosis, or a realistic concern about an observable condition.'),
  ('2013-248', 'The key localization principle is that pain and temperature fibers cross near the center of the spinal cord before ascending on the opposite side. A central cavity therefore injures these fibers before it affects more peripheral tracts. As a syrinx enlarges, it may involve anterior horn cells at that level and later corticospinal pathways. The contrast between impaired pain-temperature sensation and preserved vibration-position sensation is called dissociated sensory loss. It is a high-yield neuroanatomic pattern that should prompt evaluation for a structural cord lesion.'),
  ('2013-249', 'Cultural formulation is not a checklist of beliefs presumed from a person’s demographic background. It is a way to learn how a particular person understands distress, identity, stigma, supports, and healing. Somatic complaints can be meaningful expressions of depression or anxiety, but they still require medical and psychiatric assessment. Beginning with the patient’s own words reduces premature diagnostic closure and helps the clinician distinguish differences in expression from differences in severity. It also makes shared decisions about medication, psychotherapy, family involvement, and other supports more feasible.'),
  ('2013-250', 'Transference is the patient’s experience of the clinician through patterns shaped by important prior relationships. Countertransference refers to the clinician’s emotional reactions within the treatment relationship. Neither phenomenon automatically indicates misconduct; the ethical issue is how the clinician handles it. Consultation creates reflective distance when strong feelings could distort judgment. The treatment frame, including time, place, fees, roles, and boundaries, protects the patient’s ability to work and the clinician’s professional responsibility. Sexual or romantic involvement with a current patient is never an acceptable therapeutic intervention.'),
  ('2013-251', 'MDMA has stimulant-like and hallucinogen-like subjective effects, but its classic neurochemical teaching point is serotonin. Emotional closeness, altered sensory experience, and stimulation involve multiple systems, which can make dopamine seem tempting in a multiple-choice question. The tested distinction is between broad behavioral effects and the neurotransmitter system historically associated with MDMA’s characteristic neurotoxic concern. In clinical practice, presentations are less chemically tidy than board questions because pills sold as ecstasy may contain different substances, variable doses, or multiple agents.'),
  ('2013-252', 'First pass describes the route after gastrointestinal absorption: portal blood reaches the liver before the general circulation. Extensive gut-wall or hepatic metabolism can therefore reduce oral bioavailability. The concept is not synonymous with all drug elimination, because systemic clearance continues after a medication has reached circulation. A useful sequence is gut, portal vein, liver, then systemic circulation. Loss along the early portion of that sequence is presystemic. Routes that bypass portal drainage can reduce first-pass metabolism, although they introduce their own formulation and dosing considerations.'),
  ('2013-253', 'Neural tube formation occurs very early in embryonic development, often before pregnancy is recognized. This timing makes preconception counseling especially important for medications with known teratogenicity. Board questions commonly contrast signature anticonvulsant fetal associations: valproate with neural tube defects, phenytoin with fetal hydantoin findings, and carbamazepine with a smaller but meaningful neural tube defect risk. In practice, fetal medication exposure is not the only concern; maternal illness control, seizure prevention, and medication changes also require careful individualized planning.'),
  ('2013-254', 'The useful diagnostic question is, “What exactly feels dangerous in this setting?” Social anxiety is organized around observation and judgment by other people. Agoraphobia is organized around perceived entrapment, inability to escape, or lack of help during feared symptoms. A crowd can therefore be avoided for entirely different reasons in the two disorders. This distinction prevents clinicians from treating all situational avoidance as agoraphobia or all discomfort in public as social anxiety. It also provides a more precise starting point for treatment planning.'),
  ('2013-255', 'The prefrontal cortex is often summarized as the brain’s executive or braking system, although behavior arises from distributed networks rather than one isolated structure. Adolescence involves an uneven developmental timetable: reward, emotion, and social-salience systems can strongly influence behavior while cognitive-control networks are still becoming more efficient. This mismatch is a useful memory model for why judgment may be especially context-dependent in adolescence. It does not mean adolescents lack all capacity for control; it means that regulation continues to develop and can benefit from external support.'),
  ('2013-256', 'Abstraction is the ability to recognize a general rule, category, or relationship beyond immediate physical details. Similarities questions are a compact bedside method for assessing this ability. “Apple and orange” invites the category fruit, while “a bicycle and a train” may invite transportation. Abstract reasoning can be affected by many psychiatric, neurologic, developmental, and situational factors, so one concrete answer is nonspecific. The item is most useful when interpreted as part of the broader mental status examination and the patient’s baseline functioning.'),
  ('2013-257', 'ADHD commonly co-occurs with psychiatric and developmental conditions, and comorbidity changes both impairment and treatment priorities. Conduct disorder involves repetitive violation of rights or major social rules. Oppositional defiant disorder instead centers on angry, argumentative, and defiant behavior without the same pattern of serious rights violations. The ADHD-conduct disorder combination is a classic prognostic association because it reflects an externalizing trajectory that can persist across developmental stages. Accurate assessment must still consider environmental adversity, developmental level, and other conditions that can contribute to disruptive behavior.'),
  ('2013-258', 'CBT conceptualizes substance use as behavior influenced by cues, expectations, reinforcement, and learned coping patterns. Its practical style is recognizable: identify triggers, examine unhelpful thoughts, practice a skill, and plan for relapse risk. In this setting, emergency planning can mean a preplanned response to craving, social pressure, or another predictable vulnerability rather than only a psychiatric emergency. CBT can be combined with medication, mutual-help participation, family work, and other interventions, depending on the patient’s goals, severity, medical risks, and available supports.'),
  ('2013-259', 'Dementia affects a network rather than only the person with cognitive impairment. As cognition and function decline, family members may become historians, decision-support partners, advocates, and hands-on caregivers. These roles can create burden, grief, resentment, financial strain, and logistical challenges. Family-centered work is therefore more than emotional support: it includes education, practical problem-solving, and planning. The best plan is rarely identical for every family because caregiving capacity, relationships, cultural expectations, resources, and the patient’s needs change over time.'),
  ('2013-260', 'Cluster headache occurs in bouts of recurrent attacks and belongs to the trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias. These disorders pair severe unilateral head pain with cranial autonomic symptoms on the same side, such as tearing, nasal congestion, rhinorrhea, ptosis, or miosis. The behavioral clue is useful: patients with cluster headache are often agitated or restless, whereas migraine commonly drives patients toward darkness, quiet, and immobility. Short attack duration with dramatic ipsilateral autonomic features makes oxygen the classic acute board answer.'),
  ('2013-261', 'Suicide risk is dynamic: acute danger can change with intoxication, agitation, access to lethal means, worsening psychiatric illness, or major stress. Historical factors remain important, and prior attempts are central because they document suicidal behavior rather than thoughts alone. The clinician’s task is not to predict an individual death with certainty. It is to integrate static and changing risk factors into a careful, documented, compassionate plan focused on immediate safety, reduction of modifiable risk, treatment, follow-up, and engagement of appropriate supports.'),
  ('2013-262', 'Intimate-partner violence occurs across sexual orientations, gender identities, cultures, and socioeconomic groups. Shame, fear of outing, financial dependence, threats, and concern that services will be unwelcoming can make disclosure especially difficult for sexual-minority patients. A trauma-informed response emphasizes choice, collaboration, trustworthiness, and safety. A useful memory frame is: first listen, then assess danger, then support the patient’s own informed next steps. The psychiatrist’s role is not to adjudicate the relationship or force a particular decision.'),
  ('2013-263', 'Delirium involves impaired attention and awareness with an acute or fluctuating course. Medication effects and metabolic disturbances are common contributors, especially in older adults. SSRIs can be associated with hyponatremia through inappropriate antidiuretic hormone activity, which may initially present with subtle cognitive or behavioral changes. Severe hyponatremia can cause more serious neurologic symptoms. A normal general examination does not rule out this cause. The practical memory link is that waxing-and-waning mental-status change is a medical syndrome until proven otherwise.'),
  ('2013-264', 'Mentalization is often summarized as “thinking about thinking.” It links observable behavior with the inner world of beliefs, affects, wishes, and intentions while recognizing that these states are partly opaque and can be misread. Under intense attachment threat or affective arousal, people may lose this reflective stance and experience interpretations as concrete facts. The boss’s different “voice” illustrates a gap between an internal representation and the externally observed person. Remember: mentalization asks, “What might be going on in minds?”'),
  ('2013-265', 'Buspirone is commonly taught as a non-benzodiazepine anxiolytic with serotonergic activity and little abuse potential. Unlike benzodiazepines, it does not cause meaningful respiratory depression at therapeutic use, which is particularly relevant in pulmonary vulnerability. It also lacks the rapid calming effect that can make benzodiazepines appealing, so a useful memory pair is “safer breathing, slower benefit.” Beta-blockers pose a separate pulmonary concern because beta blockade can provoke bronchospasm in susceptible patients. Matching medication choice to the anxiety syndrome remains necessary.'),
  ('2013-266', 'The motor homunculus places the lower extremity on the medial surface of the cerebral hemisphere, supplied largely by the ACA. In contrast, lateral cortex—supplied mainly by the MCA—contains disproportionate face and upper-extremity representation. ACA strokes can therefore look behaviorally unusual: medial frontal and anterior cingulate disruption may reduce initiative, yielding abulia or akinetic features. A compact memory aid is “ACA = ankle and apathy”: leg-predominant deficits plus medial-frontal behavioral change.'),
  ('2013-267', 'Monoamine oxidase normally metabolizes monoamines, including tyramine. With MAO inhibition, tyramine can increase norepinephrine release, producing headache, palpitations, and dangerous hypertension. The historic “cheese reaction” is a memorable shorthand, although clinically relevant interactions are not limited to cheese and depend on the MAOI and exposure. Phentolamine blocks alpha receptors, countering the peripheral vasoconstrictive portion of catecholamine excess. Do not confuse this syndrome with serotonin toxicity, which has a different mechanism and treatment priorities.'),
  ('2013-268', 'Avoidance is a powerful maintaining factor in anxiety: it brings immediate relief but prevents corrective learning that a feared situation can be tolerated. CBT uses gradual, planned exposure to reverse this learning pattern while teaching children and families how anxiety works. In children, involving caregivers and matching techniques to developmental level are particularly important. A simple memory link is “mild anxiety learns”: skills practice and exposure give the child chances to learn safety and mastery before adding medication.'),
  ('2013-269', 'ECT intentionally induces a therapeutic generalized seizure under controlled conditions. Medications that affect neuronal excitability, seizure duration, and postictal recovery can alter both efficacy and safety. Lithium has a narrow therapeutic window and is sensitive to renal function, volume status, and drug interactions; its combination with ECT has long been associated with concern for neurotoxicity and confusion. A memorable pairing is “ECT plus lithium: think lingering delirium,” while recognizing that all delirium still merits a broad medical evaluation.'),
  ('2013-270', 'Capacity is a clinical, task-specific determination, whereas competency is generally a legal determination. A surrogate’s role is not to choose according to personal preference but to represent the patient’s known values through substituted judgment when possible. If those values cannot be established, the best-interests standard weighs likely benefits and burdens for the patient. This framework protects autonomy as far as possible even after capacity is lost. Remember: first find the patient’s voice through prior wishes or a surrogate, then protect the patient’s welfare.'),
  ('2013-271', 'The caudate is a basal-ganglia structure that curves alongside the lateral ventricle. Huntington disease is a hereditary neurodegenerative disorder involving corticostriatal circuits; degeneration of the caudate is therefore both anatomically visible and clinically meaningful. Because the caudate borders the frontal horn, its loss makes the ventricular contour conspicuous. A useful visual memory is that the caudate normally “hugs” the frontal horn; when it shrinks, the horn looks enlarged. This connects anatomy to the disorder’s motor and behavioral manifestations.'),
  ('2013-272', 'Panic disorder is often maintained by a feedback loop: normal bodily sensations are interpreted catastrophically, fear rises, sensations intensify, and avoidance prevents corrective learning. CBT interrupts this loop through exposure and new interpretations of sensations. Medication may be highly effective while it is taken, but CBT explicitly rehearses skills the patient can deploy after sessions stop. A memory cue is “CBT builds a toolkit”: its benefit can persist because the patient practices a different response to panic cues.'),
  ('2013-273', 'The word “selective” can misleadingly imply a willful choice. The diagnostic pattern is selective by setting: speech is present in comfortable contexts but inhibited where social anxiety is high. Avoidance brings short-term relief from feared scrutiny, which makes it more likely to continue. This is the same basic learning mechanism seen in many anxiety disorders, though the behavior is specifically failure to speak. Remember: the child is often verbally capable but socially frozen in the feared environment.'),
  ('2013-274', 'Orthostatic hypotension is a blood-pressure drop on standing that may cause lightheadedness, falls, or syncope. TCAs block several receptor systems; alpha-1 adrenergic blockade is especially relevant to postural blood-pressure regulation. Their antimuscarinic effects can also worsen constipation, urinary retention, blurred vision, and cognitive burden. SSRIs act more selectively on serotonin reuptake and generally avoid this receptor-blocking profile. A high-yield memory pair is “TCA: tripping and tachycardia,” reflecting orthostasis and cardiac concerns.'),
  ('2013-275', 'Pain is produced by the nervous system’s processing of bodily signals and is influenced by attention, mood, sleep, autonomic arousal, and prior experience. This does not make functional pain unreal; it explains why centrally acting treatments can help. TCAs have long been used at low doses for several chronic pain syndromes, partly through modulation of descending pain pathways. The term “neuromodulator” is often more useful than “antidepressant” when explaining this role. Remember: low-dose TCA, not opioid escalation, is the classic board association for functional pain.'),
  ('2013-276', 'Acute dystonia results from an abrupt imbalance in dopaminergic and cholinergic signaling within basal-ganglia circuits, often after dopamine receptor blockade. It may present dramatically with torticollis, oculogyric crisis, trismus, or tongue and laryngeal involvement. Anticholinergic agents restore the balance quickly, which is why benztropine is a classic emergency answer. The larynx is the crucial clue: unlike a bothersome limb spasm, laryngeal involvement can threaten ventilation. Think “dystonia = anticholinergic; stridor = emergency.”'),
  ('2013-277', 'The classic elements of negligence are often remembered as duty, breach, causation, and damages. Duty asks whether the defendant had a legal obligation to exercise professional care toward this particular person. A physician-patient relationship commonly creates that obligation, though duties can arise in other circumstances. General health education is not automatically individualized medical care. This distinction protects both patients and clinicians by clarifying when professional responsibility begins. Memory cue: before asking whether care was negligent, ask, “Was care owed to this person?”'),
  ('2013-278', 'Opioids suppress noradrenergic activity during intoxication; after abrupt cessation, rebound noradrenergic activation contributes to the characteristic autonomic and gastrointestinal syndrome. “Flu-like but with big pupils and bowel symptoms” is a useful broad memory image for opioid withdrawal. The mnemonic “wet, wide, and writhing” can help recall lacrimation or rhinorrhea, mydriasis, and myalgias or restlessness. Although intensely distressing, uncomplicated opioid withdrawal differs from alcohol and benzodiazepine withdrawal in its usual medical lethality profile.'),
  ('2013-279', 'Vitamin B12 is required for pathways important to myelin maintenance and cellular metabolism. The word “combined” in subacute combined degeneration is a useful memory link: both spinal-cord tracts and peripheral nerves may be affected. Posterior-column involvement explains loss of proprioception and vibration, while corticospinal involvement may yield upper-motor-neuron signs. Neuropsychiatric symptoms matter clinically because they can precede, accompany, or persist after hematologic abnormalities when deficiency is not recognized and treated promptly.'),
  ('2013-280', 'Sleep paralysis is a brief mismatch between waking awareness and the muscle atonia normally associated with REM sleep. The sensed intruder is a memorable example of dream imagery and fear intruding into partial wakefulness. Stressful schedules increase episodes by fragmenting sleep and altering REM timing. The practical sequence is straightforward: insufficient sleep increases vulnerability, while regular and sufficient sleep is the first corrective intervention. Narcolepsy should be considered when paralysis occurs with excessive daytime sleepiness or cataplexy.'),
  ('2013-281', 'NAC replenishes protective hepatic pathways and limits injury from acetaminophen’s reactive metabolite. It is most effective when begun early but may remain beneficial in delayed clinically important toxicity. The Rumack–Matthew nomogram applies only when the timing of a single acute ingestion is known, so an unknown time removes its essential reference point. This category C record corrects an earlier B/C multi-key: treatment with NAC is retained, while the claim that all nonpregnant adults require intravenous administration is rejected.'),
  ('2013-282', 'Absence seizures are generalized network events in which awareness briefly disconnects without a prolonged postictal state. The EEG anchor is a generalized spike followed by a wave, repeating rhythmically. “Absence” describes the interruption in responsiveness, which can resemble ordinary inattention. Children may otherwise appear well between episodes, so the disorder is easily missed unless caregivers and teachers describe the events precisely. Hyperventilation can help provoke typical absence seizures during an appropriately supervised evaluation.'),
  ('2013-283', 'Professional boundaries protect the asymmetry of the treatment relationship: the patient seeks care, while the clinician has power, knowledge, and fiduciary responsibilities. Boundary crossings are departures from usual practice that may sometimes be appropriate, such as flexible scheduling in an emergency or culturally sensitive handling of a modest gift. Boundary violations are harmful or exploitative departures, especially those involving sexual, financial, or self-serving interests. Financial entanglement is particularly clear because it gives the clinician a stake in the patient beyond providing care and can undermine trust.'),
  ('2013-284', 'Bupropion has a clinically important, dose-related seizure risk and is contraindicated in patients with a seizure disorder under current labeling. Remaining seizure-free during treatment does not establish that continued exposure is safe. This record’s key was corrected from the historical continuation rationale to D because contemporary safety guidance takes precedence. The listed answer says to taper and monitor, but real-world care should also include a planned transition to another effective treatment, with monitoring for depressive relapse. Medication choice remains individualized, yet an insurer’s recommendation does not replace the psychiatrist’s supervised risk assessment and transition plan.'),
  ('2013-285', 'Inhalants are chemically diverse, so toxic effects vary by product, route, and duration of exposure. Many volatile compounds are highly lipid soluble, helping explain their prominent central and peripheral nervous-system effects. A useful memory pairing is “solvents and tubules”: selected volatile-solvent exposures can disrupt renal tubular function, while chronic neurotoxicity can produce cognitive, gait, and neuropathic findings. Marked CPK elevation also requires evaluation for rhabdomyolysis and its renal and electrolyte complications.'),
  ('2013-286', 'Myoclonus refers to sudden, brief muscle jerks. In juvenile myoclonic epilepsy, the jerks commonly cluster after awakening, making sleep disruption a memorable trigger. The EEG mnemonic is “polyspikes for myoclonic jerks”: several rapid spikes precede the slow-wave component. Onset is usually in adolescence, but diagnosis can be delayed because patients consider the jerks ordinary clumsiness. Generalized tonic-clonic seizures may emerge after sleep loss or missed medication, further obscuring the earlier myoclonic history.'),
  ('2013-287', 'Platelets do not synthesize serotonin efficiently; they take it up from plasma through the serotonin transporter. SSRIs block that transporter, reducing platelet serotonin stores over time. This creates a useful bridge between psychopharmacology and consultation psychiatry: a medication prescribed for mood can influence hemostasis. The effect becomes more clinically important when antithrombotic medicines, NSAIDs, prior bleeding, or perioperative conditions are also present.'),
  ('2013-288', 'Professional ethics often focus on process as much as outcome. A treatment contract establishes predictable boundaries and reduces misunderstandings about money, time, and availability. In psychotherapy, missed sessions may have clinical meaning, but an administrative policy should not be wielded as an interpretation or punishment. The useful memory anchor is “contract first, charge second”: disclosure and agreement make a policy more ethically defensible than a surprise fee.'),
  ('2013-289', 'Duty-to-warn and duty-to-protect rules developed from cases involving foreseeable violence toward identifiable people, but no single national procedure governs every clinician. Some jurisdictions mandate particular steps, others permit disclosure, and requirements may differ regarding police, potential victims, hospitalization, or other reasonable protections. This category B item retains police notification as the historical best-listed answer while preserving that the legally required recipient and action are jurisdiction-dependent. Local statutes, case law, professional standards, institutional policy, and the immediacy of danger must guide practice.'),
  ('2013-290', 'Mandated-reporting laws are designed to trigger a child-protection assessment when professionals have reasonable suspicion, not to require clinicians to prove abuse. The threshold is intentionally lower than the criminal standard of proof. “Report suspicion, do not investigate to certainty” is a useful rule. Psychotherapy confidentiality is fundamental, but it has recognized exceptions when a child may be at risk of harm. Clinicians should know their jurisdiction’s reporting process and emergency procedures.'),
  ('2013-291', 'Hypnosis is best understood as focused attention, absorption, and increased responsiveness to therapeutic suggestion. Hypnotizability varies between individuals and helps explain why response is not uniform. A useful memory cue is “focus to lower arousal”: hypnotic techniques can redirect attention, alter the experience of pain, and support calming. It does not replace medical stabilization, trauma-informed assessment, or indicated psychotherapy. Clear consent and appropriately trained use are especially important after trauma.'),
  ('2013-292', 'Sickle cell disease results from hemoglobin polymerization under deoxygenated conditions, producing red-cell deformation, hemolysis, and vaso-occlusion. The brain is vulnerable to both large-vessel vasculopathy and ischemic injury. The memory connection is “pain crises plus strokes”: recurrent abdominal or skeletal pain can coexist with cerebrovascular complications, including deficits that fluctuate or recur. Large-vessel stenosis can lead to collateral circulation and substantial stroke risk in children and young adults.'),
  ('2013-293', 'Older adults often have less total body water and more medical conditions and medications, factors that can amplify alcohol’s effects. Later-life alcohol problems may be longstanding or may emerge after bereavement, pain, retirement, or isolation. A useful exam memory pair is “less common, more consequential”: prevalence decreases, but vulnerability to injury, cognitive effects, and medication interactions rises. Screening should not be omitted merely because an older adult reports a relatively small quantity of alcohol.'),
  ('2013-294', 'The cerebellum coordinates balance, eye movements, and movement timing. Inflammation after infection can transiently disrupt these functions, yielding the memorable triad of ataxia, nystagmus, and dysarthria. The syndrome may be called acute cerebellar ataxia or acute cerebellitis depending on clinical context and severity. The diagnostic pattern is rapid onset after infection rather than slowly progressive hereditary degeneration. Toxic ingestion and posterior-fossa pathology remain important initial exclusions in any child with sudden ataxia.'),
  ('2013-295', 'A diagnostic system can emphasize reliability, meaning different clinicians reach similar conclusions, or validity, meaning a category maps onto a distinct underlying condition. DSM-III strongly emphasized reliability through explicit descriptive criteria and a multiaxial framework. The durable memory link is “DSM-III equals operationalization”: it translated clinical syndromes into shared symptom-based rules that could be studied and communicated consistently. Its historical shift shaped research and clinical documentation well beyond its later revisions.'),
  ('2013-296', 'Psychodynamic groups develop through interacting processes rather than rigidly isolated stages. Engagement establishes safety and affiliation through recognition of shared experiences. Differentiation emerges as members seek distinct roles, status, or relationships with the therapist, often producing competition or tension. Both may be visible during the same session, especially as an early group begins moving beyond simple cohesion. The combined A/B key was explicitly adjudicated and retained because the stem deliberately contains evidence of both stages, accurately describing an overlapping developmental transition.'),
  ('2013-297', 'Normal sleep alternates between non-REM and REM stages across the night, with REM periods becoming longer later in the sleep period. Alcohol is a central nervous system depressant, but sedation does not equal normal sleep architecture. Its early effects suppress REM sleep; as alcohol levels fall, relative physiologic activation can interrupt sleep and contribute to REM-related rebound. This explains why people may fall asleep quickly after drinking yet still experience unrefreshing, fragmented sleep.'),
  ('2013-298', 'Countertransference originally referred narrowly to therapist reactions arising from personal unconscious conflicts; contemporary use more broadly includes the clinician’s emotional responses within the therapeutic relationship. Such reactions are not inherently harmful. When recognized and reflected on, they can provide useful information about the interaction. Problems arise when they are enacted through rescuing, overcontrol, withdrawal, or a need to produce a particular outcome. A clinician’s personal history can make a patient’s behavior especially emotionally charged. Reflection and supervision are important safeguards against acting from that urgency.'),
  ('2013-299', 'Psychiatric ethics balances obligations to an individual patient with obligations to others who may be harmed. On an inpatient unit, peers can be especially vulnerable because they have limited ability to leave or avoid a dangerous patient. A useful distinction is the primary purpose of an intervention: therapeutic actions aim to benefit the recipient, whereas protective actions aim to prevent harm to others. Legal requirements and hospital policies influence how a response is carried out, but they do not replace this ethical analysis.'),
  ('2013-300', 'Causal inference requires that a proposed cause occur before its proposed effect. Cohort studies begin by identifying exposure status and then observe later outcomes, making temporality their major advantage. Cross-sectional studies provide a snapshot and often cannot determine time order. Ecological studies use group-level rather than individual-level data, so population patterns may not apply to individuals. Randomized trials can strengthen causal inference in some circumstances, but they are not listed and may be unethical for a potentially harmful exposure.')
insert into flashcards (question_id, cloze_text, extra) values
  ('2009-1', 'In children and adolescents with ADHD, {{c1::anxiety disorders}} and {{c2::learning disorders}} are especially important common comorbidities to assess.', 'Anxiety disorder and learning disorder are common in pediatric ADHD and can independently worsen functioning. Persistent school difficulty should not automatically be attributed to untreated ADHD.'),
  ('2009-2', 'Extensive synaptic pruning accompanied by declining cerebral metabolism and EEG amplitude is characteristic of {{c1::adolescence}}.', 'Adolescence features major synaptic pruning and network refinement. The associated metabolic and EEG reductions reflect maturation, not ordinary neurodegeneration.'),
  ('2009-3', 'Borderline personality disorder may feature {{c1::transient, stress-related paranoid ideation or severe dissociative symptoms}}.', 'Borderline personality disorder can include brief stress-related psychotic-like or dissociative symptoms. These symptoms are usually transient rather than sustained primary psychosis.'),
  ('2009-4', '{{c1::Metoclopramide}} can cause acute dystonia with torticollis and tongue protrusion because of {{c2::dopamine D2 blockade}}.', 'Metoclopramide can produce acute dystonia because it blocks central dopamine receptors. Torticollis and tongue protrusion are classic extrapyramidal manifestations.'),
  ('2009-5', 'In Thomas and Chess’s framework, a difficult temperament commonly includes {{c1::intense expressions of mood}}.', 'Intense expressions of mood characterize the classic difficult temperament. The term describes reactivity and adaptation, not intelligence or moral character.'),
  ('2009-6', 'Among listed risk factors, {{c1::pervasive insomnia}} is particularly concerning for emergent suicidality because it is a dynamic change in clinical state.', 'Pervasive insomnia is a proximal, potentially modifiable marker of worsening suicide risk. It should prompt direct reassessment rather than serve as a stand-alone prediction rule.'),
  ('2009-7', 'Haloperidol-induced acute laryngeal dystonia requires airway management and rapid treatment with parenteral {{c1::benztropine}}.', 'Benztropine rapidly treats acute dystonia caused by dopamine blockade. Laryngeal involvement is an airway emergency, not routine akathisia or parkinsonism.'),
  ('2009-8', 'The MMPI-2 is used primarily for {{c1::identifying broad areas of psychopathologic and personality functioning}}.', 'Identifying major areas of psychopathologic functioning is the MMPI-2’s central role. It is not a substitute for neuropsychological testing or a complete clinical evaluation.'),
  ('2009-9', '{{c1::Valproate}} is an effective treatment for absence seizures and is particularly useful when other generalized seizure types coexist.', 'Valproate adequately treats absence seizures and provides broad generalized-seizure coverage. Ethosuximide is generally preferred for pure childhood absence epilepsy when appropriate.'),
  ('2009-10', 'The physician-patient relationship is fiduciary because it is fundamentally {{c1::based on trust}}.', 'Based on trust is the defining feature of a fiduciary relationship. That trust obligates the physician to use professional expertise for the patient’s welfare.'),
  ('2009-11', 'The primary goal of supportive psychotherapy is to {{c1::stabilize functioning and strengthen adaptive defenses}}.', 'Supportive psychotherapy focuses on functioning, coping, and adaptive defenses. Emotional support is a technique, not the full therapeutic aim.'),
  ('2009-12', 'Within the historical psychodynamic spectrum of histrionic personality, {{c1::subtly appealing seductiveness}} suggests relatively higher-level organization.', 'The patient exhibits a subtly appealing seductiveness, reflecting greater modulation and social nuance. The behavior’s quality, not seductiveness alone, distinguishes the historical higher-level formulation.'),
  ('2009-13', 'Vascular dementia commonly includes subcortical cerebrovascular injury involving the {{c1::basal ganglia}} and deep white matter.', 'Basal ganglia injury from lacunes or small-vessel disease can disrupt frontal-subcortical circuits. This often produces executive slowing rather than a purely amnestic syndrome.'),
  ('2009-14', 'Axial rigidity, exaggerated lumbar lordosis, stimulus-triggered spasms, and anti-GAD antibodies indicate {{c1::stiff-person syndrome}}.', 'Stiff-person syndrome produces persistent axial and proximal rigidity with painful spasms. Anti-GAD antibodies and continuous motor-unit activity support the diagnosis.'),
  ('2009-15', 'The {{c1::General Health Questionnaire}} is a self-administered screening instrument commonly used in epidemiologic studies of psychiatric distress.', 'General Health Questionnaire is efficient for subject-completed population screening. It identifies probable distress but does not replace a diagnostic interview.'),
  ('2009-16', 'The number needed to treat is the number of patients who must receive an intervention to produce {{c1::one additional positive outcome}} compared with a control.', 'Produce one additional positive outcome is the defining NNT concept. NNT is based on absolute, not merely relative, risk difference.'),
  ('2009-17', 'In acute assessment of self-harm, {{c1::recent alcohol abuse}} raises concern for admission because it increases impulsivity and suicide risk.', 'Recent alcohol abuse is the strongest admission-supporting factor here. It increases disinhibition, may impair assessment, and can heighten acute self-harm risk.'),
  ('2009-18', 'The most commonly consumed mood-altering substance worldwide is {{c1::caffeine}}.', 'Caffeine is the world’s most widely consumed psychoactive substance. Its everyday presence in beverages can make its stimulant effects easy to overlook.'),
  ('2009-19', 'The historical diagnosis of {{c1::Asperger disorder}} described social impairment and restricted interests without significant early language or cognitive delay.', 'Asperger disorder fits the DSM-IV-era pattern of pedantic speech, restricted interests, and impaired reciprocity with normal early language and cognition. Current practice classifies this presentation within autism spectrum disorder.'),
  ('2009-20', 'In bipolar disorder, {{c1::lithium}} is the mood stabilizer most associated with reduced {{c2::suicide risk}}.', 'Lithium carbonate is correct because it has the strongest established anti-suicidal association among bipolar maintenance treatments.'),
  ('2009-21', 'Early visual hallucinations, parkinsonism, fluctuating cognition, and neuroleptic sensitivity suggest {{c1::Lewy body disease}}.', 'Lewy body disease is correct because visual hallucinations and marked sensitivity to risperidone are characteristic clues.'),
  ('2009-22', 'Markedly episodic OCD symptoms that vary with mood episodes are more associated with {{c1::bipolar disorder}}.', 'Bipolar disorder is correct because obsessive-compulsive symptoms may fluctuate episodically with mood-state changes.'),
  ('2009-23', 'A study that selects participants by outcome and then compares prior exposures is a {{c1::case-control study}}.', 'Case-control studies begin with cases and controls defined by outcome status. They then compare prior exposure between the groups.'),
  ('2009-24', 'For adults with schizophrenia and recurrent suicidal behavior, {{c1::clozapine}} is the antipsychotic known to reduce suicide risk.', 'Clozapine is the schizophrenia-specific choice for reducing recurrent suicidal behavior. Its use requires careful monitoring for serious adverse effects.'),
  ('2009-25', 'In an otherwise typically developing preschool child, an {{c1::imaginary friend}} is generally {{c2::a normal and common phenomenon}}.', 'Fairly common and normal is correct because imaginary companions are typical preschool imaginative play.'),
  ('2009-26', 'When a drug-associated cue such as a needle triggers craving, the learning mechanism is {{c1::classical conditioning}}.', 'Classical conditioning is correct because the needle has become a conditioned cue for heroin-related craving.'),
  ('2009-27', '{{c1::Amphetamine intoxication}} can present with agitation, paranoia, and ideas of reference.', 'Amphetamine intoxication commonly causes agitation and paranoid psychosis. Ideas of reference, such as messages from computers, fit this presentation.'),
  ('2009-28', 'In Parkinson disease, new pathological gambling or hypersexuality is classically associated with the dopamine agonist {{c1::pramipexole}}.', 'D — Pramipexole is the corrected answer after invalidation of the original all-credit key. Dopamine agonists can cause impulse-control disorders.'),
  ('2009-29', 'Severe alcohol withdrawal with delirium, hallucinations, agitation, and possible seizures is {{c1::delirium tremens}}.', 'Delirium tremens is correct because the postoperative delirium, hallucinations, and seizure fit severe alcohol withdrawal.'),
  ('2009-30', 'Bingeing and purging can cause electrolyte-related {{c1::QT and T-wave changes}} on ECG.', 'QT and T-wave changes are correct because purging-related electrolyte disturbances can alter cardiac repolarization.'),
  ('2009-31', '{{c1::Eugen Bleuler}} first used the term {{c2::schizophrenia}}.', 'Eugen Bleuler coined the term schizophrenia. Kraepelin is associated with the earlier dementia praecox construct.'),
  ('2009-32', 'Asking what a patient ate for breakfast yesterday tests {{c1::recent memory}}.', 'Recent memory is correct because it assesses recall of information from the recent past, such as yesterday’s breakfast.'),
  ('2009-33', 'With early partial improvement after starting an SSRI, the usual next step is to {{c1::continue the current regimen long enough for an adequate trial}}.', 'Continue citalopram is correct because three weeks with partial improvement is generally too early to declare treatment failure.'),
  ('2009-34', 'Preschool vocabulary and pre-literacy skills are most supported by {{c1::responsive talk directed toward the child}}.', 'Amount of talk directed toward the child is correct because direct, responsive language exposure promotes early language skills.'),
  ('2009-35', 'The psychotherapy factor most consistently associated with outcome is the {{c1::strength of the therapeutic alliance}}.', 'Strength of the therapeutic alliance is correct because a collaborative bond around goals and tasks consistently predicts psychotherapy outcome.'),
  ('2009-36', 'For severe mental illness with recurrent hospitalization, homelessness, and poor outpatient engagement, use {{c1::assertive community treatment}}.', 'Assertive community treatment is correct because it provides intensive outreach and coordinated care for highly disengaged patients with severe mental illness.'),
  ('2009-37', '{{c1::Alogia}} is a negative symptom marked by reduced spontaneous speech and diminished verbal productivity.', 'A — alogia is the source-verified answer, although the quoted wording is an atypical illustration. Alogia specifically means poverty of speech.'),
  ('2009-38', 'The most prevalent male sexual dysfunction in classic board-style comparisons is {{c1::premature ejaculation}}.', 'Premature ejaculation is correct because it is traditionally considered the most prevalent male sexual dysfunction.'),
  ('2009-39', '{{c1::Progressive supranuclear palsy}} is suggested by early unexplained falls, dysphagia, and frontal-behavioral change.', 'Progressive supranuclear palsy causes early postural instability and falls. Dysphagia and executive or personality changes support this atypical parkinsonian syndrome.'),
  ('2009-40', '{{c1::Oxytocin}} is classically considered critical for maternal-infant bonding behavior in animal models.', 'Oxytocin is the correct answer because of its role in maternal affiliative and caregiving behavior. Estrogen and progesterone are less direct answers.'),
  ('2009-41', '{{c1::Charles Bonnet syndrome}} causes complex visual hallucinations with preserved insight in people with significant visual impairment.', 'Charles Bonnet syndrome is a visual-release hallucinosis associated with sensory loss. Cognition and insight are generally preserved.'),
  ('2009-42', 'Activated charcoal is ineffective after {{c1::lithium}} overdose because lithium is not adsorbed.', 'Lithium is not bound by activated charcoal. Management uses assessment, serial levels, supportive care, and sometimes dialysis.'),
  ('2009-43', '{{c1::Amnestic disorder}} can cause absent recall of a documented episode despite otherwise preserved baseline cognition.', 'Amnestic disorder explains the absent recall of the event. Normal EEG and no persistent delirious or psychotic findings make the alternatives less likely.'),
  ('2009-44', 'In older descriptive teaching, hallucination-like experiences in conversion disorder may have a {{c1::childish, fantastic quality}}.', 'Having a childish, fantastic quality is the keyed historical description. It is not a stand-alone diagnostic test for conversion disorder.'),
  ('2009-45', 'For the historical OBRA question, nursing-home psychotropics require {{c1::a dose-reduction or discontinuation attempt at least every 3 months}}, unless contraindicated.', 'Attempt to decrease or stop the medication at least every 3 months is the keyed historical answer. Current regulatory schedules should be checked separately.'),
  ('2009-46', 'Under this exam-era framing, {{c1::naltrexone}} is the best-listed medication associated with baseline and early follow-up liver testing, although modern schedules are individualized.', 'B — Naltrexone is the corrected best-listed answer. The specified eight-week schedule is exam-era framing, not a universal rule.'),
  ('2009-47', 'A young child saying that a deceased parent watches over them may represent {{c1::normal grief}} rather than psychopathology.', 'Normal grief can include symbolic or spiritual statements about a deceased parent. The vignette does not establish PTSD or separation anxiety disorder.'),
  ('2009-48', 'A {{c1::double bind}} gives a person conflicting relational messages such that either response risks disapproval or guilt.', 'A double bind pairs an overt instruction with a conflicting relational message. Here, college is encouraged but framed as harming the family.'),
  ('2009-49', '{{c1::Cocaine withdrawal}} commonly causes dysphoria, fatigue, hypersomnia, vivid dreams, and increased appetite.', 'Cocaine produces a characteristic post-stimulant crash. Hypersomnia and increased appetite are key clues.'),
  ('2009-50', '{{c1::Fluoxetine}}, an SSRI, is a first-line pharmacologic treatment for {{c2::panic disorder}}.', 'Fluoxetine is an SSRI and an appropriate first-line medication for panic disorder. CBT is also first-line and can be combined with an SSRI.'),
  ('2009-51', '{{c1::Sumatriptan}} is an effective acute abortive treatment for {{c2::migraine}}.', 'Sumatriptan is a triptan used to abort an acute migraine. Propranolol, valproate, and topiramate are primarily preventive choices.'),
  ('2009-52', '{{c1::Lifetime prevalence}} is the proportion assessed at one time who have had a disorder at any time in their lives.', 'Lifetime prevalence means ever having the disorder by assessment. It differs from point and period prevalence.'),
  ('2009-53', '{{c1::Lithium}} introduced routine serum-level monitoring in psychiatry because it has a narrow therapeutic index.', 'Lithium requires serum monitoring because levels shift with renal function, hydration, and interactions. Its narrow therapeutic index makes this essential.'),
  ('2009-54', 'A lesion of the {{c1::superior cervical ganglion}} can cause ipsilateral {{c2::Horner syndrome}} with miosis and mild ptosis.', 'Superior cervical ganglion injury produces an ipsilateral Horner pattern. It differs from third-nerve palsy, which often produces mydriasis.'),
  ('2009-55', 'When responding to a delusion, {{c1::validate distress without endorsing or directly confronting the belief}}.', 'Empathic, noncollusive communication preserves the therapeutic alliance. Do not investigate or reinforce the delusional premise.'),
  ('2009-56', '{{c1::Hippocrates}} described illness as resulting from imbalance among the four humors.', 'Hippocrates is associated with the four-humor theory. Melancholia retains the historical association with black bile.'),
  ('2009-57', 'Chronic excessive {{c1::pyridoxine (vitamin B6)}} exposure can cause sensory neuropathy with burning dysesthesias.', 'E — Burning sensations is the inferred best answer for pyridoxine toxicity. The stem’s vitamin label was corrected after an OCR issue, and the official key page is absent.'),
  ('2009-58', '{{c1::Carl Jung}} introduced introversion and extraversion as personality orientations.', 'Carl Jung is associated with introversion and extraversion. These constructs describe personality orientation rather than psychiatric illness.'),
  ('2009-59', '{{c1::Cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT)}} is an effective psychotherapy for {{c2::bulimia nervosa}}.', 'Cognitive-behavioral therapy is effective for bulimia nervosa. DBT was not developed specifically for bulimia nervosa.'),
  ('2009-60', 'In forensic psychiatry, {{c1::a contingency fee based on a plaintiff’s award}} is unethical because compensation must be {{c2::independent of the case outcome}}.', 'D — Percentage of plaintiff’s award is unethical because it makes the expert’s compensation contingent on the litigation outcome.'),
  ('2009-61', 'When a therapist’s attention repeatedly wanders in session, the best immediate step is to {{c1::examine the therapist’s own thoughts and feelings about the patient}}.', 'Examine the therapist''s own reactions first to understand possible countertransference. Immediate interpretation or ending the session would be premature.'),
  ('2009-62', 'The {{c1::Wide Range Achievement Test (WRAT)}} screens {{c2::academic achievement skills}} such as reading, spelling, and arithmetic.', 'C — Wide Range Achievement Test (WRAT) is useful for screening core academic achievement skills in a school-age child.'),
  ('2009-63', 'Severe lithium toxicity may cause persistent {{c1::cerebellar}} dysfunction, producing {{c2::ataxia and dysarthria}}.', 'Cerebellum — lithium neurotoxicity is classically associated with tremor, dysarthria, and ataxia. Severe poisoning can leave residual cerebellar deficits.'),
  ('2009-64', 'Abrupt withdrawal from {{c1::benzodiazepines such as lorazepam}} can cause {{c2::generalized seizures with anxiety and tremor}}.', 'D — Lorazepam withdrawal can produce severe anxiety, insomnia, tremor, and generalized tonic-clonic seizures.'),
  ('2009-65', 'The ECT adverse effect that most commonly limits its use is {{c1::cognitive impairment, particularly memory disturbance}}.', 'Cognitive impairment — transient confusion and memory disturbance are the major tolerability limitations of ECT. Other adverse effects are usually less limiting or more readily mitigated.'),
  ('2009-66', '{{c1::Shy-Drager syndrome (multiple system atrophy with autonomic failure)}} causes orthostatic hypotension with {{c2::urinary and other autonomic dysfunction}}.', 'C — Shy-Drager syndrome is the historical term for multiple system atrophy with prominent autonomic failure.'),
  ('2009-67', '{{c1::Meperidine}} can cause seizures during active use because {{c2::normeperidine}} is neurotoxic.', 'C — Meperidine can produce non-withdrawal seizures through accumulation of neurotoxic normeperidine.'),
  ('2009-68', '{{c1::Gamma hydroxybutyrate (GHB)}} is associated with increased {{c2::episodic growth-hormone secretion}}.', 'D — Gamma hydroxybutyrate is the keyed compound associated with episodic growth-hormone secretion.'),
  ('2009-69', 'Under the historical DSM-IV bereavement exclusion, symptoms generally had to persist {{c1::more than 2 months}} before diagnosing {{c2::major depression}} without severe qualifying features.', 'C — 2 months was the historical DSM-IV bereavement-exclusion threshold when severe qualifying features were absent.'),
  ('2009-70', 'The most important initial clinical assessment for early or moderate dementia is a {{c1::mental status examination}} assessing {{c2::cognition and related mental functions}}.', 'B — Mental status examination is the core clinical assessment for early and moderate dementia.'),
  ('2009-71', 'Recurrent episodes of extreme hypersomnia with hyperphagia and confusion in an adolescent suggest {{c1::Kleine-Levin syndrome}}.', 'Kleine-Levin syndrome — episodic hypersomnia, hyperphagia, and cognitive or behavioral change are its classic pattern. Normal sleep cycles may be prolonged rather than disorganized.'),
  ('2009-72', '{{c1::Fluoxetine}} is the classic first-line medication treatment for {{c2::adolescents with major depressive disorder}}.', 'D — Fluoxetine is the best-established first-line antidepressant medication for adolescent major depression.'),
  ('2009-73', 'Reliably knowing the day and year best fits {{c1::early school age, around 6 years}}.', 'C — 6 years old best fits the early school-age ability to know the day and year.'),
  ('2009-74', 'The {{c1::Abnormal Involuntary Movement Scale (AIMS)}} includes a rating of the patient’s {{c2::awareness of abnormal movements}}.', 'D — Abnormal Involuntary Movement Scale (AIMS) rates abnormal movements and the patient’s awareness of them.'),
  ('2009-75', 'The most common reason for early SSRI discontinuation is {{c1::gastrointestinal side effects, especially nausea}}.', 'Gastrointestinal side effects — nausea and related symptoms often occur immediately after starting an SSRI. They may lead to discontinuation before therapeutic benefit is apparent.'),
  ('2009-76', 'In a nursing-home resident with dementia and acute confusion, the keyed next test is {{c1::urinalysis}}, interpreted in clinical context.', 'Obtain urinalysis — acute agitation and confusion in dementia suggest superimposed delirium. Urine findings alone do not prove symptomatic urinary infection.'),
  ('2009-77', 'First-line psychotherapy for OCD is {{c1::CBT with exposure and response prevention (ERP)}}, which targets {{c2::compulsive rituals}}.', 'B — Cognitive-behavioral therapy, particularly ERP, is a safe and effective first-line treatment for OCD.'),
  ('2009-78', 'When an incapacitated patient has a durable power of attorney for health care, treatment decisions are made by the {{c1::assigned health-care surrogate}}.', 'Assigned decision maker — a valid durable health-care power of attorney identifies the authorized surrogate. This appointment generally takes priority over default family hierarchy.'),
  ('2009-79', '{{c1::Stopping cigarette smoking}} can increase haloperidol exposure and precipitate {{c2::extrapyramidal symptoms}}.', 'B — Cessation of smoking can raise haloperidol exposure and trigger acute extrapyramidal symptoms.'),
  ('2009-80', '{{c1::Interpersonal psychotherapy (IPT)}} explicitly gives a depression diagnosis and assigns a temporary {{c2::sick role}}.', 'B — Interpersonal psychotherapy uses the formal diagnosis and a temporary sick role as part of its treatment frame.'),
  ('2009-81', '{{c1::Anticholinergic delirium}} presents with agitation and hallucinations plus {{c2::dry skin, mydriasis, and constipation}}.', 'C — Anticholinergic delirium causes central delirium with peripheral antimuscarinic signs such as dry skin, mydriasis, and constipation.'),
  ('2009-82', 'For medication-refractory disabling essential tremor, the usual DBS target is the {{c1::ventral intermediate nucleus of the thalamus}}.', 'D — Ventral intermediate thalamus is correct because VIM stimulation modulates cerebellothalamocortical tremor circuitry.'),
  ('2009-83', 'When an insurer requests discharge but inpatient care remains medically necessary for safety, the physician should {{c1::continue hospitalization and document the clinical rationale}}.', 'B — Keep the patient in the hospital as long as medically necessary. Coverage pressure does not supersede clinical safety obligations.'),
  ('2009-84', 'Among trauma-exposure variables, {{c1::greater severity and direct proximity to the trauma}} most strongly increase PTSD risk.', 'B — Severity and proximity of trauma is correct because direct, high-intensity exposure most strongly raises PTSD risk.'),
  ('2009-85', 'In suspected dementia with Lewy bodies, {{c1::risperidone}} may markedly worsen parkinsonism because of dopamine blockade.', 'A — Risperidone is correct because dopamine-blocking antipsychotics can cause severe neuroleptic sensitivity in Lewy body dementia.'),
  ('2009-86', 'A key suicide-prevention intervention for adolescents is {{c1::reducing access to firearms and other lethal means}}.', 'Reduce availability of firearms in the home — limiting access to a highly lethal method can protect patients during an acute suicidal crisis. It complements, rather than replaces, assessment and treatment.'),
  ('2009-87', 'The SSRI boxed warning for children and adolescents concerns increased risk of {{c1::suicidal ideation and behavior}}, not completed suicide.', 'B — Suicidal ideation is correct because the warning concerns emergent suicidal thoughts and behaviors relative to placebo.'),
  ('2009-88', 'Bereavement with pervasive guilt, depressive symptoms, and recurrent wishes to be dead warrants {{c1::active depression treatment and explicit suicide-risk assessment}}.', 'C — Start antidepressant medication and psychotherapy is the inferred best answer because the official key page is absent. Safety assessment remains mandatory.'),
  ('2009-89', 'Peek-a-boo most classically demonstrates {{c1::object permanence}}: understanding that a hidden person or object still exists.', 'Object permanence — the child recognizes that the hidden caregiver has not disappeared from existence. Peek-a-boo uses disappearance and return to evoke that concept.'),
  ('2009-90', 'Amphetamine euphoria is mediated chiefly by {{c1::dopamine release in the nucleus accumbens}}.', 'A — Dopamine release in the nucleus accumbens is correct because mesolimbic dopamine signaling mediates stimulant reward and euphoria.'),
  ('2009-91', 'In group therapy, reality testing one’s perceptions against other members’ feedback is called {{c1::consensual validation}}.', 'A — Consensual validation is correct because members use shared feedback to test and revise interpersonal perceptions.'),
  ('2009-92', 'Combining an SSRI with {{c1::tramadol}} can precipitate {{c2::serotonin syndrome}}.', 'A — Tramadol is correct because its serotonergic activity can interact with fluoxetine to cause serotonin syndrome.'),
  ('2009-93', 'Chronic alcohol use most commonly causes a {{c1::slowly progressive distal symmetric sensory-predominant polyneuropathy}}.', 'C — Alcohol is correct because it commonly produces chronic distal symmetric sensory neuropathy, often with autonomic features.'),
  ('2009-94', 'Natalizumab treats multiple sclerosis by {{c1::blocking alpha-4 integrin-mediated leukocyte adhesion and CNS migration}}.', 'A — Natalizumab is correct because it is an alpha-4 integrin antagonist that limits leukocyte trafficking into the CNS.'),
  ('2009-95', 'Fever, focal back pain, and progressive bilateral neurologic deficits in a person who injects drugs suggest {{c1::spinal epidural abscess}}.', 'Spinal epidural abscess — injection drug use, vertebral tenderness, fever, and evolving paraparesis are an urgent pattern. Compression can cause bladder dysfunction and permanent deficits.'),
  ('2009-96', 'Delayed neurologic deterioration after carbon monoxide-induced coma is classically manifested by {{c1::parkinsonism}}.', 'A — Parkinsonism is correct because delayed carbon monoxide neurotoxicity often affects basal-ganglia and white-matter pathways.'),
  ('2009-97', 'Distress centered on questioning one’s faith without evidence of a mental disorder is coded as a {{c1::religious or spiritual problem}}.', 'D — Religious or spiritual problem is correct because the vignette describes spiritual conflict without a defined psychiatric syndrome.'),
  ('2009-98', 'In structural family therapy, excessively diffuse boundaries and overinvolvement between parent and child are called {{c1::enmeshment}}.', 'Enmeshed — the parent’s overinvolvement and speaking for the child indicate diffuse boundaries. The contrasting pattern is disengagement, with overly rigid boundaries.'),
  ('2009-99', 'A patient’s cultural identity is dynamic and is influenced by {{c1::their degree of acculturation}}.', 'C — Cultural identity is influenced by acculturation, which can shape values, affiliations, and health-care expectations.'),
  ('2009-100', 'When a patient voices a paranoid belief, the immediate therapeutic intervention is to {{c1::explore the observations and perceptions underlying the belief}}.', 'E — Ask the patient for details of the perceptions. Respectful exploration preserves alliance without validating the paranoid conclusion.'),
  ('2009-101', 'A 3-year-old who has not begun to speak intelligibly should receive {{c1::developmental and speech-language evaluation}} rather than simple reassurance.', 'A — Most 3-year-olds can communicate understandably enough to be understood. Normal hearing does not exclude a clinically important language or developmental disorder.'),
  ('2009-102', 'Addiction psychotherapy emphasizing acceptance of the disease and surrender to help beyond oneself is {{c1::twelve-step facilitation}}.', 'Twelve-step facilitation — it promotes engagement with twelve-step recovery using a disease-model framework. Acceptance and surrender are its key conceptual cues.'),
  ('2009-103', 'Headache, papilledema, and a superior sagittal sinus filling defect indicate {{c1::cerebral venous sinus thrombosis}}.', 'B — Sagittal sinus thrombosis impairs cerebral venous drainage and can raise intracranial pressure. It is suggested by headache, papilledema, and a sinus filling defect.'),
  ('2009-104', 'Measures traditionally called attention, concentration, and freedom from distractibility are included in the {{c1::Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS)}}.', 'D — The WAIS traditionally contains attention- and distractibility-related subtests. The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test is more focused on executive set shifting.'),
  ('2009-105', 'The most important risk factor for postpartum psychosis is {{c1::a previous episode of postpartum psychosis}}.', 'D — Previous postpartum psychosis is the strongest listed recurrence risk. It warrants careful anticipatory postpartum planning.'),
  ('2009-106', 'When meperidine is changed from IM to oral dosing, {{c1::a higher oral dose is required}} because oral meperidine has lower effective potency.', 'C — Oral meperidine is less potent than IM meperidine at the stated doses. This historical comparison does not itself demonstrate addiction.'),
  ('2009-107', 'Hypokalemic, hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with elevated amylase but normal lipase suggests {{c1::self-induced vomiting in bulimia nervosa, purging type}}.', 'Bulimia nervosa, purging type — recurrent vomiting causes hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis and hypokalemia. Elevated amylase with normal lipase can reflect salivary-gland stimulation from purging.'),
  ('2009-108', 'In mild to moderate Alzheimer dementia, cholinesterase inhibitors may {{c1::reduce neuropsychiatric symptoms}} but do not reverse disease progression.', 'E — Cholinesterase inhibitors may offer modest symptomatic benefit, including for some neuropsychiatric symptoms. They are not disease-modifying cures.'),
  ('2009-109', '{{c1::Valproate}} increases lamotrigine concentrations by inhibiting its {{c2::glucuronidation}}.', 'Valproate — it inhibits lamotrigine metabolism and raises serum concentrations. Lamotrigine should therefore be titrated more slowly when both medications are used.'),
  ('2009-110', 'Patients taking lithium should {{c1::maintain a consistent usual sodium intake}} to help prevent unpredictable lithium levels.', 'C — Changes in sodium balance alter renal lithium handling. Low sodium intake or dehydration can increase lithium concentrations.'),
  ('2009-111', 'In classical dream work, {{c1::condensation}} combines several unconscious wishes or feelings into one manifest dream image.', 'E — Condensation compresses multiple latent meanings into one dream representation. Displacement moves affect or emphasis to another target.'),
  ('2009-112', 'Anosognosia and failure to recognize a paralyzed left limb classically indicate a {{c1::right parietal lobe}} lesion.', 'Parietal lobe — a right-sided lesion can cause left neglect, anosognosia, and disturbed ownership of the left limb. This is a neurologic deficit in awareness, not ordinary psychological denial.'),
  ('2009-113', 'Infants are born with {{c1::sophisticated perceptual abilities that facilitate attachment}} and reciprocal caregiver interaction.', 'D — Infants have early social-perceptual capacities that support attachment. They can engage reciprocally well before the second year.'),
  ('2009-114', 'The best listed protection against malpractice claims is {{c1::maintaining a good doctor-patient relationship}}.', 'C — A strong therapeutic relationship promotes trust and communication. No-harm contracts and defensive medicine are not dependable legal protection.'),
  ('2009-115', 'Children with a reading disorder commonly have comorbid {{c1::ADHD, inattentive type}}.', 'Attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), inattentive type — it commonly co-occurs with reading disability. Assess whether inattention is pervasive rather than limited to reading tasks.'),
  ('2009-116', 'The first step in acute myasthenic crisis with respiratory compromise is {{c1::mechanical ventilation and airway support}}.', 'E — Respiratory stabilization is first in myasthenic crisis. IVIG or plasma exchange addresses the underlying disease after immediate support is secured.'),
  ('2009-117', 'Progressive cognitive decline with neurologic signs and lymphocytic inflammatory CSF should prompt evaluation for {{c1::neurosyphilis}}.', 'A — Neurosyphilis can cause chronic cognitive and neurologic decline with inflammatory CSF abnormalities. It is an important potentially treatable diagnostic consideration.'),
  ('2009-118', 'The feared visual complication of giant cell arteritis reflects ischemia in the {{c1::ophthalmic artery circulation}}.', 'Ophthalmic artery circulation is the best answer because its branches supply the retina and optic nerve. Their ischemia causes the feared sudden visual loss.'),
  ('2009-119', 'Depression in patients with multiple sclerosis can {{c1::respond to antidepressant treatment}}.', 'B — Depression is a treatable comorbidity in MS. Fatigue and disability do not fully explain every depressive presentation.'),
  ('2009-120', '{{c1::Memantine}} is an Alzheimer disease medication that antagonizes {{c2::NMDA glutamate receptors}}.', 'Memantine is the NMDA receptor antagonist. Donepezil, galantamine, rivastigmine, and tacrine are cholinesterase inhibitors.'),
  ('2009-121', 'In a young adult, {{c1::internuclear ophthalmoplegia}} is a classic ocular-motor sign of {{c2::multiple sclerosis}}.', 'Multiple sclerosis commonly causes demyelinating medial longitudinal fasciculus lesions. The resulting internuclear ophthalmoplegia causes impaired ipsilateral adduction with nystagmus of the abducting eye.'),
  ('2009-122', 'The free erythrocyte protoporphyrin test is classically associated with {{c1::lead intoxication}}.', 'A — Lead disrupts heme synthesis and raises erythrocyte protoporphyrin. Current lead evaluation requires blood lead testing rather than this marker alone.'),
  ('2009-123', 'Among the listed drugs of abuse, {{c1::cocaine}} is classically associated with especially powerful reinforcing effects.', 'A — Cocaine has potent reinforcing effects that promote repeated use. Reinforcement is distinct from withdrawal, tolerance, and toxicity.'),
  ('2009-124', 'Therapeutic empathy is the ability to {{c1::grasp a patient’s inner experience from the patient’s perspective}}.', 'Grasping the patient’s subjective perspective defines empathy. Sympathy and imagining oneself in the patient’s position are related but distinct.'),
  ('2009-125', 'In suspected SSRI-associated SIADH, {{c1::urine osmolality}} may show {{c2::inappropriately concentrated urine}} despite hyponatremia.', 'Urine osmolality is correct because SIADH impairs free-water excretion and prevents appropriate urine dilution.'),
  ('2009-126', 'In a young woman with migraine, {{c1::estrogen-containing oral contraceptives}} are a classic modifiable association with ischemic stroke risk.', 'Oral contraceptives are correct because estrogen exposure can increase thrombotic stroke risk in this setting.'),
  ('2009-127', 'A {{c1::crossover design}} gives each participant multiple interventions in sequence and is independent of whether the trial is blinded.', 'E — Double-blind is the historical closest-listed answer, not a definition of crossover. Crossover is a within-subject allocation design independent of blinding.'),
  ('2009-128', 'Individuals with {{c1::intellectual disability}} can develop the {{c2::same broad types of psychiatric disorders}} as the general population.', 'The same types of psychopathology occur in intellectual disability, although recognition and presentation may require adaptation.'),
  ('2009-129', 'For alcohol used to cope with shift-work insomnia, {{c1::relief from night shift}} addresses a key precipitating factor.', 'Relief from the night shift is correct because circadian disruption is driving both insomnia and alcohol use.'),
  ('2009-130', 'Forcing medication treatment most directly challenges patient {{c1::autonomy}}.', 'Autonomy is correct because forced treatment overrides a patient’s self-determination.'),
  ('2009-131', 'A frequent endocrine consequence of anorexia nervosa is {{c1::reproductive hormone dysfunction}} from hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal suppression.', 'Reproductive hormone dysfunction is correct because starvation suppresses gonadal-axis function.'),
  ('2009-132', 'Family treatment providing illness information, support, and relapse-management skills in schizophrenia is {{c1::psychoeducational family therapy}}.', 'Psychoeducational family therapy is correct because it combines education, support, and practical management guidance.'),
  ('2009-133', '{{c1::Dysprosody}} is an abnormality of {{c2::speech prosody, including intonation, rhythm, and stress}}.', 'Dysprosody is a speech disturbance. It affects the melody, rhythm, and emphasis of spoken language.'),
  ('2009-134', 'Severe unilateral orbital pain lasting about an hour with ipsilateral tearing and rhinorrhea is characteristic of {{c1::cluster headache}}.', 'Cluster headaches are correct because the pain is nocturnal, unilateral, stereotyped, and accompanied by ipsilateral autonomic symptoms.'),
  ('2009-135', 'Compared with early-onset depression, {{c1::late-life depression}} is more likely to include {{c2::psychotic symptoms}}.', 'Psychotic symptoms are correct because they are comparatively more common in late-life depressive episodes.'),
  ('2009-136', 'Under DSM-IV, a qualifying trauma syndrome with dissociation lasting less than one month is {{c1::acute stress disorder}}.', 'Acute stress disorder is correct because the trauma-related syndrome is present at three weeks and includes dissociation.'),
  ('2009-137', '{{c1::Ibuprofen}} can increase lithium levels by reducing {{c2::renal lithium clearance}}.', 'Ibuprofen is correct because NSAID-related renal effects can elevate lithium concentrations.'),
  ('2009-138', 'In DSM-IV terminology, persistent avoidance of partnered sexual contact despite intact solitary desire can indicate {{c1::sexual aversion disorder}}.', 'Sexual aversion disorder is correct because the central feature is partner-specific avoidance, not loss of orgasm or fantasy.'),
  ('2009-139', '{{c1::Marginalization}} is acculturation marked by weak connection to both one’s heritage culture and the host culture.', 'Lacking allegiance to both cultures describes marginalization. It may increase vulnerability through isolation and loss of social support.'),
  ('2009-140', 'Under DSM-IV, alcohol dependence with no criteria for six months is {{c1::early full remission}}.', 'Early full remission is correct because no criteria are present for more than one month but less than twelve months.'),
  ('2009-141', '{{c1::Childhood-onset schizophrenia}} typically follows a {{c2::chronic and unremitting}} course.', 'Chronic and unremitting is correct because childhood-onset schizophrenia is usually severe and persistent.'),
  ('2009-142', 'In healthy adults older than 65, executive abilities are generally {{c1::relatively preserved without a significant overall decline}}.', 'C — Show no significant change. Healthy aging more reliably slows processing and novel learning than it causes a global loss of executive ability.'),
  ('2009-143', '{{c1::Festination}}, an involuntary acceleration of short shuffling steps, is characteristic of {{c2::Parkinson disease}}.', 'B — Parkinson disease. Festination is an accelerating, short-stepped parkinsonian gait rather than alcohol-related ataxia.'),
  ('2009-144', 'In suspected acute hepatic porphyria, {{c1::antiseizure medication must be selected for porphyria safety}}, with gabapentin the best-listed option here.', 'B — Gabapentin is the corrected best-listed answer after invalidation of the all-credit key. Acute seizures still require emergency stabilization and individualized porphyria-safe treatment.'),
  ('2009-145', 'A psychodynamic formulation of paranoid personality disorder emphasizes {{c1::failure to achieve object constancy}}.', 'C — A developmental failure to achieve object constancy. This formulation helps explain persistent mistrust and sensitivity to perceived betrayal.'),
  ('2009-146', 'First-line symptomatic treatment for {{c1::cervical dystonia}} is targeted {{c2::botulinum toxin injection}}.', 'B — botulinum toxin. The sensory trick and patterned neck spasm identify cervical dystonia.'),
  ('2009-147', 'Before starting a tricyclic antidepressant in a child, obtain a {{c1::baseline ECG}}.', 'A — Before treatment begins, for all children, to establish a baseline. Baseline screening helps identify conduction abnormalities before tricyclic exposure.'),
  ('2009-148', 'Presenting an aversive stimulus after a behavior to reduce that behavior is {{c1::positive punishment}}.', 'B — punishment. The aversive stimulus is added after the response and is intended to decrease that response.'),
  ('2009-149', 'The four elements of malpractice are duty, breach, causation, and {{c1::damages or harm to the patient}}.', 'D — Harm to the patient. A malpractice claim requires damages in addition to duty, negligence, and causation.'),
  ('2009-150', 'For severe acute manic agitation during pregnancy, {{c1::haloperidol}} is a traditional comparatively safer choice among these options.', 'B — Haloperidol. It is preferred over valproate and carbamazepine in this historical pregnancy-risk comparison.'),
  ('2009-151', 'Older adults may tolerate lower antipsychotic doses partly because of {{c1::decreased hepatic metabolism and drug clearance}}.', 'B — Decreased hepatic metabolism. Reduced clearance contributes to greater antipsychotic exposure and adverse-effect risk in older adults.'),
  ('2009-152', 'First-trimester exposure to {{c1::valproic acid}} increases the risk of fetal {{c2::neural tube defects}}.', 'D — Valproic acid. It is the classic psychotropic and antiseizure medication associated with neural tube defects.'),
  ('2009-153', '{{c1::Friedreich ataxia}} is caused by an {{c2::autosomal recessive GAA trinucleotide repeat expansion}}.', 'A — Friedreich ataxia. It is the autosomal recessive trinucleotide-repeat disorder in this list.'),
  ('2009-154', 'Learning reduced fear by observing another person is {{c1::modeling, or observational learning}}.', 'Modeling is learning through observation. The child’s fear declines after watching peers ride safely and happily.'),
  ('2009-155', '{{c1::Diphenhydramine overdose}} causes an {{c2::anticholinergic toxidrome}} with dry mucosa, blurred vision, urinary retention, and tachycardia.', 'B — Diphenhydramine. Its antimuscarinic effects explain the dry skin, blurred vision, urinary retention, tachycardia, and decreased bowel sounds.'),
  ('2009-156', 'Intermittent explosive disorder is classically associated with {{c1::decreased central serotonergic activity}}.', 'Decreased serotonin is the traditional cerebrospinal-fluid association with intermittent explosive disorder. It reflects impaired inhibitory control in impulsive aggression.'),
  ('2009-157', 'By about {{c1::12 months}}, infants show {{c2::social referencing}} by using a caregiver’s emotional cues in ambiguous situations.', 'B — 12 months. Social referencing is generally evident late in the first year of life.'),
  ('2009-158', 'Early persistent {{c1::behavioral inhibition}} is associated with increased later risk of {{c2::anxiety disorders}}.', 'C — Anxiety. Early behavioral inhibition is a temperamental risk factor, particularly for later social anxiety.'),
  ('2009-159', 'Typical absence seizures are distinguished from focal impaired-awareness seizures by {{c1::lack of postictal manifestations}}.', 'D — Lack of postictal manifestations is correct because typical absence seizures end with immediate recovery. Automatisms and brief duration may occur in more than one seizure type.'),
  ('2009-160', 'Numbness of the little finger and ulnar ring finger with hypothenar atrophy indicates an {{c1::ulnar nerve lesion}}.', 'An ulnar nerve lesion affects the medial hand and hypothenar muscles. Median entrapment instead predominates in the thumb through radial ring-finger distribution.'),
  ('2009-161', 'The {{c1::Thematic Apperception Test (TAT)}} asks a person to create stories about ambiguous pictured situations.', 'B — Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is correct because it uses pictured situations as story prompts. Rorschach testing instead uses inkblots.'),
  ('2009-162', '{{c1::Bupropion}} may help hypoactive sexual desire because it has relatively few serotonergic sexual adverse effects.', 'E — Bupropion is correct because it is relatively sexually sparing and may improve low desire. Serotonergic antidepressants more commonly worsen sexual function.'),
  ('2009-163', 'In psychotherapy, respectfully pointing out a discrepancy between a patient’s words and affect is {{c1::confrontation}}.', 'E — Confrontation is correct because the psychiatrist highlights an indirectly expressed emotion. The observation invites awareness of a discrepancy between words and affect.'),
  ('2009-164', 'Unlike schizoid personality disorder, {{c1::avoidant personality disorder}} includes a {{c2::desire for social relationships}}.', 'Avoidant individuals want relationships but fear rejection. Schizoid individuals are characteristically detached and have little desire for closeness.'),
  ('2009-165', 'A {{c1::double-blind}} study minimizes bias caused by participants’ and investigators’ knowledge of treatment assignment.', 'E — Double-blind is correct because participants and relevant investigators do not know the assigned treatment. This reduces expectancy and assessment bias.'),
  ('2009-166', 'Fear acquired after personally experiencing an aversive event is {{c1::direct conditioning}}.', 'A — Direct conditioning is correct because the patient personally experienced the collision. Observing another person or receiving warnings would describe other fear-acquisition pathways.'),
  ('2009-167', 'Repeatedly redirecting psychotherapy away from emotionally relevant material is {{c1::resistance}}.', 'B — Resistance is correct because irrelevant redirection can avoid threatening therapeutic content. Derailment is a formal thought-process disturbance, not a therapy defense.'),
  ('2009-168', 'By adolescence, the ADHD symptom most likely to diminish is {{c1::overt hyperactivity}}.', 'C — Hyperactivity is correct because overt motor activity often declines during adolescence. Inattention and executive-function difficulties commonly persist.'),
  ('2009-169', 'In delirium, {{c1::insomnia}} is a symptom that may require targeted pharmacologic treatment when severe.', 'A — Insomnia is correct because severe sleep disruption may require targeted treatment in delirium. Core cognitive deficits are addressed chiefly by treating the underlying cause and providing supportive care.'),
  ('2009-170', 'In the historical PRITE epidemiologic framing, {{c1::major depressive disorder}} was the leading mental-illness cause of disability among U.S. individuals aged 15–44.', 'Major depressive disorder is the keyed leading cause of disability in this age group. Its large burden reflects both prevalence and functional impairment.'),
  ('2009-171', 'Long-term antiretroviral-treated HIV is associated with increased concern for {{c1::cerebrovascular accident}} through chronic vascular risk.', 'C — Cerebrovascular accident is correct because long-term treated HIV is associated with chronic metabolic and vascular risk. Opportunistic CNS infections are more characteristic of advanced immunosuppression.'),
  ('2009-172', 'Bupropion aids nicotine cessation partly by antagonizing {{c1::neuronal nicotinic acetylcholine receptors}}.', 'C — Nicotinic is correct because bupropion antagonizes neuronal nicotinic receptors. Its norepinephrine and dopamine reuptake inhibition may also support cessation.'),
  ('2009-173', 'The emergence of {{c1::symbolic representation}} allows toddlers to use language for things not immediately present.', 'A — Using language more proficiently is correct because words are symbols that represent absent people, objects, and actions. Symbolic thought broadens communication beyond the immediate environment.'),
  ('2009-174', '{{c1::Maple syrup urine disease}} can present in the neonatal period with metabolic encephalopathy.', 'D — Maple syrup urine disease is correct because severe forms cause neonatal metabolic decompensation and encephalopathy. Several other listed disorders are detected on screening or more often manifest later.'),
  ('2009-175', 'A psychiatrist must not confirm that someone is a patient to a third party because of {{c1::confidentiality}}.', 'C — Confidentiality is correct because it protects even the fact that a treatment relationship exists. The patient’s public greeting does not permit disclosure to the spouse.'),
  ('2009-176', 'For OCD without adequate response to a tolerated SSRI, first {{c1::optimize to the highest therapeutic dose}} before switching agents.', 'C — Increase the dose of sertraline to 200 mg is correct because OCD often needs a full high-dose SSRI trial. Switching or clomipramine is considered after adequate optimization.'),
  ('2009-177', 'In an emergency presentation resembling acute anxiety with sudden dyspnea and autonomic arousal, consider {{c1::pulmonary embolism}}.', 'Pulmonary embolism can mimic a panic attack through abrupt dyspnea, tachycardia, and intense fear.'),
  ('2009-178', '{{c1::Velocardiofacial syndrome (22q11.2 deletion syndrome)}} is associated with increased risk of {{c2::schizophrenia-spectrum psychosis}}.', 'Velocardiofacial syndrome is associated with a 22q11.2 deletion and increased schizophrenia risk.'),
  ('2009-179', 'The principal goal of {{c1::wraparound services}} is to prevent unnecessary {{c2::hospitalization or residential placement}} for youth with complex needs.', 'Wraparound services coordinate individualized, family-centered community care. Their aim is to avoid unnecessary restrictive placement.'),
  ('2009-180', '{{c1::Dependent personality disorder}} involves excessive reliance on others for decisions and distress about functioning independently during separation.', 'Dependent personality disorder features reassurance seeking and fear of being unable to cope alone.'),
  ('2009-181', '{{c1::Carbamazepine}} can decrease methadone blood levels through {{c2::hepatic enzyme induction}}.', 'Carbamazepine induces metabolism and can lower methadone exposure. Monitor for reduced effect or opioid withdrawal after it is started.'),
  ('2009-182', 'In school-age children, depressive disorder commonly presents with {{c1::irritability}} rather than only verbalized sadness.', 'Irritability is a common manifestation of depressive disorder in school-age children.'),
  ('2009-183', 'After stopping the offending drug and providing supportive care for neuroleptic malignant syndrome, a {{c1::dopamine agonist}} such as bromocriptine may be considered.', 'Dopamine agonists can be used as adjunctive treatment for neuroleptic malignant syndrome.'),
  ('2009-184', 'A low-frequency, irregular tremor that worsens near the end of a reaching movement is a {{c1::cerebellar intention tremor}}.', 'Cerebellar tremor is irregular, low frequency, and worst during target-directed movement. Parkinsonian tremor is instead classically a resting tremor.'),
  ('2009-185', 'Aggression in otherwise normally developing preschoolers is often related to {{c1::limited verbal abilities}} and immature frustration communication.', 'Limited verbal skills can cause frustration to be expressed physically in preschoolers.'),
  ('2009-186', '{{c1::Perseverative errors}} are a key scored measure on the {{c2::Wisconsin Card Sorting Test}}.', 'The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test assesses executive function and cognitive set shifting.'),
  ('2009-187', 'After dangerous mimics are excluded, {{c1::transient global amnesia}} is managed with {{c2::observation and supportive care}} rather than psychotropics.', 'Transient global amnesia is typically self-limited and is managed with observation after appropriate evaluation.'),
  ('2009-188', 'With sustained abstinence and repeated nonreinforcement, conditioned substance-use cravings generally {{c1::decrease in intensity}} over time.', 'Continued abstinence usually weakens conditioned stimulant cravings rather than abruptly eliminating them.'),
  ('2009-189', 'Along with abstinence, preventing progression of alcohol-related cognitive injury requires {{c1::adequate nutrition with thiamine and vitamin repletion}}.', 'Ensuring adequate nutrition is crucial because thiamine deficiency contributes to alcohol-related neurologic injury.'),
  ('2009-190', 'A common reason patients discontinue {{c1::valproate}} is {{c2::gastrointestinal side effects}}.', 'Gastrointestinal adverse effects commonly limit valproate tolerability and early adherence.'),
  ('2009-191', 'First-line treatment for {{c1::retentive encopresis}} is {{c2::psychoeducation plus laxative-based constipation management}}.', 'Psychoeducation and laxative treatment directly address stool retention and overflow soiling.'),
  ('2009-192', '{{c1::Fluoxetine}}, an SSRI, is a first-line pharmacologic treatment for {{c2::premenstrual dysphoric disorder}}.', 'Fluoxetine is an evidence-based SSRI option for premenstrual dysphoric disorder.'),
  ('2009-193', 'In the historical epidemiology tested here, {{c1::young adults}} report the highest rates of {{c2::cigarette smoking}}.', 'Young adults are the highest-prevalence smoking group in this question’s historical epidemiologic framing.'),
  ('2009-194', 'The individual-therapy stance in {{c1::dialectical behavior therapy}} balances validation with {{c2::accountability for behavioral change}}.', 'Nurturing the patient while requiring self-help reflects the acceptance-and-change dialectic of DBT.'),
  ('2009-195', 'A prospective power analysis estimates the {{c1::sample size}} needed to detect a prespecified effect with adequate {{c2::power}}.', 'Power analysis estimates the sample needed to detect a specified effect under stated assumptions. It is primarily a study-design tool, not a method for analyzing a completed dataset.'),
  ('2009-196', 'Prominent psychomotor slowing with upper-motor-neuron signs and apathy suggests {{c1::periventricular white-matter disease}} affecting {{c2::frontal-subcortical circuits}}.', 'Periventricular white-matter disease can present with depressive symptoms and marked slowing. Associated dysarthria and pyramidal signs favor a neurologic substrate over isolated primary depression.'),
  ('2009-197', '{{c1::Amantadine}} can be used to treat antipsychotic-induced {{c2::parkinsonism}}.', 'Amantadine is useful for antipsychotic-induced parkinsonism. It is not the standard targeted treatment for acute dystonia, akathisia, or tardive dyskinesia.'),
  ('2009-198', 'Hypochondriasis is characterized primarily by preoccupation with having or acquiring a serious {{c1::disease}}.', 'Hypochondriasis focuses on feared disease. The concern is distinct from body-image preoccupation, secondary gain, or intentional feigning.'),
  ('2009-199', 'LSD and mescaline produce classic psychedelic effects primarily through {{c1::5-HT2A receptor agonism}}.', '5-HT2A agonism is the key mechanism of LSD and mescaline. D2 and 5-HT3 receptors do not explain the classic hallucinogen effect.'),
  ('2009-200', 'A superior-division MCA stroke causes {{c1::contralateral face-and-arm-predominant weakness}} with gaze deviation {{c2::toward the lesion}}.', 'The superior MCA division supplies lateral frontal-parietal cortex controlling face and arm. Its occlusion produces gaze toward the lesion and weakness on the opposite side.'),
  ('2009-201', 'When ambivalence prevents entry into OCD treatment, use {{c1::motivational interviewing}} to increase readiness for {{c2::exposure and response prevention}}.', 'Motivational interviewing addresses resistance and ambivalence about OCD treatment. ERP remains the core behavioral intervention once the patient can engage with it.'),
  ('2009-202', 'Thoughts linked by rhyme or sound rather than meaning are called {{c1::clang associations}}.', 'Clang associations are sound-based links between thoughts. They differ from flight of ideas, which is rapid but often conceptually connected.'),
  ('2009-203', 'When a referral idealizes a special patient, the receiving clinician should anticipate {{c1::splitting between clinicians}}.', 'Anticipating splitting protects against idealization, rescue pressure, and clinician competition. Maintain clear boundaries and coordinated communication.'),
  ('2009-204', 'The most common factor in termination of parental rights cases is parental {{c1::neglect}}.', 'Neglect is the most common basis for termination of parental rights. Parental illness or incarceration alone is not automatically sufficient.'),
  ('2009-205', 'In psychodynamic therapy, an acute dramatic outburst is best met initially by inviting {{c1::reflection on triggers and painful feelings}}.', 'Encouraging reflection helps convert affective acting out into material that can be understood. Immediate reassurance, confrontation, or transference interpretation is less appropriate here.'),
  ('2009-206', 'Weakness of knee extension localizes to the {{c1::femoral nerve}} via impaired {{c2::quadriceps}} function.', 'The femoral nerve innervates the quadriceps, which extend the knee. Peroneal injury instead more characteristically produces foot drop.'),
  ('2009-207', 'Among the listed medical specialties, {{c1::emergency medicine}} has the highest rate of {{c2::substance use disorders}}.', 'Emergency medicine is the keyed highest-risk specialty among these choices. This is a population-level comparison and should not be used to stereotype individual clinicians.'),
  ('2009-208', '{{c1::Parallel play}} occurs when young children play beside one another without a shared goal or organized interaction.', 'Parallel play is side-by-side, largely independent play. Coordinated role assignment and a shared game indicate cooperative play.'),
  ('2009-209', 'Macrocytic anemia with neuropsychiatric symptoms should prompt measurement of {{c1::vitamin B12}}.', 'Vitamin B12 deficiency can cause macrocytic anemia and depression-like neuropsychiatric symptoms. The macrocytosis makes B12 testing more targeted than iron studies.'),
  ('2009-210', 'Acute fever, altered mental status, seizures, and temporal-lobe abnormalities should raise concern for {{c1::herpes simplex encephalitis}}.', 'Herpes encephalitis classically involves temporal-limbic regions. Olfactory phenomena and seizures support temporal-lobe involvement.'),
  ('2009-211', 'Mutations in the {{c1::amyloid precursor protein (APP)}} gene are associated with familial {{c2::early-onset Alzheimer disease}}.', 'Amyloid precursor protein mutations can cause familial early-onset Alzheimer disease. Alpha-synuclein and huntingtin mutations indicate different neurodegenerative disorders.'),
  ('2009-212', 'For ADHD with a co-occurring {{c1::substance use disorder}}, {{c2::atomoxetine}} is often considered to reduce stimulant misuse or diversion concerns.', 'Atomoxetine is a nonstimulant ADHD treatment. It is useful to consider when stimulant misuse or diversion is a concern.'),
  ('2009-213', 'Among tricyclic antidepressants, {{c1::desipramine}} has clinically useful {{c2::therapeutic drug monitoring}}.', 'Desipramine is a TCA with clinically useful blood-level monitoring. Tranylcypromine is an MAOI rather than a TCA.'),
  ('2009-214', 'In children, {{c1::major depressive disorder}} may present with persistent irritability and can include {{c2::mood-congruent psychotic symptoms}} when severe.', 'B — major depressive disorder is the historical best-listed answer. The abbreviated vignette does not independently establish every current diagnostic criterion.'),
  ('2009-215', 'Clozapine should be titrated gradually because rapid dose escalation increases the risk of {{c1::dose-related seizures}}.', 'Seizures are the correct answer because clozapine seizure risk increases with dose and rapid escalation. Agranulocytosis requires hematologic monitoring rather than slow titration.'),
  ('2009-216', '{{c1::Circumstantiality}} is overly detailed, indirect speech that {{c2::eventually returns to the point}}.', 'Circumstantiality includes unnecessary detail but ends with an answer. Tangentiality does not return to the original question.'),
  ('2009-217', 'When cognitive impairment may make driving unsafe, clinicians should {{c1::consult applicable state reporting law}} in addition to counseling and assessment.', 'Consulting state law is the correct answer because clinician reporting duties for unsafe driving vary by jurisdiction. Informal driving restrictions may be insufficient.'),
  ('2009-218', 'In severe alcohol intoxication with stupor, the immediate life-threatening concern is {{c1::respiratory failure from CNS depression}}.', 'Respiratory failure is the correct answer because profound ethanol intoxication can suppress consciousness and ventilation. Delirium tremens is a withdrawal complication.'),
  ('2009-219', '{{c1::Blunted affect}} is a {{c2::negative symptom}} of schizophrenia.', 'Blunted affect is the correct answer because emotional expression is reduced. Delusions and hallucinations are positive symptoms.'),
  ('2009-220', 'Hyperkalemic periodic paralysis and paramyotonia congenita are caused by mutations affecting {{c1::skeletal-muscle voltage-gated sodium channels}}.', 'Sodium is the correct answer because both disorders are classic skeletal-muscle sodium-channelopathies. Chloride-channel mutations instead classically cause myotonia congenita.'),
  ('2009-221', 'Obesity, unrefreshing sleep, and excessive daytime sleepiness suggest a {{c1::breathing-related sleep disorder}} such as {{c2::obstructive sleep apnea}}.', 'A breathing-related sleep disorder is the correct answer because it best integrates obesity, nonrestorative sleep, and unintended daytime sleep episodes. Depression or burnout alone does not explain the pattern as well.'),
  ('2009-222', 'Weakness of {{c1::initial shoulder abduction and external rotation}} suggests injury to the {{c2::suprascapular nerve}}.', 'Suprascapular is the correct answer because its injury weakens supraspinatus and infraspinatus. These muscles initiate abduction and externally rotate the arm.'),
  ('2009-223', 'In child play therapy, an appropriate early intervention is to {{c1::reflect the affect expressed in the play}} without attributing it directly to the child.', 'Describe the dolls’ affect because this supports emotional expression without premature interpretation. The play should not automatically be treated as literal family history.'),
  ('2009-224', 'A defendant is incompetent to stand trial when unable to {{c1::understand the charges and proceedings}} or {{c2::assist counsel rationally}}.', 'Failure to understand charges is the correct answer because it addresses a core element of trial competence. Diagnosis and prior hospitalization alone are insufficient.'),
  ('2009-225', 'Involuntary, recurrent obscene gestures are called {{c1::copropraxia}}.', 'Copropraxia means obscene motor gestures. Coprolalia refers to obscene vocalizations instead.'),
  ('2009-226', 'The {{c1::M’Naughten rule}} excuses a defendant who, because of mental illness, did not know the nature and quality of the act or that it was {{c2::wrong}}.', 'M’Naughten is the correct answer because it is the cognitive insanity rule focused on knowledge of the act and its wrongfulness. Irresistible impulse instead emphasizes control.'),
  ('2009-227', 'The ethical duty to avoid causing harm is {{c1::nonmaleficence}}.', 'Nonmaleficence means avoiding harm. Conditioning fear in a child most directly violates this principle.'),
  ('2009-228', '{{c1::Buprenorphine}} is commonly administered {{c2::sublingually}} for opioid use disorder.', 'Buprenorphine is the correct answer because common opioid-use-disorder formulations are absorbed sublingually. Methadone is generally administered orally.'),
  ('2009-229', 'Historically, an IQ near {{c1::65}} with adaptive deficits was associated with academic achievement around the {{c2::sixth-grade level}}.', 'Sixth grade is the correct historical association for mild intellectual disability around IQ 65. Modern assessment emphasizes adaptive functioning and individual supports.'),
  ('2009-230', 'Piaget was most interested in the {{c1::cognitive processes children use to arrive at answers}}, rather than only the answers themselves.', 'Processes is the correct answer because Piaget focused on how children think and reason. The final answer alone does not reveal cognitive process.'),
  ('2009-231', 'A patient’s early offer of a lucrative joint investment to a psychotherapist may represent {{c1::an unconscious seductive boundary test}} within the therapeutic relationship.', 'An attempt to seduce and test the psychiatrist is the correct answer because the offer may seek a special boundary-crossing relationship. It should not be accepted.'),
  ('2009-232', 'Gerstmann syndrome from a dominant angular-gyrus lesion features {{c1::agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia, and right-left disorientation}}.', 'E — an embolic stroke affecting the left angular gyrus produces the classic Gerstmann syndrome pattern. These are focal cortical signs rather than generalized encephalopathy.'),
  ('2009-233', 'Inactive {{c1::CYP2A6}} alleles alter nicotine metabolism and are associated with relative protection from {{c2::tobacco dependence}}.', 'A — Tobacco is correct because CYP2A6 is centrally involved in nicotine metabolism. Cotinine is a nicotine metabolite rather than the addictive agent.'),
  ('2009-234', 'An acute, clinically significant multiple-sclerosis relapse is commonly treated with high-dose {{c1::methylprednisolone}}.', 'E — Methylprednisolone reduces symptoms and hastens recovery during an acute MS exacerbation. Baclofen and disease-modifying agents have different roles.'),
  ('2009-235', 'In preschool children with psychiatric illness, isolated hallucinations are most commonly associated with {{c1::anxiety}} rather than schizophrenia.', 'B — anxiety is correct because isolated perceptual experiences in preschoolers are often nonspecific and stress-related. Childhood-onset schizophrenia is rare and usually more pervasive.'),
  ('2009-236', 'For pharmacologic treatment of Tourette tics, {{c1::alpha-2 adrenergic agonists}} are often first line because of their favorable risk-benefit profile.', 'B — Alpha 2 adrenergic agonists are first-line medications for many patients with Tourette tics. D2 antagonists are generally reserved when greater tic suppression justifies their risks.'),
  ('2009-237', '{{c1::Clonidine}}, an alpha-2 agonist, reduces the autonomic symptoms of opioid withdrawal.', 'D — Clonidine is a standard nonopioid medication for opioid detoxification symptoms. It reduces adrenergic arousal rather than replacing opioids.'),
  ('2009-238', 'After subarachnoid hemorrhage, abrupt headache, vomiting, declining consciousness, and bilateral sixth-nerve palsy suggest {{c1::acute hydrocephalus}}.', 'D — Acute hydrocephalus causes raised intracranial pressure after subarachnoid hemorrhage. Bilateral abducens weakness is a pressure-related clue.'),
  ('2009-239', 'Postpartum thoughts of harming an infant accompanied by {{c1::psychotic symptoms or impaired reality testing}} warrant urgent inpatient safety evaluation.', 'B — admit the patient to the hospital involuntarily is correct because possible postpartum psychosis creates acute risk. Outpatient care is inadequate when safety and reality testing are compromised.'),
  ('2009-240', 'The extent to which an assessment instrument yields reproducible findings is its {{c1::reliability}}.', 'A — reliability means consistency or replicability of measurement. It is distinct from validity, sensitivity, and specificity.'),
  ('2009-241', '{{c1::Harry Stack Sullivan}} developed the interpersonal theory of psychiatry.', 'E — Harry Stack Sullivan is the founder of interpersonal psychiatry. His theory emphasizes relationships and social experience in personality and psychopathology.'),
  ('2009-242', 'In conversion symptoms, {{c1::primary gain}} is the unconscious relief of an internal psychological conflict.', 'B — primary gain is correct because paralysis unconsciously prevents an intolerable aggressive act. Secondary gain would require an external benefit.'),
  ('2009-243', '{{c1::Mutism and echolalia}} are characteristic clinical signs of {{c2::catatonia}}.', 'A — Catatonic is the historical answer because mutism and echolalia are catatonic signs. Modern practice treats catatonia as a cross-diagnostic syndrome.'),
  ('2009-244', 'Among psychiatric diagnoses, {{c1::substance-related disorders}} are most commonly found in individuals who commit violence against others.', 'A — Substance related disorder is the keyed population-level association with violence. It must never be treated as a deterministic label for an individual patient.'),
  ('2009-245', 'A credible targeted threat requires {{c1::immediate safety coordination through the legally appropriate psychiatric and public-safety pathways}}, which are jurisdiction-dependent.', 'B — refer the patient back to the law enforcement authorities is correct under the item’s historical framing. Real-world custody and protection steps remain jurisdiction- and fact-dependent.'),
  ('2009-246', 'In traditional neurochemical teaching about aging, {{c1::serotonin}} is relatively maintained compared with several other neurotransmitter systems.', 'B — Serotonin is the relatively consistent neurotransmitter system in this aging comparison. Dopamine and acetylcholine show more prominent age-related changes.'),
  ('2009-247', 'The most clinically and legally meaningful suicide documentation includes an individualized {{c1::assessment of risk and protective factors}}.', 'An assessment of risk and protective factors demonstrates clinical reasoning. No-self-harm contracts do not replace a comprehensive assessment and plan.'),
  ('2009-248', 'Huntington disease chorea may be suppressed with the dopamine D2 antagonist {{c1::haloperidol}}.', 'Haloperidol can lessen Huntington chorea by reducing dopaminergic signaling. Its extrapyramidal adverse effects can limit use.'),
  ('2009-249', 'Restless legs syndrome was historically treated with the dopamine agonist {{c1::bromocriptine}}.', 'Bromocriptine is the keyed historical dopamine agonist for restless legs syndrome. Long-term dopaminergic therapy can cause augmentation.'),
  ('2009-250', 'A common anterior circle-of-Willis site of saccular aneurysm is the {{c1::anterior communicating artery}}.', 'Anterior communicating artery aneurysms occur where the two anterior cerebral arteries connect. Rupture may cause subarachnoid hemorrhage.')
insert into flashcards (question_id, cloze_text, extra) values
  ('2009-251', 'In an obese patient with hypersomnia and unrefreshing sleep, suspected obstructive sleep apnea is evaluated with {{c1::polysomnography}}.', 'Polysomnography evaluates sleep-disordered breathing. Obesity and nonrestorative sleep raise concern for obstructive sleep apnea.'),
  ('2009-252', 'After myocardial infarction, {{c1::depression}} is an independent adverse prognostic marker but does not replace cardiac predictors such as ejection fraction.', 'B — Depression is the intended psychiatric prognostic factor. It does not universally outrank left-ventricular ejection fraction or replace cardiac risk assessment.'),
  ('2009-253', 'Progressive ascending weakness with {{c1::areflexia}} and demyelinating nerve-conduction findings suggests {{c2::acute inflammatory polyneuropathy (Guillain-Barré syndrome)}}.', 'Acute inflammatory polyneuropathy causes weakness and absent reflexes. Demyelinating conduction abnormalities support Guillain-Barré syndrome.'),
  ('2009-254', '{{c1::Projective identification}} involves disowning an internal representation, attributing it to another person, and inducing that person to identify with it.', 'Projective identification is more than projection. It includes an interpersonal process that induces the recipient to experience or enact the projected state.'),
  ('2009-255', 'Socioeconomic disadvantage increases mental-illness risk partly through greater {{c1::vulnerability to the impact of stressful life events}}.', 'Vulnerability means stressors have a larger psychiatric effect when protective resources are limited. It is not an assertion of greater genetic risk.'),
  ('2009-256', 'An unreasonable sustained belief that remains open to doubt when challenged is an {{c1::overvalued idea}}.', 'An overvalued idea is strongly held but not fixed with delusional certainty. Residual insight is its defining feature.'),
  ('2009-257', 'The opioid classically contraindicated with an MAOI such as phenelzine is {{c1::meperidine}}.', 'Meperidine with phenelzine can cause severe hyperthermic toxicity. Avoid this combination.'),
  ('2009-258', '{{c1::Attunement}} is a caregiver’s responsive matching of an infant’s affect, timing, or rhythm in a communicative way.', 'Attunement means being in tune with the infant’s state. The caregiver matches the child’s rhythm and positive affect rather than simply imitating behavior.'),
  ('2009-259', 'Kohut’s self psychology is most associated with {{c1::empathic validation}} of the patient’s subjective experience.', 'Empathic validation is central to Kohut’s self psychology. It communicates understanding without requiring agreement.'),
  ('2009-260', 'For depression marked by social isolation, {{c1::interpersonal psychotherapy}} focuses on improving current relationships and social support.', 'Interpersonal psychotherapy targets present interpersonal functioning. In social isolation, it aims to expand contacts and strengthen existing relationships.'),
  ('2009-261', 'Carbamazepine can cause haloperidol treatment failure because it {{c1::induces hepatic drug metabolism}}.', 'Carbamazepine is a strong enzyme inducer. It can lower haloperidol exposure enough to permit psychotic relapse.'),
  ('2009-262', 'The most common cause of dementia after age 65 is {{c1::Alzheimer disease}}.', 'Alzheimer disease is the leading cause of dementia in older adults. Its usual course is gradual and progressive.'),
  ('2009-263', 'In Alcoholics Anonymous, a {{c1::sponsor}} is a member who provides one-to-one guidance in working the program.', 'An AA sponsor is a peer guide, not a meeting leader or clinician. Sponsorship supports participation in the recovery program.'),
  ('2009-264', '{{c1::Lithium}} can exacerbate {{c2::psoriasis}}.', 'Lithium is associated with psoriasis exacerbation. Monitor skin symptoms when it is used in patients with psoriatic disease.'),
  ('2009-265', 'Among the listed antipsychotics, {{c1::clozapine}} has the least sustained effect on prolactin levels.', 'Clozapine is relatively prolactin-sparing. Its other serious adverse effects still require careful monitoring.'),
  ('2009-266', 'The keyed CLAS language-access standard includes patient-related materials in the patient’s {{c1::native language}} when needed for meaningful access.', 'Patient-related materials should be understandable in the patient’s language. Qualified interpretation remains important for clinical communication.'),
  ('2009-267', 'For this exam-era item, {{c1::zolpidem}} is the closest-listed medication, while reversible-cause assessment and nonpharmacologic treatment still come first.', 'A — Zolpidem is the exam-era closest-listed answer. It is not a universal first-line treatment for insomnia in dementia.'),
  ('2009-268', 'Capgras syndrome is the delusion that a familiar person has been replaced by an {{c1::identical-appearing impostor}}.', 'Capgras syndrome is a delusional misidentification of a familiar person. The appearance is recognized, but the person’s identity is denied.'),
  ('2009-269', 'Isolation of affect is a defense mechanism in which a distressing {{c1::idea or memory is recalled without its associated emotion}}.', 'Isolation of affect separates feeling from thought. The patient can describe the event but seems emotionally detached from it.'),
  ('2009-270', 'Advance-directive discussions are most appropriate when the patient has {{c1::decision-making capacity}}.', 'When the patient is competent, preferences can be understood and documented. Crisis care may arrive after capacity is lost.'),
  ('2009-271', 'Flooding is {{c1::immediate, intense exposure}} to a feared stimulus without a gradual hierarchy.', 'Flooding confronts a high-intensity feared situation directly. It differs from systematic desensitization, which proceeds gradually.'),
  ('2009-272', 'MDMA intoxication is classically associated with {{c1::bruxism}} and {{c2::diaphoresis or hyperthermia}}.', 'MDMA commonly causes jaw clenching, sweating, anorexia, and heat-related symptoms. These features reflect serotonergic and sympathomimetic effects.'),
  ('2009-273', 'A suicidal patient too intoxicated for a reliable assessment should be {{c1::observed and reevaluated when clinically sober}}.', 'Observe the intoxicated patient in a safe setting. Reassess suicidality after sobriety permits a meaningful interview.'),
  ('2009-274', 'Severe symptomatic {{c1::lithium toxicity}} with renal impairment requires {{c2::emergent hemodialysis}}.', 'Emergent hemodialysis rapidly removes lithium in severe poisoning. Activated charcoal does not effectively adsorb lithium.'),
  ('2009-275', 'When a preschooler misses a departed long-term caregiver, parents can help by {{c1::acknowledging the relationship and using pictures or memories}}.', 'Showing pictures of the nanny helps a preschooler remember and talk about a departed caregiver. Avoiding the topic can leave the child’s grief unsupported.'),
  ('2009-276', 'An elevated {{c1::mean corpuscular volume (MCV)}} is a nonspecific hematologic marker of heavy alcohol use.', 'Mean corpuscular volume may rise with chronic heavy alcohol use. Macrocytosis has several alternative causes.'),
  ('2009-277', '{{c1::Social referencing}} is a child’s use of a caregiver’s emotional expression to guide behavior in an ambiguous situation.', 'Social referencing means looking to a caregiver’s reaction for information. It helps a child decide whether an unfamiliar situation is safe.'),
  ('2009-278', 'During electroconvulsive therapy, {{c1::lithium}} is associated with prolonged seizures and postictal confusional states.', 'Lithium can increase the risk of prolonged ECT seizures and postictal delirium. Review lithium carefully during peri-ECT medication planning.'),
  ('2009-279', '{{c1::Lorazepam}} is reliably absorbed when administered orally or intramuscularly.', 'Lorazepam has reliable oral and intramuscular absorption. Diazepam is not reliably absorbed after intramuscular administration.'),
  ('2009-280', '{{c1::Serotonin syndrome}} classically causes autonomic activation with {{c2::hyperreflexia or clonus}} and mental-status change.', 'Serotonin syndrome produces mental-status, autonomic, and neuromuscular hyperactivity. Hyperreflexia and clonus are particularly useful clues.'),
  ('2009-281', 'Psychopharmacology in psychodynamic psychotherapy is best used when medication’s {{c1::meanings and effects are integrated into the patient’s formulation}}.', 'The correct approach integrates the patient’s experience of medication into psychotherapy. Practical effects and symbolic meanings both affect treatment.'),
  ('2009-282', 'In cerebrovascular dementia, {{c1::aspirin}} may help slow progression by reducing further ischemic vascular injury.', 'Aspirin targets recurrent ischemic events in vascular disease. It is not a direct cognitive-enhancing dementia treatment.'),
  ('2009-283', '{{c1::Vitamin B12 deficiency}} can cause macrocytic anemia with impaired proprioception, dysesthesia, gait difficulty, and cognitive change.', 'E — vitamin B12 is the inferred answer after correction of an OCR error in option E. The official answer-key page is absent.'),
  ('2009-284', 'Valid informed consent requires disclosure, voluntariness, and {{c1::decision-making capacity}}.', 'Mental competence or capacity is essential for informed consent. Disclosure alone is insufficient if the patient cannot decide voluntarily and knowingly.'),
  ('2009-285', 'In children with ADHD and comorbid tics, {{c1::stimulants need not be avoided}} and may reduce tic severity.', 'Treatment with stimulants can improve ADHD and may reduce tic severity. Monitor individual symptoms rather than permanently excluding the medication class.'),
  ('2009-286', 'Transient postictal paresis or aphasia {{c1::helps localize the seizure focus}}.', 'Postictal focal deficits provide lateralizing and localizing information. They do not independently establish cause or prognosis.'),
  ('2009-287', 'Casino slot machines use a {{c1::variable-ratio}} reinforcement schedule.', 'Variable-ratio schedules reward behavior after an unpredictable number of responses. This pattern strongly sustains repeated gambling behavior.'),
  ('2009-288', '{{c1::Double depression}} is a major depressive episode superimposed on chronic dysthymic or persistent depressive symptoms.', 'Double depression means major depression occurring on a chronic dysthymic baseline. It signals that the acute syndrome should not be minimized.'),
  ('2009-289', 'Triptans are contraindicated in {{c1::coronary artery disease}} because they can cause vasoconstriction.', 'Coronary artery disease is the correct contraindication. Triptans can constrict coronary vessels and worsen ischemia.'),
  ('2009-290', 'An alcohol blackout is {{c1::anterograde amnesia during intoxication despite remaining awake}}.', 'An alcohol blackout reflects failure to form new memories while intoxicated. It is not synonymous with loss of consciousness.'),
  ('2009-291', 'A key limitation of adoption studies is {{c1::selective placement}}, in which adoptive and biological parents may resemble one another.', 'Adoptive and biological parents may resemble one another through selective placement. This confounds genetic and environmental interpretations.'),
  ('2009-292', 'Charging a patient for a missed psychiatric appointment is ethically permissible when the fee is {{c1::clearly included in the prior contractual agreement}}.', 'D — If payments for missed appointments fall within the contractual agreement. Prior disclosure and consistent application make the charge ethically supportable.'),
  ('2009-293', 'Acute vertigo with ipsilateral facial sensory loss, ataxia, and hoarseness suggests a {{c1::lateral medullary stroke}}.', 'Lateral medullary stroke combines vestibular symptoms with focal brainstem findings. Hoarseness and ipsilateral ataxia help distinguish it from peripheral vestibular disease.'),
  ('2009-294', 'Among commonly tested SSRIs, {{c1::paroxetine}} has the greatest {{c2::antimuscarinic (anticholinergic) activity}}.', 'D — Paroxetine. Its muscarinic receptor affinity makes anticholinergic adverse effects more likely than with the other listed SSRIs.'),
  ('2009-295', 'In a skewed distribution, the {{c1::mean}} is pulled furthest toward the tail and away from the mode.', 'A — Mean. Because it incorporates every value, the mean shifts most toward extreme observations in the direction of skewness.'),
  ('2009-296', 'For many people, self-recognition of sexual orientation commonly occurs during {{c1::adolescence}}, although individual timing varies.', 'C — Adolescence. Retrospective developmental data identify adolescence as the most common period of self-recognition, not a required timetable.'),
  ('2009-297', 'Drug-resistant temporal focal seizures with hippocampal atrophy and increased T2 signal indicate {{c1::mesial temporal sclerosis}}.', 'C — Mesial temporal sclerosis. The characteristic MRI pattern is hippocampal atrophy with T2 or FLAIR hyperintensity in chronic temporal lobe epilepsy.'),
  ('2009-298', 'When a psychotherapy patient reports a genuine adaptive achievement, the appropriate initial response is often {{c1::warm acknowledgment or congratulations}} before interpretation.', 'B — Offering congratulations on the patient’s accomplishment. Supportive validation is more attuned than prematurely interpreting approval-seeking.'),
  ('2009-299', 'After typical amphetamine ingestion, urine testing is usually positive for approximately {{c1::1 to 2 days}}.', 'A — 1 to 2 days. Amphetamine has a relatively short typical urine detection window, although assay and patient factors can alter it.'),
  ('2009-300', 'Genetic anticipation is {{c1::earlier onset or greater severity of a disorder in successive generations}}.', 'Genetic anticipation describes worsening severity or earlier onset across generations. Repeat-expansion disorders are the classic conceptual association.'),
  ('2010-1', 'In a psychiatric fitness-for-duty examination, the examinee must be told that the evaluation is {{c1::not confidential treatment}}.', 'A — examination is not confidential. The psychiatrist is an independent evaluator, so confidentiality limits must be explained at the outset.'),
  ('2010-2', 'During a phone call involving possible imminent violence, first obtain the caller’s {{c1::phone number and current location}}.', 'E — Obtain the phone number and address from which the patient is calling. This preserves the ability to reconnect or mobilize emergency assistance.'),
  ('2010-3', 'Immediate recall is assessed by asking a patient to {{c1::repeat information immediately after presentation}}, such as a digit sequence.', 'E — Can you repeat these six numbers? Direct repetition tests immediate registration and recall.'),
  ('2010-4', '{{c1::Asymmetrical onset and progression}} of motor symptoms is typical of idiopathic Parkinson disease.', 'Asymmetrical onset is typical of idiopathic Parkinson disease. Early gaze palsy, severe autonomic failure, or poor levodopa response suggests an atypical syndrome.'),
  ('2010-5', 'For severe neuroleptic malignant syndrome, {{c1::dopamine agonists}} such as bromocriptine can be considered with supportive care.', 'Dopamine agonists can be adjunctive treatment for severe NMS. The first steps remain stopping the offending agent and providing supportive care.'),
  ('2010-6', 'A girl with developmental regression, loss of purposeful hand use, and hand-wringing stereotypies most likely has {{c1::Rett disorder}}.', 'E — Rett disorder. The signature combination is early regression, decelerated head growth, and loss of purposeful hand movements with stereotypies.'),
  ('2010-7', 'Among the listed DSM editions, homosexuality was first excluded as a diagnosis in the {{c1::1974 DSM-II revision}}.', 'B — DSM-II (1974 edition). The removal followed the 1973 decision and appeared in the 1974 DSM-II printing.'),
  ('2010-8', 'Fetal exposure to {{c1::valproate}} increases the risk of {{c2::neural tube defects}}.', 'Valproate is classically associated with fetal neural tube defects. This association is central to reproductive medication counseling.'),
  ('2010-9', 'Recurrent seizures, headaches, and migrating stroke-like deficits in a young person suggest {{c1::MELAS syndrome}}.', 'E — Mitochondrial encephalomyopathy, lactic acidosis and stroke. MELAS causes seizures and stroke-like episodes that may not follow vascular territories.'),
  ('2010-10', 'The most common psychiatric presentation after stroke is {{c1::depression}}.', 'Depression is the most common psychiatric presentation following stroke. It can meaningfully affect recovery and rehabilitation.'),
  ('2010-11', '{{c1::Needle exchange}} is a harm-reduction intervention for people who inject opioids.', 'Needle exchange reduces harms associated with injection drug use. It does not require abstinence before receiving support.'),
  ('2010-12', 'A medication’s therapeutic index is a relative measure of its {{c1::safety margin between effective and toxic doses}}.', 'C — Safety. The therapeutic index estimates the separation between effective and toxic dosing, not efficacy or tolerability.'),
  ('2010-13', 'The classic adequate monotherapy for juvenile myoclonic epilepsy is {{c1::valproate}}.', 'B — Valproate. Its broad-spectrum antiseizure activity makes it a classic monotherapy choice for juvenile myoclonic epilepsy.'),
  ('2010-14', 'Decreased attention to social cues is a core social-communication feature of {{c1::autism spectrum disorder}}.', 'Autism spectrum disorder commonly involves reduced attention to and reciprocal use of social cues. Withdrawal alone is not sufficient for the diagnosis.'),
  ('2010-15', 'Idiopathic unilateral peripheral facial palsy is frequently associated with {{c1::herpes simplex virus reactivation}}.', 'Herpes simplex is the classic viral association with Bell palsy. Varicella-zoster is more suggestive when facial palsy occurs with painful auricular vesicles.'),
  ('2010-16', 'Historically, the largest category of psychiatric malpractice settlements and adverse verdicts involved {{c1::patient self-harm, including suicide}}.', 'Patient self-harm is the leading historical malpractice category in psychiatry. Sound assessment, communication, and documentation are central safeguards.'),
  ('2010-17', 'Kraepelin’s key contribution was recognizing that major mental illnesses differ in their {{c1::longitudinal courses and outcomes}}.', 'The major mental illnesses have different courses and outcomes. Kraepelin emphasized trajectory and prognosis in classification.'),
  ('2010-18', 'Homicide is most often related to {{c1::emotions and interpersonal circumstances not associated with mental illness}}.', 'Emotions not associated with mental illness account for most homicidal behavior. Psychosis can be relevant in individual cases but is not the most common explanation.'),
  ('2010-19', '{{c1::Fragile X syndrome}} is caused by a {{c2::CGG trinucleotide-repeat expansion in FMR1}}.', 'Fragile X syndrome is the classic developmental-disability disorder caused by a trinucleotide-repeat expansion. The repeat is CGG in FMR1.'),
  ('2010-20', 'Recurrent 15- to 180-minute attacks of severe unilateral orbital pain with ipsilateral tearing and rhinorrhea indicate {{c1::cluster headache}}.', 'Cluster headache produces severe unilateral retro-orbital pain with ipsilateral autonomic symptoms. Attack duration distinguishes it from trigeminal neuralgia.'),
  ('2010-21', 'In operant conditioning, {{c1::intermittent reinforcement}} produces behavior that is {{c2::resistant to extinction}}.', 'Maintaining a behavior that is resistant to extinction is the effect of intermittent reinforcement. Continuous reinforcement generally produces faster initial acquisition.'),
  ('2010-22', 'A {{c1::word-list learning and delayed-recall task}} is particularly useful for detecting the early episodic-memory deficit of Alzheimer disease.', '10-item word list learning task evaluates new learning and retention. These are core early cognitive vulnerabilities in Alzheimer disease.'),
  ('2010-23', 'In a securely attached preschooler, bargaining for an extra bedtime story usually reflects {{c1::age-appropriate verbal negotiation of normative conflicts}}.', 'The use of verbal skills to negotiate normative conflicts is developmentally expected here. A single bedtime request does not establish triangulation or a sleep disorder.'),
  ('2010-24', '{{c1::Narcissistic personality disorder}} is characterized in part by {{c2::interpersonal exploitation}}.', 'Narcissistic personality disorder includes exploitative use of others, entitlement, and impaired empathy.'),
  ('2010-25', 'Alcohol-withdrawal seizures usually occur {{c1::within several hours to a few days after alcohol cessation}}.', 'They develop within several hours to a few days after alcohol cessation. Alcohol-withdrawal seizures are usually generalized tonic-clonic and are not synonymous with delirium tremens.'),
  ('2010-26', 'Among common psychotropics, {{c1::lithium}} is especially associated with increased cognitive adverse effects when given with ECT.', 'Lithium is the key medication to flag before ECT. It may contribute to prolonged confusion or delirium after treatment.'),
  ('2010-27', 'The first intervention for a rape survivor in the emergency department is to {{c1::restore a sense of safety and control}}.', 'Restore a sense of safety with the patient first. Medical, forensic, legal, and psychological options can then be discussed using a choice-based approach.'),
  ('2010-28', 'For a mild, nondangerous delusion in Alzheimer dementia, the preferred initial intervention is {{c1::regular follow-up and reassurance for the family}}.', 'Arrange for regular evaluations of the patient and reassure the family. Antipsychotics are generally reserved for more severe, distressing, or dangerous symptoms.'),
  ('2010-29', '{{c1::Competence}} is a legal determination made through a {{c2::judicial hearing}}.', 'Competence is determined by a court. Psychiatric assessment informs, but does not replace, the legal determination.'),
  ('2010-30', 'For OCD, fluvoxamine commonly requires a target range of {{c1::200–300 mg daily}} rather than only a low starting dose.', 'Fluvoxamine, 200 to 300 mg daily, is an effective OCD dose range. OCD frequently requires higher-end SSRI dosing with an adequate trial.'),
  ('2010-31', 'Adolescent-onset myoclonic jerks with generalized tonic-clonic seizures and 4–6 Hz polyspike-wave EEG indicate {{c1::juvenile myoclonic epilepsy}}.', 'Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy commonly presents with dropping objects from myoclonic jerks. The EEG finding is generalized polyspike-and-wave activity.'),
  ('2010-32', 'The hippocampus and parahippocampal gyrus are essential for {{c1::declarative memory}} for facts and events.', 'Declarative memory includes consciously recalled facts and experiences. Medial temporal lobe structures are central to its formation.'),
  ('2010-33', 'Conducting both individual and family therapy with the same patient creates risk of {{c1::dual relationships and competing therapeutic loyalties}}.', 'Avoiding dual relationships is the central ethical concern. Individual and family treatment roles can create conflicting alliances and confidentiality expectations.'),
  ('2010-34', 'In this historical classification, severe early caregiving disruption with indiscriminate sociability was labeled {{c1::reactive attachment disorder}}, although current terminology favors {{c2::disinhibited social engagement disorder}}.', 'Reactive attachment disorder is the correct historical answer. Current terminology more specifically identifies this indiscriminately social pattern as disinhibited social engagement disorder.'),
  ('2010-35', '{{c1::Transient global amnesia}} causes abrupt, temporary {{c2::anterograde amnesia}} with repetitive questioning and preserved alertness.', 'Transient global amnesia is an acute isolated anterograde amnestic syndrome. Preserved identity and absent focal deficits help distinguish it from delirium or stroke.'),
  ('2010-36', 'For a patient with schizoid personality disorder seeking better interpersonal relationships, {{c1::group therapy}} provides direct social practice and feedback.', 'Group therapy provides live interpersonal experience. It can help a patient notice and modify withdrawal in a structured setting.'),
  ('2010-37', '{{c1::Content validity}} is the extent to which a test’s items represent the domain of the construct it is intended to measure.', 'Content validity concerns representative coverage of a construct. It is not merely whether a test appears plausible on its face.'),
  ('2010-38', 'Diffuse myalgias with macroglossia and percussion myoedema suggest {{c1::hypothyroidism}}.', 'Hypothyroidism can cause a myopathy with stiffness and diffuse aching. Macroglossia and percussion myoedema are particularly helpful clues.'),
  ('2010-39', '{{c1::Hypochondriasis}} involves persistent preoccupation with serious illness despite appropriate medical evaluation.', 'Hypochondriasis is driven by illness preoccupation, not conscious deception. Poor insight indicates limited recognition that the belief may be excessive.'),
  ('2010-40', '{{c1::Methadone}}, a long-acting opioid agonist, can be used to manage {{c2::opioid withdrawal}}.', 'Methadone directly suppresses opioid withdrawal through opioid agonism. Clonidine may relieve autonomic symptoms but is not the preferred primary answer here.'),
  ('2010-41', '{{c1::Malingering}} is intentional symptom feigning motivated by {{c2::external (secondary) gain}}.', 'Malingering differs from factitious disorder by the presence of an external incentive. Both can involve conscious, intentional fabrication.'),
  ('2010-42', '{{c1::Opioid toxicity}} is suggested by CNS depression, {{c2::respiratory depression}}, and miosis.', 'Codeine is an opioid and can cause respiratory depression and pinpoint pupils. Ventilatory support and naloxone are key emergency responses when indicated.'),
  ('2010-43', 'A patient receiving methadone maintenance who develops severe cancer pain may need {{c1::additional opioid analgesia}} because maintenance dosing does not ensure adequate {{c2::analgesia}}.', 'Additional opioid analgesia may be needed for severe cancer pain in a patient on methadone maintenance. Continue coordinated addiction treatment while titrating analgesia safely.'),
  ('2010-44', 'Accepting a {{c1::small, low-value token of gratitude}} near termination can be an acceptable therapeutic boundary crossing when clinically appropriate.', 'Receiving a modest gift near termination may be acceptable after considering its meaning and effect. Financial barter and role-blurring social arrangements are boundary violations.'),
  ('2010-45', 'Among classic psychological tests, the {{c1::Rorschach Inkblot Test}} is highly {{c2::unstructured}} because it uses ambiguous stimuli and open-ended responses.', 'The Rorschach is the most unstructured option because its inkblot stimulus is highly ambiguous. MMPI and WAIS are standardized, structured measures.'),
  ('2010-46', '{{c1::Migraine with brainstem aura}} (historically called basilar migraine) can cause vertigo, dysarthria, bilateral paresthesias, and occipital headache.', 'Basilar migraine is the historical name for migraine with brainstem aura. Its neurologic symptoms are typically transient and reversible.'),
  ('2010-47', '{{c1::Semantic memory}} is memory for general facts and knowledge, such as knowing a holiday parade occurs annually.', 'Semantic memory stores decontextualized facts. A personal recollection of attending the parade would instead be episodic memory.'),
  ('2010-48', 'The primary motivation in {{c1::factitious disorder}} is to {{c2::assume the sick role}}, not to obtain an external reward.', 'Factitious disorder involves intentional falsification to occupy the sick role. External incentives instead point toward malingering.'),
  ('2010-49', 'The ethical principle of “first do no harm” is {{c1::nonmaleficence}}.', 'Nonmaleficence is the duty to avoid unnecessary harm. Beneficence instead emphasizes promoting the patient’s welfare.'),
  ('2010-50', 'A characteristic MRI-associated finding in {{c1::Alzheimer disease}} is reduced {{c2::hippocampal volume}}.', 'Reduced hippocampal volume is a characteristic Alzheimer-associated MRI finding. It correlates with impaired new episodic memory formation.'),
  ('2010-51', 'Concluding that all future applications will fail after one rejection is {{c1::overgeneralization}} and {{c2::all-or-none thinking}}.', 'Overgeneralization turns one rejection into a universal prediction. This is a cognitive distortion, not formal thought disorder.'),
  ('2010-52', '{{c1::Point prevalence}} is the proportion of a defined population with a condition at {{c2::one specific time}}.', 'Point prevalence is a cross-sectional proportion of existing cases. It differs from incidence, which counts new cases over time.'),
  ('2010-53', 'Fatal familial insomnia is an inherited {{c1::prion disease}} caused by abnormal folding of {{c2::prion protein}}.', 'Prion is correct because fatal familial insomnia is a hereditary protein-misfolding neurodegenerative disorder. It is not caused by a conventional bacterial, viral, or fungal infection.'),
  ('2010-54', 'In suicide-risk documentation, record the {{c1::clinical reasons alternative responses were rejected}} and the rationale for the chosen plan.', 'Why alternatives were rejected is correct because it documents clinical judgment and disposition reasoning. Credentials or speculation about inevitable suicide do not substitute for a clear risk formulation.'),
  ('2010-55', 'The first treatment for suspected Wernicke encephalopathy is prompt {{c1::parenteral thiamine}}, given before or with {{c2::glucose}}.', 'Thiamine is correct because the triad suggests Wernicke encephalopathy. It treats the underlying deficiency; sedatives and antipsychotics do not.'),
  ('2010-56', '{{c1::Histrionic personality disorder}} is associated with rapidly shifting, shallow emotional expression.', 'Histrionic personality disorder features excessive emotionality and attention seeking. Its affect is often described as shallow and rapidly shifting.'),
  ('2010-57', 'Rapidly progressive dementia with {{c1::stimulus-sensitive myoclonus}} strongly suggests a {{c2::spongiform encephalopathy}}.', 'Spongiform encephalopathy is correct because the syndrome is classic for prion disease, particularly Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease. The alternatives do not characteristically cause this combination.'),
  ('2010-58', 'Putting a patient at ease while conveying compassion and competence primarily builds {{c1::rapport}}.', 'Rapport is correct because these behaviors strengthen trust and therapeutic connection. Reflection and active listening are techniques that may help create it.'),
  ('2010-59', 'A core motivational interviewing strategy is to {{c1::develop discrepancy}} between current behavior and the patient’s own values or goals.', 'Develop discrepancy is correct because motivational interviewing elicits the patient’s own reasons for change. Direct confrontation commonly increases resistance.'),
  ('2010-60', 'In this keyed HCV vignette, normal ammonia favors {{c1::HCV-associated CNS effects}} rather than {{c2::hepatic encephalopathy}}.', 'HCV infection of brain is the keyed answer because normal ammonia argues against hepatic encephalopathy in this vignette. Cognitive impairment still requires a broad clinical differential.'),
  ('2010-61', 'For exposure-based PTSD treatment, prognosis is generally most favorable after a {{c1::discrete traumatic event}} with {{c2::accessible memory and stable engagement}}.', 'Victim of a random violent assault is correct because it represents a circumscribed trauma without the listed major treatment complications. This does not mean other PTSD presentations are untreatable.'),
  ('2010-62', 'Recall of items after several minutes tests {{c1::short-term (recent) memory}}, whereas immediate repetition tests {{c2::immediate memory}}.', 'Short-term memory is correct because the task uses delayed recall after a few minutes. Immediate memory would be tested without a delay.'),
  ('2010-63', 'For historical interferon treatment, {{c1::depression with suicidal ideation}} is a major relative contraindication.', 'Depression with suicidal ideation raises major safety concern with interferon treatment. The question reflects interferon-era hepatitis C practice.'),
  ('2010-64', 'In a young child, intermittent {{c1::solitary genital touching in private}} is usually {{c2::normal developmental behavior}}.', 'Normal behavior is correct because the genital rubbing is private and not paired with concerning features. Context determines when further evaluation is warranted.'),
  ('2010-65', '{{c1::Fever with severe generalized muscle rigidity}} strongly suggests neuroleptic malignant syndrome.', 'Fever and severe muscle rigidity are classic findings in neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Hyperreflexia, myoclonus, and diarrhea instead suggest serotonin syndrome.'),
  ('2010-66', 'After a {{c1::near-lethal suicide attempt}} with ongoing ambivalence about survival and follow-up, the initial level of care is {{c2::inpatient psychiatric hospitalization}}.', 'Inpatient hospitalization is correct because recent lethality, concealment, and ongoing ambivalence indicate high acute risk. Lower-intensity settings are not adequate initially.'),
  ('2010-67', 'The most common cardiovascular effect of lithium is {{c1::benign ECG T-wave flattening or inversion}}.', 'Benign T wave changes are correct because they are the common lithium-associated ECG finding. More serious conduction disturbances can occur but are less common.'),
  ('2010-68', 'Under the reasonable-patient standard, informed consent requires disclosure of {{c1::material information most patients would want to know}} before deciding.', 'The information most patients would want to know is correct because it states the reasonable-patient disclosure standard. Every theoretical risk need not be listed.'),
  ('2010-69', 'Kohut’s self psychology centers on the need for {{c1::empathic selfobject interactions}} to support a cohesive {{c2::self}}.', 'The individual''s need for empathic interaction with self-objects is correct because it is central to Kohut’s self psychology. Classical conflict-and-drive theory is a different psychoanalytic model.'),
  ('2010-70', 'Progressive gait difficulty and urinary incontinence with neck pain require {{c1::urgent cervical-spine MRI}} to evaluate for cord compression.', 'MRI scan of the cervical spine is the immediate test to exclude spinal cord compression in this presentation. Cervical cord compression is the key concern.'),
  ('2010-71', 'For major depression after myocardial infarction, {{c1::sertraline}} is a preferred antidepressant option among these choices.', 'Sertraline is the keyed choice because of its favorable cardiovascular profile relative to the listed alternatives. Cardiac and psychiatric monitoring remain important.'),
  ('2010-72', 'The main purpose of a {{c1::double-blind study}} is to reduce {{c2::expectancy bias in outcome assessment}}.', 'Eliminating examiner-expectation bias is correct because blinding masks treatment assignment. Randomization, not blinding, determines group allocation.'),
  ('2010-73', 'In Kleinian object-relations theory, depression may reflect {{c1::guilt over imagined harm to loved internal objects}} and a wish for {{c2::reparation}}.', 'A — Melanie Klein linked depressive affect to guilt, ambivalence, and concern for loved objects. Her depressive position includes a wish to repair perceived damage.'),
  ('2010-74', 'A fixed false belief that others intend to harm or persecute the person is a {{c1::persecutory delusion}}.', 'B — a delusion is a fixed false belief. This statement describes a persecutory belief rather than a sensory perception.'),
  ('2010-75', 'The triad of {{c1::gait impairment, cognitive decline, and urinary incontinence}} suggests {{c2::normal pressure hydrocephalus}}.', 'E — Normal pressure hydrocephalus classically causes gait dysfunction, cognitive impairment, and urinary incontinence. Gait symptoms often precede the others.'),
  ('2010-76', 'The most evidence-based treatment for oppositional defiant disorder is {{c1::parent management training}}.', 'D — Parent management training teaches consistent reinforcement and consequences. It is first-line psychosocial treatment for core ODD behavior.'),
  ('2010-77', 'Morning headaches and daytime fatigue, especially with marked sedation from a benzodiazepine, should prompt evaluation for {{c1::obstructive sleep apnea}}.', 'B — sleep apnea can present with nonrestorative sleep, morning headaches, and daytime fatigue. Sedating drugs may compound these symptoms.'),
  ('2010-78', 'In {{c1::nonfluent (Broca) aphasia}}, simple auditory comprehension is relatively preserved while speech and repetition are impaired.', 'The patient was able to follow the verbal request, “Close your eyes,” because simple comprehension is relatively preserved in nonfluent aphasia. Speech production and repetition are impaired.'),
  ('2010-79', 'Agitation, paranoia, hypertension, ataxia, and {{c1::horizontal or vertical nystagmus}} strongly suggest {{c2::phencyclidine intoxication}}.', 'C — phencyclidine intoxication is associated with dissociation, agitation, hypertension, ataxia, and nystagmus. Nystagmus is a particularly useful examination clue.'),
  ('2010-80', '{{c1::Voices arguing}} is a classic Schneiderian first-rank symptom of schizophrenia.', 'Voices arguing is a first-rank symptom in Schneider’s historical framework. It is not by itself diagnostic of schizophrenia.'),
  ('2010-81', 'Among antidepressants, {{c1::venlafaxine}} can cause {{c2::dose-related hypertension}} because of increasing noradrenergic activity.', 'B — Venlafaxine is classically associated with dose-related blood-pressure elevation. Monitor blood pressure during treatment, especially with titration.'),
  ('2010-82', 'Difficulty identifying and describing one''s own emotions is called {{c1::alexithymia}}.', 'D — alexithymia means limited awareness and verbal description of emotions. It differs from anhedonia, which is reduced capacity for pleasure.'),
  ('2010-83', 'Historically, Asperger disorder was distinguished from autistic disorder chiefly by {{c1::absence of clinically significant early language delay}}.', 'C — Language development was the key historical discriminator. Current practice incorporates these presentations within autism spectrum disorder.'),
  ('2010-84', 'In the middle phase of interpersonal psychotherapy, therapy focuses on the patient''s {{c1::current relationships}} within a selected interpersonal problem area.', 'A — current relationships are the central focus of IPT middle sessions. Therapy works on a defined problem such as grief, a dispute, or a role transition.'),
  ('2010-85', 'The standard acute medication treatment for antipsychotic-induced {{c1::akathisia}} is {{c2::propranolol}}.', 'D — Propranolol reduces acute symptoms of neuroleptic-induced akathisia. Also reassess the antipsychotic dose and necessity.'),
  ('2010-86', 'Reinforcing a behavior after every {{c1::specified number of responses}} is a {{c2::fixed-ratio schedule}}.', 'D — fixed-ratio reinforcement is based on a constant number of responses. Every third completed chore is an FR-3 schedule.'),
  ('2010-87', 'For predictable performance-only social anxiety, a {{c1::beta blocker such as atenolol}} may be taken shortly before the performance to reduce autonomic symptoms.', 'B — Atenolol can reduce tremor and palpitations before a discrete performance. It is not a primary treatment for generalized social anxiety.'),
  ('2010-88', 'A {{c1::living will}} is an advance directive that documents a person''s preferences for future medical treatment if capacity is lost.', 'A — The living will is a central advance-directive document. Patients should receive information about it but are not required to sign one.'),
  ('2010-89', 'In Kohlberg''s preconventional punishment-and-obedience stage, an action is wrong because {{c1::an authority figure will impose a negative consequence}}.', 'E — Presence of an adult who will punish the behavior reflects preconventional moral reasoning. The judgment is based on external consequences rather than abstract principles.'),
  ('2010-90', 'The most effective symptomatic treatment for {{c1::blepharospasm}} is {{c2::botulinum toxin injection}}.', 'Botulinum toxin is the standard effective treatment for blepharospasm. It locally reduces dystonic eyelid-muscle contraction.'),
  ('2010-91', 'Learned helplessness is classically grounded in {{c1::operant conditioning}}, in which behavior seems unable to control an aversive outcome.', 'Operant conditioning is correct because uncontrollable consequences teach that responding does not change the outcome.'),
  ('2010-92', 'After an unavoidable psychotherapy-session interruption, the therapist should {{c1::explore the disruption and the patient’s feelings about it}} during the remaining scheduled time.', 'Discussing the interruption is correct because it addresses a potential alliance rupture in the here-and-now.'),
  ('2010-93', 'Pediatric bipolar I disorder requires {{c1::a distinct episodic period of abnormal mood with associated manic symptoms}}, rather than chronic irritability alone.', 'A distinct period of abnormal mood and episodic symptoms is required because mania is an episodic syndrome. Chronic hyperactivity or poor frustration tolerance is nonspecific.'),
  ('2010-94', 'The basic ethical principle traditionally used to justify paternalism is {{c1::beneficence}}.', 'Beneficence supports actions intended to promote a patient’s welfare. Paternalism may conflict with autonomy when a capable patient disagrees.'),
  ('2010-95', 'Greater alcohol-dependence risk in adoptees with an affected biological parent supports the {{c1::disease model}} through inherited vulnerability.', 'Disease model is correct because risk tracks biological relatedness even when the child is reared elsewhere.'),
  ('2010-96', 'For isolated sleep-onset insomnia after nonpharmacologic measures, {{c1::zaleplon}} is a short-acting hypnotic option.', 'Zaleplon is correct because its very short duration fits difficulty falling asleep.'),
  ('2010-97', 'In Kleinian theory, accepting good and bad qualities in the same loved person reflects the {{c1::depressive position}}.', 'Depressive position is correct because it integrates good and bad aspects of the same object.'),
  ('2010-98', 'Early falls, axial parkinsonism, and impaired {{c1::vertical saccades or gaze}} are characteristic of {{c2::progressive supranuclear palsy}}.', 'Progressive supranuclear palsy classically causes early falls and vertical supranuclear gaze palsy. Typical Parkinson disease usually lacks this early ocular motor pattern.'),
  ('2010-99', 'Untreated major depression is classically associated with {{c1::reduced REM latency}} on sleep studies.', 'Reduced REM latency is correct because REM sleep begins earlier in the classic depressive sleep pattern.'),
  ('2010-100', 'Adopting elements of a host culture while retaining valued heritage practices is called {{c1::acculturation}}.', 'Acculturation is correct because the person incorporates a new cultural practice while retaining heritage traditions.'),
  ('2010-101', 'The first treatment step for adolescent substance-use disorders is often {{c1::engaging the adolescent and family to reduce drug-seeking behavior}}.', 'Engaging the adolescent and family is correct because it establishes the alliance and structure needed for change.'),
  ('2010-102', 'For severe acute cannabis-induced panic after medical assessment, {{c1::lorazepam}} can provide rapid anxiolysis.', 'Lorazepam is correct because it can rapidly relieve severe acute panic and agitation.'),
  ('2010-103', 'A seizure with automatisms, impaired awareness, and postictal confusion is a {{c1::focal impaired-awareness (complex partial) seizure}}.', 'Complex partial seizures involve focal onset with impaired awareness. Automatisms and postictal confusion distinguish them from typical absence seizures.'),
  ('2010-104', 'Restlessness, palpitations, and psychomotor agitation after markedly increased caffeine use suggest {{c1::caffeine intoxication}}.', 'Caffeine intoxication is correct because escalating stimulant use directly explains the autonomic arousal and activation.'),
  ('2010-105', 'Mirtazapine increases norepinephrine and serotonin release through {{c1::alpha-2 adrenergic receptor antagonism}}.', 'Alpha-2 adrenergic receptor antagonism is correct because it disinhibits norepinephrine release and increases serotonin signaling.'),
  ('2010-106', 'A classic and important cause of delirium in children is {{c1::central nervous system infection}}.', 'Central nervous system infection is correct because acute brain infection can cause delirium in children.'),
  ('2010-107', 'Asymmetric parkinsonism with apraxia and an alien-limb phenomenon suggests {{c1::corticobasal degeneration}}.', 'Corticobasal ganglionic degeneration is correct because it combines asymmetric parkinsonism with apraxia and alien-limb behavior.'),
  ('2010-108', 'A {{c1::negative therapeutic reaction}} is paradoxical worsening after an accurate, appropriate therapeutic interpretation or improvement.', 'The patient gets worse despite an appropriate interpretation, which defines a negative therapeutic reaction. It may reflect unconscious conflict about recovery.'),
  ('2010-109', 'For a medically ill patient with ego-syntonic perfectionistic control needs, {{c1::provide clear answers and actively involve the patient in treatment}}.', 'Active involvement is correct because detailed information and collaboration reduce conflict for a control-seeking patient.'),
  ('2010-110', 'In an eating-disorder evaluation, enlarged parotid glands suggest {{c1::recurrent self-induced vomiting}}.', 'Self-induced vomiting can cause parotid enlargement. It is a clue to purging, not to refeeding syndrome.'),
  ('2010-111', 'Winnicott’s {{c1::holding environment}} is the safe context created by consistent, reliable caregiving.', 'Safe context provided by consistent and reliable parenting defines the holding environment. It supports early emotional integration and regulation.'),
  ('2010-112', 'In myasthenic crisis with impending respiratory failure, the first priority is {{c1::mechanical ventilation}}.', 'Mechanical ventilation is correct because respiratory failure must be stabilized immediately before disease-directed rescue therapy.'),
  ('2010-113', 'Serial 7s on the mental-status examination primarily assesses {{c1::concentration and sustained attention}}, not memory.', 'Concentration is correct because serial 7s requires sustained, effortful attention. Calculation is involved, but it is not the primary construct tested.'),
  ('2010-114', 'Chewing {{c1::khat}} leaves produces stimulant effects such as alertness and wakefulness because of amphetamine-like cathinones.', 'Khat is correct because its leaves are chewed for stimulant effects. Cathinone helps explain increased alertness and reduced fatigue.'),
  ('2010-115', 'Atomoxetine treats ADHD primarily as a {{c1::selective norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor}}.', 'Atomoxetine is correct because it blocks the norepinephrine transporter. It is neither an SSRI nor an alpha-2 adrenergic agonist.'),
  ('2010-116', 'Short stature, gonadal dysgenesis with delayed puberty, and relative verbal-over-visuospatial strength suggest {{c1::Turner syndrome}}.', 'Turner syndrome is correct because short stature, delayed puberty, and cardiac anomalies form a classic pattern. Relative verbal strength with nonverbal weaknesses is also compatible.'),
  ('2010-117', 'For chronic localized provoked vulvodynia, {{c1::nortriptyline}} may be considered as a pain-modulating tricyclic antidepressant.', 'Nortriptyline is correct as the historical best-listed pain-modulating option. Antifungal treatment and reassurance alone do not fit the negative workup and persistent symptoms.'),
  ('2010-118', 'In historical sleep staging, {{c1::stage 4}} is the deepest slow-wave sleep and corresponds to modern {{c2::N3}} sleep.', 'Stage 4 is correct in the older staging system because it denotes the deepest slow-wave sleep. Modern scoring combines stages 3 and 4 as N3.'),
  ('2010-119', 'Persistent avoidance of eating because of fear of {{c1::choking}} is most consistent with a {{c2::specific phobia}} when body-image concerns are absent.', 'Specific phobia is correct because the avoidance is tied to the discrete feared event of choking. There is no evidence of recurrent unexpected panic or weight-and-shape pathology.'),
  ('2010-120', 'When a new psychotherapy patient asks to include a spouse at intake, welcome the request if desired but arrange {{c1::private time with the patient}}.', 'The response is correct because it honors the patient’s preference while preserving a confidential individual assessment. It is neither rigidly exclusionary nor boundary-free.'),
  ('2010-121', 'Combining cocaine with the norepinephrine-reuptake-blocking TCA desipramine can cause {{c1::hypertension}} from increased sympathetic activity.', 'Hypertension is correct because cocaine and desipramine both enhance noradrenergic signaling. Their combination can intensify cardiovascular toxicity.'),
  ('2010-122', 'Adverse life events are classically associated with precipitating {{c1::depressive episodes}} in bipolar disorder.', 'Depressive episode is correct because life stress is especially associated with bipolar depressive relapse. Stress may influence other symptoms, but they are not the best answer here.'),
  ('2010-123', 'Bilateral lesions of the {{c1::anterior cingulate gyrus}} can cause akinetic mutism with markedly reduced spontaneous speech and movement.', 'Anterior cingulate gyrus is correct because it is crucial for initiation and motivated behavior. Bilateral injury can leave a patient awake but profoundly apathetic and mute.'),
  ('2010-124', 'Pulmonary talc granulomas suggest {{c1::intravenous injection of crushed oral tablets}}, classically including methylphenidate.', 'Methylphenidate is correct because crushed tablets may be injected and their talc excipient can cause pulmonary granulomas. The pathology is a route-of-use clue.'),
  ('2010-125', 'A clinically significant trauma response occurring from 3 days to {{c1::1 month}} after trauma is {{c2::acute stress disorder}} when criteria are met.', 'Acute stress disorder is correct because the trauma symptoms remain within the first month. PTSD requires symptoms lasting longer than one month.'),
  ('2010-126', 'Herpes zoster is caused by reactivation of {{c1::varicella zoster virus}} and produces a painful unilateral dermatomal vesicular rash.', 'Varicella zoster virus is correct because its reactivation causes shingles. The unilateral thoracic dermatomal vesicles are classic.'),
  ('2010-127', 'Because it {{c1::lowers the seizure threshold}}, {{c2::bupropion}} is the classic medication to consider holding before ECT.', 'Bupropion is correct because it is proconvulsant and may increase the risk of prolonged seizures during ECT. Medication decisions must be coordinated with the ECT team.'),
  ('2010-128', 'Acute functional neurological visual loss after a major stressor often {{c1::fully resolves within days to weeks}}.', 'Symptoms will fully resolve in a matter of days or weeks in this acute functional presentation. Favorable prognostic features include abrupt onset and an identifiable stressor.'),
  ('2010-129', 'A toilet-trained child who resumes accidents after a new sibling arrives is showing {{c1::normal regression}}.', 'Normal regression is a temporary return to an earlier developmental behavior during stress. The arrival of a sibling is a classic precipitant.'),
  ('2010-130', 'In split treatment, the prescribing psychiatrist manages {{c1::medication evaluation and prescribing}} while maintaining regular contact with the psychotherapist.', 'Evaluate a patient''s need for medication and prescribe accordingly while maintaining regular contact with the therapist. Coordination helps prevent fragmented care.'),
  ('2010-131', 'In a patient taking cyclosporine, {{c1::St. John’s wort}} can substantially lower drug exposure through enzyme induction.', 'St. John’s wort (Hypericum perforatum) is the corrected answer. It was added as choice E during editorial reconstruction and was not present in the printed item.'),
  ('2010-132', '{{c1::Cyclothymia}} is a chronic pattern of fluctuating hypomanic and depressive symptoms that do not meet full episode criteria.', 'Cyclothymia fits recurrent subthreshold elevated and depressive symptoms. Rapid cycling requires qualifying bipolar mood episodes.'),
  ('2010-133', 'Severe focal back pain, infection risk, and evolving weakness or bladder dysfunction suggest {{c1::spinal epidural abscess}}.', 'Spinal epidural abscess causes severe back pain followed by cord-compression deficits. Intravenous drug use is an important risk factor.'),
  ('2010-134', 'Disproportionate impairment on the {{c1::Wisconsin Card Sorting Test}} suggests executive dysfunction from {{c2::vascular cognitive impairment}}.', 'Wisconsin Card Sorting Test impairment reflects executive dysfunction and poor set shifting. These deficits are often prominent in vascular dementia.'),
  ('2010-135', 'Core gender identity is typically established by about {{c1::3 years of age}}.', '3 years is the expected age for establishment of core gender identity. Gender constancy develops later than core identity.'),
  ('2010-136', '{{c1::Tetrabenazine}}, a VMAT2 inhibitor, can reduce refractory motor and vocal tics in Tourette syndrome.', 'Tetrabenazine is effective for refractory Tourette tics through VMAT2 inhibition. Monitor for mood and extrapyramidal adverse effects.'),
  ('2010-137', 'An acute dystonic reaction caused by a dopamine-blocking medication is treated with {{c1::diphenhydramine}}.', 'Diphenhydramine is first-line treatment for acute dystonia from prochlorperazine. Haloperidol would add further dopamine blockade.'),
  ('2010-138', 'In ongoing domestic violence, {{c1::conjoint marital therapy}} is contraindicated initially because it can increase danger and coercion.', 'Conjoint marital therapy can endanger a survivor when abuse is ongoing. Initial management prioritizes confidential safety assessment and support.'),
  ('2010-139', 'In suspected Wilson disease, {{c1::increased 24-hour urinary copper excretion}} supports the diagnosis.', 'Urinary copper excretion is increased in Wilson disease. The psychiatric, neurologic, and hepatic findings form the characteristic pattern.'),
  ('2010-140', 'In a patient taking clozapine who develops fever, dehydration, confusion, and rigidity, {{c1::discontinue clozapine}} while evaluating toxicity and delirium.', 'Discontinue clozapine because acute illness with delirium and rigidity raises concern for toxicity. Increasing clozapine or adding benztropine could worsen the presentation.'),
  ('2010-141', 'For escalating agitation in a person with dementia living at home, a key intervention is {{c1::assessing caregiver burnout and support needs}}.', 'Assessing for caregiver burnout addresses whether home care remains safe and sustainable. Restraints and punitive consequences are generally unhelpful.'),
  ('2010-142', 'In a young child, morning vomiting with ataxia and diplopia suggests a posterior fossa {{c1::medulloblastoma}}.', 'Medulloblastoma is a pediatric posterior fossa malignancy. It can cause increased intracranial pressure and cerebellar signs.'),
  ('2010-143', 'Attributions are {{c1::causal explanations}} assigned to events and personal experiences.', 'Causal explanations of events and personal experiences define attribution processes. They may be internal or external and influence emotion.'),
  ('2010-144', 'After amphetamine cessation, withdrawal-related dysphoria and anhedonia may substantially improve over {{c1::2 to 4 months}}.', '2 to 4 months is the keyed interval for substantial improvement in amphetamine-withdrawal dysphoria and anhedonia. Acute symptoms begin sooner, but reward recovery can be prolonged.'),
  ('2010-145', 'For selected patients with complicated grief, {{c1::mature object relations}} predict a better response to short-term psychodynamic psychotherapy than supportive therapy.', 'Mature object relations support reflection, affect tolerance, and use of therapeutic interpretation. These capacities favor short-term psychodynamic psychotherapy.'),
  ('2010-146', 'When a patient says someone else required the psychiatric evaluation, an empathic response is {{c1::“How do you feel about that?”}}.', 'A — “How do you feel about that?” centers the patient’s experience. It opens rapport without endorsing either side of the referral conflict.'),
  ('2010-147', '{{c1::Vagus nerve stimulation}} is a device-based treatment option for {{c2::medication-refractory epilepsy}}.', 'A — Electrical stimulation of the vagus nerve is VNS. It is an adjunctive option for selected patients with drug-resistant epilepsy.'),
  ('2010-148', 'Among the listed antipsychotics, {{c1::quetiapine}} is least likely to {{c2::worsen motor symptoms in Parkinson disease}}.', 'D — Quetiapine is the best answer among these options because of relatively lower risk of worsening parkinsonism. Current care also considers medication adjustment and Parkinson disease-specific treatments.'),
  ('2010-149', '{{c1::Late-onset schizophrenia}} occurs more often in {{c2::women}} than in men.', 'C — Late-onset schizophrenia has a female predominance. It is not defined by inevitably poor response or prognosis.'),
  ('2010-150', 'A {{c1::new focal-onset seizure in an older adult}} should prompt evaluation for a {{c2::structural brain lesion such as glioblastoma}}.', 'C — Glioblastoma multiforme can present with a first focal seizure in later adulthood. Focal onset makes a structural lesion especially concerning.'),
  ('2010-151', 'The {{c1::clock drawing test}} is a rapid cognitive screen commonly used when evaluating for {{c2::Alzheimer dementia}}.', 'E — Alzheimer dementia is the classic association. Clock drawing screens multiple cognitive domains but is not diagnostic alone.'),
  ('2010-152', '{{c1::Rapid cycling}} bipolar disorder is a classic predictor of a {{c2::poorer response to lithium}}.', 'E — Rapid cycling predicts less favorable lithium response. Classic episodic mania is the contrasting lithium-responsive pattern.'),
  ('2010-153', '{{c1::Salicylate (aspirin) toxicity}} classically causes tinnitus with a {{c2::mixed respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis}}.', 'A — Aspirin overdose causes tinnitus, gastrointestinal symptoms, and acid-base abnormalities. The classic pattern often includes respiratory alkalosis plus metabolic acidosis.'),
  ('2010-154', '{{c1::Ethnicity}} denotes a shared sense of identity and history that may include {{c2::common ancestry and culture}}.', 'B — Ethnicity integrates shared ancestry with cultural identity and history. Race, religion, and language may overlap with it but are distinct constructs.'),
  ('2010-155', 'Severe headache and papilledema in a patient with thrombotic risk should prompt evaluation for {{c1::cerebral venous sinus thrombosis}} even when noncontrast CT is normal.', 'Sagittal sinus thrombosis is the corrected, best-listed answer. It is an independently inferred answer because the official answer-key page is absent.'),
  ('2010-156', 'Normal developmental regression is a {{c1::transient return to earlier behaviors despite continued overall maturation}}.', 'The transient return to earlier behaviors despite overall maturation is normal regression. Persistent loss of milestones is not explained by normal regression.'),
  ('2010-157', 'The {{c1::CRAFFT}} adolescent substance-use screen asks about riding in a {{c2::car driven by someone using alcohol or drugs}}.', 'D — This is the CRAFFT car question. It is a high-yield adolescent screen because it identifies hazardous substance-related driving exposure.'),
  ('2010-158', 'Age-related {{c1::cortical atrophy}} predisposes to chronic subdural hematoma by stretching bridging veins.', 'Cortical atrophy stretches bridging veins. Minor trauma can then produce a slowly symptomatic chronic subdural hematoma.'),
  ('2010-159', 'In adoption research, {{c1::selective placement}} confounds genetic and environmental effects when {{c2::adoptive and biological parents resemble one another}}.', 'E — Resemblance between adoptive and biological parents creates selective-placement confounding. It blurs the intended separation of genes from rearing environment.'),
  ('2010-160', '{{c1::Frotteurism}} involves sexual arousal from touching or rubbing against a {{c2::nonconsenting person}}.', 'C — Frotteurism is sexual arousal involving rubbing against nonconsenting people. Crowded transportation is a classic setting.'),
  ('2010-161', 'A resident providing supervised psychotherapy should {{c1::inform the patient from the beginning}} that the case will be discussed with a {{c2::supervisor}}.', 'B — Disclosure of supervision belongs at the start of treatment. It is part of informed consent and the psychotherapy frame.'),
  ('2010-162', 'In an older patient with depression and cognitive complaints, a persistent {{c1::naming deficit}} more strongly supports {{c2::dementia}} than depression-related cognitive inefficiency.', 'B — Naming deficits suggest a domain-specific language impairment more typical of dementia. Inattention and impaired learning can occur with depression as well.'),
  ('2010-163', 'In a young adult with abrupt headache, vomiting, and focal deficits, intratumoral hemorrhage can occur in a {{c1::hemorrhagic oligodendroglioma}}.', 'Hemorrhagic oligodendroglioma can produce abrupt neurologic deterioration through intratumoral bleeding and acute mass effect.'),
  ('2010-164', 'A test’s ability to measure the construct it is intended to measure is {{c1::validity}}.', 'Validity means a measure assesses what it is supposed to assess. Reliability instead refers to consistency.'),
  ('2010-165', 'Depression with {{c1::mood reactivity}}, hyperphagia or weight gain, and rejection sensitivity has {{c2::atypical features}}.', 'Depression with atypical features is supported by mood reactivity, increased eating or weight gain, and rejection sensitivity.'),
  ('2010-166', '{{c1::Flumazenil}} competitively reverses benzodiazepine effects at the GABA-A receptor.', 'Flumazenil is the benzodiazepine antagonist. Its use requires caution because it can precipitate seizures or withdrawal.'),
  ('2010-167', 'A persistent childhood pattern of angry defiance and blaming others without serious rights violations is {{c1::oppositional defiant disorder}}.', 'Oppositional defiant disorder involves angry irritability, defiance, argumentativeness, and blaming others.'),
  ('2010-168', 'An appropriate traditional use of projective testing is helping detect {{c1::subtle psychotic thought processes}}.', 'Detect the presence of subtle psychotic thought processes is the keyed traditional use of projective testing. It does not replace comprehensive diagnostic assessment.'),
  ('2010-169', 'Combining an MAOI with a serotonin-enhancing agent can cause {{c1::serotonin syndrome}}, often with {{c2::clonus or myoclonus}}.', 'Serotonin syndrome is supported by the MAOI–5-HTP combination and accompanying myoclonic jerks.'),
  ('2010-170', 'Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis is suggested by progressive weakness with combined {{c1::upper motor neuron signs}} and {{c2::lower motor neuron signs}}.', 'Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis causes both denervation findings and corticospinal signs. This combination distinguishes it from isolated neuromuscular-junction, peripheral-nerve, or central demyelinating disorders.'),
  ('2010-171', 'Among patients with schizophrenia, the most commonly abused substance is {{c1::nicotine}}.', 'Nicotine is the most prevalent substance in this comparison among patients with schizophrenia.'),
  ('2010-172', 'In psychotherapist-patient privilege, the {{c1::patient}} holds the privilege and may waive it.', 'Patient-held privilege protects treatment communications from compelled disclosure unless waived or limited by an applicable exception.'),
  ('2010-173', 'The best single predictor of future violent behavior is a {{c1::history of prior violent behavior}}.', 'A history of violent behavior is the strongest single predictor, although dynamic risk factors also require assessment.'),
  ('2010-174', 'In split treatment, the prescribing psychiatrist retains {{c1::full responsibility for the patient’s psychiatric care}}.', 'The psychiatrist retains full responsibility for the patient’s care. A separate therapist does not make psychiatric risk assessment a purely limited task.'),
  ('2010-175', 'Bupropion may worsen psychosis in vulnerable patients through enhanced {{c1::dopamine}} neurotransmission.', 'Dopamine is the relevant neurotransmitter because bupropion increases dopaminergic signaling.'),
  ('2010-176', 'Compared with adult-onset schizophrenia, childhood-onset schizophrenia shows {{c1::similar deficits in attention, learning, and abstraction}}.', 'Childhood-onset schizophrenia shares the core cognitive deficits of adult schizophrenia. Earlier onset does not imply a milder illness or better prognosis.'),
  ('2010-177', 'A strongly held belief that remains open to doubt and revision is an {{c1::overvalued idea}}, not a fixed {{c2::delusion}}.', 'An overvalued idea is strongly held but not fixed beyond reconsideration. The patient’s acknowledgment of possible error argues against a delusion.'),
  ('2010-178', 'After an unexpected missed visit soon after psychiatric hospitalization, initially {{c1::call the patient with concern and assess the reason for the absence}}.', 'Call the patient first because recent hospitalization makes an unexpected absence clinically important.'),
  ('2010-179', 'Social learning theory explains imitation of a parent’s gendered behavior through {{c1::modeling and observational learning}}.', 'Maternal modeling and behavior is the social-learning explanation because the child observes and imitates a salient model.'),
  ('2010-180', 'For the historical PCP-intoxication item, the keyed initial treatment is {{c1::aggressive hydration}} to reduce {{c2::rhabdomyolysis-related renal risk}}.', 'Aggressive hydration is the keyed supportive treatment because PCP intoxication can be complicated by rhabdomyolysis and acute kidney injury.'),
  ('2010-181', 'An upper-trunk brachial plexus injury causing shoulder abduction and elbow-flexion weakness involves {{c1::C5-C6}} roots.', 'C5-C6 roots form the upper brachial plexus trunk and explain this proximal arm weakness pattern.'),
  ('2010-182', 'When different clinicians give similar ratings using the same instrument, it demonstrates {{c1::interrater reliability}}.', 'Ratings that agree among different raters show interrater reliability. Reliability is consistency; validity concerns whether the intended construct is measured.'),
  ('2010-183', 'When a culturally meaningful experience causes impairment, treatment should focus on {{c1::the resulting functional problem}} while respecting its cultural context.', 'Dealing with her school problems is appropriate because impairment, not cultural difference alone, requires intervention. The visions should not automatically be treated as pathology.'),
  ('2010-184', 'Progressive acquired disinhibition, profanity, and irresponsibility most strongly localize to the {{c1::frontal lobe}}.', 'Frontal-lobe dysfunction, particularly involving orbitofrontal circuitry, can cause disinhibition and personality change.'),
  ('2010-185', 'Intentional production of symptoms to obtain an external benefit is {{c1::malingering}}.', 'Malingering is intentional symptom production for external gain; here the stated goal is time out of prison.'),
  ('2010-186', 'In the historical DSM framework, {{c1::two or more voices conversing}} could alone satisfy the special active-phase symptom exception for schizophrenia.', 'Hallucinations of two or more voices conversing were a historical single-symptom exception for schizophrenia criteria.'),
  ('2010-187', 'Among the listed co-occurring conditions in autistic disorder, {{c1::intellectual disability}} occurs most frequently.', 'Mental retardation, now termed intellectual disability, is the correct answer. Epilepsy and genetic syndromes are important but less frequent.'),
  ('2010-188', 'Fluctuating cognition, recurrent formed visual hallucinations, and spontaneous parkinsonism suggest {{c1::dementia with Lewy bodies}}.', 'Dementia with Lewy bodies is correct because it combines cognitive fluctuations, visual hallucinations, and parkinsonism. A single relatively intact examination does not exclude it.'),
  ('2010-189', 'Identifying the conceptual outlier in a group of items tests {{c1::abstract reasoning}}.', 'Abstract reasoning is correct because the task requires recognition of a shared concept. It is not primarily a test of attention, recall, or fund of knowledge.'),
  ('2010-190', 'Among the listed substance-use medications, {{c1::methadone}} is particularly prone to important interactions with antiretroviral therapy.', 'Methadone is correct because antiretroviral regimens can meaningfully alter its clinical effect. Monitor for withdrawal and excess opioid effects after regimen changes.'),
  ('2010-191', 'In Menninger’s triangle of insight, linking past relationships, current relationships, and transference is an {{c1::interpretation}}.', 'Interpretation is correct because it connects the three points of Menninger’s triangle. Working through is the longer process of integrating such interpretations.'),
  ('2010-192', 'For severe acute manic agitation with psychosis during pregnancy, a classic rapid-treatment choice is {{c1::haloperidol}}.', 'Haloperidol is correct for urgent control of severe mania and psychosis in pregnancy. Valproate and carbamazepine are generally avoided because of fetal risk.'),
  ('2010-193', 'In psychiatric disability determination, the most important consideration is the {{c1::degree of functional impairment}}.', 'Degree of functional impairment is correct because disability turns on capacity to perform relevant roles. A diagnostic label alone does not establish disability.'),
  ('2010-194', 'An infant who looks to a caregiver’s emotional reaction before responding to an unfamiliar situation is showing {{c1::social referencing}}.', 'Social referencing is using a caregiver’s cues to guide behavior in an uncertain situation. The child’s look to the mother before greeting the stranger is the key clue.'),
  ('2010-195', 'A presyncopal prodrome with pallor followed by brief convulsive movements most strongly suggests {{c1::vasovagal syncope}}.', 'Vasovagal syncope is correct because faintness and pallor precede collapse. Brief jerks can occur in convulsive syncope and do not necessarily indicate epilepsy.'),
  ('2010-196', 'A pervasive pattern of social inhibition, inadequacy, and rejection sensitivity despite wanting relationships indicates {{c1::avoidant personality disorder}}.', 'Avoidant personality disorder is correct because the patient wants relationships but fears criticism and rejection. Schizoid personality disorder involves detachment rather than longing for connection.'),
  ('2010-197', 'In psychodynamic therapy, gently pointing out a discrepancy between affect and stated content is {{c1::confrontation}}.', 'Confrontation highlights an observable inconsistency in the patient’s presentation. It differs from interpretation because it does not yet explain the underlying meaning.'),
  ('2010-198', 'Combining an SSRI with {{c1::St. John’s wort (Hypericum perforatum)}} can increase the risk of {{c2::serotonin toxicity}}.', 'Hypericum perforatum is St. John’s wort, a serotonergic herbal product. Combining it with an SSRI is a clinically important interaction.'),
  ('2010-199', 'In advanced immunosuppression, JC virus-associated multifocal white-matter disease causing focal deficits is {{c1::progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy}}.', 'Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy is a JC virus demyelinating disease in severe immunosuppression. Subacute focal deficits with cognitive change and white-matter lesions are characteristic.'),
  ('2010-200', 'Erikson’s central task of middle adulthood is {{c1::generativity versus stagnation}}, expressed by investing in future generations.', 'Feeling useful through protecting or guiding future generations reflects generativity. It is the major Eriksonian task of middle adulthood.')
insert into flashcards (question_id, cloze_text, extra) values
  ('2010-201', 'Under DSM-IV, recurrent unsuccessful efforts to cut down or control use were a criterion for {{c1::substance dependence}}.', 'Recurrent unsuccessful efforts to control use is correct because it was a DSM-IV dependence criterion. The other listed choices were abuse criteria.'),
  ('2010-202', 'The anorexia nervosa complication least likely to fully resolve after weight restoration is {{c1::osteoporosis}}.', 'Osteoporosis is correct because anorexia-related bone loss may be only partly reversible. Many endocrine, lipid, and cardiac abnormalities improve with nutritional restoration.'),
  ('2010-203', '{{c1::Clang associations}} are a thought-process abnormality in which ideas are linked by sound rather than meaning.', 'Clang associations are an impairment of thought process. Obsessions, compulsions, phobias, and preoccupations concern thought content or anxiety-related phenomena.'),
  ('2010-204', 'In the cited 2008 meta-analysis of complex chronic disorders, {{c1::long-term psychodynamic psychotherapy}} was considered most effective across broad outcomes.', 'Long-term psychodynamic psychotherapy is the keyed answer for the cited meta-analysis. The finding does not establish universal superiority over other psychotherapies.'),
  ('2010-205', 'For persistent aggression after traumatic brain injury, the medication with the best evidence among these options is {{c1::propranolol}}.', 'Propranolol is correct because it has evidence for reducing post-TBI aggression. Monitor hemodynamic effects and continue behavioral rehabilitation strategies.'),
  ('2010-206', 'For depression with atypical features, {{c1::tranylcypromine}} may be particularly effective.', 'Tranylcypromine is correct because MAOIs have a classic association with atypical depression. Dietary and drug-interaction precautions are essential.'),
  ('2010-207', 'When decreased sexual interest accompanies anhedonia, insomnia, fatigue, and appetite loss, it is most appropriately considered {{c1::a symptom of a major depressive episode}}.', 'A symptom of a major depressive episode is correct because the sexual change occurs within a depressive syndrome. Primary desire or arousal disorders require that another mental disorder not better explain the symptom.'),
  ('2010-208', '{{c1::Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy}} typically causes progressive symmetric weakness and {{c2::reduced deep tendon reflexes}} after a recent infection.', 'Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy fits postinfectious ascending weakness, paresthesias, and areflexia. Neuromuscular-junction disorders generally do not produce this sensory-reflex pattern.'),
  ('2010-209', 'Antipsychotic use for dementia-related psychosis is associated with {{c1::increased mortality}} and requires careful benefit-risk assessment.', 'Atypical antipsychotics increase mortality in elderly patients with dementia-related psychosis. Any use for severe agitation must be balanced against potential benefit.'),
  ('2010-210', 'In DSM-IV-TR, {{c1::neurasthenia}} is most closely associated with {{c2::undifferentiated somatoform disorder}}.', 'Undifferentiated somatoform disorder is the DSM-IV-TR correspondence for neurasthenia. The term describes culturally patterned fatigue and somatic distress rather than intentional symptom production.'),
  ('2010-211', 'A qualifying {{c1::medical malpractice payment}} may be reported to the NPDB even when {{c2::no wrongdoing is admitted}}.', 'Malpractice settlements where no wrong was admitted are reportable because reporting is not contingent on an admission of liability. A report does not itself establish negligence.'),
  ('2010-212', 'Advocacy for equal insurance coverage of mental and physical illnesses primarily illustrates the ethical principle of {{c1::justice}}.', 'Justice is correct because parity concerns fairness in access to and allocation of insurance benefits. Autonomy and nonmaleficence address different ethical dimensions.'),
  ('2010-213', 'In the mesolimbic reward pathway, dopaminergic neurons project from the {{c1::ventral tegmental area}} to the {{c2::nucleus accumbens}}.', 'The ventral tegmental area contains the dopaminergic cell bodies of the mesolimbic pathway. Their projection to nucleus accumbens is central to reward and reinforcement.'),
  ('2010-214', 'In Freudian psychosexual theory, the stage associated with genital focus and Oedipal fantasies is the {{c1::phallic stage}}.', 'Phallic is correct because Freud placed Oedipal fantasies and early genital focus in that stage. The genital stage refers to later mature sexual development.'),
  ('2010-215', 'The classic medication used to suppress {{c1::cataplexy}} in narcolepsy is {{c2::clomipramine}}.', 'Clomipramine is correct because it can reduce cataplexy through REM-related modulation. Modafinil and dextroamphetamine primarily address excessive daytime sleepiness.'),
  ('2010-216', 'Vertigo, ipsilateral facial sensory loss, and ipsilateral ataxia most strongly localize to a {{c1::lateral medullary stroke}}.', 'Lateral medullary stroke explains the vestibular, facial sensory, and cerebellar findings together. Ménière disease does not produce this focal brainstem pattern.'),
  ('2010-217', '{{c1::Meperidine}} has a potentially lethal interaction with {{c2::monoamine oxidase inhibitors}}.', 'Meperidine is the classic opioid contraindicated with MAOIs because the combination can cause severe, potentially fatal toxicity. It should be avoided.'),
  ('2010-218', '{{c1::Down syndrome}} is a neurodevelopmental model for Alzheimer disease because chromosome 21 contains the {{c2::amyloid precursor protein gene}}.', 'Down syndrome is correct because trisomy 21 increases amyloid precursor protein gene dosage. This creates its characteristic association with Alzheimer-type pathology.'),
  ('2010-219', 'The psychodynamic school that particularly emphasizes {{c1::defense mechanisms}} and ego functioning is {{c2::ego psychology}}.', 'Ego psychology is correct because it focuses on ego functions and defenses used to manage difficult affects. Object relations instead foregrounds internalized relationships.'),
  ('2010-220', 'Personality change, cognitive impairment, and olfactory hallucinations should raise concern for {{c1::herpes simplex virus encephalitis}} involving the temporal lobes.', 'Herpes simplex virus infection is correct because HSV encephalitis often affects temporal lobes. Temporal involvement explains behavioral change, cognitive symptoms, and olfactory or gustatory hallucinations.'),
  ('2010-221', 'CATIE showed that second-generation antipsychotics were {{c1::not more effective overall than first-generation antipsychotics}} for schizophrenia.', 'Second-generation antipsychotics were not uniformly more effective than first-generation agents in CATIE. Medication choice should weigh each agent’s benefits and adverse-effect profile.'),
  ('2010-222', 'Under DSM-IV-era terminology, {{c1::dissociative fugue}} required autobiographical amnesia or identity confusion together with {{c2::unexpected travel or wandering}}.', 'A — Dissociative fugue is the DSM-IV-era intended answer. The abbreviated stem omits its defining travel or wandering feature.'),
  ('2010-223', 'After a patient’s suicide, the psychiatrist’s initial contact with a bereaved spouse should primarily {{c1::address and listen to the spouse’s feelings}}.', 'Concentrating on the spouse’s feelings is correct because empathic listening is the appropriate first response to bereavement. Detailed care discussions and record access require separate consideration of confidentiality and boundaries.'),
  ('2010-224', 'To protect confidentiality in a public encounter, a psychiatrist should {{c1::not acknowledge the patient first}} unless the patient initiates contact.', 'Walk by without indicating recognition is correct because it preserves the patient’s control over disclosure. The psychiatrist may respond neutrally if the patient initiates contact.'),
  ('2010-225', 'For a smoker with impending orthodontia, psoriasis, and chronic allergic rhinitis, the most suitable nicotine-replacement product is a {{c1::nicotine lozenge}}.', 'Lozenges avoid chewing, adhesive skin exposure, and nasal irritation. They are the best listed fit for this patient.'),
  ('2010-226', 'Myalgias with elevated creatine kinase in a patient taking atorvastatin, particularly with {{c2::gemfibrozil}}, suggest {{c1::statin-induced myopathy}}.', 'Statin-induced myopathy is suggested by muscle symptoms and creatine kinase elevation. Gemfibrozil coadministration increases risk.'),
  ('2010-227', 'In Mahler’s theory, {{c1::rapprochement}} is the toddler’s renewed movement toward caregiver closeness amid emerging autonomy.', 'Reconciliation following a brief attachment rupture between toddlers and parents describes rapprochement. It reflects normal tension between autonomy and attachment.'),
  ('2010-228', '{{c1::Prader-Willi syndrome}} classically causes neonatal hypotonia followed by hyperphagia and food-seeking behavior.', 'Prader-Willi syndrome is suggested by the sequence of infant hypotonia followed by hyperphagia. Developmental delay and temper tantrums commonly accompany this pattern.'),
  ('2010-229', 'Elevated {{c1::plasma homocysteine}} is associated with increased risk of {{c2::ischemic stroke in younger adults}}.', 'Plasma homocysteine is a recognized vascular and thrombotic risk association.'),
  ('2010-230', 'A core cognitive-therapy intervention is to {{c1::elicit and test automatic thoughts}} that shape emotion and behavior.', 'Eliciting and testing automatic thoughts is a central cognitive-therapy technique.'),
  ('2010-231', 'Postpartum psychosis is most classically associated with {{c1::bipolar disorder}}, particularly {{c2::bipolar I disorder}}.', 'Bipolar disorder is the classic association with postpartum psychosis.'),
  ('2010-232', 'Among classic antidepressants, {{c1::imipramine}} has plasma concentrations that can correlate with {{c2::therapeutic response}}.', 'Imipramine is the classic antidepressant with clinically useful plasma-level monitoring.'),
  ('2010-233', 'REM sleep behavior disorder is commonly associated with {{c1::alpha-synuclein}} inclusions in synucleinopathies.', 'Synuclein is the key protein association in REM sleep behavior disorder. The disorder may precede other synucleinopathy manifestations.'),
  ('2010-234', 'The major event that immediately preceded and helped shape DSM-I was {{c1::World War II}}.', 'World War II is the corrected answer. Option C had been duplicated or mistranscribed as World War I and was repaired through source adjudication.'),
  ('2010-235', 'Valid informed consent requires {{c1::voluntariness}}; consent obtained through {{c2::coercion}} is invalid.', 'Coercion invalidates consent because voluntariness is essential.'),
  ('2010-236', 'In OCD exposure and response prevention, anxiety that decreases with repeated exposure without rituals demonstrates {{c1::habituation}}.', 'Habituation is the anxiety reduction that occurs with exposure without ritualizing.'),
  ('2010-237', 'For this PRITE item, combined {{c2::lithium and SSRI}} use is associated with increased risk of {{c1::seizures}}.', 'Seizures are the keyed adverse outcome with lithium plus an SSRI. New neurologic symptoms merit evaluation for toxicity and medical contributors.'),
  ('2010-238', 'Compared with other ADHD presentations, predominantly inattentive ADHD has higher rates of {{c1::learning disorders}} along with anxiety and somatic complaints.', 'Learning disorders are the key associated condition in this comparison. Evaluate academic skills rather than interpreting quiet inattention as laziness.'),
  ('2010-239', 'When a medically ill patient is reality-based and coping adaptively, the preferred psychiatric response is to {{c1::provide support without confronting presumed denial}}.', 'Support but do not confront the patient about the medical realities is preferred. Grief need not follow a visible staged pattern.'),
  ('2010-240', 'An MMSE score of 23 is an abnormal screen but cannot by itself distinguish {{c1::mild cognitive impairment from dementia}}.', 'Dementia / mild cognitive impairment is the correct combined answer. Functional independence, not the MMSE score alone, distinguishes the two.'),
  ('2010-241', 'Cold-induced paradoxical myotonia that worsens with continued exercise is associated with a {{c1::skeletal-muscle sodium-channel}} disorder.', 'Sodium is the implicated ionic channel. Chloride-channel myotonia more often improves with repeated activity.'),
  ('2010-242', 'The SSRI with established efficacy for reducing bingeing and purging in bulimia nervosa is {{c1::fluoxetine}}.', 'Fluoxetine can reduce binge-purge symptoms; bupropion should be avoided.'),
  ('2010-243', '{{c1::Stimulant intoxication}} can cause formication, agitation, stereotyped movements, and paranoia.', 'Stimulants characteristically produce formication with agitation and paranoia.'),
  ('2010-244', 'When leaving a feared setting reduces panic and makes future escape more likely, the behavior is maintained by {{c1::negative reinforcement}}.', 'Negative reinforcement maintains escape because leaving removes panic discomfort.'),
  ('2010-245', 'An important feature of friendships in midlife is that they are {{c1::less dependent on close age similarity}}.', 'Midlife friendships commonly reflect shared roles and support rather than age matching.'),
  ('2010-246', 'In a patient with severe brain injury, inconsistent but purposeful command following indicates a {{c1::minimally conscious state}}.', 'Minimally conscious state includes definite but inconsistent evidence of awareness. Persistent vegetative state lacks purposeful responses.'),
  ('2010-247', 'The conscious decision to set aside fear temporarily in order to manage an urgent task is {{c1::suppression}}.', 'Suppression is a conscious, adaptive postponement of distressing emotion. The parent remains reality-based and focused on necessary action.'),
  ('2010-248', 'A cigarette craving that reliably occurs after a meal is an {{c1::environmental trigger}} produced by conditioned cues.', 'An environmental trigger is the correct answer. The post-meal routine has become associated with smoking.'),
  ('2010-249', 'A familial genetic association supporting susceptibility to OCD is the link with {{c1::tic disorders}}.', 'Tic disorders are the classic familial association with OCD. The relationship is especially relevant in early-onset OCD.'),
  ('2010-250', 'Competence to proceed in a criminal case depends primarily on the defendant’s {{c1::current mental functioning}} and ability to assist counsel.', 'Current mental functioning is the correct focus. Mental state at the offense concerns criminal responsibility, a separate forensic question.'),
  ('2010-251', 'In severe lithium intoxication with impaired consciousness or seizures, the treatment of choice is {{c1::hemodialysis}}.', 'Hemodialysis is the correct treatment for severe lithium poisoning. IV fluids are supportive but do not replace dialysis when dangerous neurologic toxicity is present.'),
  ('2010-252', 'Among epidemiologic associations, {{c1::female sex}} is a risk factor for the {{c2::rapid-cycling}} course of bipolar disorder.', 'Female sex is the correct association for rapid-cycling bipolar disorder. Rapid cycling requires four or more mood episodes within 12 months.'),
  ('2010-253', 'A treatment goal most specific to psychoanalytic psychotherapy is {{c1::bringing unconscious conflict into awareness}}.', 'Bringing unconscious conflict into awareness is the defining psychoanalytic aim.'),
  ('2010-254', 'The {{c1::Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales}} measure adaptive functioning, including a child’s daily living and self-care skills.', 'The Vineland is the correct measure of practical adaptive functioning. It is more appropriate for self-care assessment than behavior checklists or ADHD rating scales.'),
  ('2010-255', 'For initial panic-disorder treatment, no strategy is universally superior to {{c1::psychotherapy, pharmacotherapy, or their combination}} for every patient.', 'There are insufficient data to declare one initial panic-disorder strategy universally best. Select psychotherapy, medication, or both according to the individual situation.'),
  ('2010-256', 'At the highest recommended doses, venlafaxine may additionally inhibit reuptake of {{c1::dopamine}}.', 'Dopamine is the correct monoamine at venlafaxine’s highest recommended doses. Serotonin and norepinephrine effects occur at lower dose ranges.'),
  ('2010-257', 'Nystagmus suppressed by visual fixation and increased by gaze toward its quick phase suggests {{c1::peripheral vestibular disease}}, such as labyrinthitis.', 'Labyrinthitis is the correct peripheral vestibular pathology. Fixation suppresses its nystagmus, and gaze toward the fast phase increases its intensity.'),
  ('2010-258', 'Attributing life-like qualities to a toy is a typical form of symbolic play during {{c1::preschool}} development.', 'Preschool is the correct developmental stage. A toy bear being “alive” is usually normal animistic symbolic play.'),
  ('2010-259', '{{c1::Anterior spinal artery}} occlusion causes motor and pain-temperature loss with relative preservation of vibration and proprioception.', 'Anterior spinal artery occlusion is the correct answer. It affects corticospinal and spinothalamic tracts while relatively sparing dorsal columns.'),
  ('2010-260', 'Acute lethargy, incoordination, and bradycardia in a bodybuilder with a negative routine drug screen suggest {{c1::GHB intoxication}}.', 'Gamma-hydroxybutyrate is the correct substance. It can cause CNS depression and may not appear on a routine drug screen.'),
  ('2010-261', 'When asked about a named person, a psychiatrist should protect confidentiality by {{c1::neither confirming nor denying that the person is a patient}}.', 'The psychiatrist must not confirm the treatment relationship. A neutral statement of the confidentiality rule is the appropriate response.'),
  ('2010-262', 'Among the listed benzodiazepines, {{c1::lorazepam}} is relatively safer in older adults because it lacks long-lived active metabolites.', 'Lorazepam is the correct choice. It is glucuronidated and lacks long-lived active metabolites, unlike the listed long-acting alternatives.'),
  ('2010-263', 'A core focus of interpersonal psychotherapy is helping patients adapt to {{c1::role transitions}}.', 'Role transitions are the correct IPT focus. CBT instead emphasizes automatic thoughts and cognitive distortions.'),
  ('2010-264', 'A core diagnostic feature of dissociative identity disorder is {{c1::recurrent amnesia for important personal information or everyday events}} beyond ordinary forgetting.', 'Extensive inability to recall personal information is the keyed diagnostic feature.'),
  ('2010-265', 'Subacute meningitis with cranial neuropathies, mononuclear CSF pleocytosis, high protein, and low glucose suggests {{c1::tuberculous meningitis}}.', 'Tuberculous meningitis is the correct diagnosis. It produces a subacute basilar meningitis syndrome with mononuclear cells, high protein, and low glucose.'),
  ('2010-266', 'Audible self-talk while engaged in imaginative play is a {{c1::normal aspect of preschool development}}.', 'Normative preschool play is the correct answer. The child’s narration is private speech, not by itself evidence of hallucinations or poor reality testing.'),
  ('2010-267', 'Caffeine increases dopaminergic activity primarily by {{c1::antagonizing adenosine receptors}}.', 'Adenosine receptor antagonism is how caffeine indirectly enhances dopaminergic activity.'),
  ('2010-268', 'Explaining a disorder and treatment options so a capable patient can choose respects {{c1::autonomy}}.', 'Autonomy is the correct ethical principle. Education about diagnoses and options is essential to informed, voluntary patient choice.'),
  ('2010-269', 'For an older adult with chronic high-dose clonazepam use, tolerance, and persistent insomnia, the appropriate next step is to {{c1::slowly taper clonazepam and reevaluate}} rather than increase the dose.', 'B — Slowly withdraw the patient''s clonazepam and reevaluate. A gradual taper reduces withdrawal risk and clarifies the underlying sleep problem.'),
  ('2010-270', '{{c1::Agoraphobia}} involves fear and avoidance of situations where escape may be difficult or help unavailable if panic-like symptoms occur.', 'B — agoraphobia without panic. The key feature is avoidance of multiple settings because of feared incapacitating symptoms and difficult escape.'),
  ('2010-271', 'In U.S. children and adolescents, a common precipitant of PTSD is {{c1::intrafamilial violence}}.', 'E — Intrafamilial violence. Family violence is a particularly important and common trauma exposure to assess in pediatric patients.'),
  ('2010-272', '{{c1::Fluoxetine}} can be used to manage discontinuation symptoms from shorter-acting SSRIs because of its {{c2::long half-life}}.', 'Fluoxetine is the classic SSRI bridge because of its prolonged pharmacokinetics.'),
  ('2010-273', 'A typical episode of optic neuritis is associated with later development of {{c1::multiple sclerosis}}.', 'Multiple sclerosis is classically associated with an episode of optic neuritis.'),
  ('2010-274', 'When prescribing benzodiazepines, {{c1::a history of alcohol or other drug abuse}} is a major warning sign for problematic use.', 'D — History of abuse of other drugs or alcohol. This is more concerning for misuse than tolerance or withdrawal alone during chronic prescribed treatment.'),
  ('2010-275', 'Patients with {{c1::trichotillomania}} commonly have a comorbid {{c2::mood disorder}}.', 'A — Mood disorder. Depression and other mood syndromes are important comorbidities to assess in trichotillomania.'),
  ('2010-276', 'For functional neurologic symptom disorder, {{c1::rehabilitative, function-focused treatment}} is a central intervention.', 'B — Rehabilitative treatment. A coordinated plan that restores function is more useful than reassurance alone or endless diagnostic testing.'),
  ('2010-277', '{{c1::Hydrochlorothiazide}} can significantly elevate serum lithium levels by reducing renal lithium clearance.', 'E — Hydrochlorothiazide. Thiazide diuretics increase proximal lithium reabsorption and can precipitate lithium toxicity.'),
  ('2010-278', 'Conduction aphasia with disproportionately impaired repetition is classically caused by damage to the {{c1::arcuate fasciculus}}.', 'C — Arcuate fasciculus. This tract connects major dominant-hemisphere language regions, so injury impairs repetition.'),
  ('2010-279', 'A pervasive pattern of distrust and interpreting others'' motives as malevolent characterizes {{c1::paranoid personality disorder}}.', 'A — Paranoid personality disorder. The surgeon persistently interprets ambiguous workplace events as exploitation and betrayal.'),
  ('2010-280', 'Chronic {{c1::toluene}} inhalation can cause cerebellar atrophy, diffuse white-matter injury, neuropathy, and cognitive decline.', 'B — Toluene. Chronic inhalant neurotoxicity explains the combined cerebellar, peripheral nerve, cognitive, and white-matter findings.'),
  ('2010-281', 'Delayed fluctuating focal deficits after aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage most strongly suggest {{c1::cerebral vasospasm}}.', 'B — Vasospasm. It is the classic cause of delayed ischemic neurologic deficits after subarachnoid hemorrhage.'),
  ('2010-282', 'A major therapeutic goal of self-help groups is {{c1::overcoming maladaptive behaviors}} through mutual peer support.', 'B — Overcoming maladaptive behaviors. Self-help groups emphasize practical change reinforced by shared experience and accountability.'),
  ('2010-283', 'A dissociative state of narrowed attention and altered consciousness is called a {{c1::trance}}.', 'D — trance. Trance is the term for focused attention accompanied by altered awareness in dissociative states.'),
  ('2010-284', 'In normal aging, sleep becomes more fragmented, with increased {{c1::duration of nocturnal awakenings (wake after sleep onset)}}.', 'Duration of awakenings increases because sleep is less consolidated with aging. REM and slow-wave sleep do not increase.'),
  ('2010-285', 'Charging a missed-appointment fee is ethically permissible when the patient is {{c1::specifically notified of the policy in advance}}.', 'C — ethical if the patient is specifically notified in advance. Clear prospective disclosure makes the financial policy ethically appropriate.'),
  ('2010-286', 'Delayed dysarthria, dysphagia, and quadriparesis after correction of severe hyponatremia suggest {{c1::central pontine myelinolysis (osmotic demyelination syndrome)}}.', 'Central pontine myelinolysis follows a major osmotic shift, classically during correction of severe hyponatremia. It produces corticobulbar and corticospinal deficits.'),
  ('2010-287', 'A central element of cultural formulation is the patient''s {{c1::explanation of the illness}} and its meaning.', 'C — The patient''s explanation of the illness. The patient''s explanatory model is a formal and clinically useful part of cultural formulation.'),
  ('2010-288', 'In consultation-liaison psychiatry, the liaison function principally involves {{c1::teaching other healthcare workers the psychosocial aspects of medical care}}.', 'Teaching psychosocial aspects of care is the defining liaison function. Direct clinical advice for a specific patient is consultation.'),
  ('2010-289', '{{c1::Carbamazepine}} is classically associated with an early {{c2::maculopapular rash}}.', 'Carbamazepine commonly causes an early maculopapular rash. A new rash still warrants evaluation for severe cutaneous adverse reactions.'),
  ('2010-290', 'Of the core ADHD symptom domains, {{c1::inattention}} is most likely to remain prominent in adulthood.', 'Inattention commonly persists into adult ADHD. Hyperactivity often becomes less overt or is experienced as internal restlessness.'),
  ('2010-291', '{{c1::Bell''s palsy}} causes acute unilateral {{c2::lower-motor-neuron facial weakness}}, often with hyperacusis and retroauricular pain.', 'Bell''s palsy is a peripheral facial-nerve palsy. Objective sensory loss or other focal deficits should prompt consideration of alternative diagnoses.'),
  ('2010-292', 'Freud''s {{c1::topographical model}} divides the mind into the conscious, preconscious, and unconscious.', 'The topographical model is organized by accessibility to awareness. The structural model is id, ego, and superego.'),
  ('2010-293', 'The {{c1::Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory-II (MCMI-II)}} is a self-report instrument designed to assess personality patterns and clinical syndromes.', 'The MCMI-II is the best-listed personality assessment instrument. It complements rather than replaces longitudinal clinical diagnosis.'),
  ('2010-294', 'For recurrent depression, antidepressant maintenance treatment generally continues {{c1::the same dose that achieved remission}}.', 'Continue the effective dose after remission in recurrent depression. Dose reduction is not the routine maintenance strategy.'),
  ('2010-295', 'In anorexia nervosa, purging subtype, a common metabolic abnormality is {{c1::hypokalemia}}.', 'Decreased serum potassium is common with purging. Vomiting more typically causes metabolic alkalosis than low bicarbonate.'),
  ('2010-296', 'Café-au-lait macules, neurofibromas, and Lisch nodules indicate {{c1::neurofibromatosis type 1}}, caused by pathogenic variants in {{c2::NF1}}.', 'NF1 (neurofibromatosis 1 gene) is the corrected, inferred answer. The source’s “NEF-1” gene symbol was an OCR error.'),
  ('2010-297', 'An {{c1::internal locus of control}} is the expectation that one''s own behavior can help produce a {{c2::valued outcome}}.', 'Internal locus of control links personal action to outcomes. External locus emphasizes chance, fate, or powerful others.'),
  ('2010-298', 'In successfully treated late-life depression, {{c1::information-processing speed}} is a cognitive function that may improve toward baseline.', 'Information-processing speed may improve after depression remits. Persistent cognitive impairment requires reassessment.'),
  ('2010-299', 'In the mental-status examination, asking what a patient would do in a hypothetical situation assesses {{c1::judgment}}.', 'Judgment concerns practical decision-making in a situation. Insight concerns awareness of illness, not a response to a hypothetical problem.'),
  ('2010-300', 'Progressive personality and behavioral change with frontal release signs is characteristic of {{c1::frontotemporal dementia}}.', 'Frontotemporal dementia often presents with early behavioral, personality, or language changes. Frontal release signs reinforce frontal-system involvement.'),
  ('2011-1', 'Immediate recall is assessed by asking the patient to {{c1::repeat newly presented information immediately}}, such as a string of digits.', 'Repeating six numbers tests immediate recall or registration. Questions about yesterday assess recent memory instead.'),
  ('2011-2', 'Among patients with schizophrenia, {{c1::nicotine}} is the most commonly abused substance.', 'Nicotine is the keyed most commonly abused substance in schizophrenia. Other substance-use disorders remain important to screen for.'),
  ('2011-3', 'Occlusion of the {{c1::right posterior cerebral artery}} can cause {{c2::left homonymous hemianopsia}}.', 'Left homonymous hemianopsia follows a right occipital lesion. Monocular blindness localizes anterior to the optic chiasm.'),
  ('2011-4', '{{c1::Ethnicity}} most directly refers to a shared sense of ancestry, identity, beliefs, and history.', 'Ethnicity combines cultural and historical group identity. Language or religion alone does not necessarily define it.'),
  ('2011-5', 'During pregnancy, domestic violence may specifically involve blows to the {{c1::abdomen}}.', 'Abdomen is correct because this item emphasizes that violence during pregnancy may target the pregnancy. Such trauma has obstetric and safety implications.'),
  ('2011-6', 'After acute ECT response in severe refractory depression, {{c1::continuation or maintenance ECT}} helps prevent {{c2::relapse}}.', 'Continue maintenance ECT for at least 10 weeks is correct because acute ECT response requires relapse-prevention treatment. Observation alone is inadequate.'),
  ('2011-7', 'Whipple disease is caused by {{c1::*Tropheryma whipplei*}} and is classically associated with {{c2::PAS-positive macrophages}}.', 'Cerebrospinal fluid with PAS+ cells infected by Tropheryma is the keyed best answer because the syndrome is Whipple disease. PAS-positive macrophages are classically identified in tissue.'),
  ('2011-8', 'Restating a patient’s concern to verify its meaning is {{c1::clarification}}.', 'Clarification is correct because the psychiatrist paraphrases the patient’s stated wish. No hidden motive is inferred.'),
  ('2011-9', 'DSM-IV-TR criteria were developed using {{c1::data re-analyses and field trials}}, not a priori etiologic theories.', 'Data re-analyses and field trials is correct because DSM-IV-TR retained an empirically developed descriptive framework. It was not based on presumed causes.'),
  ('2011-10', 'In DSM-IV terminology, {{c1::clinically significant early language delay}} distinguished autistic disorder from Asperger disorder.', 'Delay in the development of spoken language and communicative gestures is the historic DSM-IV distinction. Social reciprocity deficits and restricted interests could occur in both disorders.'),
  ('2011-11', 'In bipolar depression, bright-light therapy requires {{c1::careful titration and monitoring for hypomania or mania}}.', 'Can be used but requires vigilance and careful titration of dose is correct because light therapy can activate bipolar mood. It is not a treatment for mania.'),
  ('2011-12', 'The {{c1::Wisconsin Card Sort Test}} is a classic measure of {{c2::executive function and set shifting}}.', 'Wisconsin Card Sort Test is correct because it tests flexible rule shifting and feedback use. The other tests chiefly assess perception, memory, or naming.'),
  ('2011-13', '{{c1::Maternity blues}} causes transient tearfulness and emotional lability soon after childbirth without psychosis.', 'Maternity blues is correct because the symptoms are early postpartum and emotionally labile. The vignette lacks sustained depression or psychosis.'),
  ('2011-14', 'Suspected {{c1::meningovascular neurosyphilis}} requires treatment with {{c2::intravenous penicillin G}}.', 'Penicillin is correct because the CSF and stroke findings suggest neurosyphilis. Antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy does not treat the infection.'),
  ('2011-15', 'Factitious disorder involves intentional symptom falsification motivated by {{c1::assuming the sick role}}, rather than {{c2::external gain}}.', 'There is motivation to assume the sick role is correct because it defines factitious disorder. External incentives instead point toward malingering.'),
  ('2011-16', 'In classical psychoanalytic theory, awareness of the parents as a couple emerges during the {{c1::phallic stage}}.', 'Phallic is correct because this stage centers on the Oedipal family triangle. “Phaiilic” is an apparent typographical error.'),
  ('2011-17', 'Fluctuating cognition, recurrent visual hallucinations, and spontaneous parkinsonism are core features of {{c1::dementia with Lewy bodies}}.', 'Dementia with Lewy bodies classically combines cognitive fluctuations, visual hallucinations, and parkinsonism. REM sleep behavior disorder further supports the diagnosis.'),
  ('2011-18', 'A major caregiver factor associated with secure attachment is {{c1::sensitive responsiveness to the infant’s needs}}.', 'Unresponsive primary caregiving increases risk for insecure attachment. Parental employment or adoptive status alone does not determine attachment security.'),
  ('2011-19', '{{c1::Ready access to firearms}} is a major suicide-mortality risk factor because firearms are highly lethal.', 'Having ready access to firearms is correct because immediate access to a highly lethal method increases suicide mortality. It should be assessed alongside other risks.'),
  ('2011-20', 'For difficulty achieving orgasm with a partner, directed masturbation may be supplemented by {{c1::couples therapy}}.', 'Couples therapy is correct because it addresses communication and relational factors in partnered orgasm difficulty. It complements directed masturbation.'),
  ('2011-21', 'The most prevalent form of child maltreatment is {{c1::neglect}}.', 'Neglect is the most prevalent child-maltreatment category. Its chronic, omission-based nature can make it less visible than acute abuse.'),
  ('2011-22', 'An acute {{c1::cluster headache}} attack is rapidly aborted with {{c2::high-flow oxygen}}.', 'Oxygen is correct because the vignette describes cluster headache. Lithium and valproate are preventive rather than rapid abortive options.'),
  ('2011-23', 'After a single acute acetaminophen ingestion with a known time, the need for N-acetylcysteine is guided by a {{c1::serum APAP level obtained at least 4 hours after ingestion and plotted on the Rumack–Matthew nomogram}}.', 'E — A timed serum APAP concentration is the key test for nomogram-based NAC decisions. Early transaminases and the reported dose alone are insufficient.'),
  ('2011-24', 'A behavior that becomes more likely because it relieves an aversive feeling is maintained by {{c1::negative reinforcement}}.', 'E — Pulling removes uncomfortable tension, making future pulling more likely. That is negative reinforcement, not punishment.'),
  ('2011-25', 'To help ameliorate retrograde amnesia during an ECT course, clinicians can {{c1::increase the interval between treatments}}.', 'B — Less frequent ECT reduces cumulative cognitive burden. Bilateral ECT is generally more cognitively burdensome than unilateral ECT.'),
  ('2011-26', 'In {{c1::tangentiality}}, speech departs from the question and {{c2::never returns to answer it}}.', 'A — The patient never answers the psychiatrist’s question. Circumstantial speech eventually reaches the point.'),
  ('2011-27', 'Ulnar neuropathy at the elbow causes {{c1::sensory loss in the fourth and fifth digits}} with {{c2::intrinsic hand-muscle weakness}}.', 'Loss of sensation in the fourth and fifth digits with intrinsic hand weakness is the ulnar neuropathy pattern. Median and radial neuropathies have different sensory and motor findings.'),
  ('2011-28', 'In Mahler’s {{c1::rapprochement}} phase, toddlers show renewed concern about separation and seek reassurance about the caregiver’s availability.', 'B — Rapprochement features ambivalence between autonomy and proximity. It commonly appears in the latter part of the second year.'),
  ('2011-29', 'The first treatment for suspected Wernicke encephalopathy is {{c1::parenteral thiamine}}, ideally before glucose when feasible.', 'D — The triad suggests Wernicke encephalopathy. Give thiamine promptly; glucose alone can aggravate deficiency-related injury.'),
  ('2011-30', 'A classic familial association supporting genetic susceptibility to OCD is with {{c1::tic disorders}}.', 'B — OCD has a well-known familial link with tic disorders. Other comorbidities are possible but are not the classic association tested.'),
  ('2011-31', '{{c1::Memantine}} is a cognitive enhancer that acts as an {{c2::NMDA receptor antagonist}}.', 'C — Memantine is the NMDA antagonist. The other listed agents are acetylcholinesterase inhibitors.'),
  ('2011-32', 'Compared with older adolescents, school-age children with depression more often present with {{c1::somatic complaints}}.', 'E — Somatic complaints are a common depressive presentation in younger children. They should prompt a broad medical and psychiatric assessment.'),
  ('2011-33', 'The triad of photosensitive dermatitis, diarrhea, and cognitive or neuropsychiatric change suggests {{c1::niacin (vitamin B3) deficiency}}.', 'B — Niacin deficiency causes pellagra, classically dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia. Photosensitive dermatitis is a key clue.'),
  ('2011-34', 'For a patient reluctant to attend AA, an appropriate next step is to {{c1::recommend speaking with a current AA member}}.', 'C — A current member can answer questions and reduce barriers to attendance. Coercing participation or making care conditional is inappropriate.'),
  ('2011-35', 'When evaluating learning difficulties in a young school-age child, ask specifically about the family’s history of {{c1::reading with the child}}.', 'C — Shared-reading history directly informs literacy exposure and observed reading difficulties. It complements, but does not replace, school-based assessment.'),
  ('2011-36', 'For severe mental illness with recurrent hospitalizations, nonadherence, and housing instability, the indicated service model is {{c1::assertive community treatment (ACT)}}.', 'D — ACT provides intensive multidisciplinary outreach and coordination. It is suited to recurrent hospitalization and difficulty engaging in standard outpatient care.'),
  ('2011-37', 'The most prevalent movement disorder in people aged 70 years or older is {{c1::essential tremor}}.', 'C — Essential tremor is more prevalent than Parkinson disease in this age group. It is usually an action or postural tremor.'),
  ('2011-38', 'Frontotemporal dementia with parkinsonism linked to chromosome 17 is characterized by intraneuronal deposition of {{c1::tau protein}}.', 'E — Chromosome 17-linked FTD is classically a tauopathy. Synuclein, amyloid, and huntingtin indicate other neurodegenerative disease families.'),
  ('2011-39', 'In CBT for chronic pain, the central goal is to {{c1::improve functioning despite persistent pain}}, rather than require {{c2::complete pain elimination}}.', 'Improve patients'' functioning despite the presence of pain is correct because CBT targets disability, avoidance, and coping. Pain reduction is welcome but is not the defining goal.'),
  ('2011-40', 'A request for unusually high-dose alprazolam with an inconsistent panic history should raise concern for {{c1::substance abuse}}.', 'Substance abuse is correct in the historical answer set because the vignette emphasizes possible drug-seeking features. Current practice requires a structured assessment before diagnosing a substance-use disorder.'),
  ('2011-41', 'In a cognitive-behavioral formulation of major depression, {{c1::feelings of devaluation or worthlessness}} reflect a core negative self-schema.', 'Feelings of devaluation are correct because CBT emphasizes negative self-appraisals in depression. Sadness and vegetative symptoms are manifestations rather than the central cognitive mechanism.'),
  ('2011-42', 'For persistent medically unexplained somatic symptoms, a core management strategy is {{c1::regular scheduled visits with a physical examination}}.', 'Schedule regular visits with a physical examination to preserve continuity and permit reassessment. This is preferable to dismissal or repeated scattered referrals.'),
  ('2011-43', 'For alcohol withdrawal in significant liver disease, {{c1::lorazepam}} is often preferred because it undergoes {{c2::direct glucuronidation}}.', 'Lorazepam is correct because it is metabolized through glucuronide conjugation. Chlordiazepoxide may accumulate through active oxidative metabolites in hepatic impairment.'),
  ('2011-44', '{{c1::Separation anxiety disorder}} involves excessive fear that harm will occur to the child or an {{c2::attachment figure}} during separation.', 'A fear that something bad will happen to the child or caretaker is correct because separation-related catastrophe is the defining content. Other anxiety disorders can involve reassurance seeking without this specific fear.'),
  ('2011-45', 'Acute bilateral weakness with a {{c1::sensory level}}, urinary dysfunction, and hyperreflexia is most consistent with {{c2::acute transverse myelitis}}.', 'Acute transverse myelitis is correct because the findings localize to the spinal cord. Peripheral neuropathy would not explain the sensory level, brisk reflexes, and plantar responses.'),
  ('2011-46', 'Infants are born with {{c1::sophisticated perceptual abilities}} that support reciprocal interaction and {{c2::attachment}}.', 'Infants are born with sophisticated perceptual abilities that facilitate attachment. They participate in reciprocal caregiver interactions well before the second year.'),
  ('2011-47', 'A study that follows the same defined group from early characteristics to later psychiatric outcomes is a {{c1::cohort study}}.', 'Cohort is correct because the children were followed longitudinally from infancy into adulthood. No intervention was assigned, so the design is not experimental.'),
  ('2011-48', 'The delusion that a familiar person has been replaced by an identical-looking impostor is {{c1::Capgras syndrome}}.', 'Capgras syndrome is correct because the father appears identical but is believed to be an impostor. De Clérambault syndrome is erotomania.'),
  ('2011-49', '{{c1::Serotonin syndrome}} is suggested by serotonergic exposure with fever, agitation, tremor, and {{c2::clonus or myoclonus}}.', 'Serotonin syndrome is correct because fluoxetine exposure plus autonomic, mental-status, and neuromuscular findings fits serotonin toxicity. Myoclonus helps distinguish it from neuroleptic malignant syndrome.'),
  ('2011-50', 'Among citalopram, paroxetine, venlafaxine, fluvoxamine, and trazodone, {{c1::citalopram}} has the longest mean half-life.', 'Citalopram is correct within the listed options because it has the longest mean half-life. Fluoxetine would be longer overall but is not offered.'),
  ('2011-51', '{{c1::Disulfiram}} inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase, causing {{c2::acetaldehyde accumulation}} if alcohol is consumed.', 'Disulfiram is correct because it mimics reduced acetaldehyde dehydrogenase function. The resulting acetaldehyde accumulation causes an aversive alcohol reaction.'),
  ('2011-52', '{{c1::Parkinson disease}} commonly presents with asymmetric {{c2::bradykinesia and rigidity}}, often with reduced arm swing.', 'Parkinson disease is correct because the patient has asymmetric bradykinesia, rigidity, reduced arm swing, and a short-stepped gait. Early falls warrant evaluation for atypical features.'),
  ('2011-53', 'In middle childhood, moral development increasingly involves {{c1::internalization of parental values}}, allowing behavior to be guided without direct supervision.', 'Internalization of parental values is correct because middle childhood brings more stable, self-guided adherence to learned standards. Shame and delayed gratification emerge earlier.'),
  ('2011-54', 'In CBT, {{c1::catastrophic automatic thoughts}} are addressed by examining the {{c2::evidence for and against}} them.', '“Let’s make lists of the evidence for and against you failing out of college” is correct because it uses cognitive restructuring. It tests catastrophizing rather than offering reassurance or exploring childhood origins.'),
  ('2011-55', 'Diphenhydramine relieves an acute dystonic reaction primarily through central {{c1::muscarinic (M1) receptor antagonism}}.', 'Muscarinic-1 antagonism counteracts relative striatal cholinergic excess after dopamine blockade.'),
  ('2011-56', 'After an adequate methylphenidate trial fails in ADHD, the next medication trial is usually an {{c1::amphetamine-class stimulant}}.', 'Mixed amphetamine salts provide a trial of the other major stimulant class after methylphenidate nonresponse.'),
  ('2011-57', 'In schizophrenia, reduced interest or pleasure is especially associated with {{c1::social interactions}} despite blunted expression not proving global anhedonia.', 'Social interactions are the characteristic domain of reduced interest or anticipatory pleasure tested here.'),
  ('2011-58', 'A common adverse effect of acetylcholinesterase inhibitors is {{c1::diarrhea}} from increased cholinergic gastrointestinal activity.', 'Diarrhea is a classic muscarinic gastrointestinal effect of acetylcholinesterase inhibition.'),
  ('2011-59', 'Among the listed cancers, adult paraneoplastic opsoclonus-myoclonus-ataxia is associated with {{c1::breast carcinoma}}.', 'Breast carcinoma is the best listed adult paraneoplastic association; neuroblastoma is the classic childhood association.'),
  ('2011-60', 'Secure attachment in preschool-age children is associated with {{c1::empathy and altruism}}.', 'Empathy and altruism are the prosocial characteristics associated with secure preschool attachment.'),
  ('2011-61', 'In this historical exam framework, {{c1::pain relievers}} had the greatest prevalence of prescription medication nonmedical use.', 'Pain relievers are the keyed highest-prevalence prescription-misuse class. This historical population comparison does not establish an individual diagnosis.'),
  ('2011-62', 'In a child-custody evaluation, the psychiatrist should {{c1::interview all parties to the litigation}} to support a balanced assessment.', 'Interview all parties to the litigation to support a comprehensive and objective custody evaluation.'),
  ('2011-63', 'The defining mood feature of depression with atypical features is {{c1::mood reactivity}}.', 'Reactive mood distinguishes atypical features from the nonreactive mood characteristic of melancholic depression.'),
  ('2011-64', 'Compared with desmopressin, behavioral treatment of childhood enuresis has {{c1::lower relapse rates}} after treatment ends.', 'Lower relapse rates are the principal advantage of alarm-based behavioral treatment over desmopressin.'),
  ('2011-65', 'A delayed subcortical dementia from leukoencephalopathy can follow {{c1::whole-brain radiation}}.', 'Whole-brain radiation can produce delayed white-matter injury with frontal-subcortical cognitive decline.'),
  ('2011-66', '{{c1::Valproate}} increases lamotrigine blood levels by inhibiting its {{c2::glucuronidation}}.', 'Valproate decreases lamotrigine clearance; enzyme-inducing antiseizure drugs tend to do the opposite.'),
  ('2011-67', 'Vertigo, ipsilateral facial sensory loss, nystagmus, and ipsilateral ataxia localize to a {{c1::lateral medullary stroke}}.', 'Lateral medullary stroke is the Wallenberg syndrome pattern described by this presentation.'),
  ('2011-68', 'The best time to discuss an advance directive is {{c1::while the patient has decision-making capacity}}.', 'When the patient is competent, advance planning can reflect the patient’s own informed values and choices.'),
  ('2011-69', 'Competence to proceed in a criminal case is assessed by evaluating the defendant’s {{c1::current mental functioning}}.', 'Current mental functioning determines competence to understand proceedings and assist counsel.'),
  ('2011-70', 'Activated charcoal is ineffective for overdose with {{c1::lithium}} because it does not adsorb this inorganic ion.', 'Lithium is a classic activated-charcoal exception because charcoal does not bind it effectively.'),
  ('2011-71', 'Among commonly used antidepressants, {{c1::venlafaxine}} is notable for {{c2::dose-related hypertension}}.', 'Venlafaxine can increase blood pressure, particularly at higher doses. Monitor blood pressure as the dose is increased.'),
  ('2011-72', 'In the stages-of-change model, commitment to change without sustained behavior change indicates {{c1::preparation}}.', 'Preparation is marked by recognition of a problem plus commitment to change. Sustained implementation would indicate action.'),
  ('2011-73', 'The historical candidate-gene association for harm avoidance is the {{c1::serotonin transporter}} gene.', 'Serotonin transporter polymorphisms were historically associated with harm avoidance. Complex temperament traits are not determined by one gene.'),
  ('2011-74', 'A defining intervention in Davanloo therapy is to {{c1::clarify and confront defenses}} that block underlying affect.', 'Clarify and relentlessly confront the patient''s defenses is central to Davanloo therapy. The aim is access to avoided affect and conflict.'),
  ('2011-75', '{{c1::Tricyclic antidepressant overdose}} causes an anticholinergic toxidrome with {{c2::dangerous cardiac conduction abnormalities}}.', 'Tricyclic antidepressant overdose produces anticholinergic signs and cardiotoxic ECG changes. It is a medical emergency.'),
  ('2011-76', 'Among SSRIs, {{c1::paroxetine}} has high discontinuation-syndrome risk because of its {{c2::short half-life}}.', 'Paroxetine is the listed SSRI most likely to cause discontinuation symptoms after missed doses. Fluoxetine has a much lower risk.'),
  ('2011-77', 'A {{c1::blunted TSH response to TRH stimulation}} is classically associated with {{c2::major depression}}.', 'Depression is the classic association with a blunted TRH stimulation response. This is historical teaching, not routine modern diagnosis.'),
  ('2011-78', 'For initial symptomatic treatment of Parkinson disease in an older adult, choose {{c1::carbidopa/levodopa}}.', 'Carbidopa/levodopa provides strong motor benefit and is generally favored initially in older patients. Dopamine agonists have greater neuropsychiatric risk.'),
  ('2011-79', 'For incontinence with significant cognitive impairment, use {{c1::scheduled (timed) toileting}} as an initial behavioral intervention.', 'Schedule regular voiding is the correct intervention. It reduces accidents without relying on the patient to request help.'),
  ('2011-80', 'Compared with later-onset schizophrenia, {{c1::childhood-onset schizophrenia}} more often has a {{c2::gradual, insidious onset}}.', 'Childhood-onset schizophrenia is more likely to have a gradual onset. Its prognosis is generally poorer, not better.'),
  ('2011-81', 'In Freudian dream theory, the narrative remembered upon awakening is the {{c1::manifest dream}}.', 'Manifest dream is the correct term. It is the consciously remembered dream content.'),
  ('2011-82', 'Historically, the most commonly alleged patient harm in psychiatric malpractice suits is {{c1::suicide or attempted suicide}}.', 'Suicide or attempted suicide is the correct historical answer. Careful risk assessment and documentation remain essential.'),
  ('2011-83', 'In CBT, respectful humor can help patients {{c1::gain a new perspective on distorted cognitions}}.', 'Helping to create a new perspective on distorted cognitions is the correct rationale. Humor must remain respectful and collaborative.'),
  ('2011-84', 'In Tuckman group development, {{c1::norming}} is marked by growing cohesion that supports self-disclosure and experiential learning.', 'Norming is the correct stage. Growing trust allows members to reveal differences constructively.'),
  ('2011-85', '{{c1::Bradylalia}} is abnormally {{c2::slow speech}}, which may occur in depression.', 'Bradylalia is the correct term for abnormally slow speech. It can occur with depressive psychomotor retardation or parkinsonism.'),
  ('2011-86', 'Burning dysesthetic pain with electric shock-like paroxysms is most characteristic of {{c1::neuropathic pain}}.', 'Neuropathic pain commonly feels burning, shooting, or electric. Somatic and visceral pain refer to nociceptive sources rather than this characteristic quality.'),
  ('2011-87', 'Chronic opioid use commonly suppresses the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis and lowers {{c1::testosterone}}.', 'Testosterone is commonly reduced during chronic opioid exposure. The effect reflects opioid-associated suppression of gonadal-axis signaling.'),
  ('2011-88', 'A {{c1::large verbal-nonverbal IQ discrepancy}} is a reason to pursue further assessment rather than relying only on the composite IQ.', 'A verbal score significantly higher than a nonverbal score suggests an uneven profile requiring further evaluation. A composite score alone can obscure that pattern.'),
  ('2011-89', 'The most common adverse effects of stimulant medication in children are {{c1::appetite suppression}} and {{c2::sleep disturbance}}.', 'Appetite suppression and insomnia are common stimulant effects. Severe psychiatric and cardiovascular reactions are important but much less common.'),
  ('2011-90', 'Nystagmus that is vertical or multidirectional and worsens with visual fixation suggests a {{c1::central brainstem lesion}}.', 'Brainstem lesions can produce a central nystagmus pattern. Peripheral vestibular nystagmus generally diminishes with visual fixation.'),
  ('2011-91', 'For an unclear family-linked discharge conflict without an acute psychiatric emergency, the next step is often a {{c1::joint meeting with the patient, family, and medical team}}.', 'A joint meeting obtains collateral and supports safe discharge planning. Antipsychotic treatment or psychiatric admission is premature without clearer indications.'),
  ('2011-92', 'A newly invented word with a private or idiosyncratic meaning is a {{c1::neologism}}.', 'A neologism is an invented word. It differs from word salad, which is broader incomprehensible disorganization of speech.'),
  ('2011-93', 'Most of the human body’s serotonin is located in the {{c1::gastrointestinal tract}}.', 'The gastrointestinal tract contains most body serotonin. This helps explain common gastrointestinal effects of serotonergic medications.'),
  ('2011-94', '{{c1::Fragile X syndrome}} is caused by expansion of a {{c2::CGG trinucleotide repeat in FMR1}}.', 'Fragile X syndrome is the triple-repeat disorder in this list. Its molecular hallmark is CGG expansion in FMR1.'),
  ('2011-95', 'A key suicide-risk documentation practice is to record {{c1::why alternative interventions were considered and rejected}}.', 'Why alternative interventions were rejected should be documented. Record the rationale for the selected response in individualized, factual language.'),
  ('2011-96', 'The {{c1::Stroop Test}} assesses inhibitory control by requiring naming of ink colors while suppressing word reading.', 'The Stroop Test creates interference between word meaning and ink color. It primarily tests selective attention and response inhibition.'),
  ('2011-97', 'The most effective symptomatic treatment for focal hand dystonia (writer’s cramp) is {{c1::targeted botulinum toxin injection}}.', 'Botulinum toxin reduces overactivity in the specific muscles causing writer’s cramp. Systemic antiseizure drugs are not the standard best treatment.'),
  ('2011-98', 'Clinically significant depressive symptoms after an identifiable stressor that do not meet criteria for major depression indicate {{c1::adjustment disorder with depressed mood}}.', 'Adjustment disorder with depressed mood is stress-related and subthreshold for another mood disorder. A three-month course is too short for dysthymic disorder.'),
  ('2011-99', 'APA ethics prohibit sexual relationships with {{c1::current or former psychiatric patients}}.', 'Not allowed with current or former patients is the correct ethical rule. Consent or termination of treatment does not remove the boundary.'),
  ('2011-100', 'When monopolization and scapegoating divide a therapy group, the therapeutic factor most undermined is {{c1::cohesiveness}}.', 'Cohesiveness is the group’s sense of belonging and trust. Persistent monopolization and group rallying against one member damage it.'),
  ('2011-101', '{{c1::Primary-process thinking}} is mentation centered on drives and {{c2::wish fulfillment}} rather than reality-based logic.', 'Mentation focused on drives and wish fulfillment defines primary process. Secondary process is more logical, reality-oriented, and planful.'),
  ('2011-102', 'Aseptic meningitis with lymphocytic CSF pleocytosis and normal glucose is commonly caused by an enterovirus such as {{c1::Coxsackie virus}}.', 'Coxsackie virus is an enterovirus that can cause aseptic meningitis. The CSF pattern favors viral over typical bacterial meningitis.'),
  ('2011-103', 'A practical barrier to wider telepsychiatry adoption is {{c1::clinician reluctance to use the care modality}}.', 'Psychiatrist reluctance to use the care modality is the correct answer. Regulatory and workflow issues matter, but they are not categorical prohibitions.'),
  ('2011-104', 'In the historical PRITE treatment framework, {{c1::pramipexole}} is a first-line medication for {{c2::restless legs syndrome}}.', 'Pramipexole is the keyed historical first-line agent for restless legs syndrome. Current treatment selection also considers augmentation risk and non-dopaminergic options.'),
  ('2011-105', 'In cognitive therapy, {{c1::Socratic questioning}} is used to uncover and modify {{c2::automatic negative thoughts}}.', 'Socratic questioning helps patients identify and evaluate automatic negative thoughts through collaborative guided discovery.'),
  ('2011-106', 'Among the listed medications, {{c1::sertraline}} can increase methadone exposure and contribute to opioid toxicity.', 'Sertraline can increase methadone exposure in some patients. Carbamazepine instead tends to induce metabolism and lower methadone levels.'),
  ('2011-107', 'Hydrochlorothiazide can {{c1::increase serum lithium concentrations}} by promoting renal sodium loss and compensatory lithium reabsorption.', 'Hydrochlorothiazide raises lithium levels through altered renal sodium and lithium handling. This can precipitate lithium toxicity without a change in the lithium dose.'),
  ('2011-108', 'On a continuous performance test, failing to respond to a target stimulus is an {{c1::omission error}} that most strongly suggests impaired {{c2::sustained attention}}.', 'Attention is correct because the patient missed a required response. Responding when instructed not to would instead be a commission error linked to impulsivity or disinhibition.'),
  ('2011-109', 'For severe antidepressant-associated sweating when fluoxetine is otherwise effective, {{c1::terazosin}} can be used as a symptom-targeted adjunct.', 'Terazosin is the keyed adjunct for severe fluoxetine-associated sweating. It may allow continuation of an otherwise effective antidepressant.'),
  ('2011-110', '{{c1::Syringomyelia}} causes bilateral segmental loss of pain and temperature with preserved touch by injuring crossing spinothalamic fibers.', 'Syringomyelia explains the dissociated sensory loss and segmental lower-motor-neuron arm findings. A remote spinal injury can be followed by delayed syrinx formation.'),
  ('2011-111', 'For acute trauma-related dissociation with disorientation, use {{c1::grounding strategies}} to restore present-moment orientation.', 'Grounding is appropriate because the patient is dissociated and disoriented. It supports safety and orientation without reinforcing the altered perception.'),
  ('2011-112', 'Freud''s Little Hans case was used to formulate a psychodynamic theory of {{c1::phobia}} formation.', 'Phobia is correct because Little Hans''s fear of horses was interpreted as displaced anxiety.'),
  ('2011-113', '{{c1::Premenstrual dysphoric disorder}} can produce mood symptoms as severe as those of {{c2::major depressive disorder}} during the premenstrual phase.', 'PMDD can be as severe as major depressive disorder. Its key distinction is recurrent late-luteal timing with remission around menses.'),
  ('2011-114', 'Common neuropsychiatric manifestations of SLE include {{c1::depression and cognitive dysfunction}}, without either being a universal single prevalence winner.', 'Depression / cognitive dysfunction is the correct combined answer. Both are common in SLE, but their relative prevalence varies across populations and definitions.'),
  ('2011-115', 'For this 2011 question, {{c1::alcohol}} is the keyed most common adolescent substance-of-abuse answer.', 'Alcohol is the keyed historical answer for this 2011 item. Clinical screening should still assess all commonly used substances.'),
  ('2011-116', 'For pediatric delayed sleep onset driven by circadian delay, {{c1::melatonin}} is used to help restore normal circadian timing.', 'Melatonin is the best evidence-based listed option for delayed sleep onset without broader psychopathology. It addresses circadian timing rather than simply causing sedation.'),
  ('2011-117', 'The Five-Factor Model consists of {{c1::neuroticism, extraversion, openness, agreeableness, and conscientiousness}}.', 'Neuroticism, extraversion, openness, agreeableness, and conscientiousness are the five canonical trait domains. Anxiety and impulsivity are not separate Five-Factor domains.'),
  ('2011-118', 'Among these listed primary childhood CNS tumors, the best answer is {{c1::medulloblastoma}}, while broader rankings depend on tumor definitions.', 'Medulloblastoma is the corrected, best-listed answer. Neuroblastoma is usually extracranial, and omitted tumor types prevent a universal frequency claim.'),
  ('2011-119', 'In Winnicott’s model, good-enough parenting is {{c1::sensitive to the child’s exploration and growth}} while providing a reliable holding environment.', 'Being sensitive to exploration and growth is correct. Healthy parenting does not eliminate all frustration or transitional phenomena.'),
  ('2011-120', 'Flumazenil is limited in benzodiazepine overdose because it can {{c1::precipitate withdrawal seizures}} in benzodiazepine-dependent patients.', 'Flumazenil can abruptly precipitate benzodiazepine withdrawal and seizures. This makes it unsafe for many patients with chronic use or uncertain mixed ingestion.'),
  ('2011-121', 'In adults older than 60, {{c1::antidepressants}} are a medication class strongly associated with falls, alongside benzodiazepines.', 'Antidepressants are the keyed medication class associated with falls in older adults. Risk is amplified by sedation, orthostasis, hyponatremia, and polypharmacy.'),
  ('2011-122', 'Failure to arrange reasonable coverage for an established patient during an absence creates risk of {{c1::patient abandonment}}.', 'Abandonment is the failure to provide reasonable continuity or coverage for an established patient. Clear coverage and transition plans reduce this risk.'),
  ('2011-123', 'In adolescents with borderline personality disorder, serotonergic dysregulation is classically associated with suicidal behavior, aggression, and {{c1::impulsivity}}.', 'Impulsivity is correct because it is the classic behavioral correlate of serotonergic dysregulation in this risk formulation.'),
  ('2011-124', 'The {{c1::Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory (MCMI)}} is an objective, structured self-report measure of adult personality patterns.', 'Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory is correct because it is a standardized self-report personality inventory.'),
  ('2011-125', 'In this heavy smoker with suspected paraneoplastic cerebellar degeneration, the best-listed next test is {{c1::CT of the chest}}, with further cancer evaluation guided by context.', 'Computed tomography of the chest is the correct best-listed answer. Anti-Yo also warrants anatomy- and context-dependent evaluation for breast or gynecologic malignancy.'),
  ('2011-126', 'For this obsolete exam-era term, “deteriorative disorder” was distinguished from schizophrenia by the {{c1::absence of prominent positive symptoms}}.', 'Absence of prominent positive symptoms is the correct historical answer. “Deteriorative disorder” is a noncurrent, ambiguous term and should not be used as a modern diagnosis.'),
  ('2011-127', 'Philippe Pinel’s humane, structured institutional approach using work and daily routines was called {{c1::moral treatment}}.', 'Moral treatment is correct because it emphasized humane care, structured routine, and meaningful activity in institutions.'),
  ('2011-128', '{{c1::Methadone maintenance}} is an established treatment for opioid use disorder during pregnancy.', 'Methadone maintenance is correct because it provides stable, established treatment for opioid use disorder during pregnancy.'),
  ('2011-129', 'In psychodynamic therapy, feelings toward the therapist that replay a prior relationship pattern should be addressed by {{c1::interpreting and exploring the transference}}.', 'Interpretation of transference is correct because the cancellation activates the patient’s expectation of an unavailable father within therapy.'),
  ('2011-130', 'The older term “ICU psychosis” most often refers to {{c1::delirium}}.', 'Delirium is the acute, fluctuating confusional state historically called ICU psychosis.'),
  ('2011-131', 'Compared with SSRIs, {{c1::mirtazapine}} is less likely to cause {{c2::diarrhea}}.', 'Diarrhea is correct because it is a common serotonergic gastrointestinal effect with SSRIs and is less frequent with mirtazapine.'),
  ('2011-132', 'A major advantage of noncontrast head CT over MRI is rapid diagnosis of {{c1::acute epidural hemorrhage}}.', 'Acute epidural hemorrhage is correct because CT rapidly detects acute blood, whereas MRI is better for many soft-tissue lesions.'),
  ('2011-133', 'For schizophrenia with a co-occurring addictive disorder, {{c1::clozapine}} is associated with reduced psychotic symptoms and improved substance-use outcomes.', 'Clozapine is correct because it is associated with improvement in both psychosis and substance-use outcomes in this population.'),
  ('2011-134', 'A first {{c1::focal-onset seizure}} in an older adult should prompt evaluation for a {{c2::structural brain lesion such as glioblastoma}}.', 'Glioblastoma multiforme is correct because new focal-onset seizures in later life can signal an acquired structural lesion.'),
  ('2011-135', 'A major therapeutic goal of self-help groups is {{c1::overcoming maladaptive behaviors}} through peer mutual aid.', 'Overcoming maladaptive behaviors is correct because peer groups principally support practical, sustained behavior change.'),
  ('2011-136', 'Psychological stress can alter {{c1::neuro-immuno-endocrine function}}, linking nervous, hormonal, and immune responses.', 'Alteration of neuro-immuno-endocrine function is correct because stress affects interacting autonomic, endocrine, and immune pathways.'),
  ('2011-137', 'Needle-exchange programs are a {{c1::harm-reduction}} strategy for people who inject drugs.', 'Harm is correct because needle exchange lowers infectious and other injection-related risks without requiring abstinence.'),
  ('2011-138', 'Chronic {{c1::toluene}} exposure can cause toxic leukoencephalopathy with subcortical demyelinating lesions.', 'Toluene is correct because chronic solvent inhalation can cause neuropsychiatric symptoms and diffuse white-matter injury.'),
  ('2011-139', 'In schizophrenia, {{c1::age younger than 35 years}} is a consistently identified risk factor for suicide.', 'Age younger than 35 years is correct because suicide risk in schizophrenia is particularly elevated in younger adults, often early in illness.'),
  ('2011-140', 'The Tuskegee syphilis study violated research ethics by imposing an unjustifiable {{c1::risk-benefit ratio}} on participants.', 'Risk-benefit ratio is correct because participants were exposed to avoidable harm, including denial of effective treatment, without ethical justification.'),
  ('2011-141', 'Problematic internet use can show both {{c1::tolerance-like escalation}} and {{c2::withdrawal-like dysphoria or irritability when access stops}}.', 'Both tolerance and withdrawal to Internet use can develop, reflected by escalating use and dysphoria or anger when access is blocked.'),
  ('2011-142', 'A {{c1::right medial rectus}} palsy causes impaired right-eye adduction and horizontal diplopia that is worst on left gaze.', 'Right medial rectus is correct because this muscle adducts the right eye; weakness is revealed when leftward gaze requires that action.'),
  ('2011-143', 'For most patients, clinical hypnosis emphasizes using {{c1::the patient’s inner resources}} to support {{c2::adaptive coping}}.', 'Encouraging use of inner resources is correct because clinical hypnosis is typically collaborative and strengthens the patient’s own coping capacity.'),
  ('2011-144', '{{c1::Vagus nerve stimulation}} is an implanted neuromodulation treatment option for selected patients with {{c2::drug-resistant epilepsy}}.', 'Electrical stimulation of the vagus nerve is correct because VNS is used as adjunctive neuromodulation for refractory epilepsy.'),
  ('2011-145', 'When dementia-related aggression threatens home care, assess {{c1::caregiver burnout and capacity}} as part of the intervention.', 'Assessing caregiver burnout is correct because caregiver strain is a modifiable contributor to crisis and home-care failure.'),
  ('2011-146', 'A typical fine {{c1::lithium-induced tremor}} is usually reduced with {{c2::propranolol}}.', 'Propranolol is correct because beta-blockade commonly reduces the fine action tremor associated with lithium.'),
  ('2011-147', 'CBT for panic disorder targets {{c1::catastrophic misinterpretation of bodily sensations}} through {{c2::cognitive restructuring}}.', 'Using restructured interpretation of disturbing sensations is correct because panic CBT addresses catastrophic appraisals that amplify fear.'),
  ('2011-148', '{{c1::Late-onset schizophrenia}} occurs more frequently in {{c2::women}} than in men.', 'Occurs more frequently in women than men is correct; female predominance is a characteristic association of late-onset schizophrenia.'),
  ('2011-149', 'In normal aging, sleep is more fragmented, with increased {{c1::duration of nocturnal awakenings}}.', 'Duration of awakenings increases because sleep becomes lighter and less consolidated with age.'),
  ('2011-150', 'Syncope triggered by a tight collar suggests {{c1::carotid sinus hypersensitivity}}, which can cause {{c2::bradycardia and/or hypotension}}.', 'Carotid sinus syncope is correct because neck pressure from a tight collar can trigger reflex cerebral hypoperfusion.')
insert into flashcards (question_id, cloze_text, extra) values
  ('2011-151', 'In Thomas and Chess’s framework, a difficult child shows {{c1::intense expressions of mood}} along with poor adaptability and irregularity.', 'Intense expressions of mood is correct because emotional intensity is a defining feature of difficult temperament.'),
  ('2011-152', '{{c1::Salicylate (aspirin) overdose}} classically causes tinnitus and may produce {{c2::metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis}}.', 'Aspirin is correct because salicylate toxicity classically causes tinnitus and a mixed acid-base disturbance.'),
  ('2011-153', 'Among commonly tested psychotropics, {{c1::trazodone}} is most classically associated with rare {{c2::priapism}}.', 'Trazodone is correct because it has the classic psychotropic association with rare priapism.'),
  ('2011-154', 'Adolescent-onset generalized seizures with {{c1::morning myoclonic jerks and dropped objects}} suggest {{c2::juvenile myoclonic epilepsy}}.', 'Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy is correct because adolescent myoclonic jerks with generalized polyspike-and-wave activity are characteristic.'),
  ('2011-155', 'In a polarized end-of-life conflict, the consultant should first {{c1::meet separately with family and staff}} to explore and de-escalate {{c2::splitting/polarization}}.', 'Meet separately with family and staff is correct because it explores the polarized alliances without escalating confrontation.'),
  ('2011-156', 'Melanie Klein is especially associated with {{c1::early object relations}} and primitive defenses such as {{c2::splitting}}.', 'Early object relations is correct because Klein emphasized the infant’s earliest internalized relationships more strongly than Freud did.'),
  ('2011-157', 'Acute opioids inhibit adenylyl cyclase–cAMP signaling in {{c1::locus coeruleus}} noradrenergic neurons, contributing to {{c2::opioid withdrawal}} neuroadaptation.', 'Locus coeruleus is correct because opioids there acutely reduce cAMP-dependent signaling. Chronic compensation in this pathway contributes to withdrawal hyperarousal.'),
  ('2011-158', 'Under DSM-IV-TR, clinically significant compulsive skin picking was classified as {{c1::impulse control disorder NOS}}.', 'Impulse control disorder NOS is correct for DSM-IV-TR. Excoriation disorder received its own OCD-related category only in later nosology.'),
  ('2011-159', 'In classical conditioning, {{c1::extinction}} is weakening of a conditioned response when the conditioned stimulus is presented without the {{c2::unconditioned stimulus}}.', 'Weakening of the conditioned response due to withdrawal of the stimulus is correct; clinically, the unconditioned stimulus is withheld after the conditioned cue.'),
  ('2011-160', 'On the MMPI-2, unrealistically virtuous or socially desirable responding is detected by the {{c1::Lie (L) scale}}.', 'Lie scale is correct because it detects overly favorable self-presentation. The F scale instead concerns infrequent or unusual responses.'),
  ('2011-161', 'In pediatric SSRI trials, {{c1::more youths improve clinically}} than {{c2::spontaneously report treatment-emergent suicidality}}.', 'More youths respond favorably than spontaneously report suicidality. This supports treatment when indicated together with structured safety monitoring.'),
  ('2011-162', 'A {{c1::no-harm contract}} should not be used to determine suicide risk because it {{c2::lacks empirical validation}}.', 'It lacks empirical support as a suicide-risk tool. A collaborative safety plan is more actionable but does not replace full risk assessment.'),
  ('2011-163', 'The common limb-onset presentation of {{c1::amyotrophic lateral sclerosis}} is progressive {{c2::asymmetric distal weakness with muscle wasting}}.', 'Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis commonly begins with asymmetric distal limb weakness and focal atrophy. It later shows combined lower and upper motor neuron signs.'),
  ('2011-164', 'In DSM-IV schizophrenia criteria, a single characteristic symptom could suffice if the delusion was {{c1::bizarre}}.', 'Bizarre is correct under the historical DSM-IV exception. DSM-5 no longer permits this one-symptom exception.'),
  ('2011-165', '{{c1::Metformin}} can attenuate weight gain in some patients treated with valproate and a {{c2::second-generation antipsychotic}}.', 'Metformin is correct because it can moderate psychotropic-associated weight gain. It complements rather than replaces metabolic monitoring and lifestyle support.'),
  ('2011-166', 'IPT for depression begins with an interpersonal inventory that assesses the {{c1::quality of current social connections}}.', 'Quality of social connections is correct because IPT begins with an interpersonal inventory. The approach focuses on present relational contexts for depression.'),
  ('2011-167', 'Rapid dementia with {{c1::stimulus-sensitive myoclonus}} is strongly suggestive of a {{c2::spongiform encephalopathy}}.', 'Spongiform encephalopathy is correct because prion disease classically causes rapid cognitive decline and startle-provoked myoclonus. Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease is the prototypical example.'),
  ('2011-168', 'A defendant may be incompetent to stand trial if unable to {{c1::assist counsel rationally in the defense}}, even when understanding the proceedings.', 'Being unable to assist the attorney is correct because competence includes rational consultation with counsel. Criminal responsibility criteria address a different retrospective legal question.'),
  ('2011-169', '{{c1::Codeine overdose}} can cause the opioid toxidrome of {{c2::respiratory depression and pinpoint pupils}}.', 'Codeine is correct because it is an opioid. Hypoventilation with miosis is more characteristic of opioid toxicity than alcohol, LSD, cocaine, or diazepam alone.'),
  ('2011-170', 'The {{c1::WAIS-IV Vocabulary test}} is commonly used to estimate {{c2::premorbid intellectual functioning}} in early dementia.', 'WAIS-IV Vocabulary is correct because crystallized verbal knowledge is relatively resistant to early decline. It helps estimate baseline ability when interpreting current scores.'),
  ('2011-171', '{{c1::Fluoxetine}}, an SSRI, may treat premature ejaculation by {{c2::delaying ejaculation}}.', 'Fluoxetine is correct because SSRIs can prolong ejaculatory latency. This therapeutic effect reflects a serotonergic sexual effect that can be adverse in other settings.'),
  ('2011-172', 'The middle phase of interpersonal psychotherapy focuses on the patient’s {{c1::current relationships}} within the chosen interpersonal problem area.', 'Current relationships is correct because IPT’s middle phase works on present interpersonal problems. Transference and intrapsychic conflict are not its central focus.'),
  ('2011-173', 'Shoulder dislocation with deltoid weakness and numbness over the lateral shoulder indicates injury to the {{c1::axillary nerve}}.', 'Axillary nerve injury explains both deltoid dysfunction and sensory loss over the lateral shoulder.'),
  ('2011-174', 'The {{c1::observing ego}} allows unconscious impulses and inner processes to become objects of {{c2::conscious reflection}}.', 'The observing ego makes internal processes available for reflection and insight.'),
  ('2011-175', 'Psychosis arising after a seizure cluster and resolving over days is most accurately termed {{c1::postictal psychosis}}, although interictal psychosis was the closest listed option here.', 'Interictal psychosis is the retained inferred answer only because it is the closest listed epilepsy-associated diagnosis. The described timing is more accurately postictal psychosis.'),
  ('2011-176', 'A central goal of multifamily {{c1::psychoeducation}} is providing families with illness knowledge and {{c2::direct practical guidance}}.', 'Providing knowledge and direct guidance is the central psychoeducational intervention described.'),
  ('2011-177', 'Biofeedback brings selected autonomic responses under voluntary influence through {{c1::operant conditioning}}.', 'Operant conditioning reinforces desired physiologic changes during biofeedback.'),
  ('2011-178', 'The {{c1::Thematic Apperception Test (TAT)}} uses ambiguous pictures to elicit narratives about {{c2::unconscious themes and conflicts}}.', 'The TAT is a projective test used to explore psychodynamic themes.'),
  ('2011-179', 'The most common core deficit in English-language reading disorder is impaired {{c1::phonological functioning}}.', 'Phonological functioning supports decoding and fluent word recognition.'),
  ('2011-180', 'A core motivational-interviewing strategy is to {{c1::develop discrepancy}} between a patient’s current behavior and personal values or goals.', 'Developing discrepancy elicits the patient’s own motivation to change.'),
  ('2011-181', 'Vitamin {{c1::B12 deficiency}} is a potentially reversible contributor to {{c2::cognitive slowing}} in older adults.', 'Vitamin B12 testing helps evaluate a reversible cause of cognitive symptoms.'),
  ('2011-182', 'After a {{c1::concussion}}, return to sport should occur only after recovery through a {{c2::graded, supervised progression}}.', 'Can play again in 1 week is the keyed historical recommendation for this uncomplicated concussion vignette.'),
  ('2011-183', 'To compare the mean of a continuous outcome across {{c1::three or more independent groups}}, use {{c2::analysis of variance (ANOVA)}}.', 'ANOVA is the appropriate omnibus test for comparing three group means.'),
  ('2011-184', 'Putting a patient at ease, expressing compassion, and showing expertise primarily strengthen {{c1::rapport}}.', 'Rapport is the trusting collaborative relationship supported by empathic, skillful interviewing.'),
  ('2011-185', 'Among psychiatric diagnostic categories, {{c1::mood disorders}} are most common among people who die by suicide.', 'Mood disorders are the most common diagnostic category among individuals who complete suicide.'),
  ('2011-186', 'Distress centered on faith or a change in religious affiliation without another mental disorder is a {{c1::religious or spiritual problem}}.', 'Religious or spiritual problem is appropriate when faith-related distress is present without a diagnosable mental disorder.'),
  ('2011-187', 'Paresthesias of the thumb, index, middle, and radial half of the ring finger indicate {{c1::median nerve entrapment at the wrist}}.', 'Median nerve entrapment produces the classic lateral three-and-a-half-digit sensory pattern.'),
  ('2011-188', 'Among the listed co-occurring conditions in autistic disorder, the most frequent is {{c1::intellectual disability}}.', 'Mental retardation is the historical term for intellectual disability, the keyed most frequent association.'),
  ('2011-189', 'In young children, the most common cause of aseptic meningitis is {{c1::enterovirus}}.', 'Enterovirus is the best answer because it is the most common cause of viral meningitis in children. Coxsackievirus is a subtype within the enterovirus group.'),
  ('2011-190', 'Object relations theory holds that the mind contains {{c1::internal representations of the self and others}}.', 'The mind is inhabited by mental representations of the self and others; these internalized representations organize relationships.'),
  ('2011-191', 'The primary function of a {{c1::mental health court}} is to direct eligible defendants with mental illness toward {{c2::treatment rather than punishment}}.', 'Mental health courts emphasize diversion, treatment linkage, and supervision. They are distinct from competency, insanity, commitment, and drug-court proceedings.'),
  ('2011-192', 'Among these historical choices, {{c1::fentanyl}} is comparatively less problematic with an MAOI, but any coadministration requires specialist risk management.', 'Fentanyl is the comparatively safer historical option, not a generally safe MAOI combination. It still carries serotonin-toxicity risk.'),
  ('2011-193', 'IQ scores are generally considered relatively stable beginning at approximately {{c1::7 years of age}}.', 'Seven years is the conventional developmental milestone for relative IQ stability. Stability does not eliminate the need to interpret testing in clinical context.'),
  ('2011-194', '{{c1::Assertive community treatment (ACT)}} provides intensive, multidisciplinary, around-the-clock community-based care for people with serious mental illness.', 'Assertive community treatment is defined by proactive multidisciplinary outreach in the community. It is a longitudinal service model, not a brief crisis intervention.'),
  ('2011-195', 'The first post–World War II ethical directive protecting human research subjects was the {{c1::Nuremberg Code}}.', 'The Nuremberg Code is the foundational postwar research-ethics directive. Its central enduring principle is voluntary informed consent.'),
  ('2011-196', 'Vivid, formed visual hallucinations with preserved cognition can occur with {{c1::posterior cerebral artery ischemia}} affecting visual association cortex.', 'Posterior cerebral artery ischemia is correct because focal posterior visual-system dysfunction can cause formed visual hallucinations with preserved insight.'),
  ('2011-197', 'For a school-age child, diagnosing ADHD requires evidence of symptoms and impairment in {{c1::more than one setting}}, often including a {{c2::teacher report}}.', 'A similar teacher report supports the required cross-setting impairment criterion. Family history, tests, scales, and stimulant response are not independently diagnostic.'),
  ('2011-198', 'Reinforcing a behavior after every third occurrence is a {{c1::fixed-ratio}} schedule of reinforcement.', 'Fixed-ratio reinforcement occurs after a specified number of responses. Here, the specified number is three.'),
  ('2011-199', 'Recounting a painful event in detail while showing no corresponding emotion illustrates {{c1::isolation of affect}}.', 'Isolation of affect detaches emotion from a conscious idea or memory. The trauma narrative remains intact, but its expected feeling is absent.'),
  ('2011-200', 'An effective parent-focused intervention for childhood ADHD is {{c1::behavioral management training}}.', 'Behavioral management teaches parents consistent reinforcement, structure, and consequences. It is a core psychosocial treatment for childhood ADHD.'),
  ('2011-201', 'Symmetric {{c1::proximal muscle weakness}} with preserved sensation and reflexes is characteristic of {{c2::polymyositis}}.', 'Polymyositis classically causes symmetric proximal weakness. Lack of myalgia does not exclude it.'),
  ('2011-202', 'Children at greatest risk of death from abuse are those aged {{c1::birth through 3 years}}.', 'Birth - 3 years is correct because infants and toddlers are most physically vulnerable and fully dependent on caregivers.'),
  ('2011-203', 'A {{c1::flexible, non-distressing imaginary companion}} is typically normal behavior in {{c2::preschool children}}.', 'Behavior typical for preschoolers includes imaginary companions as part of symbolic play. Assess further only with distress, impairment, rigidity, or other concerning symptoms.'),
  ('2011-204', 'When a patient expresses skepticism early in psychodynamic therapy, first {{c1::explore the here-and-now alliance process}} rather than prematurely interpreting resistance.', 'Exploring the patient’s current experience of therapy addresses a possible alliance rupture. It is more appropriate than immediately interpreting skepticism as resistance.'),
  ('2011-205', 'The dietary supplement with demonstrated efficacy for some depressive symptoms is {{c1::S-adenosyl-L-methionine (SAMe)}}.', 'S-adenosyl-L-methionine (SAMe) is the supplement classically associated with some antidepressant efficacy. It does not replace full assessment or monitoring for activation and interactions.'),
  ('2011-206', 'Severe factitious disorder is most commonly associated with {{c1::borderline personality disorder}}.', 'Borderline personality disorder is the keyed personality association with severe factitious disorder. Factitious disorder is motivated by assuming the sick role rather than clear external gain.'),
  ('2011-207', 'Idiopathic intracranial hypertension classically requires {{c1::papilledema with clearly elevated intracranial pressure}} after appropriate exclusion of {{c2::secondary causes}}.', 'B — Pseudotumor cerebri is the historical best-listed diagnosis. The reported pressure is not clearly elevated by current adult criteria, and venous thrombosis may require dedicated imaging.'),
  ('2011-208', 'A criminal defendant is generally {{c1::presumed competent to stand trial}} unless {{c2::a bona fide concern raises the issue}}.', 'Competence to stand trial is presumed unless questioned, and the court makes the legal finding.'),
  ('2011-209', 'Stimulant-induced craving and cue-triggered drug seeking are most closely linked to {{c1::glutamatergic}} neuroadaptations.', 'Glutamate mediates important learned, cue-triggered mechanisms of stimulant craving and relapse.'),
  ('2011-210', '{{c1::Bulimia nervosa}} involves recurrent binge eating followed by compensatory behavior, with self-evaluation unduly influenced by weight or shape.', 'Bulimia nervosa fits recurrent binge eating and purging in a patient who is not underweight.'),
  ('2011-211', 'Chromosome {{c1::22q11 deletion syndrome}} is associated with increased risk of {{c2::schizophrenia-spectrum psychosis}}.', 'Schizophrenia is the classic substantially increased psychiatric risk in 22q11 deletion syndrome.'),
  ('2011-212', 'For severe agitation, {{c1::intramuscular lorazepam}} may be appropriate when indicated, but the intoxicant and respiratory risk must guide medication selection.', 'Lorazepam 2 mg intramuscularly, repeated in 45 minutes if no response, is the retained keyed regimen. It is not a universal choice for unspecified intoxication.'),
  ('2011-213', 'The affective kindling model predicts that recurrent depressive episodes may {{c1::occur with progressively less external stress}}.', 'Recurrence without an identifiable stressor supports the idea that prior episodes have lowered the threshold for another episode.'),
  ('2011-214', 'Simultaneous bilateral peripheral facial nerve palsies should prompt consideration of {{c1::Lyme disease due to Borrelia burgdorferi}}.', 'Borrelia burgdorferi infection is the keyed most frequent cause of simultaneous bilateral facial palsies.'),
  ('2011-215', 'In adolescents, occasional substance experimentation without impairment or adverse consequences is not by itself evidence of {{c1::a substance use disorder}}.', 'Typical adolescent behavior is the keyed characterization because the vignette provides no impairment or substance-related consequences.'),
  ('2011-216', 'The exam-era keyed drug is {{c1::nifedipine}}, but suspected MAOI hypertensive crisis requires monitored emergency treatment rather than immediate-release nifedipine self-administration.', 'Nifedipine is the historical best-listed answer for this suspected MAOI hypertensive crisis. It should not be used as unsupervised self-treatment.'),
  ('2011-217', 'A go/no-go task primarily assesses {{c1::response inhibition}}, an element of {{c2::executive functioning}}.', 'Executive functioning is tested because the patient must inhibit tapping in response to the no-go signal.'),
  ('2011-218', '{{c1::Selective mutism}} most commonly causes functional difficulty {{c2::at school}}, where speaking is expected.', 'At school is the usual locus of impairment because selective mutism is context-specific failure to speak in social-performance settings.'),
  ('2011-219', 'After bereavement, {{c1::significant depressive symptoms shortly after the loss}} increase the risk of later impairment.', 'Significant depressive symptoms shortly after the loss are the strongest listed warning sign for later functional impairment after bereavement.'),
  ('2011-220', 'Overly rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia can cause {{c1::osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis)}}.', 'Central pontine myelinolysis is the classic neurologic complication of overly rapid hyponatremia correction.'),
  ('2011-221', '{{c1::Financial stress}} is a family-level risk factor for child abuse.', 'Financial stress is correct because economic strain can increase caregiver burden and reduce protective supports.'),
  ('2011-222', 'Compared with traditional ECT, magnetic seizure therapy may offer {{c1::fewer cognitive side effects}} because stimulation can be more focal.', 'Fewer cognitive side effects is magnetic seizure therapy’s potential advantage through more targeted stimulation.'),
  ('2011-223', '{{c1::Interferon-alpha}} treatment is classically associated with depression, anxiety, and cognitive adverse effects.', 'Interferon-alpha is the historical hepatitis C treatment classically associated with depression, anxiety, and cognitive impairment.'),
  ('2011-224', 'A hypothetical question about what a patient would do after losing a prescription primarily assesses {{c1::judgment}}.', 'Judgment is correct because the question tests practical, consequence-sensitive decision-making. It does not primarily test insight or cognitive recall.'),
  ('2011-225', 'Among school-age children, the most common learning disability is {{c1::reading disorder (specific learning disorder with impairment in reading)}}.', 'Reading disorder is correct because reading impairment is the most commonly identified specific learning disability. Mathematics and written-expression impairments are less common.'),
  ('2011-226', 'Ipsilateral {{c1::third-nerve palsy}} with contralateral hemiparesis usually reflects occlusion of a {{c2::posterior cerebral artery branch}}.', 'Posterior cerebral artery branches supply the ventral midbrain involved in Weber syndrome. The crossed CN III and contralateral motor findings are the localization clue.'),
  ('2011-227', 'In a compensation-related forensic evaluation, {{c1::vaguely defined symptoms}} should raise suspicion for malingering, especially when inconsistent with observed function.', 'Vaguely defined symptoms are a warning sign because they can be difficult to reconcile with objective functioning. Financial incentive alone is insufficient to diagnose malingering.'),
  ('2011-228', 'Routine tracking of patient satisfaction for accreditation is part of {{c1::continuous quality improvement}}.', 'Continuous quality improvement uses ongoing patient-experience measurement to guide system-level care improvement.'),
  ('2011-229', 'Among childhood and adolescent psychiatric disorders, {{c1::depressive disorders}} most commonly predict later obesity in longitudinal studies.', 'Depressive disorders are the key longitudinal psychiatric predictor of later obesity in this question. The relationship is multifactorial rather than deterministic.'),
  ('2011-230', 'An {{c1::anterior cerebral artery}} infarct classically causes weakness of the {{c2::contralateral foot and leg}}.', 'Weakness of the contralateral foot and leg is correct because the anterior cerebral artery supplies medial motor cortex representing the lower extremity.'),
  ('2011-231', 'During adolescence, the {{c1::prefrontal cortex develops later than many other brain regions}}, with executive control continuing to mature.', 'Prefrontal cortical maturation extends later than that of many other brain regions. This supports continued development of executive function during adolescence.'),
  ('2011-232', 'For a {{c1::skewed distribution}}, the {{c2::median}} is generally the best measure of central tendency.', 'Median is correct because a few high doses skew the mean upward. It better reflects the dose effective for most patients.'),
  ('2011-233', 'Acute psychiatric symptoms with fluctuating confusion after a viral prodrome suggest {{c1::delirium secondary to viral encephalitis}}.', 'Delirium secondary to viral encephalitis explains the viral prodrome, confusion, and anticholinergic worsening. Primary psychosis does not readily explain the cognitive fluctuation.'),
  ('2011-234', 'Progressive {{c1::chorea}} with cognitive or behavioral decline and an autosomal dominant family history suggests {{c2::Huntington disease}}.', 'Huntington disease explains the chorea, cognitive deterioration, irritability, and paternal multigenerational history. Genetic counseling is central to evaluation.'),
  ('2011-235', 'Sexual arousal from rubbing against or touching a {{c1::nonconsenting person}} is {{c2::frotteurism}}.', 'Frotteurism is correct because the behavior involves repeated nonconsensual bodily contact. Fetishism and sadomasochism describe different sources of arousal.'),
  ('2011-236', 'Abrupt withdrawal from {{c1::sedative-hypnotics}} can be life-threatening because it may cause {{c2::seizures and delirium}}.', 'Sedative-hypnotics can cause a dangerous withdrawal syndrome with seizures, delirium, and autonomic instability.'),
  ('2011-237', 'Acknowledging that alcohol may be a problem and considering treatment without making a plan corresponds to {{c1::contemplation}}.', 'Contemplation is the corrected answer because the patient recognizes a possible problem and is considering treatment. A concrete plan is not yet present.'),
  ('2011-238', 'Kant''s key contribution to psychiatry is the philosophical basis for {{c1::clinical ethics}}, especially {{c2::respect for autonomy}}.', 'Philosophical foundations for clinical ethics follow from Kant''s emphasis on duty, autonomy, and respect for persons.'),
  ('2011-239', 'Under {{c1::Rogers v. Commissioner}}, nonemergency antipsychotic treatment over objection requires a court-approved {{c2::substituted judgment treatment plan}}.', 'A court-approved substituted judgment plan is required under the Rogers framework for nonemergency treatment over objection. Family consent or team preference alone is not enough.'),
  ('2011-240', '{{c1::Oxcarbazepine}} can reduce the efficacy of {{c2::oral hormonal contraceptives}} through enzyme induction.', 'Oxcarbazepine may lower hormonal contraceptive exposure by inducing drug metabolism, reducing contraceptive efficacy.'),
  ('2011-241', 'Diffuse white-matter rarefaction with {{c1::microglial nodules and multinucleated giant cells}} is characteristic of {{c2::HIV-associated dementia}}.', 'HIV-associated dementia produces diffuse white-matter and microglial or macrophage pathology. PML and focal opportunistic lesions have different characteristic patterns.'),
  ('2011-242', '{{c1::Déjà vu}} is a {{c2::paramnesia}} in which an unfamiliar present experience feels falsely familiar.', 'Déjà vu is a paramnesia because it is a false sense of familiarity for a current experience.'),
  ('2011-243', 'In an acute psychiatric presentation, {{c1::clouded sensorium}} strongly suggests {{c2::delirium or another medical cause}}.', 'Clouded sensorium points toward delirium or a medical or substance-related condition because attention and awareness are impaired.'),
  ('2011-244', 'A distinctive component of comprehensive {{c1::dialectical behavior therapy}} is structured {{c2::telephone coaching}} to help patients use skills between sessions.', 'Telephone consultation is correct because DBT uses between-session skills coaching. It is structured and does not replace emergency care.'),
  ('2011-245', 'In Alcoholics Anonymous, evidence supporting {{c1::spirituality as a direct cause of abstinence}} is {{c2::limited}}.', 'There is limited evidence that spirituality itself accounts for abstinence in AA. Social and behavioral mechanisms may also contribute importantly.'),
  ('2011-246', 'Bupropion can cause a false-positive urine immunoassay screen for {{c1::amphetamines}}.', 'Amphetamine is correct because bupropion metabolites can cross-react with some amphetamine screening immunoassays. Definitive testing can clarify an unexpected result.'),
  ('2011-247', 'For longer-term suicide risk assessment, {{c1::a history of prior suicide attempts}} and {{c2::hopelessness}} are especially important factors.', 'History of prior attempts and hopelessness is correct because both retain important longitudinal association with suicide risk. Assess them alongside dynamic risk and protective factors.'),
  ('2011-248', 'Foot drop with EMG denervation of tibialis anterior and tibialis posterior localizes to an {{c1::L5 radiculopathy}}.', 'L-5 radiculopathy is correct because the abnormal muscles share L5 innervation but not a peripheral nerve. Normal sensory conductions support a preganglionic root lesion.'),
  ('2011-249', 'Panic symptoms that occur only with a feared, avoided situation are most consistent with {{c1::specific phobia}}, not {{c2::panic disorder}}.', 'Specific phobia produces cue-bound fear and avoidance; panic disorder requires recurrent unexpected panic attacks.'),
  ('2011-250', 'Mirtazapine''s key antidepressant receptor action is {{c1::central alpha-2 adrenergic antagonism}}.', 'Alpha-2-adrenergic antagonism is correct because mirtazapine increases norepinephrine and serotonin release by blocking presynaptic inhibitory receptors. Its sedation is mainly due to H1 blockade.'),
  ('2011-251', 'The psychiatric symptom most classically associated with pheochromocytoma is {{c1::panic attacks}}.', 'Panic attacks are correct because episodic catecholamine release can mimic acute panic. Look for accompanying headache, diaphoresis, palpitations, and hypertension.'),
  ('2011-252', 'At minimum, physician health programs provide a {{c1::structured monitoring program}} for impaired physicians.', 'Structured monitoring program is correct because monitoring is central to physician health programs'' recovery and patient-safety mission. Confidentiality protections are not absolute.'),
  ('2011-253', 'A person who wants to appoint a future health-care surrogate during possible incapacity should complete an {{c1::advance directive}}.', 'Advance directive allows a capable person to document future health-care preferences and designate a surrogate.'),
  ('2011-254', 'In the cited 1994–2004 comparison, the highest suicide completion rate was among {{c1::Caucasian American men in their 60s}}.', 'Caucasian American men in their 60s is correct for the epidemiologic period specified. Demographic risk informs vigilance but never replaces individual assessment.'),
  ('2011-255', 'New headache, visual symptoms, scalp tenderness, and elevated inflammatory markers in an older adult suggest giant cell arteritis, classically confirmed by {{c1::temporal artery biopsy}}.', 'Biopsy is indicated because the syndrome is classic for giant cell arteritis, a vision-threatening vasculitis.'),
  ('2011-256', 'Preschool pretend play requires {{c1::symbolic representation}}, such as using one object to represent another.', 'Use of one object to represent another is the symbolic capacity underlying pretend play.'),
  ('2011-257', 'Before starting an MAOI after fluoxetine, allow a minimum {{c1::5-week washout}} because of fluoxetine''s long-lived active metabolite.', '5 weeks is correct because fluoxetine and norfluoxetine persist for weeks. This reduces serotonin-toxicity risk when phenelzine is begun.'),
  ('2011-258', 'In schizophrenia, {{c1::family psychoeducational therapy}} has been shown to reduce relapse rates.', 'Family psychoeducational therapy is correct because it reduces relapse and rehospitalization. It complements medication and coordinated care.'),
  ('2011-259', 'For school-age children and adolescents, the most common reason for mental-health referral is {{c1::disruptive behavior}}.', 'Disruptive behavior is correct as the most common referral concern in this age range. It should trigger a broad, developmentally informed assessment.'),
  ('2011-260', 'The historical neuropsychiatric syndrome of psychosis, cognitive decline, and abnormal gait treated with fever therapy was {{c1::neurosyphilis}}.', 'Syphilis causes neurosyphilis or general paresis, historically associated with severe psychiatric and neurologic decline.'),
  ('2011-261', 'Among Alcoholics Anonymous attendees, {{c1::obtaining a sponsor}} is most clearly predictive of alcohol abstinence.', 'Obtaining a sponsor is correct because it reflects active AA involvement and recovery support. It is associated with abstinence rather than guaranteeing it.'),
  ('2011-262', 'Before ECT, {{c1::lithium}} is the medication most classically withheld because it can increase delirium and prolonged-seizure risk.', 'Lithium is correct because concurrent ECT can increase delirium, prolonged seizures, and neurotoxicity. Manage it in coordination with the ECT team.'),
  ('2011-263', 'Narcolepsy type 1 is specifically associated with loss of {{c1::hypocretin (orexin)}} signaling.', 'Hypocretin is correct because its deficiency destabilizes wakefulness and permits REM phenomena to intrude into wakefulness. It is especially linked to narcolepsy with cataplexy.'),
  ('2011-264', 'In a reproductive-age patient with hyperprolactinemia, first obtain a {{c1::pregnancy test}} before further etiologic workup.', 'Order a pregnancy test is correct because pregnancy is a physiologic cause of hyperprolactinemia. Persistent marked elevation after exclusion requires further evaluation.'),
  ('2011-265', '{{c1::Postpartum psychosis}} is a psychiatric emergency because of the risk of {{c2::infanticide and suicide}}.', 'Infanticide is the key emergency association because postpartum psychosis can produce dangerous infant-focused delusions.'),
  ('2011-266', 'Compared with men, women with alcohol use disorders may show a telescoping course with {{c1::faster progression from first drink to alcohol dependence}}.', 'Have a faster progression from first drink to alcohol dependence is correct because women may show a telescoping alcohol-use trajectory. Equivalent drinking amounts can also produce greater alcohol exposure.'),
  ('2011-267', 'Mirtazapine-associated sedation and weight gain are primarily caused by {{c1::histamine H1-receptor antagonism}}.', 'Histamine H1 antagonism causes both sedation and increased appetite or weight gain with mirtazapine.'),
  ('2011-268', 'In {{c1::supportive psychotherapy}}, the therapist may appropriately provide limited gratification of dependency needs to strengthen functioning.', 'Supportive psychotherapy uses structure, reassurance, and a reliable alliance to reinforce adaptive coping.'),
  ('2011-269', 'Binge-eating disorder differs from bulimia nervosa because it lacks {{c1::recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviors}}, so patients often do not maintain normal weight.', 'Binge-eating disorder commonly leads to overweight because binges are not regularly offset by purging, fasting, or driven exercise.'),
  ('2011-270', 'Needle EMG in a primary myopathy typically shows {{c1::small, short-duration motor-unit potentials}}.', 'Small, short-duration motor-unit potentials on needle examination are characteristic of myopathy.'),
  ('2011-271', 'A false-belief task measures {{c1::theory of mind}}: understanding that another person can hold a belief different from reality.', 'Theory of mind is tested by asking where a character will act based on that character''s false belief.'),
  ('2011-272', 'The first psychological step after acute trauma is to {{c1::establish and orient the patient to immediate safety}}.', 'Help the patient recognize that the danger has passed and that she is presently safe before moving to other interventions.'),
  ('2011-273', 'For depression, repetitive TMS is typically targeted to the {{c1::prefrontal cortex}}, especially the {{c2::dorsolateral prefrontal cortex}}.', 'Prefrontal cortex is correct because standard depression TMS protocols target the dorsolateral prefrontal region.'),
  ('2011-274', 'Among antidepressants, {{c1::nortriptyline}} has a routinely used therapeutic plasma concentration range for drug-level monitoring.', 'Nortriptyline is the classic antidepressant with clinically useful therapeutic drug monitoring.'),
  ('2011-275', 'Among CDT, MCV, and routine liver enzymes, {{c1::carbohydrate-deficient transferrin (CDT)}} is the best listed marker for sustained heavy drinking.', 'Carbohydrate-deficient transferrin is more specific for sustained heavy alcohol use than MCV or routine liver enzymes.'),
  ('2011-276', 'Unlike traditional psychotherapy groups, {{c1::self-help groups}} do not make {{c2::intragroup dynamics}} a central treatment goal.', 'Self-help groups focus on peer support and the shared problem rather than analysis of intragroup dynamics.'),
  ('2011-277', 'Among the listed antipsychotics, {{c1::risperidone}} has the largest evidence base for severe reactive aggression in youth.', 'Risperidone is the best-supported listed antipsychotic for explosiveness and reactive anger in youth.'),
  ('2011-278', 'Buspirone’s anxiolytic effect is mediated primarily by {{c1::partial agonism at serotonin 5-HT1A receptors}}.', '5-HT1A receptors mediate buspirone’s primary anxiolytic action through partial agonism.'),
  ('2011-279', 'For a newly forming outpatient psychotherapy group, {{c1::antisocial personality disorder}} is the strongest listed exclusion because it can undermine safety and cohesion.', 'Antisocial personality disorder is the keyed exclusion, although group selection should assess current behavior and risk rather than diagnosis alone.'),
  ('2011-280', 'New involuntary orofacial movements after chronic antipsychotic exposure suggest {{c1::tardive dyskinesia}}, prompting reassessment of dopamine-blocking treatment.', 'Gradually discontinue thioridazine and start low dose risperidone is the best listed historical treatment choice for this tardive dyskinesia presentation.'),
  ('2011-281', 'Naloxone can be combined with sublingual buprenorphine because naloxone has {{c1::poor sublingual bioavailability}}.', 'Poor sublingual absorption keeps naloxone from blocking buprenorphine when the combination is taken as directed.'),
  ('2011-282', 'Recurrent exercise-triggered flaccid weakness with {{c1::hypokalemia}} and family history suggests {{c2::hypokalemic periodic paralysis}}.', 'Periodic paralysis causes reversible weakness episodes associated with potassium shifts; this vignette is the hypokalemic familial form.'),
  ('2011-283', 'After remission of recurrent major depression, antidepressant maintenance treatment generally continues at {{c1::the same dose that achieved remission}}.', 'The effective acute-treatment dose is generally retained for continuation and maintenance treatment in recurrent depression.'),
  ('2011-284', 'A {{c1::boundary crossing}} is not automatically unethical; it must be assessed in its specific clinical context.', 'Boundary crossings may be appropriate or harmful depending on context, intent, and effect on the patient.'),
  ('2011-285', 'In anorexia nervosa with recurrent purging, a common metabolic abnormality is {{c1::hypokalemia (decreased serum potassium)}}.', 'Decreased serum potassium is the expected electrolyte concern with purging. Potassium loss increases the risk of weakness and cardiac arrhythmia.'),
  ('2011-286', '{{c1::Bell palsy}} causes an acute unilateral {{c2::peripheral facial nerve (CN VII) palsy}}, often with retroauricular pain and hyperacusis.', 'Idiopathic Bell''s palsy is a peripheral CN VII palsy. Forehead involvement and hyperacusis support this localization.'),
  ('2011-287', 'In family therapy, {{c1::reframing}} gives a negatively described behavior a {{c2::more constructive meaning}}.', 'Reframing changes the meaning, not necessarily the behavior itself. It reduces blame and opens new responses.'),
  ('2011-288', '{{c1::Animism}} is attributing life, feelings, or intentions to an {{c2::inanimate object}}.', 'Animism means treating an inanimate object as alive. It is commonly seen in early childhood thinking.'),
  ('2011-289', 'For secondary prevention after a noncardioembolic lacunar stroke, the keyed option is {{c1::aspirin plus dipyridamole antiplatelet therapy}}.', 'Aspirin plus dipyridamole is the keyed antiplatelet strategy. Empiric anticoagulation is not routine for a noncardioembolic lacunar infarct.'),
  ('2011-290', 'A characteristic physiologic change of normal aging is declining {{c1::creatinine clearance}}, which can be masked by {{c2::lower muscle mass}}.', 'Creatinine clearance declines with normal aging, so renal drug elimination may be reduced despite a seemingly normal serum creatinine.'),
  ('2011-291', 'Giving a person individualized treatment advice can {{c1::establish a doctor-patient relationship}} even outside a formal office visit.', 'Advising CBT for the neighbor''s symptoms is individualized treatment advice. That undertaking can establish a doctor-patient relationship.'),
  ('2011-292', '{{c1::TSC1}} mutation can cause {{c2::tuberous sclerosis complex}}, a neurocutaneous disorder associated with seizures and developmental difficulties.', 'TSC-1 is the keyed gene. Tuberous sclerosis can combine seizures, neurodevelopmental symptoms, and characteristic skin findings.'),
  ('2011-293', '{{c1::Addison disease (primary adrenal insufficiency)}} classically combines weight loss, depression, and {{c2::hyperpigmentation}}.', 'Addison disease is suggested by weight loss and hyperpigmentation. Psychiatric symptoms can be a prominent part of the presentation.'),
  ('2011-294', 'In DSM-IV terminology, trauma symptoms lasting {{c1::less than one month}} are diagnosed as {{c2::acute stress disorder}} when criteria are met.', 'Acute stress is the correct early post-trauma diagnosis. PTSD requires symptoms to persist beyond one month.'),
  ('2011-295', '{{c1::Karyotyping}} analyzes {{c2::chromosome number and gross chromosomal structure}}.', 'Analyzing chromosomal structures is the correct answer. Karyotyping detects aneuploidy and large structural rearrangements.'),
  ('2011-296', 'In Parkinson disease, {{c1::COMT inhibitors}} are added to levodopa to reduce {{c2::end-of-dose wearing off}}.', 'Wearing off of levodopa effect is the correct answer. COMT inhibitors prolong levodopa action.'),
  ('2011-297', 'The keyed average methadone maintenance dose range associated with reduced illicit opioid use is {{c1::60–100 mg daily}}.', '60–100 mg daily is the keyed average effective maintenance range. Methadone dosing is individualized for efficacy and safety.'),
  ('2011-298', 'A psychiatrist may ethically charge a missed-appointment fee when the patient is {{c1::specifically notified of the policy in advance}}.', 'Ethical if the patient is specifically notified in advance is the correct answer. Transparency supports a missed-appointment fee policy.'),
  ('2011-299', 'In suspected {{c1::Wilson disease}}, {{c2::increased urinary copper excretion}} supports the diagnosis.', 'Urinary copper excretion is the correct answer. It is increased in Wilson disease, which may have hepatic, neurologic, and psychiatric features.'),
  ('2011-300', 'With ongoing intimate-partner violence, {{c1::conjoint marital therapy}} is {{c2::contraindicated initially}} because it may increase danger.', 'Conjoint marital therapy is the correct answer. Safety assessment and individual support come first in active domestic violence.'),
  ('2012-1', 'In psychotherapy, a patient’s reenactment of feelings and expectations from prior relationships toward the therapist is called {{c1::transference}}.', 'Transference is the patient’s displacement of prior relational patterns onto the therapist. It can become useful clinical material when recognized and explored.'),
  ('2012-2', 'Damage to the {{c1::frontal/prefrontal lobes}} can cause major changes in {{c2::personality, judgment, and social behavior}}.', 'Frontal lobe influence on personality and character is the key lesson of Phineas Gage’s case.'),
  ('2012-3', 'Severe unilateral orbital headache lasting minutes to hours with ipsilateral tearing and nasal congestion is {{c1::cluster headache}}.', 'Cluster headaches cause brief, stereotyped unilateral attacks with ipsilateral cranial autonomic symptoms.'),
  ('2012-4', 'In autism spectrum disorder, pharmacotherapy is most likely to help a severe associated symptom such as {{c1::aggressiveness}}, not core social communication differences.', 'Aggressiveness is an associated behavioral target that may respond to medication. Core social-communication features generally require behavioral, educational, and communication interventions.'),
  ('2012-5', 'On the expressive-supportive therapy continuum, the most supportive intervention listed is {{c1::advising}}.', 'Advising is the most supportive intervention because it provides direct practical structure.'),
  ('2012-6', 'Acute blood outlining basal cisterns or cortical sulci on noncontrast CT indicates {{c1::subarachnoid hemorrhage}}.', 'Subarachnoid hemorrhage appears as acute hyperdense blood in cerebrospinal-fluid spaces on CT.'),
  ('2012-7', 'For severe major depression with urgent suicide risk, the treatment most likely to achieve rapid remission is {{c1::electroconvulsive therapy (ECT)}}.', 'Electroconvulsive therapy is especially valuable when severe depression requires a rapid, high-efficacy intervention.'),
  ('2012-8', 'Physician recovery is commonly monitored by {{c1::state physician health programs}} with jurisdiction-dependent involvement from {{c2::state medical boards}}.', 'State medical boards / State physician health programs are the keyed entities. PHPs usually conduct direct longitudinal monitoring, while boards provide regulatory oversight.'),
  ('2012-9', 'When responding to a delusion, clinicians should {{c1::validate the patient’s emotional distress}} without {{c2::affirming the delusional belief}}.', '“It sounds terrifying” conveys empathy without reinforcing the patient’s delusional account.'),
  ('2012-10', 'To evaluate how multiple independent variables relate to an outcome, use {{c1::regression analysis}}.', 'Regression analysis evaluates multiple predictors simultaneously and can adjust for included covariates.'),
  ('2012-11', 'Spasticity is associated with reduced inhibitory neurotransmission mediated by {{c1::GABA and glycine}}.', 'Glycine / GABA are the inhibitory transmitters whose reduced spinal influence contributes to spasticity.'),
  ('2012-12', 'Acute oculogyric crisis from an antipsychotic is treated promptly with {{c1::parenteral benztropine}}.', 'Benztropine rapidly treats antipsychotic-induced acute dystonia, including oculogyric crisis.'),
  ('2012-13', 'Childhood physical or sexual abuse is a recognized risk factor for {{c1::panic disorder}}.', 'Panic disorder is the keyed anxiety disorder associated with childhood physical and sexual abuse.'),
  ('2012-14', 'The capacity to set realistic personal goals and accept one’s limitations typically first emerges in {{c1::adolescence}}.', 'Adolescence brings more integrated identity formation and realistic self-appraisal.'),
  ('2012-15', 'The historical federal eligibility requirement tested for methadone maintenance was opioid dependence for at least {{c1::1 year}}.', '1 year is the historical federal duration requirement keyed for methadone maintenance eligibility.'),
  ('2012-16', 'Slowly progressive weakness of the {{c1::quadriceps and finger flexors}} in an older adult suggests {{c2::inclusion body myositis}}.', 'Inclusion body myositis classically targets quadriceps and finger flexors in later life.'),
  ('2012-17', 'A medication classically used to target PTSD-related nightmares is {{c1::prazosin}}, an {{c2::alpha-1 adrenergic antagonist}}.', 'Prazosin is the classic pharmacologic association for PTSD-related nightmares.'),
  ('2012-18', 'In interpersonal psychotherapy, assigning a temporary sick role gives a depressed patient {{c1::permission to be ill while retaining responsibility to participate in recovery}}.', 'Interpersonal therapy uses the sick role to reduce guilt and mobilize support. It simultaneously frames recovery as the patient''s active task.'),
  ('2012-19', 'The most common classic obsessional theme in obsessive-compulsive disorder is {{c1::contamination}}.', 'Contamination obsessions commonly produce washing, cleaning, and avoidance compulsions. The intrusive fear and neutralizing ritual, not ordinary cleanliness, define the OCD pattern.'),
  ('2012-20', 'A medication that can improve orthostatic hypotension in Parkinson disease by expanding intravascular volume is {{c1::fludrocortisone}}.', 'Fludrocortisone can support blood pressure through mineralocorticoid-mediated volume expansion. Monitor for edema, electrolyte changes, and supine hypertension.'),
  ('2012-21', '{{c1::Suppression}} is the conscious or semiconscious decision to set aside a distressing thought or conflict temporarily.', 'Suppression redirects attention away from distress with some degree of awareness. Repression is unconscious exclusion from awareness.'),
  ('2012-22', 'In most US jurisdictions, child-custody decisions are guided by the {{c1::best interests of the child}} standard.', 'The best-interests standard directs courts to assess the child''s welfare using multiple factors. Attachment and continuity may inform the analysis but are not the universal legal rule.'),
  ('2012-23', 'Antipsychotic-induced hyperprolactinemia occurs because {{c1::dopamine normally exerts tonic inhibition of prolactin secretion}}.', 'Dopamine restrains prolactin release through D2 signaling at pituitary lactotrophs. D2 blockade removes this inhibitory brake.'),
  ('2012-24', 'For a hospice patient who has stopped eating and drinking, a comfort-focused measure is {{c1::frequent small sips of water with oral care, when safely tolerated}}.', 'Frequent small sips of water can relieve dry mouth and thirst when swallowing is safe. Artificial feeding or hydration may add burden without improving terminal comfort.'),
  ('2012-25', 'Following an intentional {{c1::tricyclic antidepressant overdose}}, patients require {{c2::ECG-based cardiac monitoring and medical stabilization}} before psychiatric disposition.', 'Tricyclic antidepressant overdose can cause dangerous conduction abnormalities and dysrhythmias. A serum level alone does not replace ECG evaluation and monitored care.'),
  ('2012-26', 'The most frequent acute presentation of neurosyphilis is {{c1::stroke due to meningovascular syphilis}}.', 'Meningovascular neurosyphilis causes inflammatory arteritis and can present as stroke. Dementia and tabes dorsalis are more chronic forms.'),
  ('2012-27', 'For an otherwise typically developing toddler with limit-setting tantrums, the first clinical step is to {{c1::explore the parents’ concerns and the behavior’s context before pathologizing it}}.', 'Exploring parental concerns is appropriate when toddler tantrums otherwise appear developmentally expected. Family history alone does not establish ADHD or oppositional defiant disorder.'),
  ('2012-28', '{{c1::Thiamine (vitamin B1)}} deficiency causes Wernicke encephalopathy, classically with confusion, ataxia, and ocular abnormalities.', 'Thiamine prevents Wernicke encephalopathy in patients at risk from alcohol-related malnutrition. The key findings are confusion, gait ataxia, and nystagmus or ophthalmoplegia.'),
  ('2012-29', 'A treatment plan that uses consistent consequences to change an observable behavior derives from {{c1::behaviorism}}.', 'Behaviorism applies reinforcement and other contingencies to observable behavior. Consistency across caregivers is often essential to the plan.'),
  ('2012-30', 'A treating psychiatrist subpoenaed to describe treatment records without rendering an independent legal opinion is a {{c1::fact witness}}.', 'A fact witness testifies to firsthand observations and treatment records. An expert witness provides specialized opinions on legal questions.'),
  ('2012-31', 'Starting around age 3, children increasingly use language to {{c1::describe past events}}.', 'Describing past events is an emerging language use around age 3. It provides an early foundation for autobiographical narrative.'),
  ('2012-32', '{{c1::Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine)}} can cause polyneuropathy when either deficient or consumed in extreme excess.', 'Vitamin B6 deficiency and excessive vitamin B6 intake can both cause polyneuropathy. Ask about diet as well as supplements.'),
  ('2012-33', 'A person who recognizes a harmful behavior but has not committed to changing soon is in the {{c1::contemplation}} stage of change.', 'Contemplation includes awareness of a problem and ambivalence about change. Preparation requires a near-term intention and concrete planning.'),
  ('2012-34', 'In US diagnostic practice, neurasthenia-like presentations often overlap with {{c1::anxiety disorders}} as well as depressive and somatoform disorders.', 'Anxiety disorders commonly overlap with neurasthenia-like presentations in US classification. The syndrome requires consideration of both medical and psychiatric causes.'),
  ('2012-35', 'In standard DBT, {{c1::mindfulness}} is taught first and practiced regularly because it supports learning and use of the other skills modules.', 'A — Mindfulness is the foundational DBT skills module. It supports later use of distress-tolerance, emotion-regulation, and interpersonal-effectiveness skills.'),
  ('2012-36', 'Contralateral hemisensory loss followed by central pain and hyperpathia suggests a thalamic infarct in the {{c1::posterior cerebral artery}} distribution.', 'E — Posterior cerebral artery ischemia can injure thalamic sensory pathways. The syndrome may evolve from hemisensory loss to central pain and hyperpathia.'),
  ('2012-37', 'Hallucinations and delusions temporally related to heavy alcohol use or withdrawal, with a relatively clear sensorium, suggest {{c1::alcohol-induced psychotic disorder}}.', 'D — Alcohol-induced psychotic disorder fits the temporal relationship to heavy alcohol exposure. Delirium tremens requires delirium and typical withdrawal findings.'),
  ('2012-38', 'An adverse hospital professional-review action affecting a physician’s clinical privileges for more than 30 days must be reported to the {{c1::National Practitioner Data Bank}}.', 'B — National Practitioner Data Bank is the required destination for a qualifying adverse clinical-privilege action. The other listed entities do not receive this report.'),
  ('2012-39', 'When structured assessment finds no imminent danger and caregivers can reliably supervise, {{c1::sending the child home with the parents}} may be the least restrictive appropriate disposition.', 'A — Send the patient home with the parents is supported by the restored source vignette. The OCR omission has been corrected, and structured safety assessment remains required.'),
  ('2012-40', 'For a child with behavior concerns reported across settings, an early {{c1::teacher report}} helps establish context, pervasiveness, and functional impairment.', 'E — Obtain a teacher report is the best-listed initial step. The restored source vignette corrects the prior OCR omission and shows why cross-setting collateral is needed.'),
  ('2012-41', 'When a child’s symptoms worsen amid parental disagreement about discipline, first {{c1::explore the reasons for the parents’ differences}} before imposing a plan.', 'A — Explore reasons for parental differences before taking sides. This identifies barriers to consistent behavioral management.'),
  ('2012-42', 'A tantrum maintained because it lets a child escape a demand improves when the {{c1::negative reinforcement}} of escape is removed.', 'A — Negative reinforcement of the tantrum has been removed. Calm compliance, rather than tantrumming, is no longer linked to escape from the demand.'),
  ('2012-43', 'Following the death of a spouse, the factor with the strongest protective evidence in bereavement is high-quality {{c1::social support}}.', 'Quality social support is the most consistently protective factor after spousal bereavement. It helps reduce isolation and supports practical adaptation.'),
  ('2012-44', 'Amphetamines exert their primary stimulant effect by {{c1::promoting release of catecholamines}}, especially dopamine and norepinephrine.', 'A — Release of catecholamines is the primary amphetamine mechanism. Cocaine is classically associated with monoamine reuptake blockade.'),
  ('2012-45', 'Dissociative amnesia primarily disrupts {{c1::episodic memory}} for personally experienced, often autobiographical events.', 'Episodic memory is the correct formal category for personally experienced events. Dissociative amnesia often affects autobiographical content while sparing procedural skills.'),
  ('2012-46', 'Among the listed heavy metals, {{c1::manganese}} is specifically associated with globus pallidus and striatal injury producing a parkinsonian syndrome.', 'E — Manganese is the corrected answer. The original official ABCDE response was invalidated as an all-credit key.'),
  ('2012-47', 'The {{c1::MMPI-2}} is an adult personality inventory, whereas {{c2::MMPI-A}} is the adolescent-specific version.', 'E — Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI-2) is the historical closest-listed answer. MMPI-A is the adolescent-specific instrument in current practice.'),
  ('2012-48', 'For an older couple distressed by menopausal changes, desire discrepancy, and sexual misconceptions, the initial intervention should include {{c1::sex education}}.', 'A — Sex education directly addresses misconceptions about aging, menopause, desire, and intimacy. Specialized behavioral or medication interventions may follow if indicated.'),
  ('2012-49', 'Among tricyclic antidepressants, {{c1::desipramine}} has relatively lower anticholinergic burden than tertiary-amine TCAs.', 'D — Desipramine is a secondary-amine TCA with comparatively fewer anticholinergic effects. The listed alternatives are tertiary amines with greater antimuscarinic burden.'),
  ('2012-50', 'A dominant posterior cerebral artery cortical infarct can cause {{c1::alexia without agraphia}} with {{c2::contralateral homonymous hemianopia}}.', 'E — Homonymous hemianopia with alexia without agraphia is the classic dominant PCA cortical syndrome. Writing is preserved despite impaired reading.'),
  ('2012-51', '{{c1::Adjustment disorder with anxiety}} is appropriate for clinically significant anxiety after an identifiable stressor when criteria for another disorder are not met.', 'Adjustment disorder with anxiety is a clinically significant, stressor-linked anxious response. It differs from generalized anxiety disorder by its timing and from acute stress disorder by the absence of a trauma-response syndrome.'),
  ('2012-52', 'For depression, {{c1::multiple bona fide psychotherapies}} have efficacy broadly comparable to {{c2::CBT}} rather than CBT being consistently superior.', 'Other psychotherapies have efficacy comparable to CBT for depression. CBT is effective but not uniquely superior across patients and settings.'),
  ('2012-53', 'In an interdependent view of self, positive self-esteem is strongly tied to {{c1::contributing to the group and maintaining harmonious relationships}}.', 'Positive contributions to the group and harmonious relations reflect an interdependent basis for self-esteem. The alternatives primarily emphasize an independent, self-defined model.'),
  ('2012-54', 'Progressive weakness over days with {{c1::areflexia}} and slowed nerve-conduction velocity suggests an {{c2::acute demyelinating polyneuropathy}}.', 'Acute polyneuropathy best fits diffuse weakness, areflexia, and slowed nerve conduction. This pattern is classically associated with Guillain-Barré syndrome.'),
  ('2012-55', 'Among the listed SSRIs, {{c1::fluvoxamine}} has the shortest elimination half-life.', 'Fluvoxamine has the shortest half-life among these SSRIs. Fluoxetine is the contrasting long-half-life agent.'),
  ('2012-56', 'The principal behavioral treatment for OCD is {{c1::exposure and response prevention}}, in which exposure occurs without performing the usual compulsion.', 'Exposure and response prevention directly targets the obsession-compulsion cycle. It differs from relaxation-based desensitization and thought suppression.'),
  ('2012-57', 'Alcohol-withdrawal symptoms generally peak at approximately {{c1::48 hours}} after cessation of alcohol use.', 'Alcohol withdrawal generally peaks around 48 hours after cessation. The clinical course varies, so severe symptoms require prompt evaluation at any point.'),
  ('2012-58', 'The atypical antipsychotic with consistently superior efficacy in {{c1::treatment-resistant schizophrenia}} is {{c2::clozapine}}.', 'Clozapine is the atypical antipsychotic with the clearest superior efficacy in treatment-resistant schizophrenia. Its serious risks require structured monitoring.'),
  ('2012-59', 'In a capacity evaluation, asking what will happen if treatment is refused assesses the patient’s {{c1::appreciation}} of consequences.', 'The question tests appreciation of the consequences of refusal. Capacity is decision-specific and differs from legal competence.'),
  ('2012-60', 'A therapist relationship that disconfirms maladaptive earlier relational expectations is a {{c1::corrective emotional experience}}.', 'Corrective emotional experience is the best term for a new, healthier therapeutic relational pattern. It is distinct from catharsis and transference neurosis.'),
  ('2012-61', 'Fourth- and fifth-digit paresthesias from cubital tunnel syndrome show {{c1::slowed ulnar nerve conduction across the elbow}}.', 'Slowed ulnar conduction across the elbow explains ulnar-distribution numbness. Median latency prolongation instead suggests carpal tunnel syndrome.'),
  ('2012-62', 'Severe hypertension and autonomic symptoms after a decongestant in an MAOI-treated patient indicate {{c1::MAOI-associated hypertensive crisis}} requiring monitored emergency care.', 'Nifedipine is the inferred historical answer among these options. It is not recommended as unsupervised self-treatment for an MAOI hypertensive emergency.'),
  ('2012-63', 'In CBT for depression, hourly activity logs with mastery and pleasure ratings are {{c1::activity monitoring}}.', 'Activity monitoring records what the patient is already doing and how it feels. Daily scheduling instead involves planning future activities.'),
  ('2012-64', '{{c1::Periodic limb movement disorder}} features stereotyped repetitive leg movements during non-REM sleep, often with {{c2::great-toe extension}}.', 'Periodic limb movement disorder best fits repetitive, nonepileptic leg movements during sleep. Restless legs syndrome is primarily an urge to move while awake at rest.'),
  ('2012-65', 'For adults with depression after a recent suicide attempt, {{c1::suicide-focused cognitive-behavioral therapy}} can help prevent future attempts.', 'Cognitive-behavioral therapy is the keyed treatment with evidence for reducing repeat suicide attempts. It complements comprehensive safety and depression care.'),
  ('2012-66', 'Compared with uncomplicated bereavement, traumatic or complicated grief is marked by {{c1::persistent avoidance of reminders of the death}}.', 'Persistent avoidance of death reminders best distinguishes traumatic or complicated grief from expected bereavement. Sleep and concentration changes can occur in uncomplicated grief.'),
  ('2012-67', 'Obtaining baseline metabolic testing before an atypical antipsychotic is a quality intervention aimed primarily at {{c1::safety}}.', 'Safety is correct because the lipid panel helps prevent or detect treatment-related metabolic harm.'),
  ('2012-68', '{{c1::Anterior spinal artery infarction}} causes acute weakness and pain-temperature loss with relative preservation of {{c2::vibration and proprioception}}.', 'Anterior spinal artery infarct is correct because it affects motor and spinothalamic pathways while relatively sparing dorsal columns.'),
  ('2012-69', 'For depression complicated by intolerable SSRI-associated sexual dysfunction, a low-sexual-side-effect alternative is {{c1::bupropion}}.', 'Bupropion is correct because it has comparatively low sexual adverse-effect burden and is often weight-neutral.'),
  ('2012-70', 'The psychotherapy factor most consistently associated with positive outcome across modalities is the {{c1::therapeutic alliance}}.', 'Therapeutic alliance is correct because collaboration, shared goals, and trust predict outcome across modalities.'),
  ('2012-71', 'A key management strategy for somatoform presentations is {{c1::regular scheduled follow-up with one coordinating primary-care clinician}}.', 'Ensuring regular follow-up is correct because continuity and planned visits reduce fragmented reassurance-seeking care.'),
  ('2012-72', '{{c1::Myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome}} features disabling fatigue with {{c2::postexertional worsening}} and impaired recovery.', 'Chronic fatigue syndrome is correct because fatigue, nonrestorative sleep, cognitive symptoms, and postexertional worsening cluster together.'),
  ('2012-73', 'The traditional pharmacologic treatment for REM sleep behavior disorder is {{c1::clonazepam}}.', 'Clonazepam is correct because it commonly suppresses dream-enactment behaviors in REM sleep behavior disorder.'),
  ('2012-74', 'Carbamazepine can lose effect after several weeks at a fixed dose because of {{c1::autoinduction of its metabolism}}.', 'Autoinduction of the epoxide pathway is correct because carbamazepine progressively increases its own clearance.'),
  ('2012-75', '{{c1::DSM-III}} introduced explicit operational diagnostic criteria and the {{c2::multiaxial system}}.', 'DSM-III is correct because it first paired operationalized diagnostic criteria with multiaxial diagnosis.'),
  ('2012-76', '{{c1::Gamma-hydroxybutyrate (GHB)}} intoxication may cause abrupt CNS depression and {{c2::respiratory compromise}} despite a negative routine toxicology screen.', 'Gamma-hydroxybutyrate (GHB) is correct because it can progress from sociability to labile consciousness and respiratory depression.'),
  ('2012-77', 'For major depressive disorder in youth, the antidepressant with the classic strongest established risk-benefit evidence is {{c1::fluoxetine}}.', 'Fluoxetine is correct because it has the best-established risk-benefit evidence among the listed medications for youth depression.'),
  ('2012-78', 'Piaget’s theory explains developmental changes in how individuals {{c1::learn about and understand the world}}.', 'Piaget’s major contribution was a theory of cognitive development. It focuses on changing forms of reasoning and understanding.'),
  ('2012-79', 'Under operant conditioning, a behavior decreases when an {{c1::incompatible behavior}} is consistently {{c2::positively reinforced}}.', 'Reinforcing an incompatible adaptive behavior can reduce the competing target behavior. This is a differential-reinforcement strategy.'),
  ('2012-80', 'The over-the-counter cough suppressant abused for dissociative effects is {{c1::dextromethorphan}}.', 'Dextromethorphan is the cold-remedy ingredient associated with dissociative misuse. Combination formulations may add substantial toxicity risks.'),
  ('2012-81', 'For severe acute mania, rapid treatment commonly uses an {{c1::antipsychotic}} combined with {{c2::lithium or valproate}}.', 'Combined antipsychotic medication with lithium or valproate is correct because it provides rapid, effective treatment for severe acute mania.'),
  ('2012-82', 'Persistent fear of serious illness despite appropriate medical evaluation and reassurance is {{c1::hypochondriasis}}, now often termed {{c2::illness anxiety disorder}}.', 'Hypochondriasis is correct because bodily symptoms are persistently misinterpreted as evidence of serious disease despite reassurance.'),
  ('2012-83', 'Paroxysmal lancinating pain from the posterior tongue or pharynx to the ear, triggered by swallowing, is {{c1::glossopharyngeal neuralgia}}.', 'Glossopharyngeal neuralgia is correct because posterior pharyngeal pain radiates to the ear and is triggered by swallowing.'),
  ('2012-84', 'When a therapist’s attraction to a patient impairs clinical judgment, the best first step is to {{c1::seek supervision or consultation}}.', 'Seek supervision first when countertransference threatens objectivity. Do not disclose romantic feelings or act on them.'),
  ('2012-85', 'The brain region most consistently linked to the rewarding effects of alcohol is the {{c1::nucleus accumbens}}.', 'The nucleus accumbens is central to mesolimbic reward processing. Alcohol reinforcement is strongly associated with this circuit.'),
  ('2012-86', 'In classical psychodynamic theory, adolescent guilt during autonomy struggles may reflect the {{c1::re-emergence of harsh parental introjects}}.', 'The re-emergence of harsh parental introjects is correct because autonomy struggles can reactivate an internalized punitive parental voice.'),
  ('2012-87', 'In an insanity evaluation, the central psychiatric inquiry concerns the defendant’s {{c1::mental state at the time of the alleged offense}}.', 'B — “What were the voices saying to you at the time of the crime?” directly investigates symptoms during the alleged offense. Insanity is retrospective criminal responsibility, unlike present competency.'),
  ('2012-88', 'Believing that a neutral television broadcast is specifically about oneself is an {{c1::idea of reference}}.', 'E — Ideas of reference is correct because the patient assigns personal significance to a neutral broadcast. This differs from a sensory misperception or thought insertion.'),
  ('2012-89', 'Among common APOE alleles, {{c1::ε2}} is associated with a relatively decreased likelihood of late-onset Alzheimer disease.', 'A — ε2/ε3 is correct because it contains the relatively protective ε2 allele. Genotypes containing ε4 are associated with greater Alzheimer risk.'),
  ('2012-90', 'For acute head trauma with depressed consciousness, the preferred initial brain study is {{c1::noncontrast head CT}}.', 'D — 70-year-old female who fell at home and now has depressed level of consciousness is correct because CT rapidly evaluates traumatic hemorrhage and mass effect. MRI may be more informative later but is less suitable initially.'),
  ('2012-91', 'The {{c1::central nucleus of the amygdala}} chiefly mediates central nervous system responses to fear-provoking stimuli.', 'The central amygdala is a principal organizer of fear outputs. It links threat processing to autonomic and defensive responses.'),
  ('2012-92', '{{c1::Carbohydrate-deficient transferrin (CDT)}} is a relatively specific marker of sustained heavy alcohol consumption.', 'D — Carbohydrate-deficient transferrin (CDT) is correct because it is more specific than MCV or transaminase elevation for sustained heavy alcohol use. It must still be interpreted clinically.'),
  ('2012-93', 'The family intervention with evidence for reducing relapse in schizophrenia is {{c1::family psychoeducation}}.', 'D — Family psychoeducation is correct because it teaches illness management, communication, and relapse prevention. It is distinct from insight-oriented family therapies.'),
  ('2012-94', 'Progressive weakness with grip {{c1::myotonia}} and early cataracts is characteristic of {{c2::myotonic dystrophy}}.', 'E — Myotonic dystrophy is correct because the combination of myotonia, facial or distal weakness, and cataracts is characteristic. Myotonia congenita lacks the same progressive multisystem pattern.'),
  ('2012-95', 'Outpatient groups for schizophrenia commonly emphasize {{c1::social skills development}} through modeling and role-play.', 'C — Social skills development is correct because these groups focus on teachable interpersonal and community-living skills. They are generally rehabilitative rather than insight-oriented.'),
  ('2012-96', 'A sudden regular tachycardia that terminates with a Valsalva maneuver suggests {{c1::paroxysmal supraventricular (atrial) tachycardia}}.', 'E — Paroxysmal atrial tachycardia is correct because the abrupt rapid rhythm improves with a vagal maneuver. Marked tachycardia and hypotension require medical evaluation.'),
  ('2012-97', 'Marked toxicity and an unexpectedly high desipramine level on a low dose suggest {{c1::poor cytochrome P450 metabolism}}.', 'D — Poor metabolism of cytochrome P450 substrates is correct because impaired clearance can produce toxic desipramine concentrations at low doses. The elevated level supports a pharmacokinetic explanation.'),
  ('2012-98', 'In a young child with major speech delay, an early essential evaluation step is {{c1::formal hearing assessment}}.', 'B — “Has your child''s hearing been tested?” is correct because hearing impairment must be assessed early in speech-delay evaluation. It can mimic or coexist with other developmental conditions.'),
  ('2012-99', 'Confusion and lethargy in a patient with marked polydipsia should raise concern for {{c1::hyponatremia}}.', 'Hyponatremia can present with lethargy and confusion. Polydipsia may cause or worsen dilutional hyponatremia.'),
  ('2012-100', 'After appropriate imaging excludes a mass lesion, {{c1::lumbar puncture with opening-pressure measurement}} helps establish idiopathic intracranial hypertension.', 'A — lumbar puncture is correct because it documents elevated opening pressure after imaging has excluded a mass lesion. Papilledema signals possible raised intracranial pressure.')
insert into flashcards (question_id, cloze_text, extra) values
  ('2012-101', 'A potential adverse effect shared by {{c1::naltrexone and disulfiram}} is {{c2::elevated liver enzymes}}.', 'C — Elevated liver enzymes is correct because both medications can cause hepatic enzyme elevation. Flushing is mainly associated with alcohol use while taking disulfiram.'),
  ('2012-102', 'At a population level, the presence of a {{c1::step-parent or unrelated adult caregiver}} is a recognized risk factor for child maltreatment.', 'A — Presence of a step-parent is correct as a population-level risk factor. It must never be treated as evidence that a particular caregiver has abused a child.'),
  ('2012-103', 'In suspected neuroleptic malignant syndrome, {{c1::creatine kinase}} helps assess rhabdomyolysis from severe muscle rigidity.', 'C — Creatine kinase is correct because neuroleptic malignant syndrome can cause rigidity-related rhabdomyolysis and CK elevation. CK is useful for monitoring this complication.'),
  ('2012-104', 'In a preschool child, transient and easily redirected curiosity about adult genitals is usually {{c1::normal age-appropriate sexual interest}}, not evidence of {{c2::sexual abuse}}.', 'Normal preschool-age sexual interest is the correct answer because simple, noncoercive anatomical curiosity is developmentally common. Concerning sexual behavior is more intrusive, persistent, or developmentally advanced.'),
  ('2012-105', 'Ataque de nervios should prompt assessment for comorbid {{c1::major depression, generalized anxiety disorder, and PTSD}}.', 'Major depression, generalized anxiety disorder, and PTSD are the keyed associations. Ataque de nervios is a cultural concept of distress, not a substitute for a full diagnostic assessment.'),
  ('2012-106', 'The neurocognitive function most consistently reduced in normal aging is {{c1::information processing speed}}.', 'Information processing speed commonly declines with age. Slower performance alone does not establish a neurocognitive disorder.'),
  ('2012-107', 'For acute psychosis with tachycardia, hypertension, agitation, and chest pain, a {{c1::urine toxicology screen}} is a key test for suspected {{c2::stimulant-induced psychosis}}.', 'Urine toxicology is correct because the syndrome suggests sympathomimetic or stimulant exposure. Broader medical evaluation may still be necessary based on clinical findings.'),
  ('2012-108', 'A psychiatrist who leaves without arranging appropriate coverage primarily violates {{c1::fidelity}}, the duty to honor professional commitments and continuity of care.', 'Fidelity is correct because the psychiatrist failed to maintain an expected care commitment. The resulting risk of harm is relevant but is not the most specific principle tested.'),
  ('2012-109', 'The combination of recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations, cognitive fluctuations, and parkinsonism suggests {{c1::dementia with Lewy bodies}}.', 'Dementia with Lewy bodies is correct because the hallucinations are vivid and formed, with fluctuating cognition and parkinsonian signs. Early memory may be relatively intact.'),
  ('2012-110', 'Before electroconvulsive therapy, {{c1::lithium}} is commonly held or reduced because it can increase {{c2::postictal confusion or delirium}}.', 'Lithium is the medication classically reduced or stopped before ECT. Its combination with ECT can worsen cognitive and seizure-related adverse effects.'),
  ('2012-111', 'Depersonalization—feeling detached from or unreal to oneself—is a disturbance of {{c1::perception}}.', 'Perception is correct because depersonalization alters the subjective experience of self. It often occurs with preserved insight that the sensation is not literally true.'),
  ('2012-112', 'Discordance for schizophrenia in monozygotic twins supports a {{c1::gene–environment interaction}} or “two-hit” model of pathogenesis.', 'Monozygotic twin discordance shows that genetic risk alone is insufficient for schizophrenia. Environmental and developmental factors interact with vulnerability.'),
  ('2012-113', 'Abrupt repetitive questioning with severe {{c1::anterograde amnesia}}, preserved identity, and recovery within 24 hours is typical of {{c2::transient global amnesia}}.', 'Transient global amnesia is correct because the patient cannot retain new information but otherwise functions coherently and recovers. The episode leaves an amnestic gap.'),
  ('2012-114', 'In psychodynamic psychotherapy, the {{c1::frame}} is the consistent set of treatment boundaries, rules, and practical arrangements.', 'The frame is correct because it refers to the treatment''s stable boundaries and defining rules. A patient''s reaction to those limits may become material for therapy.'),
  ('2012-115', 'REM (active) sleep is first evident {{c1::in utero}} and occupies a relatively large proportion of sleep in early infancy.', 'In utero is correct because fetal active sleep corresponds to REM sleep. REM is therefore present before birth, not first acquired in later infancy.'),
  ('2012-116', 'A {{c1::case-control study}} is especially efficient for investigating a {{c2::rare disease outcome}}.', 'A — incidence of the disease is low. Case-control designs efficiently study uncommon outcomes by selecting cases first and examining prior exposures.'),
  ('2012-117', 'The {{c1::Wisconsin Card Sorting Test}} assesses executive function through {{c2::concept formation and set shifting}}.', 'The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test is correct because it directly measures cognitive flexibility and perseveration. The other tests target visuospatial skill, speed, naming, or memory.'),
  ('2012-118', 'In self psychology, severe narcissistic vulnerability is linked to {{c1::insufficient developmentally appropriate empathy from caregivers}}.', 'A lack of developmentally appropriate empathy is correct within self psychology. The formulation emphasizes disrupted development of a cohesive self and sensitivity to narcissistic injury.'),
  ('2012-119', 'Major depression is classically associated on polysomnography with {{c1::shortened REM latency}}.', 'A — Short REM latency. Depression is associated with REM disinhibition, including earlier onset of REM sleep.'),
  ('2012-120', '{{c1::D-cycloserine}} has been studied to augment exposure-based CBT for anxiety by facilitating {{c2::extinction learning}}.', 'D-cycloserine is correct because it can facilitate extinction-learning mechanisms during CBT exposure work. It is not a replacement for a well-conducted psychotherapy intervention.'),
  ('2012-121', 'Before initiating {{c1::lithium}}, obtain a baseline {{c2::thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)}} level.', 'Thyroid-stimulating hormone is correct because lithium can cause or worsen hypothyroidism. Baseline measurement supports safe longitudinal monitoring.'),
  ('2012-122', 'New mood and cognitive symptoms accompanying a systemic lupus flare should raise concern for {{c1::disease-induced cerebritis, or neuropsychiatric lupus}}, after alternative causes are assessed.', 'E — Disease-induced cerebritis is the best-listed answer. The systemic inflammatory findings imply lupus activity beyond isolated skin-limited discoid disease.'),
  ('2012-123', 'A factor associated with better adaptation to bereavement is {{c1::finding meaning in the loss}}.', 'Finding meaning in the loss is correct because it can help integrate bereavement into an ongoing life narrative. It does not require forced positivity or a fixed timetable for grief.'),
  ('2012-124', 'APA psychiatric ethics prohibit sexual relationships with {{c1::current or former patients}}.', 'E — not permissible to have a sexual relationship with a current or former patient. Prior treatment can create persistent power and transference dynamics.'),
  ('2012-125', 'In acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy, {{c1::axonal involvement}} predicts a poorer prognosis than isolated demyelination.', 'C — Proximal axon. Axonal injury is harder to reverse than myelin injury and is associated with slower, less complete recovery.'),
  ('2012-126', 'Cannabis can impair {{c1::short-term memory}} because CB1 receptors are abundant in the {{c2::hippocampus}}.', 'E — short-term memory. Dense hippocampal CB1 receptors link cannabis intoxication to impaired encoding of new information.'),
  ('2012-127', '{{c1::Visual evoked potentials}} may help assess suspected psychogenic blindness by demonstrating intact visual-pathway conduction.', 'C — Psychogenic blindness. Visual evoked potentials can provide objective evidence of preserved visual sensory pathway function.'),
  ('2012-128', '{{c1::Therapeutic privilege}} is the narrow doctrine allowing limited withholding of information when disclosure is expected to seriously harm the patient.', 'A — Therapeutic privilege. It concerns clinician withholding of specific information for anticipated serious harm, not a patient''s voluntary waiver.'),
  ('2012-129', 'Appearance-related surgery is most likely to improve quality of life when a patient has {{c1::a noticeable defect and realistic expectations}} rather than dysmorphic preoccupation.', 'A — An improvement in quality of life. The concern is proportionate to a visible, trauma-related asymmetry and the patient has realistic goals.'),
  ('2012-130', 'The MMSE may miss subtle impairment in highly educated patients because of a {{c1::ceiling effect}}.', 'B — has a ceiling effect for well-educated persons. High-functioning patients may score normally despite meaningful decline from their baseline.'),
  ('2012-131', '{{c1::Progressive supranuclear palsy}} is suggested by early falls, axial rigidity, and {{c2::vertical gaze palsy}}.', 'D — Progressive supranuclear palsy. Vertical gaze difficulty and early falls are hallmark clues in an axial-predominant parkinsonian syndrome.'),
  ('2012-132', '{{c1::Clomipramine}} is the tricyclic antidepressant classically used for long-term treatment and relapse prevention of OCD.', 'B — Clomipramine. Its strong serotonergic action makes it the classic TCA for obsessive-compulsive disorder.'),
  ('2012-133', 'Supportive psychotherapy classically {{c1::supports and accepts defenses}} rather than interpreting them intensively.', 'C — defense mechanisms. Supportive therapy protects functioning by reinforcing coping before pursuing deeper interpretation.'),
  ('2012-134', 'In current PCP intoxication care, {{c1::parenteral benzodiazepines}} are generally preferred for severe agitation, hypertension, or seizures, although IM haloperidol was the historical best-listed option here.', 'Administer haloperidol Smg IM is the preserved historical keyed answer, with “Smg” reflecting an apparent source or OCR issue. Current practice generally prefers parenteral benzodiazepines for PCP-related agitation.'),
  ('2012-135', 'A young child''s belief that an angry thought caused an unrelated accident is {{c1::age-appropriate egocentric (magical) thinking}}.', 'A — Age-appropriate egocentric thinking. Preschool-age children may overestimate their thoughts'' power to cause outside events.'),
  ('2012-136', 'Family systems approaches emphasize that healthy families share {{c1::functional attributes}} rather than one required structure.', 'D — Functioning families tend to have certain shared characteristic attributes rather than common structures. Family assessment should focus on roles, boundaries, communication, and adaptability.'),
  ('2012-137', 'In child psychiatric assessment, {{c1::parent–teacher rating discrepancies are common}} and require contextual evaluation.', 'E — Rating discrepancies by different observers are common in clinical practice. Different settings and observers capture different aspects of a child''s functioning.'),
  ('2012-138', 'A qualifying trauma syndrome occurring {{c1::within the first month after trauma}} is {{c2::acute stress disorder}} rather than PTSD.', 'B — Acute stress disorder. The trauma-linked symptoms began one week after the assault, before the duration required for PTSD.'),
  ('2012-139', 'Horner syndrome causes ipsilateral {{c1::miosis and mild ptosis}} from disruption of the {{c2::sympathetic}} pathway.', 'Horner syndrome produces ipsilateral miosis, mild ptosis, and often facial anhidrosis. The findings are especially localizing after neck trauma.'),
  ('2012-140', 'Aripiprazole is distinguished from most antipsychotics by {{c1::partial agonism at dopamine D2 receptors}}.', 'Aripiprazole is a D2 partial agonist, not a pure D2 antagonist. Its serotonergic actions are important but are not the distinguishing feature tested here.'),
  ('2012-141', 'Mefloquine can rarely cause {{c1::neuropsychiatric adverse effects, including agitation and psychosis}}.', 'Mefloquine can produce anxiety, agitation, and rarely psychosis. The timing after exposure favors an adverse drug effect over spontaneous relapse.'),
  ('2012-142', 'For secondary prevention after an embolic stroke caused by atrial fibrillation, the historical preferred treatment is {{c1::oral anticoagulation with warfarin}} rather than antiplatelet therapy alone.', 'Warfarin addresses cardioembolic thrombus risk from atrial fibrillation. Aspirin-based strategies are less effective for this mechanism.'),
  ('2012-143', 'Both cognitive-behavioral therapy and interpersonal psychotherapy have a {{c1::here-and-now focus}}.', 'CBT and IPT are present-focused, structured treatments. They do not principally rely on transference interpretation or childhood-conflict exploration.'),
  ('2012-144', '{{c1::Sleep paralysis}} is a classic REM-intrusion symptom associated with narcolepsy.', 'B — Sleep paralysis. Narcolepsy commonly includes REM-related atonia persisting briefly into wakefulness.'),
  ('2012-145', 'Before a third-party psychiatric evaluation, the psychiatrist should explain {{c1::the evaluation’s purpose and limits of confidentiality}}.', 'The evaluator must clarify that information may be reported to the requesting program. This is distinct from a routine confidential treatment relationship.'),
  ('2012-146', 'In preadolescence, {{c1::girls’ growth spurt occurs earlier than boys’}}, so boys’ growth rate initially lags behind girls’.', 'Girls generally enter the preadolescent growth acceleration earlier than boys. Growth across body regions is not simultaneous.'),
  ('2012-147', 'As addiction becomes compulsive, substance use is increasingly maintained by {{c1::negative reinforcement}}—relief of withdrawal or dysphoria.', 'Negative reinforcement means using the substance to reduce an aversive state. It becomes increasingly important as addiction shifts from impulsive to compulsive use.'),
  ('2012-148', 'After head trauma, a {{c1::lucid interval followed by rapid neurologic deterioration}} is classic for {{c2::epidural hemorrhage}}.', 'Epidural hemorrhage often follows arterial injury and can expand quickly. CT classically shows a biconvex, lens-shaped extra-axial collection.'),
  ('2012-149', 'In couples therapy, the primary focus is the {{c1::relationship problem and interactional pattern}}, not one partner alone.', 'Couples therapy centers the relationship as the patient. Relevant background issues are explored only as they affect the current relational problem.'),
  ('2012-150', 'Normal adult EEG alpha rhythm is {{c1::posterior dominant and attenuates with eye opening}}.', 'Alpha rhythm is a normal posterior rhythm of relaxed wakefulness. It is reactive rather than persistent and diffuse.'),
  ('2012-151', 'Brief stereotyped episodes of impaired awareness with oral automatisms and post-event amnesia are {{c1::focal impaired-awareness seizures (complex partial seizures)}}.', 'Complex partial seizures cause brief impaired-awareness spells with automatisms and amnesia. A normal MRI does not rule them out.'),
  ('2012-152', 'A behavior performed to symbolically cancel or neutralize an unacceptable thought is the defense mechanism of {{c1::undoing}}.', 'Undoing attempts to cancel an unacceptable thought or feared consequence. Excessive checking can function as a neutralizing act.'),
  ('2012-153', 'The {{c1::Beck Depression Inventory (BDI)}} is a {{c2::self-report}} measure of depressive symptoms.', 'The Beck Depression Inventory is patient-completed. HAM-D, BPRS, PANSS, and CGI are clinician-rated measures.'),
  ('2012-154', 'A study’s {{c1::external validity}} is most threatened when its sample is {{c2::not representative of the target population}}.', 'B — A study population that is not representative of the population to be treated. This limits whether results can be generalized to patients who would receive treatment.'),
  ('2012-155', 'Wilson disease is supported by {{c1::low serum ceruloplasmin}} in a patient with hepatic, neurologic, and Kayser-Fleischer findings.', 'Ceruloplasmin is the correct blood test for suspected Wilson disease. The corneal rings reflect copper deposition.'),
  ('2012-156', 'For body dysmorphic disorder with repetitive appearance-focused behaviors, a first-line medication is an {{c1::SSRI such as fluvoxamine}}.', 'Fluvoxamine is an SSRI and a first-line pharmacologic option for BDD. It targets obsessive preoccupation and repetitive behaviors.'),
  ('2012-157', 'Alcohol’s anxiolytic, sedative-hypnotic, and motor-impairing effects are mediated chiefly by {{c1::enhanced GABA-A receptor signaling}}.', 'E — GABA-A. Enhanced GABA-A inhibition accounts for alcohol’s sedative and motor-impairing effects.'),
  ('2012-158', 'In the stages of change model, recognizing a problem and making a concrete plan before implementation is the {{c1::preparation}} stage.', 'Preparation is active planning for change. The action stage begins when the new behavior is actually implemented.'),
  ('2012-159', 'The principal feature distinguishing hypochondriasis from somatic-type delusional disorder is {{c1::insight into the possibility that the belief is inaccurate}}.', 'Insight distinguishes these conditions. Somatic delusions are fixed; hypochondriacal concerns generally allow some doubt.'),
  ('2012-160', 'For a child with both ADHD and Tourette syndrome, an {{c1::alpha-2 adrenergic agonist}} can improve ADHD symptoms and tics.', 'Alpha-adrenergic agonists can treat both ADHD symptoms and tics. They are a strong next medication trial in this comorbidity.'),
  ('2012-161', 'Working memory assessment must measure immediate storage plus {{c1::attention}}.', 'Attention is required to encode and sustain material in working memory. Immediate recall without attention is not a complete working-memory measure.'),
  ('2012-162', 'In classical conditioning, the previously neutral cue that acquires the ability to elicit a learned response is the {{c1::conditioned stimulus}}.', 'The rat is the conditioned stimulus. It was neutral before pairing with the frightening noise and later elicited fear alone.'),
  ('2012-163', 'Competence to stand trial requires {{c1::present factual and rational understanding of the proceedings}} and the ability to assist counsel.', 'Understanding the charge is central to competency. Competency is a present functional legal assessment, not a determination of insanity.'),
  ('2012-164', 'In Parkinson disease, {{c1::pramipexole}} is classically associated with impulse-control disorders.', 'Pramipexole is a dopamine agonist. Dopamine agonists can cause gambling, hypersexuality, compulsive spending, and related behaviors.'),
  ('2012-165', 'Buprenorphine is a {{c1::partial mu-opioid agonist}} with {{c2::kappa-opioid antagonist}} activity.', 'Buprenorphine is a mixed opioid agonist/antagonist. Its partial mu agonism and high affinity are central to its clinical use.'),
  ('2012-166', 'After violent death of a loved one, early grief may be similar to other bereavement, but {{c1::dysphoria may persist longer}}.', 'Violent loss can prolong dysphoria and complicate adaptation. Early grief reactions can still resemble ordinary bereavement.'),
  ('2012-167', 'Chronic heavy alcohol use commonly causes {{c1::hypomagnesemia}} and {{c2::hypophosphatemia}}.', 'Hypomagnesemia and hypophosphatemia are common in chronic heavy alcohol use. Both should be considered during medical assessment and repletion planning.'),
  ('2012-168', 'A catatonic feature in mood disorders can be {{c1::excessive purposeless motor activity uninfluenced by external stimuli}}.', 'Excessive purposeless motor activity can be a catatonic feature. Catatonia is not restricted to mutism or immobility.'),
  ('2012-169', 'A first focal-onset seizure in an older adult is best evaluated for a structural cause with {{c1::brain MRI}}.', 'Brain MRI best detects a structural cause of late-onset focal seizure. EEG complements imaging but primarily assesses electrical activity.'),
  ('2012-170', 'Deliberately visualizing a feared situation as treatment is {{c1::imaginal exposure}}.', 'Imaginal exposure uses mental imagery of the feared cue. In vivo exposure requires real-world contact with that cue.'),
  ('2012-171', 'Mild intellectual disability often becomes most apparent during {{c1::school age}}, when academic and adaptive demands increase.', 'School-age demands often reveal mild intellectual disability. Diagnosis requires adaptive-functioning deficits as well as low intellectual functioning.'),
  ('2012-172', 'Alcoholics Anonymous was founded by Dr. Bob Smith and {{c1::Bill Wilson (Bill W.)}}.', 'Bill Wilson is the cofounder of Alcoholics Anonymous with Dr. Bob Smith. Ebby T. and Dr. Silkworth were influential but were not AA cofounders.'),
  ('2012-173', 'In family systems theory, recruiting a third person into conflict between two family members is {{c1::triangulation}}.', 'Triangulation is a three-person conflict-regulation pattern. The child recruits the grandmother into the mother-child dispute.'),
  ('2012-174', 'Easy bruising or {{c1::ecchymoses}} supports suspected {{c2::Cushing syndrome}} because cortisol excess causes skin fragility.', 'Ecchymoses are a classic sign of Cushing syndrome. Glucocorticoid-related skin thinning makes bruising more likely.'),
  ('2012-175', 'Piagetian {{c1::assimilation}} is the integration of new experiences into pre-existing cognitive schemas.', 'Gradually integrating new experiences into existing constructs is assimilation. Accommodation instead changes the schema to fit new information.'),
  ('2012-176', '{{c1::Alprostadil}} treats erectile dysfunction when administered intraurethrally or by intracavernosal injection.', 'Alprostadil is prostaglandin E1 used locally for erectile dysfunction. It can be administered intraurethrally or intracavernosally.'),
  ('2012-177', '{{c1::Trigeminal neuralgia}} causes brief unilateral electric facial pain triggered by light touch, chewing, or tooth brushing.', 'Trigeminal neuralgia produces paroxysmal, touch-triggered facial pain. A normal examination between attacks is common in classic cases.'),
  ('2012-178', 'Erikson''s central psychosocial task of middle childhood is {{c1::industry versus inferiority}}.', 'Industry versus inferiority is the school-age developmental conflict. Mastery of academic, social, and practical tasks supports industry.'),
  ('2012-179', 'During the {{c1::contemplation}} stage, motivational treatment acknowledges ambivalence and evokes the patient''s own reasons to change.', 'Contemplation involves recognized problems but unresolved ambivalence. The intervention is to evoke change talk rather than argue.'),
  ('2012-180', 'In psychodynamic therapy, a safe idealizing boundary crossing may be explored as {{c1::the patient''s longing to identify with the therapist}}.', 'The behavior reflects idealizing identification with the psychiatrist. Maintain boundaries while interpreting its transference meaning.'),
  ('2012-181', 'The {{c1::Halstead-Reitan Battery}} is a classic comprehensive battery for detecting neuropsychological impairment.', 'The Halstead-Reitan Battery is designed for neuropsychological assessment. The WAIS measures intelligence but is less comprehensive for brain impairment.'),
  ('2012-182', 'Respiratory illness is a particularly relevant risk factor for {{c1::panic disorder}} because breathlessness can trigger fear of bodily sensations.', 'Panic disorder is most closely linked to respiratory illness among these options. Dyspnea can serve as an interoceptive panic cue.'),
  ('2012-183', '{{c1::Physostigmine}} is the acetylcholinesterase inhibitor used to reverse severe anticholinergic toxicity.', 'Physostigmine reverses anticholinergic toxicity by increasing acetylcholine. It is used selectively with monitoring for severe anticholinergic delirium.'),
  ('2012-184', 'The correctional necessaries doctrine requires care because incarcerated people are {{c1::prevented from seeking their own necessary medical care}}.', 'C — Treatment must be provided to those who are prevented from seeking their own medical care. Custody creates the corresponding institutional duty.'),
  ('2012-185', 'Historically, {{c1::methylphenidate}} could help early HIV-associated cognitive slowing but may be poorly tolerated later in disease.', 'Methylphenidate is the classic historical medication association in this item. Its stimulant adverse effects may become problematic in advanced illness.'),
  ('2012-186', 'Bilateral hippocampal and parahippocampal injury most typically causes {{c1::anterograde amnesia}}.', 'Amnesia is the intended keyed answer. Bilateral medial temporal injury especially disrupts formation of new declarative memories.'),
  ('2012-187', '{{c1::Head injuries}}, including abusive head trauma, are the leading cause of child maltreatment fatalities in this board-style framework.', 'C — Head injuries. Severe inflicted cranial injury is a leading fatal mechanism of child maltreatment.'),
  ('2012-188', 'A young child''s emphatic use of “mine” can demonstrate {{c1::awareness of being a separate self}} from the caregiver.', 'Awareness of being a separate self is shown by the child''s contrasting claim of “my” versus “mommy''s.” This is not primarily object permanence.'),
  ('2012-189', 'Untreated withdrawal from the short-acting opioid heroin typically peaks at about {{c1::36–72 hours}} after the last dose.', 'C — 36 hours. This is the best listed approximation for the early peak of heroin withdrawal.'),
  ('2012-190', 'In hypnosis, intense focus on a central experience with reduced awareness of surrounding distractions is called {{c1::absorption}}.', 'Absorption is correct because the patient is intensely focused and less aware of competing stimuli. Suggestibility concerns responses to suggestions, whereas dissociation concerns separation of mental processes.'),
  ('2012-191', 'After an adequate but no longer effective methylphenidate trial for ADHD, the next stimulant-class trial is often {{c1::mixed amphetamine salts}}.', 'Mixed amphetamine salts are correct because they represent the alternate first-line stimulant class. Atomoxetine is useful in other circumstances but is not the best next choice here.'),
  ('2012-192', 'In a malnourished patient with arrhythmia shortly after nutritional rehabilitation, check {{c1::phosphate}} for {{c2::refeeding syndrome}}.', 'Phosphate is correct because refeeding can cause severe hypophosphatemia. This depletion can impair cardiac function and contribute to arrhythmias.'),
  ('2012-193', 'The {{c1::Nuremberg Code}} first articulated that voluntary human-subject consent is essential to ethical research.', 'B — Nuremberg Code. It established voluntary consent as a foundational requirement for research involving humans.'),
  ('2012-194', 'Among common clinical risk factors, a {{c1::history of a prior suicide attempt}} carries the highest risk for later suicide death.', 'History of a prior suicide attempt is correct because prior suicidal behavior is the strongest listed predictor. Depression and substance use remain important contributors to overall risk.'),
  ('2012-195', 'The most informative assessment of current family functioning examines {{c1::interaction patterns among family members who reside together}}.', 'Interaction patterns among household members are correct because they directly reflect the current functional system. Biological or legal relatedness alone does not determine day-to-day influence.'),
  ('2012-196', 'In an older adult with acute confusion soon after starting an SSRI, first check {{c1::electrolytes}} for possible {{c2::hyponatremia}}.', 'Electrolytes are correct because SSRIs may cause hyponatremia. Abrupt confusion should be approached as possible delirium rather than assumed dementia progression.'),
  ('2012-197', 'Weight loss, salt craving, nausea, hyperpigmentation, and apathy suggest {{c1::primary adrenocortical insufficiency}}.', 'D — Adrenocortical insufficiency. The salt craving and hyperpigmentation strongly support primary adrenal failure.'),
  ('2012-198', 'The {{c1::Child Behavior Checklist (CBCL)}} is a widely used broad-band behavioral rating scale for school-aged children with age- and sex-based norms.', 'Child Behavior Checklist is correct because it is a broad-band behavioral assessment instrument. Conners scales are more specifically associated with ADHD symptom assessment.'),
  ('2012-199', 'Task-specific writer’s cramp is a focal dystonia commonly treated most effectively with {{c1::botulinum toxin}} injections.', 'Botulinum toxin is correct because writer’s cramp is a focal dystonia. Targeted injections reduce involuntary contraction in the involved muscles.'),
  ('2012-200', 'When a benzodiazepine is necessary in severe hepatic dysfunction, choose {{c1::lorazepam}} because it is cleared mainly by {{c2::glucuronidation}}.', 'Lorazepam is correct because it has less reliance on hepatic oxidation than several alternatives. Its use still requires careful sedation and mental-status monitoring.'),
  ('2012-201', 'Among personality disorders, {{c1::antisocial personality disorder}} is most classically associated with alcoholism.', 'Antisocial personality disorder is correct because it has the strongest listed association with alcohol use disorder. Borderline personality disorder may also be comorbid but is not the best answer here.'),
  ('2012-202', '{{c1::Interpersonal psychotherapy (IPT)}} is a structured therapy focused on current relationships, role transitions, grief, and role disputes rather than transference interpretation.', 'Interpersonal psychotherapy is correct because it actively addresses present relational problems and decision-making. Psychodynamic approaches place greater emphasis on transference and unconscious conflict.'),
  ('2012-203', 'Infants show a preference for {{c1::the human voice and speech}} from {{c2::birth}}.', 'A — Birth. Preference for human voice and speech is evident in newborns.'),
  ('2012-204', 'In the DSM-IV framework, mania attributable to an antidepressant is diagnosed as {{c1::substance-induced mood disorder}}.', 'Substance-induced mood disorder is correct because the manic episode follows and is attributed to SSRI exposure. It does not automatically establish a primary bipolar disorder.'),
  ('2012-205', 'For severe major depression with {{c1::catatonia}} and life-threatening refusal of food or fluids, the treatment of choice is {{c2::ECT}}.', 'Electroconvulsive therapy is correct because it can act rapidly in severe, catatonic, medically compromising depression. Standard antidepressants are generally slower acting.'),
  ('2012-206', 'ADHD diagnosis requires impairment from symptoms in {{c1::two or more settings}}, such as home and school.', 'Attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder is correct because its criteria require cross-setting impairment. A problem limited to one setting warrants evaluation for situational causes.'),
  ('2012-207', 'An acute infarct of the occipital visual cortex is most classically in the {{c1::posterior cerebral artery}} distribution.', 'Posterior cerebral is correct because the PCA supplies the occipital visual cortex. Middle and anterior cerebral strokes have different characteristic patterns.'),
  ('2012-208', 'The first step in an insanity evaluation is to determine {{c1::the jurisdiction’s legal insanity standard}}.', 'E — Determine the legal insanity standard to be used. It defines the precise question the forensic evaluation must answer.'),
  ('2012-209', 'Abrupt cessation of combustible tobacco smoking can raise {{c1::clozapine levels}} by reducing {{c2::CYP1A2 induction}}.', 'Prohibition of smoking can increase clozapine exposure. Loss of tobacco-smoke CYP1A2 induction can make a previously tolerated dose toxic.'),
  ('2012-210', 'Cross-cultural awareness broadens the range of {{c1::normal grief}} and helps prevent pathologizing culturally congruent mourning practices.', 'An increased awareness of cross-cultural grief practices widens normal grief. Symptoms and continuing bonds should be interpreted by context and impact.'),
  ('2012-211', 'Mentalization-based therapy is primarily grounded in {{c1::Bowlby''s attachment theory}}.', 'Bowlby''s attachment theory underlies MBT. Attachment stress can disrupt mentalizing, so therapy builds reflective understanding of self and others.'),
  ('2012-212', 'A frequent feature of cluster A personality disorders is {{c1::social withdrawal}}.', 'Social withdrawal is common in cluster A disorders. It may reflect detachment, suspiciousness, or eccentric interpersonal style rather than overt psychosis.'),
  ('2012-213', 'Initial treatment for {{c1::selective mutism}} is behavioral intervention using {{c2::graded exposure}} to speaking situations.', 'Behavioral treatment is the best initial intervention for selective mutism. It targets anxiety-driven avoidance through gradual, supported speaking practice.'),
  ('2012-214', 'The {{c1::Trail-Making Test}} assesses rapid visual scanning, attention, and cognitive sequencing; Part B also tests {{c2::set shifting}}.', 'The Trail-Making Test best combines attention, visual scanning, and sequencing. Its alternating-sequence component adds executive flexibility.'),
  ('2012-215', '{{c1::Peritraumatic dissociation}} is an acute trauma response associated with increased risk of later PTSD.', 'Dissociation immediately around a traumatic event predicts higher PTSD risk. It supports follow-up but is not itself a diagnosis.'),
  ('2012-216', 'Lithium is often harder to use in older adults because age-related {{c1::reduced renal clearance}} raises toxicity risk.', 'Changes in renal clearance make lithium problematic in older adults. Lithium is renally eliminated and vulnerable to dehydration, illness, and interacting medications.'),
  ('2012-217', 'In schizophrenia family psychoeducation, validating relatives'' strengths and resources is part of the {{c1::engagement}} stage.', 'Engagement is the correct stage. It builds alliance by recognizing family strengths and validating their experience with the illness.'),
  ('2012-218', 'When medically feasible, first-line management of {{c1::corticosteroid-induced mania-like symptoms}} is {{c2::tapering or reducing the corticosteroid}}.', 'Begin tapering prednisone when feasible. Reducing the causative corticosteroid is the preferred initial response to steroid-induced activation.'),
  ('2012-219', '{{c1::Cisplatin}} can cause a predominantly sensory peripheral neuropathy with loss of {{c2::vibration and position sense}}.', 'Cisplatin is the likely causative agent. Its neurotoxicity commonly causes distal sensory symptoms and prominent proprioceptive and vibration deficits.'),
  ('2012-220', 'In DSM-IV-TR, {{c1::normal language acquisition}} distinguished Asperger disorder from autistic disorder.', 'Normal language acquisition is the DSM-IV-TR distinction. Asperger disorder is now subsumed within autism spectrum disorder in DSM-5.'),
  ('2012-221', '{{c1::Separation anxiety disorder}} commonly presents with school refusal, reluctance to sleep alone, and somatic complaints around separation.', 'Separation anxiety best explains the pattern. The child''s somatic distress and avoidance are tied specifically to separation from attachment figures.'),
  ('2012-222', 'Drug interactions raise {{c1::lithium}} levels primarily through {{c2::reduced renal clearance}}.', 'Reduced renal clearance is the mechanism. Lithium is renally eliminated, so antihypertensive-related changes in kidney handling can cause toxicity.'),
  ('2012-223', 'For an eligible patient with acute ischemic stroke and a known recent onset, {{c1::intravenous thrombolysis}} is time-sensitive treatment after {{c2::hemorrhage is excluded}}.', 'Intravenous thrombolytic agents are the keyed treatment in this acute-stroke scenario. The current deficit began recently and CT excludes hemorrhage.'),
  ('2012-224', 'In alcohol withdrawal, shorter half-life benzodiazepines can increase the likelihood of {{c1::withdrawal-related grand mal seizures}} because of {{c2::interdose declines in drug effect}}.', 'Increased likelihood of grand mal seizures is the disadvantage of shorter half-life benzodiazepines. Their shorter duration can permit breakthrough withdrawal between doses.'),
  ('2012-225', 'For depression, CBT directly targets {{c1::current maladaptive beliefs and automatic thoughts}}, such as an assumption of {{c2::personal failure}} after a stressor.', 'Assumptions of personal failure resulting from this crisis are the key CBT target. CBT works with present interpretations and maintaining behaviors.'),
  ('2012-226', 'OCD is classically associated with increased activity in the {{c1::caudate}} within {{c2::cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical circuits}}.', 'Caudate is the classic PET association in OCD. It is part of the striatal circuit implicated in obsessions and compulsions.'),
  ('2012-227', 'A comprehensive child or adolescent psychiatric assessment should always include {{c1::parental or primary-caregiver interviews}}.', 'B — parental interviews. Caregivers provide essential developmental, collateral, and contextual information.'),
  ('2012-228', 'Diarrhea, tremor, and confusion after a dose increase of {{c1::lithium}} suggest {{c2::lithium toxicity}}.', 'Lithium is most likely responsible for this syndrome. Its toxicity commonly combines gastrointestinal symptoms, tremor, and neurologic impairment.'),
  ('2012-229', 'Myasthenia gravis is most commonly associated with serum autoantibodies against {{c1::postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors}}.', 'Acetylcholine receptors are the principal antibody target in myasthenia gravis. The disorder impairs postsynaptic neuromuscular transmission.'),
  ('2012-230', 'In the cognitive model, {{c1::hopelessness}} is especially associated with {{c2::suicide risk}}.', 'Hopelessness is the cognitive construct most strongly associated with suicide risk. It reflects a perceived absence of a viable future.'),
  ('2012-231', 'In brief psychodynamic psychotherapy, {{c1::discrepancy}} is recognition of a mismatch between a patient’s goals and recurrent maladaptive behavior.', 'Discrepancy is the relevant treatment element. The patient recognizes a maladaptive interpersonal pattern that conflicts with desired outcomes.'),
  ('2012-232', 'In psychoanalytic theory, self-directed behavior can represent {{c1::turning anger against the self}} when anger toward an attachment figure feels intolerable.', 'Turning against the self as a defense against intolerable anger is the best psychoanalytic explanation. The child redirects unacceptable anger at attachment figures inward.'),
  ('2012-233', 'Compared with TCAs, SSRIs have lower {{c1::dropout rates due to side effects}} because they are generally {{c2::better tolerated}}.', 'Lower dropout rates due to side effects is the key advantage of SSRIs over TCAs. Their better tolerability often improves continuation.'),
  ('2012-234', 'Reciting the alphabet backward is a bedside test of {{c1::concentration}} because it requires sustained {{c2::attention and mental manipulation}}.', 'Say the letters of the alphabet backward starting with Z tests concentration. It is a backward-sequencing task requiring sustained attention.'),
  ('2012-235', 'Treatment-emergent {{c1::hallucinations}} during stimulant therapy warrant {{c2::immediate discontinuation and reassessment}}.', 'Hallucinations require immediate discontinuation of the stimulant and reassessment. They may signal a serious treatment-emergent psychiatric adverse effect.'),
  ('2012-236', 'Attachment promotes infant survival by {{c1::creating proximity to a protective caregiver}} when the infant is vulnerable.', 'Creating a bond so that the adult will protect the infant from danger is attachment’s survival function. Attachment behavior keeps the infant close to a caregiver during threat.'),
  ('2012-237', 'In this historical exam framing, the most common basis for malpractice claims is {{c1::incorrect treatment}}.', 'Incorrect treatment is the keyed historical answer for the most common malpractice-claim category. Claim patterns can vary across jurisdictions and datasets.'),
  ('2012-238', 'Elevated IgG in both CSF and serum is most consistent with {{c1::sarcoidosis with CNS involvement}}, rather than isolated {{c2::intrathecal IgG synthesis}}.', 'Sarcoidosis best fits elevated IgG in both CSF and serum. The pattern suggests systemic immune activation with neurologic involvement.'),
  ('2012-239', 'A first-line adjunct for antipsychotic-induced {{c1::akathisia}} is {{c2::propranolol}}.', 'Propranolol is the classic adjunctive treatment for antipsychotic-induced akathisia. It targets the distressing inner restlessness and pacing.'),
  ('2012-240', 'In a malnourished patient with alcohol dependence and hypoglycemia, give {{c1::thiamine}} before or alongside {{c2::glucose}} when feasible.', 'Thiamine is the correct adjunct in this alcohol-associated hypoglycemia vignette. Urgent glucose treatment should not be delayed if thiamine is not immediately available.'),
  ('2012-241', 'A pervasive pattern of covert resistance, procrastination, and stubborn inefficiency is historically called {{c1::passive-aggressive personality disorder}}.', 'Passive-aggressive personality disorder is the historical label for indirect oppositional behavior. It is not a current standalone DSM personality-disorder diagnosis.'),
  ('2012-242', 'After elective abortion, the strongest predictor of later depression is a {{c1::history of pre-pregnancy depression}}.', 'C — The strongest predictor is a history of pre-pregnancy depression. Assessment should also address current safety, support, and intimate-partner violence.'),
  ('2012-243', 'In Erikson’s late-life stage, acceptance that one’s life has been meaningful reflects {{c1::ego integrity}} rather than despair.', 'Integrity is Erikson’s successful late-life outcome. It involves accepting one’s life as meaningful despite imperfections.'),
  ('2012-244', 'Among many Puerto Rican and other Latin American patients, depression may be described through the idiom of {{c1::nervousness}}.', 'Nervousness is the culturally associated depressive presentation tested here. It should prompt open-ended clarification rather than stereotyping.'),
  ('2012-245', 'Acute unilateral weakness of the forehead and lower face with hyperacusis is most consistent with {{c1::Bell''s palsy}}.', 'Bell’s palsy is a peripheral CN VII palsy. Forehead involvement and hyperacusis support this localization.'),
  ('2012-246', 'In bipolar disorder, a {{c1::mixed manic/depressed episode}} is a classic predictor of relatively poorer lithium response.', 'Mixed manic-depressed presentations are less predictably lithium-responsive than classic episodic mania. They also require careful acute risk assessment.'),
  ('2012-247', 'Generalized anxiety disorder commonly includes {{c1::feeling keyed up or restless}} and {{c2::easy fatigability}} with excessive difficult-to-control worry.', 'Being easily fatigued and feeling keyed up are characteristic GAD symptoms. Panic-like fears of dying or going crazy are not defining associated symptoms.'),
  ('2012-248', 'The {{c1::entorhinal cortex}} is a characteristic site of early neuronal loss in Alzheimer disease.', 'The entorhinal cortex is central to the medial temporal memory network. Early involvement helps explain episodic-memory impairment in Alzheimer disease.'),
  ('2012-249', 'The historical term {{c1::simple schizophrenia}} denotes a schizophrenia presentation diagnosed chiefly from progressive deficit or negative symptoms.', 'Simple schizophrenia is a non-DSM historical subtype. It emphasizes negative symptoms rather than prominent positive psychotic symptoms.'),
  ('2012-250', 'For opioid dependence during pregnancy, the classic maintenance treatment is {{c1::methadone maintenance}} rather than planned detoxification.', 'Methadone maintenance is the keyed treatment for this pregnancy vignette. The key principle is avoiding withdrawal-and-relapse cycles.'),
  ('2012-251', 'Lithium is effectively dialyzed because it is a {{c1::small, water-soluble, minimally protein-bound ion}}, although the keyed option notes that it has no metabolite.', 'Has no metabolite is the only compatible listed answer because lithium is excreted unchanged. Its size, water solubility, minimal protein binding, and distribution more directly determine dialyzability.'),
  ('2012-252', 'When an adolescent presents with chronic disruptive behavior and acute aggression, the initial psychiatric priority is to assess for {{c1::additional psychopathology and comorbidity}}.', 'Evaluate aggression within a broad diagnostic and risk framework. Comorbid conditions commonly affect formulation and immediate management.'),
  ('2012-253', 'In {{c1::tricyclic antidepressant overdose}}, an immediate {{c2::EKG}} is essential to assess conduction toxicity.', 'Electrocardiogram (EKG) assessment is essential in TCA overdose. QRS widening can signal life-threatening sodium-channel blockade.'),
  ('2012-254', 'An acute fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness with altered consciousness is {{c1::delirium}}.', 'Delirium is defined by acute onset and fluctuation in attention and awareness. Search promptly for an underlying medical or medication-related cause.'),
  ('2012-255', 'A central goal of the New Freedom Commission on Mental Health was to address {{c1::mental health as vigorously as physical health}}.', 'Addressing mental health as vigorously as physical health is the correct policy goal. The commission promoted parity and integration rather than separation from general medicine.'),
  ('2012-256', 'Metachromatic leukodystrophy is most commonly caused by deficiency of {{c1::arylsulfatase A}}, resulting in sulfatide accumulation and demyelination.', 'Aryl sulfatase deficiency is the enzyme association tested for metachromatic leukodystrophy. Galactocerebrosidase deficiency instead causes Krabbe disease.'),
  ('2012-257', '{{c1::Seasonal affective disorder}} is the depressive condition most classically treated with {{c2::bright-light therapy}}.', 'Seasonal affective disorder is the classic indication for bright-light therapy. Other depressive conditions may sometimes use light treatment, but the association is strongest here.'),
  ('2012-258', 'A major depressive episode with fixed guilt- or illness-themed delusions is {{c1::major depression with psychotic features}}.', 'Major depression with psychosis best explains the depressive syndrome plus a mood-congruent somatic delusion. Hypochondriasis does not require delusional conviction.'),
  ('2012-259', 'Recurrent binge eating with loss of control but without recurrent compensatory behaviors defines {{c1::binge-eating disorder}}.', 'Binge-eating disorder involves recurrent binge episodes without compensatory behaviors. Bulimia nervosa requires recurrent compensatory behavior.'),
  ('2012-260', 'For persistent posttraumatic stress disorder symptoms, {{c1::sertraline}} is a first-line pharmacologic option.', 'Sertraline is an SSRI with an established role in PTSD treatment. Clonazepam is not a preferred long-term treatment for core PTSD symptoms.'),
  ('2012-261', 'Myasthenia gravis classically shows a {{c1::decremental compound muscle action potential}} with repetitive nerve stimulation.', 'Decreased amplitude with repetitive stimulation is the classic myasthenia gravis finding. It reflects impaired postsynaptic neuromuscular transmission.'),
  ('2012-262', 'When antipsychotic-induced parkinsonism develops after clinical improvement, the initial intervention is usually to {{c1::lower the antipsychotic dose}}.', 'Lower the antipsychotic dose to reduce dopamine blockade and improve parkinsonism. Dopamine-enhancing treatment can aggravate psychosis.'),
  ('2012-263', 'Childhood absence seizures classically produce {{c1::generalized 3-Hz spike-and-wave activity}} on EEG.', 'Three cycles per second spike-and-wave activity is the classic EEG signature of absence seizures. Hypsarrhythmia instead suggests infantile spasms.'),
  ('2012-264', 'FDA approval of a generic drug requires demonstration of {{c1::bioequivalence}} to the reference product.', 'Bioequivalence is the required comparison for generic approval. Cost, appearance, and inactive ingredients may differ from the reference product.'),
  ('2012-265', '{{c1::MDMA}} intoxication can cause autonomic activation, dehydration or hyperthermia, and elevated CPK from rhabdomyolysis.', 'MDMA can cause mydriasis, hypertension, dehydration, and rhabdomyolysis. GHB more typically causes central nervous system depression.'),
  ('2012-266', '{{c1::Behavioral therapy}} emphasizes the situational antecedents and consequences that maintain a patient’s presenting behavior.', 'Behavioral therapy focuses on observable behavior and reinforcement contingencies. Cognitive therapy instead chiefly targets thoughts and beliefs.'),
  ('2012-267', 'In a young person with psychiatric change and liver disease, low {{c1::ceruloplasmin}} supports Wilson disease but is not independently diagnostic.', 'B — Ceruloplasmin is the best-listed blood clue for Wilson disease. A low level is supportive and must be integrated with other copper studies and clinical findings.'),
  ('2012-268', 'First-line treatment for catatonia is {{c1::lorazepam}}, with {{c2::ECT}} used for severe or refractory cases.', 'Lorazepam is first-line treatment for catatonic signs such as posturing and echopraxia. ECT is crucial for severe or refractory catatonia.'),
  ('2012-269', 'Severe headache with papilledema and markedly raised opening pressure despite a normal noncontrast CT should prompt concern for {{c1::cerebral venous sinus thrombosis}}.', 'Sagittal sinus thrombosis can cause intracranial hypertension with an initially normal noncontrast CT. Pseudotumor cerebri is considered after secondary causes are evaluated.'),
  ('2012-270', 'The major empirical limitation of analytic libidinal-stage theory is reliance on {{c1::idiosyncratic retrospective reconstructions from adults}}.', 'Adult reconstructions of development are difficult to verify consistently. This limits clinical validation of rigid psychosexual-stage claims.'),
  ('2012-271', 'In the learned-helplessness model, behavioral escape deficits are classically reversed by {{c1::antidepressants}}.', 'Antidepressants reverse the classic learned-helplessness behavioral deficit. The model concerns passivity after uncontrollable stress.'),
  ('2012-272', 'Historically, Asperger disorder was most strongly associated with a {{c1::nonverbal learning disability}} profile.', 'Nonverbal learning disability describes verbal strengths with visuospatial and social-perceptual weaknesses. It is a historical association, not a synonym for autism spectrum disorder.'),
  ('2012-273', 'In acculturation, {{c1::integration}} means maintaining one’s heritage culture while participating in the broader culture.', 'Integration reflects engagement with both heritage and broader cultures. Assimilation would involve relinquishing more of the heritage culture.'),
  ('2012-274', 'Conversion disorder is distinguished from factitious disorder and malingering because its symptoms are {{c1::not intentionally produced}}.', 'Symptoms not intentionally produced is correct because conversion symptoms are involuntary. Factitious disorder and malingering require intentional production or falsification.'),
  ('2012-275', 'In an older adult with a stable modest alcohol pattern and no identified alcohol-related harm, the best next step is to {{c1::continue monitoring the unchanged pattern rather than mandate cessation}}.', 'Continue the alcohol as long as the pattern of use does not change is correct in this vignette. Stable use without consequences should still be reassessed for emerging risks.'),
  ('2012-276', 'Among common TCAs, {{c1::nortriptyline}} is often preferred in older adults when minimizing {{c2::orthostatic hypotension}}.', 'Nortriptyline is correct because it is a secondary-amine TCA with comparatively less orthostatic burden. It still carries important geriatric safety risks.'),
  ('2012-277', 'Ipsilateral dysmetria, intention tremor, and dysdiadochokinesia localize to the {{c1::cerebellum}}.', 'Cerebellar damage causes ipsilateral limb incoordination. Intention tremor and dysmetria are classic localizing signs.'),
  ('2012-278', 'In psychotherapy ethics, a {{c1::boundary violation}} is characteristically exploitative, unlike a context-dependent boundary crossing.', 'Boundary violations are exploitative. Boundary crossings can be clinically appropriate depending on their purpose and effect.'),
  ('2012-279', 'A key indication for residential substance-use treatment is {{c1::lack of a supportive, recovery-safe home environment}}.', 'Lack of family support is correct because residential care can provide the structured recovery setting absent at home. Other complexities may matter but are less specific.'),
  ('2012-280', '{{c1::Hypothalamic hamartoma}} classically causes {{c2::gelastic seizures with precocious puberty}}.', 'Hypothalamic hamartoma is correct because it links emotion-incongruent laughter spells with central precocious puberty. This pairing is highly characteristic.'),
  ('2012-281', 'Compared with unilateral ECT, {{c1::bilateral electrode placement}} is associated with greater {{c2::retrograde amnesia}}.', 'Bilateral electrode placement is correct because it has a greater cognitive adverse-effect burden than unilateral ECT. Retrograde amnesia is the tested effect.'),
  ('2012-282', 'Occasional private sexual fantasies without harmful behavior or impairment can be {{c1::a normal part of sexual development}}.', 'Part of normal sexual development is the correct answer. Fantasy content alone does not establish trauma, orientation, or a paraphilic disorder.'),
  ('2012-283', 'Twelve-step facilitation therapy promotes recovery by {{c1::encouraging active participation in Twelve-Step meetings and supports}}.', 'You did the most important thing, you went to the meeting is correct because it reinforces attendance at AA. That is the defining behavioral focus of twelve-step facilitation.'),
  ('2012-284', 'In older terminology, acute daytime impairment from marked sleep curtailment can be classified as {{c1::dyssomnia NOS}} rather than ADHD.', 'Dyssomnia NOS is correct because the symptoms follow behaviorally restricted sleep. The vignette does not establish chronic ADHD or primary hypersomnia.'),
  ('2012-285', 'Post-lumbar-puncture headache is classically {{c1::worse when sitting or standing}} and relieved by lying down.', 'Headache worse with sitting upright is correct because post-dural-puncture CSF leak causes an orthostatic headache. Fever or autonomic symptoms suggest alternatives.'),
  ('2012-286', '{{c1::Williams syndrome}} is caused by a {{c2::7q11.23 microdeletion}} and often features hypersociability with visuospatial weakness.', 'Microdeletion on chromosome 7q11.23 is correct because it causes Williams syndrome. Its characteristic profile includes visuospatial deficits, anxiety, and hypersociability.'),
  ('2012-287', 'The ability to shift between general principles and specific examples is {{c1::abstract reasoning}}.', 'Abstract reasoning links concepts to examples. It is assessed with tasks such as similarities and proverb interpretation.'),
  ('2012-288', 'On psychiatric inpatient units, {{c1::change of shift}} is a situational period associated with increased assault risk.', 'Change of shift is correct because handoffs can disrupt communication and supervision. Structured transition practices reduce this predictable environmental risk.'),
  ('2012-289', 'Routinely explaining confidentiality limits is an example of {{c1::building ethical safeguards into clinical work}}.', 'Building ethical safeguards into work is correct because routine disclosure anticipates foreseeable confidentiality issues. It is a preventive ethical practice.'),
  ('2012-290', 'For essential tremor in a patient with asthma, {{c1::primidone}} is preferred over {{c2::propranolol}}.', 'Primidone is correct because asthma makes nonselective propranolol less appropriate. Both are first-line options for essential tremor in other contexts.'),
  ('2012-291', 'A {{c1::writ of habeas corpus}} seeks prompt judicial review of whether a person’s detention is lawful.', 'Writ of habeas corpus is correct because it challenges the legality of confinement. Commitment and capacity hearings address different legal questions.'),
  ('2012-292', 'For irritable bowel syndrome, {{c1::gut-directed hypnosis}} is a psychological treatment with demonstrated efficacy in controlled trials.', 'Hypnosis is correct because gut-directed hypnotherapy has evidence for IBS symptom improvement. It is distinct from surgery, opioids, or nonspecific supportive therapy.'),
  ('2012-293', '{{c1::Last observation carried forward}} imputes missing follow-up data using a participant''s most recent measured outcome.', 'Last observation carried forward addresses missing outcome data after dropout. It is a historical imputation method, not a guarantee against bias.'),
  ('2012-294', 'Hypermotor seizures with asymmetric tonic “fencing” postures are classically associated with {{c1::frontal lobe seizures}}.', 'Frontal lobe seizures can cause dramatic hypermotor activity and tonic posturing. Their brief, stereotyped, often nocturnal pattern helps distinguish them from many mimics.'),
  ('2012-295', '{{c1::Rizatriptan}} is a triptan used for acute abortive treatment of migraine.', 'Rizatriptan aborts an acute migraine attack. Topiramate is generally used for migraine prevention.'),
  ('2012-296', 'Because of a {{c1::ceiling effect}}, the MMSE may fail to detect {{c2::very mild cognitive impairment}}.', 'May fail to detect very mild cognitive impairment is the key limitation. The MMSE is a screen, not a definitive exclusion test for early cognitive decline.'),
  ('2012-297', 'Among major CNS neurotransmitter systems, {{c1::dopaminergic}} circuitry is classically considered the last to mature during childhood and adolescence.', 'Dopaminergic systems, particularly prefrontal circuits, mature relatively late. This is linked conceptually to adolescent reward processing and executive control.'),
  ('2012-298', '{{c1::Bilateral vestibular schwannomas}} with hearing loss are characteristic of {{c2::neurofibromatosis type 2 (NF2)}}.', 'NF-2 mutation is associated with bilateral vestibular schwannomas. Hearing loss and gait imbalance reflect vestibulocochlear involvement.'),
  ('2012-299', 'A DBT adaptation for older adults may add {{c1::emotional constriction}} as a treatment target beyond the standard skills modules.', 'Emotional constriction is the distinctive added target in geriatric DBT adaptations. Mindfulness, distress tolerance, and interpersonal effectiveness are standard DBT modules.'),
  ('2012-300', 'Adoptees whose biological parent died by suicide have an elevated risk of {{c1::completed suicide}} relative to other adoptees.', 'The child''s risk of completed suicide is elevated relative to other adoptees. This is a population-level familial risk, not an individual prediction.'),
  ('2013-1', 'Intentional symptom production for {{c1::external incentive}}, such as financial gain, is {{c2::malingering}}.', 'Financial incentive supports malingering because the gain is external. Factitious disorder is driven primarily by the internal sick role.'),
  ('2013-2', 'The most effective treatment for spasmodic torticollis, or cervical dystonia, is {{c1::botulinum toxin injection}}.', 'Botulinum toxin is first-line focal therapy for cervical dystonia. It targets the muscles producing the abnormal neck posture.'),
  ('2013-3', 'The defense mechanism of internalizing qualities of another person or object is {{c1::introjection}}.', 'Introjection brings external qualities into the self. It differs from displacement, rationalization, and reaction formation.'),
  ('2013-4', 'In preschool children, age-congruent, noncoercive, easily redirected sexual curiosity is usually {{c1::normal sexual development}}.', 'Normal sexual development is the best explanation for the isolated behavior described. Assessment should still screen for coercion, distress, and developmentally unusual features.'),
  ('2013-5', 'For recurrent suicidal behavior in schizophrenia, the antipsychotic with the strongest evidence is {{c1::clozapine}}.', 'Clozapine is the key medication for recurrent suicidal behavior in schizophrenia. It requires careful medical monitoring and comprehensive suicide-risk management.'),
  ('2013-6', 'Confusion, ataxia, and ophthalmoplegia or nystagmus after malnutrition or bariatric surgery indicate {{c1::thiamine deficiency (Wernicke encephalopathy)}}.', 'Thiamine deficiency causes Wernicke encephalopathy. Bariatric surgery and prolonged vomiting are important nonalcohol-related risk settings.'),
  ('2013-7', 'A belief that current suffering is a consequence of past deeds reflects {{c1::karma}}, classically associated here with {{c2::Buddhism}}.', 'Buddhism is keyed because the vignette invokes karma. In practice, assess each patient’s own interpretation rather than stereotyping religious beliefs.'),
  ('2013-8', 'The initial phase of dual sex therapy uses {{c1::sensate-focus exercises}} to heighten sensory awareness and reduce performance pressure.', 'Exercise to heighten sensory awareness describes sensate focus. Initial work generally de-emphasizes intercourse and orgasm goals.'),
  ('2013-9', 'In a standard EMG biofeedback paradigm, {{c1::muscle tension}} can be operantly conditioned.', 'Muscle tension is a classic biofeedback target. EMG provides immediate feedback that supports relaxation training.'),
  ('2013-10', 'The {{c1::Continuous Performance Test (CPT)}} specifically measures sustained attention, or vigilance.', 'The CPT is the classic test of vigilance. Its results are supportive data, not a stand-alone diagnosis.'),
  ('2013-11', 'High-dose {{c1::dextromethorphan intoxication}} can cause dissociation, hallucinations, paranoia, and tachycardia.', 'Dextromethorphan intoxication can present with dissociation and psychosis-like symptoms. Ask about cough preparations and possible co-ingestants.'),
  ('2013-12', 'Combining {{c1::alcohol}} with alprazolam increases the risk of {{c2::behavioral disinhibition}} and dangerous CNS depression.', 'Alcohol is the key interaction with alprazolam. The combination can cause disinhibition, amnesia, impaired coordination, and respiratory danger.'),
  ('2013-13', 'Among psychological traits, {{c1::hostility}} has the most consistent association with elevated blood pressure.', 'Hostility is the correct answer because it is the specific trait classically linked to hypertension. Broad Type A personality is less consistently associated.'),
  ('2013-14', 'A clinician who declines care beyond current competence to avoid foreseeable patient harm is applying {{c1::nonmaleficence}}.', 'Nonmaleficence is the correct answer because the psychiatrist avoids harm from practicing outside current competence. Beneficence instead emphasizes affirmative provision of benefit.'),
  ('2013-15', 'Marked individual differences in executive functioning first become apparent during {{c1::preschool age}}.', 'Preschool age is the correct answer because inhibition, working memory, and shifting become observable in everyday tasks. These abilities continue to mature through adolescence.'),
  ('2013-16', 'A delayed {{c1::isodense crescent-shaped extra-axial collection}} on CT after head trauma suggests a {{c2::subdural hematoma}}.', 'An isodense crescentic collection is the correct finding because it characterizes a subacute subdural hematoma. The delayed course after trauma supports that diagnosis.'),
  ('2013-17', 'MMPI-2 validity scales primarily assess the patient''s {{c1::test-taking attitude and response style}}.', 'Test-taking attitude is the correct answer because MMPI-2 validity scales evaluate response consistency and presentation style. They do not measure reliability or construct validity.'),
  ('2013-18', 'Insomnia maintained by conditioned bedtime anxiety and effortful attempts to force sleep is {{c1::psychophysiological insomnia}}.', 'Psychophysiological insomnia is the correct answer because the patient has conditioned arousal and rumination at bedtime. The bed has become associated with wakefulness rather than sleep.'),
  ('2013-19', 'For psychophysiological insomnia, stimulus control includes going to bed {{c1::only when sleepy}}.', 'Go to bed only when sleepy is the correct answer because it restores bed as a cue for sleep. Lying awake in bed reinforces conditioned arousal.'),
  ('2013-20', '{{c1::Corticosteroids}} can exacerbate enhanced physiologic tremor.', 'Corticosteroids can worsen physiologic tremor. Beta-adrenergic antagonists more commonly reduce tremor.'),
  ('2013-21', 'Misinterpreting a real external stimulus as something else is an {{c1::illusion}}.', 'An illusion is a distorted perception of a real stimulus. Hearing a name in rustling leaves is therefore an illusion.'),
  ('2013-22', 'In historical intellectual-disability severity descriptions, academic achievement around the sixth-grade level most closely suggests {{c1::mild intellectual disability}}.', 'Mild intellectual disability is the correct historical answer because elementary-level academic attainment fits older severity descriptions. Formal diagnosis also requires adaptive-functioning deficits.'),
  ('2013-23', 'Historically, {{c1::fluoxetine}} had the best-defined breast-milk excretion pharmacokinetic profile among these antidepressants.', 'Fluoxetine is the correct historical answer because its milk-excretion kinetics were most extensively characterized. This does not make it an automatic first choice for every lactating patient.'),
  ('2013-24', 'Among common supportive-expressive interventions, {{c1::clarification}} is the most insight-oriented when interpretation is not offered.', 'Clarification is the correct answer because it organizes and brings awareness to the patient''s experience. Validation and praise are primarily supportive interventions.'),
  ('2013-25', 'Pain radiating to the thumb with biceps weakness and loss of the biceps reflex suggests {{c1::C6 radiculopathy}}.', 'C6 radiculopathy is the correct answer because the thumb distribution, biceps weakness, and biceps-reflex loss localize to C6. Ulnar neuropathy instead favors the fourth and fifth digits.'),
  ('2013-26', 'The rigor of a capacity assessment is most affected by the decision''s {{c1::risk-benefit ratio}}.', 'The risk-benefit ratio is the correct answer because capacity is decision-specific and assessed more carefully when consequences are greater. Diagnosis alone does not determine capacity.'),
  ('2013-27', 'The {{c1::SCL-90-R}} is a broad self-report inventory for general assessment of psychiatric symptom patterns.', 'The SCL-90-R is the correct answer because it surveys multiple psychiatric symptom domains. The other instruments target neuropsychology, anxiety, academic skills, or suicide-protective beliefs.'),
  ('2013-28', 'An {{c1::inhibited}} infant or toddler temperament is associated with increased risk of later childhood anxiety symptoms.', 'Inhibited temperament is the classic early risk marker for childhood anxiety. It indicates vulnerability, not an inevitable disorder.'),
  ('2013-29', 'Dissociative identity disorder is most classically comorbid with {{c1::posttraumatic stress disorder}}.', 'Posttraumatic stress disorder is the correct answer because dissociative identity disorder is strongly associated with trauma-spectrum symptoms and comorbidity. The other options are not the classic association.'),
  ('2013-30', 'A lateral hemispheric infarct with contralateral face-and-arm-predominant deficits is most consistent with the {{c1::middle cerebral artery}} territory.', 'C — Middle cerebral is the visually adjudicated answer. The referenced CT was inspected before the MCA key was retained.'),
  ('2013-31', '{{c1::Vascular dementia}} is suggested by cognitive decline accompanied by focal neurologic deficits such as aphasia or an extensor plantar response.', 'Vascular dementia commonly coexists with focal neurologic signs. Aphasia and an asymmetric Babinski response argue against uncomplicated primary neurodegenerative dementia.'),
  ('2013-32', 'For trichotillomania, the historical medication-focused key is {{c1::fluoxetine}}, although contemporary first-line care generally emphasizes {{c2::habit-reversal-based behavioral therapy}}.', 'C — Begin fluoxetine for at least 12 weeks titrating to a maximal approved level is the historical keyed choice. Its efficacy for core hair pulling is limited and inconsistent.'),
  ('2013-33', 'The {{c1::Wisconsin Card-Sorting Test}} assesses executive functioning through abstract reasoning, feedback use, and cognitive set shifting.', 'The Wisconsin Card-Sorting Test is a classic measure of cognitive flexibility. Perseverative errors indicate difficulty shifting from a no-longer-correct rule.'),
  ('2013-34', 'Biofeedback is a behavioral adjunct that may help {{c1::chronic insomnia}} by reducing physiologic arousal.', 'Chronic insomnia is the keyed condition. Biofeedback can reduce hyperarousal within a broader behavioral sleep-treatment plan.'),
  ('2013-35', '{{c1::Catastrophic thinking}} is the cognitive distortion of predicting an extreme disastrous outcome from a relatively limited setback.', 'Catastrophic thinking turns one adverse event into a cascade of ruinous consequences. It differs from all-or-nothing thinking, which emphasizes rigid extremes.'),
  ('2013-36', 'In PMDD, symptoms are typically minimal or absent during the {{c1::week after menses}}.', 'Week after menses is the keyed low-symptom interval. PMDD symptoms are concentrated in the late luteal phase.'),
  ('2013-37', 'Polymyalgia rheumatica is commonly supported by an elevated {{c1::erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR)}} with usually normal CK.', 'Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is often elevated in polymyalgia rheumatica. Normal strength and generally normal CK help distinguish it from inflammatory myopathy.'),
  ('2013-38', 'In Erikson’s theory, {{c1::generativity}} can be expressed through creative projects that contribute to the community.', 'Being involved in creative community projects is the keyed example of generativity. Generativity extends beyond parenting to mentorship, stewardship, and contribution.'),
  ('2013-39', 'A one-time population screening survey estimates {{c1::point prevalence}}, not incidence.', 'Point prevalence is the proportion with the measured finding at a specified time. Incidence requires follow-up for newly occurring cases.'),
  ('2013-40', 'After renal transplantation, prior successful response makes {{c1::fluoxetine}} defensible for recurrent depression, provided calcineurin-inhibitor interactions are monitored.', 'C — Fluoxetine is the corrected single answer. Prior response supports its selection, but transplant-team monitoring remains necessary.'),
  ('2013-41', 'In a child, hallucinations with disorganized thought, social withdrawal, and functional decline suggest {{c1::early-onset schizophrenia}} rather than ADHD alone.', 'Early-onset schizophrenia presents with psychosis, disorganization, negative symptoms, and deterioration in functioning. ADHD does not explain this full syndrome.'),
  ('2013-42', 'For sleep-onset insomnia in an older patient vulnerable to confusion, {{c1::ramelteon}} is a lower-risk alternative to sedative-hypnotics.', 'Ramelteon is the keyed choice for sleep initiation with fewer cognitive adverse effects. Avoid anticholinergic and unnecessary antipsychotic sedation in dementia.'),
  ('2013-43', 'The intermediate phase of IPT focuses on {{c1::strengthening links between interpersonal events and psychiatric symptoms}} while addressing the focal problem area.', 'Strengthening interpersonal-symptom links is the keyed intermediate-phase task. IPT begins with formulation and consolidates gains at termination.'),
  ('2013-44', 'Among the listed medications, {{c1::bupropion}} is the historical promising option for methamphetamine-use relapse prevention, while psychosocial treatment remains central.', 'Bupropion is the historical keyed promising treatment. It is not a universal or stand-alone solution for methamphetamine relapse prevention.'),
  ('2013-45', '{{c1::Herpes simplex virus type 1}} is the most common sporadic cause of acute viral encephalitis.', 'Herpes simplex virus type 1 is the keyed leading cause of sporadic acute viral encephalitis. Suspected disease warrants urgent medical evaluation and empiric antiviral treatment.'),
  ('2013-46', 'In this exam-era option set, the best-listed analgesic associated with a severe phenelzine interaction is {{c1::oxycodone}}, although meperidine and tramadol are more classic high-risk opioids.', 'B — Oxycodone is the best-listed historical answer. Meperidine and tramadol are more classic high-risk opioids with MAOIs.'),
  ('2013-47', 'In this item, {{c1::confusion with impaired recent memory}} is the feature that favors brief psychotic disorder over schizophrenia.', 'Confusion with impaired recent memory is the keyed differentiating feature. Duration and return to baseline remain the central clinical distinctions.'),
  ('2013-48', 'A {{c1::familial bilateral postural or action tremor}} is most characteristic of {{c2::essential tremor}}.', 'Essential tremor is the keyed diagnosis. Postural worsening and affected relatives support it.'),
  ('2013-49', '{{c1::Bilateral vestibular schwannomas}} are the hallmark finding of {{c2::neurofibromatosis type 2}}.', 'Neurofibromatosis type 2 is the correct diagnosis. Multiple meningiomas and cataracts further support it.'),
  ('2013-50', '{{c1::Escitalopram}} is FDA-approved for major depressive disorder in adolescents.', 'Escitalopram is the keyed FDA-approved first-line option. It should be paired with safety monitoring and appropriate psychotherapy planning.')
insert into flashcards (question_id, cloze_text, extra) values
  ('2013-51', 'Providing take-home-dose privileges contingent on treatment adherence is {{c1::contingency management}}.', 'Contingency management is the correct intervention. It reinforces recovery-supporting behavior with a valued consequence.'),
  ('2013-52', 'For consent to a voluntary psychiatric admission to be valid, the patient''s decision must be {{c1::free of coercion}}.', 'B — Coercion defeats the voluntariness required for informed consent. Commitment must rest on applicable legal criteria, not bargaining pressure.'),
  ('2013-53', 'Infants can form {{c1::multiple attachments}}, and the {{c2::strength}} of each may differ across caregivers.', 'C — Attachment is not limited to one parent or caregiver. Multiple meaningful attachment relationships can coexist.'),
  ('2013-54', 'Gradual memory loss with declining daily function but preserved social etiquette is typical of {{c1::Alzheimer disease}}.', 'Alzheimer dementia is the correct diagnosis. Polished social behavior can remain relatively preserved early in the illness.'),
  ('2013-55', 'Bilateral internuclear ophthalmoplegia in a young adult strongly suggests {{c1::multiple sclerosis}}.', 'Multiple sclerosis is the correct diagnosis. Medial longitudinal fasciculus lesions cause impaired adduction with abducting nystagmus.'),
  ('2013-56', '{{c1::Alexithymia}} is difficulty identifying and describing one’s own emotions.', 'Alexithymia is the correct definition. Emotional experience may be present but poorly recognized or verbalized.'),
  ('2013-57', 'The major threat to validity in a crossover trial is {{c1::carryover (residual) effects}} from an earlier treatment period.', 'B — Residual effects can contaminate later treatment periods. Washout intervals help but may not fully remove carryover.'),
  ('2013-58', '{{c1::Oxcarbazepine}} can reduce the effectiveness of {{c2::oral hormonal contraceptives}} by increasing their metabolism.', 'Oxcarbazepine is the interacting medication. It may lower oral-contraceptive hormone exposure and reduce contraceptive reliability.'),
  ('2013-59', 'Atomoxetine acts primarily by {{c1::inhibiting the norepinephrine transporter}}.', 'Atomoxetine is the correct medication mechanism. It is a selective norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor.'),
  ('2013-60', 'In older adults, the most common brain tumor overall is {{c1::metastatic cancer}}, often from {{c2::lung carcinoma}}.', 'Metastatic lung carcinoma is the keyed best answer. Metastatic disease is more common overall than primary malignant brain tumors.'),
  ('2013-61', 'A pregnant patient stable on {{c1::methadone maintenance}} should generally {{c2::continue maintenance treatment}} rather than abruptly discontinue it.', 'Continue methadone maintenance during pregnancy when the patient is stable. Abrupt withdrawal can destabilize recovery and prenatal care.'),
  ('2013-62', 'Untreated fetal or neonatal {{c1::thyroid hormone deficiency}} can cause severe {{c2::intellectual disability}}.', 'Thyroid hormone is the correct answer. Untreated congenital hypothyroidism can cause severe cognitive impairment.'),
  ('2013-63', 'In correctional settings, substance-use treatment is {{c1::commonly included but not universally mandated}} as a separate legal entitlement.', 'Not mandated but usually included in correctional programs is the correct answer. Clinical need and legal obligations must be considered in context.'),
  ('2013-64', 'Dopamine agonists such as pramipexole can cause {{c1::impulse-control disorders, including pathological gambling}} in patients with Parkinson disease.', 'Use of dopaminergic agents can trigger pathological gambling and other compulsive reward-seeking behaviors.'),
  ('2013-65', 'Therapist self-disclosure is appropriate when it can {{c1::model adaptive behavior or strengthen the therapeutic alliance}} while serving the patient’s goals.', 'Model appropriate behavior and foster the therapeutic alliance are appropriate purposes for limited, patient-centered self-disclosure.'),
  ('2013-66', '{{c1::Progressive supranuclear palsy}} is a tauopathy associated with neurofibrillary tangles; {{c2::dementia with Lewy bodies}} may also show this pathology.', 'Dementia with Lewy bodies and progressive supranuclear palsy are associated with neurofibrillary tangles, with PSP being the classic tauopathy.'),
  ('2013-67', 'A psychotic syndrome with functional decline lasting at least {{c1::6 months}} is consistent with {{c2::schizophrenia}} when other causes are excluded.', 'Schizophrenia best explains the prolonged withdrawal, paranoia, apparent hallucinations, and abnormal behavior.'),
  ('2013-68', 'For serotonin syndrome, initial symptomatic treatment after stopping serotonergic agents includes {{c1::benzodiazepines such as lorazepam}}.', 'Lorazepam reduces agitation and neuromuscular hyperactivity in serotonin syndrome while supportive care is provided.'),
  ('2013-69', 'Timed {{c1::melatonin}} is useful for {{c2::delayed sleep-wake phase disorder}} because it can shift circadian timing earlier.', 'Sleep phase delay is a circadian rhythm disorder for which melatonin agonists can be useful.'),
  ('2013-70', 'Children with {{c1::separation anxiety disorder}} commonly have comorbid {{c2::generalized anxiety disorder}}.', 'Generalized anxiety disorder is the most common comorbid diagnosis listed for childhood separation anxiety disorder.'),
  ('2013-71', 'Resumption of heavy tobacco smoking can cause clozapine relapse risk by {{c1::inducing CYP1A2 and lowering clozapine concentrations}}.', 'E — Tobacco smoke induces CYP1A2 and can lower clozapine levels. Ask about smoking changes when symptoms recur.'),
  ('2013-72', 'Compared with SSRIs, {{c1::bupropion}} may be advantageous in older adults because of {{c2::fewer drug interactions}}.', 'Bupropion may have fewer drug interactions than SSRIs, an important consideration in older adults with polypharmacy.'),
  ('2013-73', 'A highly predictive risk factor for elder abuse is {{c1::caregiver alcohol abuse}}.', 'Caregiver alcohol abuse is a major predictor of elder abuse because risk is often driven by the caregiving environment.'),
  ('2013-74', 'The classic deep brain stimulation target for primary generalized dystonia is the {{c1::internal globus pallidus (GPi)}}.', 'Globus pallidus stimulation, specifically GPi DBS, is useful for primary generalized dystonia.'),
  ('2013-75', '{{c1::Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease}} markedly increases concern for respiratory and anesthetic complications during ECT.', 'Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease raises ECT risk because anesthesia and assisted ventilation can be more hazardous with limited pulmonary reserve.'),
  ('2013-76', 'School refusal with excessive fear of being away from attachment figures suggests {{c1::separation anxiety disorder}}.', 'Separation anxiety disorder commonly presents with school refusal, somatic complaints, and distress around leaving parents.'),
  ('2013-77', 'Compared with methadone, {{c1::buprenorphine}} has less risk of respiratory suppression because it is a {{c2::partial mu-opioid agonist}}.', 'Buprenorphine has a ceiling effect on respiratory depression, so it carries less respiratory-suppression risk than methadone.'),
  ('2013-78', 'The ability to engage in symbolic pretend play typically first appears in {{c1::toddlerhood}}.', 'Toddlerhood is when symbolic capacity first supports pretend play, such as using one object to represent another.'),
  ('2013-79', 'Excessive daytime sleepiness with morning headaches is a classic presentation of {{c1::obstructive sleep apnea}}.', 'E — OSA causes recurrent airway obstruction, sleep fragmentation, and daytime sleepiness. Morning headaches support nocturnal respiratory disturbance.'),
  ('2013-80', '{{c1::Fluoxetine}}, used with cognitive-behavioral therapy, is an effective treatment for {{c2::bulimia nervosa}}.', 'Fluoxetine is the extensively studied SSRI used with CBT for bulimia nervosa; avoid bupropion because of seizure risk.'),
  ('2013-81', 'A rare, nonexploitative, patient-centered departure from the usual psychotherapy frame that can be openly discussed is a {{c1::boundary crossing}}.', 'Boundary crossings are nonexploitative deviations from usual therapeutic boundaries. Their appropriateness depends on context, purpose, and transparent clinical discussion.'),
  ('2013-82', 'Progressive neck pain with spastic gait, hyperreflexia, and bladder dysfunction suggests {{c1::cervical myelopathy}}.', 'Cervical myelopathy causes bilateral upper motor neuron signs below the cervical lesion. Gait decline and urinary symptoms make the syndrome especially concerning.'),
  ('2013-83', 'For alcohol use disorder with prominent craving, {{c1::naltrexone}} can reduce alcohol reward and help reduce heavy drinking.', 'C — Naltrexone is an evidence-based medication for alcohol use disorder with craving. Screen for opioid use before prescribing.'),
  ('2013-84', '{{c1::Thiazide diuretics}} can increase lithium concentrations and precipitate {{c2::lithium toxicity}}.', 'Thiazide diuretics reduce lithium clearance. New tremor, ataxia, and confusion in a lithium-treated patient warrant urgent evaluation for toxicity.'),
  ('2013-85', 'The fixed false belief that a higher-status person is in love with the patient is {{c1::erotomanic delusional disorder}}.', 'Delusional disorder can present with an erotomanic theme. A broader manic, schizophrenic, or delirious syndrome would change the diagnosis.'),
  ('2013-86', 'After remission of a {{c1::third major depressive episode}}, an effective antidepressant is generally continued as {{c2::maintenance treatment}}.', 'Continue fluoxetine because it is effective prophylaxis in recurrent depression. Manage sexual adverse effects without reflexively abandoning relapse prevention.'),
  ('2013-87', 'Topiramate potentiates inhibitory neurotransmission at the {{c1::GABA-A receptor}}.', 'GABA-A receptor potentiation is one of topiramate’s anticonvulsant actions. It also inhibits sodium-channel firing and antagonizes AMPA/kainate glutamate signaling.'),
  ('2013-88', 'In older sleep-stage terminology, the deepest restorative slow-wave sleep is {{c1::stage 4 non-REM sleep}}.', 'Stage 4 non-REM is the historical label for the deepest slow-wave sleep. Modern scoring combines former stages 3 and 4 as N3.'),
  ('2013-89', 'A {{c1::power analysis}} estimates the sample size needed to detect a prespecified effect size with a chosen probability.', 'A — Power analysis is used for prospective sample-size planning. It incorporates effect size, variability, alpha, and desired power.'),
  ('2013-90', 'During acute refeeding for severe anorexia nervosa, psychotherapy should emphasize {{c1::coaching, support, and positive behavioral reinforcement}}.', 'Coaching and reinforcement directly support nutritional rehabilitation. Relapse planning and deeper cognitive or developmental work are generally more useful after stabilization.'),
  ('2013-91', 'Fatigue, hypersomnolence, and depressed mood after stopping a stimulant are characteristic of {{c1::cocaine withdrawal}}.', 'Cocaine withdrawal commonly causes a depressive, fatigued crash with increased sleep. It contrasts with the hyperarousal typical of alcohol withdrawal.'),
  ('2013-92', 'A {{c1::superior homonymous quadrantanopia}} localizes to the contralateral {{c2::temporal optic radiations}}.', 'Temporal optic-radiation lesions produce contralateral superior quadrantanopia. Parietal radiation lesions instead produce inferior quadrantanopia.'),
  ('2013-93', 'Among core ADHD symptoms, {{c1::hyperactivity}} is most likely to become less overt as children age.', 'Hyperactivity often diminishes in its visible childhood form. Inattention and impulsivity commonly persist into later development.'),
  ('2013-94', 'Competence to stand trial requires the ability to consult with counsel and a {{c1::factual and rational understanding of the proceedings}}.', 'A factual and rational understanding of proceedings is required for trial competence. Competence is distinct from insanity or criminal responsibility at the time of the offense.'),
  ('2013-95', 'In the United States, {{c1::Medicaid}} is the primary payer for long-term nursing-home care.', 'Medicaid is the major source of long-term nursing-home funding. Medicare coverage is generally limited to qualifying short-term skilled nursing care.'),
  ('2013-96', 'Progressive cognitive decline and seizures in a patient receiving long-term dialysis classically suggest {{c1::dialysis dementia}}.', 'Dialysis dementia is a historical syndrome classically associated with aluminum toxicity. Modern dialysis practices have made it uncommon.'),
  ('2013-97', 'Among psychiatric diagnoses, {{c1::dissociative disorders}} are classically associated with higher hypnotizability potential.', 'C — Dissociative disorders are classically linked to higher hypnotizability. Hypnotizability is not a diagnostic test.'),
  ('2013-98', 'Patients taking nonselective irreversible MAOIs should avoid tyramine-rich foods such as {{c1::fava beans}}.', 'Fava beans are a classic food relevant to MAOI dietary counseling. Excess tyramine can trigger a hypertensive reaction.'),
  ('2013-99', 'In neuroleptic malignant syndrome with rhabdomyolysis and immobility, supportive care includes {{c1::IV hydration to maintain diuresis}} and {{c2::venous thromboembolism prophylaxis when safe}}.', 'A, C — Diuresis and anticoagulation are the keyed supportive measures for NMS. Hydration and urine-output monitoring reduce renal injury risk from rhabdomyolysis.'),
  ('2013-100', 'A psychiatrist who reasonably suspects {{c1::child abuse}} must make a {{c2::mandatory protective report}} despite usual confidentiality.', 'B — Child abuse requires a mandatory protective report when reasonably suspected. The clinician reports concern rather than independently proving the allegation.'),
  ('2013-101', 'In psychoanalytic theory, {{c1::primary process thinking}} is unconscious mentation that is not constrained by ordinary logic.', 'C — Primary process thinking is unconscious and illogical. It contrasts with reality-oriented secondary process.'),
  ('2013-102', 'Compared with young adults, adults over 65 typically have more {{c1::fragmented sleep}} with more nocturnal awakenings.', 'B — Normal aging is associated with less consolidated sleep. Slow-wave sleep generally decreases rather than increases.'),
  ('2013-103', 'For adolescents with moderate to severe depression, TADS supported {{c1::fluoxetine combined with cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT)}}.', 'E — Fluoxetine and CBT is the TADS combined-treatment answer. CBT complements medication with behavioral and cognitive skills.'),
  ('2013-104', 'For alcohol use disorder in cirrhosis with adequate renal function, {{c1::acamprosate}} is favored because it is {{c2::renally eliminated}}.', 'C — Acamprosate is favored in cirrhosis because it avoids substantial hepatic metabolism. Renal function must be assessed before use.'),
  ('2013-105', 'In DBT, the dialectical stance seeks {{c1::synthesis between acceptance and change}} rather than choosing either extreme.', 'A — Searching for synthesis between contradictory ideas defines DBT’s dialectical stance. Its key example is balancing validation with change.'),
  ('2013-106', 'The feature most specific for distinguishing delirium from Alzheimer dementia is {{c1::fluctuating arousal and attention}}.', 'C — Fluctuating arousal is the best discriminator. Delirium is acute and variable, whereas Alzheimer dementia usually has stable alertness early.'),
  ('2013-107', 'Rapid learning in school-age children is paralleled by {{c1::prefrontal cortical synaptic pruning}}, which improves circuit efficiency.', 'D — Prefrontal cortical pruning is the keyed developmental correlate. It refines neural networks rather than merely increasing brain size.'),
  ('2013-108', 'A child who recognizes that parental divorce was not caused by the child demonstrates {{c1::decentration}}.', 'A — Decentration is the correct answer because the child can consider causes beyond the self. This contrasts with egocentric self-blame.'),
  ('2013-109', 'A molecular strategy investigated to improve cognition in schizophrenia is {{c1::dopamine D1 receptor agonism}}.', 'B — D1 receptor agonism is the keyed cognitive-enhancement mechanism. D2 antagonism primarily targets positive psychotic symptoms.'),
  ('2013-110', 'Attributing one’s own unacceptable impulses or feelings to another person is {{c1::projection}}.', 'C — Projection assigns unwanted internal content to someone else. Projective identification additionally involves an interpersonal enactment.'),
  ('2013-111', 'Suspected ulnar neuropathy causing fourth- and fifth-digit paresthesias is confirmed with {{c1::nerve conduction studies}}.', 'B — Nerve conduction studies are the correct confirmatory test for suspected ulnar entrapment. They localize peripheral nerve dysfunction.'),
  ('2013-112', 'Among major mood disorders, {{c1::bipolar disorder}} has broadly comparable prevalence in males and females.', 'A — Bipolar disorder has an approximately equal overall sex distribution. Major depressive disorders are more common in females.'),
  ('2013-113', 'Dantrolene treats neuroleptic malignant syndrome by producing {{c1::skeletal muscle relaxation}} and reducing rigidity-related heat production.', 'E — Skeletal muscle relaxation is dantrolene’s relevant effect in NMS. It is not a dopamine agonist or antagonist.'),
  ('2013-114', 'René Spitz’s historical term for depressive withdrawal after prolonged separation from an established caregiver is {{c1::anaclitic depression}}.', 'C — Anaclitic depression is Spitz’s historical term. It describes depressive-like withdrawal after loss of an attachment figure.'),
  ('2013-115', 'Using a 1-to-10 readiness ruler and asking why the score is not lower is {{c1::motivational enhancement therapy}}, designed to elicit {{c2::change talk}}.', 'D — Motivational enhancement therapy is the correct modality. The readiness-ruler question elicits the patient’s own reasons to change.'),
  ('2013-116', 'In a recent transplant recipient, new tremor with confusion or psychosis should raise concern for {{c1::cyclosporine neurotoxicity}}.', 'Cyclosporine can cause tremor and, in severe cases, encephalopathy, seizures, or psychosis. The post-transplant setting makes it the best fit.'),
  ('2013-117', 'The best-supported psychosocial treatment for childhood oppositional behavior is {{c1::parent management training}}.', 'Parent management training targets reinforcement patterns that sustain oppositional outbursts. It is better supported than medication for oppositional behavior without a specific medication-responsive comorbidity.'),
  ('2013-118', 'Before starting {{c1::lithium}}, obtain renal function testing with {{c2::BUN and creatinine}}.', 'Lithium is cleared by the kidneys. Baseline BUN and creatinine are essential to assess clearance and establish a monitoring baseline.'),
  ('2013-119', 'In covert sensitization, a target sexually arousing fantasy is paired with an {{c1::aversive fantasy or imagined unpleasant consequence}}.', 'Aversive fantasy is correct because covert sensitization is imaginal conditioning. Electric shock would be an overt, not covert, aversive method.'),
  ('2013-120', 'The probability that a patient truly lacks disease given a negative test result is the {{c1::negative predictive value}}.', 'Negative predictive value answers, “With a negative test, what is the chance the patient truly does not have the disease?” It is influenced by disease prevalence.'),
  ('2013-121', 'D-cycloserine modulates NMDA receptors at the {{c1::glycine co-agonist site}}.', 'Glycine is correct because d-cycloserine acts as a partial agonist at the NMDA receptor glycine site. Glutamate is the primary agonist at a different site.'),
  ('2013-122', 'In Eriksonian theory, uncertainty about one''s values, roles, and vocational direction most directly reflects the task of {{c1::identity}}.', 'A — The central issue is self-definition and vocational direction. That is an identity conflict.'),
  ('2013-123', '{{c1::Phenytoin}} is classically associated with gingival hypertrophy, hirsutism, and dose-related cerebellar ataxia.', 'Phenytoin produces the characteristic triad of gingival hypertrophy, hirsutism, and ataxia. The ataxia may signal excessive drug exposure.'),
  ('2013-124', 'Among antidepressants, {{c1::clomipramine}} is a highly effective pharmacologic treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder.', 'Clomipramine has strong anti-obsessional efficacy through potent serotonin reuptake inhibition. Its side-effect burden is greater than that of many SSRIs.'),
  ('2013-125', 'The core psychotherapy for agoraphobia is {{c1::cognitive-behavioral therapy with graduated exposure}}.', 'Cognitive-behavioral therapy with exposure addresses the avoidance that maintains agoraphobia. Benzodiazepines may relieve symptoms but do not provide the same long-term behavioral learning.'),
  ('2013-126', 'A common co-occurring condition in autism spectrum disorder is {{c1::intellectual disability}}.', 'Intellectual disability is a frequent comorbidity in autism and should be assessed alongside adaptive functioning. ADHD is also common but is not the keyed best answer here.'),
  ('2013-127', 'When neuropsychiatric symptoms raise concern for neurosyphilis, evaluate for CNS involvement with {{c1::cerebrospinal fluid testing}}.', 'Cerebrospinal fluid evaluation is appropriate when syphilis serology accompanies cognitive, behavioral, or neurologic findings. The supplied answer specifies CSF FTA-ABS as the next test.'),
  ('2013-128', 'Agitation, hypertension, psychosis, and {{c1::vertical nystagmus}} are classic findings of {{c2::PCP intoxication}}.', 'Phencyclidine is strongly suggested by vertical nystagmus with agitation and autonomic activation. It can mimic primary psychosis or mania.'),
  ('2013-129', 'Parkinson disease is a {{c1::synucleinopathy characterized by Lewy bodies and degeneration of substantia nigra dopaminergic neurons}}.', 'Parkinson disease is associated with Lewy bodies containing alpha-synuclein. Nigrostriatal dopamine loss produces its core motor syndrome.'),
  ('2013-130', '{{c1::Recalling personally experienced daily events}} is a form of declarative episodic memory.', 'Recalling daily events is episodic declarative memory. Skills, habits, priming, and conditioning are nondeclarative forms of memory.'),
  ('2013-131', 'In a school-age child after bereavement, {{c1::persistent separation anxiety and detachment from peers}} can suggest unresolved grief.', 'Separation anxiety from parents and persistent peer detachment indicate impaired adaptation to the loss. Sadness, questions, and intermittent cheerfulness can occur in normative grief.'),
  ('2013-132', 'In a patient taking an MAOI, {{c1::fentanyl}} can contribute to a dangerous serotonergic drug interaction.', 'Fentanyl is the correct answer because MAOI coadministration can cause severe, potentially fatal reactions. Careful medication reconciliation is essential when an MAOI-treated patient needs analgesia.'),
  ('2013-133', 'New or inadequately evaluated physical symptoms with health anxiety warrant {{c1::appropriate medical evaluation}} before concluding they are psychiatric.', 'Referral for further medical evaluation is correct because the patient has active, insufficiently characterized gastrointestinal complaints. CBT may be useful after a reasonable medical assessment.'),
  ('2013-134', 'The medication historically explored for gout and later promoted by John Cade for mania was {{c1::lithium}}.', 'A — Lithium has the historical gout-to-mania association. It became the prototypical mood stabilizer.'),
  ('2013-135', 'Craving triggered by a place repeatedly paired with drug use is {{c1::classical conditioning}}.', 'B — The location has become a conditioned cue for cocaine craving. Operant conditioning concerns reinforcement of drug-taking behavior.'),
  ('2013-136', 'Immediately after sexual assault, the most helpful preventive intervention is {{c1::psychosocial support that establishes safety from further trauma}}.', 'Psychosocial safety interventions are correct because they address immediate danger and restore control. Routine debriefing or medication is not the primary universal preventive strategy.'),
  ('2013-137', 'Among mood stabilizers, {{c1::lithium}} is most associated with a suicide-protective effect beyond mood stabilization.', 'Lithium is correct because it has a distinctive association with reduced suicide risk in mood disorders. This benefit complements but does not replace acute suicide-risk management.'),
  ('2013-138', 'The common autoimmune form of myasthenia gravis is caused by antibodies against {{c1::postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors}}.', 'Acetylcholine receptors are correct because autoimmune targeting disrupts postsynaptic neuromuscular transmission. Voltage-dependent calcium-channel antibodies instead suggest Lambert-Eaton syndrome.'),
  ('2013-139', 'Medication may be given without consent when {{c1::a documented emergency creates an immediate need to prevent serious harm}}.', 'An emergency situation is correct because it can justify immediate treatment to prevent imminent harm. Psychosis, delusions, or hospital location alone are insufficient.'),
  ('2013-140', 'For a pregnant patient with severe bipolar disorder that repeatedly relapses off treatment, {{c1::continuing effective lithium}} may be the best option after individualized risk-benefit assessment.', 'Continue lithium because prior discontinuation caused severe relapse and alternatives pose important limitations. Pregnancy decisions must weigh fetal medication risk against maternal relapse risk.'),
  ('2013-141', 'New-onset psychotic symptoms with an atypical presentation should prompt a {{c1::general medical and neurologic workup}}.', 'A general medical/neurological workup is correct because new psychotic symptoms may be secondary to an underlying condition. Collateral and psychological testing may supplement but not replace this step.'),
  ('2013-142', 'Freud''s latency stage most closely corresponds to Erikson''s {{c1::industry vs. inferiority}} stage.', 'B — Both frameworks point to the school-age period. Erikson emphasizes competence and the risk of feeling inferior.'),
  ('2013-143', 'Historically, suspected myasthenia gravis with fatigable weakness was supported by the {{c1::edrophonium test}}.', 'Edrophonium is the keyed historical answer because transient improvement after acetylcholinesterase inhibition supports myasthenia gravis. Thoracic imaging evaluates associated thymic disease rather than directly establishing the diagnosis.'),
  ('2013-144', 'New aggression and irritability in a strength-focused athlete should raise suspicion for {{c1::anabolic-androgenic steroid abuse}}.', 'Anabolic steroid abuse is correct because the vignette pairs escalating weight training with new irritability and aggression. Primary mania and other substances remain differentials but fit less well.'),
  ('2013-145', 'The historical PRITE key identifies {{c1::CYP3A4}}, but racial identity should not be used to predict an individual patient’s enzyme activity or dose.', 'D — 3A4 is the historical keyed association. It is a non-deterministic population generalization, not an individual dosing rule.'),
  ('2013-146', 'Rapidly progressive dementia with startle myoclonus and periodic EEG complexes suggests {{c1::subacute spongiform encephalopathy (prion disease)}}.', 'Subacute spongiform encephalopathy is correct because the syndrome is classic for prion disease. Rapid progression, myoclonus, ataxia, and periodic EEG abnormalities are key clues.'),
  ('2013-147', 'Compared with individual therapy, group therapy more commonly uses therapist {{c1::transparency and judicious self-disclosure}} to facilitate here-and-now process.', 'Transparency and self-disclosure are correct because group therapists may use them to clarify interpersonal process. They should remain limited and therapeutically purposeful.'),
  ('2013-148', 'The CBC monitoring requirement for clozapine primarily detects {{c1::neutropenia (low absolute neutrophil count)}}.', 'Abnormal neutrophil count is correct because clozapine can cause severe neutropenia or agranulocytosis. ANC monitoring is the central hematologic safeguard.'),
  ('2013-149', 'In Parkinson disease, {{c1::ropinirole}} can cause sudden sleep episodes, including falling asleep while driving.', 'Ropinirole is correct because dopamine agonists can cause excessive somnolence and sudden sleep attacks. This adverse effect has immediate driving-safety implications.'),
  ('2013-150', '{{c1::Effectiveness}} research evaluates whether a treatment works in routine, real-world clinical practice.', 'Effectiveness evaluates outcomes under usual clinical conditions. It contrasts with efficacy, which tests benefit under more controlled and selective conditions.'),
  ('2013-151', 'When a family member asks about a patient’s treatment, the psychiatrist should explain that {{c1::the patient’s consent is required to discuss treatment}}.', 'The correct answer is that the patient’s consent is required before discussing treatment. The psychiatrist may still listen to collateral information without confirming care details.'),
  ('2013-152', 'The combination of {{c1::fluctuating cognition, visual hallucinations, and parkinsonism}} suggests {{c2::dementia with Lewy bodies}}.', 'Dementia with Lewy bodies classically causes cognitive fluctuations, visual hallucinations, and parkinsonism. Severe sensitivity to antipsychotic extrapyramidal effects supports the diagnosis.'),
  ('2013-153', '{{c1::Delirium tremens}} is severe alcohol withdrawal marked by delirium with autonomic hyperactivity and tremor.', 'Delirium tremens causes acute delirium, tremor, and marked autonomic activation during alcohol withdrawal. Chronic liver disease stigmata support the alcohol-use context.'),
  ('2013-154', 'Persistent thoughts and sadness about a major loss can be {{c1::an appropriate grief reaction}} when functioning and engagement with life are preserved.', 'An appropriate reaction to a significant loss can include lifelong sadness and memories. Lack of pervasive impairment or a depressive syndrome argues against a grief disorder.'),
  ('2013-155', 'Mirtazapine increases norepinephrine and serotonin release through {{c1::presynaptic alpha-2 adrenergic receptor blockade}}.', 'Alpha-2 adrenergic blockade removes presynaptic inhibitory feedback and increases norepinephrine and serotonin release. Mirtazapine is not primarily a serotonin reuptake inhibitor.'),
  ('2013-156', 'A persistent, impairing fear triggered by a specific object or situation is {{c1::specific phobia}}.', 'Specific phobia is a circumscribed, cue-linked fear with avoidance or intense distress. The trigger here is hiking after a snake encounter.'),
  ('2013-157', '{{c1::Cannabis use during adolescence}} is associated with increased later risk of {{c2::schizophrenia}}.', 'Cannabis use in adolescence is associated with later schizophrenia risk. This association is not proof that cannabis alone causes schizophrenia in an individual.'),
  ('2013-158', '{{c1::Early falls and vertical supranuclear gaze palsy}} are characteristic of {{c2::progressive supranuclear palsy}}.', 'Progressive supranuclear palsy causes early falls, symmetric parkinsonism, and impaired vertical voluntary eye movements. Preserved doll’s-head eye movements support a supranuclear lesion.'),
  ('2013-159', '{{c1::Paradoxical insomnia}} is the perception of severe insomnia despite relatively normal objective sleep findings.', 'Paradoxical insomnia is a mismatch between severe perceived insomnia and near-normal polysomnographic sleep. It is not intentional fabrication.'),
  ('2013-160', 'An empathic, alliance-building response to distress is {{c1::reflection of the patient’s feeling followed by an open-ended question}}.', 'The correct response validates the patient’s pain and invites discussion of coping. It is more therapeutic initially than advice, self-disclosure, or fact-focused questioning.'),
  ('2013-161', 'Acamprosate is thought to support abstinence in alcohol use disorder by modulating {{c1::glutamatergic neurotransmission}}.', 'Acamprosate’s key neurotransmitter association is glutamate. It is used in alcohol use disorder to support maintenance of abstinence.'),
  ('2013-162', '{{c1::Cocaine withdrawal}} can raise prolactin by reducing {{c2::dopaminergic inhibition of prolactin secretion}}.', 'Cocaine withdrawal can be associated with hyperprolactinemia because dopamine normally inhibits prolactin release. Persistent or concerning hyperprolactinemia still needs medical evaluation.'),
  ('2013-163', 'Unexpectedly poor academic achievement despite average general intelligence suggests {{c1::specific learning disorder (learning disability)}}.', 'Learning disability is indicated by specific academic underachievement despite average IQ. It differs from intellectual disability, which involves global intellectual and adaptive deficits.'),
  ('2013-164', '{{c1::Ciprofloxacin}} can increase methadone exposure and contribute to {{c2::opioid toxicity with respiratory depression}}.', 'Ciprofloxacin can increase methadone exposure and contribute to opioid toxicity. New sedation and respiratory depression in a methadone-treated patient require emergency evaluation.'),
  ('2013-165', 'A midline optic-chiasm lesion causes {{c1::bitemporal hemianopia}} by damaging {{c2::crossing nasal retinal fibers}}.', 'Bitemporal hemianopia results from optic-chiasm injury. Crossing nasal retinal fibers carry temporal visual-field information.'),
  ('2013-166', 'Blood-injection-injury phobia characteristically has an initial sympathetic surge followed by {{c1::vasovagal hypotension}}.', 'Hypotension is the defining second phase of the blood-phobia response. It can cause presyncope or syncope.'),
  ('2013-167', 'Compared with younger adults with bipolar disorder, older adults are more likely to have {{c1::mixed episodes}}.', 'More mixed episodes is the keyed late-life bipolar distinction. Assess for simultaneous activation and depressive symptoms.'),
  ('2013-168', 'Progressive disinhibition or personality/language change with relatively preserved early memory suggests {{c1::frontotemporal dementia}}.', 'C — FTD commonly presents with early behavioral, executive, or language change. Preserved early memory helps distinguish it from typical Alzheimer disease.'),
  ('2013-169', 'Medication {{c1::potency}} is the relative dose required to achieve a specified effect.', 'Potency concerns the dose required for an effect. Maximal achievable response is efficacy, not potency.'),
  ('2013-170', 'For the keyed female sexual-arousal presentation, {{c1::sensate focus exercises}} reduce performance pressure through graded non-demand touch.', 'Sensate focus exercises are the keyed treatment for sexual arousal difficulty. They shift attention from performance to sensory experience.'),
  ('2013-171', 'A school-age language milestone is {{c1::understanding double meanings in jokes}}, reflecting metalinguistic awareness.', 'Understanding double meanings is a school-age language ability. It requires moving beyond literal interpretation.'),
  ('2013-172', 'When traffic becomes fear-provoking after an accident, the acquired fear association is {{c1::classical conditioning}}.', 'Classical conditioning explains trauma-cue fear acquisition. Exposure promotes extinction of the conditioned response.'),
  ('2013-173', 'Recurrent sexual arousal centered on {{c1::nonliving objects}} is characteristic of {{c2::fetishism}}.', 'Fetishism is defined by object-focused sexual arousal. Disorder-level diagnosis requires distress, impairment, or nonconsenting involvement.'),
  ('2013-174', 'For acute cocaine-related agitation, first-line medication treatment is a {{c1::benzodiazepine such as lorazepam}}.', 'Lorazepam is the keyed initial treatment for cocaine-associated agitation. It reduces anxiety and sympathetic overactivation.'),
  ('2013-175', 'A painful unilateral vesicular rash in a dermatome is herpes zoster caused by {{c1::varicella zoster virus}}.', 'Varicella zoster virus causes shingles. Reactivation from sensory ganglia produces a dermatomal eruption.'),
  ('2013-176', 'After an adequate unsuccessful methylphenidate trial for ADHD, the next class to try is {{c1::an amphetamine stimulant, such as dextroamphetamine}}.', 'Dextroamphetamine is the appropriate next trial after methylphenidate nonresponse. It tests response to the alternate stimulant class.'),
  ('2013-177', 'The ethical principle expressed by “first, do no harm” is {{c1::nonmaleficence}}.', 'Nonmaleficence means avoiding unnecessary harm. Beneficence instead emphasizes promoting the patient’s welfare.'),
  ('2013-178', 'In a febrile, confused patient with abnormal movements after haloperidol, {{c1::CSF evaluation}} is the best-listed test when CNS infection remains a meaningful concern.', 'D — Cerebrospinal fluid evaluation is the source-adjudicated answer. It is clinically selective rather than universally required for suspected NMS.'),
  ('2013-179', 'In taijin kyofusho-like social anxiety, the central fear may be {{c1::offending or making others uncomfortable}}.', 'Offending others is the keyed culturally shaped social fear. It contrasts with fear of embarrassing oneself.'),
  ('2013-180', 'Supportive psychotherapy works primarily in the {{c1::present}}, emphasizing current coping and functioning.', 'Working in the present is central to supportive therapy. It emphasizes stabilization, coping, and practical adaptation.'),
  ('2013-181', 'When lamotrigine is added to valproate, {{c1::lamotrigine clearance decreases}}, so it requires {{c2::a lower, slower titration}}.', 'Valproate inhibits lamotrigine glucuronidation and increases lamotrigine concentration. Use a reduced, slower lamotrigine titration to limit serious rash risk.'),
  ('2013-182', 'The most common specific learning disorder identified in special-education settings is {{c1::impairment in reading (dyslexia)}}.', 'Reading disorder is the most common learning disorder in special-education populations. It is characterized by persistent problems with decoding, fluency, and/or comprehension.'),
  ('2013-183', 'Uncomplicated alcohol withdrawal is typically treated with benzodiazepine detoxification over {{c1::3 to 5 days}}.', 'Alcohol detoxification with benzodiazepines generally lasts 3 to 5 days. The exact regimen should be adjusted to withdrawal severity and complication risk.'),
  ('2013-184', 'In encephalitis, {{c1::asymmetric flaccid monoparesis}} suggests {{c2::West Nile virus}} neuroinvasive disease.', 'West Nile virus can injure anterior horn cells, producing acute asymmetric flaccid weakness. Monoparesis is more specific than headache or confusion.'),
  ('2013-185', 'The culturally patterned complaint of {{c1::altered nerves (nervios)}} is classically associated with {{c2::Hispanic/Latino}} patients.', 'Hispanic/Latino cultural formulations may use nervios to describe stress-related distress. Clarify the patient’s own meaning before assigning a psychiatric diagnosis.'),
  ('2013-186', 'Major depressive disorder is classically associated with {{c1::decreased REM latency}} (earlier onset of REM sleep).', 'Major depression is associated with shortened REM latency. REM density is typically increased rather than reduced.'),
  ('2013-187', 'Recurrent {{c1::lobar intracerebral hemorrhages}} in an older adult suggest {{c2::cerebral amyloid angiopathy}}.', 'Amyloid angiopathy causes fragile cortical vessels and recurrent lobar hemorrhage. Hypertensive hemorrhage more often occurs in deep brain structures.'),
  ('2013-188', '{{c1::Aminophylline (theophylline)}} can {{c2::decrease serum lithium concentration}} by increasing renal lithium clearance.', 'Aminophylline lowers lithium concentrations through increased renal clearance. NSAIDs, thiazides, and ACE inhibitors more often raise lithium levels.'),
  ('2013-189', 'To diagnose intellectual disability after a low IQ score, assess {{c1::adaptive functioning}}, for example with the {{c2::Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales}}.', 'Vineland measures adaptive functioning, a required component of intellectual disability assessment. IQ testing alone is insufficient.'),
  ('2013-190', 'Among psychiatric disorders after organ transplantation, {{c1::major depression}} is the most common.', 'Major depression is the most common psychiatric disorder after organ transplant. Evaluate concurrent medical illness and medication effects as part of the assessment.'),
  ('2013-191', 'In major depression, {{c1::comorbid borderline personality disorder}} predicts a less favorable response to {{c2::ECT}}.', 'Borderline personality disorder is a negative predictor of ECT response in major depression. Psychosis, catatonia, older age, and shorter episodes generally predict better response.'),
  ('2013-192', 'Solution-focused therapy identifies {{c1::exceptions to the problem}} and helps clients repeat what is different during those times.', 'Solution-focused therapy focuses on exceptions, strengths, and achievable change. Asking about times of not arguing is a classic exception-focused intervention.'),
  ('2013-193', 'Children usually first recognize that others can think and feel differently from themselves during {{c1::the preschool years}}.', 'Preschool-aged children begin to develop theory of mind. School-age and adolescent perspective-taking are more sophisticated later developments.'),
  ('2013-194', 'Experiencing colors in response to musical tones is {{c1::synesthesia}}, a {{c2::cross-modal sensory experience}}.', 'Synesthesia is the involuntary linking of sensory modalities. Hearing tones as colors is a classic example.'),
  ('2013-195', 'In the United States, {{c1::firearms (shooting)}} are the most common method of {{c2::completed suicide}}.', 'Shooting is the most common method of completed suicide in the United States. Assess firearm access directly as part of suicide-risk evaluation.'),
  ('2013-196', 'Cisplatin is classically associated with a cumulative {{c1::distal sensory polyneuropathy}}.', 'Cisplatin commonly causes cumulative, predominantly sensory peripheral neuropathy. Distal paresthesias and impaired vibration or proprioception are typical.'),
  ('2013-197', 'A criminal conviction generally requires both {{c1::actus reus (the prohibited act)}} and {{c2::mens rea (the required culpable mental state)}}.', 'C — Conviction of a crime requires the prohibited act and the requisite mental state.'),
  ('2013-198', '{{c1::Oppositional defiant disorder}} is characterized by a persistent pattern of irritable mood and argumentative or defiant behavior toward authority figures.', 'D — Oppositional defiant disorder best fits recurrent anger and defiance when limits are set.'),
  ('2013-199', 'A prominent neurocognitive deficit in schizophrenia is {{c1::working memory}}, the ability to briefly hold and manipulate information.', 'B — Working memory is the keyed deficit most strongly associated with schizophrenia.'),
  ('2013-200', 'The characteristic cardiac conduction toxicity of tricyclic antidepressants results from blockade of {{c1::fast voltage-gated sodium channels}}.', 'C — Fast sodium-channel blockade slows cardiac conduction and can widen the QRS in TCA toxicity.'),
  ('2013-201', 'By late childhood, close friendship is most centrally defined by {{c1::mutual caring, trust, and reciprocity}}.', 'C — Feeling cared about reflects the reciprocal, emotionally supportive friendship expected in late childhood.'),
  ('2013-202', 'Bilateral CA1 hippocampal neuronal loss is classically associated with {{c1::mesial temporal epilepsy}} and {{c2::Alzheimer disease}}.', 'A, E — Epilepsy and Alzheimer dementia are the keyed associations with bilateral CA1 neuronal loss.'),
  ('2013-203', '{{c1::Periodic limb movements during sleep}} are repetitive leg jerks that can cause brief arousals and daytime sleepiness.', 'D — Periodic limb movements explain unrecognized nocturnal leg jerks and sleep fragmentation.'),
  ('2013-204', 'For a high-risk irreversible treatment opposed by the physician, a guardian’s demand may require {{c1::court authorization}} before it can proceed.', 'A — Court authorization is the keyed route for resolving this high-risk treatment dispute.'),
  ('2013-205', 'Among the listed traditional laboratory tests, {{c1::carbohydrate-deficient transferrin (CDT)}} is the most sensitive and specific marker of recent heavy alcohol use.', 'E — Carbohydrate-deficient transferrin is the keyed marker for recent heavy alcohol use.'),
  ('2013-206', '{{c1::Cluster headache}} presents with severe unilateral orbital or temporal pain plus ipsilateral autonomic symptoms such as lacrimation or rhinorrhea.', 'D — Cluster headache best explains unilateral nocturnal periorbital pain with rhinorrhea and conjunctival injection.'),
  ('2013-207', 'Both the {{c1::Beck Anxiety Inventory}} and the {{c2::Taylor Manifest Anxiety Scale}} are patient self-report questionnaires.', 'A, C — The Beck Anxiety Inventory and Taylor Manifest Anxiety Scale are both patient self-report questionnaires. The corrected key resolves an ambiguous single-answer source item.'),
  ('2013-208', '{{c1::Thermal biofeedback}} is an effective behavioral treatment option for mild-to-moderate idiopathic Raynaud disease.', 'C — Thermal biofeedback is the keyed treatment for mild-to-moderate idiopathic Raynaud disease.'),
  ('2013-209', 'In the absence of impairment, coercion, or harmful behavior, adolescent interest in sexual material most often reflects {{c1::developmentally appropriate curiosity}}.', 'D — Developmentally appropriate curiosity best fits the described periodic website viewing.'),
  ('2013-210', 'Brief, frequent unilateral orbital headaches with ipsilateral autonomic symptoms suggest {{c1::paroxysmal hemicrania}}, which characteristically responds to {{c2::indomethacin}}.', 'A — Indomethacin is the corrected single answer. It is the syndrome-specific treatment for paroxysmal hemicrania.'),
  ('2013-211', '{{c1::Dissociative identity disorder}} involves identity disruption with recurrent amnesia for everyday events beyond ordinary forgetting.', 'C — Dissociative identity disorder best explains behavioral changes with missing time and amnesia for actions.'),
  ('2013-212', 'An effective consultation-liaison psychiatry program can improve {{c1::treatment compliance}} in medical patients.', 'Improved treatment compliance is correct because C-L psychiatry addresses psychiatric and psychosocial barriers to medical care.'),
  ('2013-213', 'A common terminally ill patient concern about a “bad death” is {{c1::uncontrolled pain}}.', 'In pain is correct because uncontrolled physical suffering is a prototypical end-of-life fear. Other concerns may still be important for an individual patient.'),
  ('2013-214', 'In hepatic encephalopathy, EEG may show {{c1::bilateral triphasic waves}} as part of a diffuse metabolic encephalopathy.', 'Bilateral triphasic waves are correct in this classic presentation of hepatic encephalopathy.'),
  ('2013-215', 'Erikson described middle childhood as {{c1::industry versus inferiority}}, centered on personal worth and {{c2::competence}}.', 'Personal worth and competence are correct because school-age children are navigating industry versus inferiority.'),
  ('2013-216', '{{c1::Sensitization}} is increased responsiveness to later stimuli after exposure to an aversive stimulus.', 'Sensitization is correct because the response becomes stronger even to subthreshold stimulation. Habituation instead produces a diminished response.'),
  ('2013-217', 'A {{c1::concerning rash with systemic symptoms}} after starting lamotrigine requires {{c2::immediate discontinuation and urgent evaluation}}.', 'Discontinue the lamotrigine, and come to the appropriate emergency facility because this may be a serious cutaneous reaction.'),
  ('2013-218', 'Under the Goldwater Rule, psychiatrists should not offer a professional diagnosis of an {{c1::unexamined public figure}}.', 'It is unethical because the psychiatrist has not examined the entertainer and lacks authorization to diagnose publicly.'),
  ('2013-219', 'Historically, {{c1::pramipexole}}, a dopamine agonist, was a first-line treatment for restless legs syndrome.', 'Pramipexole is correct as the historical board answer for restless legs syndrome treatment.'),
  ('2013-220', 'For this historical exam association, pregnant victims of domestic violence may have injury to the {{c1::abdomen}}.', 'Abdomen is correct as the keyed historical association in pregnancy-related domestic violence.'),
  ('2013-221', '{{c1::Arbitrary inference}} is drawing a conclusion despite insufficient evidence or evidence to the contrary.', 'Drawing a conclusion despite absent or contrary evidence defines arbitrary inference.'),
  ('2013-222', 'A positive response to ECT has been correlated with increased {{c1::frontal delta activity}} on EEG.', 'Increased ECT-induced frontal delta activity is the keyed positive-response correlate.'),
  ('2013-223', 'Among broad psychiatric categories, {{c1::anxiety disorders}} are highly comorbid with autism spectrum disorder in youth.', 'Anxiety is correct because it is a common psychiatric comorbidity in children and adolescents with ASD.'),
  ('2013-224', 'Headache with papilledema and unrevealing structural imaging in an obese young adult suggests {{c1::idiopathic intracranial hypertension}}.', 'Idiopathic intracranial hypertension is correct because the vignette pairs obesity, headache, papilledema, and normal structural imaging.'),
  ('2013-225', 'Panic disorder requires recurrent {{c1::unexpected (unprovoked) panic attacks}} plus persistent concern or maladaptive behavioral change.', 'At least some anxiety attacks that are unprovoked are required because panic disorder involves recurrent unexpected panic attacks.'),
  ('2013-226', 'When a patient lacks capacity, a {{c1::living will}} states treatment preferences and a {{c2::durable power of attorney for health care}} designates a surrogate.', 'Living will / Durable power of attorney is correct because these directives state prior wishes or appoint a surrogate.'),
  ('2013-227', 'Among common antidepressants, {{c1::paroxetine}} has the strongest ejaculation-delaying effect for premature ejaculation.', 'Paroxetine is correct because it has a particularly strong ejaculation-delaying effect among the listed antidepressants.'),
  ('2013-228', 'Subacute cerebellar ataxia with {{c1::anti-Yo antibodies}} warrants a {{c2::systemic search for occult malignancy}}.', 'Computed tomography of the chest, abdomen and pelvis is correct because anti-Yo cerebellar degeneration is paraneoplastic.'),
  ('2013-229', 'Before initiating couples therapy, the clinician should assess for {{c1::domestic violence and coercive control}} because conjoint treatment may be unsafe.', 'Domestic violence is the priority because it can make conjoint therapy unsafe. Assess each partner privately before routine couple interventions.'),
  ('2013-230', 'After a brief separation, a securely attached toddler typically {{c1::seeks contact with the returning caregiver}} and is soothed.', 'Seek contact eagerly reflects secure attachment at reunion. The child may protest separation but uses the caregiver to regain regulation.'),
  ('2013-231', 'When evaluating suspected illness anxiety in a medical inpatient, the most important first differential is an {{c1::underlying medical condition}}.', 'Medical condition is the priority differential. Psychiatric explanations must not replace an adequate disease evaluation.'),
  ('2013-232', 'In brain-death determination, the {{c1::apnea test}} evaluates whether a patient has spontaneous respiratory drive despite rising carbon dioxide.', 'Apnea test is the relevant brain-death component. It tests for absent spontaneous breathing under an appropriate carbon dioxide stimulus.'),
  ('2013-233', '{{c1::Derealization}}—a sense that surroundings are unreal or dreamlike—can occur during a panic attack.', 'Derealization is a recognized panic-attack symptom. It is a subjective sense of unreality with usually intact reality testing.'),
  ('2013-234', 'Chronic {{c1::nitrous oxide}} exposure can cause functional vitamin B12 inactivation and a {{c2::myeloneuropathy}} with numbness and ataxia.', 'Nitrous oxide best fits progressive numbness, dexterity loss, and ataxia in a dentist. Repeated exposure can cause functional B12-related neurologic injury.'),
  ('2013-235', 'Prominent {{c1::isolation of affect and intellectualization}} are classically associated with {{c2::obsessive-compulsive personality traits}}.', 'Obsessive-compulsive is correct because isolation of affect and intellectualization are characteristic defenses of this personality style. Emotional control and excessive reliance on analysis are typical.'),
  ('2013-236', 'For compulsive sexual behavior, {{c1::Twelve-step groups}} are the historical keyed approach for structured peer support and accountability.', 'Twelve-step groups are the historical keyed approach. Their contribution is peer support, accountability, and recovery structure.'),
  ('2013-237', 'Peek-a-boo illustrates {{c1::object permanence}}, the understanding that unseen objects continue to exist.', 'Object permanence is correct because the infant recognizes that the hidden person has not ceased to exist. Peek-a-boo depends on disappearance followed by expected return.'),
  ('2013-238', 'In group therapy, recognizing that others share one’s painful experience is {{c1::universalization}}, while amplification of suicidal affect may represent {{c2::contagion}}.', 'Contagion / Universalization captures both risk and connection. The leader should validate belonging while assessing and containing suicide risk.'),
  ('2013-239', '{{c1::Clozapine}} is classically indicated for {{c2::treatment-resistant schizophrenia}} after adequate prior trials fail.', 'Clozapine is the key treatment for refractory schizophrenia. Its benefits require careful hematologic and medical adverse-effect monitoring.'),
  ('2013-240', '{{c1::Inhalant intoxication}} may cause an alcohol-like syndrome of slurred speech, nystagmus, ataxia, and impaired judgment.', 'Inhalants best explain the alcohol-like intoxication signs. Ask directly about volatile solvents, aerosols, and gases.'),
  ('2013-241', 'For hypertension with hyponatremia and depression, the historical keyed choices are {{c1::atenolol or spironolactone}}, avoiding {{c2::hydrochlorothiazide}}.', 'Atenolol / Spironolactone are the historical keyed safer options. They avoid the concerns tested by thiazide use and older depression-associated agents.'),
  ('2013-242', 'New bilateral leg symptoms with a sensory level and bladder dysfunction require urgent {{c1::MRI of the spine}} to evaluate myelopathy.', 'Magnetic resonance imaging of the spine is the next test for suspected spinal cord disease. The sensory level and urinary symptoms make this urgent.'),
  ('2013-243', '{{c1::Schizotypal personality disorder}} is the personality disorder most strongly associated with schizophrenia-spectrum illness in family history.', 'Schizotypal personality disorder has the closest familial schizophrenia-spectrum association. Odd beliefs and perceptual distortions distinguish it from simple detachment.'),
  ('2013-244', 'When initiating care for a hostile patient, {{c1::acknowledge and validate the patient’s affect}} while maintaining clear safety limits.', 'Acknowledge and validate the patient’s affect to begin de-escalation. Validation is not agreement with unsafe behavior.'),
  ('2013-245', 'Omitting essential symptoms from a depression questionnaire most directly impairs {{c1::content validity}}.', 'Content validity is correct because the questionnaire fails to sample a core part of major depression. Spending-spree items do not substitute for depressive-symptom coverage.'),
  ('2013-246', 'In a patient with depression, fever, weight loss, ataxia, and progressive cognitive decline, test for {{c1::HIV infection}} as a potentially unifying medical cause.', 'HIV can unify constitutional, neurologic, cognitive, and psychiatric symptoms. This presentation warrants medical evaluation rather than attribution to primary depression alone.'),
  ('2013-247', 'For body dysmorphic disorder, a first-line medication option is the SSRI {{c1::fluoxetine}}.', 'Fluoxetine is an SSRI used to treat the obsessive preoccupation of body dysmorphic disorder. Antipsychotics are not standard first-line monotherapy.'),
  ('2013-248', '{{c1::Syringomyelia}} classically causes dissociated loss of pain and temperature over the shoulders and arms in a cape-like distribution.', 'Syringomyelia injures crossing spinothalamic fibers in the central cord. It may also damage anterior horn cells, causing segmental weakness and hyporeflexia.'),
  ('2013-249', 'In culturally responsive assessment, first ask the patient {{c1::what they believe explains their symptoms}}.', 'Inquiring about the patient’s explanatory model builds alliance and improves diagnostic understanding. It accompanies, rather than replaces, suicide-risk assessment.'),
  ('2013-250', 'When a therapist notices attraction to a patient with erotic transference, the appropriate next step is {{c1::professional consultation or supervision}}.', 'Seek consultation with a colleague to manage countertransference safely. Do not enact, dismiss, or abruptly withdraw from the transference.'),
  ('2013-251', 'MDMA-associated neurotoxicity is classically linked to {{c1::serotonergic}} neurons.', 'Serotonin is the neurotransmitter system classically associated with MDMA neurotoxicity. MDMA can also cause acute sympathomimetic and serotonergic complications.'),
  ('2013-252', 'The first-pass effect is {{c1::presystemic elimination}} of a drug before it reaches systemic circulation.', 'Presystemic elimination defines the first-pass effect. It commonly reflects intestinal and hepatic metabolism after oral administration.'),
  ('2013-253', 'Among commonly tested anticonvulsants, {{c1::valproate}} carries the greatest risk of fetal neural tube defects.', 'Valproate is most strongly linked to neural tube defects. Carbamazepine also carries risk, but less prominently in this comparison.'),
  ('2013-254', 'The key distinction between social anxiety disorder and agoraphobia is the {{c1::nature of the feared object or consequence}}.', 'Nature of the feared object is decisive: scrutiny in social anxiety versus escape or help concerns in agoraphobia. Avoidance occurs in both disorders.'),
  ('2013-255', 'Improved behavioral inhibition during adolescence is most closely linked to {{c1::maturation of the prefrontal cortex}}.', 'Maturation of the prefrontal cortex strengthens executive control and response inhibition. Reward sensitivity alone does not explain this developmental gain.'),
  ('2013-256', 'Asking how an apple and an orange are alike assesses {{c1::abstract reasoning}}.', 'Abstract reasoning is tested by asking for a higher-order similarity between two objects. A shared category such as “fruit” is an abstract response.'),
  ('2013-257', 'The ADHD comorbidity associated with the greatest worsening of later prognosis is {{c1::conduct disorder}}.', 'Conduct disorder substantially worsens the long-term prognosis of childhood ADHD. It signals a need to assess serious externalizing behavior beyond core ADHD symptoms.'),
  ('2013-258', 'Drink-refusal rehearsal and relapse-prevention planning are hallmark interventions of {{c1::cognitive-behavioral therapy}} for alcohol use disorder.', 'Cognitive-behavioral therapy teaches concrete coping, refusal, and relapse-prevention skills. Motivational enhancement therapy instead centers on ambivalence and readiness for change.'),
  ('2013-259', 'The central goal of family work in dementia is to {{c1::enhance the effectiveness of care for the patient}} while supporting caregivers.', 'Work with families should enhance patient care and caregiver sustainability. Equal caregiving roles or immediate placement decisions are not universal requirements.'),
  ('2013-260', 'A typical acute cluster headache attack can be aborted with {{c1::high-flow oxygen}}.', 'Oxygen is a first-line acute abortive treatment for cluster headache. Topiramate is preventive, and oral agents may act too slowly.'),
  ('2013-261', 'The strongest predictor of completed suicide among the listed risk factors is a {{c1::history of prior suicide attempts}}.', 'History of suicide attempts is the strongest listed predictor of completed suicide. It must be integrated with current intent, means, symptoms, and supports.'),
  ('2013-262', 'When a patient discloses intimate-partner violence, the best initial psychiatric response is to {{c1::listen nonjudgmentally and further explore safety and concerns}}.', 'Listen to the patient''s concerns and further explore them because disclosure should be met with validation and collaborative safety assessment. Pressuring the patient to leave can reduce trust and may increase danger.'),
  ('2013-263', 'New fluctuating confusion after starting an SSRI in an older adult should prompt {{c1::serum electrolyte testing for hyponatremia}}.', 'Serum electrolytes are correct because SSRIs can cause hyponatremia, particularly in older adults. Hyponatremia can present as delirium with fluctuating attention and alertness.'),
  ('2013-264', '{{c1::Mentalization}} is the capacity to interpret one’s own and others’ behavior in terms of underlying mental states.', 'Mentalization is correct because the patient recognizes that an internal image of the boss may differ from the boss’s actual perspective and behavior. This is reflective functioning, not a defense mechanism.'),
  ('2013-265', 'For generalized anxiety in a patient with pulmonary disease, {{c1::buspirone}} is preferred over benzodiazepines because it lacks significant respiratory depression.', 'Buspirone is correct because it is anxiolytic without significant respiratory depression. Benzodiazepines can worsen respiratory compromise, and propranolol can cause bronchospasm.'),
  ('2013-266', 'A {{c1::contralateral leg-predominant weakness}} with abulia localizes most strongly to an {{c2::anterior cerebral artery}} infarct.', 'Left anterior cerebral artery is correct because it supplies medial motor cortex controlling the contralateral leg. Medial frontal involvement also explains abulia or passivity.'),
  ('2013-267', 'A hypertensive crisis caused by an MAOI interaction may be treated acutely with {{c1::phentolamine}}, an alpha-adrenergic antagonist.', 'Phentolamine is correct because alpha blockade counteracts catecholamine-mediated vasoconstriction in MAOI-related hypertensive crisis. Dantrolene and bromocriptine instead suggest neuroleptic malignant syndrome.'),
  ('2013-268', 'For a child with a {{c1::mild anxiety disorder}}, the initial treatment of choice is {{c2::psychotherapy, typically CBT}}.', 'Psychotherapy alone is correct as the initial approach for mild childhood anxiety. SSRIs are more often reserved for greater severity, persistence, or inadequate response to therapy.'),
  ('2013-269', 'During an ECT course, concurrent {{c1::lithium}} can increase the risk of {{c2::delirium and prolonged seizures}}.', 'Lithium is correct because it has a recognized association with delirium and prolonged seizures when continued with ECT. The other listed drugs have different effects on seizure threshold or tolerability.'),
  ('2013-270', 'For a patient who lacks decision-making capacity, clinicians should {{c1::seek an appropriate surrogate}} and make decisions in the patient’s {{c2::best interests when wishes are unknown}}.', 'Seek out decision-making surrogates and deliver treatment in the patient''s best interests because incapacity requires a patient-centered substitute for autonomous consent. Hospitalization and full-code status are not automatic consequences of incapacity.'),
  ('2013-271', 'In Huntington disease, {{c1::caudate atrophy}} causes the characteristic enlargement and altered contour of the {{c2::frontal horns of the lateral ventricles}}.', 'Caudate is correct because it borders the frontal horns of the lateral ventricles. Its degeneration produces the characteristic imaging appearance in Huntington disease.'),
  ('2013-272', 'Compared with pharmacotherapy alone, CBT for panic disorder is associated with a {{c1::lower relapse rate after treatment discontinuation}}.', 'Lower relapse following discontinuation is correct because CBT teaches exposure and cognitive skills that can be retained after formal treatment ends. It does not imply that CBT is always less costly or more accessible.'),
  ('2013-273', 'The key behavioral mechanism in {{c1::selective mutism}} is {{c2::anxiety-driven avoidance of speaking}}.', 'Avoidant behavior is correct because selective mutism reflects anxiety-linked avoidance in specific social settings. It is not chiefly a language disorder or oppositional refusal.'),
  ('2013-274', 'In late-life depression, SSRIs are preferred over TCAs in part because they are {{c1::less likely to cause orthostatic hypotension}}.', 'Less likely to cause orthostatic hypotension is correct because SSRIs lack the prominent alpha-adrenergic blockade of TCAs. This reduces an important fall-related risk in older adults.'),
  ('2013-275', 'After appropriate evaluation of functional abdominal pain, a {{c1::low-dose tricyclic antidepressant such as imipramine}} can be used for pain modulation.', 'Start low dose imipramine because TCAs can reduce chronic functional pain at low doses while also addressing depressive symptoms. Opioids are not preferred for this long-term pain presentation.'),
  ('2013-276', 'Suspected {{c1::acute laryngeal dystonia}} should be treated urgently with parenteral {{c2::benztropine}} and airway support.', 'Benztropine is correct because acute dystonia responds rapidly to parenteral anticholinergic treatment. Laryngeal involvement makes airway assessment and emergency response essential.'),
  ('2013-277', 'A malpractice claim generally requires {{c1::a duty of care}}, which usually arises from a physician-patient relationship or comparable professional undertaking.', 'Duty is correct because the psychiatrist did not form a physician-patient relationship or undertake this patient’s treatment. Complications alone establish damage, not a duty owed by the lecturer.'),
  ('2013-278', '{{c1::Lacrimation, yawning, myalgias, and diarrhea}} are characteristic signs of {{c2::opioid (including heroin) withdrawal}}.', 'Heroin is correct because the symptom cluster is classic opioid withdrawal. Alcohol and benzodiazepine withdrawal instead raise concern for tremor, seizures, and delirium.'),
  ('2013-279', 'Vitamin B12 deficiency can cause {{c1::subacute combined degeneration with posterior-column dysfunction}} plus {{c2::peripheral neuropathy and cognitive change}}.', 'Vitamin B12 deficiency causes a characteristic myeloneuropathy with neurocognitive manifestations. Posterior-column dysfunction is the key localizing clue.'),
  ('2013-280', 'The initial treatment for recurrent isolated sleep paralysis is {{c1::adequate, regular sleep}}.', 'Getting sufficient sleep addresses a common precipitant of isolated sleep paralysis. Stress and sleep loss often make episodes more frequent.'),
  ('2013-281', 'When acetaminophen ingestion timing is {{c1::unknown}}, {{c2::N-acetylcysteine}} should be started if clinically meaningful toxicity cannot be excluded.', 'C — be administered in cases when the timing of acetaminophen ingestion is unknown is the corrected answer. Intravenous administration is not mandatory solely because an adult is nonpregnant.'),
  ('2013-282', 'In a school-aged child, {{c1::generalized spike-and-wave EEG activity}} with brief lapses of awareness suggests {{c2::childhood absence epilepsy}}.', 'Childhood absence epilepsy commonly appears as brief staring or inattentive episodes. Generalized spike-and-wave activity supports the diagnosis.'),
  ('2013-283', 'A psychiatrist’s {{c1::financial investment in a current patient’s business}} is a boundary violation.', 'Investing in a patient’s business is correct because it creates a conflicted financial dual relationship. It risks exploitation and compromises clinical objectivity.'),
  ('2013-284', 'In a patient with a seizure disorder taking bupropion, the safest listed response is to {{c1::taper off bupropion SR and monitor}} while arranging supervised alternative treatment.', 'D — Taper off bupropion SR and monitor because seizure disorder is a current contraindication. In practice, provide a supervised transition to a safer antidepressant rather than leaving depression untreated.'),
  ('2013-285', 'Chronic {{c1::inhalant}} exposure can cause neurotoxicity with {{c2::renal tubular acidosis}}.', 'Inhalants can cause chronic CNS and peripheral nerve toxicity. Renal tubular acidosis is an important systemic clue.'),
  ('2013-286', 'Generalized {{c1::4–6 Hz irregular polyspike-and-wave activity}} is characteristic of {{c2::juvenile myoclonic epilepsy}}.', 'Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy has generalized polyspike-wave discharges. Morning myoclonic jerks are a key clinical clue.'),
  ('2013-287', 'SSRIs may increase bleeding risk because they {{c1::inhibit platelet serotonin uptake and platelet function}}.', 'Serotonergic antidepressants can impair platelet aggregation. This matters when patients also receive antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy.'),
  ('2013-288', 'An ethically permissible missed-appointment fee should be {{c1::clearly contracted with the patient in advance}}.', 'The APA position supports a missed-appointment charge when it is part of an agreed treatment contract. Transparency and consistency are essential.'),
  ('2013-289', 'A credible threat toward an identifiable person can trigger a {{c1::duty to protect}}, with the required actions determined by {{c2::applicable jurisdictional law}}.', 'A — inform the police that the patient poses a potential danger is the historical best-listed response. The exact duty and required recipient vary by jurisdiction.'),
  ('2013-290', 'A clinician who reasonably suspects {{c1::child physical abuse}} must {{c2::report it to child protective services}}.', 'Frequent violent handling of a child is reportable suspected abuse. Lack of an obvious injury does not remove the reporting duty.'),
  ('2013-291', 'In hypnotizable individuals, hypnosis can safely {{c1::decrease pain and stress}} after a traumatic event.', 'Hypnosis can be a useful adjunct for pain and stress in receptive patients. It is not limited to physical symptoms alone.'),
  ('2013-292', '{{c1::Sickle cell disease}} can cause intracranial vasculopathy and recurrent {{c2::ischemic strokes}} in young patients.', 'Sickle cell disease can produce recurrent cerebrovascular events through intracranial vasculopathy. Recurrent pain episodes support the systemic diagnosis.'),
  ('2013-293', 'Compared with younger adults, alcohol dependence in adults over 65 is {{c1::less common}} but may have {{c2::more serious adverse consequences}}.', 'Alcohol dependence is less common after age 65. Medical comorbidity and medication interactions can magnify its harms.'),
  ('2013-294', 'Sudden {{c1::ataxia, nystagmus, and dysarthria}} after a viral illness in a child suggest {{c2::acute cerebellitis}}.', 'Acute cerebellitis is often postinfectious in children. Abrupt cerebellar signs distinguish it from progressive hereditary ataxias.'),
  ('2013-295', 'DSM-III marked a shift toward {{c1::explicit descriptive diagnostic criteria}} to improve diagnostic {{c2::reliability}}.', 'DSM-III introduced operationalized descriptive diagnostic criteria. Its central goal was more reliable diagnosis across clinicians.'),
  ('2013-296', 'In early psychodynamic group development, shared identification alongside competition for the leader’s attention reflects {{c1::engagement and differentiation}}.', 'A, B — Engagement / Differentiation is the retained combined answer. The stem depicts an overlapping transition between affiliation and individuality.'),
  ('2013-297', 'Acute alcohol use classically produces {{c1::decreased REM sleep}}, even when it initially feels sedating.', 'B — Decrease in REM sleep: alcohol suppresses REM early in the night and often disrupts sleep later.'),
  ('2013-298', 'A clinician’s personal history that drives excessive urgency to change a patient’s behavior is {{c1::countertransference}}.', 'Countertransference is correct because the psychiatrist’s past with an alcoholic parent is shaping the current therapeutic stance. The patient’s ambivalence does not explain the clinician’s insistence.'),
  ('2013-299', 'When an inpatient’s dangerous conduct prompts action primarily to protect vulnerable peers, the ethical basis is {{c1::duty to other hospitalized patients}}.', 'D — Duty to other hospitalized patients: the central rationale is protection of other vulnerable inpatients from foreseeable harm.'),
  ('2013-300', 'Among the listed observational designs, a {{c1::cohort study}} can establish {{c2::that exposure precedes outcome}}.', 'A — Cohort: following exposure groups over time establishes temporality, which is necessary for causal inference.')
